{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking","id":1511902,"title":"Garou Gifts: Ranking, Thinking, Overthinking","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-05-17T12:59:31-07:00","id":1511902,"title":"Garou Gifts: Ranking, Thinking, Overthinking","content":"I'm going to break down Gifts by level and category, bit by bit. If it's Wyrmish, Fera-only, obscure or lost it's going to feature much much later, if at all. This is intended to focus on the Gifts you will actually be getting in play, hence starting at level 1 and working up. I'll be considering utility (both inside and outside combat), room for creativity, fun ways to get the most enjoyment out of a Gift, when you should take it, why, and so on. Accessibility is also going to come up when relevant.<br /> <br /> Given how cheap Gifts are, especially the level 1 and 2 gifts, finding value and fun in a great number of them can lead to a fun and varied playstyle. Enjoy and let me know what you think! I'd love to hear if I missed any whacky or obvious applications.<br /> <br /> Let's begin with <u><b>Rank 1 Homid Gifts</b></u>!<br /> <br /> Note that I will be dividing Gifts into three descriptive categories intended to give a really quick distillation of my thoughts. Think of it like traffic lights. <b><span style=\"color:#27ae60\">Green</span></b> are must-haves, or possibly must-reworks depending on your table and how adversarial or cheeky the environment is. Do what works for your table of course. <b><span style=\"color:#f39c12\">Amber</span> </b>are fun, cool, perhaps niche or minor effects, have good RP potential perhaps, and are ultimately worth taking if you like the concept or have spare EXP. Thankfully most Gifts are, based on what I know before starting this thorough review, Green or Amber! <b><span style=\"color:#c0392b\">Red</span> </b>are Gifts which are underwhelming, overcosted, too pricey to use, have minor or ultra-niche effects, and don't have any &quot;hook&quot; for RP that makes them worth taking. I'm hoping very, very few will be Red, but I can think of a few!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Apecraft's Blessings:</b></u></span> we start off VERY strong here. Even with a modest Wits + Crafts pool, -1 or -2 difficulty to most practical tasks you can imagine is exceptional. This Gift is good enough that it would still be viable at Rank 3 or 4. It's taken very frequently at char-gen and with good reason; it's one of Homid's superpowers, and STs should be cautious about Ancestor Spirits teaching it to Metis and Lupus, even with the extra cost. Driving, shooting, cooking, sewing, pole vaulting, piloting an airplane without any training... the near universal applications are incredible even if you were to limit the difficulty decrease to 1 or 2. RAW there's no reason you shouldn't shoot for a 5 or 6 success Wits + Crafts check.<br /> <br /> If you find this one is creating problems, adding a Gnosis cost or making it once per scene wouldn't be too absurd. See what works at your table.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>City Running:</b></u></span> this one is pretty neat. Spending a point of Rage can hurt especially if you don't have much to spend, but it's a fun ability which makes you impossible to catch for many. Picture someone scaling a fire escape as swiftly as they could run the 100 meters. The difficulty reduction is also nice, and while this one is somewhat niche it's bound to come up in a traditionally urban WoD game; back alleys, fire exits, rooftops and so on are pretty likely places to be chasing Fomori, Vampires and so on.<br /> <br /> On the proactive front, having an urban base which is almost impossible to access without this Gift could be a pretty neat setup. You can also try to drag your confrontations and battles onto scaffolding for cool cinematic fights in which you are much more able to navigate easily.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Master of Fire:</b></u></span> underrated because people don't imagine their Garou burning to death, and because spending Gnosis on a niche ability seems eh. The thing is that while you don't often run through burning buildings, fight hunters with flamethrowers or arsonist blood mages, nor will you frequently run into a Flambeau duelist or a fire-breathing Bane/Fomor, all these threats do add up to coming up at least once per campaign I'd wager, and perhaps much more often than that.<br /> <br /> Aggravated damage is still hellish for Garou, as you can't soak it in your Breed form (unless you're Metis) and you can't regenerate it either. Turning fires into Bashing damage makes them somewhat trivial sources of damage, and also allows you to proactively use fire as a weapon. Yes you won't often have an incendiary grenade thrown at you, but a packmate chucking napalm or a fire-starting Gift at you while you have an enemy pinned down... it's messy and risky, but &quot;I take (3-soak) bashing per turn, you take 3 aggravated damage per turn&quot; is a pretty good trade off and will make you look pretty badass.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b><u>Persuasion:</u></b></span> They did it again! This one's just Apecraft's Blessings but for social stuff! A wonderfully strong social Gift which you'll see being utilized more frequently than any other Gift in some PBP servers, Persuasion is a neat power which could also do fine as a level 3 or 4, not that it's necessarily a problem at level 1. The lack of a Gnosis cost does stand out.<br /> <br /> My only serious suggestion to change this power which should be used at all tables is to note that &quot;has the potential to change long-held views&quot;, &quot;successes are much more powerful than normal social checks&quot; etc. should only apply to regular mortals. If Garou could un-bigot eachother, or re-bigot eachother, the setting would look entirely different. It's also a power which plays up the necessity of having some form of check to notice when supernatural powers are being used, much like Presence does. Garou should consider utilizing this Gift upon other Garou without good justification to be inflammatory.<br /> <br /> Noting its lack of cost and it lasting all scene, the most optimal way to use this Gift is forever, always, in all situations. It is however not very fun. If you are left to police your own usage of this power by a relaxed ST or a server where homebrew is avoided, I'd advise utilizing it only when you can picture the character going &quot;hmm, this is tricky - perhaps I need a little help&quot;. Your Garou will come off as more relatable and less like a character in a game applying their standard buff before a fight.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Smell of Man:</b></u></span> This one is fun and quite niche, and I quite enjoy it. I don't have much to say besides the obvious; it'll be handy when people have guard dogs, angry cats, wild wolves, other animal kinfolk and so on. If you want to actively utilize this power aggressively, dragging a fight you're having into a situation with aggressive domesticated or wild animals will have the favour tilted slightly in your direction, as you are treated like a friend by guard dogs and attacked less savagely by wild animals, but the benefit is very mild compared to the effort you've taken to arrange it. If you are a Galliard this combines very well with Beast Speech, since not only can you talk to domesticated animals they will also treat you like a great friend.<br /> <br /> Much like City Running and Master of Fire, this is one where you can go out of your way to try and trigger it to your advantage, and you can build a fun concept around it. The crazy cat lady Bone Gnawer Galliard, the security firm owning Glass Walker Ragabash who has a charmed touch with vicious guard dogs, the Child of Gaia vet who helps to rehabilitate traumatized and abused animals from fighting rings. If you want the fun RP flex of animals adoring you this is a neat one, and that alone is worth 3 EXP.<br /> <br /> <hr /><br /> <br /> As you can see Homids start off quite strong! Two Greens and three Ambers, and the RP potential and fun held within these offerings is pretty great. Given how prevalent Homids are, it's good that these Gifts are fun to use and RP with; they're some of the most common Gifts in WTA!<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1550575#post1550575\">See this post for the Glossary.</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 05-02-2025, 11:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-17T13:26:05-07:00","id":1511906,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 METIS GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Create Element:</b></u></span> This one raises a lot of questions. Do you have to see where you're summoning the water/earth/fire/air? Does your ST have a good sense of volume and understand physics? Are people likely to be dying of thirst in your game? How much time are you going to be spending in abandoned Etherships with critical air supply? Is inflicting 1-3 Agg damage on a target within 60 feet a good attack? Can Create Element be dodged? Can earth appear in the air to drop?<br /> <br /> My take is that the material can only appear in places you can visualize, check for a good comparison when size is important, talk to your ST about expectations, and earth appears connected to the ground. As earth is want to do. The attacking potential is kinda interesting given that a Metis Ahroun might, potentially, lack ranged options. And a cubic foot is much bigger than you think.<br /> <br /> If you're forced to hold your breath for any reason, you could fill your huge Garou lungs with this Gift. And if you're on the run and can't stop for a drink, you can potentially fill your stomach with water. Both uses are niche but could save your life if they come up.<br /> <br /> Spending 1 Gnosis per pop is expensive, but the RP potential of the Gift is neat. It's obviously magical in a fun-ly demonstrative way you could show to a Fallow Kinfolk or new Cub. It has religious significance; pure elements conjured from Gaia's Gnosis, a sign that she lives, that creation is still possible, that there can be abundance again. It's among the more &quot;obviously magical&quot; level 1 gifts and I like that. <span style=\"color:#f39c12\">Amber</span> in most games, but <span style=\"color:#27ae60\">Green</span> if your ST is super permissive with it and access to water/air/fire are critical in your game.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Primal Anger:</b></u></span> Given that my Metis PC doesn't hate himself, this one raises interesting questions for me. Is self-hatred a necessary component of these Metis Gifts, or is it granted to Metis by the spirits because a typical Metis deals with shame and repression?<br /> <br /> Emotional stuff aside this power is wonderfully strong. If you have Huge Size or a means of healing, taking 1 Agg isn't that bad. This also combines really well with any powerful Gift that utilizes Rage, such as Channeling. Getting your Rage to go above your usual max could also be useful if you need to get yourself into a Frenzy for some reason, say to evade mind control or provide a threat which can't be reasoned with. I don't think this power needs any changes at all, it's a perfect Green; strong in an engaging way, without straying into feeling 'busted'. It's good on all Auspices, it's good for all Tribes, and it combos amazingly with the various powers you'll pick up as you go up in Rank.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Rat Head:</b></u></span> I adore this one. It has a body horror feeling to it which is nice and creepy. If your ST will let you use it in creative ways it's even better; can you fit your whole arm into a space you can fit your fingers into? If you can fit your face into a pipe, can you hide your whole body in it? The idea of my giant Metis Ahroun moving through someone's walls like a rat or snake is horrible, and I love it.<br /> <br /> The Gnosis cost... really isn't necessary. But it's there, and so this one takes a bit of a hit. Not only that you have to spend Gnosis and also pass a difficulty 7 roll. In all honesty this Gift would work with no cost and no roll, but we work with what we're given. It's neat, it's fun, it's a conversation starter.<br /> <br /> I am a Strength 9 Dexterity 6 Rage 5 Ahroun with Razor Claws. I am in your walls.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sense Wyrm:</b></u></span> if you aren't an idiot, one of the best Gifts in the game. Top tier. Wonderful. Such a life-saver. Sense Wyrm on its own makes you a useful pack member. Any Pack which lacks someone with Sense Wyrm is going to be metaphysically blind to the enemy in a way which could cause real trouble; always have a Metis, Theurge or Elder Brother!<br /> <br /> I'd suggest rolling for your PC with this one, sometimes. &quot;Roll at difficulty 9&quot; gives meta information; there is a really subtle Wyrm taint nearby. Roll some dice. Maybe there's no taint. Maybe it's really subtle. Maybe it's really obvious. Then tell them what they detect, based on whether they succeed or fail, or whether there was taint at all. Make sure that your fellow players / your table understands that Wyrm Taint isn't Alignment; a Garou who just climbed out of a pit where they slaughtered a hundred Banes and twenty BSDs will reek of the Wyrm. A serial killer might have a similar taint to someone using a highly tainted perfume. Oh and don't use it in Malfeas. Or do, I'm not your dad.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Shed:</b></u></span> This one is really interesting! You can slip out of ropes, chains, all kinds of bonds, and grapples too. Given that many Wyrmish enemies cover you in nasty gunk from various orifices which can have all sorts of bad effects, the ability to 'refresh' your fur is also pretty great. If your ST will let you leave behind a fake pelt with this Gift it's also pretty neat.<br /> <br /> Strictly RAW you can only lose a part of your fur you need to lose to escape from something. This also leads to a complicated full body rope tie intended to help you shed all your fur, so the strict RAW approach might be a little silly here. I see no problem with letting people shed freely with this power, if they succeed on the roll of course.<br /> <br /> Shed also feeds into this &quot;gross survivalist animal-themed powers&quot; vibe Metis have, which is neat. I like the idea of Metis being granted Gifts by a generous spirit world that wants them to survive in spite of mistreatment. So it ticks some nice RP boxes too.<br /> <br /> ========================================<br /> <br /> Still looking good! No Reds yet, which doesn't surprise me. Level 1 Gifts are staples with a lot of attention paid to them. We'll get to the bad ones eventually.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-12-2023, 01:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-17T17:13:39-07:00","id":1511942,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 LUPUS GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Level 1 Lupus Gifts lose out compared to Homid and Metis somewhat, so you'll lean more on your Tribal and Auspice lists. Let's get into it!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Hare's Leap:</b></u></span> Passing a roll to double your jumping distances for a scene is handy. Tripling with WP is also pretty good. Given how far you can leap in Lupus, this is pretty amazing.<br /> <br /> For a running leap you roll Strength + Athletics difficulty 3, and in Lupus you leap 7 feet per success. With Hare's Leap you can get 14 or 21 per success. Given the absurd distances you can leap with this power, it is in fact Green. I always wrote it off as kinda meh, not too good not too bad, but a significant weakness for Garou is that they are better in melee than in ranged. Well the Corax with the razor sharp agg damage feathers isn't laughing now, is he.<br /> <br /> If a Metis grabs this, reflexively shifting into Crinos midair makes for a fun combo. You can use this to reach helicopters, distant boats, ranged Fomori, snipers, and you can proactively set traps where leaping away 60 foot or so is the only way to avoid harm. Fighting in places with horribly complex platforms and the need for constant jumping is also pretty handy.<br /> <br /> In a way this is just a superior City Running, and it's one that your ST isn't likely to gatekeep; I'd argue it's worth 5 EXP if you've got it going spare.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Heightened Senses:</b></u></span> The Gnosis cost is painful, but if you're Lupus you've got Gnosis to spare. +1 Primal-Urge and -3 Perception difficulties in Crinos and Hispo are pretty amazing. Technically RAW you have -4 Perception difficulties in Hispo, as we're only told that the power doesn't stack with the Perception bonus of Lupus, but that's clearly not the intent.<br /> <br /> This is a great one for hunts, tracking and investigations. It also lets you pull off insane wolf-like scenting while in your Homid form, making this a handy investment for detectives in urban scenarios.<br /> <br /> The RP potential of becoming a super-detective is neat, and a private eye lupus who sniffs crime scenes to learn the brand of aftershave of the murderer's landlord is fun. 1 Gnosis is costly for that, but you have 5 to spend if you're a Lupus and the investigation may happen in a session of its own where there aren't other calls on your spirit power. In a PBP server this one would let you pick up stuff like who's bunking with who, which is bound to make you very unpopular, but also very knowledgeable. Could combo well with a Shadow Lord concept.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Sense Prey:</b></u></span> You are a Werewolf. You can find dinner.<br /> <br /> The fringe RP benefit of &quot;I provide for my whole pack&quot; is neat and all, but your pack should be thriving with the help of a supernaturally gifted transforming spirit-touched generation-spanning ultra-leader. To reiterate, you are a Werewolf. You can find dinner.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Predator's Arsenal:</b></u></span> If you're in a scenario where you don't mind leaving a body with claw or bite marks, you're in a scenario where you can use Glabro, Hispo or Lupus, or perhaps even Crinos. If you desperately need a claw in Homid form, you can do a Partial Transformation. This Gift is just plain bad, especially since the weapons you manifest only do Lethal.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Revising this up to Amber after feedback; it's still lower down in my opinion but the subtlety of it is neat. A situation where having claws/fangs (even subtle ones) is something you can get away with but slipping into Glabro isn't is a tiny bit niche, but such situations do exist, and of course you don't have to spend WP or do a tricky roll to get these Lethal claws. The Agg claws accessible through partial transformation are awesome but they are also very obvious and would look weird on a human body! Basically this one fits Amber, on reflection, but I don't have to like the gift either :'D<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Prey Mind:</b></u></span> Each success on a difficult check, one which will typically be difficulty 9, gives you +1 <i>die</i> to checks to run away, escape and hide. The niche is already narrow. You need to be on the back foot and in need of escape, forced to fall back.<br /> <br /> Even if you get 2 or 3 bonus dice, those might not do you much good; impacting difficulty would be much more powerful. Furthermore you're now outrunning your Pack.<br /> <br /> If you need a swift escape, Hare's Leap in addition to the natural speed of Lupus form has you covered. Prey Mind is too hard to use, too narrow, and only helps you when you're losing. Having Gifts for emergencies is good, don't get me wrong. Gifts that heal or help you survive are awesome. But this one is just so marginal and so sad. Can only give it red.<br /> <br /> ================================================<br /> <br /> Sense Prey, Predator's Arsenal and Prey Mind are very sad. I would suggest keeping Sense Prey as is; it's a meh power for a PC to have, but it's meant to solve a problem that matters more &quot;IRL&quot; than it does in a typical game. For Predator's Arsenal, simply use the Partial Transformation system as the gift's mechanic; the Gift makes it so that the partial transformation of growing claws and fangs happens without spending WP or making a roll; that's handy! Finally Prey Mind would work better if it influenced difficulties instead of adding dice.<br /> <br /> I was shocked by how useful Hare's Leap is however, when you get into the fine details of the mechanics.<br /> <br /> If you're playing a Lupus, I'd suggest spending more EXP on your other lists at Rank 1, or saving up for a good Gift from another Breed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 03:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-17T20:01:18-07:00","id":1511960,"title":"","content":"While in general I'm enjoying this, there feels like an odds assumption of urban focused play where the Veil doesn't matter.<br /> <br /> It also feels like you're a bit overly generous to the Crinos-born Gifts, and pretty harsh to the Lupus ones. Primal Anger, out of combat, is a huge risk with all the downsides of having high Rage, and in-combat taking an Agg damage for some Rage is a huge gamble. Rage is cheap to raise, and there are better source of extra points like certain Totems that give their packs a pool of more Rage. You completely toss aside Rat-head's cost and roll, for a far more niche Gift than you seen to want to acknowledge. The flavor might be cool, for your Huge Size PC's head isn't fitting in the extra space between walls in Homid, let alone Crinos. If your Crinos head can fit through it, you could do it in Homid or Lupus without any really issue; Rat-Head also doesn't do anything about the material strength of what you're trying to squeeze through.<br /> <br /> Heightened Senses isn't really given any creativity points despite the freedom to try crazy superhuman sensory tricks with it. It's solid and incredibly broad in utility. You have a point about plugging Predator's Arsenal into the Partial Transformation rules, but your suggestion doesn't actually change what the Gift would do. Glabro's teeth and claws don't do any extra damage besides the +2 Str over Homid. Having constant access to lethal damage for no roll or cost, that you can hide around humans, ignore metal detectors or being disarmed, and the wounds you level would look more like a vampire with Protean than a wolf... I can understand not giving it a green, but red feels like you haven't thought about all the stuff you could do with it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-18T02:01:50-07:00","id":1511988,"title":"","content":"Rat-head is best used in lupus or homid, not in crinos. Primal-Anger is seriously useless, you gain Rage by stubbing your toe, you are not going to run out.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-18T04:30:04-07:00","id":1511998,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1511960#post1511960\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While in general I'm enjoying this, there feels like an odds assumption of urban focused play where the Veil doesn't matter.<br /> <br /> It also feels like you're a bit overly generous to the Crinos-born Gifts, and pretty harsh to the Lupus ones. Primal Anger, out of combat, is a huge risk with all the downsides of having high Rage, and in-combat taking an Agg damage for some Rage is a huge gamble. Rage is cheap to raise, and there are better source of extra points like certain Totems that give their packs a pool of more Rage. You completely toss aside Rat-head's cost and roll, for a far more niche Gift than you seen to want to acknowledge. The flavor might be cool, for your Huge Size PC's head isn't fitting in the extra space between walls in Homid, let alone Crinos. If your Crinos head can fit through it, you could do it in Homid or Lupus without any really issue; Rat-Head also doesn't do anything about the material strength of what you're trying to squeeze through.<br /> <br /> Heightened Senses isn't really given any creativity points despite the freedom to try crazy superhuman sensory tricks with it. It's solid and incredibly broad in utility. You have a point about plugging Predator's Arsenal into the Partial Transformation rules, but your suggestion doesn't actually change what the Gift would do. Glabro's teeth and claws don't do any extra damage besides the +2 Str over Homid. Having constant access to lethal damage for no roll or cost, that you can hide around humans, ignore metal detectors or being disarmed, and the wounds you level would look more like a vampire with Protean than a wolf... I can understand not giving it a green, but red feels like you haven't thought about all the stuff you could do with it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I do acknowledge that Rat Head has a cost and a roll - it would be fine without them, but it's there. And yeah the idea of a giant Garou slipping through spaces the size of their head is just fun; obviously they'd fit into smaller spaces in Lupus! It depends on what you're squeezing through and why. The creep factor is part of why you'd want the Gift, even if it's niche. Ultimately I think the Gift is fun enough to be an Amber even if it's a bit of a niche one.<br /> <br /> Now if your use Partial Transformation to make your head become a Lupus head while you're in Crinos... that would make the power more useful... Especially once you have the level 2 gift that makes partial transformations easier...<br /> <br /> Primal Anger is one where you choose when to activate it, so you're unlikely to use it when your Rage pool is full unless you specifically want to do so; if your permanent Rage score is 1, going from 0 to 3 rage takes your chance of a Berserk frenzy from 0% to 0% after all, unless you gain more rage! The power can be misused, and it has its risks, but it's a strong one overall; the examples I give include access to healing (which costs Gnosis of course), and synergies with Gifts that cost Rage like channeling; part of why Primal Anger is good is that every time you get a Gift that spends Rage, you get another reason to use Primal Anger.<br /> <br /> Of course if you're an Ahroun you can get Silver Claws, which is a source of unlimited Rage if you want it to be! So it depends on what Rank you expect to reach, your party composition, your Tribe, your Auspice. To me Primal Anger is a must-have, but if you're more risk averse it might be one to avoid. At the end of the day your opinion on risk and resource management will effect which powers you like!<br /> <br /> Heightened Senses I was too harsh towards; Amber is accurate, but I talk it down a lot and don't mention some of the cool applications. I kinda wish that certain aspects of it were wholly passive, instead of costing Gnosis, but the power's workable. There will be a lot of Amber Gifts which would be Green if their Gnosis cost was removed.<br /> <br /> The reason I brought up partial transformation for Predator's Arsenal is that you can grow claws and fangs with partial transformation which deal Agg damage, although they would be pretty obvious! Having easily concealed Brawl-based lethal weapons is handy enough, and no cost is neat, but I personally don't particularly care for it. You, as a Garou, are a weapon that can pass through any metal detector unnoticed. Fighting in Homid in a situation where if you're spotted from behind in Homid you're fine but from behind in Glabro you're screwed, but there isn't enough scrutiny to see the hand you're attacking with has claws... like it's definitely more discreet, it's easier to hide, there are advantages to it, and at the end of the day most enemies are as screwed over by Lethal as they are by Agg. So... with all that in mind it's kinda Amber, but I don't have to like :'D Very glad to hear your thoughts though! I think differing perspectives really can just be down to what you value and expect in a story, which is interesting.<br /> <br /> And Ana, &quot;you get Rage for stubbing your toe&quot; feels like a pretty huge exaggeration. If your ST runs it so that you can recover Rage almost 100% reliably with little effort at short notice then yeah, Primal Urge drops to Amber or Red. I'm assuming the game is being run so that Rage is a resource that waxes and wanes with the beats of the story; my PC has gained it mid-session from watching two Fomori taunting rhinos in a zoo by throwing stones at them for instance, but that was a story-specific beat which made sense. There will be many situations where he needs more Rage and there aren't shitty bane-infested youths around.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 05-18-2023, 07:40 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-18T10:52:38-07:00","id":1512027,"title":"","content":"I get that some things are just a matter of personal taste. That's why I'm not really interested in debating if Sense Prey should get a pass or not. The value of what are essentially &quot;make not so hard downtime activities automatic/awesome,&quot; as Gifts is something beyond one Gift (as there's a bunch of them esp. if we get beyond W20 core). For some play styles they're useless, some they're essential, and most they're just highly niche. This sort of ranking guide can't try to address all of that. Maybe another color or tag for Gifts that are so playstyle/chronicle topic dependent.<br /> <br /> Like, I completely agree that regardless of context Prey Mind is a weak-sauce Gift. Like far too many Gifts, it's made worse by WtA tending to make rolls high difficulty and higher difficulty in the moments when you need them the most if they get rolled.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-18T11:42:43-07:00","id":1512031,"title":"","content":"I think there are must-take-regardless gifts (resist toxin) and some why-ever-bother powers but most are either obvious or contextual to your character/story. <br /> <br /> <br /> Also<br /> IIRC<br /> Create element with a good bit of gnosis can have you set multiple targets ablaze or drop multiple lethal boulders on multiple heads in a single turn without doing anything conspicuous yourself. Anywhere you can see within 18m: incredible. Unfortunately, it's a Gnosis roll, so it's status as a god-power is dependent on your character investing heavily in Gnosis (weeps in fetish-creating theurge) and I think not being in a dense Gauntlet area","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-18T11:57:05-07:00","id":1512033,"title":"","content":"The difficulty for Create Element's roll isn't actually specified, and isn't stated to care at all about the Gauntlet. I can't quickly find a reference for an errata to that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-18T12:25:53-07:00","id":1512036,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1512033#post1512033\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The difficulty for Create Element's roll isn't actually specified, and isn't stated to care at all about the Gauntlet. I can't quickly find a reference for an errata to that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Unstated difficulty is default difficulty, i.e. 6.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-18T12:53:24-07:00","id":1512040,"title":"","content":"That's not actually how the book states it*, and plenty of Gifts specify being difficulty 6; which would be unnecessary if unstated was 6 anyway. Considering how few Gifts call for a roll and don't specify a difficulty or method of determining one, there's more evidence towards this being an oversight rather than all the instances of the Gift diff being a flat 6 being overly cautious (and if the authors were going to be clear that the diff is 6 if not stated, they should use the Rank 1 and 2 Gifts as reminders of that when it comes up).<br /> <br /> Create Element is also a Gift the screams for a quick chart of different difficulties for how exact you're trying to be with it (filling an empty tub in front of your PC vs. filling the driver's side of a car that's speeding by) to help create a more consistent expectation of what the Gift can do (or at least can do reliably).<br /> <br /> * - by the Chapter 5 rules, if the difficulty is unstated by the book, the ST gets the option to make it whatever they want, and only if neither the book nor the ST sets a difficulty do you default to 6.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-20T04:52:02-07:00","id":1512206,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 RAGABASH GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Time for some RAGABASH GIFTS - these five are meant to support being the mercurial trickster, questioner and intrigue delver. Let's see!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Blur of the Milky Eye:</b></u></span> this one's interesting because its mechanics and flavour diverge. We're told that the effect is visual, which is an interesting limitation, but the mechanics tell us that it effects difficulties of Perception checks; implying it applies to scent, smell etc.<br /> <br /> Given that my PC is blind I find this gift interesting. I'm going to assume for the purposes of ranking that it impacts all five senses. &quot;Perception rolls made to detect him&quot; is interesting because it could apply to mystic senses too; that wording is very broad. Have fun arguing that even if a Hermetic Mage scries your location, they should have a hard time actually spotting you in the scene they witness.<br /> <br /> A Difficulty 8 Manipulation + Stealth is hard though. Ultimately the gift is a very solid <span style=\"color:#f39c12\">Amber</span>; it's a great one to have, but the difficulty to use it is very high for some reason. With a good dice pool you'll get great mileage out of it. Remaining undetected and leading ambushes is a very great swing in the party's direction as turn economy is everything, and a round without resistance will help bring a fight to a close much sooner.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Infectious Laughter:</b></u></span> Much like Liar's Face, this one is a sort of pseudo-mind control situation. You can make people lose hold of their anger and forget what made them angry. That's potent! One thing to keep in mind is that it says nothing of the memories of those who were not angry. Spirits are noteworthy in the sense that what makes them angry is quite different to what makes others angry. You could make Great White Tail forget you did not greet him respectfully, which is great. However the Squirrel Jaggling that follows one of the Caern's Elders around might have been more curious about you than angry, and later remind their friend of what you said that angered them.<br /> <br /> In a way this is a sort of &quot;undo&quot; button in RP. A CTRL+Z. A rewind. The utility of this in roleplay with both PCs and NPCs is pretty amazing. One question worth resolving with your ST is whether people who are familiar with the power can recognize it being done. Do people wonder if they were recently mind wiped when they hear Ragabash laughter?<br /> <br /> And of course you should remember that if there is suspicion of fuckery, you could be dragged before a Philodox so that truth can be sorted from fiction.<br /> <br /> This Gift is best used with good intentions, for the good of all present. Don't cover up your fuckup. Even if you're only caught 1 times in 10, that will tank your Honour. Cover up the missteps of your friends and enemies to help keep the peace. Cover up the insult that almost caused a fight, the faux pas that enraged a thin-skinned Fang, the random insult from a stranger which was about to make your Packmate frenzy. In a way this Gift is a wonderful tool for a Child of Gaia. Knowing when not to use it and when to use it makes it <span style=\"color:#27ae60\">Green</span>. If you use it indiscriminately it'll come out as <span style=\"color:#f39c12\">Amber</span>.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Liar's Face:</b></u></span> This one's a bit of a puzzle, fittingly. I won't often be mentioning the teachers of Gifts, but dear god finding a Platypus spirit sounds like a chore. This Gift is a kind of selective mind control, forcing people to disbelieve any true statement you've made, or more precisely making them believe that you lied. &quot;A giant wolf monster tore these people apart - I'm one of them&quot; &quot;they're headed to the helipad!&quot; &quot;we're going to try and escape&quot; &quot;you definitely shouldn't leave us unguarded&quot;. How this will play out will depend both on how cleverly you use it and how adversarial your ST is about such things. This is <span style=\"color:#27ae60\">Green</span> assuming you are clever, that the ST is happy to give you your Ws, and that humans will be showing up reliably. If every single npc you interact with is a Garou, Fera, Fomor, Mockery Breed, Psychic, Sorcerer, Mage, Vampire etc. then this Gift's utility drops significantly. Getting more successes than the target has Willpower is hard, and you can't rely on it.<br /> <br /> A niche yet noteworthy application of this Gift is tricking low-WP spirits. Some spirits do indeed have a low WP score, and if you manage to trick a Bane into believing you're a BSD with a &quot;oh yeah I TOTALLY serve Gaia&quot; then you'll be having a good time. And of course an ST could have a Ragabash Dancer hit a spirit with &quot;oh of course I am ABSOLUTELY a Black Spiral Dancer&quot;.<br /> <br /> One thing that does stand out about the Gift is that the target believes that you were lying. Thus a liar. This can bring scrutiny onto you. A player might have their character tell the police that the gunfire they heard came from THAT house officers - pointing to the one their packmates are inside. This is effective in the sense that the cops believe this is a lie, and thus the gunshot came from another house... but they also now have a civilian lying to their face about the source of a suspicious sound.<br /> <br /> As a result it's wiser to use this flexibly, defensively and in response to questions. Don't volunteer true info to get it to be disbelieved unless you can see an immediate chance to benefit from it without putting yourself at risk. Instead tell the truth when interrogated and make it feel like a lie. One aspect the Gift's text doesn't quite get into is whether the target is able to consider the possibility you are mistook, so YMMV. We also can't be 100% certain how the Gift interacts with Fetishes and Gifts that tell truths from lies. <span style=\"color:#27ae60\">Green</span> is the absolute best case scenario. It may come out <span style=\"color:#f39c12\">Amber</span> in the wash.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Open Seal:</b></u></span> part of what I love about Garou Gifts is how absolute they can be. &quot;We need the key to this sarcophagus&quot; - or perhaps we don't, as even if it's protected by magic you can get it open with a point of Gnosis and a Gnosis roll. For ease of access, try bringing such objects into deep wilderness or Caerns before attempting to bust in.<br /> <br /> I love this Gift and have no suggestions to change it; it works perfect. I rate it as a Green because it has great utility and does what it's meant to do pretty much perfectly. &quot;Closed or locked physical device&quot; leaves some room for interpretation, but personally I wouldn't include a locked phone or laptop; to my taste the Gift opens physical locks, overcoming their intricacies through deeper spiritual magic. Keep in mind that you won't be able to close the lock again unless there is some mechanism available to you; a padlock can be clicked back into a locked state, an intricate mechanism you've forced open without the key code won't necessarily close again.<br /> <br /> It also emphasizes the importance of guards, watchful spirits, and magical defenses in Garou society. A vault full of Silver Fang Klaives is 1 Gnosis roll, and perhaps 1 point of Gnosis, away from any greedy Cliath Ragabash... were it not for all the Garou, Kinfolk and Spirits guarding it.<br /> <br /> Fun uses include securing a place to hide such as a storage container, breaking into a target location, breaking out of a cell, performing &quot;street magic&quot;, stealing supercars, forcing the door of a vehicle open during a car chase, escaping a commercial airplane, getting a stuck window open, stealing a window, opening a container an antagonist needs the contents of so that said contents will spill or fall out during the action, and so on. It's niche yet versatile in a fun way.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Scent of Running Water:</b></u></span> flavour text says you can entirely eliminate your scent, which has implications outside of there being +2 difficulty to track you. In theory an enemy who relies largely on scent would have a hard time locating you. Worth bringing up now and then.<br /> <br /> Given that you'll often be traveling with others, having your own scent suppressed has limited utility. This power is great for clever and tricksy plans where you need to travel alone, but it's niche by design.<br /> <br /> Part of what's cool about the Gift, however, is that you can suppress it at will. And I would argue that this is stealthily the best part of the Gift. &quot;He has no scent, we're relying on his pawprints&quot; is hard to track. &quot;Oh fuck his scent is thick, we can track him easily - wait where the hell did the trail go?!&quot; is harder. You can play all sorts of clever games by turning on and suppressing your scent at will. Lead your enemies into traps. Divert those pursuing you. Go into a trapped location with your scent turned on, and leave the way you came with your scent turned off.<br /> <br /> ================================================== ====<br /> <br /> The new moons start off strong with some flavourful and strong Gifts. Next up will be Theurges, and some obvious Greens come to mind immediately.<br /> <br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 03:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-20T14:43:05-07:00","id":1512239,"title":"","content":"Open Seal's combo of costing a point of Gnosis and being a Gnosis roll difficulty of Gauntlet has always made it less fun than it could be IME. It's one of many Gifts where the difficulty is really high, especially for when it's going to matter the most. There's not going to be a lot of high tech security systems out in places with Gauntlets of 4-5. Breaking into a Pentex office building to gather intel on their local operations can easily shove the difficulty to 7-8. Foes like DNA are bumping that up to 9; being a Weaver science lab focused group and all.<br /> <br /> Considering how easily Garou can just brute force their way past locks, and mundane lock-picking/security exploits are also an option (Persuasion + Liar's Face is a social engineer's dream combo), there's really no need to make this Gift so unreliable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-21T05:40:31-07:00","id":1512278,"title":"","content":"Yeah the Gnosis pool is mean to poor Homid Ragabash who, RAW, start at Rage 1 Gnosis 1. Very choppy. At my table the Gnosis spread of Garou is 2/3/4 instead of 1/3/5 so it's a little more even, but still.<br /> <br /> Gnosis is as expensive to increase as an Ability, so it isn't that bad, so long as you get justifications to increase it. Surviving a trip to a Realm. Charming a Spirit. Being bestowed a Fetish. Bonding with a Pack Totem, and so on. Given how often it's rolled and how many powers use it for fuel it's a key thing to increase, if you have justification and want to do so. After all every Garou shouldn't *have* to be deeply connected to their spiritual side. A Mage will increase their Arete, it goes without saying, but staying at Gnosis 1 should be valid and workable.<br /> <br /> Open Seal might be a tad better with a Wits + Larceny or Willpower roll, but still, even with it using Gnosis it ain't bad. After all it costs nothing to use on mundane systems, even high tech ones; you can try again (potentially at +1 difficulty).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-21T14:02:32-07:00","id":1512299,"title":"","content":"I'm not saying it's bad. I'm saying the effect is fairly niche, and in many of those niche cases the Gift's difficulty is going to be a problem. If it weren't for tying the difficulty to the Gauntlet, making it a Gnosis roll, and making it potentially cost a Gnosis point, green would be justified... but with all that how can it not get bumped to amber? It's no red, sure, but I've just seen way too many players that want to make Raggies that get past locks just grab Computer and Mechanical Aptitudes (1 freebie each) and then use Larceny/Technology/Computers as appopriate with -2 diff rather than bother with Open Seal. Open Seal is what lupus Raggies take because of their larger base Gnosis and being less likely to be able to justify having high human thieving skills at start.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-21T15:45:13-07:00","id":1512311,"title":"","content":"IMO Open Seal would be niche if it opened a niche sort of thing. Say it only opened doors; that would be niche, but even then you can think of so many really great uses.<br /> <br /> Anything that's locked or closed covers a huge number of potential solutions both directly relevant to the plot (breaking into an uncrackable safe, freeing the ancient Strider trapped inside a magical sarcophagus) and indirectly relevant, the solutions you come up with on the fly (breaking into a car when you desperately need one, opening a water lock to flood a drydock and drown a Pentex operation). You don't need access to the lock or opening mechanism after all; you don't need to understand anything at all. Touch a closed segment of a dam, use the gift, open sesame. Touch the stone exterior of a secret passage which can only be opened from the inside? Open sesame! You get the picture.<br /> <br /> If you can spend WP on the Gnosis roll it also helps a lot - I don't know if the books give a clear answer on that, to the best of my knowledge it's just &quot;ask your ST&quot;, so YMMV.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-21T17:05:25-07:00","id":1512315,"title":"","content":"I think you're taking the Gift beyond it's intention. It can open, &quot;any sort of closed or locked physical device.&quot; The idea that you can use it to force a water lock in a drydock open is stretching the Gift's wording a lot, as is the idea of causing dams to open their gates. The Gift doesn't say it's capable of a lot of the secondary steps you're skimming over. You're making Open Seal stronger than the Control Machine Gifts.<br /> <br /> Just because you can unlock a door that can normally only be unlocked from the other side doesn't mean you automatically overcome any physicals that would stop you from actually opening the door now that the lock isn't stopping you. If you need Strength 15 to push a door open, it doesn't matter if you can unlock it magically if you can't bring Str 15 to bare.<br /> <br /> Also, look at your examples. &quot;Uncrackable safe,&quot; &quot;magical sacrcophaus,&quot; &quot;large scale water infrastructure,&quot; &quot;secret doors that the power doesn't actually let you detect,&quot; and... you're trying to make the case that this isn't a niche Gift? The only non-niche example is breaking into a car, and mundane skills are going to be vastly superior to the Gift unless the car is parked in the middle of the woods.<br /> <br /> Part of the whole problem with the Gift's design is that it isn't complimentary to characters that would want magically lock-picking the most. As I pointed out, concepts that are going to be good at breaking locks get the least out of this. Tossing a tone into Gnosis is useful, sure, but it doesn't really make you a great thief/etc<br /> <br /> I get we seem to have a difference in taste here, since you seem to value big story beat moments where Gifts can generate some big memorable beat that people will talk about for years, where I seem more concerned with the more practical utility of the Gifts so all the times they don't help is a bigger detractor to me. But it really doesn't feel like you're listening to the points I'm trying to make here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-22T01:52:35-07:00","id":1512336,"title":"","content":"&quot;Any sort of closed or locked physical device&quot; can be opened with the Gift, just as a Child of Gaia can stop &quot;any Weaver-born weapon&quot; with Jam Weapon. If a storyteller or player balks at Jam Weapon working on a nuke (with good timing and daredevil positioning to get within howling range) or Open Seal allowing you to get a water lock open (potentially with a physical push, depending on interpretation, or indeed whether the water does the pushing for you) then a sense of scale, size and &quot;well SURELY the Gift can't do something THAT big&quot; is being applied to ancient spirit magic. Open Seal does have a Nearly stuck in front of Any, which is a bit illusive. What kind of locked or closed device can't be opened by Open Seal? All we know is that it's <i>nearly</i> any.<br /> <br /> This is an important point because part of what's neat about Gifts is how global, piercing and absolute they can be. Most of them don't care about &quot;this is a level one power, thus it is weak/limited&quot;. The absoluteness feels like an intentional aspect of Gifts baked into them; you get a lot of &quot;the tunnel runner ALWAYS knows they're in danger 3 seconds before danger arrives&quot;, a lot of immunities, a lot of &quot;can open any&quot;, a lot of &quot;can stop any&quot;. I think that's good and makes Gifts feel unique and cool.<br /> <br /> The examples I gave are intentionally niche, but varied. When you can do a million and one niche things with a Gift, it stops being niche. For instance Jam Weapon can stop a HIT Mark V's plasma pistol from going off (very niche) and it can also stop a Sidhe Prince's bejeweled blade from cutting you (quite unlikely to come up)... and it can stop a goon's pistol from working, or a mugger's knife from stabbing (the &quot;break into a car&quot; examples). You can illustrate effectiveness with a variety of examples.<br /> <br /> As for needing complementary skills, with any Gift you do need mundane skill to back it up. For instance, take Razor Claws. Excellent gift. It's far less useful if you don't have good physical stats and brawl. Breaking into a car with Open Seal is best if you can then hotwire the car swiftly. Cracking open a safe with Open Seal is best if you can escape without getting caught once you get the hard drive inside.<br /> <br /> When it comes to Control Machine, I think it's worth keeping in mind that all we're doing here is making things unlock and open. With the Control Machine Gifts you could, for instance, close a water lock. Or make a ladder extend up to meet you. Or get a revolving door to freeze behind you so your pursuers pile in and get stuck. Another Gift having a little bit of overlap isn't a problem. It doing something is not a guarantee that no other Gift can do the same thing in a different way.<br /> <br /> To be clear regarding my taste, day to day utility is key and so is the ceiling of potential RP. I personally rate Open Seal as Green both because it comes up frequently enough to be useful, and because if you go out of your way to find a use for it you can do really potent stuff. And to really get deep into the mechanics, keep in mind how swift this Gift is compared to mundanely picking a lock. Picking a lock can be slow. Crouch down, pull out your tools, work your way through a number of tumblers. We're talking a matter of minutes, not seconds. Even if you're a master lockpicker, waving your hand and hoping the door will spring open on a difficulty 7 1-5 dice roll is pretty amazing. That's not considering situations where you can't physically reach the lock itself, or where the lock is protected by some sort of magic or anti-tampering.<br /> <br /> It's a powerful tool both for the amateur and the expert when it comes to thieving. For the amateur it's a cheat to outdo what you could naturally achieve, for the expert it's a means of going faster and circumventing protections which are designed to delay and frustrate expert thieves. Then consider that a person who opens a door immediately is less suspicious than someone who spends a minute crouched in front of it. Open Seal makes it look, at a glance, like you had a keycard or a key, if you time it with a bit of miming or use of a prop. That's before thinking about how often you may find yourself working without your usual tools. You get the idea.<br /> <br /> Finally, if you think I'm not listening to your points I genuinely am; I just feel differently. You'll notice that I agreed with you about rolling Gnosis, difficulty Gauntlet, spending Gnosis, being an unfortunate combo for a typical starting Homid, and also making the Gift harder to use. I also agree that the standout story defining moments are relatively niche, it's just that those exist in addition to the regular utility of going &quot;oh you think this passage/container is sealed? think again&quot;, which is bound to see use more than once per mission.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-22T02:20:00-07:00","id":1512337,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 THEURGE GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Theurge time! This should be good.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Mother's Touch:</b></u></span> the main healing Gift of Werewolves is incredible. Right out of the gate a healing PC in WTA, who will be a Theurge or a Child of Gaia, can heal Aggravated damage, Battle Scars (if caught in time) and can heal the same target repeatedly. All for one point of Gnosis per go. Having a pack member with this Gift is a huge lifesaver. On top of the above it also counters certain Gifts and Powers. Systems for disease and poisoning also make use of health levels. It's simply an incredible tool to have in your arsenal both as a fighter and in any other context.<br /> <br /> A memorable use of the Gift for me was restoring the health of a woman with the Permanent Wound flaw in a cross-splat game, to show kindness and mercy to her. Because of the nature of her flaw she regained her exceptionally brittle bones the next day, but for one day she was able to run around with the wind in her hair, do as she pleased, bump into strangers, go where she liked.<br /> <br /> It's also restored more Aggravated damage than I can count!<br /> <br /> A difficulty of 5 as the default is amazing, and very few Garou PCs will have a Rage above 5. This Gift only suffers when you're trying to heal a powerful Elder with Rage 8+. A really clear <span style=\"color:#27ae60\">Green</span>.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sense Wyrm:</b></u></span> As in the Metis section.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Spirit Snare:</b></u></span> This costs Gnosis, is hard to use, only effects spirits (which is fine), reduces their Willpower (fine) but only for combat actions (getting nicher), doesn't stack (removing a big potential application) and has a low range. The only reason this is <span style=\"color:#f39c12\">Amber</span> rather than <span style=\"color:#c0392b\">Red </span>is that slapping a -2 Willpower, which is equivalent to Dexterity and Stamina, onto a powerful spirit enemy who you are going to be battling for a huge confrontation is pretty neat*. If you're thinking of cool combos, unfortunately it's -2 WP for combat actions. If you have a Gift where the target's WP sets the difficulty it won't apply. I find this one boring and that's a shame, as if it were better it would be a really cool power.<br /> <br /> If I were buffing it I'd make it range of sight, or being able to be heard, reduce the activation difficulty, allow stacking (it costs 1 Gnosis per use after all) OR remove the Gnosis cost, and make it a reduction in Willpower which applies to all uses. RAW a spirit who is trying to run away from you won't be impacted by a snare. A snare. That thing that traps enemies. Unless your ST interprets running away in combat to be a combat action.<br /> <br /> *Reducing their attack and soak pool by 2 means 0.7 fewer successes on average, which means you are dodging their attacks more often and dealing damage more often<br /> <br /> It is a bad Gift, but it's better than Prey Mind and Sense Prey, let's put it like that. If you're going hunting for a dangerous spirit, or a common spirit enemy has a WP of 1-2, this might be worth 3 EXP.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Spirit Speech:</b></u></span> a must-have. Opens so many doors for a party, which we know I love <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Some spirits can understand and speak human languages, but not all, and a universal translator for spirit happenings is incredible. Take this ASAP. If you lack a Theurge/Elder Brother, convince a Theurge your Pack needs to speak to spirits and ask for one of you to be tested by a trustworthy spirit. It's easily worth 5 EXP. I'd pay twice that.<br /> <br /> I could talk about potential applications of this Gift, but it's too obvious and does too many things. I also can't think of any clever tricks you can pull off using this Gift, besides pretending not to understand a spirit when you can, or mistranslating what a spirit says, neither of which directly utilize the Gift.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Umbral Tether:</b></u></span> getting lost in the Umbra is a little bit niche, but I like this one. An invisible tether only the Theurge can sense which guides them back to their point of origin in the Umbra is really great. No matter what illusions or trickery stand in your way, you can glance behind you and see the way back. It's a really good safety harness for Umbral adventures, and if you can acquire Gnosis from spirits it will last as long as you need it to.<br /> <br /> An aggressive use of this a Theurge could try is entering the Umbra while pursued by enemies, intentionally going to the most tricky and mindfucking place they can think of, and then once their enemies have fallen into the trap they can make their way home freely.<br /> <br /> =========================================<br /> <br /> Theurges are in a similar spot to Ragabash with three Green Gifts at level 1, so no complaints here. I wouldn't change anything about Umbral Tether. Spirit Snare needs a lot of adjustments, and with those it would be a solid Amber instead of barely scraping by.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-01-2025, 04:46 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-22T02:54:48-07:00","id":1512338,"title":"","content":"Deleting - website messed up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 05-24-2023, 07:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-22T02:56:14-07:00","id":1512339,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 PHILODOX GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Philodox Gifts start off pretty spectacularly.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Fangs of Judgement:</b></u></span> Today I learnt that this Gift applies to your natural weaponry as a whole, not just your fangs. I like the flavour of this one, but I want more from it. Give us flavour that those who take the +2 damage dice can feel Gaia's judgement, or that they have fallen from balance. I also wish that &quot;fallen from their original purpose&quot; was the only restriction, rather than having had to fall into the Wyrm's service from your original purpose. Making it more universal would have some fun crossover applications, but still, that's a minor thing.<br /> <br /> +2 damage dice to BSDs, Fomori (though presumably not if the Fomor was born into the Wyrm's service?), corrupted spirits, Skin Dancers (unless they were BSD kinfolk?), traitorous Kinfolk, Malphean Nephandi etc. is pretty neat. It's not amazing, but it's neat. In all honesty making this a permanent buff that doesn't have an activation at all would be more cool; the moment you learn this Gift your fangs and claws become instruments of Gaia's judgement, forever. That said a cost of 1 WP and a duration of a day is pretty good. Flavourful and useful, if niche, happy to stick it in the middle pack.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Persuasion:</b></u></span> As in the Homid section; this makes Philodox a great Auspice for Metis and Lupus who want a more social focus.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Resist Pain:</b></u></span> A huge buff to a standard mechanic; you can spend 1 WP to ignore wound penalties for a round (or one action, depending on your corebook: at my table we say one round). This Gift lets you spend 1 WP to ignore wound penalties for the whole scene. These penalties include significant discomfort which impacts RP, up to -5 dice, your speed being reduced to half, or to crawling only, and generally make you a liability in combat. Having access to this Gift makes you a much better fighter in many roles. This Gift should never de-prioritize getting yourself healed ASAP. It's a highly effective measure to use until you can access healing.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Scent of the True Form:</b></u></span> Instantly recognizing Kinfolk and Garou is amazing already, and will serve you well in any game. Knowing that the Pentex rep is in fact Kinfolk or a BSD is helpful. Knowing for certain that the scrappy kid who lashed out at you is in fact Kinfolk / a pre First Change Garou (depending on how your ST runs it) is amazing. Now it's worth noting that it doesn't specify Garou Kinfolk; we can assume this is the case, but it's still interesting. Would the scent of a Fera Kinfolk be mistaken for Garou Kinfolk? Hell, how many non-Garou Kinfolk live their whole life thinking they're Garou Kinfolk? The only way Garou can find Kinfolk is with this Gift, through tracing ancestry, and through noting Delirium immunity. 2/3 of those things could take place without any blood tie to the wolves... I'm sidetracked, but a plot about a respected and beloved Kinfolk being revealed to be related by blood to another Breed would be really interesting. Where do their obligations lie? Are they suddenly less trustworthy to certain people? What does blood matter compared to service and loyalty?<br /> <br /> Anyways, this Gift is spectacular even before you get to recognizing Fera, Mages, Mummies (if they ever escape the pages of that unused book on your shelf), Changelings, Demons and Vampires. Having to develop a Pokedex of scents as you play is also hilarious and fun. This Gift is already Green to my taste, but if you're in a cross-splat game it becomes Green with several exclamation marks. I'm now imagining a Get of Fenris Philodox who has become expert in sniffing out Changelings, then determining which ones are Sidhe...<br /> <br /> Another thing to keep in mind with this Gift is how helpful it is in overcoming fakery. A Fomor might be able to perfectly imitate the voice and appearance of a Packmate, but you know instantly that they are not a Garou, and with some luck you know they're a Fomor. Whether Obfuscate overrides Scent of the True Form gets into Crossover Rulings territory, but the potential to overcome Obfuscate is also pretty neat. Even if you can't see past the disguise, you'll know that this &quot;Kinfolk&quot; is in fact a Vampire.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Truth of Gaia:</b></u></span> An immensely helpful problem solver which is useful in both open use &quot;I am going to ask you some questions with the ear of Falcon; you would be wise to speak truthfully my friend&quot; and covert use &quot;hey there, I'm with the press, I just wanted to ask you a few questions...&quot;<br /> <br /> Separating True from False, and True/False from Neither, is incredibly helpful in solving mysteries, rooting out treachery, casing a joint, interrogating captured foes, and the list goes on and on. My only recommendation would be not to become over-reliant on this Gift. Even if it's impossible for others to sense that you're using it, turning it on for every conversation is boring and arguably a fast track to the Paranoia Derangement. In all honesty I'm shocked there is no Gnosis cost; you'd think they would have wanted to make people pick their moment with this one!<br /> <br /> ===========================================<br /> <br /> As we can see Philodox start off very strong; I'd gladly pick up all five of these over time.\u200b<br /> <br /> (btw attempting to edit this post go it marked as spam - mods can you please fix this? ironically having to repost stuff CREATES spam)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 04:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-22T05:41:03-07:00","id":1512352,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 GALLIARD GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Galliards get a slightly odd starting kit.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Beast Speech:</b></u></span> I like this one a lot, but its utility will depend a lot on the Storyteller. Powers which let you communicate with things you normally wouldn't be able to communicate with, such as Wraiths and animals can put an unprepared ST on the spot and are a great window into whether they are facilitating a story being told or more &quot;adversarial&quot;. Spirit Speech is useful even if a typical spirit acts like a dick at a given table; understanding and speaking with spirits is key to many Garou activities regardless of the disposition of the spirit. Talking to animals is still important to Garou, given their role as protectors of nature, but how the RP actually shapes up is key to this Gift.<br /> <br /> When using this Gift, make sure to go through the section on The Curse in the book and confirm whether or not animals are bothered by your Rage, and to what extent. RAW suggests that animals are less upset by your Rage than humans are, but I've also seen people imply that animals are utterly terrified of Garou. Of course this is before you get into the fact that some animals will just flee from a large biped regardless of any supernatural energies.<br /> <br /> Combined with Smell of Man, and with a good ST, this Gift could have really remarkable results. It's a solid Amber, but could be Red if you have an ST who is committed to making unexpected approaches crash and burn. In a way this applies to all Gifts, but I feel like powers which permit communication with unusual NPCs are particularly prone to this.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Call of the Wyld:</b></u></span> This will be hit and miss depending on whether your ST lets it do anything. Making your howl be heard over a much larger area is handy, extra emotional power is handy, an extra die or point of Willpower here or there is handy, but it's so table and situation dependent it's hard to say anything definitive about the Gift.<br /> <br /> One thing that's handy is that if a Gift doesn't specifically bar being amplified, this will spread a supernatural howl or sound based Gift over a much larger area, which is handy. Howls in the Night is a good example, as is Song of the Siren. Given that a wolf can already be heard about 10 miles away however, this isn't enough to make the Gift Green. It's good, but you could absolutely do your job without it and rarely miss it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Heightened Senses:</b></u></span> As in the Lupus section. This doesn't impact the rating, but I find it slightly confusing that this is on the Galliard list; the Philodox are the detectives.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Mindspeak:</b></u></span> Very nearly Green, but not for the reason you think.<br /> <br /> Covert communications are handy. The cost is exorbitant, which sucks, but being able to have a private conversation with others has great RP potential. You can speak, conspire, flirt, haggle, all without anyone else getting involved. Having to spend 1+ WP is bad of course, and super technically RAW you are not included for free as a participant. Of course that's a bad reading, but worth noting. All in all the intended effect of this power is good, allowing you to perform any social checks or perhaps even Gifts you need to perform within a semi-private environment. How NPCs and PCs react to this will vary, but it's a handy tool to have in your arsenal, especially if you're gagged or otherwise prevented from communicating.<br /> <br /> The reason this is almost Green is because a somewhat reliable -2 dice penalty you can inflict on anyone within line of sight is pretty great. Drawing a significant foe into a waking dream which includes only the two of you means that you are at -2, they are at -2, and the rest of your pack are running with a normal dice pool. Its hidden utility is basically in debuffing a very dangerous opponent; after all they cannot make a check to escape, the only check is the one you make each turn. All dice pools decreasing by two means weaker attacks, less dodging, less chance to escape by Stepping Sideways, less luck running away... however this assumes that keeping Mindspeak going is a &quot;free action&quot; for the user each round. It seems intuitive that the Manipulation + Expression roll is a sort of automatic check to try and maintain the effect against unwilling participants; if it counts as an actual action for your turn this combo becomes extremely niche and isn't worth it in most cases.<br /> <br /> It's worth noting that how this waking dream works is left up for us to figure out. Can you use Gifts such as Distractions via this waking dream with a full dice pool? We don't know. The book doesn't tell us. In ideal circumstances this could possibly be Green, but it's safest to leave it as a solid Amber. Talk through the Gift's powers and limitations before purchasing with your ST and see how they feel about it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Perfect Recall:</b></u></span> Any one detail. Detail is a precise wording. What's annoying is that this means that RAW you can recall the phone number of that suspicious element you tailed 10 years ago; you can't remember his known aliases, place of education, modus operandi and so on. Those would be 1 Gnosis each.<br /> <br /> This Gift desperately needs a buff, but working with the Gift as is... I'd call it Red. Needing to remember something IC is a little bit niche. It comes up, but not that often. The ability to jab holes into memory wipes and amnesia is good, but again very niche. Niche is fine, but niche with a conscious activation which costs Gnosis in 100% of cases...<br /> <br /> My edit would be to make the Gift free to use, no cost, but have a Gnosis cost for breaking through mind wipes and amnesia to grab a detail you question yourself on. For instance a Garou whose history was wiped from their mind by a Vampire could ask themselves &quot;What Tribe was I initiated into&quot; using the Gift, when prompted by another Garou, and remember that they were a Glass Walker etc.<br /> <hr /><br /> I don't want to discourage anyone from playing a Galliard with this! They're really cool. Their starting list suffers compared to the other Auspices, but they're still a cool Auspice with a unique role. Their best Gifts just come later on. I'd suggest going for Beast Speech or Call of the Wyld at char-gen. Beast Speech, Call of the Wyld and Mindspeak are all worthwhile purchases for almost anyone, and Heightened Senses is handy if you want to be a super detective or the pack's best tracker.<br /> <br /> The absence of Persuasion is noteworthy. Arguably the Galliards need it more than the Philodox. Overall a non-Homid Galliard will suffer slightly for lacking Persuasion, unless they are Fianna or Glass Walkers. If you are a Metis/Lupus Galliard outside those Tribes, I'd strongly suggest picking up Persuasion out of list, or asking your ST to add it to the Galliard list.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 04-02-2025, 11:29 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-22T06:36:19-07:00","id":1512354,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 AHROUN GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> A note on quality of starting Gift lists: You don't need a huge list of amazing Gifts to choose from at Rank 1. You won't be buying every single Gift available to you, even at 3XP each. Having a single amazing Gift in your list is great; Ahroun exemplify this in a way.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Falling Touch:</b></u></span> I enjoy this one a lot. A difficulty of Stamina + Athletics can be very punishing against a Crinos form BSD. But against Spirits? A WP above 10 is possible, but against the vast majority of Spirits, Fomori and other foes it's going to be amazing. The mechanics of a prone enemy against a pack of Garou are not very fun for the enemy.<br /> <br /> It's important to consider using a Sweep attack against many foes; a Crinos form BSD who will never fall to Falling Touch may fall very easily to a sweep attack. There are also enemies you could never trip over normally but are very vulnerable to this Gift (such as a giant spirit or a tentacled Fomor).<br /> <br /> It's a good idea to make this part of another attack (including a sweep attack if you want to ensure they fall over), but if you're low on Rage you could devote your whole action to just lightly touching the enemy. Make sure they're grappled once prone to keep them there. How the ST runs &quot;standing up&quot; will also matter here; at my table it's a difficulty 3 check which you can split your dicepool for of course.<br /> <br /> Used against the right foes this will turn a tricky fight into this: <a href=\"https://media.tenor.com/R24f2JXzuhgAAAAC/jojo-kick.gif\" target=\"_blank\">https://media.tenor.com/R24f2JXzuhgAAAAC/jojo-kick.gif</a><br /> <br /> Being able to spend WP instead of Rage is great as well, though if you have this Gift you will almost certainly have high Rage. A Ragabash Stargazer will enjoy the Willpower option!<br /> <br /> Consider using this outside of combat as well; knock someone over as you brush past them to create a distraction. Knock someone off a boat. Win a race. Activate it when someone tries to punch or shove you for a &quot;I never touched him!&quot;. There are a great many situations where knocking someone over without any obvious effort is handy.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Inspiration:</b></u></span> This is influential if you have a large number of allies, Gnosis to spend, and the fight is dragging on long enough that people have run out of WP to spend. In a prolonged series of encounters this could also help allies to spend 1-2 WP each scene without exhausting themselves. A &quot;ghost&quot; point of WP for every one of your allies is handy, and a good power to grab RP-wise if your Ahroun is meant to be an inspiring vanguard in battle.<br /> <br /> For me personally, my Ahroun is a Child of Gaia. I'd rather keep his Gnosis for healing others. If your Ahroun is in a Tribe/Breed which lacks a good thing to spend Gnosis on then Inspiration becomes more tempting.<br /> <br /> It's noteworthy that this does not have to be used in a fight. You'd assume so, but it can be used in any context. Say the himbo Ahroun of a Pack uses this Gift to keep a pack topped up with Willpower as they hack into a mainframe... it lets him feel useful!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Pack Tactics:</b></u></span> If you have a Leadership of 5 and a pack of 5 this adds up to +1 die each. This isn't bad by any means, but most of the RAW pack tactics described in W20 aren't that amazing and worthwhile regardless. Generally speaking you're better off having one PC do a sweep and everyone else then follow this up with attacks, &quot;manually&quot;, instead of doing a Pack Tactic dedicated to that combo. The best Pack Tactics seem to be in prior editions, such as the Forward Pass (a Crinos throws a Homid/Lupus to propel them towards an enemy).<br /> <br /> If you have good Pack Tactics this is an Amber. If you have access to a short list of ??? ones which just describe things you can do manually, without much explanation of how the tactic is actually executed in play, then it could be Red. Playing it safe and making this one Amber, but it's a weird one. Needing a good Leadership pool is also annoying, but incentivizes purchasing the ability.<br /> <br /> For me the ability to have +2 dice to my giant Metis yeeting a wolf at someone, and +3 dice to the yeeted wolf's tackle of the enemy they collide with, would make this worth it... but only once I've got his Leadership up to 5!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Razor Claws:</b></u></span> This is excellent. The -1 difficulty and damage buff are great. I prefer claws to biting; putting any part of a Fomor or Bane inside your mouth is asking for trouble in my book. The downside is that Claws are +1 difficulty compared to Bites, and Hispo get bonus damage to Bites. Well Razor Claws help to make clawing better as well as safer; +3 damage total, difficulty 5 base. This then stacks later with Silver Claws for an amazing combo when fighting BSDs, corrupted and rebellious Garou, violent Fera etc.<br /> <br /> Having to take a full turn sharpening your claws almost makes this one Amber. I love it, I use it often, it's great for ambushes and when battle is approaching you, when you have a heads up, when you're the instigator, when you're about to charge into a Pit. But the preparatory aspect and having to spend a whole turn on it is a real downside. Keep it in mind and always think about how you can prepare for fights and ensure you get a 3 second heads up.<br /> <br /> This might also be helpful for cutting through things with your claws outside of combat, but non-combat applications are relatively limited.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Spur Claws:</b></u></span> 10/10 for flavour, Bee Ahroun is neat.<br /> <br /> It isn't clear whether both sets of claws transform with 1 rage point, and if that gives you two spur claw attacks before you run out of claws. I am assuming currently that you grow two sets of spur claws, and a successful claw attack empties one of your paws instead of both.<br /> <br /> This Gift is best against a single powerful tanky enemy; if you're struggling to deal damage, instead you can inflict a +2 difficulty which will significantly hamper a dangerous foe. We aren't told whether this stacks. If you can get another set of claws into them in a bonus action from Rage... that could be +4 difficulty. Ask your ST. I also like to imagine that leaving a set of silver claws (from the Gift of that name) embedded into a Werewolf's body would make the difficulty spike +3 instead of +2, but speak to your ST.<br /> <br /> If you have spare EXP to purchase this consider it a specialized tool in your arsenal; in many fights you're better off just blitzing with regular claw attacks.<br /> <br /> Outside of battle this is a wonderful way to hamper fleeing enemies. They either take +2 diff to everything, or waste time stopping to remove the claws.<br /> <br /> ================================================== ========<br /> <br /> It's worth noting that Ahroun get nothing that helps with melee fighting at Rank 1! Klaive/Fetish Weapon users don't get much or anything from this list. But to use Ahroun as an example of what I described at the top of this post; say you hate Spur Claws, Pack Tactics and Inspiration. Can't see the point of them.<br /> <br /> You don't really need them. Between Razor Claws, Falling Touch, and 2-4 Gifts from your Breed and Tribe you're set. If you have a list without much you want to buy, put more EXP into a different list, or into Attributes or Abilities. A list of powerful Gifts is good, but draws in a lot of EXP. Work with what you've got and buy what works for you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 04:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-24T02:48:25-07:00","id":1512553,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 BLACK FURY GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> We're told that the Black Furies have Gifts that tie them to the Wyld, and that theirs are some of the most effective war-Gifts. Let's get into them!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Breath of the Wyld:</b></u></span> +1 die to mental rolls for the rest of the scene is handy, and will make your character a handy support to have around for any Perception/Intelligence/Wits based problem. Arguably this alone wins you a spot in the Glass Walkers' good books, and you're handy to have around for a tricky hunt. No cost means you can apply this to the whole party; the only risk of overuse is the potential for it being boring.<br /> <br /> The +1 difficulty to Rage rolls is a mixed blessing; this is handy when the moon is gibbous/full, or when it's half-full for a Philodox, and can offset the -1 Rage difficulty &quot;penalty&quot; of Crinos. It's also handy for keeping Garou/Fera/Fomori/possibly Vampires calm... but only slightly.<br /> <br /> The RP potential of this gift is handy; if you feel like your character would feel too defeated to stand up for what's right, feeling a rush of the raw power of the Wyld is a great impetus to start turning things around. Paired with the right words and actions this could make for very memorable moments. Rolling Gnosis is a little bit eh, but it's an important stat which is worth investing in. All in all a solid Amber and well worth taking.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Man's Skin:</b></u></span> The RP potential of this one is an obvious draw, as is the potential for disguising oneself to avoid drawing attention.<br /> <br /> I'm also charmed by a Seahorse being a potential teacher!<br /> <br /> No cost, pretty workable roll and difficulty, lasts for a scene, grants a handy yet fixed disguise; I'm down. Handy if your Black Fury needs to enter a place only men typically go (men's room, certain bars, competing in certain events).<br /> <br /> Weirdly this Gift reminds me of a quote by Sylvia Plath:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yes, my consuming desire to mingle with road crews, sailors and soldiers, bar room regulars \u2014 to be part of a scene, anonymous, listening, recording \u2014 all is spoiled by the fact that I am a girl, a female always in danger of assault and battery. My consuming interest in men and their lives is often misconstrued as a desire to seduce them, or as an invitation to intimacy. Yet, God, I want to talk to everybody I can as deeply as I can. I want to be able to sleep in an open field, to travel west, to walk freely at night\u2026 </div> </div> </div>I'm desperately tempted to mark this one down as Green, but it's an Amber that I particularly like instead. I also like that the Gift establishes how it will work for male users.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Heightened Senses:</b></u></span> See the Lupus section; makes sense to have it given how wild/wyld the Furies are meant to be.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sense Wyrm:</b></u></span> See the Metis section; I had no idea Black Furies got this one! Makes sense with them being the favoured daughters of the Wyld. It saves them from having an all-amber starting list. Any good Pack needs to have this, even if you need someone to purchase it out of list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Wyld Resurgence:</b></u></span> the description of this power is epic! It sounds like a really potent ability, and there are prismatic lights and everything! There are level 5 gifts with more boring, grounded descriptions. It's almost too much.<br /> <br /> The mechanics are bad, unfortunately. You must spend a turn concentrating. You must spend 1 Gnosis. By default it heals 3 bashing or 2 lethal. If you spend 1 WP it heals 1 Agg, but not Agg caused by Silver of course.<br /> <br /> If you can wait a full round and do nothing but concentrate, then you have a way of getting rid of lethal and bashing damage; regeneration. Short of being stuck in your breed form this isn't a helpful Gift. Restoring Agg at 1 Gnosis and 1 WP a point is... having the option to do it is a bonus. But it's so expensive, time-consuming and far worse than simply being healed with Mother's Touch (or Grandmother's Touch, for those lucky Rank 2 Children of Gaia).<br /> <br /> I can see a few handy situational applications: you're a Kinfolk (with Gnosis) and thus lack regen. You have 4+ bashing or 3+ lethal on your sheet and have enough wiggle room to pause for 1 turn, but no more. You are poisoned or diseased, supernaturally, and are racking up Bashing/Lethal your regular Regen can't handle. You have access to a lot of Gnosis, you have high WP, and you need to remove some Agg.<br /> <br /> On top of being very niche, this Gift also is very obvious in a way which risks the Veil. I'll call it Amber because it can have its uses, but it's low down. I would never bother learning this out-of-list, and a Black Fury could easily skip it. If you lack a healer this Gift is a bit stronger.<br /> <br /> =======================================<br /> <br /> I enjoy this starting list; most are middle of the pack, not incredible, not awful, which means it's pretty well balanced. Lacking an astounding top tier and unique level 1 Gift is a shame; Sense Wyrm is incredible, but shared with many others.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 04:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-24T03:41:25-07:00","id":1512555,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 BONE GNAWER GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> The Bone Gnawers have Gifts that help them to survive.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Cooking:</b></u></span> Useful to have in real life, genuinely, and I like that it's on the list, but this one is almost Red. Much as with Sense Prey my feedback is &quot;you are a Garou, you can find dinner&quot;. This has the advantage of not requiring you to hunt animals, having a handy roll, fixed difficulty, easy ingredients, but honestly would be better balanced as a level 1 Mystic Rite which is generally learnt by Bone Gnawers.<br /> <br /> The RP potential of turning a pot full of newspaper, mud and empty food cartons into nutritious food is good. The Gift also allows you to theoretically feed an unlimited number of animals/people, Veil permitting. If you care about the environment and poverty, and you have a game that is heavily focused on social issues and urban decay over fighting the manifestations of such decay, this Gift could see frequent valuable use.<br /> <br /> A stealth aspect of this Gift is that it permits you to dispose of things you might otherwise struggle to dispose of. Incriminating documents can be burnt, yes, but eating them is hilarious. We are told you can fill it with &quot;whatever you can find&quot;, so now we may start to wonder about disposing of plastic. Famously a nightmare for the environment. A Gift that mystically transmutes shopping bags into a bland mush that can be used as fertilizer, pig feed, or safely burnt without any nasty emissions... we're deep into the kind of territory where an ST's opinion matters a great deal, but arguably the Gift is a sort of environmentalism hack, which is nice.<br /> <br /> All that said, a disposal unit is ultimately quite handy, especially if it only takes 1 minute to use; no time is given.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Desperate Strength:</b></u></span> This Gift is awful when you get into the fine print. It doesn't add to your Strength for Feats of Strength, 1 point per point of Bashing taken. That would be good! Sometimes you need to lift something super heavy in Homid. Sometimes you can't lift something in Crinos, and will gladly take some regenerate-able bashing damage to do so. But it doesn't do that.<br /> <br /> It adds dice to the Feat of Strength roll. Which is a WP roll, difficulty 9. More dice on that check is good and all, but it's a difficulty 9 check. You could take 6 bashing to fuel it and get 0 successes out of it.<br /> <br /> There's also the fact that if you botch a Feat of Strength roll, which is a real risk at difficulty 9, you can wind up taking unsoakable lethal damage. You know, after taking a bunch of unsoakable bashing damage...<br /> <br /> And then you consider that the maximum benefit from a Feat of Strength roll is +5 to your Strength. Even if everything works perfectly for you, there is a hard cap on the mechanic it feeds into. Red.<br /> <br /> My fix would be simple; the damage you take grants you an extra point of Strength (1:1 ratio) without having to roll Willpower at all, unless you want to.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Resist Toxin:</b></u></span> Total immunity to natural toxins (from arsenic to alcohol) and +3 dice to resist Wyrmish toxins. This Gift will come up frequently, and saves you from potentially deadly threats, along with a huge variety of debuffs which range from annoying to debilitating. A great many Wyrmish gifts, Spirit Charms and Fomori Powers will trigger this Gift.<br /> <br /> If you want to use it aggressively, have a water bottle laced with a deadly poison, drink freely from it, and offer it to your target. Stuff in that vein. Make yourself sick with poison to seem helpless, then turn the Gift back on to become a major threat. Win any drinking contest you like. Pour alcohol very freely at events to ply Pentex Reps as they get inebriated while you stay mostly sober at your own will.<br /> <br /> The ability to turn it off at will is great for roleplaying of course. Expect accusations of cheating in drinking contests with other Garou... though of course only Homids can partake in those.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Scent of Sweet Honey:</b></u></span> Being able to trigger this within an hour, regardless of distance, is great. Even if you have nothing to gain, it's fun to ruin the day of a dick who crossed you. Just be mindful that if they're a Garou, and there's even the tiniest chance you'll ever meet again, it's a bad idea.<br /> <br /> This Gift also has impacts which aren't mechanically explored. For instance you make the target smell of sweet honey; that's quite easy to track, say, compared to a scentless opponent or a conceptual spirit that lacks scent. Many people will freak out and behave erratically when covered in stinging insects. Some people are allergic. Some people are phobic. The -1 die penalty and obvious RP implications are already great, but the Gift can do a lot more.<br /> <br /> Tell someone they're cursed for a misdeed and then slam them with this later. Teach kids a lesson when they mess with nature or pick on the homeless. Make a mysterious foe stick out like a sore thumb. Prevent a Pentex rep from being presentable for their big presentation. Make a frog's day. Mess with a horse you're betting against. Use it on yourself to make an avant-garde and suitably disrespectful fashion statement for a moot. The limitless applications of the Gift are great.<br /> <br /> And then, if we think of the most boring possible use... is 1 Gnosis worth it to subtract 1 die from an enemy's dice pools for a fight? If they're a &quot;boss&quot; enemy, fighting solo, then potentially yes. If you lack a good use for your Gnosis and you aren't a powerful combatant, simply debuffing them could pull a lot of weight over time. Even if it prevents a few Agg from being dealt or dodged over the course of the fight it will be worthwhile I'd wager.<br /> <br /> There is also the potential that this Gift could do even more; if you release a huge container of insects nearby... who knows. Perhaps the penalty could end up larger. The -1 is a pretty good floor to build off of, but there's no reason the scent couldn't attract thousands of flying insects and completely blind/deafen the target.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Trash is Treasure:</b></u></span> Even in the realm of ordinary objects this is pretty good. Utilizing broken items as though they worked, potentially for a full day with WP, is a great power to have in your arsenal.<br /> <br /> If you're crossover-minded it's interesting to wonder whether this will allow broken Wonders, Treasures (and indeed Fetishes!) to function. A strict RAW approach from &quot;full functionality and usefulness&quot; could argue that such objects would be temporarily restored, but I don't believe this is intentional. If you bust up such an object the Magick/Art/Spirit escapes.<br /> <br /> An offensive application is to carry around busted up items; they are easier to acquire, ironically easier to maintain, and your enemies can't use them. &quot;It's obviously broken&quot; will also get your &quot;replica&quot; or defunctioned weapon past some kinds of security, but I don't believe it would fly with any serious security staff. You can do tricksy and fun stuff with this, and engineer fun situations where you become critical. You're the only one who can work the plane, the only one with a working gun, the only one who can get the damn lift to work. The RP potential of slipping into the broken cracks of a city's underbelly and living in the places others have written off as &quot;in need of maintenance&quot; is wonderful.<br /> <br /> Given the standard use is a roll with no cost, you can also do this to show off, which is fun. And at the upper end of absurdity you can restore a destroyed military helicopter or abandoned World War tanks for a full day, if you've got ammunition and the right fuel.<br /> <br /> Glass Walkers should try to learn this Gift since it will allow them to work with a busted computer, which counters attempts to destroy such tech.<br /> <br /> ======================================<br /> <br /> Bone Gnawer Gifts start off dripping with flavour, and all 5 Gifts are unique. I'd gladly pick up 4/5 of these on any BG character, eventually. Scent and Trash I'd gladly pick up for 5 EXP, given the chance, and given the right concept (a witchy Black Fury who punishes wrongdoers for Scent, a jury-rigging Glass Walker for Trash). Resist Toxin is worth the purchase on any Garou I'd argue. Eventually it'll save you from something bad.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 04:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-24T04:02:13-07:00","id":1512557,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 CHILDREN OF GAIA GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> &quot;Surprisingly good warriors&quot;, healers, negotiators, peacemakers. CoG Gifts have a lot of ground to cover, and I reckon they deliver. They're my favourite Tribe, so be aware. Still, I think I can be objective about their starting offerings.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Brother's Scent:</b></u></span> This Gift feels a bit like the Obfuscate Discipline. You aren't invisible, you just go overlooked. Psychic invisibility. The example of wandering around naked, soaked in blood and holding a Klaive at a gala is fun. I also enjoy the lore behind it; drawing upon the brotherhood of all living things establishes some truth to the ideals of the Tribe.<br /> <br /> The roll is simple enough, it costs Gnosis which is a little annoying, and it automatically works against the vast majority of NPCs. Where it falls down is with security. If the ST decides someone would reasonably be watching out for people who are out of place, you'll be spotted, short of having an insanely good dice pool.<br /> <br /> It's easy to overlook but this power does work on supernatural foes. Just be mindful that their supernatural abilities may help them to pierce the illusion more easily.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Jam Weapon:</b></u></span> This is a potent Gift to use both offensively and defensively.<br /> <br /> Invest heavily in Brawl. Focus on your claws and fangs. Klaive duelists, staff users, Fetish weapon experts, gun users, enemies from many other splats, Pentex First Teams, some Fomori, plenty of enemies and potential opponents in challenges will wield weapons. Just make sure you aren't breaking any rules by invoking this power. Some might think it dishonourable to use a Gift of peace to win a fight, but the Gift isn't explicitly about peace; it's about making weapons not work.<br /> <br /> Defensively you can stop everything from a mugger's knife to a pre-detonation nuke. You simply need to have the weapon within the range of your &quot;voice&quot; - which can be quite far indeed. The Gnosis cost is well worth paying when you have a good target, be it a crowd of mortals with silver bullets loaded into their automatics, or an enraged Silver Fang with a Grand Klaive.<br /> <br /> Rolling WP against a difficulty of the highest WP of an armed individual in earshot favours you in most cases. Clarify with your ST whether your allies' WP's count if they are armed, or whether those who are not resisting the Gift are ignored.<br /> <br /> Various traps will also fail with this power, which is handy when you're wandering through the home of a paranoid werewolf hunter. Striding across a minefield safely would also be a good application, so long as the mines are within the reach of your voice when you shout.<br /> <br /> Finally, and most earnestly, weapons failing is a great opportunity to get up on your soapbox and tell people to give peace a chance. They're more likely to listen if you're a ripped giant who is infamous for being as master of vivisecting enemies with his claws, and if their gun/klaive/rocket launcher just stopped working.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Mercy:</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis, you can only inflict Bashing. Niche, but definitely worth the investment.<br /> <br /> Great to use in Frenzy, as the Gift's flavour suggests. Amazing RP potential if used in a mock battle or hunt; my Ahroun has ragdolled a defeated ally with their entire head inside his mouth only for them to wake up with a few bruises, which feels neat and mystical.<br /> <br /> Don't use this Gift in a life or death battle unless you have a strong upper hand and a plan for what to do with your spared foe. Executions feel worse than killing in battle. Switching from Agg to Bashing is a nasty debuff, unless your foe soaks Aggravated. Even then, a foe is more likely to be able to Regenerate/Heal bashing. If you're looking to fight a Frenzying Garou, wayward Ronin, mind controlled ally etc. then this is a good Gift to trigger.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Mother's Touch:</b></u></span> See the Theurge entry. This healing ability is the envy of the whole World of Darkness, perhaps with the exception of Changelings, who still have to invest a decent amount of EXP into Spring; this only costs 3 EXP, and you're done. Ideally you should have a Theurge who has this, and a Child of Gaia who also has this, so they can heal one another.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Resist Pain:</b></u></span> See the Philodox entry. Having access to this Gift outside of being a Philodox is incredible; the role it complements most, of course, is the Ahroun's.<br /> <br /> ====================================<br /> <br /> Despite 2/5 Gifts being found elsewhere, the list ends up feeling unique and getting across the Gaian flavour. All five have their place and are well worth learning, which is unusual.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 04:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-24T04:34:24-07:00","id":1512558,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 FIANNA GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Fianna Gifts have fae ties and also link to their vigour. Given that 3/5 are copied, this should be a quick one!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Faerie Light:</b></u></span> Given a surprising lack of Gnosis cost, this Gift is a pretty handy tool for lighting your way, tricking foes, luring parties off the road, and creating all kinds of distractions.<br /> <br /> Appearing anywhere within line of sight is handy for messing with people; from a high vantage point and with good eyes that's far indeed! There's also nothing stopping you from creating multiple lights for bonus confusion, or having them head in directions which mislead enemies. Generally speaking you should only start messing around with this gift to deceive if doing so is worth the risk of losing an ambush.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Hare's Leap:</b></u></span> See the Lupus entry; as I said there this is shockingly strong once you investigate the rules for jumping.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Persuasion:</b></u></span> See the Homid entry. It's a genuine shame that the Fianna get this as a Tribe while the Galliards don't get this as an Auspice; a Theurge of the Fianna could wind up a better 'face' than a Galliard of another Tribe. It's great to see here, but does add up to a problem with the Tribe's identity; they're seen as being Galliard: The Tribe at times.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Resist Toxin:</b></u></span> See the Bone Gnawer entry. I can't for the life of me figure out why this one is on the Fianna list. Because of the 'vigour' theme? Because the snakes (some of whom are venomous) were cast out of Ireland? Still, it's a strong addition.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Two Tongues:</b></u></span> This is a handy way to maintain the veil and trick enemies. It also 100% feels like it should be a level 1 Galliard Gift. Two Tongues fits better there than Heightened Senses, at least.<br /> <br /> The potential applications are pretty readily apparent, but here's a few neat spins on it. Speak one conversation into the phone (&quot;Pretty sure they're Fomori, get here ASAP&quot;) and another to those standing nearby (&quot;Listen I'm in a meeting, can you call me back later?&quot;). Arrange one meeting location and time with one mark, and another with the other. Call emergency services and sound like you're ordering pizza or just carrying on a conversation with whoever is nearby. Insult one person while everyone else hears complements, then gaslight them when they fly off the handle. Provoke a Frenzy while looking like the innocent party.<br /> <br /> As with all Gifts that allow you to do tricky manipulative things, keep in mind that Truth of Gaia exists. Pick your deceptions carefully when it comes to your fellow Garou.<br /> <br /> Given the cost is 1 WP, there's no roll, and people only roll to see past it if they have a reason to be suspicious (say, for instance, you say your favourite colour is blue and someone yells &quot;YOU BASTARD, WHERE ARE YOU KEEPING HER?!&quot; in response to the threat only they heard), this one is green. Overuse it and it drops to Amber. Combined with Persuasion and good Charisma/Manipulation along with Social Abilities, and indeed Pure Breed, this Gift can make you a pretty incredible face without too much difficulty, and allows infinite creative applications.<br /> <br /> And on top of that, a double convo is just such a good RP prompt, especially in text RP.<br /> <br /> =====================================<br /> <br /> Fianna are off to a great start, though honestly they shouldn't have Persuasion. As I say above, they wind up feeling like a Tribe defined by an Auspice; the Bone Gnawers feel distinct from Ragabash, despite new players understandably conflating the two after their first skim of the book. When I look at the Fianna sometimes, as much as I like them, it feels like they're defined by a role that is too close to one Auspice in particular. This means Fianna Galliards feel kind of &quot;obvious&quot;, and non-Galliard Fianna are dipping too easily into the best face gifts without having the appropriate Auspice. I can imagine being a Galliard of another Tribe and finding myself wound up by a non-Galliard Fianna stealing my thunder with Persuasion and Two Tongues.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 04:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-24T04:59:16-07:00","id":1512559,"title":"","content":"I'm pretty sure Jam Weapon forces you to roll against your own allies if you use it and they're armed. There's no provision in the Gift to avoid it working against your friends, or for people accede to it to reduce the difficulty. If it wasn't for the high likelihood of nerfing your own allies as well, Jam Weapon might be the most OP Gift in the game.<br /> <br /> It doesn't deserve to be red, but it almost is for a lot of play groups because it's so good it can be disruptive and hated to the point where you can piss people off just by taking it. STs hate it because it so drastically narrows the kinds of antagonists they can use and combat threats start getting repetitive, while any other players in your group that wanted to use weapons impacted by the Gift will be pressured to go with something else. It's the W20 Gift I've seen banned from the most games because yes it is that good, but also it's powerful in fashion that can be really unfun for everyone else.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-24T05:28:09-07:00","id":1512560,"title":"","content":"It being so good that it flips around to being un-good is actually an angle I'd overlooked - in all honestly it would fit in well among the level 3 and 4 Gifts.<br /> <br /> If you wanted to nerf it you could decrease the duration, make the duration success-dependent, give it a visible veil-risking manifestation originating from the person who speaks the word etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-24T05:31:57-07:00","id":1512561,"title":"","content":"I think I just realized why they gave Fianna Resist Toxin... it helps them drink people under the table...<br /> <br /> If that's the reason, my best response is a long and pained sigh.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-24T08:31:59-07:00","id":1512577,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 GET OF FENRIS GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Get of Fenris get off to an alright start, but lack unique Gifts - again we have a Tribe which is similar-ish to an Auspice, but unlike the Fianna there isn't a problem of role/identity being stolen. A Get of Fenris Philodox is unusually skilled as a warrior for their Auspice, but they don't steal anything critical to the Ahroun's role, or get anything that totally clowns on the Ahroun regardless of Auspice.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Lightning Reflexes:</b></u></span> I originally rated this Gift a Red as it interferes with rules basically no-one uses. That said, let's get into the details of what it does, assuming the ST is playing the initiative system 100% RAW.<br /> <br /> Normally you have to roll Willpower (difficulty 6) or spend a point of Willpower to abort an action. Everyone goes from lowest initiative to highest, declaring their actions. If you're last in the fight you might declare an action of &quot;claw that guy!&quot;, only to find that before it gets to you someone is trying to claw you instead, and whoops, looks like if they hit you'll be taking 13 dice of Aggravated. If you would rather dodge or block, you need to roll or spend Willpower to abort.<br /> <br /> This Gift, which you can activate as a reflex, lets you spend a point of Willpower and for the rest of the day you can abort an action to switch to defense for free, no roll, no expenditure.<br /> <br /> How good is this? Well, if you aren't going to use any Rage actions this could be very good. Say you're a Rage 1 Get Ragabash; you don't have the luxury of going &quot;I'm going to use my main action to defend, and all of my Rage actions will be attacks&quot;. This Gift lets you take the risk of going &quot;I claw on my turn&quot; and then if no one attacks you you're going to claw, if someone attacks you then you get to dodge instead. Given that everyone faster than you gets to see what you're going to do (usually) this could help a lot.<br /> <br /> But a difficulty 6 Willpower is pretty easy to pass. So this Gift is at its best for a low Willpower character and a low Rage character. I can't identify with either of those especially, but that's personal preference!<br /> <br /> In an ideal situation of playing a Willpower 7+ Ahroun, for example, the Gift's advantage is that instead of going &quot;I'm going to dodge for my main action and attack 3 times with my bonus Rage actions&quot; you can instead go &quot;claw for main action, 3 more attacks for Rage actions&quot;. If no one happens to attack you for the whole round then you have the advantage of a bonus attack! And a 1 Willpower cost for the whole day is... good. So even if you obsolete this Gift mostly with great Willpower and Rage, I'd say that it's still useful enough to be worth an Amber. If you aren't using the rules to declare actions and rules to abort your action naturally this becomes unusable since it would need reworking.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Master of Fire:</b></u></span> See the Homid entry. Handy for a character leaping headfirst into danger, and for a Get who prefers to bring incendiary weapons with them.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Razor Claws:</b></u></span> See the Ahroun entry. Does nothing for Get who want to focus on melee, but an amazing Gift if you have any intention of focusing on claw attacks.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Resist Pain:</b></u></span> See the Philodox entry. Given the martial focus of the Tribe this will complement any Get member.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Visage of Fenris:</b></u></span> Against non-Garou, and Garou of your Rank and below, this is budget Persuasion. -1 difficulty to Social checks is pretty neat. Becoming larger and more fearsome could potentially have Veil implications, or help NPCs to determine what you are; the flavour hardly sounds subtle, shall we say, and I would reckon that in 99% of interactions with Mages and Fera a Garou who already seems to be on the more violent side growing larger and bristling with intimidating energies would make things worse, not better; but it's spirit magic. Let's be nice.<br /> <br /> Using this Gift against Garou of higher Rank is very difficult. If it weren't for Visage working on humans and Kinfolk, and your peers in Rank, this would be a Red.<br /> <br /> The impact on initiative would be a Red. Useless. Clearly not playtested. Negligible. My fix would be that the impact on Initiative is instead equal to the Get's Intimidation rating.<br /> <br /> ===========================================<br /> <br /> Despite having two Greens, the Get have a pretty mixed bag. A Homid gets a far better social Gift in Persuasion, making Visage useless, and also gets Master of Fire. An Ahroun already gets Razor Claws. A Philodox already gets Resist Pain. They're good to have on the list, for the Ragabash, Theurge and Galliards, but it's still not ideal to lack unique Gifts and unique identity.<br /> <br /> Ultimately it really sucks that their two unique starting Gifts aren't very good.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-14-2025, 11:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-24T09:17:52-07:00","id":1512590,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 GLASS WALKER GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Glass Walkers get Weaver-related Gifts and have a focus on technology as well as human society.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Control Simple Machine:</b></u></span> This one is pretty neat. A Willpower point and a manageable roll aren't too bad, and the control lasting for a scene is really good. Being able to command ladders to extend, doors to bolt, chairs to unfold and so on emphasizes that the Tribe's relationship with the cutting edge of technology is an ancient one. I'm shocked that &quot;push a button&quot; is not listed in this Gift. You can man a keyboard remotely with this Gift. You can press buttons and pull levers of much more complex machines.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately we aren't given an indicator of what kind of Strength we're working with, what the range is and so on, so it's hard to explore the most shocking and powerful applications. When you command a pulley to pull, how powerful is it? What is the most complex machine that falls under 'simple machine'? How far is your reach? Can you command something to take a more complex action, like a pulley lifting and lowering in sequence?<br /> <br /> Anyways mastering the world of dials, switches and so on is pretty neat. Turning off light switches alone can be pretty influential.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Diagnostics:</b></u></span> No special tricks with this one, it's pretty straightforward. Always know how to fix a broken object, can spend Gnosis, time and successes needed are halved.<br /> <br /> Always succeeding at attempts to figure out why a machine isn't working is pretty amazing; you'll know it's the humidity or the ambient temperature or the shoe someone threw into the mechanism. This is the sort of gift that would be Green to actually have, but it's Amber in practice in a TTRPG. If you want to get the most out of this, have some kind of venture where diagnosing the complaints of machines is critical to pleasing customers, and perhaps use this Gift to justify building up Resources and influence within your field.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Persuasion:</b></u></span> See the Homid entry. Simply an amazing Gift, and I do get why it's here. They're the Tribe who pride themselves on immersing themselves in city life and human societies, guiding humanity since the beginning. But many other Tribes can claim such a heritage. This Gift is handy for those rare Lupus Glass Walkers, and for Metis of course. I kind of wish we didn't see so many repeats, but it is what it is. In all honesty this slot should have gone to Apecraft's Blessings; it's also a repeat, but one that makes a lot of sense, and would help establish that all Breeds within the Tribe are incredible at working with human technology. At least that's what I do at my table.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Plug and Play:</b></u></span> One of my favourite Gifts. Wrap an N64 controller around a fork lift's steering wheel. Break into a security system with your Tamagotchi or Gameboy. Turn on an MRI scanner with your smart watch. If you have Apecraft's Blessings and a good Intelligence + Computers, this Gift turns all high tech situations into a personal playground. The duration of a day and the cost of 1 WP are also pretty helpful; in a way it would make more sense to use Gnosis.<br /> <br /> An underrated aspect of this Gift is that you do not need to know a single thing about computers to use it. The most tech illiterate Lupus imaginable can control things with a joystick or button if they like. The mileage you'll get out of the Gift is severely diminished, that should go without saying, but a saboteur or thief who does the simplest things you can think of with machines they've connected to using this Gift is a fun concept.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Trick Shot:</b></u></span> Given that a great many PCs will have 0-1 Glory when they start out, this is an odd one. If you're a firearms focused character and you manage to get a few dots of Glory this is a neat one. The ban on shots that directly damage foes is quite annoying, and there's only so far you'll get on dropping chandeliers and scaffolding on enemies. Still, with a high Glory and support from Apecraft's this could pull off some utterly insane tricks, which is neat.<br /> <br /> If you want to use this aggressively, try going somewhere with a lot of suspended objects, volatile substances and so on. A building site or laboratory for instance. The potential for a memorable kill is pretty neat if nothing else.<br /> <br /> ===========================================<br /> <br /> If you're 100% dedicated to gunplay, getting Trick Shot out of list might be worth it once you have very high Glory.<br /> <br /> If you have any interest in computers or machines, getting Plug and Play out of list would be a great addition. Many thieves and tinkerers could learn a lot from the Gift, and I could easily see a Ragabash and Glass Walker doing a &quot;trade&quot; of Trash is Treasure and Plug and Play.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 04:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-24T12:35:27-07:00","id":1512618,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1512590#post1512590\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Lightning Reflexes:</b></u></span> This Gift interferes with minutiae of the rules that no one, and I mean no one, uses. I follow the rules pretty close to the book, hell I switch up the ini order each round! But in order for this Gift to matter you need to be declaring your actions in reverse order each round. If you are strictly following that setup, going from slowest to fastest and asking people to state what they'll be doing this turn, then this would be Amber.<br /> <br /> Since most people don't do that... this'll be Red. You can also pull off a defensive action using one of your Rage actions if needs be.<br /> <br /> I reckon 99% of players won't know that aborting an action is a thing until they read this gift. I've been playing this system for close to 10 years and I've yet to see anyone use the rules, but that's a limited number of tables. Like I say, Amber if you follow the rules to the letter, including the weird ones you rarely see implemented.<br /> <br /> My change for this would be a pretty simple; -1 difficulty to defensive actions against individuals you've already attacked in the scene, benefit disappears if you turn to flee or try to stop the fighting.\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>So, it's invaluable to molding old BattleTech players like myself, in other words. (^VVVVV^)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Plug and Play:</b></u></span> One of my favourite Gifts. Wrap an N64 controller around a fork lift's steering wheel. Break into a security system with your Tamagotchi or Gameboy. Turn on an MRI scanner with your smart watch. If you have Apecraft's Blessings and a good Intelligence + Computers, this Gift turns all high tech situations into a personal playground. The duration of a day and the cost of 1 WP are also pretty helpful; in a way it would make more sense to use Gnosis.<br /> <br /> An underrated aspect of this Gift is that you do not need to know a single thing about computers to use it. The most tech illiterate Lupus imaginable can control things with a joystick or button if they like. </div> </div> </div><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CXOjc3b_FZ8\" target=\"_blank\">GAME! OVER!</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SW8DgKm-7vs\" target=\"_blank\">Alternately...</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-24T15:47:18-07:00","id":1512645,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1512560#post1512560\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It being so good that it flips around to being un-good is actually an angle I'd overlooked - in all honestly it would fit in well among the level 3 and 4 Gifts.<br /> <br /> If you wanted to nerf it you could decrease the duration, make the duration success-dependent, give it a visible veil-risking manifestation originating from the person who speaks the word etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>One of my groups dropped the Gnosis point and made it only work on a specific target, and made it eye sight instead of hearing. While it sounds like a massive nerf (it is), no longer having to worry about hitting your allies, is a huge bonus, and being able to debuff one target is plenty for a level 1 Gift. We actually kept it stealthy so Coggie Raggies could sabotage fights, while Doxes could play into staring people down, etc..<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1512561#post1512561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If that's the reason, my best response is a long and pained sigh.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep.<br /> <br /> ------------<br /> <br /> Also, on Lightning Reflexes... a lot of people do actually use the full rules. Knocking a Gift down to Red because it... uses the RAW is pretty harsh. Lightning Reflexes is really useful tactically for Get that aren't Ahrouns and aren't going to be drowning in Rage for lots of extra actions, or getting Spirit of the Fray on the cheap. Being able to declare all offenses and abort to defense for no cost in-combat lets Get be aggressive tactically without all of them having to build like Ahrouns.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-25T06:55:11-07:00","id":1512723,"title":"","content":"Like I say in the post it's Amber if you play the initiative system as it's presented in the book, I've just yet to play at a table where that actually happens!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-25T07:14:46-07:00","id":1512726,"title":"","content":"RANK 1 RED TALON GIFTS<br /> <br /> Red Talon gifts are supposed to help them get back at humanity for their sins. A lot are tied to nature and to it triumphing over humans.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Beast Speech:</b></u></span> See Galliard entry. Glad the Red Talons get this as it would be pretty weird if they didn't! Being able to converse with predators, prey, other canids etc. is pretty neat. When you're playing a character who was not only born a wolf, but also rejects human society, being able to RP with wolves and nature more directly is pretty key. Of course all Garou can communicate with wolves in Lupus form but that's based on body language and howling, not full on conversations playing out human-style.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Eye of the Hunter:</b></u></span> This provides useful information regarding the pecking order of a group, and a marginal benefit to focusing down the weakest member of that group. Being able to identify the Alpha of a Pack at a glance is pretty handy on its own, and this Gift will come up in most fights against groups which is handy. The flavour tying into predator animal tactics of picking off a weak member of a group is pretty nice.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Hidden Killer:</b></u></span> Handy Veil-preserving Gift which helps to explain the Tribe's activities. Removing all evidence you were present at a fight scene is handy, but of course it only applies to your own trail, and the wounds you inflicted. As it stands this is pretty niche, and doesn't have any super smart unique applications either. It's handy enough to be worth grabbing for when you pull off a lone kill.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Scent of Running Water:</b></u></span> See the Ragabash entry. Lots of unique ways to use this to trick enemies in that writeup.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Wolf at the Door:</b></u></span> This effect is flexible in the ways you can apply it, with good flavour. It has no cost, the roll will be quite easy against most NPCs but very hard against major antagonists. Turning an enemy into a shut-in is quite handy. Draining their Willpower is quite handy. Impacting their Social and Mental pools is neat. It's still niche in the sense that you need to be dealing with an NPC who is a recurring issue but you can't or shouldn't kill, which doesn't describe most Garou enemies. You can use it to teach someone a lesson, punishing humans for mistreating the wilderness, which has fun RP potential but won't solve many mission-critical problems.<br /> <br /> Being able to target a big crowd, if you have a high Rage, is nice. There are real-life situations where getting a group of 1-5 people to stay in their homes and refuse to come out would be handy, and perhaps you'll make a splash by making some Pentex executive miss an important meeting, or getting a party of cryptozoologists to stay away from the Sept's hunting grounds during the Moot, or making a land developer cower in his home instead of furthering his plans to despoil the wilderness, but ultimately these people can try again later, or work via phone from home, or stew over their hatred of the wilderness and how much it terrifies them and later emerge even more dedicated to destroying it. Unfortunately the Gift doesn't seem to trigger instantly; you can't make someone lose 3 dice from their mental/social pools instantly, it triggers if they can make themselves leave their home.<br /> <br /> =========================================<br /> <br /> Red Talons have a pretty consistent set of Gifts; none of them are incredible, none of them are bad. Eye of the Hunter is potentially worth picking up regardless of Tribe, if you have the opportunity and think it would complement your skillset. Hidden Killer is also pretty great for any Garou who makes their living as an assassin.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 05:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-25T07:30:17-07:00","id":1512727,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 SHADOW LORD GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Shadow Lord Gifts have a lot of themes; politics, dominance, storms, secrets, shadows.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Aura of Confidence:</b></u></span> Attempts to read your Aura or find weaknesses just convey a sense that you are superior. This is handy in the sense that it protects you from aura readers (Vampires and Mages, near exclusively) determining you're a Werewolf, knowing your alive/dead status, whether you're Ghouled or not etc. but the problem is that it makes you stand out.<br /> <br /> Being resistant to weaknesses being detected protects you from a lot of Gifts, and also from some uses of the Entropy Sphere. A cool Gift for crossover. In a Garou-focused game this one is likely going to come up once or twice a chronicle. Amber, but could easily be Red depending on the content of a game; I like the flavour however.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Fatal Flaw:</b></u></span> Great for sizing someone up before a fight. You get to know a weakness, which could be very critical with interesting enemies like spirits and Vampires, and +1 damage die for the whole fight is neat. Naturally the previous Gift guards against this. The difficulty could be quite high depending on the enemy as it's Wits + Subterfuge, but it could also be very low. Keep in mind that a Spirit's &quot;Wits+Subterfuge&quot; will always be equal to their Gnosis.<br /> <br /> The RP potential of this includes finding a flaw in a sparring partner, or revealing a weakness of a foe in conversation without intending to fight them. Finding out the person you're looking at is terrified of fire, or sunlight, gives you a pretty good clue as to what they might be. Knowing when to keep such info close to your chest and when to reveal it is key. You could also find and fix weaknesses in your allies.<br /> <br /> Given that the idealized role of the Shadow Lords in the Garou Nation is the &quot;beta&quot; to the Silver Fangs, recognizing when someone is weak and having the opportunity to either exploit it or compensate for it is great flavour for them. All-around great Gift design.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Seizing the Edge:</b></u></span> Could potentially be niche, but draws on checks will come up semi-regularly if you are sneaking around, lying to people, intimidating people and so on. Winning those draws makes you a little bit sneakier, a little bit more subtle, a little bit stronger, when you really need it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Shadow Weaving:</b></u></span> Budget Lasombra powers, activate! This costs 1 Gnosis, the range is good, the buff to stealth and intimidation is pretty small, but the potential to send messages, terrify bystanders, reveal hidden things, protect the Veil, hide objects and groups etc. is pretty neat.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Whisper Catching:</b></u></span> An amazing Gift for Moots, investigations, and generally enjoying roleplay. The potential to learn critical information is great, as is the potential for other PCs to whisper things to you.<br /> <br /> Keep in mind that canny Garou and Kinfolk may know there's every chance this Gift is in use, if there are several Shadow Lords present. Also keep in mind that people sometimes whisper things because they're sensitive, but at times it's simply because they don't want cannier ears to call out some nonsense. &quot;I heard it whispered&quot; is about as good a source as &quot;I read it in a comments section&quot;.<br /> <br /> ========================================<br /> <br /> I was almost tempted to bump Fatal Flaw and Seizing the Edge up to Green in all honesty, but Amber feels most accurate for them. Whisper Catching is worth learning for any political character, but I reckon an ST would be quite slow to relinquish that one!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 05:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-25T07:53:20-07:00","id":1512734,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 1 Silent Strider Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Overall I'm not a fan of the Silent Strider Gifts, but that's across the whole board of 1-5, not just level 1. They start off alright; many of their Gifts relate to traveling and speed, along with ghostly stuff.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Heaven's Guidance:</b></u></span> Easily overlooked, but having a magical sense of direction and being able to trace back your path is pretty amazing. The Gift's main impact is not having to make a bunch of rolls which could otherwise get you into trouble.<br /> <br /> As with Umbral Tether, the most aggressive use of this is to make your way into a very tumultuous and hard to navigate place while pursued by enemies, then easily find your own way out once they are mired. A solid Amber, assuming your ST isn't handwaving navigation; generally speaking they should be thinking about practical stuff like this!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sense Wyrm:</b></u></span> Elevates any Tribe who has it. See the Metis entry.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Silence:</b></u></span> +2 to stealth is handy. But it's only to Dexterity + Stealth, notably. Silently sneaking up on enemies would be effected, but checks using attributes other than Dex won't be.<br /> <br /> For a Ragabash this makes you even better at sneaking than others of your Auspice. For other Auspices it helps you to add stealth to your repertoire, which complements basically all playstyles in my experience; whether you're sneaking around with a klaive, a camera, a stolen egg, you name it, stealth is just good.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Speed of Thought:</b></u></span> The Striders are meant to run alone, at least conceptually, but if you're playing one you're likely in a mixed pack. Outpacing your allies has its uses, don't get me wrong; you can run ahead to send a message, run back to scout then catch up etc.<br /> <br /> The cost of 1 Gnosis a pop is just annoying though. The Gift is handy, don't get me wrong, staying out of reach of foes, being nearly impossible to catch, winning races and having the edge in hunts, it's all good. A Rage cost, or WP cost, would just be more workable.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Visions of Duat:</b></u></span> This gives you eyes on a world most Garou can't see. Unfortunately it's a world which has much less relevance to the Garou than the Middle Umbra but it's still good! Understanding that a place is being harmed by vengeful ghosts, rather than Banes, can save a lot of time and effort. The Veil risk of your eyes visibly changing is a little cumbersome, but it has cool flavour. Generally speaking this is a great way to pick up clues other Garou will miss.<br /> <br /> If Wraiths and the undead are critical to a plot, or your enemies include necromantic mages or vampires, this Gift could be Green.<br /> <br /> =============================================<br /> <br /> If you find yourself getting lost a lot, picking up Heaven's Guidance might be handy. If you're able to get your hands on Visions of Duat, it helps to complement both Elder Brother and Theurge concepts. Silence is a good addition to any Garou, though keep in mind that for 5 EXP you can just get Stealth 1 and Stealth 2; this Gift is a good investment once you already have Stealth 2-3.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 05:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-25T08:08:27-07:00","id":1512736,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 SILVER FANG GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Silver Fang gifts are meant to help them to lead, though many relate to silver, the moon and personal battle prowess.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Eye of the Falcon:</b></u></span> Pretty great for a sniper, though unfortunately the Silver Fangs are among the Tribes less likely to produce a sniper! Still, -1 difficulty to long range attacks, visual perception rolls, and a really great long range vision are great. If you have a Gift you can use on anyone you can see this combos well.<br /> <br /> This complements a scouting role, which is handy for a Ragabash especially.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Falcon's Grasp:</b></u></span> +3 dice to grappling is pretty handy, especially since one Garou grappling an enemy while everyone else claws them is so effective. Not much else to say really!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Inspiration:</b></u></span> See the Ahroun entry.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Lambent Flame:</b></u></span> +1 difficulty to incoming attacks increases your survivability considerably. The Veil risk of emitting light means you should save this for proper Garou on Garou battles, fighting in the Umbra and remote locations etc. The cost being WP is excellent, it lasts all scene, and it grants light to your allies. An amazing Gift.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sense Wyrm:</b></u></span> Green, as always! See the Metis writeup.<br /> <br /> ==============================================<br /> <br /> The Silver Fangs start off relatively strong; if you do a huge favour to Luna, Lambent Flame is a better reward than most.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 05:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-25T08:17:57-07:00","id":1512737,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 STARGAZER GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Stargazer Gifts have an emphasis on visions, wisdom, balance and non-lethal methods.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Balance:</b></u></span> The effects are permanent and don't have an activation cost. I much prefer Gifts with niche benefits working like this! You can always keep your balance and have -3 difficulty to climbing checks. You could combo this with other Gifts or Merits, but honestly it's good enough on its own.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Channeling:</b></u></span> This Gift makes you better at everything. Everything. Being able to spend 3 Rage for +3 dice is incredible. You're better at Rites, at crafting, at telling a great tale at a moot.<br /> <br /> The other use of this Gift is disposing of excessive Rage. If you're gaining Rage from an emotional or supernatural source, being able to 'destroy' it at a rate of 3 points per turn is pretty amazing!<br /> <br /> Utilizing this in combat is less optimal, as your Rage will almost always be best spent on bonus actions. Any Ahroun or Metis, and especially a Metis Ahroun, would benefit enormously from this Gift. An easy Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Falling Touch:</b></u></span> See the Ahroun section. Great martial arts flavour. Fits with the balance concept, and non-lethal means of fighting.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Iron Resolve:</b></u></span> Much like Channeling, this makes you better at everything. Crafting a Fetish. Binding a spirit. Making an impossible jump. Combine the two on a critical check you need to do amazingly on and you'll see great results. Iron Resolve is another Gift that is well worth getting at 5xp, though you'll likely have to jump through a lot of hoops.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b><u>Sense Wyrm:</u></b></span> Still Green, see the Metis entry.<br /> <br /> ================================================== =====<br /> <br /> Stargazers give the Children of Gaia competition for starting lists. I reckon this Tribe have the best set of level one Gifts, in fact. Anyone would benefit from learning Channeling and Iron Resolve, whether from spirits or from Stargazers. Spending 10xp on both Gifts is well worth it, if you can find a way to obtain them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 05:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-25T11:31:33-07:00","id":1512755,"title":"","content":"While not necessarily enough to move it up to Green, Eyes of the Falcon works for archery and thrown weapons, which makes it a lot more likely for Silver Fangs to pick up depending on their cultural background. That said, &quot;sniping&quot; does come from aristocrats hunting snipes with guns, because snipes were considered particularly difficult to hit. Gun based game hunting has been a large part of upper class society for a long time; lots of Silver Fangs should have some firearms training even if the Tribe likes swords more.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T00:54:05-07:00","id":1512833,"title":"","content":"Great point with aristocrats hunting! I considered bows and thrown weapons, but keep in mind that the gift specifies Long Range Attack Rolls - we are given rules in the ranged section which specify that using a gun at its suggested range is difficulty 6, up to 2x that range is difficulty 8 (is this what the Gift means by Long Range?) and point blank is difficulty 4.<br /> <br /> Offsetting a penalty makes the most sense for this Gift's effect, as long range attacks are where sharp eyes matter most. So unless you are strong enough to throw a silver dagger 50+ meters (far from impossible given that Garou are Garou, and the thrown weapon could have a Gift or some kind of magic behind it) it won't be applying to thrown weapons.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T01:15:46-07:00","id":1512834,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 ELDER BROTHER GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Uktena (I prefer Elder Brother) Gifts are often blessings from animals, or let them deal with Occult stuff. These magical Gifts aren't all just Theurge powers 'copied over', so they have their own unique mystical identity; I like that a pack can have an Elder Brother Ahroun and a Get of Fenris Theurge who both have a solid role and identity. Still, them getting Sense Wyrm and Spirit Speech as in-tribe Gifts at level 1 does mean that playing an Uktena of any Auspice other than Theurge almost feels like playing with two Auspices.<br /> <br /> We're told that some of these Gifts were only recently unearthed, which is intriguing.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sense Magic:</b></u></span> Picking up on Gifts, Fetishes, Disciplines, Sorcery, Magick, Cantrips and so on is incredible. The radius is generous and gets even bigger with more successes, and this Gift can reveal endless secrets. It almost feels like cheating.<br /> <br /> If this one is giving you grief, I'd suggest adding a Gnosis cost to gain the ability to use the Gift for a whole Scene, or charging one point of Willpower per use.<br /> <br /> While already amazing in a standard game, this becomes 100x better in a Crossover game. If you're a mystically inclined Garou of any Tribe, and you ever get the chance to learn this, go for it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sense Wyrm:</b></u></span> See the Metis entry. Amazing as always. Critical to the Elder Brother's role of finding, understanding and sealing away the Wyrm. Great for locating those dark places which require your Tribe's touch.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Shroud:</b></u></span> Creating an obscuring effect which doesn't impact you is incredible. There's a reason that Darkness + Devil's Sight is such a potent and frequently seen combination in DND5e.<br /> <br /> If you have allies who are skilled at fighting in the dark, and/or have Gifts which grant them special senses, this Gift becomes even better. Use it when it disadvantages your enemies the most in fights, and ensure your allies are well prepared to work around this Gift. Generally speaking you will be looking at attacking enemies who cannot dodge or parry, sometimes at -2 difficulty, while they attack back at +2 difficulty.<br /> <br /> Outside of combat this Gift can help to minimize Veil breaches, hide fights, block lines of sight, handle security, terrify humans and animals alike, delay someone, cause chaos in traffic, disrupt a search party and so on.<br /> <br /> Shadow Lords will be jealous of you.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Spirit of the Lizard:</b></u></span> This Gift is good, though the potential to be dislodged by taking strenuous actions is a little worrying. Generally speaking being able to move at walking speed across any surface, even a ceiling, opens up a great many approaches to problems which could be hard to navigate otherwise. Try to do things with your mouth when possible.<br /> <br /> Note that the surface you're walking on doesn't matter it seems; you can climb glass as easily as granite. Possibly this Gift could help you to &quot;climb&quot; on a tricky floor, such as slippery ice, or to work your way up a giant enemy.<br /> <br /> And honestly the image of a giant bipedal wolf beast mission impossible'ing across a ceiling is just too good to pass up.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Spirit Speech:</b></u></span> See the Theurge entry. Key to the Tribe's identity, and they're lucky to be the only Tribe who get it on their list. If you're not a Theurge, playing down your possession of this Gift might be wise; a Ragabash trickster or himbo Ahroun who surprises a spirit hours into accompanying them and their Brood with &quot;that isn't a very nice thing to say&quot; could be a memorable moment.<br /> <br /> ==================================================<br /> <br /> Much like the Children of Gaia and Stargazers the Elder Brothers start with an incredible Gift list.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 05:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T01:25:31-07:00","id":1512836,"title":"","content":"Little personal note on Shroud, from my own game. My Metis is blind (though this isn't actually his Metis Deformity weirdly enough), and has trained to be as proficient in combat as possible given he's an Ahroun. My ST let me take the Blind Fighting skill from Mage's Book of Secrets, as the game has a lot of crossover. When one of his packmates drops Shroud, it is always a massive advantage, as it blinds opponents without bothering him at all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T02:02:51-07:00","id":1512837,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1512833#post1512833\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Great point with aristocrats hunting! I considered bows and thrown weapons, but keep in mind that the gift specifies Long Range Attack Rolls - we are given rules in the ranged section which specify that using a gun at its suggested range is difficulty 6, up to 2x that range is difficulty 8 (is this what the Gift means by Long Range?) and point blank is difficulty 4.<br /> <br /> Offsetting a penalty makes the most sense for this Gift's effect, as long range attacks are where sharp eyes matter most. So unless you are strong enough to throw a silver dagger 50+ meters (far from impossible given that Garou are Garou, and the thrown weapon could have a Gift or some kind of magic behind it) it won't be applying to thrown weapons.</div> </div> </div> </div>The section on throwing weapons (before general combat maneuvers) makes it clear that thrown weapons still have close and long ranges, but instead of being determined by the weapon, the ST is just supposed to figure it out based on the aerodynamics and Strength of the character (and allows a greater range of difficulty modifiers). While W20 forgot to actually say how to figure out the ranges for thrown weapons in any way, they're still capable of long ranged attacks like firearms and bows.<br /> <br /> The RAW don't really care what the actual distance is, just that it's beyond the base range of the weapon (yes, I agree it's meant to reduce the penalty for up to double range from 8 to 7). A light pistol with a range of 20 gets the benefit of this Gift shooting from 21m to 40m. Thrown is no different. As well, while I don't think W20 addressed this, the WoD games do generally treat throwing assist mechanisms like slings as spear-throwers as still operating as thrown weapons mechanically, vastly increasing their ranges beyond 50m. There's at least a few legendary figure worthy of Silver Fang emulate from early history that used slings to great effect.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T02:05:29-07:00","id":1512838,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 1 YOUNGER BROTHER GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Wendigo (Younger Brother) Gifts relate to the cold, and also have themes of surviving and killing in nature.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Beat of the Heart-Drum:</b></u></span> This is a peerless tracking ability. A weapon left at a crime scene, some blood under a victim's nails, a torn piece of their clothing, and you have a proximity detector for your foe. In practically any mystery this will help you to overcome a dead end, which means it will come up quite often.<br /> <br /> Note that the target needs to HAVE a heartbeat. This rules out most Vampires, and Spirits, and some Fomori. Even then, &quot;they don't have a heartbeat&quot; is a potentially handy clue.<br /> <br /> A less obvious application of this power is defensive. Taste the blood of your whole Pack, your Kinfolk, a prisoner, and have them in a mental library of heartbeats you can track down when they are missing or lost. This could be good inspiration for a Deed Name in fact. A duration of one day per success, a pretty manageable roll and a cost of 1 Gnosis all add up to the power being pretty well balanced for what it does; it's Green because the core concept is very strong, not because of overpowered execution.<br /> <br /> Another neat application is returning lost things to their owners, which is pretty strong indigenous flavour. &quot;Belonging&quot; goes undefined, but I would personally let a Garou who used this Gift on a traditional Zulu shield they liberated from a museum use it to track down its rightful owner, given the nature of spirit magic.<br /> <br /> The RP potential of this Gift includes a permanent bond between two people, &quot;I shall always find you my love&quot;, but could also stray into darker territory; imagine playing a Wendigo Kinfolk who knows that their spouse/sibling/parent/stalker will always be able to find them, having tasted their blood long ago.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Call the Breeze:</b></u></span> Situational, but lacks a cost or a roll, and you can direct this as you wish. Calling a frigid wind into existence at will is pretty neat and magical without being an overt Veil risk, unless used in the most stupid ways possible.<br /> <br /> Debuffing perception and freezing people over time is handy and mean. Exhaling gently from a hiding place to slowly debilitate a group of humans with hypothermia is a neat assassination method in theory, but the mechanics don't go any further than a -1 die penalty from the frigid cold.<br /> <br /> Being able to disperse toxins and insects is neat, and given how many Wyrmish threats are miasmas, swarms, toxic clouds and so on this will come up often enough to be useful.<br /> <br /> The RP potential of the Gift is just great drama.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Camouflage:</b></u></span> +3 difficulty to detect you is great. Naturally this doesn't help your Pack, but it's handy for a scout and would work well on any Ragabash or Ahroun in particular. Ambushes are, as always, incredible. The main issue is that this only works in the wilderness, which means it will apply in good chunks of the world and Umbra, but not when you desperately need it in a city or a stranger Realm.<br /> <br /> It's a solid amber, especially since you don't need to activate it and +3 difficulty is so good, but would be a pretty clear-cut Green if it worked everywhere.<br /> <br /> If you want to use this offensively or defensively, try to prioritize heading into the wilderness when you are able to choose where to go.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Ice Echo:</b></u></span> This Gift is shockingly magical, and feels like it belongs at a higher level. The potential for trickery, defense, setting up ambushes and so on is incredible here.<br /> <br /> Do keep in mind that even if your enemy fails the check to determine which one is the real you, it's still only a 50/50 chance to hit the right target.<br /> <br /> The echo looking exactly like you (but mirrored), and speaking exactly like you, is incredible. The Gift has amazing range and there is no roll to use it, just a Gnosis cost. There's also nothing stopping you from making multiple echoes for next level deceptions; have the 2nd echo hide nearby the 1st to be mistaken for the &quot;real&quot; you by a foe who thinks they've outsmarted you. Or mix it up and wander around in the open while a bunch of Echoes hide nearby, which is bound to confuse smarter enemies.<br /> <br /> This Gift combos incredibly well with Scent of Running Water, so a Lupus Ragabash Younger Brother is a really great combination. Six scentless Garou who can only be told apart through touch, magic senses or heat vision, moving around like a pack of identical sextuplets, all dodging past one another until you have no idea where to strike. Since the difficulty of the check to figure out who the &quot;real&quot; Garou is is based on Gnosis, Lupus combos well with this for an additional reason. Keep in mind that many enemies simply won't HAVE the Enigmas knowledge however.<br /> <br /> 100 feet is a good range, and allows you to trigger ambushes from a safe distance as well. This Gift has endless applications and makes me wish I saw more people playing this Tribe!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Resist Pain:</b></u></span> See the Philodox list. Amazing as ever, and suits a Tribe which is stewing in the bitter cold.<br /> <br /> ================================================== ============<br /> <br /> Given how rarely I see this Tribe being played, I was expecting a much weaker starting list! Overall I'm very impressed. If you ever get a chance to learn Ice Echo or Beat of the Heart-Drum, do so. Having two amazing unique Gifts is pretty good going.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-01-2025, 05:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T02:09:12-07:00","id":1512839,"title":"","content":"And that's all the level 1 Gifts done! There are a few that are left, offshoots and oddities, but those are all of the level 1 Gifts available to a standard starting PC in WTA. I was pleased by how few Gifts were Red, and I learnt a lot going through the list.<br /> <br /> Sense Wyrm is less rare than you'd initially think, given a number of Tribes get it. Certain Tribes feel like they do an Auspice's job better than the Auspice, and ironically encourage you to pair the Tribe with a different Auspice to cover more ground. There's no rhyme or reason to which Gifts cost Gnosis and which don't in many cases. Far more Gifts are focused on things outside combat than you'd expect. The power level of Level 1 Gifts is, as we know, enormously varied but this was also reinforced.<br /> <br /> Given that each of these powers costs 3 or 5 XP, Garou are pretty well off when it comes to their cheapest powers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T02:11:34-07:00","id":1512840,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1512837#post1512837\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The section on throwing weapons (before general combat maneuvers) makes it clear that thrown weapons still have close and long ranges, but instead of being determined by the weapon, the ST is just supposed to figure it out based on the aerodynamics and Strength of the character (and allows a greater range of difficulty modifiers). While W20 forgot to actually say how to figure out the ranges for thrown weapons in any way, they're still capable of long ranged attacks like firearms and bows.<br /> <br /> The RAW don't really care what the actual distance is, just that it's beyond the base range of the weapon (yes, I agree it's meant to reduce the penalty for up to double range from 8 to 7). A light pistol with a range of 20 gets the benefit of this Gift shooting from 21m to 40m. Thrown is no different. As well, while I don't think W20 addressed this, the WoD games do generally treat throwing assist mechanisms like slings as spear-throwers as still operating as thrown weapons mechanically, vastly increasing their ranges beyond 50m. There's at least a few legendary figure worthy of Silver Fang emulate from early history that used slings to great effect.</div> </div> </div> </div>Nice - and yeah agreed on the RAW. It's a handy Gift to offset a penalty, which is kinda handy for situations where foes are out of reach. Plus while the Fangs are super into melee weapons, there's no reason you can't play a tricksy Fang who has a Fetish Bow or Fetish Rifle with talen arrows/bullets, gleefully outperforming their relatives who are too married to melee.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T02:20:35-07:00","id":1512843,"title":"","content":"Since I don't think higher level Gifts making lower ones obsolete has really come up yet, how do you feel about how Beat of the Heart's Drum sits as a Green when Pulse of the Prey is there at Rank 2 (though a Rabagash Gift). IME players will always take Pulse of the Prey over Beat of the Heart's Drum unless they think there's going to be a road block in terms of find a spirit to teach them (though dog and wolf spirits teach Pulse of the Prey which is generally a much easier task to get to than a Gift specific to a Tribe's Totem's brood).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T02:23:42-07:00","id":1512844,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1512840#post1512840\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Nice - and yeah agreed on the RAW. It's a handy Gift to offset a penalty, which is kinda handy for situations where foes are out of reach. Plus while the Fangs are super into melee weapons, there's no reason you can't play a tricksy Fang who has a Fetish Bow or Fetish Rifle with talen arrows/bullets, gleefully outperforming their relatives who are too married to melee.</div> </div> </div> </div>You can also be really cheeky and pull a not-yet-King David vs. Goliath: Incapacitate at range, and then saunter up and us their sword to finish the job (though perhaps forcing a surrender in a duel instead of straight up decapitating them if appropriate).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T02:35:07-07:00","id":1512845,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1512843#post1512843\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Since I don't think higher level Gifts making lower ones obsolete has really come up yet, how do you feel about how Beat of the Heart's Drum sits as a Green when Pulse of the Prey is there at Rank 2 (though a Rabagash Gift). IME players will always take Pulse of the Prey over Beat of the Heart's Drum unless they think there's going to be a road block in terms of find a spirit to teach them (though dog and wolf spirits teach Pulse of the Prey which is generally a much easier task to get to than a Gift specific to a Tribe's Totem's brood).</div> </div> </div> </div>I guess that if you get access to Pulse of the Prey then it could make Beat of the Heart-Drum obsolete, but two things to keep in mind:<ul><li>Pulse of the Prey requires you to know something about your target. If you find a pool of blood, or an abandoned weapon, that isn't a description of a foe. The examples given are having their name, initials, a crude description etc. It's unlikely that &quot;whoever owned this murder weapon&quot; would apply. So there are situations where Beat of the Heart-Drum will work and Pulse of the Prey won't.</li> <li>If the person you're searching for is hiding, that can foil Pulse of the Prey. It won't foil Beat of the Heart-Drum.</li> </ul>PotP is a much stronger Gift, should go without saying, and if you're a Ragabash Younger Brother then perhaps saving 3xp could be wise... but IMO BotHD is still well worth it. You might be Rank 1 for a long time, and even at Rank 2 you may find yourself in situations where one Gift works and the other one doesn't.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T08:06:00-07:00","id":1512873,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 HOMID GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Onto some Level 2 Gifts! Homids are up first as always; as usual their Gifts are a mix of social and technological stuff. There are only 4 Gifts in some of these level 2 lists, so hopefully this should be a bit faster!<br /> <br /> I should note that while I will be taking the level of a Gift into account, it's hard to keep that in mind all of the time; there are enough incredible level 1 Gifts that it's hard to gauge where that leaves incredible level 2 Gifts. Level will be most likely to be taken into account at 4 and 5, where you can have a perfectly functional Gift... which is just 2 or 3 levels higher than it should be!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Jam Technology:</b></u></span> A Gnosis point, a Manipulation + Crafts with difficulty of 4-9, and a range of 50 feet. Being able to stop cars, guns, planes, machinery in factories and so on is pretty great! In a way this is a bit like Jam Weapon, however it's less effective at jamming weapons in exchange for being much broader. The lowest difficulty being the most complex machinery is also good; stopping a HIT Mark with a difficulty 4 check ain't bad at all!<br /> <br /> One fun use of this would be completely knocking your opponents out of a car chase; just keep in mind that the Gift does not discriminate. If you're riding a Hispo Garou, then stopping all cars within 15 meters is a great idea. If you're in a car yourself, you've turned a car chase into a standing fight. Which might be handy, who knows.<br /> <br /> Used without care this Gift could have unintended consequences. Be sure to have a think about what you're inside, riding on, and what's nearby before you turn off every nearby phone.<br /> <br /> Given the tricky targeting and relatively small range I'm going to give this an Amber, but it's still a pretty neat Gift which could cause some huge moments if you use it surgically. If your campaign is about fighting Cyberdogs or a high tech Pentex division or Etherites or the Technocracy, or causing havoc in the Digital Web, then this will jump up to Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Mark of the Wolf:</b></u></span> Very variable by its very nature. Let's start by keeping in mind that the most fixed mechanic of The Curse is that humans who have a WP lower than your Rage will be afraid of you and go out of their way to avoid or exclude you as a threat. If you have a Rage of 1 or 2, this Gift does next to nothing. At 3 it's dubious. At 4 and 5 things start to get somewhere. Unfortunately this means that this Gift would have, ultimately, made more sense as a Galliard or Ahroun Gift!<br /> <br /> Assuming a Rage of 5, this Gift is easy to use, has a pretty big impact, and is poetic justice when applied to someone who mistreats you because of your Rage. The duration is also pretty good. Unfortunately this is another one of these Gifts where someone suffers offscreen, which means it will only help you in niche situations. Do you care whether the Pentex rep's hookup decided he had bad vibes or not? Whether his driver put in a report of workplace bullying? Whether the person at reception thought he was a dick? Short of your ST deciding some butterfly effect takes place, it's unlikely to make that big a difference.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Speech of the World:</b></u></span> A global translator for humans is pretty amazing, and suits the Breed quite well. Being able to speak to any human NPC you encounter regardless of any language barrier is handy, and even for people who do speak English (or whatever language is the base in your campaign) there's a rapport and ease of communication that comes with speaking to someone in their own language.<br /> <br /> Use this to show off, make friends, travel, and link up with the indigenous communities who can matter so much in WTA's politics and lore. The only thing keeping this from being a Green is that you have to roll to activate it in addition to it solving a problem that is a little hit and miss in coming up.<br /> <br /> Notably a Homid Galliard of the Elder Brother Tribe winds up having this Gift in addition to Spirit Speech and Beast Speech for the ultimate translator.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Staredown:</b></u></span> Targeting your opponent's Rank is pretty neat - against mortals and most supernatural foes this will have a difficulty of 5. One difficulty is that a target does have the option to spend a point of WP to ignore the effect for one turn, which means NPCs can still get up to quite a lot of mischief. Draining the WP of NPCs can be handy, but unless they're going to run out in the course of your interaction with them...<br /> <br /> It's hard to be certain whether a pacified Garou/Fera can spend WP to ignore the anti-combat effect for one turn; I think they can, but the way the Gift is worded is a little vague.<br /> <br /> Overall this is a pretty good effect, the base difficulty is low, there's no activation cost, and it can banish an NPC from a scene quite effectively. It has some rough edges and isn't perfect, but it sits at the higher end of Amber. Removing the option to spend WP would make it Green, but that would also make it a very un-fun Gift to play with.<br /> <br /> ================================================== =<br /> <br /> Nothing too iconic here - Speech of the World is worth picking up as a Metis or Lupus if travel and isolated communities are key parts of the story. There are other Gifts that do Staredown's job better, such as True Fear (at least in my opinion).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 08:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T08:18:56-07:00","id":1512875,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 METIS GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> As always I love Metis, and Metis Gifts. Rank 2 has some very exciting ones.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Burrow:</b></u></span> This is a weird yet effective travel option. Making huge tunnels through solid rock is just funny, especially since with enough successes you can basically go at a walking pace. No cost, a roll you'll likely ace with softer targets, and overall it's just a very fun Gift. Good for undermining places, making a subterranean base swiftly, evading bad weather, creating a back entrance into a Pit, and generally moving unseen.<br /> <br /> You do leave a huge tunnel without support beams behind you, if you're in Crinos, and that could spell disaster. It's also a tunnel others can follow, and by its very nature it's a potential Veil risk.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Curse of Hatred:</b></u></span> Spending Gnosis means this is more of a pre-combat attack than anything else. You only need 1 success, so spend WP on the check. The check itself could be very easy or very difficult depending on the target's WP.<br /> <br /> Losing 2 temporary WP and 2 Rage is a pretty handy debuff, the Rage especially; softening up a target so they have less Rage to spend on Gifts and Bonus actions will make a fight go more smoothly. Unfortunately this Gift doesn't really do anything to Spirits as it were, since they fuel Charms with Essence, not temporary Rage points.<br /> <br /> The RP potential of this Gift is kind of fun, given that you're hurling an insult packed with the trauma most Metis suffer every day.<br /> <br /> A niche effect is removing excess Rage from someone who has gone over their maximum and is risking Frenzy.<br /> <br /> All in all if you assume that you can get this Gift to go off before a fight (semi-reliable) and your target has Rage which they can spend on extra actions (BSDs, rival Garou, some Fomori), you could picture this Gift as a way to steal 2 actions from an enemy for 1 Gnosis. Overall it's an Amber, but it has good potential.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Form Mastery:</b></u></span> This one is a flat buff which makes you better at transforming, which will save Rage and help you get cool mixed forms off more easily. Removing the WP cost of partial transformation and giving a difficulty reduction is great. With this Gift feel free to accumulate partial transformations until your form is a mix of all five forms. Why? Because you can!<br /> <br /> The RP potential of showing the protean, fluid nature of the Garou body is pretty neat. It also speaks to the Metis' nature, being Garou from birth, and thus expert in controlling the natural gifts of Garou. Play that up and have fun showing the weirder side of the wolf.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Sense Silver:</b></u></span> 100 yard range, pretty achievable roll, and this helps you locate Klaives, werewolf hunters, silver bullets and so on. It'll also help you prospect for silver if you want to invest in mining. I'm sure Gaia won't mind taking one for the team with your mining venture...<br /> <br /> This is Amber because while the execution is really good, and the power works very well, it's ultimately sensing one very specific thing. An important thing, but still niche. I'd suggest picking this up once you have 6xp spare, then using it intermittently to see what pops up.<br /> <br /> ================================================<br /> <br /> Form Mastery is the standout here, and is worth 10xp for Homid and Lupus in my opinion. The others are neat and have their place.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 08:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T09:20:26-07:00","id":1512880,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 LUPUS GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Lupus Gifts are a bit of a mixed bag for me at this level.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Axis Mundi:</b></u></span> The effect only works in the &quot;Gaia Realm&quot;, as in on earth. Being a flawless compass would be kind of cute as a level 1 Gift, with another weak effect tacked on. Having it on its own at level 2 is... weird. Silent Striders also get a better version of this of course.<br /> <br /> Short of luring your foes into a magical maze garden, it's impossible to use this offensively. I'm sure it would be very handy in a real war, but it's unlikely to impact many rolls or important checks, especially since your mundane senses are so good and you should have some Survival.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Eye of the Eagle:</b></u></span> Eagle and Falcon have likely had litigation over Gift names. This Gift and Eye of the Falcon both existing feels like a joke, but they do quite different things. This Gift adds miles onto your visual perception range, but doesn't ignore obstacles. This means that if you are atop a good vantage you can see for miles and miles. This is handy for scouting and guarding, both of which matter a lot to Garou. It isn't my cup of tea but it's good to have in your Pack.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Name the Spirit:</b></u></span> Determining the variety and &quot;power level&quot; of Spirits at a glance is amazing; this information can be very hard to come by otherwise. Theurges also get this Gift, thankfully.<br /> <br /> Being able to determine that a Spirit is impersonating another Spirit is useful, and knowing which Spirits could one-shot a typical Garou could be a lifesaver.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Scent of Sight:</b></u></span> This Gift makes dark, foggy and otherwise obscured places your best friend. Both for stealth and combat. Being able to passively see perfectly with your scent alone is incredible, and will positive impact a lot of scenarios. This will also be useful underground and underwater, and allow you to defeat a lot of visual illusions. Many Gifts and Powers could be more easily overcome if you keep your eyes closed, along with powers from other supernaturals such as Dominate.<br /> <br /> Using this Gift offensively is handy - try using flashbangs, Gifts that generate cover, smoke, cover of night and so on.<br /> <br /> Well worth 10xp to buy out-of-list, especially if you are blind.<br /> <br /> ================================================== ================<br /> <br /> Two of these are very solid, one is pretty good, and one is bad. I don't even mind that - level 2 Gifts are pricey. Having too many good options is a big tax on XP at times.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 08:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T10:38:07-07:00","id":1512890,"title":"","content":"Burrow gets even more ridiculous in the claws of huge Warding-or-equivalent Mokole. Now <i>that's</i> a Veil-endangering tunnel complex cut into solid rock...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T12:44:52-07:00","id":1512902,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1512845#post1512845\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I guess that if you get access to Pulse of the Prey then it could make Beat of the Heart-Drum obsolete, but two things to keep in mind:</div> </div> </div> </div>My point here was less about debating the specifics, and more about how Gifts ratings will account for similar Gifts that exist. You point out True Fear in regards to Staredown, and Heaven's Guidance regarding Axis Mundi (which is going to be even more relevant since Axis Mundi is also a Strider Gift).<br /> <br /> If you're taking similar Gifts into account, shouldn't Beat of the Heart-Drum have to be measured up to Pulse of the Prey? Even if Beat of the Heart-Drum isn't useless if you have Pulse of the Prey (and unless the pack is separated, you generally only need one tracker), certainly it's a lot more niche in comparison.<br /> <br /> Sure there's a downside to higher Rank Gifts meaning they're less likely to come up because you have to get Rank, and even more so if you're going out of your lists, but I think there's a big difference between a Rank 4 Gift that blows a Rank 1 Gift out of the water, and a Rank 2 Gift vs. a Rank 1 Gift.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1512875#post1512875\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is Amber because while the execution is really good, and the power works very well, it's ultimately sensing one very specific thing. An important thing, but still niche. I'd suggest picking this up once you have 6xp spare, then using it intermittently to see what pops up.</div> </div> </div> </div>To me a big downside of this is how easily it turns into a &quot;spam&quot; Gift where there's pretty much no reason not to use it constantly, outside of combat, but then you have to roll all the time, which is a drag on play.<br /> <br /> As well, the 100 yards thing is almost a negative if you get into suburbs or more urban areas. If you only get 1-2 successes, most people have some silver in their homes, so the Gift really starts to suffer in utility You have to have a really big dice-pool (or your ST rule that sensory diff bonuses apply to Gifts like this) to reliably get 3+ successes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T15:24:45-07:00","id":1512915,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1512890#post1512890\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Burrow gets even more ridiculous in the claws of huge Warding-or-equivalent Mokole. Now <i>that's</i> a Veil-endangering tunnel complex cut into solid rock...</div> </div> </div> </div>Geologists in the WoD probably have an explanation for how micro-earthquakes can make Godzilla sized holes in rock, stretching on for miles.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T15:25:48-07:00","id":1512916,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1512902#post1512902\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> To me a big downside of this is how easily it turns into a &quot;spam&quot; Gift where there's pretty much no reason not to use it constantly, outside of combat, but then you have to roll all the time, which is a drag on play.<br /> <br /> As well, the 100 yards thing is almost a negative if you get into suburbs or more urban areas. If you only get 1-2 successes, most people have some silver in their homes, so the Gift really starts to suffer in utility You have to have a really big dice-pool (or your ST rule that sensory diff bonuses apply to Gifts like this) to reliably get 3+ successes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah Gifts you can 'spam' are annoying, and this one is a bad culprit. I'd rather it had a cost you have to pay once per scene, and then you can use it freely. No cost, and no downside to failure or botching, means constant annoyance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-27T08:21:28-07:00","id":1512961,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 RAGABASH GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Auspice Gifts get pretty intense at 2nd level in some cases; Ragabash get some interesting ones!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Blissful </b><b>Ignorance</b><b>:</b></u></span> Requiring you to stay still for the power to work is quite restrained for a level 2 Gift, but this is pretty neat. Stamina + Stealth is a good combo to roll, especially since it's so heavily buffed in Crinos form. The fact that you are hidden from everything is also pretty great; more successes means better hiding, but 1 success is enough to hide from people who aren't actively searching.<br /> <br /> Comparing this to Brother's Scent... it's better in some ways, worse in others. All in all it's good to have a small variety of stealth Gifts which work in different ways, if you're committed to hiding.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Pulse of the Prey:</b></u></span> As discussed above this is another amazing tracking Gift. The prompt to use it is some kind of idea who you're looking for, a description, a sketch, their signature or initials and so on. This does mean there are different means of tracking which could work better, such as Heart-Drum if you have a sample of blood, or a possession, but no description or signature etc.<br /> <br /> This Gift can be outdone by actively hiding, which also is a step down from Heart-Drum, but it's superior in the sense that it helps you nail an exact location and isn't just a hot/cold. Well worth having both, if you happen to be a Younger Brother.<br /> <br /> Handy for finding packmates, finding spirits, and tracking down targets in an investigation. Sometimes a plot will have to have some mcguffin block this Gift, but that's alright in my book as long as it's done sparingly.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Spider's Song:</b></u></span> This is excellent; intercepting calls and texts from mobiles is very handy, and being able to do so from a good distance as long as you keep your eyes on the target is great. This could provide incredibly good clues.<br /> <br /> RPwise being privy to the texts of PCs and NPCs could be handy. The fact that a Lupus Ragabash with Technology 0 and Computers 0 can have this while a Glass Walker won't is also very funny to me.<br /> <br /> A cost of 1 Gnosis is a little annoying, especially since the power is convo-specific. Being able to graze through a variety of text conversations happening in a single cafe would be pretty neat. As it stands this is a great way to get insight you'd otherwise lack and catch out enemies. It's only Amber because it has a price and you need to suspect foul play before using it; wasting Gnosis to read &quot;love you see you soon&quot; won't feel good.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Taking the Forgotten:</b></u></span> I'm in two minds about this one. Because reading it, you assume you'd succeed.<br /> <br /> Let's break it down. Imagine you have a Wits + Larceny of 9, and even have an applicable specialty. And let's imagine that you're trying to take something from a smart yet sheltered Theurge. Intelligence 5, Streetwise 0, say. 9 dice, with a spec, difficulty 5, and you need 3 successes. With WP spent you'll get the Gift to go off 95% of the time.<br /> <br /> Now let's imagine your foe is a canny Ragabash with Int 4 and Streetwise 3 - difficulty 7. And let's imagine your pool is a more realistic 7 dice, and you don't have a spec, since finding one that would trigger for this power is a little tricky. If you spend WP you &quot;only&quot; have a 72% chance for it to work.<br /> <br /> The takeaway is that if you have a decent dice pool for Wits + Larceny, and you choose the right targets (many Garou won't have good Streetwise, and you'll often be able to gauge roughly who will) you should be fine.<br /> <br /> Make sure that you don't show off what you've stolen, and make sure to pick something that other people won't miss; if you get a Silver Fang Elder's legendary blade off him it won't take long for someone to notice it's missing and jog his memory!<br /> <br /> Think of this as a tool to reduce the risk of getting in trouble when you steal something, rather than eliminating that risk entirely. If you grab something from a vault, make its owner forget they ever owned it and escape clean into the night then the odds of the theft being noticed are reduced, but not 100% eliminated. This is handy for stealing a phone from a pentex rep, getting sensitive data from an enemy, snatching a Fetish you like the look of from a lone wanderer and so on.<br /> <br /> This is Green on the assumption you have a great pool, pick a sensible target and use the power wisely. Otherwise it would be a solid amber.<br /> <br /> =========================================<br /> <br /> Some of these are pretty excellent, and they lean into the thief/rogue/scout aspect of the Auspice quite well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 08:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-27T09:25:55-07:00","id":1512969,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 THEURGE GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Theurge Gifts are key in my opinion, since their main role as knowing more Rites and having more access to Rites is relatively negotiable; there's no reason you can't be an Ahroun with a high Rituals knowledge and convince Rite masters to teach you more Rites with Talen gifts and so on, it'll just depend on the Sept. Their unique Gifts mean that they have a defined role even when Rites get shared around.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Battle Mandala:</b></u></span> This one goes out of its way to be Veil friendly which is nice; only those who have Gnosis can see the mark, which has a stealthy bonus use. Ask Kinfolk to draw a symbol you've made on the ground, and you'll get &quot;what symbol&quot; from 99%; those who have Gnosis may unwittingly reveal themselves to you.<br /> <br /> The primary use of this is an AOE pseudo-attack against spirits. A 100 or 150 foot area where all spirits lose 1 Essence per turn is very handy, since Essence both fuels Charms and acts as Health. If a pack fights a horde of spirits with relatively low health this will be an amazing power, and could destroy some weaker spirits in as few as 3-5 turns... which is still unfortunately a very long time!<br /> <br /> This Gift forces Spirits to fight or flee, which is handy, and gives a marginal debuff to enemy's Essence pool which is cool, and dealing 1 point of guaranteed damage each round to 5+ enemies is pretty great. Better than launching an attack which may well miss, perhaps. Note that allied spirits other than your totem will lose Essence too.<br /> <br /> One application would be to place this in an area a spirit can't leave - say they're guarding something within the Umbra - and then kite, evade and hide from them. Overall it's useful but not incredible.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Command Spirit:</b></u></span> Incredible. Use this on spirits who are your enemies, outright. Using it on neutral and allied spirits outside of the most dire circumstances is more likely to bite you. It isn't clear how long a command lasts unfortunately; if you tell a Scrag to kill its companions, does it keep going until it's done or the Scrag is destroyed?<br /> <br /> Regardless of the specifics, this is a very potent Gift which can do great things, and also get you into a lot of trouble. Keep in mind that can and should are different. You CAN force a city father to tell you where your enemies are hiding, but SHOULD you. If you abuse this power it could create far more grief than it will ever solve. The difference between a Theurge and a Mage, Sorcerer or Vampire Thaumaturge is that you are supposed to be working with spirits, working naturally within a kind of 'natural right' as it were. If you bully spirits as obnoxiously as a Hermetic, Tremere or similar then you will get a bad reputation among spirits, which is awful for a Theurge.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Name the Spirit:</b></u></span> See the Lupus entry above. Key to a Theurge doing their job, and fixes a lot of problems relating to deception and disguises.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Sight from Beyond:</b></u></span> I know a lot of storytellers give Theurges visions 'just because', so this is a tricky one. Having a Gift which entitles you to receive regular Visions could be great, or mediocre, depending on your ST. Ask them for their opinion and explain that you want to be sure you aren't just paying a tax to receive visions the party would have been sent regardless.<br /> <br /> If I were adjusting this Gift I would make it so that it makes it easier to navigate and understand existing visions, perhaps allowing you to change your POV, glimpse the spirit that sent the vision, or change the type of symbolism being used so you can see the vision from a different angle; for instance replacing a weather metaphor with an animal metaphor.<br /> <br /> One approach would be to make it so that you can spend Gnosis while receiving a vision to get more symbolism/details, or perhaps make a roll when you receive a vision to then gain bonus dice when you make checks relating to the warning you were sent; for instance having bonus dice to stake a vampire after you had a vision about a vampiric threat.<br /> <br /> Like I say, mileage may vary. I've yet to see a table where lacking this Gift meant a Theurge receives 0 visions, but obviously that's ST-dependent and people will have different experiences.<br /> <br /> =========================================<br /> <br /> Command and Name are key, and must-haves. The others are good to grab if they seem to fit the Chronicle. Massive spirit battles, if they are going to happen, will be shaped quite well by a Battle Mandala.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 08:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-27T11:21:57-07:00","id":1512986,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 PHILODOX GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Philodox have amazing level 2 Gifts with a strong emphasis on leading the Garou.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Call to Duty:</b></u></span> I'm glad there are non-Theurge spirit controlling Gifts, and this one suits the half moons well. I am not 100% sure whether the spirit arrives immediately (&quot;summoned&quot; in the magical sense) or arrives after travel (&quot;summoned&quot; in the linguistic sense). I'm assuming they arrive immediately.<br /> <br /> If you know the name of a spirit who has healing, tracking or fighting abilities you now sort of have those abilities. It also COSTS NO GNOSIS! This must be an oversight right? This feels like a very magical power, conjuring a spirit that could be a long distance away and forcing it to perform a task for you. There are also no restrictions on what kind of spirit you may summon it seems.<br /> <br /> I worry that this one might be in a similar camp to Jam Weapon - it's so good it could create problems and wind up getting nerfed or banned at a table. What are people's experiences with it? Naturally the Gift has inbuilt defenses against being abused in a proper in-universe sense, but IC abuse and OOC abuse are very different. Summoning a war spirit to help you fight regularly is a kind of &quot;abuse&quot; in the OOC sense. Forcing a spirit which has an important job to come to you and help you despite it saying it needs to guard a Caern etc. is IC abuse. The latter is easier to regulate.<br /> <br /> Overall I'll call this green, even before considering the &quot;SOS&quot; effect which summons all allied spirits in the vicinity. This is a potent effect which could do a lot of good in some dangerous situations. Just remember that flushing every Gaian spirit out of hiding in a dangerous area could spell disaster for the local ecology. 2 Gnosis is pricey but works well enough. And even Homids should have Gnosis 2 by this point. Discuss with your ST how they imagine the gift being used.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Command the Gathering:</b></u></span> The Garou Nation needing this power means that their rating is looking a bit more dubious, but the Gift itself is good.<br /> <br /> A funny use of this Gift is to filibuster; there is no max duration. If you can keep speaking for hours, you can force people to listen and refuse to yield the floor, perhaps while waiting for some crucial evidence to arrive. Stalling is a viable strategy here, even with enemies. Now people can spend 2 WP to interrupt you, or to leave, that is true, but there's also the possibility of you simply reapplying the power.<br /> <br /> RP wise a &quot;it's time for my dramatic speech&quot; power is great, especially for the lawyer/judge/leader Auspice.<br /> <br /> An underrated aspect of this Gift is that in forcing people to fall silent, stop what they're doing and listen you can interrupt Wyrmish rites, Mage rituals, Garou rites and so on... in theory. The ST might argue that people can automatically spend 2 WP to opt out of the silencing effect immediately, but even then a -2 WP debuff to all your foes as you try to interrupt their ritual is pretty good!<br /> <br /> Given that a Philodox will have Persuasion, and thus can convince practically any regular mortal of anything with some effort and a few rolls (in theory, YMMV, social stuff varies massively by table), the ability to shut people up and force them to listen is pretty great. I like this one; you can live without it but it's worth taking.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>King of the Beasts:</b></u></span> Permanent control over a single animal could be good; having a powerful predator or megaherbivore (thinking lion, elephant, moose etc.) guarding your wild base is pretty awesome. This is a good power for an NPC, helps explain why there's a hippo who guards the river Caern ultra-religiously without ever bothering any of the Kinfolk or Garou.<br /> <br /> In urban environments you'd want to use this on smarter animals like rats, stray cats and dogs and so on, to fetch, carry, deliver, go on watch and so on. It's neat, and combines well with other animal-based Gifts like Scent of Man and Beast Speech. Note that this Gift lets you command an animal, it doesn't let you understand them!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Strength of Purpose:</b></u></span> Infinite Willpower. End of post.<br /> <br /> I should elaborate. Once per scene you can roll Stamina + Rituals, and every 2 successes grants you a point of WP.<br /> <br /> Keep in mind that Crinos form has more Stamina. You could get quite a lot of WP out of this Gift if you use it at every opportunity... which runs into the spam issue. I would defend using it on car journeys and so on as the Gift simply doing what it does - you are a Garou who thanks to spirit magic can replenish your mental fortitude rapidly! That's great! What feels like abuse is simply use; it's just a very strong Gift that restores a crucial resource.<br /> <br /> If you were gonna nerf this: make it once per session, diff 7 stamina + rituals, and you regain 1 WP per success. Once per scene is the issue.<br /> <br /> ==================================================<br /> <br /> Four great powers, all of which can be used creatively to get memorable outcomes. Strength of Purpose and Call to Duty are both so good that you might see them wind up nerfed, but even with adjustments they should be great.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-01-2025, 05:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-28T14:51:49-07:00","id":1513118,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 GALLIARD GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Galliard get 5 level 2 Gifts for some reason! I was expecting 4 in each Auspice, can't lie.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Call of the Wyrm:</b></u></span> A Manipulation + Performance check which forces creatures of the Wyrm to rush your position is a huge gamechanger. Creatures of the Wyrm presumably includes BSDs, corrupted Fera and so on as well as Fomori, Banes, Vampires with Humanity below 7, Malphean Nephandi...<br /> <br /> The given uses are flushing out foes, pulling them into traps and ambushes, but even in a flat battle the ability to force all your enemies to move to a specific location could be lifesaving, as you draw them away from something critical.<br /> <br /> Naturally this Gift has potential for causing huge problems, but even if you can't handle the Wyrm creatures you draw out you can flee; few things are faster than a Lupus Garou sprinting! Some things are, of course, but you have a head start. Remember that just as calling all the local good spirits can leave good things unguarded, calling all the local evil spirits can leave evil things unguarded, and also leave victims of their attacks and attempted corruption free to flee, rally or fight back.<br /> <br /> It feels weird that the free will of intelligent and highly driven Wyrmish creatures is neglected in this power; even with a WP of 10 you can lose the contested roll and just become possessed by a need to rush a position, which is... cool in one way, but could feel silly in another. A meticulous plotter of a Fomori or Mockery Breed who holds great sway in Pentex having no choice but to run out of the lobby and into traffic to be sniped by a Galliard's packmates whose guns are on a nearby rooftop feels... silly? Awesome? Unearned? Brilliant?<br /> <br /> Regardless of some rough edges and risk of a situational nerf (truly powerful and self-possessed wyrm creatures can spend 2 WP to ignore the call etc. but are helpless to stop their minions), this is a solid Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Command the Gathering:</b></u></span> See the Philodox entry.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Distractions:</b></u></span> This is a big upgrade of the Taunt combat maneuver, and I love it. It has a 1:1 impact instead of 2:1, and it lasts for 3 turns. Being able to effectively taunt enemies is great, and will help you significantly debuff enemies in many cases. Make sure to check if your Pure Breed applies to using Distractions against other Garou, including BSDs. I wouldn't suggest allowing Persuasion to apply however; this is ultimately a combat check using social abilities, not a social check.<br /> <br /> This Gift is a rare example of a situation where the Manipulation debuffs of the various forms actually matter in fact! Though of course neither Spirits nor Garou are included in that Manipulation debuff.<br /> <br /> It's worth noting that nothing in this Gift requires you to use it in combat. Use it in a race, a challenge, against someone trying to intimidate your packmate, to undermine escape attempts or ritual efforts. Hell, RAW, this Gift absolutely obliterates M20 Mages. It also will interfere exceptionally well with Stepping Sideways, activating dangerous Gifts and so on. Brilliant power to grab.<br /> <br /> The examples of things you can do to distract is a little odd though; barks and yips. What are you supposed to do in Homid form? Bark like a dog? I mean that's a fun limitation of the Gift, don't get me wrong, but it would be nice if it was more clear!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Dreamspeak:</b></u></span> Reaching out to any sleeping PC or NPC you know and altering the course of their dreams is... amazing. It has no cost too!<br /> <br /> You can try to inception a Pentex rep into dissolving the firm he is inheriting. You can send messages to allies. You can play with your sleeping child a continent away. You can give your love a kiss. You can disguise yourself as a portent of some kind. You can visit the dreams of a Garou blessed with visions, and deliberately interfere with those visions. You can threaten or cajole your enemies, turning dreams to nightmares. You can apologize to a Garou who might otherwise kill you on sight. You can communicate with someone in a coma. The uses, both mission-critical and personal, are pretty amazing.<br /> <br /> Make sure you aren't on your last Gnosis point, in case you get kicked out of course.<br /> <br /> There is also great crossover potential with Changelings, especially if you are Fianna and/or fae blooded. In theory you could also help a Mage (say a Kinfolk) with a Seeking, though personally I rule that attempts to help someone undergoing a Seeking always fail.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Howls in the Night:</b></u></span> Situational, but neat. It depends whether you're going to see any returns on stopping your enemies from sleeping. Will missing out on some WP regeneration and getting a bit fatigued effect NPCs? In a huge simulationist approach 100%, it could sway a battle or effect ongoing skirmishes. But will the PCs see returns on it? That depends on the ST.<br /> <br /> I like it enough that it has to be Amber, especially since taking revenge on Vampires by keeping all of the low Humanity ones awake at night is funny.<br /> <br /> ========================================<br /> <br /> The Greens here feel like must-haves to me, but I could picture someone who is less interested in RP not picking up Dreamspeak. Howls and Command are a little more down to your preference.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-06-2023, 08:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-28T15:35:27-07:00","id":1513126,"title":"","content":"The potential dark comedy of Call of the Wyrm in an interaction with a vampire is... (chef's kiss). Like, they keep trying to convince the pack that they're no concern, but the pack calls a GW galliard waiting some distance away and gets them to use the Gift, and the vampire tries to do a variant of the &quot;zombie-bitten guy trying to hide that he's changing&quot; bit. Blood sweating down the forehead, biting the lip and shaking, and then finally, the dam bursts...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-29T00:43:24-07:00","id":1513173,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 AHROUN GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Ahroun get 4 level 2 Gifts, they're a mixed bag, but that doesn't matter. The only level 2 Gift that actually matters is here.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Sense Silver:</b></u></span> See the Metis entry. Nice to see the Ahroun get some powers which are combat-adjacent without directly influencing combat; sensing silver bullets, klaives and so on could shift a conflict, or help the Garou arm themselves.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Shield of Rage:</b></u></span> For a cost of 1WP, you can make all spirit enemies have -2 to their damage dice pool against you. Assuming a difficulty of 6, which is pretty universal outside of some weird edge cases (whomst among us hasn't been buffed by Dragon's Ire) this will mean the damage inflicted to you by spirits is reduced by 1.7. It's worth noting that this Gift is very handy against weak spirits; you can wade through an army of Sludge Elementals without any concerns of them actually hurting you; their 3 dice of Agg damage becomes 1 die, which is pitiful against an Ahroun with a Soak of 8 or so.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately the buff is to yourself only, which is a shame. If it applied to the whole pack it would be much more useful. Weirdly it incentivizes spirits to attack everyone but you, which is the last thing you want to do!<br /> <br /> In a typical 'dangerous confrontation', say with a Spirit with a Rage of 10, going from 4.2 Agg per attack to 3.7 Agg per attack is neat enough to get some use out of the Gift.<br /> <br /> I almost wish the Gift added your Rage to soak pools against Spirit attacks now that I think about it... the name would then make more sense.<br /> <br /> A marginal use of this Gift is in challenges; challenging another Garou to withstand a certain number of blows from a powerful spirit. Still, you'd want to find a spirit who is able to inflict bashing or lethal damage, and in WTA they seem to all do agg by default.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Spirit of the Fray:</b></u></span> You probably knew this was coming.<br /> <br /> In a serious confrontation between two dangerous enemies, whoever goes first will often win. Two Ahroun stand there, each planning to knock the other over and then eviscerate them with 3 bonus actions. Who wins? Whoever goes first. Confronting a powerful Mage. Confronting a powerful Fae. Confronting a powerful Spirit. In the WoD combat can be, in some cases, exceptionally fast and deadly. One on one fights with an Ahroun are skewed even more in this direction. You need to kill your foe ASAP because the moment they have any time to do anything they will drop a nuke on you, whether it's an absurdly high damage attack, teleporting you to another country, forcing you into a Frenzy, freezing you in place...<br /> <br /> Turn economy simply means that +10 to Initiative is game-changing. Even with a Dexterity and Wits of 1 (at which point you can't really take bonus actions... why have you done this...) your modifier of 12 will beat most opponents.<br /> <br /> The bonus +10 for spending a point of Gnosis is interesting. If you're rolling Ini once and then keeping that number for the whole fight (some STs do this) then the Gnosis expenditure is pretty great. If you are re-rolling Ini every round, as you should be, then it's less powerful. However if things are at their culmination, and you just need to get in one more attack, push that boulder a little further, complete one last action, and getting there before someone else is utterly critical? Consider spending that point of Gnosis. And of course if you have a way to spend Gnosis and Rage in the same turn (which there is a Gift for down the line) then you can do this more freely.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>True Fear:</b></u></span> As an intimidation power, I much prefer this one. The mechanics are fixed enough that you won't see it getting worked around; in my experience if a &quot;you can't do anything&quot; power is too good, it winds up either being very un-fun and annoying the table as a whole (dnd5e is riddled with these) or it winds up being nerfed and worked around and undermined.<br /> <br /> True Fear hits a sweet spot for my taste; you overwhelm a target with terror, they can't attack you, but they defend themselves and otherwise act normally (with immense terror impacting their RP of course). Strength + Intimidation is a great thing to roll, and it makes me laugh that my sweetheart Ahroun has Intimidation 0 and will probably need to learn how to 'act mean' when he gets this Gift. Difficulty being the target's Willpower means that badass NPCs are harder to hit but not impossible, and of course you can use Crinos.<br /> <br /> My only question is whether this power should interact with Fox Frenzy or not. &quot;If you get 5+ successes against a target who is capable of Fox Frenzy or similar, it will trigger&quot; etc. I mean I could easily imagine this Gift putting a Vampire into Rotschrek for instance!<br /> <br /> Strong in all the right ways, a great way to avoid killing someone, complements Delirium and helps you to banish annoying security guards who are &quot;just here to feed their families&quot;.<br /> <br /> &quot;Simply looming ominously&quot; is a good thing to note - you can use this in Homid, and with some degree of subtlety, even if the reaction of your foe will be anything but subtle. Having a great intimidation option which doesn't rely on the Manipulation attribute is wonderful for a meathead PC as well.<br /> <br /> ===============================================<br /> <br /> To my taste, having one or two amazing Gifts is better than a variety of above average ones, as it leaves you with more XP to spend elsewhere; Ahroun continue on that trend!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 04-15-2025, 07:10 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-29T02:27:50-07:00","id":1513178,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 BLACK FURY GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Level 2 Black Fury Gifts are a bit odd, but there are some strong ones.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Curse of Aeolus:</b></u></span> This one is odd. The fog 'obscures vision' but halves Perception pools... so can you see or not? As far as I can tell, RAW, this Gift doesn't blind the target, so they don't suffer the mechanical effects of having been blinded. Some guidance would be great, but we don't even get any hints. Halving a Per+Alertness pool of 10 down to 5 is great and all, but in a fight I don't generally care how well you can perceive me, I care whether you can perceive me.<br /> <br /> If your ST infers a +1 difficulty, great, but there's nothing like that in the power as far as I can tell.<br /> <br /> Losing 1 die from Willpower rolls is neat and all... but how often are people rolling Willpower? Even if people are rolling WP to abort to defensive actions, how often is that happening? And how much will 1 die impact it? It doesn't even make it easier to hit your enemies with most powers; most powers use WP as a target difficulty, not as a resisted roll.<br /> <br /> Ultimately this feels like a far worse version of Shroud, the level 1 Elder Brother Gift. I'm going to make this Red, but if your ST counts enemies inflicted with this power as &quot;partially blinded&quot; with difficulty modifiers applying as a result, then its rating would be higher. The power also gives no indication of how large the fog is or how long it lasts. Basically the power is horribly underwritten.<br /> <br /> My fix? Just make it Shroud (fixed area, blinds everyone in it but you can see through it, can be defeated by sensory gifts which allow you to see with scent etc.) with a few changes. It uses fog instead of darkness, it's level 2 instead of level 1, the difficulty is based on humidity instead of darkness, lose the pointless WP thing, and so on. Because right now I'm not sure what it does, for how long, or how large the area it effects is. It could cover a whole beach in fog and help you and your party sneak into a base. It could cover a small brawl. I have no clue. Basically this is red out of spite, as much as anything.<br /> <br /> EDIT: It turns out this covers a city block, as per the final version of the PDF. It's still bad, but not AS bad. With that edit in mind <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Curse of Aeolus</b></u></span> would be amber.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Form Mastery:</b></u></span> See the Metis entry. Cool to see more Wyld themed 'empowering the natural powers of the Garou' Gifts.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Kali's Tongue:</b></u></span> I wish they pulled through with the flavour for this power. The legend is almost irrelevant; it gives us the name, and it gives us the idea of stopping a foe from regenerating, but the actual execution of the Gift itself has nothing to do with the legend really. If the user had to bite their foe and touch them with their tongue for the Gift to work that would be cool!<br /> <br /> Regardless of missing some points for flavour, this Gift is pretty amazing! Many dangerous foes such as certain Fomori, BSDs, Fera, some Spirits and so on will heal and regenerate. A relatively easy check to block healing is great! I'm mindful of the fact that Regen is only 1 point per turn, and it only effects Bashing/Lethal - typically speaking your foes won't be regenerating Aggravated damage. So I'm in two minds of where to place this; against an enemy who can regenerate or heal Aggravated damage, in a prolonged and strenuous fight where attrition is the best way forward, this would be Green. In most situations Amber feels right.<br /> <br /> A weird and niche application of this power which is bound to get you in trouble would be to use it on someone who is suffering in Erebus; they'll die quite swiftly unless a Crone fishes them out. Say you did it to settle a feud between Septs or families or whatever. The problem is that you'll likely be thrown into the silver lake for your crimes, but still, fun idea. Alternatively you can get into a fist fight with a Metis or Lupus, in homid form, and disable their regeneration to catch them off guard. Also well worth having to use against Vampires, who can heal themselves quite rapidly with blood, and against certain Mage threats who may have regeneration or do some huge heals now and then.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Kneel:</b></u></span> This one feels weird to me.<br /> <br /> For starters, the flavour and reasoning behind this makes me cringe a little - I feel like when you have &quot;Amazonian&quot; type characters, whether in a book or a game or any medium, it winds up feeling very obvious and a little embarrassing when part of their thing is forcing men to kneel. It's a bit Girlboss. It's a bit recidivist. It's a bit lame. The takeaway from women being subjugated by men should be &quot;subjugation is wrong&quot; not &quot;subjugation would be very awesome and cool if we were the ones doing it&quot;. I don't buy into the hate that the Black Furies get, this idea that they're 'misandrist', it feels like a misreading of the Tribe (a Tribe which does have problems!), but this power really feeds into that bad vibe. Ultimately the only Tribe that makes sense for this in my head is the Silver Fangs. Theirs is the only Tribal culture in which I can imagine someone supposedly wise forcing someone to kneel magically is considered to have okay vibes, given they believe in Divine Right.<br /> <br /> Anyways, mechanically it's also a bit weird. You roll Manipulation+Intimidation against a difficulty of their Willpower, all good so far, works for one turn per success, and they can spend Gnosis/Quint/Blood/etc. to ignore the power.<br /> <br /> See the problem here? Either it's amazing, in which case you've neutered a threat, or it doesn't work at all because they spent a bit of a resource. It isn't even a question of the target showing off how willful they are or how much of a badass they are; that would be better represented by spending or rolling Willpower, or taking a point of Bashing damage to ignore the impulse to kneel, or something. The fact that Rank isn't factored into this Gift either is weird. Many such Gifts give protections to Rank, but for some reason this Gift comes off as inherently insubordinate, which might be part of the point; bringing a more entitled man to his knees or whatever. I almost want to give this Gift a Red out of spite, but it's useful enough against mortals / Fomori who lack Gnosis / Homid Garou etc. that it can grab an Amber.<br /> <br /> My fix? Flip the Gift. Change the name and mechanics to be about resisting such commands. The difficulty of powers which control or command you are increased by 1, and the difficulty is increased to 10 if the command relates to kneeling, bowing, begging and so on. Now I can see brave sisterhood and resistance towards patriarchal bullshit; in fighting back against such commands, not in giving them.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Pulse of the Prey:</b></u></span> See the Ragabash section.<br /> <br /> =============================================<br /> <br /> I'm starting to worry that this deep dive will see more and more disappointing Gifts in the Tribal sections, as these may have seen the least playtesting or been given the least thought, especially at higher levels; we'll have to see!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-07-2023, 05:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-29T04:20:31-07:00","id":1513185,"title":"","content":"True Fear is great, but if your ST is sadistic (like my first one was), they will have a BSD pack following Hakaken, and every character's first action is True Fear. It got to the point where PCs could do paltry damage before getting True Feared once more.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-29T04:31:48-07:00","id":1513189,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1513185#post1513185\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">True Fear is great, but if your ST is sadistic (like my first one was), they will have a BSD pack following Hakaken, and every character's first action is True Fear. It got to the point where PCs could do paltry damage before getting True Feared once more.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah... at that point I'm thinking two things:<br /> <br /> 1) this ST shouldn't be STing, they are stuck in the &quot;adversarial&quot; mindset which will ultimately lead to upset<br /> 2) the point of this encounter is to realize that the best possible move is to take Lupus form and flee, and find a way to fight the pack without them getting a chance to use the Gift (laying traps, ambushing, hit and runs, guerilla tactics, long range attacks, spirit allies, forcing them into Frenzy somehow etc.)<br /> <br /> I hope it would be 2 but who can say! Depends on how much you trust the ST.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-29T13:32:38-07:00","id":1513226,"title":"","content":"There's also just the WoD's insistence on building NPCs with the PC rules here. Anything that's Green for the PCs is potentially frustrating in the hands of the ST no matter how well intentioned the ST is... esp. the stuff that's the top tier Green that should probably get nerfed.<br /> <br /> WtA, probably more than any of the other WoD games, desperately needed something closer to modern QC style NPCs. The sheer number of Gifts in the game (even just the unique count for W20 core alone) is huge, with many concepts repeated at multiple rank levels. Having multiple different Gifts that do the same thing but with different quirks and so on depending on Rank is great for PCs, but for NPCs it's a lot better to just have a single Intimidate power that can be scaled to the level of threat the NPC is supposed to be.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-29T14:03:56-07:00","id":1513230,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1513226#post1513226\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's also just the WoD's insistence on building NPCs with the PC rules here. Anything that's Green for the PCs is potentially frustrating in the hands of the ST no matter how well intentioned the ST is... esp. the stuff that's the top tier Green that should probably get nerfed.<br /> <br /> WtA, probably more than any of the other WoD games, desperately needed something closer to modern QC style NPCs. The sheer number of Gifts in the game (even just the unique count for W20 core alone) is huge, with many concepts repeated at multiple rank levels. Having multiple different Gifts that do the same thing but with different quirks and so on depending on Rank is great for PCs, but for NPCs it's a lot better to just have a single Intimidate power that can be scaled to the level of threat the NPC is supposed to be.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think DnD5e is a good system, but it very sensibly builds npcs with different rules than pcs. Neither the gameplay nor the setting will work well if you think of npcs as being too similar to pcs.<br /> <br /> Imagine if every single Ragabash of rank 2 and higher in the setting had Pulse of the Prey - the gameplay, story and setting will all suffer. A clever plan to hide from a BSD plan? Fails. You have a smart idea of how to track down someone hiding form the Sept? Tracky McGee has the Sept covered. You managed to give another pack the slip and send them down the wrong-<br /> <br /> You get the idea. You start to wonder how any character in the lore ever goes missing. At least any noteworthy character.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-30T01:14:06-07:00","id":1513273,"title":"","content":"I just started looking into the gifts recently, but there seem a lot of Gifts where you roll against a difficulty set by the stats (or is some cases a fixed difficulty) of you opponent and when you succeed, your opponent is heavily debuffend for a number of rounds, without giving your opponents an option to counter it.<br /> <br /> I really dislike this type of powers, they are pretty frustrating for the Players when used against them, and make combat pretty boring for them if they use them.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5199,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5199-aahz","user":"Aahz","date":". 05-30-2023, 04:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5199-aahz","name":"Aahz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-31T03:51:31-07:00","id":1513369,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 BONE GNAWER GIFTS</b></u>\u200b<br /> <br /> The Bone Gnawers get some nice utility and a slightly strange combat Gift here.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Between the Cracks:</b></u></span> I don't have too much to say here; being able to find abandoned and unused urban spaces is great, and this Gift facilitates Garou/Garou and Garou/Wyrm battles playing out on subway platforms, in abandoned alleyways and so on without Veil risk.<br /> <br /> The Willpower cost is entirely needless, and in fact given that the power is a little niche I wonder why a roll is even necessary. A Rank 2 Bone Gnawer is a character I trust to pass a difficulty 5 Wits+Streetwise.<br /> <br /> In terms of using this power offensively, you can surprise opponents by going WAY over the top with an ambush. Generally speaking while BSDs can be very mad, they know that Garou need to respect the Veil and will be careful about things like planting dangerous traps, preparing Crinos ambushes and so on, if the location could be stumbled upon by innocent bystanders or potential witnesses. A BSD who was expecting to chase you into some obviously discreet location instead gets caught out in a place which looks unsecured but the Gift has promised will likely remain abandoned.<br /> <br /> I don't have much more to say about this Gift really - it does its job and it has its uses, but there isn't that much to overthink. I could question the utility of more successes making the area you find EVEN MORE secluded and hard to locate - that isn't necessarily desirable. Knowing an alleyway in a busy part of the city will happen to stay empty tonight is really handy; finding my way to an abandoned sub-basement which hasn't been touched for decades isn't as helpful, especially since I could have taken one look at that location and gone &quot;yes, this is well hidden&quot;. In that case the utility would be in the power drawing you to that location, which does have its uses of course. In all likelihood the ST will let intent drive the power somewhat.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Blissful Ignorance:</b></u></span> See the Ragabash entry. I'm glad that Ragabash Gifts pop up in this list, as it serves as a reminder to new STs and players that this Tribe isn't just a bunch of Ragabash.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Cornered Rat's Ferocity:</b></u></span> Spend a Rage, roll Rage (difficulty 8) and get +1 die to Brawl pools for the remainder of a fight.<br /> <br /> Analyzing this on its own, it's underwhelming. You pay a resource you only have 1-5 points of, most likely. Rolling against a difficulty of 8 means you can expect to botch, fail or get 1-2 successes with regularity. Taking this on its own it would be Amber.<br /> <br /> The Gift also, unfortunately, sends you into an automatic Berserk Frenzy, and ending that frenzy ends the Gift's effect. Frenzying has niche benefits, you ignore wounds and you don't need good Wits to do bonus actions, you automatically go Crinos or Hispo, you're harder to control with a bunch of Gifts. You also avoid any risk of entering Thrall, which could be relevant if your Rage is very high. Strangely the Gift also doesn't require you to be &quot;cornered&quot; per se - it can be used in any fight.<br /> <br /> Personally I would significantly strengthen the Gift (adding your Rage rating to your Brawl pools etc.) but make it conditional upon a failed escape attempt in which the ST considers you to be 'cornered', literally or figuratively. Frenzying is flavourful and makes sense for the Gift, but the Gift needs to be worth it. Another way of doing it would be making the difficulty of the Rage check simply be... the difficulty Rage checks usually are. That is, base it on the moon, adjust by Auspice if necessary, adjust by Crinos if necessary.<br /> <br /> Given the high difficulty and all the weirdness of this Gift I consider it one to avoid. The best possible scenario is rolling 5 successes and getting +5 brawl in a fight (0.243% chance of this happening btw), but the odds are very slim and being out of control isn't worth it. Given how much I love getting bonus dice to combat rolls, it has to be VERY bad for me to not want to take it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Guise of the Hound:</b></u></span> Given that this has no cost, and the roll is pretty achievable (though it wasn't play-tested it seems; Manipulation is heavily debuffed in Lupus, and I don't think the writers kept that in mind at all), you'd be fine just giving this a duration of a day, or even making it a passive effect you can repress.<br /> <br /> Making it truly permanent, and something you can't repress, would actually give another reason to look down on the Bone Gnawers - having permanently tainted one of their forms out of fear of humans, at least in the view of more Traditional Tribes.<br /> <br /> Anyways, apart from people still feeling your Rage, you can be a big shaggy lovable dog with this power. Combine with Beast Speech to speak to dogs in the park about their owners, combine with King of Beasts to lead a pack of feral dogs, hell you can even attack people in this form (with Agg damage if you aren't Lupus, and most BGs aren't) without risking the Veil, though of course it will still cause a big stir!<br /> <br /> If you want to reach even more inaccessible places in this form, wear a yellow vest intended for guide dogs. Now you can't legally be kept out of most locations, Rage and The Curse notwithstanding.<br /> <br /> The RP potential and utility of this Gift are excellent, I just wish it were level 1 as it would help to give the Tribe an immediate reinforcement to its identity as the 'dogs' of the nation.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Odious Aroma:</b></u></span> Interestingly, if you smell amazing, it's possible that this Gift will still work as intended, you'll simply overwhelm others with the intensity of your scent.<br /> <br /> Anyways, this is pretty great; it's a solid short range debuff which requires no roll, and -2 dice to everything is pretty great; running away, using Gifts, rolling Rage to remain active, attacking, dodging... in crossover a Mage would be completely debilitated by this, unless the ST kindly ruled that your Arete is one of those special exceptions; for instance this Gift is unlikely to effect Soak pools.<br /> <br /> Regardless of precise interpretation this one is great, and your packmates not being effected is very handy. Solid Green, only to be avoided or used carefully if you are very frail or focus on ranged attacks etc. If you're a high Rage PC keep in mind that you should trigger this before combat if at all possible, since it will stop you from taking bonus actions, though of course using this power could give away your location before a fight starts (unless you have Scent of Running Water!).<br /> <br /> =================================<br /> <br /> Odious Aroma is worth taking for any melee character, but of course it comes with its own distinct flavour which makes it feel very unsuitable to many Garou melee experts; at the end of the day you'll find a different combat power which is a good use of your Gnosis, ideally in-list.<br /> <br /> Guise of the Hound is also amazing on anyone, but you'll run into a similar issue - Glass Walkers and perhaps Children of Gaia might appreciate the ability to look like dogs, as it helps navigate human locations peacefully, and there's always scope for members of other Tribes to want it, but it will come with some insults if you're surrounded by typical Garou.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-07-2023, 05:04 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-31T04:46:01-07:00","id":1513373,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 CHILDREN OF GAIA GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Children of Gaia continue to have a lot of Gifts themed around peace, as you'd expect, with some shining better than others.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Calm:</b></u></span> This one is tricky. A Gnosis cost in a Gift which might be meant to be used in Combat is bad, of course, but often such Gifts have a powerful effect. Here you roll Cha+Emp difficulty of the target's WP, and for each success you remove a point of Rage.<br /> <br /> If you have an ally who has been overloaded with Rage by a spiritual location, a Gift, some infuriating situation, a charm or power etc. then quickly removing some Rage is handy. It's very niche, but handy. The thing is that they could easily burn off that Rage themselves by using it for extra actions and shifting; even in the worst case scenario of a maximum Rage score of 1, they could burn it at a rate of 1 point per turn.<br /> <br /> In a fight, if you can rob an enemy Garou of all or most of their Rage you will have severely handicapped them. However dangerous enemies will often have decent WP pools, and you can't depend on them all having ratings of 4-6 or so. You also have to weigh it against the utility of simply destroying them with 1-4 claw attacks.<br /> <br /> If you are in a life or death fight and you want to end it by showing that you are far more powerful, and they can't possibly win, starting by robbing them of all their Rage is a good way to go down that path.<br /> <br /> Finally you can end non-Rage related Frenzies if you get 3+ successes. Which is nice and all, but as a Garou I'm more interested in Rage-based Frenzies; I'm guessing this Gift can't handle those because it would step on the toes of other Gifts, but it's annoying all the same.<br /> <br /> My CoG has a Charisma + Empathy of 6, loves ending fights without killing, and even then I'm looking at this Gift and wondering if it's worth it for 6XP. Perhaps eventually, but it's way down the list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Grandmother's Touch:</b></u></span> Mother's Touch, but you can target yourself. Instantly incredible. My boy has a Gnosis of 5, a Rage of 5, and a healing pool of 5 dice; his Intelligence is stuck at 1 because it's key to his concept that he be relatively unintelligent. Even with that limitation the Gift will be the first thing I grab. 5 dice, difficulty 5, healing 1 agg per success, and you can do it up to 5 times? Completely changes things, even if you're in a party with a Theurge like he is. Might be selfish, but I'm actually kinda glad it's limited to the CoG as well.<br /> <br /> A teacher isn't specified, but you can infer that the teacher is the same as Mother's Touch.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Luna's Armour:</b></u></span> Expect some ranting when we get to Troll Skin and Steel Fur.<br /> <br /> Lack of balance aside, this is a great Gift, and makes the Tribes that get it (CoG, Shadow Lords, Silver fangs) all the better.<br /> <br /> - only difficulty 6<br /> - Stamina + Survival is a great pool to roll, especially in Crinos<br /> - +1 dice to soak per success<br /> - the bonus dice soak silver<br /> - no reason it can't stack with armour<br /> - cool flavour (though it risks the veil)<br /> <br /> The main downside is that spending 1 Gnosis means you can't attack the same turn. At the same time this Gift is a potent boon and you can throw it out the moment there's any kind of pause in a battle, or when the fight is approaching, or before entering the fight's location, or before ambushing (though the light could give you away!), or when you need to take a moment to regenerate, or while kiting... you get the idea. Getting a dice pool of 10 on this Gift, in Crinos, is perfectly achievable and will serve you in good stead in any fight.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Para Bellum:</b></u></span> When your enemy shoots first you can spend a point of Rage, and as a result you'll have +1 Str and +1 Dex when you attack that specific enemy, or an enemy who has harmed one of your packmates (presumably not including you). This one has a lot of caveats which makes it a bit niche; if you ambush, if you pre-empt an attack you see coming, if the person who fires first is a minor threat, if you need to dodge or play defensive, this won't do much.<br /> <br /> Overall +1 die is +0.7 successes to most combat checks, and that will pay dividends in the long run. It not costing Gnosis is also good. However it costing Rage is actually annoying; is this Gift worth missing out on a whole extra attack, which could finish off a powerful enemy or take out a foe outright?<br /> <br /> Personally if I had written this power I'd make it entirely passive with no cost; a boon which kicks in whenever you are in a fight your side didn't start. Still, it's not awful, just not one I'd pick up.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Unicorn's Arsenal:</b></u></span> Much like Luna's Armour and Razor Claws, this is a prep power. Find out from your ST whether you can do more than one of these at once, or whether you need a turn dedicated to each of them, ideally before purchasing more than one.<br /> <br /> The cost of a Gnosis is a little annoying, but the resulting debuff is decent; any character suffering a wound inflicted by your claws or fangs has -2 dice from all attack rolls. Notably this means Brawl, Firearms, Melee etc. checks and not the Damage roll that follows.<br /> <br /> You might struggle to land an attack, you might not deal damage, they might heal or even regenerate it in rare cases, you might just kill them outright... this power will only stick around if the target takes damage from you and then has subsequent turns. Generally speaking your aim should be to focus down a dangerous target ASAP, and the problem here is that the power will only matter if the foe keeps going after you land a strike. Bad luck exists, dangerous foes exist, but it's still a slightly niche situation. At the same time, the best case scenario could save you a lot of pain; -2 dice to 3 claw attacks could save your life.<br /> <br /> I'd call this an Amber; how useful you find it will depend on what calls you have on your Gnosis and whether you have other preparation based Gifts or not.<br /> <br /> ==============================================<br /> <br /> Despite a few average or mixed Gifts, the CoG still stay very strong here IMO; two amazing Gifts for 12XP is better than 5 above average Gifts for 30XP after all. And it's important not to neglect your Attributes + Abilities, as they are what your Gifts often depend upon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 05-06-2025, 07:57 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-31T09:45:55-07:00","id":1513412,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 FIANNA GIFTS</b></u>\u200b<br /> <br /> Fianna Gifts are a bit of an odd mix at this level, but the flavour remains pretty good. Notably the alphabetical order gets messed up here. I don't know why, and I don't know if it is interesting to anyone other than me, but it's a thing.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Glib Tongue:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis, roll a Wits + Expression against a difficulty set by their Wits + Alertness, and they'll agree with any lie no matter how absurd...<br /> <br /> For one turn per success.<br /> <br /> This Gift heavily relies on your ST to have longer turns in social scenarios, and even then, this Gift is quite odd. Often a power will have a chart for durations, especially in C20 and M20; one turn, three turns, a scene, a day etc. instead even if you ace this, it will last as little as 15 seconds with 5 successes, or perhaps as long as 5 minutes.<br /> <br /> Chat with your ST about the flow of time at your table.<br /> <br /> Assuming you're able to get it to last a sensible and useful period of time, convincing someone that you're the ruler of a country, a government official, authorized to be present, that you in fact own the sacred Klaive you just picked up, that you're the one true King of the Garou... it's all very helpful. Until it stops working. This Gift is, unfortunately, not as good as simply being a really good liar. Yes what you can convince people of, even with Persuasion, is limited to the realm of sanity... but lies that are limited to the realm of sanity are better and more useful, and tend to produce more reliable results. They just rely on more thought.<br /> <br /> If your ST allows the following lies, they could be quite effective:<br /> <br /> Stabbing yourself in the abdomen will kill our whole pack<br /> You need to surrender<br /> We'll win this fight no matter what<br /> What I'm holding is an antidote to a deadly poison you drank earlier<br /> We're your allies, this fight was a misunderstanding<br /> That bird is our totem, killing it will weaken us<br /> <br /> I can't see any reason they wouldn't, but the ST could argue that how the target chooses to respond to the lie is down to their personality or tactics.<br /> <br /> Based on the assumption that time will be kind to you here, and work out reasonably, this is amber. In all honesty they should have said &quot;one minute per success&quot; or given a table.<br /> <br /> It's also worth noting that the power is poorly written; the target &quot;hears what you want them to&quot; and &quot;agrees wholeheartedly&quot; - what does that mean? It's possible that the intent of this power is that you can say &quot;we should all jump off that cliff&quot; and make your target go &quot;yes, absolutely, great idea&quot; - which is them agreeing with what you said. The text of the power doesn't explicitly guarantee that the target will jump off a cliff, and in all honesty it would be a little silly if it did.<br /> <br /> I almost want to give this one a red out of spite, but we'll go with amber. I feel like there are themes in quality with some of these Tribal gifts, like they were all copy+pasted from the same handful of Revised books or something.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Flame Dance:</b></u></span> This is great; you can attack once per round and save the rest of your Rage for dodging EXACTLY as often as you need to, never wasting a single point of Rage or a single action. The ability to enhance a dodge by +3 dice is neat, and worth using if you ever have a 7 agg plasma bolt flying at your eye or a 10 Rage spirit crashing down upon you with an attack.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Form Mastery:</b></u></span> A Metis Gift? In my perfect Fianna list? See the Metis entry.<br /> <br /> <i><span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Howl of the Banshee*:</b></u></span></i> I know that the Gift is a terrifying scream, but I just hate the idea of another PC using this, me failing, and being forced to flee in terror. It feels like a very un-fun interaction.<br /> <br /> Anyways, this Gift is another fear effect; this one hits everyone who hears the Banshee's scream. Given that a Garou's howl can reach for miles... this could cause a &quot;mass hallucination&quot; or incite city-wide panic. The cost and roll work fine, having two difficulties for the WP roll to resist is fine, and the Gift is STRONG don't get me wrong, I just don't like the design or setting implications. Green on strength, red on design. And keep in mind that all enemies, even ones you can't see yet, will be hit... yeah.<br /> <br /> My best 'fix' for this would be for the Gift to be bigged up as a very dangerous nuclear option, the Fostern who learns it is taught by the spirit that it is very dangerous for a banshee's yell to be in a wolf's howl, that if they aren't careful they could destroy things they cannot see, inflict death and terror etc. and even have an NPC say that they should think about their volume, who is present, will they tolerate the yell - but honestly your powers shouldn't have their balance and general implications for setting integrity determined by the sanity of everyone who has them!<br /> <br /> *This power's Green rating is dependent on it being kept as is, which I don't think is a particularly sane choice given that a 10 mile radius mass panic is... an interesting level 2 power. The fact that it can ruin the fun of other PCs also means you may see it being discouraged or banned, at which point it's functionally red. I consider this to be a worse version of what Jam Weapon has going on.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Howl of the Unseen:</b></u></span> A really solid utility power which allows communication across the veil, and usage of sound-based powers too. You can haunt someone, spy for your allies on spirits and feed back to them, follow an ally and silently guide them from the umbra, or talk to someone about what the real world looks like while they're on the other side.<br /> <br /> The applications are very broad, and it's a great piece of utility; I just wonder if I'd feel delighted to spend 6xp on it? It feels like a quality of life improvement more than anything.<br /> <br /> I really enjoy that it doesn't have a cost, and that it's a plain old roll, which is handy. This is a solid amber, and definitely a great investment for Theurges. If you're very into spirit stuff or playing around the Gauntlet with a split party frequently comes up, this could be a green.<br /> <br /> ====================================<br /> <br /> If Howl is kept RAW, it's a great take for anyone with a high Charisma + Intimidation and Gnosis to spare. Form Mastery is great as ever, and you might have an easier time ingratiating yourself with the Fianna and their wolf spirits than with those tied to Metis, if you find such a distinction matters to spirits. Flame Dance is an excellent addition to any character with more than 2 or 3 points of Rage to spend, and worth taking on any fighter (or survivor) really.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-07-2023, 05:05 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-31T14:43:10-07:00","id":1513455,"title":"","content":"It's really a shame that the Gnawers get really bad mechanics for two really on-brand Gifts in Desperate Strength and Cornered Rat's Ferocity.<br /> <br /> Blissful Ignorance used to be a bit of a higher tier Gift in the hands of the Gnawers, but in conjunction with a Gift that didn't make the W20 cut: On Patrol. If your ST lets you take On Patrol, the ability to be safe from attention for the duration of activating that Gift is a great combo.<br /> <br /> Howl of the Banshee's range and friendly-fire issues have definitely been problems for my groups. One time a player used it thinking they were debuffing an attacking Wyrm force to help defend our Sept. Not only did dozens of NPCs suddenly have to make a save against it, only a couple of low ranked Fomori fail, while the dice just hated our side... not enough we lost the battle, but instead of a well planned defense that would have meant minimal loses, it was a bloody mess with multiple dead PCs and NPCs. I really dislike designs were &quot;this is really powerful, but also potentially really dangerous, and thus can be low level.&quot; They're either way too powerful,or way to dangerous.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-31T14:50:05-07:00","id":1513457,"title":"","content":"What's odd is that the gift's flavour text doesn't even take any notice of the huge risk to the Veil and to your allies. I'm not surprised that it was a bloodbath - even if 30% of the attackers panic and 10% of the defense panic, a disorganized defense can massively swing a battle.<br /> <br /> Part of the idea with Gifts having levels is that you need to be very responsible and proven to access the most potent Gifts - instead you have psychological nukes like this which could kill 100s of people if used in high density areas, or in crowds.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-01T04:53:41-07:00","id":1513521,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 GET OF FENRIS GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> Get of Fenris Gifts improve at this level - they start 'coming online' as a better combat Tribe at Rank 2.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Fangs of the North:</b></u></span> This is a preparation power, and it combines well with Razor Claws given enough time to prepare. This won't be all fights, but many of them. You inflict a -1 penalty onto those you have injured with your attacks (a penalty to everything, notably, including attempts to dodge, block, run away etc.), which is a small debuff but applies to everything. With less chance of dodging, landing attacks or harming you a dangerous enemy is a tiny bit more manageable. The power also blocks all regeneration and healing, which is great, but keep in mind that your claws deal Aggravated damage; very few enemies will be regenerating or healing that.<br /> <br /> The ability to send a foe away with a festering frigid wound which won't heal is neat, but generally speaking you'll be aiming to finish the job within a given scene.<br /> <br /> Given that neither the -1 die penalty and the no-healing rule will have a HUGE impact, and a Gnosis cost is relatively tricky to work around, this is a solid amber which is well worth taking if it's on your list. I couldn't imagine paying a premium for it though. If you will frequently be facing enemies who regenerate or heal Aggravated damage (which some STs might have a habit of doing) then this would be Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Halt the Coward's Flight:</b></u></span> No cost, a combination of two worthwhile stats (Charisma and Intimidation), and the target difficulty is WP. Given that you could try again on the next turn, even a difficulty of 9 or 10 is manageable. Being able to half the speed of a fleeing foe is pretty neat; half is not zero, so this foe could still pull some clever escape, but it will severely hamper retreats.<br /> <br /> It's worth clarifying with your ST whether &quot;Exit (NPC)&quot; is more of a stage direction, or more of a challenge; if their intent is simply &quot;yes, they get away&quot; then intervening with the intent of fully stopping it is only going to lead to frustration. I wouldn't consider this ideal Storytelling, especially if the ST isn't transparent; if a character is simply 'meant to get away' (something you want to do very sparingly) then the right thing is to be up front about it. Anyways, assuming the fleeing NPC is more of a workable challenge with stakes, being able to half their speed will greatly assist you in hunting them down for capture or killing.<br /> <br /> This power is useful on hunts, in large scale battles (when applied to a leader it will either split the force or create other difficulties as they all adjust pace and try to help their hampered ally), against Garou packs and Fomori packs, though notably not in races.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Snarl of the Predator:</b></u></span> No cost, a very good difficulty band (4-8), a sensible combo of Charisma + Intimidation, and the duration is 3 turns - it may take &quot;a full turn&quot; to execute, but it's a great power. Hitting a target with -2, -3, even -4 and beyond for 3 turns is great.<br /> <br /> It's worth noting that you can use this outside of combat, in any kind of confrontation really, so long as snarling at them like a wolf is acceptable and won't risk the Veil. Ideally you'll get this off before combat starts, in an attempt to scare them into not fighting. Given that Garou can take anywhere between 1 and 4, or even 5+ actions in a single round, a -3 dice penalty is huge as it applies many times in a single round, and this lasts three rounds!<br /> <br /> Besides remembering you can use this outside of combat, there aren't many ingenious uses. a Charisma or Intimidation spec which will proc for this Gift could help, and ideally you'll have a Merit or Gift which helps reduce the difficulty... at your table. Different STs will vary on what applies. Worth grabbing even for 10XP!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Troll Skin:</b></u></span> Very frustrating, and pretty clear evidence of copy+pasting without giving much thought to balance or standardizing.<br /> <br /> It's very similar to Luna's Armour, but it comes with a +1 difficulty, a small social downside, the flavour is much 'uglier' (though it suits a martial character well) and it doesn't work on silver.<br /> <br /> Rolling Primal-Urge is good in the sense that you're encouraged to upgrade a very useful ability.<br /> <br /> There isn't too much to say here; use it the way you use Luna's, but honestly I even wonder whether getting Luna's is a better idea; it's only a difference of 4XP, and if you're going to be fighting a lot of battles a difference of 1 difficulty and silver soak could be crucial.<br /> <br /> If you're going to adjust Troll Skin, I'd suggest reducing the difficulty to activate it by 1, and making it provide some other benefit to make it unique; for instance halving Bashing damage after soak, or reducing the difficulty to soak Bashing, or fire, or electricity, or something!<br /> <br /> EDIT: As per the final version of the PDF, Troll Skin is a difficulty 5 check; this is a boring upgrade, but it's good. As a result <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Troll Skin</b></u></span> would be Green.<br /> <br /> ================================================== =====<br /> <br /> Not too much to say about these, besides Snarl being a much better way of handling &quot;enemies are spooked!&quot; than Gifts like Howl of the Banshee, and even True Terror - I like True Terror as it only prevents attacks, not paralyzes foes or forcing them to flee, but it's still not as well designed as powers that focus on debuffs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-01-2025, 08:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-01T05:27:00-07:00","id":1513522,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 GLASS WALKER GIFTS</b></u>\u200b<br /> <br /> Glass Walker Gifts continue to be weird, in a good way.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Cybersenses:</b></u></span> Heat vision. Xray vision. Echolocation. Radar hearing. Picking up ultrasonic noises. Seeing UV light, such as those reflected by urine and plants. Scenting chemicals. Run a PCR with your taste. RAW this power lets you pack the effect of any diagnostic, investigative or perceptive tool into one of your five senses. Make your sense of touch become an accurate Seismometer. Make your sense of temperature upon touch entirely accurate. Measure altitude. Sense magnetism and become a compass.<br /> <br /> You should chat through the exact limitations of this power with your ST, for instance you might not be able to do senses which are higher order processes. For instance determining the age of something with a machine, or sensing the presence of a particular kind of viral DNA, are both multi-step processes with various reagents and so on; does this mean the sense is very slow, or that you can't do it? Ask! Similar story with trying to get a CT or MRI or Ultrasound using your senses, as those rely on emitting something, and also can involve a lot of processing of the information that returns in a computer to get a useful image.<br /> <br /> A solid Green, so long as you have any creativity and real-world scientific know-how. This also pairs VERY well with high Perception, high Science, high Technology, high Medicine and so on. The more cybernetic senses you know about, the more this can do!<br /> <br /> Just make sure you spend your Gnosis wisely here; if you go too wild it could become a sink for a precious resource, and of course nothing lasts longer than one scene...<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Hands Full of Thunder:</b></u></span> Unlimited ammo; on a gun which can fire as fast as you can load it, this is a potent Gift indeed. Depending on your equipment this could be a QOL upgrade or a serious buff to damage potential. Make sure to have, or even make, a gun which is ideal for this Gift. I'm gonna call it amber, but with the right weapon this could be a green.<br /> <br /> It's unclear whether the bullets being fired are 'real' or ephemeral. If they're real then you're turning 1 point of Gnosis into a potentially unlimited amount of metal, which is interesting. My best guess is that the bullets fired with this Gift are like 'ghost bullets'. Grabbing an unloaded gun and using this Gift is also quite fun.<br /> <br /> Combining with a special gun, such as a Fetish or even a Wonder, would make this all the better. It's unclear whether the special properties of the bullets in the weapon would translate over; my best guess is no. So unfortunately you can't reuse a single Talen bullet 1000 times, as cool as that would be. The big problem with guns is Lethal damage, but against mortals and many other enemies the difference between Lethal and Aggravated is entirely moot regardless.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Jam Technology:</b></u></span> See the Homid entry. Good for Metis and Lupus members of the Tribe.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Power Surge:</b></u></span> Another hard to control Gift! You spend a Gnosis, roll Wits + Science, and it seems that luck determines how big an area you black out. The possibility you could overshoot is a fun idea, but it also means that this Gift arguably becomes less useful with a really good dice pool.<br /> <br /> Personally I would adjust this so that your difficulty is based on the area you're trying to shut down, and 1 success means you undershoot a bit, 2 successes means you overshoot a bit, and 3 successes you're bang on target. Imprecision is cool, but RAW the Gift is just bound to cause widespread chaos and can't form a reliable part of any plan.<br /> <br /> &quot;Ah I might overshoot and shut off power to something we do need, too risky.&quot;<br /> <br /> There's also the fact that the Gift doesn't really qualify what is 'blacked out'. Just the electricity GRID, or all electrical currents? A power cut is handy, it'll interfere with a lot of security measures, but it would be good to know whether this Gift messes with laptops, earpieces, phones, robots, pacemakers and so on. I'll assume it only impacts the grid, but still.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#e74c3c\"><u><b>Steel Fur:</b></u></span> I desperately wish this one were better. Immediately it has a leg up on Troll's Skin and Luna's Armour; it costs WP, not Gnosis, so you can use it and then do a bunch of Rage attacks (or dodges) on the same turn. I am also delighted by rolling Stamina + Science, I love that. What's unfortunate is that it shoots itself in the foot with a +1 difficulty to Dexterity, and being restricted to certain forms isn't great either (though it does make sense, and is hardly an issue on its own).<br /> <br /> If you want to fix this Gift, make it difficulty 6 and change the downside; instead its weakness is that any attack which bypasses your fur (targeted attacks of the inside of your mouth, your eyes, a spike being shot up your snout etc.) ignore the bonus armour. Imagine if Luna's Armour made you have +2 difficulty to visual Perception checks because the light was blinding you... it's spirit magic, why should the metal fur make you less flexible?<br /> <br /> As much as I like this one, it has enough problems stuck to it that it gets red. +1 difficulty to all your attacks and dodges is a cardinal sin for a Gift.<br /> <br /> ================================================<br /> <br /> Despite some bad design, I continue to really enjoy GW gifts. Cybersenses is a great standout, and I'd argue that it's worth grabbing on any Knowledge-focused PC, especially if they have a strong understanding of the various Sciences and the associated Knowledges.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-07-2023, 05:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-01T10:57:22-07:00","id":1513545,"title":"","content":"Troll Skin is already -1 difficulty compared to Luna's Armor. Which is a decent boost where every success counts. The social penalty is also pretty minor for Garou about to get in a fight. Something I've had success with to give it some extra oomph and keep it distinct from Luna's Armor (aka the soaking silver part): increase natural regeneration by 1HL/turn, or heal 1 extra HL whenever receiving healing. This means whatever damage you don't soak, damage you (or your Theurge) can clear from you that much faster; as well as offsetting the potential issues for high Rage combat characters trying to get aid from Mother's Touch.<br /> <br /> A small nitpick but I'm really not sure how Cybersense would let you turn your sense of taste into a PCR. PCR machines aren't sensory: they rapidly and artificially replicate an existing DNA sample, you need to run them through a separate apparatus to actually make use of that. While I agree with the overall take on the Gift, I think it's pretty clear anything that's not a sensor in some fashion is off the table. Giving yourself UV sensing is fine, but you still need some luminol/etc. to spot things that don't naturally give off UV radiation to go full CSI on something.<br /> <br /> I also think you're overlooking how powerful Hands Full of Thunder can be with a simple thing: silver bullets. One of the big problems for Garou carrying silver weapons, is that it negatively impacts their Gnosis. Having a lot of silver ammo is really rough on that front. Having a single clip of it last a whole fight is utterly brutal to foes weak to silver (like BSDs) with the small impact on your Gnosis as a klaive would have. The Gift also explicitly doesn't work with an gun with no pre-existing ammo. It doesn't create &quot;ghost bullets.&quot; It clearly has to copy existing ones. So recovering all that silver bullet machine gun fire you can toss around could also be a fun payday for a kin cleaning up the mess.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-01T11:38:38-07:00","id":1513553,"title":"","content":"Troll Skin is difficulty 7 in my book, and Luna's Armour is difficulty 6 - are you looking at W20? Or maybe it's a PDF thing?<br /> <br /> As for the PCR example, it's worth keeping in mind that radar involves transmitting electromagnetic waves and then measuring what bounces back; so one of the given examples in the book is already more complicated than a purely sensory effect! PCR is a complicated process and it does involve replication, but Xrays work by exposing a sheet of material to the electromagnetic waves that make it through an object; there's a &quot;higher order process&quot; to most kinds of cybernetic sense! You could argue that if a machine can sense something, even if it involves a complicated process, you can make one of your senses do it with this Gift, even if the process involves stuff you could label as &quot;not sensory&quot;. But like I say, discuss it with your ST and see what they're down with. &quot;I can taste viruses b/c there are machines that can determine whether a sample has a viral gene in it&quot; might work at some tables, but not others.<br /> <br /> As for replicating silver bullets, the Gift simply says it won't &quot;run out of ammo, so long as it had bullets to begin with&quot; - you could interpret that as it spewing out silver rounds until the cows come home, or you could interpret that as it producing regular statted shots for what the weapon normally does. I can see why someone would assume it would keep firing copies of the first thing fired, but given the potential for the Gift to fire 100s of copies of a single Talen bullet, that's gonna be a conversation to have at your table. Even if the silver example does work, it's worth noting that the only enemies you'll fight with a weakness to silver are Garou and Fera - common enough to be good, but not common enough to be a Green unless your campaign is heavily themed around fighting Garou or other Luna-touched werecreatures. Still, it's made me consider an angle to the Gift I hadn't, so I appreciate it!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-01T13:19:09-07:00","id":1513565,"title":"","content":"I'm using my W20 pdf, yeah, which has Troll Skin at Diff 5. I distinctly remember this getting changed because we complained at the W20s devs during the Kickstarter feedback cycle for keeping the old diff of 7 despite giving you less than Luna's Armor did.<br /> <br /> On PCR: the problem is that PCR isn't an active sensor like radar. All PCR does is take a sample and copy it a lot. Like my point about UV and luminol, PCR &quot;senses&quot; wouldn't let you sense anything, because PCR doesn't make DNA fragments &quot;visible&quot; through some process. It just makes more of those DNA fragments so techniques that do visualize DNA have more to work with. If you said your character could have &quot;electrophoresis&quot; sense where they could perfectly weigh different DNA fragment samples to mentally create comparative weights and ID matching samples... OK, I can see that. But then you're actually measuring something to sense. PCR doesn't measure anything. Letting Cybersenses work with machines that don't measure anything means they're not &quot;senses.&quot; Running a PCR with your tongue is like firing off sound wave, but never measuring the returns to act as sonar/echolocation. You're creating data but not collecting it. And you're clearly moving beyond the spirit - if not letter - of the Gift if you let a character perform functions that aren't part of a measuring apparatus: the ability to use active pinging microwave sensor-sense shouldn't let you cook food by bombarding it with microwaves. Letting the Gift perform a PCR is like letting it cook food.<br /> <br /> On Hands Full of Thunder: &quot;Unlimited Talen bullets,&quot; is a bit overblown as something to worry about. You'd still need to activate each one, which is a Gnosis action, with all the complicates that would add to a gun-focused character. Talens aren't hard to make. A gun-focused Glass Walker can easily come in with a revolver full of Talen bullets, and probably not get a chance to use them all in one fight thanks to the whole constant activation issue, HFoT and &quot;unlimited&quot; Talens would only really matter for extremely long fights, or multiple fights back to back with little rest (it only takes 10 minutes to make a few Talen bullets).<br /> <br /> Also, frankly, I think people underestimate lethal damage in WtA because Garou can regen it... but the regen rates for it aren't that bad, and soaking it is the same as soaking non-silver Agg. Even if your ST disallows the Gift from copying specialty ammo like silver, the damage output and range of guns with one of the biggest limits of ammunition taken off the table is a huge deal .","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-01T13:41:38-07:00","id":1513566,"title":"","content":"The knowledge that the version of the book I'm using may be out of date fills me with dread... I must reflect on this...<br /> <br /> Great point about Lethal damage though - I also feel Bashing damage winds up a bit underrated. To many supernatural creatures Bashing is basically the same as Lethal for most practical concerns; a spirit or Fomori will likely soak both, will regen neither or both, and whether they take 24 hours or 1 to heal it won't matter much if you're aiming to kill them in the next minute.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-02T03:51:01-07:00","id":1513626,"title":"","content":"I have managed to get a text comparison of the two versions using an amazing online tool! Just fed in the two PDFs (the out of date and up to date versions) and compared text.<br /> <br /> - Ancestors is buffed considerably (the pool is buffed for a scene, not one roll)<br /> - There's a brand new 6th level Theurge Gift<br /> - Spending Willpower to activate a Gift is AGAINST THE RULES! Massive shift in the utility of some high difficulty Gifts!<br /> - Garou prior to the First Change smell like Kinfolk to Scent of the True Form, which is a GREAT lore detail<br /> - Curse of Aeolus covers &quot;roughly one city block&quot;, which means it's much more useful than previously thought for utility, though still meh in a typical fight scene<br /> - Troll Skin has its difficulty reduced to 5<br /> - Grand Klaives have their damage upped from STR+3 to STR+5<br /> - Jarlhammer deals STR+6 Aggravated<br /> - Performing a vile act in the Thrall of the Wyrm loses you Renown, rather than gaining it (though I think most people already read this as a typo)<br /> - Multiple Actions are changed to be like the other 20th books (splitting pools rather than going -1, -2, -4, -7 etc. dice)<br /> - Fine tuning of how defensive actions work<br /> - CLAW ATTACKS DEAL STRENGTH + 2 DAMAGE YESSSSSSSSS<br /> - The Rite of the Silver Forge is fixed so that you need +2 successes to Frenzy<br /> - Some of the errors in the Example Of Play section are removed<br /> - Calm Heart is fixed so you roll for Frenzy at +2 difficulty... though that is ALL Rage rolls. Problem with this is that it makes Remaining Active and other important Rage rolls harder too; &quot;+1 successes needed to Frenzy&quot; would have been so much more elegant!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-02T04:29:42-07:00","id":1513629,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 RED TALON GIFTS</b></u>\u200b<br /> <br /> Red Talons get a standout here.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Beastmind:</b></u></span> No cost, a pretty simple roll, and you can reduce a target to animal-like intelligence. As much as a lawyer in a trial having a &quot;sudden mental breakdown&quot; could risk the Veil, it's a very potent disabling tool which can be greatly lengthened against regular humans by spending WP. The only problem with this Gift is that it's stuck onto the Red Talons. I will not be elaborating on that, you already know what I mean.<br /> <br /> This will have limited utility in a fight against a claw or fang user, but might be useful against sword wielders (debatable) and will definitely be useful against gun users. Most types of Mages will be left powerless by this Gift, in crossover land, and that's pretty handy. Though watch out for the animistic shaman who has a Seeking accidentally triggered by the bestial clarity of this Gift and proceeds to turn you all into fleeing deer /j<br /> <br /> RAW there's no reason you can't use this on a Spirit, or any number of supernatural beings. In a complex fight filled with traps, this is great. When language and communication are key, this is great. Making the Alpha of a BSD pack (who you can pick out remember - Red Talons have a Gift that lets you identify the leader of a group) go feral and lose the ability to give orders could sway things in your favour, as could making a Pentex First Team lose all coordination and fight like apes. Basically this Gift lets you drag enemies down to the level of a brute. Brute vs Brute is a matchup that you, as a Garou, will win quite frequently... and there is nothing which forces you to fight like a brute against a victim of this Gift.<br /> <br /> If you found this Gift problematic, a nerf which would suit the Tribe's vibe would be to make it so that the user also falls into the same bestial mindset if the Gift succeeds. I don't think it's necessary, but it's a neat idea that popped into my head.<br /> <br /> Try using this against enemies leading rites, driving vehicles... if someone is using a tool, you can sabotage the operator. The ability to silence people, even in a suspicious manner which draws attention, is great. This Gift has so many infinite uses it's almost hard to come up with examples. The RP potential is also pretty neat; a &quot;human&quot; (Kinfolk, Homid Garou, allied supernatural) who wishes to know more about nature might learn a lot from the experience. Plus giving a trophy hunter an animalistic mind and hunting him like he's prey for a whole day might turn his life around.<br /> <br /> Oh... and there's nothing stopping you from using this every turn until all your foes are running around like wild animals. Just saying.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Pulse of the Prey:</b></u></span> See the Ragabash entry.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Howls in the Night:</b></u></span> See the Galliard entry.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Shadows of the Impergium:</b></u></span> If you're regularly encountering regular humans (remember that supernaturals are all Delerium immune!) with WP 9 and 10... something weird is going on. Delirium is a double edged sword, but I'm gonna say that overall it's beneficial to you in a lot of ways. Making foes panic, lose coordination, flee, attack wildly, forget you were ever there... and this Gift grants it (if weaker) to Hispo, which may be your preferred fighting form, and also eliminates the risk of humans who react in clever ways when you go Crinos. The ability to repress this also lets you avoid collateral damage, and means the Hispo form doesn't get permanently altered in a way which removes utility.<br /> <br /> All in all this Gift has some handy utility, but it's more like a Quality of Life improvement than a good buff. Regular Delirium does the job fine against humans. Humans who have WP of 7+ and are not supernatural in some way (Bane, Kinfolk, psychic etc.) will be rare in most games. This might be handy if you're regularly being hunted by badass hunters and mercenaries and First Teams.<br /> <br /> If you prefer Crinos form to Hispo in fights, and you aren't going to be facing high-WP mundane human enemies, this becomes red.<br /> <br /> ================================================== ====<br /> <br /> Strong entry from the Red Talons. Beastmind would be worth 10xp on pretty much any character... though Griffin is noteworthy for being scornful of Homids especially, and to a lesser extent Metis, so a non-Lupus learning this is unlikely.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-07-2023, 05:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-03T09:45:01-07:00","id":1513785,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 SHADOW LORD GIFTS</b></u>\u200b<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><i><u><b>Clap of Thunder*:</b></u></i></span> Spend a Gnosis, all characters within 20 feet / 6m (a small range, but not tiny) must succeed in a WP roll (diff 8 for &quot;foes&quot;, 4 for &quot;packmates&quot;, no difficulty listed for any other category of person, so you could assume 6 or 8 for bystanders, non-pack allies etc.). The target must succeed or be entirely stunned for one turn.<br /> <br /> So they can't flee, or defend themselves, they're just stunned. And yes, you, the Shadow Lord, have used Gnosis and thus your whole action for the round on this... but your whole pack can utterly savage whatever individual or group you've stunned.<br /> <br /> The fact you make a loud thunderclap does risk the Veil, and it does draw attention from other enemies, so there are potential balancing factors written into the power (accidentally perhaps), however the Gift is still far too strong in most standard TTRPG &quot;encounters&quot;. I don't know why W20 is so afraid of enemies being debuffed in interesting ways, and has so many &quot;can't act&quot; powers.<br /> <br /> My fix for this would be that the target is only stunned for one round on a botch; otherwise they should suffer a difficulty spike to all their rolls for the round (+2 or 3 perhaps). RAW this one falls into &quot;so good it's bad&quot;.<br /> <br /> (Despite this being too good, it's also funny to note that if a target has a WP of 7 there's only a 30% chance they'll be stunned, and as low as about a 20% chance with WP 10, meanwhile if you have a pack of 4 allies standing around you, there's still like a 5-10% chance of each of them being stunned, so you might stun a packmate and not stun the dangerous boss enemy you wanted to, which is funny)<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Cold Voice of Reason:</b></u></span> This Gift is better designed, but it's insane to have two stunning Gifts in a single list, at the same level. You make a witty remark and a target can't attack you. Costs one Gnosis, you roll Man+Sub diff 8, and the target has no roll to resist, and no way of increasing the difficulty. However they can attack anyone else other than you, and they can make efforts to stop you escaping. You attacking them also breaks the spell. Buying yourself a bit of time where a character can't attack you is a neat little power, and I like it.<br /> <br /> You could use this after confronting a very powerful and angry spirit to stop it from attacking you; this fits very well into a Theurge's kit. Alternatively if you're a scout you could use this to stop yourself from being ganked by a single powerful enemy if caught. You could use this as a diplomat in negotiations to stop someone from striking you, so long as there's enough time for that person to then be restrained or stopped. The problem is that Garou work in packs; there will almost always be more than two people in a given scenario. Other Fomori. Other allies, around you, who can still be attacked. This Gift basically stops you, and you alone, from being attacked by one target. An option to protect your whole pack for a second point of Gnosis would be much appreciated in all honesty.<br /> <br /> All these ideas are nice of course, but I don't see why you'd buy this if you can get Clap of Thunder RAW!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Howls in the Night:</b></u></span> I don't know why they thought so many lists needed this Gift; costing a Vampire a night of sleep ain't that great! See the Galliard list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Luna's Armour:</b></u></span> Amazing, as always, but I'm biased so I wish it was a CoG exclusive. See the CoG entry.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Song of the Earth Mother:</b></u></span> This one has flavour that feels a bit more Elder Brother than Shadow Lord, but I'm guessing the writers were thinking along the lines of eastern europe, being tied to the soil and the land itself... which is a bit more Tzimisce-flavoured in the WoD. Anyways, we'll overlook the Gift feeling like it's in the wrong list.<br /> <br /> Takes 10 minutes, costs 2 Gnosis, so the result had better be good! Perception + Occult, difficulty 7, okay... and the result is that you know about supernatural activity within 100 yards of your location, per success. Even with 5 successes, 500 yards isn't that much. In the wilderness. And that's what's weird about this power; it shines most in tight, dense, urban areas. Which is where its flavour makes the least sense; why should the spirits of the earth know about a guy in the penthouse of your apartment building who is practicing magic?<br /> <br /> Essentially this is a slow, expensive Sense Magic which effects a larger area, but not large enough to be useful in the umbra or wilderness. The saving grace is that you can sit down in the middle of a village, or a town square, or a city block or park, and find wyrm tainted locations, along with other locations touched by magic. The problem is that Sense Wyrm already does a key aspect of what this Gift does, though this one will get around there being places you can't access, or don't have time to check.<br /> <br /> Do I think this gift is useless? No. It's just very weird and rough around the edges. If the range granted per success were more narrative than strict measured ranges I might like it more, or perhaps if it covered a pretty big area by default and more successes just narrowed down the location further and gave a better impression. The flavour is also weird in that it suggests the place touched by magic should be close to the ground, and that isn't the gift's mechanic.<br /> <br /> =========================================<br /> <br /> Luna's is worth 10xp as always, and there are probably harder Gifts to learn given its teacher is quite common. Clap of Thunder would also be worth 10xp RAW, but should probably be adjusted. Given the teacher is a Stormcrow it will be hard to acquire out of list!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-07-2023, 05:13 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-03T09:57:36-07:00","id":1513788,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 SILENT STRIDER GIFTS</b></u>\u200b<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Axis Mundi:</b></u></span> See the Lupus section. Silent Striders REALLY suffer from having too many gifts like this, for what it's worth. They already get a compass Gift at level 1, so WHAT is this doing here?! Replace it with a reasonably realistic Gift of the player's choice, perhaps tied to birds, or travel, or tracking. Pulse of the Prey would really help the Striders.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Blissful Ignorance:</b></u></span> See the Ragabash entry.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Messenger's Fortitude:</b></u></span> Allows you to run at full speed without any breaks or rests for three days, act for 10 minutes, then collapse to sleep for a full day. This is horribly weak for a level 2 Gift, and I just can't imagine a PC getting any worthwhile use out of it. It's a niche of a niche, especially in a setting with planes and moon bridges. Doing ANYTHING but running cancels the Gift. The ability to add others to the Gift for one Gnosis each is cool, but I would edit the Gift so that adding your whole Pack is only one Gnosis.<br /> <br /> Sadly the gift is just bad, even if you need to flee there is an uninteractive and unfun element to it; running and ONLY running is monotonous by its very nature, and it means you can't handle obstacles in any interesting way. Either you can run, in which case the Gift works, or you can't, in which case you wasted 6xp and a bunch of Gnosis.<br /> <br /> Owl's kids deserved better. Feels like they're under more than one curse with these Gifts.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Speech of the World:</b></u></span> See the Homid entry. Fits the Striders really well, but a Homid Ragabash Strider faces a real slap to the face.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Tread Sebek's Back:</b></u></span> Costs a Gnosis, has a roll, and lets you run across any liquid.<br /> <br /> In Erebus, RAW, you could run across the surface of the lakes of molten silver. You'd be taking 1 point of agg every turn, but regenerating it too! Though I imagine if you were supposed to be IN the silver, the Crones would forcibly dunk you in like a kid refusing to go for a swim.<br /> <br /> This gift is handy enough, and could help you if you need to cross a pool of radioactive waste, lava and so on. However Garou are pretty good at leaping, especially with Gifts like Hare's Leap and Great Leap, and even without them. They also have ways of flying. This Gift could be very useful, don't get me wrong, and is great if you need to run across a lake or sea, or avoid Rokea or so on, and lore-wise the tribe having this is helpful for the tribe, but given how rare the need to run across liquid is they could've made this a no-cost, or had the cost only apply if you want to grant it to your pack too.<br /> <br /> Still, it's handy, and the utility can't be denied. Perhaps less urgent to pick up if you have Hare's Leap, or a Totem/Fetish which permits flight or similar.<br /> <br /> ===============================================<br /> <br /> This list needs Axis Mundi removed. So that leaves two gaps; which two Gifts you add would be down to personal preference, but I'd be drawn to anything that helps them do their job whilst also being USEFUL IN AN RPG.<br /> <br /> Gifts being repeated across lists also did give me an idea; if a Gift appears in more than one of your three lists you get a small discount. If it appears in two of your lists, you get a 1xp discount, if it appears in all three, you get a 2xp discount. The Gift is even more natural for you to acquire. For instance a Metis Theurge from a Tribe who get Sense Wyrm, such as the Silent Striders and Elder Brothers would pay 1xp for Sense Wyrm if they acquired it in-game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-07-2023, 05:13 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-03T11:30:13-07:00","id":1513797,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 SILVER FANG GIFTS</b></u>\u200b<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Empathy:</b></u></span> Atrocious flavour text. Wolves are highly social creatures, and W20 seems to feed into the myth of the 'alpha male' as often as it denies it. This is likely an issue of copy+pasting without thought. In all honesty it would be just as accurate to say that men respect strength above all else, while wolves care about empathy. Both ourselves and canids care about a mixture of things. Power, tenderness, social dynamics, family, territory, food. Wolves have concepts of good and bad, in a sense, and they will choose a member of the pack over someone powerful.<br /> <br /> It's also worth noting that a key flaw of the Silver Fangs is that they believe in divine right, ruling through might, through power. This Gift could speak to a better side to the Tribe, or it could be a cynical tool for those who don't really care about others. I do like that in fact; a Gift isn't good or bad, it's simply a power to be used as the user wishes.<br /> <br /> Spend Gnosis, roll a low difficulty Int+Emp, more successes means you have a better understanding of the group's wishes and needs, what they expect. I would also like to point out that the Silver Fangs could make a habit of <i>asking</i>.<br /> <br /> Used against enemies, this could glean a lot of useful information without risking running into traps or defenses against mind reading and similar. Looking at a gang of bikers and knowing that their expectations of their leader include beautiful kine ready to be drained of blood provides a lot of context - though with one success this might be mistaken for wishing to be provided with food and lovers. You could use this on wolf packs, Garou packs, groups of other supernaturals, business meetings, and use it to play havoc. You can give them what their leader doesn't understand, can't provide, WON'T provide.<br /> <br /> Group 'vibe' checking is a pretty great ability overall, just remember that the Gift is about picking up expectations and general group emotions, it isn't a sort of group mind reading. Its precision is both a neat limitation and actually helps direct and focus the Gift, making it good. If not for the Gnosis cost this would be Green. It would be nice if the Gnosis cost were waived for Garou of lower ranks than the user, or for the Garou's pack.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Hand Blade:</b></u></span> If only Garou already had access to a weapon that deals Str+2 aggravated!<br /> <br /> Jokes aside, this is a very handy QOL upgrade for Garou who rely on the Melee skill; if you have this, then you can safely leave Brawl relatively weak... but you'll still need Brawl to escape from grapples! The RP potential of being able to turn both of your hands into swords, really hammering home the idea of a living weapon, is great. Hell, combine this with partial transformation and a permissive ST and you could do all kinds of cool things.<br /> <br /> A potential cinematic use is swinging your arm and transforming it mid swing, perhaps after the point where an object would intercede. If you're using weapon length rules this is also handy.<br /> <br /> It would be neat if the difficulty were lower, but 6 is perfectly fine to work with. If gifts that buff claws apply to this blade then it gets even better, but RAW gives us no clues there. I would argue that Razor Claws and Silver Claws should both work on this.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Luna's Armour:</b></u></span> See the Children of Gaia entry. Especially important given the importance of Klaives, silver weapons and wealth in Silver Fang culture.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Sense Silver:</b></u></span> See the Metis entry. Ditto Luna's armour.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Unity of the Pack:</b></u></span> This suffers from W20's Pack Tactics not being particularly good, or engaging, and the system being poorly written. Most of them are simply descriptions of things you can do using existing maneuvers.<br /> <br /> Even if you're using good, well written pack tactics and the rules for them are laid out well at your table, +1 die to everyone is... a marginal benefit. It's alright. Just not incredible. This Gift also feels like a very, very obvious retread of a level one Ahroun Gift, Pack Tactics. Whether you prefer this or Pack Tactics will depend on the size of your pack, and how much leadership you have or intend to get. All in all I feel this is too similar, and also not a great level two Gift. Despite it not being objectively terrible, it's a rare example of a red which has clear utility. The utility is just so marginal that it would only scrape by as an amber at level one.<br /> <br /> ============================================<br /> <br /> Hand Blade might be worth 10xp for any melee Garou, especially if you don't want to invest in Brawl. For 9xp you could buy Brawl from 0 to 3; if you have Melee of 4 or 5, and a Brawl of 0, buying this Gift out of list would be worth it. Given that all tribes have Klaive Master Ancestors (though some will have them be much rarer) you shouldn't have much issue, just a higher price. Were it not for Empathy having a Gnosis cost it would definitely be worth 10xp on any Garou who cares about supporting or destroying groups of people.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-07-2023, 05:14 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-03T12:41:02-07:00","id":1513804,"title":"","content":"You skipped Tread Sobek's Back for the Striders.<br /> <br /> While I get that you're not a big fan of the Pack Tactics in core (though W20 does have rules for custom pack tactics in supplements), I think Unity of the Pack has two things being skipped. First, it's a permanent benefit no cost or roll. Second, nothing stops a pack with multiple Silver Fangs getting this. We had an all Silver Fang pack once,and +5 dice to ever pack tactic roll, was kinda insane. That might be too niche to bump it up to Amber and all, but it's also a great way to explain how the Silver Fangs managed to stay on top of the Garou pecking order. A pack of even Fostern Silver Fangs have teamwork skills that put anyone else to shame.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-03T14:40:26-07:00","id":1513818,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1513804#post1513804\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You skipped Tread Sobek's Back for the Striders.<br /> <br /> While I get that you're not a big fan of the Pack Tactics in core (though W20 does have rules for custom pack tactics in supplements), I think Unity of the Pack has two things being skipped. First, it's a permanent benefit no cost or roll. Second, nothing stops a pack with multiple Silver Fangs getting this. We had an all Silver Fang pack once,and +5 dice to ever pack tactic roll, was kinda insane. That might be too niche to bump it up to Amber and all, but it's also a great way to explain how the Silver Fangs managed to stay on top of the Garou pecking order. A pack of even Fostern Silver Fangs have teamwork skills that put anyone else to shame.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fixed!<br /> <br /> And yeah that's a great point about a pack of silver fangs - +5 dice to all such effects would be amazing! And lore-wise there's no reason a Pack can't be as big as 13 - that kind of coordination would be terrifying, especially with a few Ahroun with Pack Tactics!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-03T15:05:16-07:00","id":1513819,"title":"","content":"Though that also brings up distinction between the two Gifts: Pack Tactics (on top of being capped at +5 dice spread as evenly as possible between participants), requires the user to &quot;initiate&quot; the maneuver. While you can debate over if they means narratively coordinate or literally has to be the first character to roll, they have to participate in the tactic for the Gift to apply. Unity of the Pack works just by being there. A Silver Fang could use a debuff or stun style Gift on a target and still give their packmates +1 die each for group attack. That versatility is definitely a bonus over Pack Tactics; esp. if you incorporate some of the older pack tactics or use the create your own ones rules (esp. when it gets to non-combat pack tactics that can make it very different in which PCs are going to be the main ones up for things).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-03T16:27:04-07:00","id":1513825,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11620_5.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11620\" data-vbnamecard=\"11620\">twig</a> Love the thread, will you be doing something similar for Merits, Rites and Fetishes?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-03T17:33:34-07:00","id":1513830,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>lbeaumanior</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1513825#post1513825\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11620_5.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11620\" data-vbnamecard=\"11620\">twig</a> Love the thread, will you be doing something similar for Merits, Rites and Fetishes?</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you so much, I really appreciate that!<br /> <br /> Fetishes would be top of that list! But still have all level 3-6 Gifts, and maybe the BSD and weird regional Gifts, plus maybe the Fera Gifts...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-03-2023, 08:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-03T19:41:19-07:00","id":1513841,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1513830#post1513830\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Thank you so much, I really appreciate that!<br /> <br /> Fetishes would be top of that list! But still have all level 3-6 Gifts, and maybe the BSD and weird regional Gifts, plus maybe the Fera Gifts...</div> </div> </div> </div>It honest appreciation, the points you make are accurate and insightful. Some things I had never even considered (<b>Hare's Leap</b> is the main thing that got me rethinking, but there are others).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-04T01:22:58-07:00","id":1513845,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>lbeaumanior</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1513841#post1513841\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It honest appreciation, the points you make are accurate and insightful. Some things I had never even considered (<b>Hare's Leap</b> is the main thing that got me rethinking, but there are others).</div> </div> </div> </div>Totally! My PC has a STR+Athletics of 11 in Lupus, so with Hare's Leap he would be able to leap 210 feet, assuming 10 successes on the STR+Athletics check, which is difficulty 3 after all! If you have a specialty, and 12 dice, and spend WP, you could get closer to 250 or 300 feet even.<br /> <br /> Hell, if you made a Pack Tactic based on the whole pack doing a running leap together, and you had gifts to support it, you could leap even further...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-04T01:48:12-07:00","id":1513847,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 STARGAZER GIFTS</b></u>\u200b<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Inner Light:</b></u></span> A great QOL upgrade for Stargazers which makes entering and leaving the Umbra far easier. This would be a PERFECT Silent Strider Gift! A replacement for Axis Mundi; accessing and leaving the Umbra freely, while running even potentially (if you got enough sux to cross over immediately), is so important to smooth travel.<br /> <br /> Obvious green simply because it lets you do so many things with stepping sideways which would be hard otherwise.<br /> <br /> Hiding you have this might allow for some fun surprise escapes, even from other Garou.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Inner Strength:</b></u></span> This Gift allows you to meditate and turn Rage into Willpower. Though this is very good, I would point out that Strength of Purpose lets you generate Willpower from nothing once per scene.<br /> <br /> This Gift combines well with Primal Anger, of course. But it has dis-synergy with Channeling, which makes Rage a very valuable resource you can spend for bonus dice. The ability to refresh your mind with Willpower before regaining Rage from the moon or similar is handy. I like the Gift, it's a good QOL upgrade, but given that for an additional 4xp you can get a better version of the same Gift it's Amber. TBH I would consider replacing Inner Strength with Strength of Purpose, or the other way around.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Resist Temptation:</b></u></span> Great flavour, niche effect. This power lets you use a Gnosis point and a Wits + Rituals roll to make yourself harder to tempt or manipulate with supernatural powers. As a fun bonus, mundane attempts also fail by default when the Gift is active.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Surface Attunement:</b></u></span> Wow, the Silent Striders continue to get bad luck! This Gift makes the Stargazers better at the Striders' job than they are.<br /> <br /> No cost, only a turn concentrating and an easy roll, and you can cross all natural obstacles it seems, including water, sand, mud etc. without leaving any traces or falling.<br /> <br /> This one is still amber, but it's far better than Sebek's. Perhaps giving it to the Striders would be sensible. It's uncertain whether this Gift would let you run across lava, or liquid silver, or Wyrm corrupted waters, but it's at least implied by the power's text.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Wuxing:</b></u></span> This is a weird one! It's extremely magical, with no Gnosis cost, simply a regular roll. This means you can realistically use it in combat; action to turn the water an aquatic bane is immersed in into wood or fire, Rage actions to attack other enemies and so on.<br /> <br /> Keep in mind, as with Create Element, that a square foot is WAY bigger than you'd think. A duration of 1-5+ turns (Gnosis) is also handy; I'd say that a duration of 3 turns is typically all you need in WOD combat.<br /> <br /> Applications which come to mind: turn water into a form of containment, turn someone's shield into water or fire so you can bypass it, turn a door or floor into water so you can bypass it, start a fire, create a safe path through a fire, disable a flame weapon, stop a rite reliant on a flame, possibly interfere with Balefire (uncertain), bypass a stone surface, turn a fire or layer of water into a stone barrier, mess with Elementals (uncertain).<br /> <br /> Part of why I struggle with this Gift is that the image of creating a big block of wood in the middle of a pool of water, or a huge fire, feels like Minecraft hacks. It feels fake. I read this and go &quot;nah, that's not true, Werewolves can't do that, you made that bit up&quot; at the book.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-07-2023, 05:14 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-04T05:13:24-07:00","id":1513861,"title":"","content":"That feels a bit generous to Inner Light. Since W20 allows personal mirrors to work (though you might hit a +1 diff penalty if your ST feels that's a bit cheesy), it's extremely simple for most Garou to carry around a reflective surface for hopping into the Umbra, and Pulse of the Invisible (which is, yes, a Rank 3 Gift) negates the need for reflective surfaces and does way more on top of it.<br /> <br /> Inner Light feels like it would be Green if it was Rank 1, but at 2, you should easily have access to a dedicated object that's reflective, which reduces the number of circumstances where Inner Light is really going to matter; even if it means a +1 diff from your ST. Inner Light always felt like it only exists for Stargazers to eschew material objects (including dedicating something shiny and reflective to get into the Umbra anywhere instead of meditating in front of a &quot;proper&quot; surface) than it really adds much to play.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-04T06:11:16-07:00","id":1513868,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1513861#post1513861\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That feels a bit generous to Inner Light. Since W20 allows personal mirrors to work (though you might hit a +1 diff penalty if your ST feels that's a bit cheesy), it's extremely simple for most Garou to carry around a reflective surface for hopping into the Umbra, and Pulse of the Invisible (which is, yes, a Rank 3 Gift) negates the need for reflective surfaces and does way more on top of it.<br /> <br /> Inner Light feels like it would be Green if it was Rank 1, but at 2, you should easily have access to a dedicated object that's reflective, which reduces the number of circumstances where Inner Light is really going to matter; even if it means a +1 diff from your ST. Inner Light always felt like it only exists for Stargazers to eschew material objects (including dedicating something shiny and reflective to get into the Umbra anywhere instead of meditating in front of a &quot;proper&quot; surface) than it really adds much to play.</div> </div> </div> </div>See I was thinking of situations where having a reflective surface of any kind is hard to pull off, like Lupus and Hispo, and also that a total lack of need for reflections will help optimize an attempt to cross over in the middle of a scene/conflict.<br /> <br /> As for Pulse of the Invisible, I checked the Gift and it didn't say anything about helping cross over, so I went &quot;you misremembered twig&quot; - your reply just jogged my memory. The rules specifically mention Pulse, separately, don't they. The advice I was going to write in the first place is &quot;this is less good if you get access to Pulse of the Invisible&quot;, which 4/5 Stargazers won't.<br /> <br /> But yeah I think it's easy to overlook how cumbersome pulling out and looking into a mirror is. It takes up a hand and also limits your ability to join hands with packmates to help them come with you. A high-Gnosis PC with Inner Light could be a very effective means of swiftly crossing over without giving any warning. After all, &quot;he's pulled out a mirror&quot; is a HUGE tell to most of the enemies Garou face.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-04T10:04:57-07:00","id":1513879,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 ELDER BROTHER GIFTS</b></u>\u200b<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Coils of the Serpent*:</b></u></span> I feel like Clan Lasombra are owed royalties.<br /> <br /> You summon tendrils of fog, without cost or special condition, with a DEX+Occult roll, meaning this is best done in Lupus/Hispo funnily enough. They're 30 foot long and have your characteristics, so in Hispo they could have 8/7/8 potentially, and it would be 4/3/4 as a bare minimum.<br /> <br /> This Gift has two major uses. The explained effect is to bind a target, or targets (with split dice pools or rage actions) with tendrils of fog. This uses DEX+Brawl. The target becomes constrained on a success, and on their turn they may roll Strength, not Str+Brawl, but Strength alone, diff 7 to break free. They need as many successes as there are tendrils to escape; I will assume that if you are bound by 3 tendrils and get 2 successes, you manage to break free of 2/3.<br /> <br /> This alone is incredible, especially without a Gnosis cost; you can summon tendrils every single turn for your main action and attack with Rage actions.<br /> <br /> The second use is to create the tendrils and use them... freeform. They're 30 foot long and have physical stats for a reason after all, and that reason to my taste is grabbing objects in your environment, lifting you up to a roof, holding you out of reach of foes, sweeping away Wyrm tainted materials, throwing stuff, holding doors closed, knocking over motorbikes, slamming open doors, smashing windows, and a million other uses. Directing the tendrils is an action, and it seems that it takes up your full action to get them to do one thing, but 1-5 or so tendrils utterly wrecking a piece of the environment in a scene could be incredibly useful. &quot;Pick us all up and lift us up to the roof&quot; would be fun for a pack as well.<br /> <br /> Make sure your ST appreciates how long 30 feet is as well; keeping a BSD 30 feet away while you and your packmates pepper them with silver bullets from a rifle is great.<br /> <br /> I would genuinely add a Gnosis cost to balance this; with a Gnosis cost you can go wild with the applications. Without it, you may find it does too much, hence the asterisk.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Fetish Fetch*:</b></u></span> Too expensive... typically. Could be anything from Red to Green depending on context.<br /> <br /> You set up a hiding spot by spending Gnosis. You summon your Fetish by spending Gnosis. You send it back by spending Gnosis. Simply remove the cost to dismiss your Fetish; it can return on its own if you dismiss it.<br /> <br /> A fun application of this Gift is that size isn't referenced. A ship or car that's a Fetish will come to you, including in a small space. Being able to summon and banish your car is also hilarious, like a magical mount in an RPG. If you have a super useful Fetish which is too awkward to carry around with you, like a huge weapon or a big rifle or something evil looking or Wyrmish, or a car or motorbike or boat, or something so precious it can never leave a hole in a sacred ultra well guarded Caern, this Gift could be anything from Amber to Green. Generally speaking it will be Red. I'm going with Amber as that's what it will average out to, if you bother to buy it; you wouldn't buy it if you didn't have a Fetish worth using it with.<br /> <br /> EDIT: This being compatible with Magpie's Swag, however, is a huge boon to a pack. You've found the sacred egg, you just need to escape unnoticed... summon your bag, put it inside, dismiss your bag. Spending 2 Gnosis to skip the &quot;get the precious object out without it being stolen back, tracked or destroyed&quot; step is pretty amazing! You could also summon a bag full of gear, explosives, weapons, Talens, silver, food and drink, sensitive data, a ransom, whatever you like!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Shadows at Dawn*:</b></u></span> This Gift has solid utility and lets you delete a memory from a foe with a semi-reliable check. You only get one attempt, so using this Gift is inherently risky. You could trade secrets, test the waters for revealing a hidden bit of lore which has been kept secret... and that's it. The Gift is lore-specific. No revealing you love your packmate just before sunrise with this Gift to see how they react.<br /> <br /> It's really annoying that this Gift has been so thoroughly niched. Furthermore unless the Gift's existence is 100% a secret, people may recognize it and guard against it. Even if someone is entirely ignorant, between receiving the secret and dawn coming they could write it down, tell another, make plans to go somewhere or do something which they might follow through on even forgetting the reason why... &quot;huh, I searched up a specific Native American myth on my phone last night and can't remember why I did that&quot; is catnip to many Garou, including most Theurges.<br /> <br /> The main purpose of this Gift is being selfish, which also loses it points, or potentially hiding something that can't be revealed... in which case why are you risking it with a Gift that might fail? A Gift which helps you to do social PVP with the Theurge in your party, or mess with a neutral/allied spirit, is all well and good but if it's revealed it will make you wish you hadn't wasted 6xp on the &quot;everyone hates me&quot; power.<br /> <br /> The * is because I don't think this Gift is irredeemably bad as a piece of lore, or as an NPC power! It's bad for a PC to invest in. It's too niched, not 100% reliable for how niche it is, and generally speaking is best used to mess with people who are in theory on the same side as you. Carrying on a longstanding tradition of not trusting the rest of the Garou Nation doesn't help your PC out; it's the very thing the Tribe needs to find a way to outgrow, little as members of the &quot;Wyrmbringer&quot; Tribes may deserve it in some cases.<br /> <br /> If you're going to be consistently RPing with outsiders with mystic knowledge, from Arcanum to Hermetics, this becomes better.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Spirit of the Bird:</b></u></span> Flight is amazing. There are very few places you can't reach with it, and a Crinos form flying around like a bird is simply terrifying. Leaps are all well and good, but relies upon moving from platform to platform. With flight you can simply navigate spaces freely.<br /> <br /> The +1 difficulty to combat moves while this is turned on is annoying, but you can offset that with a low difficulty weapon, or with point blank shooting from just out of reach, or with Gifts like Razor Claws.<br /> <br /> The Gnosis cost is very fair given you are flying. 20mph with hovering is pretty handy, though it's notably less than half the running speed of a wolf. Basically in the world of Garou and Fera this is clumsy, slow flight. Corax may make fun of you, especially if you think this Gift is going to let you catch them.<br /> <br /> Against foes who aren't fleeing, kiting or similar, and don't have a means of outflying you, this is pretty great. Your capacity to carry others is also potentially noteworthy; I'd rule you can carry another if you can take their weight while walking, so Crinos could carry two Homids or so, with worse difficulty spikes and lower speed.<br /> <br /> This is also a great remedy to falling, and lets you visit a lot of Umbral Realms which would otherwise be totally inaccessible. The potential for defense, offense and navigation means that this perk is so general it's wonderful.<br /> <br /> Veil risk is only a tiny downside - you might not want to fly to a Pentex board meeting for an assault with this Gift... though if you're in Crinos, the Delirium might cover for you, depends on context.<br /> <br /> One thing to keep in mind with this Gift is that both running (on all 4s in Crinos, in Hispo, in Lupus) and leaping can be faster than it; so try to mix up your movement to be as confusing and difficult to fight as possible. If you don't need to be in the air, stick to the ground. Surprise enemies. Lure them off cliffs. Go for an impossible leap, hope foes follow, and hover in the air. Fake a fall to your death and return for an ambush. Grapple someone, drag them a mile up and drop them.<br /> <br /> It's also worth remembering that almost all spirits can fly by default, so this Gift is very useful for chasing spirits.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Spirit of the Fish:</b></u></span> Spend one Gnosis, do a Stamina + Animal Ken, swim at Hispo run speed (very fast) and breathe underwater for 1 hour per success. Not restricted to a particular kind of water, either.<br /> <br /> Underwater missions aren't going to be super common, but if you do need to swim or go underwater this will be great. Drowning is one of the few mundane ways you can reliably kill a Werewolf, assuming you submerge them in Homid form and keep them busy (transforming once submerged doesn't grant you extra time, and if you're forced to fight you have a matter of turns, not minutes).<br /> <br /> This Gift is pretty perfect at what it does, and allows you to escape from many foes using water. If you use this proactively you could fight underwater where you have the advantage. Your Pack lacking this isn't ideal, nor is the Gnosis cost, but if you are able to drive the engagement and choose how it plays out this Gift is pretty great.<br /> <br /> A solid Amber, but would be Green if you were playing in a setting filled with water (a city like Venice, a lake district, a region of small islands, a local Caern is underwater etc.).<br /> <br /> ============================================<br /> <br /> Are Coils and Bird worth buying out of list? I'd say yes; Coils is great on any concept with good Occult (my Ahroun would have a pool of 8 for Coils' activation, and if he had it I'd buy more Occult too, plus his Dex+Brawl is amazing of course), Bird is good on anyone with Gnosis to spend. Earning these Gifts would be the tricky part.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-07-2023, 05:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-04T12:15:59-07:00","id":1513885,"title":"","content":"Shadows at Dawn was always something I thought was mostly for using on non-Garou targets; aka characters highly unlikely to be prepared for what any Gift's specifics are, not just this specific one Part of Older Brother's whole thing is willing to go to mystic players other Garou don't to learn occult secrets. It's the kind of thing you use on other supernaturals that aren't going to expect this sort of thing coming. IME red vs amber is highly dependent on if you're facing NPC antagonists stated up using their official rules, or approximated versions. If you're tricking a Namer out of some magical secrets you don't have to worry as much about it backfiring compared to a mage that has Time magic to go back and magically see what happened according to Mage rules.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-04T12:23:57-07:00","id":1513887,"title":"","content":"I realized that Fetish Fetch could be used with a Magpie's Swag, and updated the entry!<br /> <br /> As for using it on Mages/Thaumaturges/Sorcerers/Arcanum types... I can see that! Still a bit niche, but it's a good angle on the Gift.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-04T13:13:12-07:00","id":1513889,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 2 YOUNGER BROTHER GIFTS</b></u>\u200b<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Cutting Wind:</b></u></span> Spend a Willpower, roll a Dex + Occult with difficulty based on ranged weapon rules (which is fun), those hit by the winds lose 2 dice from their pools for 2 turns; there are definitely worse debuffs. The Gift also can throw people into obstacles or traffic etc. though how this plays out mechanically is anyone's guess. The wind lasts for a number of turns equal to your successes, but it's unclear if you can manipulate it after it is created. Like many YB Gifts this doesn't feel particularly playtested. The flavour text mentions that they can &quot;direct it at will&quot; but this could be referring to directing the wind when you make the initial attack.<br /> <br /> Begrudgingly this is a Green. The ability to slap a whole pack with -2 dice to all checks for 2 turns, very reliably, is pretty great. I don't have to like it though!<br /> <br /> It would have been neat to make this Gift an advancement of Call the Breeze, upgrading the power to add an immediate debuff.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Claws of Frozen Death:</b></u></span> See Get of Fenris entry for Fangs of the North.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Salmon Swim:</b></u></span>Replace this with Spirit of the Fish. Salmon Swim only works on natural freshwater bodies, and lets you swim as 'easily as a fish' (no idea whether this means you're as fast as a salmon, or a wolf running) and walk on water. Useless in pools, artificial lakes, sewers, seas and so on.<br /> <br /> The Gift has the advantage of allowing you to leap like a salmon from freshwater body to freshwater body, which is neat, but I'd rather have Hare's Leap. It's only 5xp.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Speak With Wind Spirits*:</b></u></span> Depending on execution this could be much stronger or weaker. I find the flavour a little weird since wind spirits would move around over a large area, but instead they are accurately specific to the area you're stood in. It'd make more sense if they, you know, followed the wind.<br /> <br /> Being able to &quot;automatically speak to wind spirits in the umbra&quot; is a weird one. Can people not normally find and speak to wind spirits? Is this meant to refer to overcoming the need for spirit speech?<br /> <br /> Being able to speak to Umbral spirits from the real world without a Gnosis cost, or a Gnosis roll, or a difficulty based on the Gauntlet... is odd. But still. The Gift is the Gift. And it could be very useful if the ST lets it be; it falls into this issue of whether wind spirits know or care about human murders, whether they can see through the Gauntlet (especially in thick areas).<br /> <br /> Super strictly RAW a spirit could just go &quot;yeah so I can't see through the gauntlet here, and I'm not really that interested in these 'humans' you speak of - but I know a fun rumour about some storm clouds down south!&quot; This Gift seems to have been written for the spirits of a different setting. It would make a lot more sense if this Gift let you ask questions to wind spirits about events which took place upwind. But then the Gift wouldn't be useful.<br /> <br /> Basically this Gift is a headache; it's meant to be a &quot;ask the ST for a clue&quot; power but the flavour gets so mangled it isn't really ideal.<br /> <br /> If there were much chance of anyone ever using this Gift I'd care more for a deep dive; I get the impression that Elder Brother are more likely to be played, and more likely to actually reach rank 2 as a character. Perhaps there are really committed Younger Brother fans out there, but I've yet to meet anyone who is a HUGE FAN of YB, the way people are fans of EB, Get, GW, CoG etc. Depending on implementation this could be Red or Amber, or even potentially Green if these wind spirits are omniscient (despite their &quot;short attention spans&quot;).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>True Fear:</b></u></span> See Ahroun entry.<br /> <br /> =======================================<br /> <br /> Hope I'm not coming off as actively biased against the YB - they just need better written Gifts that don't feel like afterthoughts!<br /> <br /> This finishes level two off! Level 3 is where things will get a little trickier; 9 or 15 XP is a big enough investment that a Gift genuinely needs to shine to be worth purchasing at all. So some Gifts will likely be Red simply because they don't cut a cost of 9XP.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-07-2023, 05:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-05T06:07:01-07:00","id":1513945,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 3 HOMID GIFTS</b></u>\u200b<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Calm the Savage Beast:</b></u></span> Cancel a frenzy within 30 feet by spending a WP and passing a reasonably difficult roll. For a second WP you can end the frenzies of Vampires, Fera and so on.<br /> <br /> This is a great power for Homids and CoG, and definitely worthwhile to prevent Veil breaches, save allies from harming their loved ones, dragging an NPC back to conversation and so on. It's hard to really give examples where it's useful; there's only one example. Someone within 9m is frenzying and you don't want them to be.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Cowing the Bullet:</b></u></span> +2 soak dice to all weapons not made of silver is a neat upside. If it were passive it would be pretty great! But it costs a point of Gnosis, so it's just... eh.<br /> <br /> If you want this Gift to be worth a level 3 spot, consider removing the activation requirement and making it entirely passive, or buffing the effect, such as adding your Crafts rating to all soak against weapons not made of silver, which would suit the flavour. The more objects fear your talents and authority over them the less they will dare harm you.<br /> <br /> In a world where Jam Weapon exists this is even more silly.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Disquiet:</b></u></span> Without a cost you can roll Manipulation + Empathy, difficulty of the target's Willpower, to make them become depressed. This can stop Rage being gained (great against Silver Claws, Gurahl defending something they care about, Metis using Primal Anger etc.) and also prevent people from focusing on extended actions. The RP impact is also decent. Making a security guard decide there's no point investigating noises is a fun example of the power in use, but it's hardly one that fills me with great excitement.<br /> <br /> Ultimately against any real threat this Gift won't do much; they'll go from killing you to killing you while feeling bad. Your enemy needing to schedule a therapy session after cleaning you off their claws is cold comfort.<br /> <br /> If you regularly face foes who recover Rage mid-scene or who engage in extended actions during a scene, this is alright. Generally speaking Red, though it would be Amber at lower levels.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Reshape Object:</b></u></span> I...<br /> <br /> A Homid Garou can make shelter out of a tree already. They have Apecraft's. A Homid Garou is a weapon themselves; they don't need to turn antlers into a sword or whatever. The transformation lasting for one scene per success is horrible, and needing 5+ successes for permanence feels really bad. In addition the Gnosis cost being 2 to make a weapon Aggravated for a scene is just bad.<br /> <br /> What you can change shape is relatively limited (once living materials only), and if you want to make anything interesting the difficulty will be very high. A canoe, not a super complex object, is difficulty 8. Basically the Gift is proof against you trying to make it do something good, by default.<br /> <br /> When I think Rank 3 Garou I think mighty powers, not &quot;can turn a stick into a walking stick&quot;. If you want to fix this, remove the base Gnosis cost, and have making a weapon Aggravated cost one Gnosis. Then it has utility in arming Kinfolk; for 3 Gnosis you could give three Kinfolk warriors Aggravated damage polearms.<br /> <br /> This Gift is red for the same reason as Prey Sense; it solves a mundane problem a regular human/wolf could solve using their skills alone, and the problem is one that a Werewolf should find to be a cakewalk.<br /> <br /> ==============================================<br /> <br /> A shockingly bad turnout for the Homids! Calm the Savage Beast is worthwhile on anyone who cares about diplomacy or peace, so Metis/Lupus who aren't CoG might enjoy buying this out of list for 15xp... or they could rely on a homid packmate! It's pricy, but could be worth it if you desperately need it.<br /> <br /> ==============================================<br /> <br /> <u><b>RANK 3 METIS GIFTS</b></u>\u200b<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Chameleon:</b></u></span> This is weirdly good, even for an invisibility power. Anyone trying to see you needs to pass a Perception roll (JUST Perception it seems!) difficulty of your Wits + Stealth, which could well be 8-10. Unless you're fighting enemies who can reliably detect your exact location using only sound or scent, or footprints in the snow or similar, this will give you -2 difficulty to most combat checks and give your enemies +2 difficulty to theirs most likely, if they can strike you at all.<br /> <br /> Once you've attacked the difficulty to see you drops by 3, but that's still potentially a check enemies won't pass first time.<br /> <br /> This is good in combat, and for sneaking around. It has a Gnosis cost, but as a Metis at Rank 3 you'll have 4+ points to spend most likely. If you stay still and have little scent, or even suppress your scent entirely with the Ragabash Gift Scent of Running Water, you'll be next to undetectable save through magic means.<br /> <br /> Green on the assumption that you get a good Wits + Stealth pool, and 99% of your encounters aren't with Daredevil.<br /> <br /> If you are worried, you can combine this Gift with active stealth, rather than walking around freely. It depends on your skillset and how concerned you are by enemies knowing you're around; even if they can locate you, it's hard to fight a transparent blur. Speaking of Blur, combine with Blur of the Milky Eye, which weirdly effects all Perception difficulties to locate you and not just the ones for sight RAW, for best results. It might feel like the Gifts would be a waste together, but if anything they combine very well.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Eyes of the Cat:</b></u></span> At will your eyes can turn green (though I'd permit another cat colour, so long as it's obviously a Veil breach) and permit you to see perfectly in the dark.<br /> <br /> My Metis is blind, so he won't bother with this one.<br /> <br /> Still, this Gift is good! No roll, no cost, which is what level 3+ Gifts often need to be relevant. There are a lot of lower level Gifts which handle this topic somewhat, and you'll probably have a better outcome with Scent of Sight (10xp out of list, vs 9xp in-list for this).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Mental Speech:</b></u></span> Either you need to know your target (presumably including acquaintances, but not someone you met in passing) or have something of theirs. Charisma + Empathy diff 8, spend a WP, 16KM range per success. This is a great power for communicating with allies and enemies alike.<br /> <br /> Found a human body savaged by what looks like a Garou? Drop a message to the killer informing them that they are in your Sept without introducing themselves, and shall do so immediately. Even if they tell you to piss off, now you know for sure they're within a certain range, and you might know their voice or learn other details about them. You can play lost and found, harass a murderer and make them think they're going insane, give instructions to someone prone to religious hysteria or mental illness, coordinate a conspiracy without ever meeting in public, taunt or mislead a foe, arrange meetings, do secret back door diplomacy, apologize safely from afar, threaten someone... the social options are endless.<br /> <br /> Pretending to be God to manipulate a bunch of humans to go after Pentex holdings sounds like an immoral yet fun gambit; I can imagine a PC getting in touch with a megapriest, telling them to fight against environmental destruction or suffer an eternity in hell, only for this priest to go &quot;my master has promised Set shall protect me! begone angels!&quot;. This Gift combines well with good social abilities, and I love that. It is a bridge for you to use your whole sheet, not simply a power which goes &quot;and then X happens&quot;. Those are cool, but getting a means of using your whole social repertoire in a fun way is amazing.<br /> <br /> Anyways between this and Sense Wyrm anti-Metis sentiment becomes more and more self destructive for Septs which lean into it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Shell:</b></u></span> Roll WP, difficulty of your Rage. With a success you become immune to all mind-altering magics for the whole scene. You can't get successes on Empathy, Primal-Urge or Rage rolls, or spend Rage however.<br /> <br /> A key vulnerability of a Garou is that they are very dangerous bodies who can be puppeteered by powers like Presence, Dominate, various Arts, Magick and a bunch of Gifts, along with Spirit Charms and Fomori Powers. If you were to go through the WoD, look for powers that can mess with a Garou and render them helpless, I imagine it would be about half of them. After all you don't want to be sobbing on the floor begging for forgiveness or killing your party members because that sexy stranger with the curves told you to.<br /> <br /> The only powers I can see overcoming this Gift are Charms of Incarnae and other singularly powerful Spirits, Level 9-10 Disciplines, Unleashings, Level 6 Gifts etc. It does after all give a very clear cut and absolute protection. Immune means Immune.<br /> <br /> Even if you ignore the semi-niche situation of defending yourself from effects which Shell protects against (True Fear, Howl of the Banshee, most deceptive Gifts etc.) it also stops you from winding up Frenzying, which is great! There are also a lot of locations in the Umbra which are inherently cognitotoxic. The despair of being forced to labour in The Scar, the terror of your ancestor's crimes in the Atrocity Realm, the overwhelming nostalgia of a Tribal Homeland... being the PC who can opt out of being fucked around with by the haunted house, or the Wyrm-tainted temple of Urges, is great.<br /> <br /> Perhaps, arguably, this Gift even shields you from the subtle innate manipulations of Pure Breed... just a thought. Is it a mind-altering magic? Would the mystical or psychological barriers of Shell defend a Metis against the impulse to obey the pure blooded? Who knows! Interesting thought though.<br /> <br /> ==============================================<br /> <br /> Any Ahroun would benefit from Shell; is it worth 15xp however? Maybe. Depends how severe your Rage issues are, and how good your Willpower is. Mental Speech could also be handy for any Pack, but again 15xp is pricey!<br /> <br /> Chameleon is good on any Ragabash of course, given the combination with Blur working well.<br /> <br /> ==============================================\u200b<br /> <br /> <u><b>RANK 3 LUPUS GIFTS</b></u>\u200b<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Catfeet:</b></u></span> Immunity to falls of 100 feet or less. Perfect balance even on the most slippery surfaces. Body slams and grappling at -2 difficulty. No cost, no activation, no roll.<br /> <br /> This is good enough to get a Green given how good grapples are.<br /> <br /> A creative use is to body slam someone into a 100 foot drop; you won't be hurt, they will. Up to 9 dice of bashing damage, or even 10 dice of lethal damage, could hit them, while you'll be just fine. Against a Garou enemy this isn't amazing, but it's good.<br /> <br /> This is also good for frauds, playing dead, luring people down into a dangerous position to help you or rob you, and all sorts of fun hijinks. Simply stepping off a rooftop, landing next to your car and driving off is also fun in RP. The ability to ignore a bunch of things you'd otherwise have to roll for is great; it may save you having to split dicepools or spend rage, or risk rolling at all.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Monkey Tail:</b></u></span> An automatic prehensile tail, without cost, in all tail-having forms is pretty neat. To the best of my knowledge this is Crinos, Hispo and Lupus.<br /> <br /> Hang from the ceiling, hide an object behind your back, hold a light source, carry a cub, drag someone by the ankle, fight without having to worry about your footing, zipline down to places, hold the wheel steady while you fight the monsters in the back seat.<br /> <br /> RP wise being able to grab something with a lengthy tail is pretty neat; partial transformation to activate this Gift might be your friend. Is it worth 15xp? No. But 10? Perhaps. If you have a means of hovering, or manipulating objects without your hands, this becomes pretty pointless. It's a nice upgrade to 3/5 of your forms, just nothing too special.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sense the Unnatural:</b></u></span> This is a budget combo of Sense Magic (very Tribe specific) and Scent of the True Form (relatively Auspice specific). That said, for 10xp you could get both of those out-of-list. This Gift costs 9xp in-list. Just a thought.<br /> <br /> This picks up supernatural presences, and gives you kind of vague feedback. It can handle locations, targets of powers, sources of them, and supernatural beings. Its range isn't defined. It also has no cost. Ultimately it's very similar to Sense Magic, with the added aspect of having a budget Scent of the True Form. This is a good Gift, it's a handy catchall source of clues and information, it could solve any number of problems... and given that the two Gifts which outdo it when combined are hard to get off list, I think it's fair to say that they don't make this Gift bad. For most Lupus this Gift will be good.<br /> <br /> Do I relish spending 9xp on it? Not particularly! But it still manages to be Green for its broad utility and its ability to provide you with clues you'd otherwise never get, like realizing an NPC is a well-hidden Vampire, or that a person is haunted by ghosts or being pranked by Fae etc.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Silence the Weaver:</b></u></span> Howling, spending a point of Rage and rolling a Manipulation + Primal-Urge lets you destroy sensitive electronic instruments. The range of 20 yards per success is pretty great! The distinction between simple and complex electronic instruments is a little vague, but the general gist seems to be that smaller, more portable and less robust objects are sensitive.<br /> <br /> To be quite honest there are too many Gifts that destroy machines. They've worn out their welcome. But let's compare.<br /> <br /> Jam Technology has a Gnosis cost, but lets you choose a level of complexity to ruin. It can do anything from MRIs to knives. Gremlins, which will be in the next post, has no cost at all. Silence the Weaver is just... kinda meh? Given that a non-Ragabash non-Glass Walker Lupus won't have any other anti-tech options in-list, I'm giving this an amber, but it's hardly excellent. Taking out all the mobile phones in an area is helpful enough, often enough, that I don't mind it too much. Just keep in mind that howls draw attention!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Strength of Gaia:</b></u></span> By spending a Rage point you can increase the bonus Strength of the Lupus form to 4. This makes Lupus a decently strong form to fight in...<br /> <br /> For Homids and Metis.<br /> <br /> For Lupus, you lack regeneration in Breed form, and you don't deal Aggravated damage either. If you want to make this Gift worthwhile, add Regeneration and Aggravated damage, along with full Garou soak, to the Lupus form. Weirdly this Gift is better on a Metis or Lupus.<br /> <br /> Red on the basis of it being so pricey for what it does, having a Rage cost, and not being innate, along with its very weak and somewhat niche power. If you want to fight in Lupus, chances are you can fight in Lupus too.<br /> <br /> If fighting in Lupus is a big advantage to you for some reason, say preserving the Veil while you &quot;savage dog&quot; someone with a disguise such as Guise of the Hound, great. But even then it's +3 Strength to help you fulfil some niche purpose. 9+Sux dice of Lethal is better than 6+Sux dice, of course it is, but a target you intend to kill in Lupus, swiftly and believably, is probably one you can handle without super strength.<br /> <br /> ==============================================<br /> <br /> \u200bTwo good options, which is ideal. The others will depend on what you like, how much XP you get.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-07-2023, 05:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-05T06:51:32-07:00","id":1513949,"title":"","content":"Strength of Gaia jumps up to at least Amber if you get Guise of the Hound and Catfeet: Tackle, Jaw Lock, neutralize your target without breaking the Veil. Lethal for lupus instead of Agg isn't that bad. Lots of things can't soak lethal,and most things that can soak lethal in WtA can soak Agg too. Going for the immobilization makes the lack of regen and soak less important: if your target has to try to get free to attack you, and will take a lot of damage if they try.<br /> <br /> This is also pretty nasty for Lupus from high Pure Breed Tribes (though drop the Guise of the Hound part) when in comes to challenges by combat. Petition for the fight to be in lupus (you're not trying to kill them after all!) and then do the Jaw Lock of doom on them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-05T08:12:53-07:00","id":1513952,"title":"","content":"True, but it's kind of annoying for the Gift's greatest potential to be in the hands of a non-Lupus! Veil-protecting murder with dog disguise is an angle I hadn't considered though, thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-05T15:05:53-07:00","id":1513973,"title":"","content":"Yeah, though I think it slots in with things like the pack tactics boosting Gifts where the issues are things outside of the Gift's design that drag it down.<br /> <br /> In my games we house rule the damage and soak differences by Breed significantly (everyone soaks lethal in any form, everyone does lethal in lupus). It makes it way easier to remember in the middle of combat, non-lupus can engage in wolf-dominance contests without worrying about Agg-murdering lupus*, and it creates an interesting tactical choice for homids and lupus when they know the enemy is packing silver weapons (revert to breed form for some soak dice but weaker stats, and no general regen but you can't regen silver anyway). As you can imagine, that alone makes Strength of Gaia way more popular for my groups.<br /> <br /> * - One of my PCs in a large persistent chat game had to stop a homid cub and a lupus cub who decided to have a play fight to help the homid learn more about fighting in lupus because the players were new to WtA and didn't have these minutiae down, while the narrator (kind of an assistant ST to help run scenes when the full ST can't be around if you're not familiar with this kind of play) was of the mind to &quot;let them learn the hard way,&quot; since neither PC was experienced enough to know about these quirks in the rules either. Fortunately I had Spirit of the Fray so there wasn't really a question about making sure I could interrupt.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-05T22:07:50-07:00","id":1513987,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11620_5.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11620\" data-vbnamecard=\"11620\">twig</a> could you add titles like &quot;<b>Rank 1 Homid Gifts</b>&quot; to your posts, that would make it later much easier to navigate, when using this as a guide.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5199-aahz","name":"Aahz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-06T09:17:30-07:00","id":1514018,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aahz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1513987#post1513987\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11620_5.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11620\" data-vbnamecard=\"11620\">twig</a> could you add titles like &quot;<b>Rank 1 Homid Gifts</b>&quot; to your posts, that would make it later much easier to navigate, when using this as a guide.</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11620_5.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11620\" data-vbnamecard=\"11620\">twig</a> I second this proposal.<br /> <br /> I am aware this is a bit of more effort but it will help a lot to check the content.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-06T12:42:52-07:00","id":1514037,"title":"","content":"Good point - it's a pain to do it now, rather than as I went along, but sure XD","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-06T13:03:39-07:00","id":1514042,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1514037#post1514037\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Good point - it's a pain to do it now, rather than as I went along, but sure XD</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks, looking forward for you to continue the guide.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5199-aahz","name":"Aahz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-07T03:05:25-07:00","id":1514101,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 3 RAGABASH GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Gremlins:</b></u></span> You roll Manipulation + Intimidation, difficulty determined by complexity of the device (simpler = harder), and if you get 3+ successes you scare the spirit of the object into fleeing, permanently wrecking it.<br /> <br /> I like this a lot, despite there being far too many tech-destroying Gifts.<br /> <br /> There's no cost, no real risk, and the baseline use of the power doesn't demand you prance around hunting the object, you can give it a mean glare. The RP potential of terrifying someone's toaster to death is very funny, and I love the idea of a technician (especially a Nocker, Etherite etc.) puzzling over a piece of equipment that simply cannot work anymore, despite all laws of science suggesting it is in perfect condition post repairs.<br /> <br /> If you want a fun artefact in some collection, have a sharp knife that won't cut. Guests are invited to try it out using those gloves-through-the-barrier things.<br /> <br /> An interesting potential use of this Gift is to have your Theurge in the Penumbra ready to command or capture the spirit you've banished. Yes, the spirits living inside all objects are relatively weak in most cases, but you could find uses for them. If you need to get a large number of weak spirits, there are worse ways of generating ones no one will miss.<br /> <br /> Use this on the vehicles, tools and weapons of enemies, and if you run into a techno-mage or similar you'll have a field day. Outside of Primium countermagic dice, a HIT MARK has very few defenses against this power, and might be shut off permanently by you netting 3 successes on a difficulty 3 check.<br /> <br /> Why do I like this more than Jam Technology or Silence the Weaver, or even Power Surge? You can pick a specific target. Simple as that. You can turn off the crazed cyborg without turning off the plane you're in. You can get rid of a guy's gun without getting rid of your own flamethrower. All of those Gifts lost points for being indiscriminate and risky.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Liar's Craft:</b></u></span> This one is interesting. It's essentially a better defined and written Glib Tongue.<br /> <br /> Roll Wits + Subterfuge, difficulty of the Wits + Subterfuge of the target (or highest among multiple targets); 1 success convinces an individual, 3 successes convince a group.<br /> <br /> I like that you roll after you lie, as it adds real risk.<br /> <br /> The ability to lie to a whole Sept and convince them all, in theory, is amazing. The ability to con a Pentex rep in a one on one conversation, the ability to hoodwink a spirit, another supernatural, convince a BSD you're a Wyrmish spy, set foes against one another, it's all great. This is one of those Gifts that turns the impossible into possible; you can completely transform a person or organization, a war, with a single lie. Just keep in mind that the Gift has not protections against the target verifying, or others acting on their behaviour regarding the lie. Risks exist, so keep a close eye on whoever you've sent on a mission to save their soul.<br /> <br /> One thing we aren't explicitly told is how this will interact with powers such as Truth of Gaia. Truth of Gaia reveals which words spoken are True or False, and you activate it after the fact judging by the wording; &quot;which of the words spoken have been&quot;, past tense. Liar's Craft will, RAW, have you utterly convinced. So in theory you wouldn't activate Truth of Gaia unless it was part of a protocol; for instance in a trial a Philodox might have the duty of fact-checking every statement made before the elder council, regardless of whether they seem to be true or not.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Monkey Tail:</b></u></span> See the Lupus entry.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Open Moon Bridge*:</b></u></span> This Gift lets you open a pre-existing Moon Bridge in a Caern. Since you can do this immediately by spending a single point of Gnosis without a roll, it's a great convenience. It's worth keeping in mind that the Gift is heavily restricted. You must be within a Caern. You must open an existing Bridge, meaning you are opening a path to a specific Caern, of which a Caern may have none, or a limited number. You have to run through the portal and travel the distance of the bridge (which is 1/1000th the normal distance, yes, but still not teleportation).<br /> <br /> Again this Gift is highly variable. Do you have access to a Caern? Not in all games. Do the antagonists have a Caern? Is it linked to other Caerns? Will you be invading a Pit? Does that Pit link to a different weaker one you'll find easier to escape from, or enter through? How often will NPC packs have a rank 3+ Ragabash?<br /> <br /> If you have access to a Caern which has moon bridges spanning a whole continent, to a variety of powerful Caerns in important locations, then this Gift could be Green. If it's unlikely to come up at all, it could be Red. Overall I'd call this a solid Amber, with a lot of potential.<br /> <br /> One aspect of this Gift to keep in mind is that it could, at best, turn you into an invaluable member of a Sept who acts like a sort of boatman. At worst it could make you a put-upon doorman, though likely only if you mess up. If doing a day to day job for your Caern wins you plenty of Renown and favours, this could be better.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Pathfinder*:</b></u></span> This Gift makes you harder to track, gives you a swifter route, but notably does not guarantee a safe route. Keep that in mind.<br /> <br /> Difficulty 7 Perception + Survival/Streetwise, with 5 successes you half travel time (-50%), and you'll be +2 difficulty to track.<br /> <br /> Not too much to say about this one beyond it being good; there's nothing saying you can't use it while aboard a plane, or in the back seat of your pack's van. The RP potential of going &quot;no, you take a left&quot; &quot;what onto that dirt trail?&quot; &quot;yep&quot; is fun. I do wonder how the magic of this Gift... works however. Say you're getting from one end of a jungle to another. How do you cut travel time by 50%? It's spirit magic, yes, but if a Technocrat were perfectly tracking the pack from space... does their weird implausible route bend space? Does it cause them to teleport in ways that they don't notice? Does it reshape the wilderness?<br /> <br /> The key question is &quot;does cutting your travel time by 10-50% and making yourself harder to track significantly impact the game&quot;, and that's more of a question for your ST than for me. If your ST throws you a lot of cool stuff for showing up earlier than you would have otherwise, keeps timings and travel time of NPCs in mind, has competing moving elements running around the world, this is incredible. In real life it would be amazing, of course. So if your ST is simulationist with Packs being tracked, perhaps even the private jets and SUV convoys of Pentex execs being tracked, then this gift is Green. If your ST doesn't track stuff like this (and there will be STs who say they do, but actually don't let it impact the plot or mechanics in any meaningful sense), it'll be a Red. And I reckon in most games this will be Amber. Hence the asterisk.<br /> <br /> ================================================== ==<br /> <br /> 4/5 of these might be worth 15XP, depending on your character and what they want or need. If you're constantly just slightly too late, and you truly trust your ST not to just continue that trend even if you arrive in half the time, Pathfinder could be amazing. Open Moon Bridge would be amazing in a game where it's Green, if you lack a Ragabash PC. Liar's Craft is amazing on any Face. Gremlins would be great on any Garou really, but most especially those who work closely with tech (Glass Walkers, Bone Gnawers) and those who fight it (Red Talons).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-08-2023, 08:10 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-07T06:26:07-07:00","id":1514111,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 3 THEURGE GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> For some reason we continue this trend of lists having varying numbers of Gifts.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Exorcism*:</b></u></span> To the best of my knowledge this is the only exorcising power available to Garou, outside of the infinite potential of Fetishes, Totem Gift (the Elder Metis Gift), and the potential to acquire a Fera Rite. We can call it the only feasibly obtained exorcising power. With this you can free Fomori from the spirits within, rip Banes from powerful Fetishes you can't destroy, drive spirits out of their prisons to free them and more besides. In theory you could attempt to drive a Caern or Pit's Totem from the location, though you'd likely be killed by said spirit or by its guardian Garou in the process. Many successes would also be required.<br /> <br /> Note that this takes 3 turns of uninterrupted focus, and a Bane actually leaving a living creature seemingly takes 10 rounds. In the meantime said Fomori is likely writhing on the ground instead of fighting, however.<br /> <br /> If you're part of a pack who care about saving Fomori, such as my own, then this Gift is great to have. You can save people from their endless suffering as Fomori so long as you have a Theurge or Child of Gaia able to heal them.<br /> <br /> Note that there is no Gnosis cost, which is pretty good.<br /> <br /> Overall this Gift is a solid Amber most of the time, but could be a Green if saving the ordinary people who become Fomori floats the party's boat. Even then you need to deal with them having memories of Veil breaches! Another reason for Green would be if the party were constantly interacting with objects and locations imprisoning good spirits (who they can free) and Banes (who they can destroy).<br /> <br /> Note that the Elder Brother Tribe may well be very wary of this Gift being used indiscriminately, given the lengths they have gone to to bind Banes under the earth.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Pulse of the Invisible*:</b></u></span> See past the Gauntlet into the Umbra (though not the other way around!) if your Gnosis equals or exceeds the Gauntlet rating. If it doesn't you can do so by rolling Gnosis, difficulty of the local Gauntlet, which essentially makes Peeking as easy from physical to spiritual as it is the other way around. By default such sight would be at Gauntlet + 3 difficulty. The wording also only mentions sight, not hearing nor scent, but it does say you can &quot;interact with&quot; the spirits of the penumbra, which suggests being able to touch them or hear them speak.<br /> <br /> This Gift means you don't require a reflective surface to reach the Umbra, and is particularly helpful to the blind. According to the Blind flaw you are at +1 difficulty to enter the Umbra when you find a reflective surface, as you can't actually see the reflection.<br /> <br /> With a Gnosis of 10 you'll see past the Gauntlet everywhere, which is pretty great, though getting there will cost you at least 70xp!<br /> <br /> The awareness granted by this Gift is worthy of a Green, on the assumption that you have half-decent Gnosis. If your Gnosis is 3 or lower this Gift will essentially be useless. At 5-6 it will function about half of the time. At 7+ it will be working most of the time. Even if you have a Gnosis of 4 or 5, being able to roll to see past the Gauntlet for a whole scene without becoming blind to the world around you is great, especially without a +3 difficulty hike or glowing eyes. So much activity in the Penumbra could catch you off guard without this Gift, and with it you'll be a huge asset to your team.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Umbral Camouflage:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis, no roll, and for a scene you are invisible to &quot;spiritual senses&quot; - this is also described as being &quot;undetectable&quot; to spirits. So you won't be seen, heard, scented or so on. Long range scanning would also likely fail.<br /> <br /> Attacking ends the Gift, of course.<br /> <br /> This Gift would be very helpful for exploring the Penumbra of most cities, sneaking into Caerns, ambushing Banes and so on. Note that there's nothing stopping you from enacting a Rite while this Gift is up, so long as it doesn't take longer than &quot;a scene&quot; (or if it does, you might want to spend an additional Gnosis to keep the Gift going). Your ST could be very mean and say that performing a Rite while under Umbral Camouflage doesn't work, as Rites by definition require the attention of the spirit world and you have made yourself invisible to it.<br /> <br /> Getting close enough to a Nexus Crawler to prepare a trap, or use Command Spirit or a Rite upon it, could make all the difference. You could also sneak into a location and activate your Battle Mandala, so long as your ST doesn't deem this to be an attack, and force spirits to remain to lose their essence or flee. You can also turn this on in an emergency to escape from a dangerous spirit, or to escape from a spirit who is spying on you, tailing you, and so on. Keep in mind that your allied spirits also won't know your location, which could lead to you dying in a ditch because your Pack's Totem couldn't locate you.<br /> <br /> This could also be very useful against Ghosts.<br /> <br /> I'm making this Green because while it is slightly niche (just slightly) it does its job perfectly, and if it's in-list for you it will be key to your job.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Web Walker*:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis, make a Charisma + Science roll (diff 7), and you get two benefits on a success. You won't physically struggle to navigate the Pattern Web, and you won't be treated as hostile by the various Weaver Spirits that travel along it. These benefits apply to your pack so long as they stay close. The Gift does not that the strands of the web might not lead where you wish to go; W20 describes it as &quot;labyrinthine&quot;. A Weaver Spirit with Airt Sense will navigate this web far better than any Theurge, notably, so commanding such a spirit to guide you (perhaps with some Chiminage to avoid upset if a more powerful spirit determines what you did) might be wise.<br /> <br /> The Corebook doesn't really get into what the Pattern Web looks like, how it manifests in the Penumbra, where it goes, how one would navigate it. W20 Umbra has this to say:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Anchorheads are the most reliable means of crossing to the Deep Umbra but they are not the only way. Beings knowledgeable of the Weaver\u2019s ways understand that the Pattern Web connects everything except the most chaotic Wyld reaches or most corrupt Wyrm blights. Every area that has a hint of structure or form has at least a few strands of the Weaver\u2019s influence that travelers can use as a thread to cross between worlds \u2014 if they can survive the Pattern Spiders who maintain it. </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Webs are bridges, Webs are circuits. Webs serve as gates, ladders, or bridges to the Weaver\u2019s deeper Umbral realms. A sufficiently powerful and motivated pack of Garou could fight through and climb the Web to reach Gaia-knows-where in the Weaver\u2019s Near Umbral strongholds. That sounds easier than it is. Every rung of that Web grabs, sticks, pulls, stings. Every few feet, another spider attacks or another monstrosity is ready to pull a werewolf in. Just being close to the Web sucks out a Garou\u2019s Gnosis, with the intent to render her an automaton \u2014 another cog in the machine. And in the end, all of that struggle only gets her as far as the door to an Umbral realm. A Web\u2019s other main purpose is that of a circuit, a free-flowing passageway for information flowing between the Weaver and Earth. Electrons flow, spiders carry, and the web reverberates with data constantly. For recon-minded Garou, Webs can provide dangerous but highly valuable information about the Weaver\u2019s machinations. With rerouted information and creative cryptography, Garou can suss out the Weaver\u2019s upcoming plans and projects. </div> </div> </div>There is much more written in the book, and I recommend reading it yourself! But suffice to say that having access to the Pattern Web is a pretty big deal, even if you have to learn how to navigate it well; those without the Gift have to roll Intelligence + Enigmas difficulty 7 to navigate it, possibly 8 or 9 if they're trying to avoid danger or find a particular piece of web. Presumably someone with the Gift will either pass this check automatically or roll it at much lower difficulty. If your ST runs the Pattern Web as it is written it is an incalculable resource.<br /> <br /> It is worth noting that if any part of the Wyrm is still imprisoned by the Weaver, it dwells within a piece of the Pattern Web, deep within the Umbra.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately this Gift does get a * due to the fact that the omnipresence and significance of the Pattern Web may vary. RAW it reaches all but the most Wyrm and Wyld controlled places in reality; it won't get you into the Dreaming now matter how hard you try, nor will it reach Malfeas, but you get the sense that it's easier to list the places it can't go than the places it can, since the latter list would be too long.<br /> <br /> ================================================== ====<br /> <br /> Web Walker would be worth 15xp to anyone who loves exploring the Umbra, especially a Glass Walker, but not exclusively. Any Lupus or Metis would likely benefit from Pulse of the Invisible, especially after buying up more Gnosis. Umbra Camouflage could be great on a Ragabash who wants to fully master stealth, or a Glass Walker / Bone Gnawer who wants to wander the streets of a city's penumbra without being harassed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-07T11:27:10-07:00","id":1514122,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1514101#post1514101\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><u><b>RANK 3 RAGABASH GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Gremlins:</b></u></span> You roll Manipulation + Intimidation, difficulty determined by complexity of the device (simpler = harder), and if you get 3+ successes you scare the spirit of the object into fleeing, permanently wrecking it.<br /> <br /> I like this a lot, despite there being far too many tech-destroying Gifts.<br /> <br /> There's no cost, no real risk, and the baseline use of the power doesn't demand you prance around hunting the object, you can give it a mean glare. The RP potential of terrifying someone's toaster to death is very funny, and I love the idea of a technician (especially a Nocker, Etherite etc.) puzzling over a piece of equipment that simply cannot work anymore, despite all laws of science suggesting it is in perfect condition post repairs.<br /> <br /> If you want a fun artefact in some collection, have a sharp knife that won't cut. Guests are invited to try it out using those gloves-through-the-barrier things.<br /> <br /> An interesting potential use of this Gift is to have your Theurge in the Penumbra ready to command or capture the spirit you've banished. Yes, the spirits living inside all objects are relatively weak in most cases, but you could find uses for them. If you need to get a large number of weak spirits, there are worse ways of generating ones no one will miss.<br /> <br /> Use this on the vehicles, tools and weapons of enemies, and if you run into a techno-mage or similar you'll have a field day. Outside of Primium countermagic dice, a HIT MARK has very few defenses against this power, and might be shut off permanently by you netting 3 successes on a difficulty 3 check.</div> </div> </div> </div>Countermagick that has to roll an 8 or higher doesn't really stand much of a chance of stopping it, though. Even if each success counts double, that's still an average of only 0.4 successes per die, with a far greater chance to botch with the meager dice pools typically available to countermagick (three, maybe four, typically). It's quite the risk to take, in all honesty.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-07T14:37:04-07:00","id":1514132,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1514122#post1514122\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Countermagick that has to roll an 8 or higher doesn't really stand much of a chance of stopping it, though. Even if each success counts double, that's still an average of only 0.4 successes per die, with a far greater chance to botch with the meager dice pools typically available to countermagick (three, maybe four, typically). It's quite the risk to take, in all honesty.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah I'm not liking their chances - I note that the defensive measure exists, but I'm not saying it's likely to actually save them at all. Even with 10 dice of countermagick and a meager pool from the Garou, it's very much stacked in their favour. And you only need one success to win the fight really - shutting them down temporarily gives you enough time to rip them to pieces or repeat the Gift.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-07T15:18:51-07:00","id":1514136,"title":"","content":"Something I wish Gremlins addressed, which would also get into how to handle it for cyborgs and such, is that it targets &quot;devices&quot; without really saying if it can isolate part of a system. While it's a bit nitpicky to say, &quot;You can use it on a computer, but not just the computer's monitor,&quot; it's worth a lot more consideration if you can target just the pacemaker, or if the human counts as the target and isn't valid as not being a &quot;device.&quot; And of course if &quot;device&quot; gets into things with true intelligence.<br /> <br /> Honestly, it's a Green to me even if your ST rules pretty harshly against the Gift on these things, but it's a Gift that's ripe for causing debates (damn Ragabash!) when you start getting clever with it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-08T03:23:32-07:00","id":1514179,"title":"","content":"By the way, an important rules matter: you spend Rage to get extra ACTIONS within the same TURN. If something takes a &quot;turn of concentration&quot; or a transformation takes a &quot;turn to complete&quot;, it won't be online until the turn has completed. You might run it differently at your table, knowingly or unknowingly, but that's how I'm interpreting the word &quot;turn&quot; just so people know.<br /> <br /> <u><b>RANK 3 PHILODOX GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Mental Speech:</b></u></span> See the Metis entry. Quite important to any role arguably, but suits the judges and lawkeepers quite well.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Scent of the Oathbreaker:</b></u></span> Budget Contract (C20). This Gift allows you to spend a point of Gnosis when a promise is sanctified. When that promise is broken by any party, you will know. The Gift implies that you will know which person broke the oath. This alone is helpful, and as an added bonus your difficulty to track their scent is reduced to 4. This is interesting, since it implies that otherwise impossible checks (beyond difficulty 10, depending on how you run difficulty thresholds) become possible; essentially if there's any chance at all of their scent being tracked, if it's even slightly feasible, it will now be difficulty 4 for you. Combined with Lupus form this means you will pick up the faintest trail imaginable, reducing the difficulty to 3, even if there is a +1 difficulty modifier from something such as rain.<br /> <br /> This Gift helps you to keep track of all sorts of things, from the loyalty of Sept officers to the integrity of Kinfolk and other Supernaturals, and whether a traitor or double agent is keeping to their word. In roleplay choosing not to use this Gift could be the ultimate sign of trust, or using it frequently could show how jaded a judge has become. One thing I've found invaluable in using Contract in C20 is its ability to make impossible arrangements possible. So many of the barriers and conflicts in the WoD are down to mistrust. Being able to say to the Elder Council &quot;you may find this arrangement worrisome, but if it is broken I shall know immediately&quot; could take the edge off of a difficult scenario. The ability to spare, the ability to trust, the ability to let people live is... well it's good.<br /> <br /> Following Exorcism of a Bane from a Fomor by a packmate you might well drop a &quot;swear to me you'll never tell anyone about this, and that you won't go looking for (corporation, cult) again, that you'll get on with your life as best you can and try to forget this ever happened&quot; - it's still risky, but more excusable.<br /> <br /> Overall I'm giving this Gift a Green given its unique ability to threaten and manipulate others into compliance, along with its ability to act as insurance. It's great for social control, for information gathering, for helping keep the session moving without you having to track and investigate the random NPC who promised they'd do something. All in all a great utility. When you combine this with Mental Speech... it's even better.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Sense Balance*:</b></u></span> Given that the Triat can be quite sneaky, and their influence can pop up in ways you wouldn't expect, and that there are spirits and locations which you could squint and see as one or the other, this could be very handy. There are Gaian/Wyld spirits who you could, reasonably, mistake for Weaver or Wyrm, especially when you get into the weirdest spirits and umbral locations which have only come into existence in the past 100 years or so.<br /> <br /> Gnosis cost makes sense, Perception + Enigmas difficulty 8, need to be free of distractions and at peace, and with that you can pick up the Triatic flavour of a location. Pretty handy, especially to provide information to your Theurge.<br /> <br /> There are a few things that count against this Gift however. Quite often an 'overabundance' of one of the Triat's influence will be very obvious indeed. There will also be many situations where such an overabundance just gets a shrug - it isn't directly immediately relevant to anything you're doing. Getting an objective set of eyes on that meter is great, but it's still the kind of information which could lure a pack down a very esoteric route when confronted by a very boring, ordinary problem.<br /> <br /> I'd invite you to think about a common Garou problem, assume the pack has Sense Wyrm, and then imagine how their response to investigating the location varies between &quot;has Sense Balance&quot; and &quot;doesn't have Sense Balance&quot;. If you're doing super weird super esoteric stuff constantly, this Gift might become more relevant. Add to that the fact it'll set you back 9/15xp... not looking amazing.<br /> <br /> I'm making this Amber because it provides something unique, so if you need to measure those energies and there's no other way for you to do it (using common sense and investigating, asking a spirit, relying on a Fetish, utilizing Sense Wyrm), this Gift could be worth it. The * indicates that in some games, and some Pack compositions and with some STs, this Gift could be red.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Weak Arm:</b></u></span> Analyzing an opponent, you make a Perception + Brawl difficulty 8. Requires a full turn of concentration. Successes can be split between bonus dice to attack or damage against the specific opponent analyzed.<br /> <br /> Keeping in mind that you must watch the opponent's fighting style, not their stance, not some spiritual aura or their body shape, specifically their fighting style, this Gift will be hard to activate before a fight. In a series of challenges or competitions you might see someone fight someone else before you get to them. If you're preparing to ambush a BSD who is slaughtering Gaian spirits or other allies, you could callously delay your assault to get the best measure of your opponent. But in a typical fight your opponent will only begin to show off their &quot;fighting style&quot; once the fight has started, and that means wasting the first turn of a fight on a high difficulty Perception + Brawl check. Though in Lupus it could be difficulty 6, notably.<br /> <br /> Assuming you get the Gift off before the fight starts... and have a pool of 8-10... and you have a difficulty of 6... and you manage to get 3-5 successes... is +3-5 dice against that target (divided between attacking and damaging at your preference) much good? It could be. It could be massively impactful against a ridiculously powerful spirit, or against a far more dangerous Garou combatant, say an Elder Ahroun. Given that you won't be able to get this off most of the time, however, unless you sacrifice your entire turn at the start of a fight (which could kill you), this one is tricky to rate.<br /> <br /> How often do you get a chance to watch your opponent fight before you fight them, in the same scene you fight them? You might arrive only to watch them murder an NPC who was fighting them, sure, or interrupt some slaughter, but you should be aiming to get the drop on your unprepared foes whenever possible. Short of having battles to fight in Atrocity Realm or happening upon a BSD who is already in Crinos having a great time, you won't be activating this gift outside of sparring and maybe, maybe more casual challenges done sequentially.<br /> <br /> One &quot;offensive&quot; use of this Gift is to have a party member Challenge and NPC, one who prefers fights when challenged, and use the scene to analyze their fighting style. Then challenge them immediately after; technically they can't refuse without losing face, even if they're tired.<br /> <br /> Given a combination of how hard it is to activate without sacrificing a whole turn, and the high difficulty, and the other limitations, and this being a 9/15xp Gift, I'm making this one a Red, which is a shame! At a lower level it absolutely would have been an Amber. And of course there's the risk that the foe flees, or someone else ganks them anyways, or it turns out there's a much bigger bad behind them... you get the idea.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Wisdom of the Ancient Ways*:</b></u></span> Obviously ancient lore is important to any role, but I would have pegged this as a more intellectual Galliard Gift. The Gift is also a little poorly written. The flavour suggests you can use the Gift to remember &quot;ancient lore&quot;, while the system talks about the &quot;answer&quot; you receive, which is more general. It feels like the Gift is missing another sentence which spells out exactly what sorts of information you can receive.<br /> <br /> I will read the flavour and description together and come to the conclusion that this Gift allows you to ask questions about the ancient lore of the Garou. More successes means a more precise answer. And of course the stronger your Ancestors rating the better this Gift will be; this Gift shall be very hard to use for Bone Gnawers and Glass Walkers, fittingly, and it's hard to rate given a difficulty curve of 4-9. Given there's no cost listed, no limit on how often you can use it, and no success table for how far back you reach, this Gift is hardly a Gift at all. It's just a vague suggestion of a Gift. STs will be stepping in with &quot;you don't get an answer&quot; &quot;that reaches too far back&quot; and all sorts of responses to questions like &quot;were the original Garou Kami&quot; or &quot;did the Children of Gaia actually end the Impergium&quot; or &quot;did any White Howlers in packs around the world survive the fall&quot; &quot;what happened to them&quot;, &quot;what is the secret of Erebus&quot; etc.<br /> <br /> Perhaps these questions don't seem too game breaking or perhaps setting-breaking, more importantly, but the Gift gives so little guidance that it might do everything or nothing.<br /> <br /> If it were better written there would be guidance on what category of questions might be asked, perhaps with an example, and an example of something you couldn't ask. You can do X, but you can't do Y is a method used by a lot of Gifts. It would also be helpful to have an outline of how far back you can reach, and how many successes are equivalent to what kind of answer, or what distance into the past, or how well hidden or secret the information is.<br /> <br /> Given that we're given next to no information and the ST is left to basically create the Gift almost from scratch, possibly to much frustration for them or the player, I'm giving this a red. The * is because it could be alright or even excellent depending on how the ST &quot;reads&quot; it, quotes given how little there is to read!<br /> <br /> ==================================================<br /> <br /> Sadly quite a few Gifts from this Rank upwards seem to have not been playtested or carefully edited, or well written. Furthermore many that would have been Amber at 1-2 are Red at 3+. Still, between Mental Speech and Oathbreaker you have a great set of tools to speak to people over a great distance about the promise they just shattered. Both Gifts could be worth purchasing out of list, especially Mental Speech.<br /> <br /> Sense Balance might be worth 15xp to a Theurge in a very esoteric game or setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-08T05:38:31-07:00","id":1514186,"title":"","content":"I read the wording on the difficulty of Scent of the Oathbreaker differently: Basically, I parse it as if the difficulty would be greater than 4, it &quot;drops&quot; to 4. I wouldn't let it then be modified further by the lupus form bonus or situational issues like rain. Minor, and I wouldn't change the rating for it, but I think it makes more sense with how it's phrased.<br /> <br /> I think a big part of the question of the worth of Sense Balance is that if your ST lets you take Sense Wyld and Sense Weaver from previous editions. If you ST lets these into the game, Sense Balance as written is fairly pointless. Theurges are the only group that used to be able to snag the trio for 9 XP total, and they rolled different Abilities, but IME the trio is more useful and as all Rank 1 Gifts don't really need any oomph like you'd hope Sense Balance gives you. <br /> <br /> Weak Arm feels like it should be a Ragabash Gift as written, where you have the stealth chops to stalk and watch an opponent to study them; even then it would be lackluster (maybe Amber?). What I really want out of the Gift to make it feel like a Philodox one would be to allow it to activate against any foe you've seen fight before, with a variable difficulty based on how well you've observed them, including the best difficulty for having fought them in the past. This would make it great for Philodox keeping Ahrouns in line, or to back up Scent of the Oathbreaker when you come in to stomp lawbreakers and traitors. That said, I think you're a bit hung up n the &quot;fighting style&quot; phrasing a bit.<br /> <br /> I would advocate for Wisdom of the Ancient Ways to be bumped up to Amber just to be in keeping with Sight from Beyond having the same basic issues of how variable any sort of &quot;The ST decides how much info you get&quot; is going to be. Or at least Red or Amber based on your access to Ancestors since difficulty 9 is steep for a Gift that's so unstructured, but rolling a bunch of successes at diff 4 should be getting you something useful. Even, &quot;no Ancestor spirits know anything about what you're meditating on,&quot; can be useful as that entails something unique or new to the extent that all the collective ancestor spirits of the Garou have nothing to offer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-08T06:07:58-07:00","id":1514191,"title":"","content":"Small note - I haven't mentioned Pure Breed that often in many of these reviews. Keep in mind that whether Pure Breed and Persuasion apply to a Gift's activation roll will depend on the context and the taste of the Storyteller; generally speaking PB1-5 is a noteworthy buff to many social Gifts, but YMMV.<br /> <br /> <u><b>RANK 3 GALLIARD GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Eye of the Cobra:</b></u></span> Roll Appearance + Enigmas, difficulty of the target's Willpower. I like the Enigmas Knowledge being used here. You're so good at puzzles that your eyes are extra <i>mysterious</i>. This Gift forces the target to come towards you; with 3 successes they are drawn to your side, 1-2 successes get them heading in the right direction.<br /> <br /> No cost, a neat effect, significantly overpriced at a 3rd level Gift... but not bad. Handy for a melee Garou trying to lure an enemy towards their pack. It's worth noting that there is no special means of resisting this Gift, outside of total immunity granted by effects such as Shell. Keep in mind that your target can do as they please once they reach you, and that they may well do something other than just approach you on their way to you, with 1-2 successes.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Song of Heroes*:</b></u></span> After reciting tales of ancient heroism (Does it have to be ancient? I would imagine many Garou, especially Glass Walkers, would rather give a more grounded and recent anecdote!) for a few minutes. Then the Galliard spends 2 Gnosis, and rolls Charisma + Performance (difficulty 8). Every two successes buys +1 to an Ability for all Garou and Kinfolk (Fera? Non-Garou Kinfolk?) listening to the tale. This bonus then lasts until sunrise, so up to about 24 hours.<br /> <br /> Or...<br /> <br /> Sunrise. Arctic circle... there are stranger places for Garou and Kinfolk to travel and fight. A months-long duration would be quite neat... and works RAW!<br /> <br /> Assuming your ST is sensible, it will last for up to 24 hours. Between the difficulty of the check, and the 2:1 ratio of buffing abilities, this Gift is hard to use. With the right Merits and Backgrounds it becomes much stronger; my pack's Galliard has Supporter, Enchanting Voice, Pure Breed, Persuasion, and in addition to this they could also have Ancestors if they'd bought it in char-gen. With a dice pool of 15-20 (achievable) and a difficulty of 3-4 (ditto) this Gift will pull MUCH more weight. If you manage to average about 12 successes you can add +6 to the Athletics scores of a whole sept, massively swinging a battle by making your side swifter, harder to hit and evade, and more besides. Other standouts would be Firearms, Brawl, Melee...<br /> <br /> But do keep in mind that this Gift isn't just for battles. Like many Gifts and mechanics in W20 the flavour suggests you're running into battle, but you could easily use this Gift before a huge crafting project, a hack attempt, a ritual, dance, anything you can imagine. Overall this Gift could be Amber or Green depending on your dice and difficulty modifiers. That's true of all Gifts, but most especially one like this. Hence the *.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Song of Rage:</b></u></span> Pick a target, roll Manipulation + Leadership (difficulty of WP), on a success they either fly into a berserk rage, or Frenzy if they are able to. This lasts for one turn per success; we aren't told much about what a human in a rage will do, but we can assume that it's a state just shy of a supernatural frenzy.<br /> <br /> This is a handy Gift to debilitate NPCs, humiliating them or potentially freeing them from control, or goading them into fighting. I don't have that much to say about this Gift beyond it being handy. The uses are all relatively obvious, once you get past using it on trapped or controlled allies, or a grievously wounded ally, or an ally who is refusing to fight enemies (though there is a risk they'll attack you if their Rage exceeds their Gnosis!).<br /> <br /> Edit: It's worth explicitly mentioning that this Gift is handy against a Pack, or multiple Packs, of Garou enemies. Even if their Gnosis exceeds their Rage (try and target the Homid Ahroun!) they are still at risk of attacking allies other than their Pack! If you use this on a group of BSD packs, or even indiscriminately in a Pit, you could cause a lot of friendly fire.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Song of the Siren*:</b></u></span> Charisma + Performance, difficulty equal to the Willpower rating of the relevant targets, comparing with each in turn. Targets who are effected can't act for one turn per success. Targets can spend a point of WP to act for a turn. Furthermore your Pack automatically resist.<br /> <br /> This is good... especially when you keep in mind that <u>Spirits do not have temporary Willpower points</u>, they only have dots in the Willpower trait. So RAW this is a hugely powerful anti-spirit power which can stun huge crowds of Banes. This combines well with Howl of the Unseen, the Fianna Gift. A Fianna Galliard could stun huge swarms of Banes from the physical world by singing, while their Pack slowly cut their way through them. In fact, combine with Call of the Wyrm... just make sure you have guards ready to deal with any Vampires or BSDs who show up in the physical world!<br /> <br /> Against dangerous physical targets this will consistently drain Willpower points, exhausting enemies swiftly. There is no cost and no limitation to how often you can use it.<br /> <br /> A problem arises when you consider that this power used two or three times can turn a whole crowd of enemies into drooling zombies who can't resist, as you apply the Gift over and over.<br /> <br /> I would limit this Gift so that effecting a target a second time requires expending a point of Gnosis. Or add a cost. Or mention that if your check &quot;botches&quot; against a target's Willpower when compared, they become immune.<br /> <br /> If this Gift is being used against you, deafen yourself, or destroy the source ASAP. You should have a few WP to spend until that's sorted.<br /> <br /> Overall my problem with this Gift is that it either does everything or nothing, which is either frustrating for the ST or the players. At least the party are immune, and there are worse written Gifts. It's more a design issue with W20's debilitating Gifts in general; they either stun or remove dice, with very little else on the table.<br /> <br /> ================================================== =========<br /> <br /> None of these stand out as obvious picks for non-Galliards; if you wanted these Gifts chances are you'd be a Galliard! Still, if you're playing a performance-heavy non-Galliard Song of Heroes would be an excellent addition.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-09-2023, 06:59 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-08T06:39:07-07:00","id":1514194,"title":"","content":"Given that the Garou basekit is a huge aspect of what makes them so powerful (if you compare splats, a starting Garou PC vs an advanced Garou PC are closer together than, say, a starting Troll vs an advanced Troll, Gangrel vs Gangrel, Akashayana vs Akashayana and so on), it's really important that some of the Ahroun Gifts genuinely advance your combat potential.<br /> <br /> <u><b>RANK 3 AHROUN GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Combat Healing:</b></u></span> Every turn if you want to regenerate you need to roll Stamina, difficulty 8. On a success you regenerate a point of lethal or bashing, on a failure you don't, on a botch you lose your regen for the whole scene. In many fights your regeneration will be critical. Even with a Stamina of 7-8 you will botch the Stamina check roughly 3-5% of the time!<br /> <br /> This Gift is boring, and you won't want to spend 9xp on it, but it's eating your vegetables. Ultimately it will come in handy, and not having to roll Stamina at such a high difficulty every round is a nice QOL upgrade. This makes your regeneration much more reliable, and I like that a lot. If you have this Gift it's in your best interests to ensure that there are no breaks or pauses in combat, as your foes will be relatively advantaged by a chance to catch their breath and freely regenerate, while you need no such breaks.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Heart of Fury*:</b></u></span> Roll Willpower, difficulty of your Permanent Rage, and for every 2 successes you increase the difficulty of rolls to Frenzy by 1. The 2:1 ratio makes this tight.<br /> <br /> Best case scenario you roll 8ish dice difficulty 5, get 4ish successes, and are at +2 difficulty to Frenzy. Given that you have 5 points of Rage, and let's say it's a full moon and you're in Crinos so difficulty 3 to frenzy (base 4, -1 from Crinos, -1 from moon matching Auspice, +1 from Rank 3), you increase that difficulty from 3 to 5. At difficulty 3 your chances of a Berserk Frenzy are 53.2%. At difficulty 5 your odds are 27.2%. So this isn't awful by any means.<br /> <br /> However if you ever increase your Rage, the difficulty to activate the Gift goes up. And as you increase your Rank your difficulty to Frenzy increases regardless. This Gift will wind up being obsoleted, essentially, and if you have even a single Homid or Child of Gaia packmate they can use a Gift to calm you. This is before you consider that you can immediately halt a normal Frenzy by spending a single point of Willpower as it starts, and wasting a turn. Or that the Gift makes you Frenzy at the end of the scene anyways (though it does let you spend a point of WP to stop it).<br /> <br /> Given that the Gift winds up obsoleted, and that the worse your Rage problem is the harder it is to use, despite you need it more rather than less, and that unless you're at risk of Thrall of the Wyrm you shouldn't have too much problem anways... unless you have low Willpower... which you need to roll well for this Gift anyways... yeah it's a bit of a complicated mess. It isn't awful, yet at 9xp it winds up Red. Know why? For the same cost you could increase your Willpower by 1. That's one Frenzy ignored per session, right there. You could even buy Calm Heart for 9xp, with the right justification!<br /> <br /> If you're rewriting this Gift, I'd suggest adjusting it. Make it so that while you are fighting, and letting out your rage, you need additional successes to Frenzy or enter Thrall. That fits the Ahroun flavour and role well, and means it contributes something semi-unique. It helps explain how a Rage 10 Ahroun could function well at Ranks 3-5, theoretically, as while they are fighting their threshold to Frenzy is relatively high.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Silver Claws:</b></u></span> Absolutely incredible. No cost, simply a Gnosis roll (diff 7). This does slow down your Rage attacks getting going, so try and do this before the fight.<ul><li>Your claw attacks are silver, meaning they ignore the soak pool of Garou in most forms entirely, override many defensive Gifts, and are resistant to many forms of healing and regeneration even compared to normal Aggravated damage.</li> <li>Your claw attacks deal Aggravated damage to all targets, potentially overriding any obscure features which downgrade damage from claws.</li> <li>You gain a point of Rage every single turn.</li> </ul>So long as you don't let your Rage exceed your Willpower, which should be easy as you can spend 3 points of Rage each turn, this is hugely useful. If you have a low Willpower, make sure your Rage pool is depleted before you activate this. In a long fight this Gift will ensure you can get at least one bonus action off each turn!<br /> <br /> Good uses include fighting Garou and Fera and generating huge amounts of Rage over time (including outside of battle) to spend on various Gifts. There's no reason a Stargazer Ahroun can't fuel Channeling with this Gift after all.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Wind Claws*:</b></u></span> Spend a point of Rage, ignore all armour the target is wearing for the rest of the turn. You could hit 3 times (main action + 2 rage actions) without a target having any special soak from their powerful armour. This is nice to have since it could remove up to 5 points of soak from a target, but it's potentially overkill. If you're going to be fighting Technocrats, Sidhe and Nockers, this could be a Green, but typically it will be Amber. Those few Fomori, BSDs and executives who are wearing some form of armour will be a tad easier to target, but even then the Gift lasts for a turn, not a scene. Simply bypassing the 5 dots of soak a target gets from their insane Fae/Matter/Fetish armour is a fun idea, and it will feel great when it does happen.<br /> <br /> If this Gift ignored all forms of bonuses outside of Stamina, from Fortitude to &quot;armour Gifts&quot; to Powers and the Armour Charm etc. then this would be much better.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-08T07:15:18-07:00","id":1514195,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1514186#post1514186\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I read the wording on the difficulty of Scent of the Oathbreaker differently: Basically, I parse it as if the difficulty would be greater than 4, it &quot;drops&quot; to 4. I wouldn't let it then be modified further by the lupus form bonus or situational issues like rain. Minor, and I wouldn't change the rating for it, but I think it makes more sense with how it's phrased.<br /> <br /> I think a big part of the question of the worth of Sense Balance is that if your ST lets you take Sense Wyld and Sense Weaver from previous editions. If you ST lets these into the game, Sense Balance as written is fairly pointless. Theurges are the only group that used to be able to snag the trio for 9 XP total, and they rolled different Abilities, but IME the trio is more useful and as all Rank 1 Gifts don't really need any oomph like you'd hope Sense Balance gives you.<br /> <br /> Weak Arm feels like it should be a Ragabash Gift as written, where you have the stealth chops to stalk and watch an opponent to study them; even then it would be lackluster (maybe Amber?). What I really want out of the Gift to make it feel like a Philodox one would be to allow it to activate against any foe you've seen fight before, with a variable difficulty based on how well you've observed them, including the best difficulty for having fought them in the past. This would make it great for Philodox keeping Ahrouns in line, or to back up Scent of the Oathbreaker when you come in to stomp lawbreakers and traitors. That said, I think you're a bit hung up n the &quot;fighting style&quot; phrasing a bit.<br /> <br /> I would advocate for Wisdom of the Ancient Ways to be bumped up to Amber just to be in keeping with Sight from Beyond having the same basic issues of how variable any sort of &quot;The ST decides how much info you get&quot; is going to be. Or at least Red or Amber based on your access to Ancestors since difficulty 9 is steep for a Gift that's so unstructured, but rolling a bunch of successes at diff 4 should be getting you something useful. Even, &quot;no Ancestor spirits know anything about what you're meditating on,&quot; can be useful as that entails something unique or new to the extent that all the collective ancestor spirits of the Garou have nothing to offer.</div> </div> </div> </div>First point makes sense.<br /> <br /> I'm aware that Sense Wyld and Sense Wyrm existed in old editions, and they ARE in W20, but only as Fera Gifts; I believe Ratkin get a Sense Weaver, and some group get a Sense Wyld. So these Gifts existing for Garou is at least feasible, but you might need to discover, rediscover or link up with Fera to learn them etc. And absolutely, if you have access to those Gifts, Sense Balance becomes even more of an expensive waste of time.<br /> <br /> I agree that Weak Arm has some Ragabash vibes! Your suggested changes make sense and line up with some of my thoughts; having studied the foe in the past would suit the &quot;judge and executioner&quot; role of the Auspice quite well. RE the 'fighting style' thing, I read it as meaning you need to get a glimpse of how the target fights, in the scene, before you activate the gift. So you can't just stare at a BSD standing with their arms crossed. Seeing them firing a gun would be enough, it isn't super strict, but it makes it very hard to activate before a fight starts.<br /> <br /> Sight from Beyond is vague, but it's intentionally vague; you get visions from the Storyteller. Since it's ultimately decently well written, and the question of whether it's useful is down to whether the ST gives Theurges visions regardless of whether they have the Gift or not (I know many STs will give a rank 1 Theurge visions to prompt missions), a base of Amber, but could be Red at your table, feels fair. Wisdom of the Ancient Ways is in a similar situation, absolutely, but it's meant to be something you activate at will and the wording give us next to no idea of how that should work. It could be Amber, or Green, depending on context, but Red feels like a reasonable baseline given how little you have to work with. With so little concrete info you could easily make it Amber, sure, but the rating is almost meaningless. You can imagine it as &quot;unrated&quot; if that helps, in fact I might even change it to &quot;Black&quot; when I edit, i.e. no rating given.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-08T15:35:49-07:00","id":1514248,"title":"","content":"It might fall under &quot;obvious uses,&quot; and I'm not sure it really changes the overall value of the Gift, but I'd point out that Song of Rage is great for ruining attacks be multiple enemies. If you're being attacked by a BSD Pack and other Wyrm forces, even if they have enough Gnosis to not attack their packmates, they might take out some fomori for you. If you're facing multiple BSD packs, you have a decent chance of a tactic I call &quot;Frenzy lock,&quot; where you send one enemy pack's heaviest hitter at their own allied pack. A worst, both enemy packs are disrupted for a bit trying to deal with that and your allies counter attack, at best your target hits one of their own allies hard enough that they roll to Remain Active... sending them into a Frenzy as well, and potentially sending off a chain of Frenzies within their own Ranks if they don't have enough characters with the ability to shut it down before it starts.<br /> <br /> It's still very niche, but one of my PCs killed six enemies with on roll via a Frenzy chain between three packs thinking that just coming up 20 strong as one group meant they didn't need better strategic thinking.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-09T03:58:31-07:00","id":1514291,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1514248#post1514248\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It might fall under &quot;obvious uses,&quot; and I'm not sure it really changes the overall value of the Gift, but I'd point out that Song of Rage is great for ruining attacks be multiple enemies. If you're being attacked by a BSD Pack and other Wyrm forces, even if they have enough Gnosis to not attack their packmates, they might take out some fomori for you. If you're facing multiple BSD packs, you have a decent chance of a tactic I call &quot;Frenzy lock,&quot; where you send one enemy pack's heaviest hitter at their own allied pack. A worst, both enemy packs are disrupted for a bit trying to deal with that and your allies counter attack, at best your target hits one of their own allies hard enough that they roll to Remain Active... sending them into a Frenzy as well, and potentially sending off a chain of Frenzies within their own Ranks if they don't have enough characters with the ability to shut it down before it starts.<br /> <br /> It's still very niche, but one of my PCs killed six enemies with on roll via a Frenzy chain between three packs thinking that just coming up 20 strong as one group meant they didn't need better strategic thinking.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's actually great to hear about! Honestly it's mad that I didn't explicitly mention such a use - it was in the back of my head but it's very pertinent. It's one of those very swingy things where if you get lucky you could do insane damage to a Pit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-09T07:48:04-07:00","id":1514306,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 3 BLACK FURY GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Coup de Grace:</b></u></span> Spend a WP. Roll Perception + Brawl, difficulty of the target's Stamina + Athletics. If you're successful you double the damage dice pool of your next attack on the target. Even with an extremely large dice pool or a source of guaranteed damage this will significantly increase your lethality against high Essence spirits and enemies with absurd soak or health pools.<br /> <br /> Given that Crinos foes will have a Stamina + Athletics of 10+ this Gift can be hard to use. But if your foe is, say, a Spirit... their &quot;Stamina + Athletics&quot; will be their Willpower trait!<br /> <br /> This is a very solid power, and since it doesn't take a full turn of concentration or cost or roll Gnosis, you can use it and then do an attack using a Rage action. Doubling your damage dice pool of a claw attack, or a melee weapon, or a gun, could be amazing. The one-shot potential of this Gift makes it a Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Heart Claw:</b></u></span> Spend a point of WP after making a successful claw attack. The claw you implant will dig deeper into them, inflicting 1 point of unsoakable lethal damage each turn until they are dead or the claw is removed. A full turn of Dexterity + Medicine roll, difficulty 7, removes the claw.<br /> <br /> This Gift is great on a hit and run, especially if you are harassing the target from ranged with bows or guns. It's also handy against a target with insane soak, as it will counter their regen... which you can shut off as well with another BF Gift! Losing 1 health or essence each turn will have a big impact on a target.<br /> <br /> Given that this Gift has no cost in terms of the action economy, and you can realistically use it once per round (typically I don't let players spend WP more than once in a given round) you could use it repeatedly on a single enemy.<br /> <br /> This would combo very nicely with Silver Claws if you have it, making the damage Aggravated to Garou and most Fera (most likely) and potentially making it more hazardous to remove. A pack of Black Furies would also be nearly unparalleled in taking down a dangerous foe. Technically speaking RAW you only need to successfully hit your foe, not deal damage!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Visceral Agony:</b></u></span> Spend a Rage before attacking. Any wound penalties suffered from attacks you launch during that turn will be doubled for the scene, and resistant foes will suffer regular penalties instead. Turning a -1 into a -2 is neat, a -2 into a -4 is good, a -5 to a -10 is incredible. Note that spirits don't have wound penalties however!<br /> <br /> Be careful if your foe might be drawing strength from pain before you use this Gift.<br /> <br /> One downside of this Gift is that your tactic should be to destroy your foe, not to leave them wounded, but of course you won't always kill someone in a single round, especially if you only attack 2 times in the round or they're very tough. So weakening their ability to fight back or dodge is good.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Wings of Pegasus*:</b></u></span> You can sprout Pegasus wings in Hispo form which fly at 50mph. It has no mechanical downsides in combat, it costs Gnosis, and it's great for fights and exploration. Separating one foe from the rest with a grapple, or providing ranged support with Gifts and the Taunt maneuver, could work well. It'll depend on your Auspice. If your foes lack ranged options, you can use this to get a moment to regenerate, activate buffing Gifts, await allies. It's also worth noting that this Gift removes a significant weakness of the Garou - you're better in close quarters, but many enemies will try to keep away. Quickly ascend a tower to fight a sniper, fly up to beat up a cheeky Corax, take to the skies to hunt down a pack of flying Fomori.<br /> <br /> If you need to flee there are Garou who will outspeed you, but a running Lupus can only leap so far. Head straight up and then fly over obstacles.<br /> <br /> It's worth noting that if you're reliant on Melee, you've forsaken HIspo as a fighting form for the most part. On top of that flying around in Hispo does come with risks; no Delirium to influence the memories of human onlookers, images or recordings of you won't be protected by Delirium's secondary effects, and so on. Within locations with humans this Gift's mobility will be far more useful if you have an appropriate Gift or Fetish, of which there are many. A Ragabash could use Blur of the Milky Eye, a Metis could use Camouflage, anyone else could use a Nyx's Bangle or Monkey Puzzle, just to name a few options.<br /> <br /> Even if the Gift winds up restricted to being used in the Umbra and deep wilderness, and in fights where you don't need to hide your true nature, it is definitely worth 9xp.<br /> <br /> ================================================== ==================<br /> <br /> What on earth... I'm aware that lore-wise the Black Furies are considered by some, including the BSDs, to be the truest representation of what Gaia wanted the Garou to be, but god these are good!<br /> <br /> Part of what's great about Coup, Heart Claw and Visceral Agony is that you can use 2/3 of them on the same turn. There's no reason you can't use Visceral Agony and Heart Claw on the same turn, or Visceral and Coup. They use up resources, but they apply them to do quite effective things you'd otherwise struggle with.<br /> <br /> Coup could be worth 15xp to a combat-focused PC. Heart Claw would suit many Ragabash, and any PC who struggles to land kills and might benefit from hit and run tactics. Visceral Agony will best support a character who is able to damage foes consistently, but finds they often don't die within one round. And of course Wings of the Pegasus is... possibly worth the 15xp. It depends on your priorities and whether you have another source of flight.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-10-2023, 04:29 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-09T12:08:48-07:00","id":1514329,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure where it falls into considerations, but Coup de Grace is rarely taken in my experience because by Rank 3 getting your hands on a Fang Dagger shouldn't be hard, and Fang Daggers tend to perform better as one-hit weapons (doubling your damage after the roll is statistically going to be better than doubling your damage dice by a smidge, and it's always diff 6 to activate if you want to roll it, regardless of the target's stats). It's not a bad Gift, but it's got at least serious competition from a Fetish weapon you can start with. I think if it had a set difficulty instead of a variable one, it would be way more desirable as the effect doesn't scale with successes.<br /> <br /> Wings of Pegasus also has a serious downside of having to be a flying monster-wolf. In the physical world, the more useful flight would be, the more you'd be risking the Veil with this Gift. In the Umbra, flight is useful, but there's other ways to get it, and you have to weigh how any spirits might react to you swooping into their territory in a combat form because you decided to fly instead of some other option.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-09T13:33:41-07:00","id":1514335,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1514329#post1514329\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure where it falls into considerations, but Coup de Grace is rarely taken in my experience because by Rank 3 getting your hands on a Fang Dagger shouldn't be hard, and Fang Daggers tend to perform better as one-hit weapons (doubling your damage after the roll is statistically going to be better than doubling your damage dice by a smidge, and it's always diff 6 to activate if you want to roll it, regardless of the target's stats). It's not a bad Gift, but it's got at least serious competition from a Fetish weapon you can start with. I think if it had a set difficulty instead of a variable one, it would be way more desirable as the effect doesn't scale with successes.<br /> <br /> Wings of Pegasus also has a serious downside of having to be a flying monster-wolf. In the physical world, the more useful flight would be, the more you'd be risking the Veil with this Gift. In the Umbra, flight is useful, but there's other ways to get it, and you have to weigh how any spirits might react to you swooping into their territory in a combat form because you decided to fly instead of some other option.</div> </div> </div> </div>At my table the Fang Dagger specifically is banned - also activating the dagger is a Gnosis roll, and thus impacts your ability to do additional attacks.<br /> <br /> The wings being limited to Hispo is an annoying restriction, but the kiting opportunities in supernaturally obvious fights (Fomori, BSDs, manifested Banes etc.) are pretty great. The leg up from Spirit of the Bird is that you don't suffer any penalties, though of course the form restriction is indeed annoying! Thankfully it's arguably the best form to fight in. The veil implications are bothersome, but it's also not impossible you'll have a Gift or Fetish allowing you to hide as you fly by this point either.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-09T13:59:17-07:00","id":1514336,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1514335#post1514335\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <b>At my table the Fang Dagger specifically is banned </b>- also activating the dagger is a Gnosis roll, and thus impacts your ability to do additional attacks.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a houserule, you should evaluate the gifts / merits / fetishes as they are stated for general usage.*<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1514329#post1514329\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure where it falls into considerations, but Coup de Grace is rarely taken in my experience because by Rank 3 getting your hands on a Fang Dagger shouldn't be hard, and Fang Daggers tend to perform better as one-hit weapons (doubling your damage after the roll is statistically going to be better than doubling your damage dice by a smidge, and it's always diff 6 to activate if you want to roll it, regardless of the target's stats). It's not a bad Gift, but it's got at least serious competition from a Fetish weapon you can start with. I think if it had a set difficulty instead of a variable one, it would be way more desirable as the effect doesn't scale with successes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agree with you.<br /> <br /> A starting character could have 12 dice of damage where any success is doubled (Strength 5, Crinos form, <i>Bear</i> Totem, <i>Polar Bear</i> Sept Totem, \u200ba Strength specialty on Daggers, and a <i>Fang Dagger</i>).\u200b That would be not unusual at all, and would be more reliable for the start of the game than waiting to get a Rank 3 gift that might not even be possible with the target difficulty that some creatures would have.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> A quick check of the <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1OK1pf7N3tIoPYYhX0kL37s-awQ0owwKNZlopwZ4EjHI/edit#gid=0\" target=\"_blank\">OWOD probability calculator</a> at difficulty 6 on 12 dice with specialty, gives us 6.2 successes, rounding down to 6 and duplicating it makes a nice 12 damage before soak, enough to one hit kill most opponents, and hurt significantly those that do not die from the hit.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> *IF you want to check the math of the theoretically optimal ways to hit (Brawl, Melee) and to do damage (Brawl, Melee), please check this <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1436799-w20-enhancing-the-pack-optimal-initial-char-gen-pack?p=1442413#post1442413\">post</a> (it also has a Soak calculation).<br /> <br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4967,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","user":"lbeaumanior","date":". 06-09-2023, 05:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-09T14:07:57-07:00","id":1514337,"title":"","content":"I think not accounting for Fang Daggers because of a house rule would at least deserve a * or something because that's not something like a lot of people finding the declaration phase of combat tedious. Coup de Grace only affects your next attack, so using one action (and your WP for that turn) to maybe activate it, and then have to wait for your Rage actions to use it that turn, is pretty harsh on the action economy for a non-Gnosis option. If you're going for a bunch of Rage attacks, you can activate a Gift will at least last the whole turn, or the whole fight, for more damage per-hit over all those attacks. Trying to get one big hit that takes down an enemy is better for low-Rage characters that can't do lots of damage by spamming attacks.<br /> <br /> It feels kinda cheap to turn around and say that a Wings of Pegasus isn't so bad because you might have a Rank 4 Fetish (Monkey Puzzle is the only official one that could start to cover up a flying wolf-monster), or have bought enough stealth Gifts to avoid problems (esp. since the Black Furies don't get much stealth in their Gift lists).<br /> <br /> Kitting via flight is cool in theory, but not well supported in the rules. It's not like Hispo is a good form for making ranged attacks while you fly around, so having to close in to make attacks is just leaving yourself open to getting smacked: you fly in, you attack, and the earliest you can fly back away is going to be in your Rage actions after any enemies that haven't gone yet have a chance to attack you. It's better for flying around using buff/debuff Gifts as a support tactic to try and keep away from the enemy while you're at it. And you're making yourself a prime target either way, as plenty of Wyrm enemies don't mind using guns and what-not.<br /> <br /> Edit: Also, in terms of kitting potential, remember that Garou are insanely fast without Gifts as it is. 50 mph sounds impressive... until you remember that with a base Dex of 5, you can get to 56 mph max speed in lupus with just shifting forms. Sure, flying means less terrain and obstacles to have to deal with in general, but in terms of combat, you're sitting in the middle of hispo and lupus at a full run.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":617,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","user":"Heavy Arms","date":". 06-09-2023, 05:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-10T01:17:11-07:00","id":1514422,"title":"","content":"It's worth noting that there's no reason you can't combine Coup with the effect of a Fang Dagger for even more damage - furthermore unless you have a Gift like Hand Blade or perhaps even Fetish Fetch, investing in Melee over Brawl is building on shifting sand. Even without considering Gifts which disable weapons or technology (though a dagger would be higher difficulty as it's very simple technology), there are threats of being disarmed (though this being done with the Disarm maneuver is extremely unlikely outside of Homid), having your weapon arm trapped, having to carry a weapon on your person, being caught out in a place like a sauna or beach, foes pickpocketing you or snatching your weapon, and so on.<br /> <br /> Not to say that the Fang Dagger is bad - it's very potent. Doubling your damage output is great. Which is why Coup de Grace is Green; because that's a very strong effect. The effect existing in another instance elsewhere doesn't really impact its balance given that the effects stack, and especially since Brawl is the bread and butter of many Garou, and is far better supported by Gifts. I'd add a * and say 'if you have a fang dagger this may be lower priority, but this also doubles your damage output there as well' if it pleased people, but by that logic I might as well add a * to Razor Claws and say 'don't take this if you fight with melee over brawl'.<br /> <br /> If your ST has let you purchase a weapon which consistently oneshots dangerous enemies in character generation, with low difficulty to attack, for a handful of Freebies, chances are they will have escalated to spirit enemies with insane Essence pools, or enemies with insane soak and health pools, so in <i>that</i> arms race doubling your damage again will be great.<br /> <br /> When I say &quot;A Gift or Fetish&quot; I mean any appropriate Gift or a Fetish, not Monkey Puzzle. Wings of the Pegasus has more utility if you have anything that helps you hide as you fly around, from Nyx's Bangle to Chameleon or Blur of the Milky Eye, or hell even fog cover from Curse of Aeolus (while the Gift isn't great, it does cover a city block and would leave someone who swears they saw a giant wolf with pegasus wings being treated like they're insane or high). That said I'll edit the section on Wings and add more context!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-10T02:18:13-07:00","id":1514435,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 3 BONE GNAWER GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Call the Rust:</b></u></span> Whistle, spend a point of Gnosis, a mass of metal of your choice rusts into total disrepair or even collapse if you succeed on a Wits + Crafts. The difficulty curve here is a little annoying however. Difficulty 6 for a knife, which is very small, difficulty 8 for a car, which is big but not huge. It suggests that a huge piece of machinery would be impossible, which annoys me! It lacks the totality of certain Gifts. Remember those summer days, reviewing the level 1 Gifts, remarking that they could stop nukes due to the absolute way they're worded? Good times.<br /> <br /> It's just very conservative.<br /> <br /> The ability to total somebody's car mid chase is fun, as is reducing a fancy silver-firing gun to rust. But at this point we have so many Gifts that disable technology... a Gift which specifically destroys metal objects isn't much advance. Almost anything you can achieve with this Gift you can achieve with another, better Gift. In theory this Gift could destroy a Klaive, but disabling a Klaive is already possible with Jam Weapon and Jam Technology, and Gremlins could in theory destroy such an object as well.<br /> <br /> They should have avoided this sort of thing by giving a variant - for instance the Bone Gnawers could get a variant of Jam Technology or Gremlins which is slightly more effective against metal objects (add a die, -1 difficulty etc.), but leaves the object distinctively tarnished, rusted, and so on.<br /> <br /> I'm giving this a red because at rank 3 you have both Gifts and the necessary general Garou talents to destroy a car. You don't need a weird off-brand tech-destroying Gift which only works on metal objects. If the difficulty curve were kinder, say a handheld object were difficulty 4, a car were difficulty 6, a ship were difficulty 10, I'd find it much more exciting.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Gift of the Skunk:</b></u></span> Dexterity + Primal-Urge, difficulty 7. No cost. If you hit a target with your stink attack (within 10 foot range) they must spend a point of WP to act at all on their turn. On subsequent turns they suffer a penalty of 1 die for every 2 successes you rolled.<br /> <br /> So... a 2 dice penalty. Even with 12 dice (the most you'll have) that's the average; 4 successes.<br /> <br /> Even when we keep in mind that spirits can't spend WP, you'll find yourself at the mercy of the ST when it comes to questions like &quot;do Nexus Crawlers have a sense of smell&quot; - I can't imagine this Gift working on many Banes to be quite honest.<br /> <br /> Focusing on physical targets, a point of WP isn't that bad a cost to avoid being stunned, and a penalty of 1 or 2 dice is almost negligible. There are far better Gifts you can use; the advantage of this one is that it has no cost, however you'll find Odious Aroma is a far better debuff all in all. It's also very weak for a level 3 Gift. Hell, many dangerous foes will simply dodge. Your spray is difficulty 7 to hit, their roll to dodge will be better on average.<br /> <br /> Given the potential to repeatedly spray a foe until they're exhausted and stunned, I'm going to give this an Amber. It wouldn't be one I'd leap to buy for 9xp.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Gift of the Termite:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis, roll Intelligence + Crafts (difficulty 7), and you can cause paper and wood to rot, get eaten away and collapse. At five successes you can collapse a roof.<br /> <br /> The issue with this is that even with 10 dice you have a 23% chance to get your Gift to do something impressive. The rest of the time you will have wasted some Gnosis on infesting a building with small bites in its wooden support beams. Destroying documents, furniture and so on is neat, but walls aren't often made primarily of wood; supporting beams are important, but the wall itself will be plaster, drywall, insulation.<br /> <br /> Even if you broaden the Gift up to include fabric (carpet, clothing), insulation, plants, it's so weird and narrow. At Rank 3 I expect my major nemesis to be a corporation or sept, not a chair, document or rooftop. And perhaps that's a little unfair, but this Gift is just so strange at Rank 3. Even at 1 it would be odd.<br /> <br /> I'm going to go ahead and give this a Red. At lower levels it might have been Amber.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Laugh of the Hyena*:</b></u> A pseudo-passive effect where you get some resistance to mind-altering effects so long as you laugh at loud at them. This is good flavour, however a +2 difficulty penalty which is conditional upon you knowing you're being placed under some kind of control is... tricky. Dominate, mind-altering Gifts and Powers and Charms, Sovereign and so on, many of these abilities will sometimes be used in ways where you don't realize you're under control. We aren't given clear guidance on how or when you might recognize such an ability being applied, and what kind of grace period you have to laugh at it. A Bane whispering through the Gauntlet that you should do vile things, triggering your worst impulses, may go entirely unnoticed. A subtle command from a leech might also not be recognized.<br /> <br /> How this Gift is run will be critical to how good it is. If your ST allows you to recognize you're being controlled, laugh it off and increase the difficulty of the effect... well then you're increasing the difficulty of a power after you recognize the power is being used on you. which intuitively be after the roll. See the problem? So to me it is implicit in the power, i.e. you sort of need to run it this way for the power to work at all, that you will recognize incoming attempts at mental control and get an opportunity to laugh in their face. Basically this Gift could be a handy protection, or impossible to use.<br /> <br /> Now let's imagine you always get this to activate. And say your WP is 5 - pretty average. And your foe is hitting you with a 6 dice power which has a difficulty of your Willpower. Their odds of controlling you would fall from 93% to 80%. So this Gift will be best when your WP is high anyways, or when the power targeting you has a high base difficulty (WP+3, a combination of an Attribute and Ability). Going from difficulty 8 to difficulty 10 is hugely significant.<br /> <br /> To sum up, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\">Red</span> if the power doesn't work (and it might not), <span style=\"color:#f39c12\">Amber</span> if it works and the incoming powers will tend to be middling difficulty (WP of 4-6 etc.), <span style=\"color:#27ae60\">Green</span> if you have high WP regardless, as it will tip an effect from hard to impossible.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Reshape Object:</b></u></span> See the Homid entry.<br /> <br /> ================================================== =======<br /> <br /> None of these are really worth buying off-list. If you want to be a skunk chances are you're already playing a Bone Gnawer; it's a very specific kind of flavour, even if you like the mechanic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-10T06:33:13-07:00","id":1514460,"title":"","content":"<u><b>RANK 3 CHILDREN OF GAIA GIFTS</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Calm the Savage Beast:</b></u></span> See the Homid entry. Suits the tribe very well, and given my CoG is a Metis I'm very glad this made the list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Dazzle*:</b></u></span> Roll Charisma + Empathy, difficulty of the target's WP, +1 if they are a &quot;creature of the Wyrm&quot; - which presumably includes Fomori and BSDs, along with Banes, but not a typical Pentex employee. On a success the target is stunned for the whole scene as they become overwhelmed with love for Gaia, standing there in mute awe until the Gift wears out or they are attacked.<br /> <br /> The Gift can only be attempted against a target once per scene, but it has a lot of amazing uses.<ul><li>When it wears off, reapply it to keep someone perpetually frozen until help arrives.</li> <li>Show a servant of the Wyrm what Gaia's love feels like, as a first step on the road to redemption.</li> <li>Stun an enemy so that the whole Pack can prepare a coordinated strike. Note that Unicorn may not appreciate this one.</li> <li>Remove a dangerous enemy, forcing their allies to attack them to get them to wake up.</li> <li>Stun a Garou, then adorn them with silver until they can't step sideways and place them in restraints for arrest.</li> <li>Prevent a fight by using this power on both participants.</li> <li>Remove someone from a conversation when they prove to be a distraction or are only serving to aggravate the discussion.</li> <li>Freeze a Frenzying Garou or Fera.</li> <li>Remind spirits of their ultimate duty to Gaia.</li> <li>Freeze the members of an enemy pack one by one.</li> <li>Win most challenges where &quot;no Gifts&quot; is not a stipulation.</li> </ul>It's worth keeping in mind that experiencing this Gift could prove lifechanging to many NPCs, and also doesn't sound like an unpleasant experience either. Also consider that because you don't spend or roll Gnosis, you could easily freeze a redeemable enemy in their tracks, then use 1-3 Rage actions to tear apart all the Scrags or Banes they brought with them.<br /> <br /> Placing a * just in case this Gift winds up in the 'so good that it's bad' camp. If you find this Gift is too powerful, consider adding a Gnosis cost.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Lover's Touch:</b></u></span> Very poorly named - I get what they're going for, but my Metis is not going to be calling it Lover's Touch in-universe. Gaia's or Mother's Embrace might work well.<br /> <br /> Regardless, this Gift unlocks a few new things. It heals Bashing and Lethal with Intelligence + Medicine, so if your Medicine &gt;&gt;&gt; your Empathy, this Gift will potentially be very effective in healing non-Aggravated damage. Note that the difficulty is either the Rage or Willpower, whichever is higher, giving Mother's Touch a significant edge. This means that Lover's Touch being superior for healing will be a very rare edge case; the difficulty will often be higher, and it doesn't work on Aggravated.<br /> <br /> What's new are the following: healing Essence, healing Willpower, and suppressing a Derangement for a full day per success. The ability to heal a spirit is niche, and won't often come up, but being able to restore a Totem or Guardian or similar to their full power after they reform in a weaker state, or healing a powerful spirit so it can win a battle, or restoring a spirit's Essence so it can unleash a powerful Charm... they're all handy. Healing the Willpower of allies is a good use of Gnosis if you are confident you can roll well on the check.<br /> <br /> The real standout is healing a Derangement. Being able to free a Garou from crushing depression or psychosis, for instance, could be hugely impactful. The storytelling potential of a Pack of Garou who respect their Alpha because they're a Child of Gaia who helped them on the long road to recovery from PTSD or similar could be really cool. There's also nothing stopping you from pulling a BSD or a spirit from madness with this Gift. If your character has a Rage of 1 or 2 this Gift could help them to be an exceptional therapist, psychologist or psychiatrist<br /> <br /> The big drawbacks of this power are it costing Gnosis, even if you only intend to heal lethal/bashing, and it having a difficulty of the higher of Rage/Willpower. This means someone with a large Willpower pool is harder to be healed of their tiredness or their mental illness!<br /> <br /> Ultimately this Gift provides a lot of new utility which Mother's Touch lacks, but it's by no means a replacement. It could be worth 9xp if healing spirits, restoring Willpower and suppressing madness are things you're likely to need.<br /> <br /> If I were adjusting this Gift I would simply adjust it to be an upgrade of Grandmother's Touch, though at that point it would be a total rewrite of the Gift. Basically it would make it so that the Gift can restore Willpower, Essence and suppress derangements, and the system would remain the same in terms of determining difficulty (Current Rage, or 5 if the target lacks Rage).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Spirit Friend:</b></u></span> This Gift's flavour is nice; you project peace and tranquility, and this inspires non-Bane spirits to treat you with chivalry and courtesy. Given that I can't imagine Great Fenris is particularly impressed by tranquility, I imagine this is a supernatural override of sorts; Gaia exists above all of these spirits and ultimately her love and compassion is impressive to all spirits, including Weaver Drones and explosive Wyldlings. Note that based on the flavour of the Gift it likely effects the disposition of spirits; 3 successes with this Gift turned on might have a different result to 3 successes without this Gift, based on how the ST imagines the spirit will behave under its effects.<br /> <br /> Charisma + Empathy, difficulty 7, and every success you roll grants you +1 die to &quot;interacting with&quot; non-Bane spirits for the rest of the scene. Optimally you would average at about +3 dice from this Gift. What's slightly bothersome is that +3 dice to social rolls with spirits for the scene, even without a cost, isn't that great in and of itself. It could help you to activate certain Gifts, or perhaps would be a great boon if used before summoning a spirit with a Rite, and it might help significantly sometimes... but additional dice are a bit fickle. Difficulty breaks are where it's at.<br /> <br /> If your ST reads into the flavour of this Gift you'll find it pulls more weight. The pure mechanics are pretty average, and overall lean quite niche. Given that you'll likely only take this if you're big into Rites and social stuff with spirits, it's a pretty solid amber, and it does increase the potential of the Tribe's Theurges somewhat.<br /> <br /> ================================================== =====<br /> <br /> Interestingly, if you combine these Gifts you have a great &quot;redeem a corrupted spirit/garou&quot; starter pack. A BSD can be frozen with an overwhelming sense of Gaia's love, have their Derangements silenced, and come around to find themselves besides a campfire holding a cup of tea. &quot;Ah, you're finally awake - how are you feeling?&quot; - quite the combo given you might be free from those damned whispers for the first time in your life. Is it a good idea? Depends. But is it right to try? Yes.<br /> <br /> Similar story with a corrupted nature spirit or similar - Spirit Friend makes a great follow-up to Dazzle there.<br /> <br /> Dazzle is easily worth 15xp, but it's one of these Gifts that a Spirit should be very restrictive with; does Unicorn truly, truly trust that you will use the Gift for good? Proving that, earning that trust, would be a great story in itself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-10T06:35:16-07:00","id":1514462,"title":"","content":"Something to note for how I think the books intend Laugh of the Hyena to be run: Garou are supposed to be allowed to roll Perception + Primal-Urge as a vague warning of supernatural shenanigans; not as good as Awareness is generally written in Mage, but enough to provide a reason for a Gnawer to laugh as their hairs raise on edge as they can tell something more than talking is going on. The write-up for Primal-Urge calls this an instinctive response, which implies something reflexive, and thus if successful would let Garou try to toss up a defensive Gift/etc. (not just for Laugh of the Hyena but not that many Gifts fall into needing to rely on this).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-12T10:35:26-07:00","id":1514718,"title":"","content":"A great use of Create Element completely slipped my mind: filling your own lungs when stuck underwater, or holding your breath for any reason. Similarly you could fill your stomach with water while on the run to circumvent the need to stop for a drink.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-14T08:57:10-07:00","id":1514962,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 3 Fianna Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Faerie Kin*:</b></u></span> Make a special howl, spend a point of Gnosis (or more), then roll Manipulation + Occult (difficulty 8).<br /> <br /> The writers have forgotten about the Manipulation modifier of the howling forms! RAW this Gift should almost always have a -3 modifier to its roll. Ignore that.<br /> <br /> More successes = more &quot;faeries&quot; answer<br /> More Gnosis spent = more powerful faeries<br /> <br /> We're told that typically this Gift summoners Chimera (see C20), but that at higher Gnosis expenditures it might summon Changelings &quot;or their dark-kin cousins&quot;. In C20 terms it seems likely this Gift would summon a mixture of Chimera, Kithain, Thallain, Dark-Kin and so on to assist... depending on who is nearby, what you spend, and how many successes you get. We are told that the creatures summoned will obey, but &quot;not without question&quot;. Presumably this means they will help to protect you, and to fight the good fight, but they may balk at certain requests which are beneath them, suicidal, and so on.<br /> <br /> The potency of this Gift will depend on where you are, how the ST reads the Gift, how powerful the WTA!Fae are, or how well acquainted your ST is with CTD. In a Crossover game the applications of this are limitless; Fae could come to aid in any kind of problem, not just fights. Getting a party going, stealing a trinket, infiltrating a keep. It's worth remembering that you don't choose what you get, and the various Chimera and Changelings who arrive to help you may be foes of one another, so there is potential for conflict. It's also a sort of Veil risk.<br /> <br /> Use the Gift wisely, and save it for when you desperately need it. Having a good relationship with the local Fae will also help; this Gift will work much better if you are a full-on Kinain yourself, as you'll be able to see the local Chimera before you summon them to your aid.<br /> <br /> If you're interested in the crossover side of things, I imagine that Chimera and Changelings pulled in by this Gift feel a duty to help, from the Dreaming, and are rewarded with Glamour (courtesy of the ancient pacts) if they do so.<br /> <br /> I'm marking this as Green, but if your ST has an attitude about Changelings (I've met some WoD players who have a serious hatred for CTD and the Dreaming), or is adversarial, it could be Red or Amber. If you find that this Gift consistently summons &quot;help&quot; which is inappropriate for the situation at hand, actively antagonistic or a huge Veil risk, creates endless complications or is just plain weak, consider asking for a refund on the 9xp (or switching ST).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Fair Fortune:</b></u></span> Once per scene you can reroll a fail or botch by spending a point of Gnosis.<br /> <br /> Nothing complicated to say here, beyond pointing out how significant this could wind up being; converting a botch into a success could massively change the outcome of a whole session or wider story.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Ley Lines:</b></u></span> It's kind of funny to have the existence of a significant supernatural occurrence confirmed in a solitary Gift - though I'm sure Ley Lines are namedropped in the Umbra book somewhere, and likely in Mage a few times. This Gift lets you manipulate them, but annoyingly only lets you manipulate them for a single purpose; frustrating people trying to pursue you.<br /> <br /> Spend a Gnosis, roll Wits + Occult (diff 7). If you're successful, any attempt to track you has to begin with a successful Perception + Occult (diff 8), and it needs to get more successes than you rolled. What's great about this is that many beings in the World of Darkness won't have an Occult rating. If a target fails to beat this Gift they automatically Botch on the tracking attempt; going the wrong way, finding trouble, that sort of thing.<br /> <br /> This is actually remarkably strong; it has a niche purpose, throwing off pursuers, but it does so in a very potent way. With a good Wits + Occult the odds are very stacked in your favour, especially since the enemy's roll is harder. Keep in mind that Garou pursuers might get -2 difficulty from being in Lupus, which could tilt the odds in their favour. The ability to simply make yourself impossible to follow as you flee from enemies, or a crime scene or similar, is pretty amazing!<br /> <br /> It's also worth noting that if 4/5 members of a pack chasing you fail the check, and 1 succeeds, there's no guarantee the correct individual will be trusted to lead the way; it will depend on how confident the pack are in that Garou's abilities!<br /> <br /> If you want to use this offensively, try activating it once you are passing through a very dangerous location, especially certain Umbral realms. Forcing someone to botch a tracking roll in certain parts of the Umbra could lead them into very deadly situations.<br /> <br /> Given that this Gift could potentially help you escape a force which would otherwise destroy the party (a far more powerful pack, a huge swarm of dangerous spirits), this gets a Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Reshape Object:</b></u></span> See the Homid entry.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Song of the Siren*:</b></u></span> See the Galliard entry.<br /> <br /> ================================================== ==========<br /> <br /> If you have Fae blood, or a special interest in the Dreaming, Faerie Kin would be a great purchase. Fair Fortune would be good on any character, especially Theurges and crafters however, given the importance of rolls with months building up to them. Ley Lines is good, but I'm not sure if I'd pick it up out of list unless escaping enemies were a constant theme.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-14T13:50:09-07:00","id":1515012,"title":"","content":"The form modifiers for Manipulation shouldn't apply based on how they're written in the shape-shifting section. They're explicitly a factor of the inability to communicate clearly: for example they don't apply to spirits or Garou by default. A magical howl that doesn't care about being mutually intelligible would fall into the exceptions the forms imply.<br /> <br /> I personally would rank Ley Lines Amber. It's good, but there's plenty of lower level stealth Gifts (and other things like Fetishes) that are going to make tracking you nearly impossible for non-supernaturals. It's nice that it's a big boost to stealth without having to be a stealthy character, but high Wits + Occult isn't necessarily something you'll have either. I would be more likely to consider it Green if it was a Theurge Gift (and it feels more like one to me anyway) where having high Wits, Occult, and Gnosis goes hand-in-hand with your role. A Fianna Ragabash isn't going to need this (though it doesn't hurt, it's still 9 XP when they have very effective stuff at lower level), and having high Occult on the three higher Rage Auspices is hit or miss on concept. If it was something like a pure Gnosis roll for the user, it would feel more Green to me as something a Fianna of any concept that isn't already stealthy would absolutely take over Rank 2 out of list stealth Gift.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-15T04:54:32-07:00","id":1515100,"title":"","content":"The reason I see Ley Lines as a step above those other Gifts, is that no matter how hard you make yourself to track with regular methods, and lower level Gifts, people know there's one road out of the farm, people can see you in the distance, people know you're probably headed back to your Caern or camp and so on.<br /> <br /> Ley Lines is a tracking override. It seems to basically auto-confuse anyone trying to follow you, regardless of their method, and my reading is that the Gift would even mess with someone trying to follow you on a straight motorway which they know you're on. The Gift's flavour mentions that it makes people go in circles! It's a properly magical &quot;mess with anyone trying to follow me&quot; which is much more reliable than a simple &quot;following me is +2 difficulty&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-15T05:16:55-07:00","id":1515101,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 3 Get of Fenris Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Might of Thor:</b></u></span> I desperately want this one to be good.<br /> <br /> It costs Gnosis, which is immediately a problem; it stops you from using the Strength well.<br /> <br /> You roll Willpower, difficulty 8. Get suffer from a low starting WP, and that difficulty is pretty punishing as well.<br /> <br /> If you succeed, your Strength is doubled for one turn per success. Having a Strength of 10-18 in Crinos is pretty neat, don't get me wrong, but it's one turn per success. And you can't even properly use it on the first turn. If you set this up before an ambush, get 2 successes, and on your second turn launch 4 attacks, that's pretty great. But even then that's assuming you have a Permanent Rage of 5, and the Get aren't all Ahroun. Given the high Difficulty there will be times where this doesn't activate at all.<br /> <br /> I'm a sucker for strength. My Garou has Huge Size and started with Strength 5, and he spends as much time in Crinos as he can because he likes his breed form. Even if he were a Get I'm not sure if I'd grab this.<br /> <br /> Outside of combat this Gift could help you to lift something huge or do a huge leap, but even then you have a whole pack, and outside of combat it's less likely you'll need an insane strength like 18. Winning an arm wrestle at a moot now and then also doesn't make this worth it.<br /> <br /> My suggestion would be decreasing the difficulty to 6 to make this Gift more reliable; if you can reliably get 3-4 successes, suddenly this Gift may as well last for a whole scene, since few fights last longer. Hell, if this Gift added a passive +1 Strength modifier to all of your Forms I'd grab it in a heartbeat.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Redirect Pain:</b></u></span> Spend a Rage, roll Manipulation + Primal-Urge (diff 8). On a success you can make an opponent suffer your wound penalties, even if you're ignoring those penalties (say with Resist Pain, or Willpower).<br /> <br /> Note that the flavour for this Gift does mention that you make people suffer for the wounds they've inflicted; it isn't in the System section, but I'd argue that the Gift is intended to only function against people who have inflicted wounds on you.<br /> <br /> Overall this is a neat effect, and very fun, but ideally you should be avoiding getting wounded in the first place. And if you are badly wounded, your turn is best used on dodging, attacking threats and so on. Arguably this Gift falls into a trap; you inflict a -2 dice penalty on someone, and then they use their Rage actions to turn you into jelly. Best case scenario you could debilitate someone with intense pain you can't feel, but that &quot;best case scenario&quot; has you inches from death, which is best avoided with good strategy, picking your fights and having genuinely good Gifts which help you win fights, rather than have retribution when you're losing them. Also keep in mind that with a difficulty of 8 the Gift may fail to turn on at all, in which case you've wasted a perfectly good life-saving dodge or attack.<br /> <br /> My fix for this Gift would be to make it so you roll for the power, and thereafter in the same scene anyone who inflicts a wound upon you suffers the pain of that wound.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Venom Blood:</b></u></span> This one is interesting. If you interpret claw and bite attacks as innately causing someone to come into contact with your blood, this is pretty great. If your ST rules that the blood damages objects, as well, dissolving swords and so on, it's also buffed quite neatly.<br /> <br /> Spend a Rage, roll Stamina + Primal Urge (diff 7), every success is a die of Aggravated inflicted upon contact with your blood. Given that your Stamina pool could be 7 or 8, and your Primal-Urge will likely be 3+ at this Rank, that could be anywhere from 10 to 13 dice. If you luck out and get 3+ successes, this Gift becomes a pretty potent counterattack; a Garou opponent may claw or bite you 2-4 times in a single round, which could add up to 2-4 instances of 3+ dice of Aggravated. The problem is that a dangerous opponent very rarely cares about ~3 dice of Aggravated; they'll soak it quite reliably.<br /> <br /> This Gift does make you very dangerous to Vampires, and indeed one relatively reliable way of killing a lone Werewolf is a whole Coterie of Neonates jumping on the Garou and all draining them simultaneously (taking 3 blood per round each), but chances are at Rank 3, and as a Get, even a whole group of Vampires is unlikely to be a serious threat to you, and if they are it won't be because of blood draining. It's also worth noting that Ratkin get something like this for free, without a Gift, and it's passive. Though that only triggers when you drink the blood of course!<br /> <br /> I don't think there's anything wrong with this Gift, there's just a lot of moving parts which mean it could do a lot or nothing. Best case scenario is that you deal that 1 last point of Aggravated needed to kill a Garou opponent or push them into rolling Rage to Remain Active, which is pretty neat. Every bit of damage helps, especially given people only have 7-8 health levels. If you can set this up before a fight it's most ideal.<br /> <br /> Overall I'm calling this an Amber because in a typical fight it could do 0 health levels, or 1 or 2 across the whole fight. Even if you roll 5 successes, that's 5 dice of Aggravated, which means once you roll those dice, and roll a Garou's soak (which will likely be 6-8ish), it averages out to 0 damage inflicted. And if your opponent can't soak Aggravated, a Rank 3 Get is going to destroy them most likely regardless!<br /> <br /> If your ST has this Gift damaging weapons that slice and stab you, that helps a tad as well. A creative use would be to drip your blood onto something to corrode it, which could help you to break through things which are too strong for you to muscle past; even the strongest barrier will be weaker after exposure to a caustic substance.<br /> <br /> I also find it funny that the Gift never explicitly says you aren't harmed by the transformation it provides; RAW, if you're really pedantic, this Gift kills you - an acidic black bile makes a poor substitute for life-giving blood. I know that isn't the case at any table, but I just find it amusing.<br /> <br /> ================================================== ====<br /> <br /> Only three Gifts! Three! Be nice and let your Get PC pick a reasonable level 3 Ahroun or Breed Gift to complete this list, Jesus. I'd suggest adding Combat Healing to the Get of Fenris list at this Rank. It's a healthy staple which can make non-Ahroun Get more reliable in the long run, building on their legend as the most martial Tribe.<br /> <br /> I wouldn't really recommend buying any of these out-of-list. If you're Get they're all perfectly serviceable and worth getting, perhaps not at top priority.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-15T13:44:39-07:00","id":1515178,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1515100#post1515100\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The reason I see Ley Lines as a step above those other Gifts, is that no matter how hard you make yourself to track with regular methods, and lower level Gifts, people know there's one road out of the farm, people can see you in the distance, people know you're probably headed back to your Caern or camp and so on.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ley Lines doesn't prevent this. Botching, as per the shadowing and tracking rules in the book, just means you lose the subject's trail completely. You can still try to head towards a place you'll suspect they'll go. So the descriptive text and the rules text result in the same thing: you lose the target completely (so you can't retry at +1 diff like you can on a normal failure), but once the narrative events of the botch play out (a cop thinks your sketchy and holds you up with questions until they get away, you fall into a marsh and take enough time getting free they're gone, magical ley line confusion), you don't magically forget things like knowing your target's favorite bar, or where their house is.<br /> <br /> I also think it's a pretty weak move as a ST to have a PC successfully evade being tracked, only to find their pursuer(s) constantly waiting for them by guessing where they're going to go instead of trying to follow them... esp. since Ley Lines doesn't even stop that from happening. You magically confuse a Pentex First team.. and then they just call in a chopper to rescue them and fly to where they think you're going (and the pilot isn't impacted by the Gift on top of that) and use flight and speed to beat you there.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Ley Lines is a tracking override. </div> </div> </div>Still contingent on winning a contested roll. This doesn't even touch on things like what happens if you use Ley Lines against an opponent with Pulse of the Prey (which is an override in the opposite direction).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's a properly magical &quot;mess with anyone trying to follow me&quot; which is much more reliable than a simple &quot;following me is +2 difficulty&quot;. </div> </div> </div>Given that the base difficulty for tracking is already 7, getting it up to 9 or 10 is very reliable for not getting tracked given the tracking rules (even as just a template as one presumes human trackers use Survival instead of Primal-Urge); and that base 7 is before taking the prey's skills into account a target with high Stealth or appropriate things like Streetwise or Survival is supposed to increase that mundanely before a Gift bump it up more. Like, seriously, go back to pages 284-285 and reread the tracking rules. If the difficulty of tracking hits 11+ it literally has the same impact as Ley Lines does. A +2 on top of the base for being hard to track through skills and a +2 from a level 1 Gift that doesn't have a Gnosis cost... where is the love for this Gift coming from?<br /> <br /> I'm not saying it's Red, but I'm saying it's really niche for what it does and thus seems obviously Amber; esp. for a Rank 3 Gift.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-16T03:36:15-07:00","id":1515224,"title":"","content":"The Gift's writeup says it makes people go in circles as they try to follow you - to me that's powerful. In addition the resisted roll is stacked in your favour.<br /> <br /> It also says <u>any</u> attempt to follow you is frustrated - the Gift doesn't have a specific target, the writeup is written in a way which seems to protect your passage, a particular journey you take. It doesn't say you frustrate a particular group, or even require you to know you're being followed. If you're going somewhere secret, somewhere you need to go without being followed, you can activate it.<br /> <br /> As for it being overridden by Beat, and similar, you're dealing with two sets of absolutes. Beat lets you hear the heartbeat of your target growing stronger or weaker; Ley Lines causes people to innately go in circles, take wrong turns and so on. The Garou effected by the Gift would note that the heartbeat isn't getting any louder, they'd realize they're lost, but it wouldn't override the wording of Ley Lines in my opinion, both based on wording and the relative levels of the Gifts. If it happened at my table I might rule that having Beat of the Heart Drum reduces the difficulty of the Occult roll to track the Ley Lines user, and like I say it would help you realize you're being misled sooner.<br /> <br /> In the case of Pulse, someone using Ley Lines is attempting to hide, so you'd need to roll the Perception + Enigmas. Having an unerring sense of what direction your prey is from you is great, but if you're under the effect of a Gift which forces you to go in circles or take wrong turns... it's an absolute vs an absolute. Given that PotP has a resisted roll, and Ley Lines has a resisted roll, the simplest way to settle it is to do both of the resisted rolls separately and then interpret the results as fairly as you can.<br /> <br /> It's worth noting that Ley Lines isn't all that great if you have a poor Wits + Occult dice pool, but Silver Claws isn't that great if you have a poor Dexterity + Brawl pool; I don't want to make every single Gift a *! Ultimately my taste and my opinions will inform decision-making here, but I appreciate your take all the same.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-16T04:20:05-07:00","id":1515227,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 3 Glass Walker Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Control Complex Machine:</b></u></span> Spend a Willpower, roll Manipulation + Science/Computer, difficulty determined by complexity, and on a success you can command the relevant machine. Given that you could command any device to unlock, alarm, freeze, shut down, provide you with information... hell, if you're desperate for money, this could get you a huge amount from a cash machine. Just make sure you disable the camera first!<br /> <br /> This Gift is a bit like a sonic screwdriver, especially when you have Control Simple Machine in addition. One thing that bothers me about the two Gifts is that Control Simple Machine covers the simplest of mechanisms, Complex Machine covers electronics, and there's a gap. Complex non-electronic devices aren't covered by either Gift. There's no level that will set off a simple non-electronic mine, or cause an old-fashioned printing press to produce a different headline. It feels like there should be a level 2 Gift in the middle; Control Simple Machine, Control Mechanism, Control Electronics, that sort of thing.<br /> <br /> Still, based on the RAW, this is a pretty handy Gift which I quite enjoy. The limitless potential of the Gift does make it a Green, but I will point out that Plug and Play is often a better choice. Sure, it's hard to wrap your N64 controller around the mobile phone or detonator a bad guy is holding, and this Gift will let you do some things that Plug and Play won't! But in most situations I think you'll get more mileage out of Plug. It lasts longer, lets you apply your skills to the device consistently, and gives you a degree of ongoing intuitive control which this Gift might struggle with; having to spend WP again, and roll again, could prove tiresome. Also Plug has no difficulty to set up, and you may find the rolls you have to make once you're &quot;plugged in&quot; are easier than the relatively high difficulty of this Gift. Getting a laptop to do anything at all is difficulty 8!<br /> <br /> Still Green, but use it wisely and try mundane methods and lower level Gifts first.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Intrusion:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis, roll Gnosis (difficulty 7), and for one minute all barriers you approach will unlock and so on to let you pass. This includes barricades, boards nailed to doors, vaults. It's cool to envisage a Glass Walker running up to a random car and piling into it without even having to pause to disable the lock.<br /> <br /> Still... we have Control Simple Machine and Control Complex Machine. We have Open Seal, which costs 3/5 against this costing 9/15. Walking around with the freedom to use other Gifts, and not have to roll to unlock anything, is neat. But is it worth 9xp?<br /> <br /> Furthermore it's worth noting that this gift doesn't open doors ahead of you, it unlocks them. If barriers literally sprang open as you charged at them this would be much cooler, and much more useful.<br /> <br /> Were I fixing this Gift I might remove the Gnosis cost, and make it so that the Gift causes barriers to unlock and open at your whim, unless they are magical. I'd then put in a specific system for overcoming such barriers. As it stands Intrusion gives you automatic successes on your checks to overcome such magical locks and barriers, equal to your successes on the Gnosis check. This combos well with Open Seal, funnily enough, but having both feels like a waste.<br /> <br /> All in all I'll call it Amber - it isn't god-awful, but I wouldn't take it personally.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Electroshock*:</b></u></span> This Gift is relatively auspice-dependent; it's naturally stronger the higher your permanent Rage rating. The good thing about this Gift is that it inflicts automatic, unrolled, undodgeable Aggravated damage. You aren't rolling 5 dice of Aggravated, you're inflicting 5 points of Aggravated. I'm slightly reminded of Create Elements' fire option, though that has a cap of 3 levels of Aggravated, and of course spending and rolling Gnosis is a poor combo for most.<br /> <br /> At the upper end, with 9 Rage, you can inflict 10 Aggravated against a target by spending 5 Rage in one go, which will kill most targets, or force a Remain Active check. 7 Rage is perhaps more realistic, and permits you to slam a Garou / Fera / other corporeal powerhouse with 8 Aggravated in one go. And a typical Ahroun/Galliard, with Rage 5, could hit a target with 6 Aggravated.<br /> <br /> One good thing about this Gift is how precise, how controlled it is. Slamming a Garou, or any standard character in the WoD, with 6 Aggravated is a great way to almost kill them; it's ironically a pacifist's ideal opening gambit. Yeah, we can fight, if you feel like it, but right now you're crawling and adding a -5 penalty to all your checks.<br /> <br /> The ability to split damage among multiple targets is nice-ish, but a bit niche. It could help you to kill a bunch of really weak spirits, or finish off a bunch of wounded foes, but you're unlikely to know precisely how much damage each of them needs to take to die! One possible situation where it could shine is if you're against a pack of BSDs where more than one has just rolled to Remain Active; it means you know that they're all very close to death, so applying 1-2 damage each could be hugely impactful.<br /> <br /> Of course having a permanent Rage of 7 or 9 can completely transform your life for the worse, especially as a Glass Walker, and it would be strange to rate the Gift on the assumption of a Rage rating that high! Overall I'd say that an Ahroun of this Tribe having the ability to spend 3 Rage to inflict 6 Aggravated (before soak of course!) at will, and in such a cool and appealing way, is just about worthy of a Green. It should go without saying that the * relates to your starting Auspice and what level of Rage you've reached by Rank 3!<br /> <br /> <u><b>Elemental Favor*:</b></u> This Gift doesn't quite explain what it does well enough. We are told that you can beg, threaten or cajole an Urban Elemental Spirit into doing you a favour. For instance you might convince a glass elemental to shatter its pane of glass. The other examples are forcing a door to remain shut, or making a car's breaks fail... which would be thanks to bribing a metal or plastic elemental, perhaps? We aren't really told how this gift defines an &quot;urban elemental&quot;, we aren't told whether we need to be speaking to the spirit in the Umbra, we aren't told whether the spirit will understand us or not without Spirit Speech.<br /> <br /> Does this Gift let you walk up to a glass skyscraper, and threaten the glass elementals within to shatter their glass? From the real world? Who knows. And honestly... we have too many Gifts that do this! Gremlins would be ideal in the Glass Walker list, and I'd strongly recommend you simply replace this Gift with Gremlins. And ultimately Command Spirit is a level 2 Gift; paying 10xp for that pretty universal Gift beats paying 9xp for whatever this does.<br /> <br /> I'm leaving this one unrated. If you want to know how good it is, your guess is as good as mine. My best guess would be Red, since Control Simple Machine and Control Complex Machine, along with Gifts like Jam Technology and even Gremlins if you can access it, do all this and better. I'm left wondering what this level 3 tribe-specific Gift is even meant to shine at. What is it doing that the others aren't? What are its limitations and requirements?<br /> <br /> ================================================== =<br /> <br /> If you have 5+ Rage anyways, Electroshock might be worth purchasing off-list. And if you're dedicated to handling technology, Control Complex Machine might be worth 15xp... but overall I'd suggest going for Plug and Play. It's far cheaper (5 vs 15xp) and will help you handle most situations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-16-2023, 07:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-16T04:44:44-07:00","id":1515228,"title":"","content":"I understand you want to move on, but I'm feeling kinda strong about this...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1515224#post1515224\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In addition the resisted roll is stacked in your favour.</div> </div> </div> </div>As you already noted, potentially stacked in your favor. WtA tends to make it pretty easy to get a Perception bonus. And diff 7 vs. diff 8 is good, but it's not a massive stacking.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It also says <u>any</u> attempt to follow you is frustrated - the Gift doesn't have a specific target, the writeup is written in a way which seems to protect your passage, a particular journey you take. It doesn't say you frustrate a particular group, or even require you to know you're being followed. If you're going somewhere secret, somewhere you need to go without being followed, you can activate it. </div> </div> </div>This feels like a pretty big goal-post shift. You were saying that using a Gift that causes people tracking you to fail because of the high diff you impose on them wouldn't stop them from just intuiting where you're headed and basically override failing to track you, but that Ley Lines protects against this. Neither is supported by the text. You've hit Gifts with Red for the systems that they're built off of, but you're ignoring the tracking system in the game and how it works to make Ley Lines sound better.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ... it's an absolute vs an absolute. Given that PotP has a resisted roll, and Ley Lines has a resisted roll, the simplest way to settle it is to do both of the resisted rolls separately and then interpret the results as fairly as you can. </div> </div> </div>While I appreciate you don't want to have every Gift get a *, this is a pretty strong case for one isn't it? One Gift lets you automatically succeed at tracking someone, and one Gift lets you automatically fail an attempt to track you. Without an official way to settle it... that's a pretty big thing to see how your ST feels about it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's worth noting that Ley Lines isn't all that great if you have a poor Wits + Occult dice pool, but Silver Claws isn't that great if you have a poor Dexterity + Brawl pool; </div> </div> </div>OK, but as I pointed out, if this was a Theurge Gift I wouldn't have bothered with that point. The Fianna aren't a &quot;we're really focusing on Occult&quot; splat like Theurges or Older Brother were not having a decent Wits + Occult pool would be weird. Nobody would balk at the idea of a Fianna that only has Wits 3/Occult 1 if they're not specifically focused on mystic stuff.<br /> <br /> Brawl is definitely in a smaller category of Abilities (along with Primal-Urge, Intimidation, Enigmas, and Rituals) where by Rank 3 you're going to want a decent rating regardless of what your character focus is, because they cover very key things to Garou life.<br /> <br /> I didn't point this out because of the obvious &quot;of course you need a good dice pool for a Gift that requires a roll,&quot; I pointed it out because Occult is a more niche thing for Garou to invest a lot into; esp. since even with some of the really good Occult based Gifts in the game, you don't really need everyone to have them. The devalues it as a Rank 3 Fianna Gift (as it's not a big part of their niche) or a Rank 3 out-of-list Gift since that's a lot of XP and there are lower level options that might be in list and are still really good at this same general function even if not necessarily as good as Ley Lines going off fully.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-16T04:50:15-07:00","id":1515229,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 3 Red Talon Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <u><b>Elemental Favor*:</b></u> Not again!<br /> <br /> This one is identical to the Glass Walker version, except it deals with the four classical western elements, fire water earth and air. While that part is easier to understand, I still don't know what this Gift does, or how it does it. Unrated for the same reasons.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Render Down*:</b></u></span> This Gift lets you roll your Rage, difficulty 7, or perhaps 6 if you're in Crinos depending on your reading of the Rage rules, and each success reduces 20 pounds of artificial substance down to its raw components. For instance you might convert a car into a pile of raw metals, oils and unworked leather, along with sand for the glass. This is a showy, magic-like and decently powerful Gift. It has no cost, so the potential to simply melt a whole village piece by piece is kinda interesting.<br /> <br /> The risk is that you could roll 4+ successes on the Rage check and Frenzy. So keep that in mind.<br /> <br /> Very few dangerous machines can stand to lose 20 pounds of critical materials; that could punch a whole in an engine, or a console, or the wiring, so you only really need one success to disable most machines. And yes we have too many Gifts which work to that effect, way too many, but all in all I like this one well enough. This is essentially a much better Call the Rust and Gift of the Termite.<br /> <br /> Calling this one Amber, since you irreparably destroy what you're targeting instead of disabling it, and in many situations you could simply destroy artificial objects using your insane strength and wicked claws. The ability to total a helicopter or car mid chase is great though, of course. This might be <span style=\"color:#27ae60\">Green</span> if you're playing a Red Talon who is immersed in a world of high tech contraptions, especially going after Glass Walkers, BSDs in cities, Pentex, Technomages and so on. In that case the world is made of wood, and you're a fucking flamethrower.<br /> <br /> As for why this isn't as good as Gremlins, it's a question of dice pool and outcome. Gremlins is Attribute + Ability, and has no cost. This is Rage, and has no cost. Similar enough, except realistically your dice pool will be 1-5 and you risk Frenzying when you use it, if you have a pool of 4+ dice. The risk might not be huge, but it'll happen eventually if you make frequent use of this Gift.<br /> <br /> Furthermore with Gremlins you disable the whole machine quite reliably, even with 1 success (though it's temporary). Render Down forces you to reduce a chunk of the machine to its base components, which is worse for the Veil (it's an inexplicable event) and also more obvious to onlookers. It's still good, don't get me wrong, and in the right game it could easily be Green. When all the risks and little differences are taken into account, and Render Down being limited to manmade materials while Gremlins will make a wooden club or shield fail, you can see how Gremlins works out better.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Territory*:</b></u></span> This Gift makes you a pretty superb guardian of a Caern or Sept, even if you aren't currently physically present. In a game focused on traveling this is less useful, but even then, marking your hotel room and seeing it freely for 5 days since is pretty great.<br /> <br /> The flavour isn't ideal, especially for a female garou in Homid form, but even in urban environments this is a great tool to spy on others. I actually wish this were a Lupus Gift; it seems a shame to attach it to a Tribe which is relatively rarely played in my experience.<br /> <br /> A difficulty 7 Perception + Primal-Urge is simple enough, and as a Lupus Garou you'll be doing well at Perception in your breed form, and also have the Gnosis to support this Gift quite well. Having 5 places you can view at will is pretty great for spying, tracking, patrolling and so on.<br /> <br /> Calling this one Green, but if you're constantly on the move, or are pushed into environments which you couldn't have possibly prepared in every session, it might wind up <span style=\"color:#f39c12\">Amber</span>.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Trackless Waste*:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis, roll Intelligence + Primal-Urge (difficulty 6), effect a 3.2km radius of <u>wilderness</u> per success - based on the Gift's introduction, this area of wilderness should be your home or territory, you can't use this to launch a psychic terror attack against another Sept! Within that area humans fail all attempts to navigate. Garou may roll Perception + Primal-Urge, difficulty unstated (so 4-6 depending on form, most likely), and if they beat the Talon's successes they can navigate freely.<br /> <br /> The Gift only lasts until sunrise.<br /> <br /> I like the absoluteness of humans being automatically effected. I like that Garou can resist. The short duration annoys me, as does it being so restricted in where you can use it. It feels a bit like an NPC power. I'm also unsure how Mages, Changelings, Vampires and so on would fare, as they aren't wholly human. I'd personally rule they get a high difficulty Perception + Awareness (or equivalent) to navigate normally.<br /> <br /> If your game is static, and features defending a Red Talon Caern from being despoiled by humans, this Gift could be <span style=\"color:#27ae60\">Green</span>. In most games I'd call it a <span style=\"color:#c0392b\">Red</span>, due to its very precise restrictions. Since it isn't useless, and the mechanics are easy enough to understand and utilize well, I'm calling it an Amber with a * - pick it up if it applies to you, don't if it doesn't.<br /> <br /> ================================================== =======<br /> <br /> Territory might be worth 15xp on other Lupus, and other high-Gnosis PCs, especially if you are committed to guarding a place or acting as a spy/patrolman.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-16-2023, 08:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-16T05:00:56-07:00","id":1515230,"title":"","content":"Heavy, you're welcome to put an asterisk next to Ley Lines if you like, but I'm not convinced for the purposes of writing this list. IMO making people trying to follow you go in circles and pick the wrong paths over and over is a pretty solid Green, even at level 3, you feel differently. That's fine. I've already explained why I think what I think, why I think the dice pool is typically going to be stacked in your favour (like I say, many things in the WoD don't have Occult at all, and you know ahead of time you have this power while your opponents don't), why it would be helpful, why it's worth 9xp. I'd rather not relitigate it endlessly. I've read the tracking systems, I know how they work, and I know how the other tracking / avoiding being tracked Gifts discussed so far work. I like Ley Lines for its absoluteness, and its pretty clear potential to completely block attempts to track you. It's as simple as that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-07T19:09:48-07:00","id":1517439,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11620_5.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11620\" data-vbnamecard=\"11620\">twig</a> are you planning on continuing the thread? I find it fascinating, given that you sometimes post known powerful gifts but also offer a new take on other that I thought underpowered. And of course, the other posters comments make this interesting from a theoretical standpoint.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-08T01:24:39-07:00","id":1517455,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>lbeaumanior</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1517439#post1517439\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11620_5.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11620\" data-vbnamecard=\"11620\">twig</a> are you planning on continuing the thread? I find it fascinating, given that you sometimes post known powerful gifts but also offer a new take on other that I thought underpowered. And of course, the other posters comments make this interesting from a theoretical standpoint.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh totally - just on a bit of a break. Will get back to this at some point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-10T02:52:07-07:00","id":1517635,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 3 Shadow Lord Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Direct the Storm:</b></u></span> I love this. It has amazing flavour; the puppetmaster, the director, the Beta who is secretly the Alpha. This Gift lets you guide a frenzying Garou to do as you please. Note that this works on someone in Fox Frenzy as well! So if a member of your pack has tucked tail to run, you can direct them to attack regardless.<br /> <br /> Spend a Gnosis, roll your Willpower against a difficulty of the target's Rage; you control them for two turns per success, basically picking their targets. You can set them on anyone you choose, potentially even themselves! If you try to use this on someone in Thrall, it works just as well, but you risk Frenzying yourself.<br /> <br /> A good use for this is against a frenzying BSD. RAW it doesn't work on Fera or Vampires however; personally I'd increase the difficulty but allow it. If your target lacks Rage, I'd set the difficulty to 9 by default; the frenzy you're controlling is alien to you.<br /> <br /> This is incredible; it has utility as both a support power to back up a frenzying ally and make sure they stay 'on target', and also as an offensive power to mess with a rival pack or BSDs. The uses are pretty obvious, it's just a solid win for the Shadow Lords.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Icy Chill of Despair*:</b></u> This one's a little vague. You become very intimidating and begin to grow larger, colder and darker as shadows grow around you. This is impressive, and a Veil risk. You concentrate for a turn (annoying) and spend a point of Gnosis (ditto), and roll Manipulation + Intimidation difficulty 7. Anyone meaning you harm has to roll Willpower difficulty 8 - if they fail then they have to spend WP to oppose you in any way, whether it's attacking you or speaking against your ideas.<br /> <br /> The problem is that we don't get a duration. Does it last the whole scene? Who knows. Does someone have to spend WP to oppose you once, or a WP every time they want to oppose you? Who knows. You can make good guesses as to these, but it's hard to say. As a result I'm leaving this unrated.<br /> <br /> A very problematic aspect of this Gift, however you rule it, is that spirits cannot spend Willpower RAW. By the books, a Nexus Crawler has dots in Willpower, but no temporary points. Same with Gnosis and Rage. They literally cannot spend a point of WP to ignore this Gift and attack you, which is mad. That, in combination with the Gift's vagueness, means I'm leaving it unrated. However if it works as I'd expect, you need to spend a WP each time you resist the target (lasting for a turn each time) and it lasts for a scene, it would be Green... despite the high cost of initiating it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Paralyzing Stare:</b></u></span> Concentrating for one turn sucks. A turn is the whole turn - it is composed of actions. But you concentrate for a whole turn, sure. Spend a Gnosis, roll Charisma + Intimidation (difficulty of the target's Willpower), and they become frozen for one turn per success (can't move or attack, but they CAN defend themselves). And they need to be able to see you.<br /> <br /> This is perfectly fine. It isn't busted, it isn't awful. It's a tad conservative for a Rank 3 Gift, and it feels like most of its job is done by True Fear (which doesn't cost Gnosis, or take up the whole round, or stop you from doing bonus actions), but it's good.<br /> <br /> The advantage of this Gift is that you can simply freeze an NPC and stop them from being an issue; True Fear makes foes run around in terror, which can cause problems. This neutralizes. That's pretty good! I'm making this one a Green, but not for its use in combat (though that is handy) - it's more for its use outside of combat, or in preventing combat. Outside of a fight, concentrating for a whole turn is a much smaller cost. And being able to completely freeze anyone who can see you is great!<br /> <br /> Keep in mind that if you can't be seen (I'm a bit smug about my Metis being blind right now) this Gift won't do anything. Many Banes, and some Fomori, will be immune to this Gift as a result.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Shadow Cutting:</b></u></span> Being able to attack someone's shadow is gold. I love this. Amazing flavour, incredible stuff.<br /> <br /> Then you get to the mechanics... you have to spit into their shadow. Sure, a bit of flavour, fine. It makes it obvious, but that's a cool drawback for a strong gift. And you have to spend Gnosis... so you're in a fight and you have just given up your ability to do bonus attacks... and it only works with claws and Fetish weapons, such as Klaives. And only you can strike the shadow.<br /> <br /> All of that would be fine if you had a -2 difficulty to hit or something. But all it does, ALL it does, is remove 2 dice from their dodge pool and make parrying impossible.<br /> <br /> Many, many enemies don't have an action to dodge with. Of those who do, -2 dice is a minor hindrance. This is atrocious. And it feels like such a waste of such a cool idea!<br /> <br /> Some ideas to change this Gift, which can be used in combination with eachother:<br /> <br /> - Pack members can also strike the shadow<br /> - All attacks, even bullets and so on, harm the target if they hit the shadow<br /> - The target has +2 difficulty to defensive actions, rather than losing 2 dice from dodge<br /> <br /> A niche and interesting use of this Gift would be to set things up, either through magic or lighting or just planning ahead of time (the sun will be behind him if we strike at this time of day...), so that your foe has a VERY long shadow. A person with a light source behind them can cast a huge shadow, and narratively speaking that would let you stab the air fifty meters away from them and kill them. But even then? RAW it's just -2 dice to dodge. The mechanics give us nothing.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b><u>Under the Gun:</u></b></span> This Gift makes a target a magnet for bullets and other missiles - at least in theory. The name feels a little anachronistic to me personally, and I'd rather it be about bows or rocks or similar, but still, could be a modern rename. The Gift is taught by a Raven spirit after all, not a Gun spirit, so it feels potentially ancient.<br /> <br /> You touch your target specifically with your fingertips, spend a Gnosis, and roll Manipulation + Firearms (personally I'd allow Archery as well). If you succeed the target is -2 difficulty to hit with missiles (arrows, bullets, rocks) for 1 day per success.<br /> <br /> Part of the appeal of this Gift is that it helps to trigger a 'hunting accident', but there's nothing in the mechanics which suggests you are more likely to be accidentally hit by gunfire or arrows; it's -2 difficulty to someone trying to hit you. If you can get this off against an enemy before a fight it's neat enough, especially if you're luring a BSD into an ambush of Kinfolk with silver ammo... it's just quite restrictive.<br /> <br /> The cost, the fact you have to roll, the slightly limited mechanics... it's going to shine most in the hands of an effective deceiver who interacts with enemies cordially with traps laid in the future. The long duration saves this from being an auto-ignore, and I like the flavour. Personally I'd go a little further and make it -2 difficulty to hit with missiles and -2 difficulty on the damage rolls as well; this would help feed into the idea of the 'hunting accident' - any stray rock or arrow or bullet that hits you is more likely to find a major artery or some vital organ.<br /> <br /> Uses of this feel pretty obvious; handshake with a business partner the day before their attempted assassination, patting the back of a Pentex foreman for a job well done, but honestly I don't want to be spending Gnosis while making direct contact with mysterious NPCs if I can help it - it feels like asking for trouble.<br /> <br /> ================================================== ====================<br /> <br /> Direct the Storm and Paralyzing Stare are both worth getting out-of-list if you can, though Stormcrows are very unlikely to teach outsiders! If your Pack Totem is a Stormcrow you might fare better. Under the Gun, though meh, could be worth buying if you're a gun-obsessed BG or GW who wants to add another tool to their arsenal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-10T03:45:17-07:00","id":1517636,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 3 Silent Strider Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Adaptation:</b></u></span> You'll be unharmed by poison, disease and any environment (space, volcano, and so on) so long as you have this Gift active. Spend a Gnosis, roll Stamina + Survival (difficulty 7), and this lasts for one hour per success. Amazing in the Umbra, and in extreme situations such as Pentex HQs and poison swamps.<br /> <br /> Used offensively, RAW you can grab a foe and jump into lava, out the airlock, and so on. This Gift is amazing both defensively and aggressively. It's also pretty great when used deceptively; you won't be drowned or killed by an airless environment, or poison, but playing dead could be very strong indeed.<br /> <br /> The ability to put poisons and diseases on pause is pretty amazing against Banes and Fomori as well. The cost and roll is a little annoying, but if this Gift were fully passive it would be far too good!<br /> <br /> If you're being chased, or are able to set the scene for a fight, this is pretty great. Talk to your ST about the difference between a hazardous environment (toxic air) and a hazardous situation (falling) though!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Great Leap:</b></u></span> Spend a Willpower, roll Strength + Athletics, leap 100 feet per success. Any form, any situation really. Moving 300 feet in 3 seconds is 68 miles per hour, so with a pretty conservative result on the roll you'd catch up to almost anything short of a vehicle moving at maximum speed.<br /> <br /> The mobility granted by this Gift, even in Homid, is amazing. A Lupus Garou can get 21 feet per success if they use Hare's Leap to its fullest extent, and the roll is difficulty 3; they'd need 5+ successes to have any chance of matching this Gift.<br /> <br /> Let's compare a Lupus with Hare's Leap and 11 dice on their roll with a Silent Strider with this Gift and 11 dice. The Lupus averages 7.7 successes, which is potentially 168 feet. If the Strider rolls even 2 successes, and indeed they average 4.6, they will leap further. Furthermore the Strider could be in Crinos form, and also they aren't doing a regular jump; this power doesn't demand you run up and move athletically, it's magic (my logic there being that Hare's Leap interacts with the regular jumping roll, whereas the Strength + Athletics roll for this Gift is the Gift's activation roll).<br /> <br /> The HL user does also need to pass a difficulty 7 check to use their Gift in the first place. But interestingly these Gifts do combine! You can double the distance covered by Great Leap to 200 feet per success!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Mark of the Death-Wolf*:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis point, and one turn, carving the mark with your claw. Roll Wits + Occult (difficulty 7). For one week per success Ghosts are attracted to the mark, causing the area to become &quot;badly haunted&quot;.<br /> <br /> I wish this one were less vague, or gave examples of what might happen as a result. Making someone's house badly haunted could wind up killing them, sure, but it could also just make their house a hangout for Ghosts who play pranks. It's a bit like a much, much, much weaker version of the Fianna's fae-summoning Gift. You're calling upon another splat, inherently variable (depending on your ST's knowledge of the splat, their interest in involving them, how powerful they think they are, and the precise NPCs who show up), and letting things unfold as they may. At least with Fae they're showing up immediately and feel compelled by the Dreaming to help you. Here it's just... putting up a lure for ghosts, who may well hate you for bothering them!<br /> <br /> Potential uses include luring the cause of a haunting to a particular location to make it easier to fight, messing with a rival pack, harassing a Pentex executive's home, and much more. But the results are so ST-dependent (I know next to nothing about Arcanoi and Wraith mechanics, some STs know a lot) that it's basically impossible to rate. Assuming a basically competent haunting, I'll make this an Amber.<br /> <br /> &quot;Angered Wraiths can be very dangerous&quot; &quot;this Gift causes Ghosts to become very angry with nearby humans, blaming them for sins which are not their own&quot;, &quot;this Gift draws in the worst kinds of Ghosts&quot; etc. would make it so much more clear what you can expect. Personally I'd love it if this Gift had another option to it; the sign you carve draws Wraiths in, and you can fill the sign with positive or negative energies. That way you could draw in regular old sane Wraiths to make them a hangout, where you can gossip with them and so on, or you can draw in the worst of the worst, at your own option.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sense the Unnatural:</b></u></span> See the Lupus entry. Great for hunting down ghostly and vampiric influences!<br /> <br /> ================================================== =================<br /> <br /> Adaptation is worth it on any Garou, I'd argue. Ditto with Great Leap.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-10T07:14:17-07:00","id":1517644,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 3 Silver Fang Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Burning Blade:</b></u></span> Pretty disappointing, despite being conceptually very cool.<br /> <br /> Roll your Gnosis, difficulty 7, and your weapon is set on fire for one turn per success. This means you deal 2 additional dice of Aggravated fire damage - pretty good at ignoring some kinds of armour, setting things alight, making ancient vampires flee in terror, and so on. What's annoying is how wimpy the duration is; rolling Gnosis at a difficulty of 7 may reliably give 1-2 successes, and that means the Gift will run out. It also makes it harder to use as a setup in advance; Silver Claws lasts for a whole scene, while if you use this a turn before combat starts it might elapse too swiftly.<br /> <br /> You then have to deal with the fact that melee simply isn't the best possible option for Garou - though this would combine well with difficulty 5 Fetish weapons and the hand blade Gift.<br /> <br /> I'm marking this as Amber - if you're a melee focused Garou there's no reason not to have this, but you'll find it elapses mid fight in many cases. Being able to start fires is handy, sure, but as a rank 3 Garou you aren't hankering for a way to start small fires by setting a knife ablaze.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Silver Claws:</b></u></span> See the Ahroun entry. This Gift is potentially more risky on a non-Ahroun, it should be noted; an Ahroun can burn off excess Rage produced by this Gift very swiftly, while a character with a Rage of 1 or 2 may struggle if they fall behind for some reason; on a turn where you spend or roll Gnosis you won't be able to burn off the Rage given by this Gift, and it could accumulate over time. Regardless of your Rage, however, it's a great Gift.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Talons of the Falcon*:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis, and make a Dexterity + Brawl attack with your claws. The attack deals 3 additional LEVELS of damage, and all the damage from the attack can't be regenerated for the rest of the scene. Though note that it says regenerated, not healed. As we've seen with the Glass Walker force lightning Gift, reliable damage is pretty good, though of course you do have to hit your opponent, and soak is still a thing. Coup de Grace is better, in my opinion, as it costs no Gnosis, applies to your next successful attack, and DOUBLES damage output.<br /> <br /> This is great on a character with a Rage of 1 or 2, so a Ragabash or Theurge who has left their Rage at starting levels. Ultimately a single powerful strike may be much better than two wimpy ones. On a more dedicated combat character with a Rage of 3+, the tradeoff is worse.<br /> <br /> I'm marking this as a Amber, but with potential to be Green, because its potential as a finishing move against a foe on their last legs is pretty great! I personally wouldn't bother with it, because my Ahroun's action economy is very Rage focused (and they'll be regenerating Rage with Silver Claws at this Rank), but on many, many characters it would be very strong, even for 9xp. Would I suggest buying it out of list? Probably not. Overall the fact that you need to consider whether you should take it or not is, to me, a sign that it should be Amber as a baseline.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Wrath of Gaia:</b></u></span> This is a good execution of a terror power. Only creatures of the Wyrm are targeted. It lasts for a whole scene. You spend a Gnosis and roll Charisma + Intimidation (IMO Appearance would work better), and any creature of the Wyrm who looks upon you must get equal or greater successes on a Willpower roll, difficulty 7, or flee from you. Given how much of a threat huge swarms of weak Banes and Fomori can be, the power to make an army of them flee from you is pretty great!<br /> <br /> The only reason not to take this would be if you have poor social stats for the roll, so I'm not even sticking a * on it - it'll prove worthy even if you only use it as a prep power before ambushes and similar.<br /> <br /> ================================================== ===============<br /> <br /> Wrath of Gaia is great to buy on anyone.<br /> <br /> Talons of the Falcon... perhaps. Possibly. But it's a tricky one - if you'd benefit from a decisive damage boost of +3 levels of damage (equivalent to +6 dice of damage!), it might be worth it, especially if you're a surgical assassin type fighter with lower Rage, focused on a single big hit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-14T03:01:47-07:00","id":1517930,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 3 Stargazer Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Clarity*:</b></u></span> You may roll for this Gift (Perception + Enigmas difficulty 7) to ignore things which impede your vision, such as fog, rain and pitch darkness.<br /> <br /> Against illusions, you can see past them if you get more successes on the check than the creator did on the illusion.<br /> <br /> Given that this has no cost, and the roll is doable, there's no reason not to activate this constantly, in every single scene, which is a problem some costless Gifts have. Still, it's definitely usable, and the ability to see past illusions could be very handy. There are other Gifts that let you see in the dark or ignore visual penalties, but this is a pretty usable one. I don't have anything against it, it isn't particularly exciting or particularly bad.<br /> <br /> This could be Green in a game with many enemies wielding illusions, from Gifts to Arts to Magick.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Merciful Blow:</b></u></span> This is an odd one. Of course it's annoyingly similar to Mercy, the Level 1 CoG Gift, but this is different in some ways.<br /> <br /> Spend a Gnosis, attune yourself to your foe, and your attacks don't apply any lasting injury. They are not downgraded to Bashing damage; you can still dish out Aggravated, and avoid soak and regeneration, but you know your foe won't die.<br /> <br /> A target you defeat with this power regains all health lost to your attacks and remains unconscious for the rest of the scene; this is 100% guaranteed.<br /> <br /> There are a few things worth keeping in mind here:<ul><li>You are guaranteed a scene's worth of unconsciousness, which is hard to achieve with some kinds of enemies (such as spirits); RAW it's very hard to defeat a spirit without disincorporating it</li> <li>This Gift is not explicitly restricted to your fangs, claws and punches; RAW it works with bullets, Klaives and so on</li> <li>The target heals the damage you apply instantly, whereas in Mercy they have to take time to heal the Bashing damage</li> </ul><br /> It's worth getting if you're a pacifist Stargazer, and it's better/worse than Mercy in some weird niche ways. You might consider spending 5xp on Mercy, and getting it at the start of the game, instead of spending 9xp on this when you're deep into it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Sense Balance*:</b></u></span> See the Philodox entry.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Wind's Returning Favor:</b></u></span> This Gift provides a defensive action which disarms foes and takes their weapon. When attacked with a melee weapon you may spend 1 WP, roll Dexterity + Athletics (difficulty of the target's Wits + Melee), and if you get more successes than the target you take their weapon. You also subtract successes from their attack, making this similar to a dodge/parry.<br /> <br /> Stealing a foe's Klaive is neat, though any self-respecting Garou will have a Wits of 2+ and if they're swinging a melee weapon they'll have a Melee of 3+. A difficulty as low as 5 is great, but as high as 9 or 10 is pretty bad. Spirit enemies, some of whom clearly use some kind of weapon, will have a difficulty of their Gnosis as it replaces Wits, so often 3-6ish, but sometimes as high as 9-10.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately the Gift has a pretty mean curve; the higher your foe's Dexterity + Melee, the more successes they will have on their attack. The higher their Wits + Melee, the higher your difficulty to fight back. If they're stabbing you at a difficulty of 5-7, with 8-10 dice, and you're resisting with 8-10 dice at a difficulty of 8+, then you're in trouble as the Gift will almost never trigger.<br /> <br /> And that's the real problem here; if your foe is so dangerous with a melee weapon you want to disarm them in a single defensive move, that you NEED this Gift, the Gift is ultimately at its weakest. Because the mighty Grand Klaive won't, typically, be in the hands of a fool with little training in wielding it. It could happen, sure, Silver Fang Failson holding a legendary Klaive is certainly a thing, but even then.<br /> <br /> If you're mugged by a drunken fool, this Gift will trigger. But you hardly need that, do you? What you need is to take a dangerous Aggravated damage weapon off someone, and that's where this Gift is at its worst. With a fixed difficulty of 6 it would be solid, but as it stands the variable difficulty works against it.<br /> <br /> Why is this almost Red? Because ultimately this Gift could prove detrimental, it could cause you harm. When 4 successes with a Grand Klaive are headed your way, attempting a difficulty 6 dodge is far superior to attempting a difficulty 9 disarm. The Gift is basically a trap; you waste 9xp on it, you feel good disarming some idiot who couldn't have hurt you anyways, you spend your precious WP, and when you truly need it you get sliced in half because the probability was never in your favour against a real threat.<br /> <br /> The saving grace of this Gift is that you can choose to activate it when your foe fails or botches, or gets 1 success on their swing. Even then it's a niche of a niche.<br /> <br /> ================================================== ========<br /> <br /> Clarity might be worth 15xp if you desperately need it; if illusions will be a major theme of the chronicle then go for it. Wind's Returning Favor could be useful to Klaive Duelists as a way of stunting on weaker foes or as a way of capitalizing on blunders.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 07-14-2023, 05:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-14T13:53:38-07:00","id":1517976,"title":"","content":"While we're in a section of Gifts that I highly doubt is going to have me commenting much, I am kinda curious about a meta analysis at the end of this process to see if there's a reason to think some of this is more of a conceptual problem with the number of Tribes and trying to give them all unique sets of powers. Obviously there needs to be some leeway for powers that are on-point for a splat but just written badly (Icy Chill of Despair is definitely a mess, but it also very much what you'd think Shadow Lords should be capable of), but actually looking at the totals at the end could be interesting.<br /> <br /> Oh, also, you did Silent Striders twice, the second one is the Rank 3 Stargazers Gifts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-14T14:38:04-07:00","id":1517982,"title":"","content":"Honestly I think they needed to have Gifts repeated and reflavoured more often, and use more variants/upgrades of Gifts. A Tribe can have a unique combination of 3-4 Gifts at each rank without having an endless list of annoyingly similar entries. For instance both the Children of Gaia and Stargazers could have Mercy at the first level without stepping on eachother's toes; that's only an overlap of a single Gift. But my biggest complaint is that there are too many &quot;destroy/disable an object&quot; Gifts. With the endless possibilities of infinite spirits of animals and plants and places and concepts and mythical beasts, we get a lot of the same repeated. Where are the Gifts where you're nourished by sunlight like a plant, or can hide Gnosis for later like a squirrel burying nuts, or can recover Willpower by stealing something shiny like a magpie.<br /> <br /> At the same time, idiosyncrasies can be cool and good if presented correctly; one Tribe might have a relatively simple flight Gift, another a Gift which gives you huge Pegasus wings, and if you acknowledge that this is weird it takes some of the stupidity out of it. Not all Gifts are equally good and useful; they're the blessings of spirits, some are better suited to the modern world. These idiosyncrasies should be the exception and not the rule however, since good game design and a fun set of balanced powers should come first.<br /> <br /> And thanks for catching that!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-14T15:27:44-07:00","id":1517984,"title":"","content":"I know how I feel about the WtA broad approach to Gifts, but it's always nice to have something more objective(-ish) to point to. While I believe it was only for Revised (including a bunch of supplements), someone did a great set of graphs with things like Gifts by Breed/Auspice/Tribe, per Rank across the board, how often different Attributes and Abilities are used, etc. It's one thing to feel like Gifts use Gnosis a lot, and another to see it's something like 2/3s of Gifts that use Gnosis/Rage/Willpower using Gnosis with 1/6 even split between the other two traits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-27T07:08:03-07:00","id":1518928,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1517984#post1517984\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I know how I feel about the WtA broad approach to Gifts, but it's always nice to have something more objective(-ish) to point to. While I believe it was only for Revised (including a bunch of supplements), someone did a great set of graphs with things like Gifts by Breed/Auspice/Tribe, per Rank across the board, how often different Attributes and Abilities are used, etc. It's one thing to feel like Gifts use Gnosis a lot, and another to see it's something like 2/3s of Gifts that use Gnosis/Rage/Willpower using Gnosis with 1/6 even split between the other two traits.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you have access to that graph, please share it with me, I would be very interested on checking it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4967,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","user":"lbeaumanior","date":". 07-27-2023, 12:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-27T10:42:30-07:00","id":1518939,"title":"","content":"Spoiled for size and all that.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">\u200b<img width=\"600\" height=\"2870\" data-align=\"none\" data-size=\"full\" loading=\"lazy\" border=\"0\" src=\"http://www.wyrmfoe.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/12/werewolf-gifts-stats1.jpg\" alt=\"\" data-fullsize-url=\"http://www.wyrmfoe.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/12/werewolf-gifts-stats1.jpg\" data-thumb-url=\"http://www.wyrmfoe.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/12/werewolf-gifts-stats1.jpg\" data-title=\"Click on the image to see the original version\" data-caption=\"\" class=\"bbcode-attachment thumbnail js-lightbox bbcode-attachment--lightbox\" itemprop=\"image\"/></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-29T10:14:45-07:00","id":1519118,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-11T03:58:03-07:00","id":1520368,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 3 Elder Brother Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Banish Totem*</b></u></span><br /> <br /> A few problems with this Gift:<ul><li>You need to know the name of the Totem.</li> <li>A difficulty of Totem score will often be difficulty 10 when dealing with a mid-to-large pack of PCs.</li> <li>Costs 1 Gnosis and 1 Willpower.</li> <li>Rolling Gnosis isn't amazing.</li> </ul>Still, if you get it right, and have the right targets, this Gift could prove pretty effective... in the niche situation of your enemies having a pretty powerful magical link. If you're fighting a pack who are all psychically linked by a spirit which is giving them powerful combat buffs, sure, nice. But -1 Dexterity, or -2 Strength, and turning off telepathy isn't THAT amazing. It's situational, and also not THAT good in the situations where it comes up.<br /> <br /> For this to be better, the mechanical benefit of being coordinated by a totem spirit would need to be stronger, or more important, or being suddenly robbed of it would need to be more harmful.<br /> <br /> Anyways, the * is for abnormal games where you're constantly fighting packs with powerful spirit patrons, and get to watch a whole group of Garou fall to their death after you cut them off from Owl in the Umbra.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Chains of Mist</b></u></span><br /> <br /> Concentrating for a turn is bad. Spending Gnosis is bad. A difficulty 7 activation is bad. And your reward is that you hit 1 target per success with a weak, if broad, debuff. Their WP, Rage and Gnosis are treated as being 1 lower for the purpose of rolls (which arguably includes rolls where their WP, Rage or Gnosis are the difficulty). The issue is that this is just &quot;-1 dice to all rolls&quot; in most situations. You can't apply multiple chains to one target either.<br /> <br /> It's pricey, difficult, and doesn't do much. It also doesn't stop your target from running away or disappearing. Very similar to a disappointing Theurge Gift with similar mechanics.<br /> <br /> The edge case for this is that 10 Elder Brothers of this rank or higher could bind any conventional Spirit (only exceptionally powerful ones have stats of 11+). But there are better ways to do that, and it doesn't matter to a PC. Even in a game with 5 Elder Brother PCs, which is a push, it comes online far too late.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Invisibility</b></u></span><br /> <br /> Between the Gnosis cost, halving your walking speed, being broken by actions which draw attention to yourself, having an activation roll, being beatable with a mundane roll, and having a very high difficulty in a situation where you'd actually want invisibility... well it's just invisibility. You're a Garou. Scent, sound, these are huge. It's not like you can fight with this turned on. It's not like you can track; you're moving too slowly for most quarry. It's not like you can carry out a half decent investigation or lay down the base of a ritual. It's good for escaping, thieving, some forms of spying. If you want to escape, Lupus, or fly. If you want to spy, Scry.<br /> <br /> All in all this is just much too conservative for a 3rd level Gift. But it's definitely not red.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Rending the Craft</b></u></span><br /> <br /> Ignoring this Gift contradicting itself (the Armour Charm is disabled, apparently, but the passive strength from Potence isn't, despite those both being... the same), this is amazing. After a successful claw attack you need only spend a point of WP to turn off all active magical effects enhancing the target. Sadly this does mean you can't claw away curses and so on, but that's probably for the best. Given that the most dangerous of enemies will be carrying around a number of magical enhancements, this is amazing. It's even more amazing in crossover games, too, but it'll shine just fine against Banes, Fomori, BSDs, mad Fera and rival Garou.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Scrying</b></u></span><br /> <br /> A remote viewing power, keep in mind that RAW this grants sight only. IMO that makes sense; you're looking at a reflection of distant events in a reflective surface.<br /> <br /> Perception + Occult, difficulty 10 if you lack a link to your target, difficulty 8 if you have something tied to the person or location.<br /> <br /> I'm not going to list a bunch of creative applications because it's so broad, but if you're going to get this Gift it doesn't hurt to be a magpie. A shiny rock from the Caern, a spoon from that fancy Kinfolk manor, a pen from the experimental gene clinic, and so on.<br /> <br /> Absolutely invest in the necessary Language merit / spec in an Ability / secondary Ability to lip-read, whatever that is at your table. The Gnosis cost is a little annoying but well worth it, and helps to make this power less un-fun for the table; you have to carefully pick where you view!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-11T09:50:12-07:00","id":1520397,"title":"","content":"Um, I don't see how Rending the Craft contradicts itself? The Armor Charm has a spirit spend 2 Essence for +Gnosis soak dice for the rest of the scene. It's not a permanent buff to soak.<br /> <br /> One of the reasons Rend the Craft is such a solid Green is that so few Charms (and Gifts for that matter) are permanent boosts instead of temporary Essence fueled ones. A lot of the best tricks your main big threats (banes and BSDs) can toss out are subject to being deactivated with this. Even against something like a Nexus Crawler that can spam crazy effects, having one character that can (at least as long as their WP reserves hold out) undo the bane's reality warping shenanigans and let the rest of the pack fight the enemy on a consistent battlefield is awesome.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-20T10:58:55-07:00","id":1521045,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1512577#post1512577\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Lightning Reflexes:</b></u></span> This Gift interferes with minutiae of the rules that no one, and I mean no one, uses. I follow the rules pretty close to the book, hell I switch up the ini order each round! But in order for this Gift to matter you need to be declaring your actions in reverse order each round. If you are strictly following that setup, going from slowest to fastest and asking people to state what they'll be doing this turn, then this would be Amber.<br /> <br /> Since most people don't do that... this'll be Red. You can also pull off a defensive action using one of your Rage actions if needs be.<br /> <br /> I reckon 99% of players won't know that aborting an action is a thing until they read this gift. I've been playing this system for close to 10 years and I've yet to see anyone use the rules, but that's a limited number of tables. Like I say, Amber if you follow the rules to the letter, including the weird ones you rarely see implemented.<br /> <br /> My change for this would be a pretty simple; -1 difficulty to defensive actions against individuals you've already attacked in the scene, benefit disappears if you turn to flee or try to stop the fighting.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200b<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1512645#post1512645\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Also, on Lightning Reflexes... a lot of people do actually use the full rules. Knocking a Gift down to Red because it... uses the RAW is pretty harsh. Lightning Reflexes is really useful tactically for Get that aren't Ahrouns and aren't going to be drowning in Rage for lots of extra actions, or getting Spirit of the Fray on the cheap. Being able to declare all offenses and abort to defense for no cost in-combat lets Get be aggressive tactically without all of them having to build like Ahrouns.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Agreeing here with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> RAW the gift is not bad, as per your own listing, this should be Amber. Not a gift that you must take (not with Razor Claws or Resist Pain there) but certainly a reasonable choice for non-Ahrouns.\u200b\u200b<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1520397#post1520397\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Um, I don't see how Rending the Craft contradicts itself? The Armor Charm has a spirit spend 2 Essence for +Gnosis soak dice for the rest of the scene. It's not a permanent buff to soak.<br /> <br /> One of the reasons Rend the Craft is such a solid Green is that so few Charms (and Gifts for that matter) are permanent boosts instead of temporary Essence fueled ones. A lot of the best tricks your main big threats (banes and BSDs) can toss out are subject to being deactivated with this. Even against something like a Nexus Crawler that can spam crazy effects, having one character that can (at least as long as their WP reserves hold out) undo the bane's reality warping shenanigans and let the rest of the pack fight the enemy on a consistent battlefield is awesome.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also agreed, where is the contradiction in the gift <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11620_5.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11620\" data-vbnamecard=\"11620\">twig</a>?<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I must say, liking the thread so much. What about the Gifts on the <b>Changing Ways</b> book? I love the Auspice gifts there but I would like to see your ratings.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-20T13:56:37-07:00","id":1521063,"title":"","content":"Given how time intensive thee things are, and there's still Ranks 4, 5, 6, and the core book Camp Gifts to cover? I think any plans for other W20 supplements with Gifts in them can wait a bit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-25T04:59:58-07:00","id":1521373,"title":"","content":"I'd misremembered the Armor charm as being a flat soak buff, rather than an activated power!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-25T05:45:14-07:00","id":1521375,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 3 Younger Brother Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Blood of the North*:</b></u></span> I like the permanence. This lets you ignore all penalties from the cold, gain +5 soak against cold-based attacks, and reduce the difficulty of all Survival rolls (including activating Gifts?) in cold environments.<br /> <br /> Naturally this gets a * because if you're playing a game set in Alaska or an Ice Age, it's amazing! In such a setting spirits and fomori will be far more likely to have icy attacks, and such penalties and survival rolls will come up more often.<br /> <br /> Typically this will get an Amber. Nice to have, but a bit weak for a level 3 Gift. Better if you can lure your enemies into a freezing cold environment, or expose the entire scene to a source of cold-based damage (ultra-cold air or water which inflicts lethal damage each turn, tanks of liquid nitrogen exploding, the breath of a freezing cold spirit filling the battlefield etc.). Like with fire immunity for rank 1 homids, make this gift work for you.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Bloody Feast*:</b></u></span> Neat flavour, weird mechanics.<br /> <br /> You must inflict at least 1 lethal or agg with a bite on a creature which bleeds, but doesn't have toxic blood. So... Garou and Fera, excluding Ratkin. Spirits don't have blood, by and large. Fomori do, but often have toxic blood of some kind, and in some cases won't have blood at all. Humans work with this Gift, but you don't need bonus Strength to rip them apart with your teeth.<br /> <br /> Assuming the target is valid, and worth using this on, you roll Gnosis (hopefully 3+ dice) against a difficulty of the target's Stamina + 3. So against a proper Werewolf enemy, the kind you will struggle against and need bonus strength to help with, chances are it'll be a difficulty of 10. You then gain +1 strength for every TWO levels of damage you've inflicted. So if they soak, if you roll poorly on strength...<br /> <br /> This bonus Strength lasts for two turns per success on the Gnosis roll.<br /> <br /> Ultimately this bonus strength doesn't last for long enough, it is too swingy, and is best used against a chicken. Or innocent bystander. After all with them the Gnosis roll will have a difficulty of 4-5, and you'll deal a bunch of damage for +2-5 strength.<br /> <br /> Given that rolling Gnosis means no bonus actions, and you have to test for Frenzy after using this, meaning you're bound to wind up in Frenzy or even Thrall if you rely on it, this Gift is just bad. If your ST lets you feast on rats you keep in your pockets, sure, so this can get an asterisk to account for the possibility your ST is vulnerable to &quot;my rogue has a pet rat who helps him always get sneak attack&quot; level shenanigans. It's also worth remembering that generally speaking claws are better than bites, if you're a dedicated build with combat gifts, such as an Ahroun.<br /> <br /> If you want to fix this, make it last for the whole scene, and make it grant a fixed bonus such as gaining additional Strength equal to your Rank. The Frenzy risk is cool, and flavourful, so keep that so long as the Gift is good and worth using.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sky Running:</b></u></span> Much faster than Spirit of the Bird. Doesn't cost Gnosis. Willpower, typically, is easier to replenish and more plentiful. Works in any form, so hilariously you can run through the air leaving a trail of fire in Homid. Doesn't debuff you.<br /> <br /> The main drawback of this power is that you must keep moving or it fails, and furthermore you leave a trail of fire which is very conspicuous and also Veil-risking.<br /> <br /> All in all this is strong in the Umbra, great in high octane werewolf fights, amazing for fleeing and chasing, but should be used carefully if you require any subtlety at all. I'm going Green because I struggle to imagine passing on gaining the ability to fly in exchange for 1WP for 4 hours, even if it has some strings attached.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Wisdom of Ancient Ways*:</b></u></span> See the Philodox entry.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-25T15:11:49-07:00","id":1521406,"title":"","content":"Not arguing the rating on Bloody Feast... the mechanics alone make it a overwrought mess that near impossible to use and is never, ever worth it (It's a Gnosis roll, no Rage actions on the turn you activate it, basically cutting off one turn off it's real duration). If the mechanics were better, I don't think the bite vs. claw issue would be a consideration. The small hit of not being able to pile on a bunch of claw boosting Gifts to activate some bonus strength via a bite that you can then boost your claws with even more is well worth it in theory. Garou bites are plenty dangerous.<br /> <br /> The whole blood thing is less of an issue for me on enemies that count (mages and changelings are valid targets too, and in a pure WtA game you can make vampires a valid target on interpretation grounds), but rather it doesn't make any sense to have a blood-based restriction at all. It's a Gift taught by a cannibal spirit... cannibals aren't just blood suckers. Get someone hungry enough and they'll eat tainted meat to survive now even if they know they'll be sick later. Garou can even &quot;eat&quot; spirits in a fashion, while being part spirit themselves.<br /> <br /> It would be way better to say that you need to swallow something while biting the enemy. Then you're taking a tactical risk when it comes to toxic enemies, vampire blood, and so on. That also makes the Frenzy check more sensible.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-26T08:55:19-07:00","id":1521429,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1521406#post1521406\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not arguing the rating on Bloody Feast... the mechanics alone make it a overwrought mess that near impossible to use and is never, ever worth it (It's a Gnosis roll, no Rage actions on the turn you activate it, basically cutting off one turn off it's real duration). If the mechanics were better, I don't think the bite vs. claw issue would be a consideration. The small hit of not being able to pile on a bunch of claw boosting Gifts to activate some bonus strength via a bite that you can then boost your claws with even more is well worth it in theory. Garou bites are plenty dangerous.<br /> <br /> The whole blood thing is less of an issue for me on enemies that count (mages and changelings are valid targets too, and in a pure WtA game you can make vampires a valid target on interpretation grounds), but rather it doesn't make any sense to have a blood-based restriction at all. It's a Gift taught by a cannibal spirit... cannibals aren't just blood suckers. Get someone hungry enough and they'll eat tainted meat to survive now even if they know they'll be sick later. Garou can even &quot;eat&quot; spirits in a fashion, while being part spirit themselves.<br /> <br /> It would be way better to say that you need to swallow something while biting the enemy. Then you're taking a tactical risk when it comes to toxic enemies, vampire blood, and so on. That also makes the Frenzy check more sensible.</div> </div> </div> </div>All very sensible, agreed. Just having the Gift be based on eating part of an enemy would be better design.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-26T15:39:12-07:00","id":1521448,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Homid Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Body Shift:</b></u></span> I don't get the logic behind this being a Homid Gift, but the Gift fills a useful niche. Unfortunately it does so terribly.<br /> <br /> A difficulty 9 Stamina + Primal-Urge is horrible. Even with a large dice pool, you'll often be botching, failing, or getting 1-2 successes. And the Gift only lets you transfer dots from one Attribute to another for a Scene, and only one dot per two successes!<br /> <br /> Essentially this Gift is far too hard to use, doesn't last long enough, and doesn't do enough. Also, it doesn't explicitly allow you to increase an Attribute above 5, though arguably that's implied. All in all this one is awful.<br /> <br /> My change to this Gift would be to make it so that on a success you can freely rearrange your base dots in physical Attributes, with duration of one scene per success. Yes, it would let people just shift all their Strength and Stamina into Dexterity to be a glass stiletto of ultra-precise doom, but they're going to suffer from their low strength and stamina. It would also only let you move around the regular dots, not the bonus dots from the Forms.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Bury the Wolf*:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis, roll Gnosis, difficulty of your own Willpower. Since your own Willpower should be as high as you can get it or justify, from a char-op perspective, this isn't ideal.<br /> <br /> On a success you overcome The Curse, and hide away your Rage, and all your other forms. You are stuck in Homid, you seem human, and you pass as human to all forms of supernatural detection. That's pretty neat! The duration can be up to a lunar month as well.<br /> <br /> For all that's bad about this Gift, it provides something very important which is difficult to acquire otherwise. For a character with a Rage of 5 or higher, The Curse can be exceptionally debilitating. I'm giving this a ranking of Green, on the assumption you have a decent Gnosis pool, and a Rage which is worth hiding. The asterisk is for those who don't need this Gift. Unfortunately this Gift becomes semi-useless, at at least pretty risky, if you have a Willpower of 10.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Cocoon:</b></u></span> This 100% feels like a Metis Gift. Taking bizarre, sometimes creepy aspects of a long list of critters is more their deal.<br /> <br /> You spend a Gnosis to generate the cocoon, and while it exists enemies must deal damage equal to your Stamina + Rituals to harm you. Since your Stamina could be as high as 8, and your Rituals could be as high as 5, this could be very effective! I'd say a Stamina + Rituals of 7 is the bare minimum to want to use this Gift, and even then 7 might be risky with powerful enemies. Given a potential &quot;soak&quot; of 13, you could be nigh-invincible with this cocoon, however if it is pierced it is instantly destroyed.<br /> <br /> This Gift is a good way to survive against exposure, the elements, disease, corruption, dangerous crowds with weak attacks, but of course it is a Veil risk, best used in Crinos for highest soak, and is a last resort. You shouldn't really be planning on getting into a situation where you need to use this Gift, but it's handy to have. The duration is good, and it can be extended with more Gnosis.<br /> <br /> Decent, but not a must-have, especially at a cost of 12/20 XP.<br /> <br /> One pretty nasty use of this is as a suicide bomb, minus the suicide. Bring along a very dangerous wide area weapon, set it off, and cocoon yourself. Do keep in mind that this cocoon could be carried places, thrown into acid, buried deep under the earth, and you gain no protections against starvation and thirst. This is an emergency defense which is meant for short term use.<br /> <br /> If you don't mind a bad reputation, this could cleverly win you a lot of Challenges. Say someone is tasked with taking something from you; they won't be able to do that if you're inside a cocoon which is far too strong for them to break.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Spirit Ward:</b></u></span> This feels like a Lupus Gift, but that's alright.<br /> <br /> You roll Manipulation + Rituals, difficulty 7, and for every success you subtract one die from the dice pools of all spirits within 100 feet. Spirits within 50 feet, except for Caern Spirits and your own pack spirit, lose 1 point of Essence each turn per success. So for example, with 4 successes, you'd be subtracting 4 dice from the dice pools of all spirits within 100 feet, and any spirit within 50 feet would lose 4 points of Essence (which is both health and fuel) per turn. That's pretty great! The Gnosis cost is annoying, but you can set this up at the start of a fight to debilitate foes. There are even some dangerous spirits who have very wonky stats, such as a Rage of 4-5 or Willpower of 4-5, and so subtracting a few dice will seriously hamper them.<br /> <br /> A good example are spirits with Rage as high as 7-8 but very, very low Willpower; they're heavy hitters who typically miss, and with this ward they'll almost always miss, or lose the ability to attack at all.<br /> <br /> Assuming a good dice pool and some Gnosis to spend, this is a great Gift.<br /> <br /> One good use of this Gift would be to place it, with other Garou defending you as you do so, in a place of great corruption. Even if you die, there's nothing to indicate the ward would stop. Presumably the ward could be erased or destroyed, and thus it will need defending to do its work, but there are worse ways to deal with huge crowds of spirits. A cheeky combo would be to go into a cocoon and then use this, but since you're &quot;immobilized&quot; in the cocoon that might be impossible.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-30T13:46:58-07:00","id":1521693,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1521448#post1521448\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Cocoon:</b></u></span> This 100% feels like a Metis Gift. Taking bizarre, sometimes creepy aspects of a long list of critters is more their deal.<br /> <br /> You spend a Gnosis to generate the cocoon, and while it exists enemies must deal damage equal to your Stamina + Rituals to harm you. Since your Stamina could be as high as 8, and your Rituals could be as high as 5, this could be very effective! I'd say a Stamina + Rituals of 7 is the bare minimum to want to use this Gift, and even then 7 might be risky with powerful enemies. Given a potential &quot;soak&quot; of 13, you could be nigh-invincible with this cocoon, however if it is pierced it is instantly destroyed.<br /> <br /> This Gift is a good way to survive against exposure, the elements, disease, corruption, dangerous crowds with weak attacks, but of course it is a Veil risk, best used in Crinos for highest soak, and is a last resort. You shouldn't really be planning on getting into a situation where you need to use this Gift, but it's handy to have. The duration is good, and it can be extended with more Gnosis.<br /> <br /> Decent, but not a must-have, especially at a cost of 12/20 XP.<br /> <br /> One pretty nasty use of this is as a suicide bomb, minus the suicide. Bring along a very dangerous wide area weapon, set it off, and cocoon yourself. Do keep in mind that this cocoon could be carried places, thrown into acid, buried deep under the earth, and you gain no protections against starvation and thirst. This is an emergency defense which is meant for short term use.<br /> <br /> <b>If you don't mind a bad reputation, this could cleverly win you a lot of Challenges. Say someone is tasked with taking something from you; they won't be able to do that if you're inside a cocoon which is far too strong for them to break.</b></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Well, it shows cunning and checking the wordplay. Not necessarily a bad reputation.<br /> <br /> <br /> Regarding the Cocoon itself, there is another scenario: protecting something valuable from the environment, it has to be small enough to fit with you in the cocoon, but reasonable. Also depending on how the cocoon is ruled, you could cover something alive and small? A bird, a rat, a monkey? Small baby?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-30T14:43:34-07:00","id":1523372,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1513629#post1513629\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Shadows of the Impergium:</b></u></span> If you're regularly encountering regular humans (remember that supernaturals are all Delerium immune!) with WP 9 and 10... something weird is going on. Delirium is a double edged sword, but I'm gonna say that overall it's beneficial to you in a lot of ways. Making foes panic, lose coordination, flee, attack wildly, forget you were ever there... and this Gift grants it (if weaker) to Hispo, which may be your preferred fighting form, and also eliminates the risk of humans who react in clever ways when you go Crinos. The ability to repress this also lets you avoid collateral damage, and means the Hispo form doesn't get permanently altered in a way which removes utility.<br /> <br /> All in all this Gift has some handy utility, but it's more like a Quality of Life improvement than a good buff. Regular Delirium does the job fine against humans. Humans who have WP of 7+ and are not supernatural in some way (Bane, Kinfolk, psychic etc.) will be rare in most games. This might be handy if you're regularly being hunted by badass hunters and mercenaries and First Teams.<br /> <br /> If you prefer Crinos form to Hispo in fights, and you aren't going to be facing high-WP mundane human enemies, this becomes red.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> There is a niche combo (requires Rank 4), but very VERY powerful:<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Terror of the Dire Wolf</i> (Rank 4), which can inflict Delirium on partly or formerly human creatures (practically anything other than garou and fera, spirits and the like) including mages, vampires, and wraiths.<br /> <br /> Combine that with <i>Shadows of the Impergium</i>, always active with no test required, which causes those affected by your Delirium to be treated as though their Willpower is <b>five</b> lower when determining the effects.<br /> <br /> RAW, basically anything that isn't another changer or a spirit is probably helpless if you manage to activate <i>Terror of the Dire Wolf</i>. Per the Delirium chart, once the -5 is applied (WP 6 or less), 86% of affected enemies curl into a fetal position and do nothing. Even those with 10 WP forget.\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-28T08:34:51-08:00","id":1526067,"title":"","content":"Another combo for Red Talons, again requires Rank 4:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1512880#post1512880\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Eye of the Eagle:</b></u></span> Eagle and Falcon have likely had litigation over Gift names. This Gift and Eye of the Falcon both existing feels like a joke, but they do quite different things. This Gift adds miles onto your visual perception range, but doesn't ignore obstacles. This means that if you are atop a good vantage you can see for miles and miles. This is handy for scouting and guarding, both of which matter a lot to Garou. It isn't my cup of tea but it's good to have in your Pack.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200b<br /> <br /> Combine <i>Eye of the Eagle</i>, with <i>Howl of Death</i>, which only requires that you can see the target, and they can hear you (and a howl can carry for miles).\u200b Since neither gift costs Gnosis, Rage or Willpower, and few things can move fast enough to hit you back, you can with good positioning kill enemies one by one as long as you keep retreating before they get to you.<br /> <br /> So in a good vantage point, you can see and be heard from ABSURD distances, and use <i>Howl of Death</i> to murder rivals. Not bad for the Lupus Red Talon combination (that just from the gifts listed here tends to be on the weaker side).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4967,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","user":"lbeaumanior","date":"as it is not applicable. 11-28-2023, 02:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-30T16:34:29-08:00","id":1526195,"title":"","content":"I've been away from the WW forums for a while and only now just saw this thread, I made a complete (as of 2018) Gift compilation that I saved as a Word docfile on my GoogleDocs (found <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1AnhMAU9rRQIztRRVprVc7Fsec1nmhjwA/edit?usp=drive_link&amp;ouid=117821193628514848482&amp;rtpof=true&amp;sd=true\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>). I just decided to see if it needed updating and I just found a long undiscovered typo in the Gift: Terrain Shift found in Changing Ways, p.152. There's a sentence that reads as follows: &quot;These shifts are minor and localized, limited to an area of up to ; he couldn't build a bridge to cross the Grand Canyon this way.&quot; As you can all tell, the area limit of the Gift is missing and Onyx Path never got around to fixing the typo even in the latest PDF of Changing Ways (which I just downloaded from DriveThruRPG and checked les than ten minutes ago). Has anyone come up with a house rule to determine the area of effect for this Gift?<br /> <br /> Also, my link above might become obsolete soon. If you want to find that Gift compilation again after I've updated it, just go the link to my GoogleDocs in my signature and find it in the My Drive&gt;Public Stuff&gt;Public Classic World of Darkness&gt; Public Werewolf the Apocalypse folder.\u200b<br /> <br /> EDIT: Yeah, I'm finding a bunch of typos and even came across a couple of Bone Gnawer Gifts that might be Level Four instead of Level Three as listed as a result of a possible typo. I updated the Gift Compilation again for the first time in five years and the link above has likewise been updated.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/835/7xu8.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/546/wpur.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/folderview?id=0By9UuUWWH6CiMVlhWHMwNWcyaG8&amp;usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Complete Garou Gifts List, Fera Gifts List, Garou Totems List and other assorted OPP/White Wolf goodies</a>","edited":{"userid":2710,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2710-su-tehp","user":"Su-tehp","date":". 11-30-2023, 09:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2710-su-tehp","name":"Su-tehp","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2710_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"532"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-16T13:55:27-08:00","id":1526941,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1518939#post1518939\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Spoiled for size and all that.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><img width=\"600\" height=\"2870\" data-align=\"none\" data-size=\"full\" loading=\"lazy\" border=\"0\" src=\"http://www.wyrmfoe.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/12/werewolf-gifts-stats1.jpg\" alt=\"\" data-fullsize-url=\"http://www.wyrmfoe.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/12/werewolf-gifts-stats1.jpg\" data-thumb-url=\"http://www.wyrmfoe.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/12/werewolf-gifts-stats1.jpg\" data-title=\"Click on the image to see the original version\" data-caption=\"\" class=\"bbcode-attachment thumbnail js-lightbox bbcode-attachment--lightbox\" itemprop=\"image\"/></div></div> </div> </div></div> </div> </div> </div>I just realized something, some Merits and Fetishes are incredible value for cost based on this image:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li><b>Friend of Sorcery</b> (5 pt. Merit) gives you breed costs for the Theurge gifts (largest Auspice list), AND lowers the difficulty for Gift and Knowledge Occult rolls (the most used skill on Gifts). One of the best posible investments, especially for a Philodox (shortest Auspice list)</li> <li><b>Good Instincts</b> (3 pt. Merit), a -2 difficulty reduction on all all rolls involving Primal Urge or Survival. Probably even better than the previous merit: cheaper, involving more skills (the second and sixth most used), and a larger diff reduction.</li> <li><b>Land Amulet</b> (level 1 Fetish), +2 Primal-Urge dice when active, good with the previous merit.</li> <li><b>Uktena Scale</b> (level 2 Fetish), the owner may make any Enigmas rolls at -2 difficulty.</li> <li><b>Inner Sight </b>(2 pt. Merit), +2 dice for all Enigma rolls.</li> <li><b>Cunning Fetish</b> (level 1 Fetish), activation successes to Enigmas rating.</li> <li><b>Mother\u2019s Insight</b> (4 pt. Merit), -1 difficulty to all Wits rolls (except initiative), Wits is the fourth most used Attribute for Gifts, but you also use it for Mystic Rites. Quite potent for a Mystic focused ritualist like a Theurge.</li> <li><b>Born Leader</b> (1 pt. Merit), a -1 difficulty reduction on all all rolls involving Leadership.</li> <li><b>Natural Leader</b> (2 pt. Merit), +2 dice for all Leadership rolls, prerequisite Charisma 3 (the most used Attribute for Gifts), awesome in combination with the previous merit.</li> </ol><br /> <br /> Any other similar Merits or Fetishes?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4967,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","user":"lbeaumanior","date":". 02-14-2026, 03:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-16T16:05:31-08:00","id":1526949,"title":"","content":"I would just remind that it's a chart based on Revised, so a pure W20 game might be a bit different in how much bang you're really getting (esp. since it doesn't differentiate between Gifts that use static values or rolls).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-07T04:55:26-08:00","id":1548287,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b>HE'S BACK! HE CAN'T BE STOPPED! HE'S GONNA FINISH THE RANK 4 GIFTS AT LEAST!</b></span><br /> <br /> <u><b>Rank 4 Metis Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Gift of the Porcupine:</b></u></span> As always, I love drawing on weird, small, nuisance animals. Great to see that the Umbra is kind to Metis even if Garou Society isn't! Spending a Gnosis point on activation is a bad start, but as always a great Gift can make up for that. Porcupine does that in spades. Inflicting STR+1 Agg on anyone you tackle, grapple or immobilize is amazing on its own. That's great, already, even for a level 4 Gift. Then you factor in that the power also applies a thorns-like effect against anyone who strikes you while you're using the power! They take their own Strength in Aggravated damage unless they get 5+ successes on their attack to hit you.<br /> <br /> The key advantage of this Gift is that it lets you passively deal damage while taking hits and while immobilizing enemies. You get to focus your attention on activating Gifts, parrying or dodging dangerous attacks, handling a non-combat objective and so on.<br /> <br /> It's worth remembering however that this Gift will grievously wound bystanders. You become a walking hazard with this power, especially if you're forced into a Frenzy. Used intelligently this Gift is among the best (non-broken) Gifts in WTA, as it simply goes with everything and multiplies your damage output.<br /> <br /> There are many clever ways you could use this Gift in a Challenge, for example boasting that you can win a fight without making a single attack, but the Aggravated damage make it deadly by default. If you were to run into problems with this Gift in game and find it was making the experience worse for everyone, changing the damage to Lethal could make sense, but IMO this is unlikely to be necessary.<br /> <br /> Worth noting that the language of the Gift is a bit sloppy and it isn't clear whether this Gift inflicts dice of damage or levels of damage. Given that it doesn't explicitly say levels of damage, I'm going to assume dice.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Lash of Rage*:</b></u></span> Spend a Rage, roll Rage. Target must be within 100 feet and takes Aggravated damage equal to your successes. This is Unsoakable! If you repeat this power within a scene it hurts you as well as your target. While this power would be unstoppable in the hands of a Spirit, as a Garou you have 7-8ish levels of health to work with, and you risk killing yourself if you use it unwisely. Having a healer taking care of you while you unleash this attack repeatedly is feasible.<br /> <br /> This is another Metis Gift which focuses on self-hate and being loathed by society, which is interesting to navigate if your Metis comes from a background of tolerance and acceptance. Still, regardless of the individual, the bigotry against Metis has left its mark on the spirit world and the gifts it offers them!<br /> <br /> How good is this? The first attack is a no-projectile ranged attack you can use on any enemy you perceive within 100 feet. There's no roll to hit, you just roll Rage and inflict LEVELS of unsoakable damage as per the result. You can't spend WP naturally, as this is a Gift activation roll. The lack of a given difficulty either means difficulty 6, or that you roll against your general Rage difficulty which is determined by the phase of the moon, your Auspice, and with a -1 modifier if you're in Crinos. Given the potential to inflict 5 unsoakable Aggravated damage on a foe who doesn't even know they're fighting yet, leaving them gasping for air and filled with their own hemorrhaging blood, the power is good, but only if you have a Rage of 5 or higher.<br /> <br /> If your Rage is too low, this Gift is just not gonna cut it at Rank 4.<br /> <br /> All in all I'd say that this power just makes its way into Green, but it's only an auto-take if you have decent or great Rage, and if you otherwise struggle with ranged options. If you have an Aggravated damage ranged weapon via Fetishes or Talens or a Gift, this becomes more niche, but far from useless.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Rattler's Bite*:</b></u></span> Unlikely to matter, but keep in mind that the bonus damage from this gift is a venom, which means certain enemies will ignore it. This Gift is bite-only, and as a claw-lover that's a mark against it, but bites are pretty great and my dislike of them is more personal preference (I'm a fan of the Claw-only Gifts, and I think that putting a Bane or Fomor inside your mouth is asking for trouble!). Difficulty 5 to hit with no setup. Doubling the post-soak damage is pretty great, and it only costs a point of Rage which shouldn't be a precious resource at Rank 4 given the variety of ways to regenerate it, especially for a Metis.<br /> <br /> Note that you activate this Gift when you ATTEMPT the bite, so it's possible to waste the Rage, which is a lil annoying but in line with the RAW.<br /> <br /> I'd say I prefer Coup de Grace to this, but that's a Tribe-specific Gift. Worth noting that Coup is much cheaper all the same! The * is mostly to say that if you are heavily invested into Claws via Gifts and a Brawl spec, this Gift likely isn't for you and would be better to skip.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Wither Limb:</b></u></span> Disability is an under-utilized theme in Metis Gifts, and I like to see it played up here.<br /> <br /> This debuff power adds +2 to dexterity difficulties for the target, and if you target a leg they move half normal speed. The roll to activate is Willpower, which ought to be good, against the target's Stamina +4, or Willpower +4 in the case of most spirits of course, assuming they are valid targets with limbs.<br /> <br /> The Gnosis cost, and this being Rank 4, is where problems show up. At a lower rank, or with a WP/Rage cost, this would be Green. But while this Gift is balanced, and I don't think any changes are needed, it's on the weaker end. There are lots of cool uses! You can cripple a foe out of combat as a warning or threat or punishment. You can hobble a prisoner so they can't escape. You can make a packmate look like a harmless weakling for the scene.<br /> <br /> It's also worth noting that if an enemy has a Stamina of 5+, which is very common in BSDs (unless you catch them in Homid or out of Crinos), you'll waste your turn, AND a point of Gnosis, AND you won't get a bunch of Rage attacks/dodges this round. So this Gift winds up being best against... weaklings! Enemies who you could beat easily anyways. Given the high cost of a Rank 4 Gift, this winds up Amber, though that doesn't mean I dislike it by any means.<br /> <br /> As a final note, by the point you're this deep in the game, you probably have a better debuff depending on your Auspice or Tribe, so this becomes even more niche. Why not hit them with Paralyzing Stare or True Fear? I find myself wondering how many times this power has ever been used across all tables, given what I've mentioned above. Perhaps more often by NPCs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-07T05:24:38-08:00","id":1548288,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Lupus Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <u><b>Beast Life:</b></u> Leaving this one unrated, but it could be Green depending on assumptions and setting.<br /> <br /> Isn't it weird that Lupus don't get Beast Speech? This is a complicated and weird Gift, but my initial reaction is &quot;just buy Beast Speech for 5xp; you're a Lupus, it's easy to justify&quot;. However, Beast Life does a lot more than let you communicate with animals.<br /> <br /> It lets you spend Gnosis to draw in animals for miles around, up to 10 miles per success in fact. It also lets you give orders to the animals that show up, up to and including sacrificing their lives, so long as you pay or repay the appropriate animal spirit.<br /> <br /> This is a highly setting-dependent power. If you're in the Ice Age, you could get a small army of mammoths and sabretooths. If you're in South Africa, you could set a few Prides of Lions and a small family of Rhinos on your enemies, not to mention hippos. New York? All the rats and dogs and cats you could possibly ask for, along with 1000s of pigeons, crows and so on.<br /> <br /> That said, something about this power doesn't sit right with me. It feels like an Elder Animalism power. This ability is not in tune with nature, and even a Rank 4 Lupus isn't guaranteed to be a wise and sensible wielder of it. Yes, to reach Rank 4 you need to have shown &quot;wisdom&quot; (learning Rites, agreeing with and obeying your Elders, and perhaps shown some wisdom now and then, though that appears to be optional given the history of the Garou Nation!), but if all Rank 4+ Garou were sensible the WoD would be a very different place.<br /> <br /> If you can look past the &quot;I get every animal to come to me and do my bidding&quot; part, which is slightly easier to forgive given that it's a Rank 4 Gift, there's also the Veil implications, and the indiscriminate nature of the power. What you're triggering is essentially a natural disaster and ecocide. How many millions of insects die en route to you? How many pets run away and never come back? How much livestock break free? How many zoos and so on get torn asunder? How many Ratkin, Corax and other Fera now have you on the Kill List for completely fucking up the ecology of their respective species for a night, if not longer?<br /> <br /> Assuming you handwave all that and read the intent of the power, assuming they mean that useful animals who can safely come to you show up... it's good. Messy, but good. With enough assumptions to fill Noah's Arc, I'd call this Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Gnaw*:</b></u></span> This is a weird one. You roll Strength + 4 against a variable difficulty to chew through various objects.<br /> <br /> Here's the thing.<br /> <br /> You're a Rank 4 Garou. I don't care that you're a Lupus. So often it feels like the WTA writers imagined they were writing Gifts for WOLVES when they wrote Lupus Gifts. You are a Rank 4 Garou! You don't need a Gift to break a pair of handcuffs! You can hulk out at will by spending a point of Rage! This is where the insane power of the Werewolf basekit makes design space limited, but still, that's something you can be inventive with.<br /> <br /> The other benefit is +2 dice of damage to your Bites for a scene. I'm more of a Claw fan, but that's personal preference.<br /> <br /> Given that this costs 1 WP, and it adds +2 damage to your attacks IF you are into bites, I'd say that this is an auto-take for Bite Enjoyers. Yes, it costs 12xp, but 12xp for +2 damage to most of your attacks (again, assuming you default to biting!) is great. And the ability to gnaw your way through a titanium bar or similar is pretty great, though I still find it infuriating that we're getting Gifts intended to overcome wooden and steel objects at Rank 4, when you're standing at the base of the mountains of legend.<br /> <br /> Amber with a *, but scope to be Green if you're obsessed with Bite attacks.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Scream of Gaia*:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis, roll Rage. Everyone within 50 feet, besides you and your Pack, is blasted onto their ass and takes an unsoakable level of Bashing per success.<br /> <br /> Say you have a Rage of 3, 5 or 7. The difficulty is unstated, so it's presumably 6. 1-2ish, 3ish or 4ish damage on every foe in a huge AOE is pretty great. This could absolutely gut a small army of weak spirits with minimal essence! The effect of putting a load of enemies on their ass is pretty neat, but the benefit will depend on how the ST runs enemies standing back up, which varies a lot in my experience.<br /> <br /> Yes, enemies with regeneration and healing will recover, and you can't do anything else that turn, but assuming a Rage of 5 or more (*) this is pretty neat. It isn't your go-to, but it's a great option to adopt for only 12xp, which could help you to shatter a crowd of weak enemies, cow a crowd, and so on.<br /> <br /> Keep in mind that this power will hit friendlies, and within the range is basically a Small Animal Genocide. Cats, spiders, bugs etc. will all get messed up by this. That's great if you're fighting against a Pentex monstrosity which leaks subservient bugs everywhere, but terrible in a woodland. Used indiscriminately and repeatedly, you'll find yourself killing allies and bystanders, precious wildlife, Kinfolk and so on. Just because you can't see the hostages doesn't mean they can't get hurt by this Gift!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Terror of the Dire Wolf:</b></u></span> Spend a Rage, Manipulation + P-U roll, difficulty of WP, and can target groups. This power inflicts Delirium onto recipients, even if you aren't in Crinos, and also effects targets who are usually immune, such as Mages, Vampires and so on. If you are human, or were EVER human, this applies. Even Kinfolk fall victim, and only Garou/Fera are immune.<br /> <br /> That said, Delirium is already WP-dependent. Yes, many many many foes will flee in terror. But anyone with a Willpower of 7+ will still be able to fight you or mess with you as appropriate, despite their terror.<br /> <br /> This Gift can be incredibly powerful and can solve a bunch of problems on its own, but Delirium is a double-edged blade and can create as many problems as it solves. Used correctly this will protect against Veil Breaches and help to peacefully end fights. Used unwisely this could make things worse!<br /> <br /> I'd suggest using this against crowds, and to forcefully push away non-Changer enemies and leave BSDs/similar on their own.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-07T06:31:20-08:00","id":1548290,"title":"","content":"I'm back too then! :P<br /> <br /> Nothing much to add though:<br /> <br /> For Lash of Rage and Scream of Gaia it's important to remember than all Rage rolls count towards the potential to Frenzy. Rolling 4+ Success means you Frenzy from using these (5+ when you hit Rank 5, and 6+ if you ever hit Legendary). That's a big tactical consideration for these two Gifts, since the better you can use them the more likely you are to flip out. And it means you have to invest in even more Gnosis to keep up with your Rage so you don't attack your packmates too. The difficulty for any unspecified Rage check is based on moon phase too, which complicates how reliable your damage output is going to be as well. Though a positive rules note is that you roll the higher of your temporary or permanent Rage when making a Rage roll, so even if your permanent Rage is 1, if you purposefully angry up your blood up to 10... you can unleash one hell of an attack if you're going in expecting to open with one of these.<br /> <br /> A second note just for Scream of Gaia, it takes two turns to use, because you can't use Gnosis and Rage at the same time (at least until you snag a certain Rank 5 Glass Walker Gift).<br /> <br /> I'm not sure it changes much, but it's worth remembering that Gnaw works in any form... even homid. Sure, the big draw is still +2 damage for biting for a scene, but it's really useful for when you can't hulk out. If you just want a universal lock pick, Open Seal is better (esp. for lupus), but it's really more for when you can't hulk out and still need to bust through something. A very specific use-case that has come up twice for me.... is that a homid or lupus in their birth forms can use Gnaw to safely chew through silver. I get this falls under &quot;being creative&quot; with it, but after seeing a Ragabash lupus troll a BSD by using Gnaw to trash the BSD's grand klaive during a recon of the BSD's camp... it has a stronger place in my heart than my opposing preference for biting getting more love from the Gifts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-07T16:29:35-08:00","id":1548313,"title":"","content":"Good to see you!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1548290#post1548290\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A second note just for Scream of Gaia, it takes two turns to use, because you can't use Gnosis and Rage at the same time (at least until you snag a certain Rank 5 Glass Walker Gift).</div> </div> </div> </div>You can't use Rage and Gnosis on the same turn unless explicitly permitted to do so by a Gift! So if a Gift asks you to spend 1 Gnosis and 1 Rage to activate it, you can do that in a single turn. What you can't do is, for example, spend a Gnosis to turn on a Gift, and then spend Rage to get a bunch of bonus actions (unless you have a Gift or perhaps Fetish etc. which lets you do so, as you mention).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A player cannot use both Rage and Gnosis in the same turn, whether spending points or rolling the Trait. The only exceptions are certain Gifts that demand both to function. </div> </div> </div>This is from the W20 corebook.<br /> <br /> Great points on Frenzying! I'd forgotten that ANY Rage roll can make you frenzy!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-07T16:39:27-08:00","id":1548314,"title":"","content":"Doh, forgot that W20 went for that instead of the two round solution.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-07T16:50:16-08:00","id":1548315,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Ragabash Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Luna's Blessing:</b></u></span> This is an innate Gift, if a niche one. You basically become resistant to silver when the moon is visible in the sky. If the moon is behind clouds, brand new, hidden by an eclipse, below the horizon, or it's daytime (though the moon is sometimes visible during the day of course) then this Gift doesn't function. Given that this is arbitrary in many cases (e.g. whether it's a moonless night is something the ST just decides, it isn't usually plot-relevant) it falls to the ST to either let this Gift work or be a dick about it!<br /> <br /> Instead of dealing Aggravated damage, this Gift makes silver deal B/L damage depending on weapon type. It also adds a single 1 to the dice roll of an attacker, or two 1s during a full moon.<br /> <br /> This is handy, all in all, but so niche that it can never be a guaranteed good investment. Assuming that Klaives deal Lethal/Bludgeoning with this Gift, which is arguably a push since they deal Agg to targets with no special relationship with silver at all... this is okayish. But the problem is that while the Gift does point-blank say that silver deals B/L, Klaives say that they deal A. Which takes precedence? I'd go for the Gift, since it's a niche Rank 4 Gift, but still. It isn't certain.<br /> <br /> A really important part of the Gift, however, which is easily overlooked is the auto-1 added onto the roll. The impact on incoming damage, how often enemies hit, and how often you can dodge, might be great! But it remains niche since most foes won't be hitting you with silver clubs, though that is setting and campaign dependent. Kinfolk Rebellion? Silver mallets abound. Fighting spirits in the Umbra? Unlikely they'll be wielding silver weapons.<br /> <br /> Regardless of the exact implementation I'd call this Amber. It's never going to be Red, since it has its uses, but it's definitely on the lower end of Amber in my book.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Umbral Dodge:</b></u></span> This is just so fun that I can't put it below Green. Yes, it's niched by the fact that you have to spend Gnosis, and you have to be dodging a melee range attack, and you need to roll your dodge against the difficulty of the Gauntlet or +1 from the normal difficulty (so 7 minimum, typically, as you take the higher result)... but if the power goes off you can strand an enemy in the Gauntlet or the physical world! It's just such a fun maneuver and such an auto death sentence for vampires, humans, most Changelings, Mages and so on. Even the vast majority of Kinfolk, and some Fera, will really suffer if they're stranded in the Umbra.<br /> <br /> But what about spirits and BSDs and Fomori? Those are the big guys you run into constantly.<br /> <br /> Well, it's a great trap if you are confident in your Dexterity + Athletics. You're right there waiting to be struck. Your pack? They're on the other side of the Gauntlet awaiting a shocked enemy who they can pummel into the dirt. If you use this power to shunt enemies into curbstomp parties this is wonderful. Is the Umbra incredibly hostile in this area? Even a BSD might be in trouble if they're blasted into it without warning. Be inventive and cheeky and there are some really cool victories you can get with this power.<br /> <br /> I just wish that the Gift also let you dodge by crossing the Gauntlet! It would suit the name so well, though it might be more fitting for a Rank 5 Gift.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b><u>Whelp Body:</u></b></span> Permanent reductions to physical attributes for targets, gated behind some very difficult rolls. If you have good Gnosis and your target is weak it will work... but you'd only want to use this on a strong target.<br /> <br /> If your campaign has recurring antagonists you can't kill for some reason this might help. But it's already niche, then you consider the Rank 4 cost of 12xp minimum. This is a power for NPCs in my opinion. You CAN make it work, but you're going out of your way to use the Gift for a bit of a silly power trip RP moment where you punish a lower Rank NPC Garou. I remember this power being used on a Vampire PC in a cross-splat PBP server set in Miami! Yes, in that server the Garou didn't kill Vampires on sight, and in fact showed up to Elysium meetings... it was weird. So yeah, if you're in a living world environment or some unusual game setup this power might have mileage, but even then, &quot;I can permanently rob you of xp from your sheet&quot; isn't a fun interaction, whether it's with PCs or NPCs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-08T04:39:23-08:00","id":1548322,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Theurge Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Blurring the Mirror:</b></u></span> This Gift has a use, and it's good at what it does, but it's very sad for its Rank. Spending 1 Gnosis per target sucks. If you want to trap a BSD pack so they can't cross the Gauntlet, you need to hit them with 5 Gnosis for a 5 member pack. A starting Lupus can manage that, but spending all your Gnosis in a single action is a bold move, especially when it's almost certain you're the party's sole healer.<br /> <br /> I won't give this Red, since at the end of the day it's 12xp to gain the ability to fully block a Step Sideways escape for Garou and anyone else who can pull the old sidestep. And there is a niche use of it that people will miss; use it on yourself to stop yourself (or others) from being kidnapped into the Umbra using things like Grasp the Beyond! It's ST-dependent since it depends on how you interpret the power, but it's interesting to try.<br /> <br /> Part of what is awful about this Gift, for what it's worth, is that it only lasts for one scene. If it lasted for a variable period, or more time for more Gnosis, or a time depending on the difference between your Gnosis and theirs, it could be great. But instead it lasts for a scene! Which contradicts the flavour of the power being used as a punishment on Garou. If you're playing a Theurge I see no reason not to get this, but it's low priority.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Grasp the Beyond:</b></u></span> Being able to bring objects and people into and out of the Umbra without Dedication or other normal methods is amazing. This power is great for thieving, for kidnappings, for interactions with Kinfolk and various night-folk, and it is a power which many creatures simply have no way of fighting back against. If you get through the Gauntlet with a powerful supernatural being that can't step sideways, chances are they're really going to suffer. Their best case scenario is bargaining their way back to their world or becoming a disembodied spirit.<br /> <br /> I'd consider this a must-have on a Theurge. This is the sort of thing higher rank Gifts should be doing, letting you break important rules which restrict the Garou basekit. The WP cost is significant, but it's not abysmal either.<br /> <br /> A fun use for this Gift would be stepping sideways with a foe, even a Garou or other spiritually capable enemy, and dumping them into a trap or hazard they haven't had time to prepare for. A Spiral Dancer could have a lot of fun using this Gift on Garou in cities, given how toxic the Umbra gets there.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Spirit Drain*:</b></u></span> Resisted Gnosis roll with no cost. For every success you gain, a spirit will lose one Essence. For every two Essence taken you'll gain a point of Willpower. Any points exceeding your maximum are lost at the end of the Scene, however...<br /> <br /> This is a great way to regenerate Willpower, assuming you have a source of energy. If you have a very good relationship with the Umbra, acting like a predator and taking energy from Gafflings when you need it could work. This power is ripe for abuse, but I think it works well since abusing the power is an in-universe abuse of power, which will have consequences. If you treat the spirit world like your own food store, obliterating helpful spirits to nourish your soul, you'll invite in corruption and accusations of mistreating the Umbra.<br /> <br /> It's worth noting that the power makes no mention of taint. I would argue that stealing Essence from a Bane is a terrible idea, and using this Gift on dark spirits of all kinds is a bad idea. But I'm also relatively averse to using bite attacks on Fomori and Spirits since you're putting the possible explosion of pus or cancer inside your mouth, so I'm more cautious than most.<br /> <br /> Used as an attack, this is a pretty poor ability, unless you are fighting a spirit who is incredibly hard to hit (high WP) or damage (high WP) but whose Gnosis is very low, or has very little Essence. Against a low-Gnosis low-Essence spirit this is possibly an instakill, but a Rank 4 Theurge shouldn't be struggling to kill such a spirit.<br /> <br /> If the other Willpower-restoring Gifts, which are Rank 1, are inaccessible to you then this is a Green. If you can access other Gifts which grant or restore Willpower, this is arguably Red, and certainly not Green. If this Gift restored Gnosis instead of Willpower... now that would be a gamechanger!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Spirit Ward:</b></u></span> See Homid list. Noteworthy that this Gift is even better on the Theurge list since your starting Gnosis could be 3 or 5 instead of 1, and as a Theurge your opportunities to raise your Gnosis will be relatively common.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-08T06:59:54-08:00","id":1548324,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Philodox Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <u><b>Roll Over:</b></u> It's kind of funny to compare this to Kneel from the Black Furies list, given the big disparity in Rank. Still, this Gift is great since it works on a crowd, and once you've forced a target to kneel or submit they are stuck submitting and cannot do ANYTHING without your approval.<br /> <br /> Here's the problem.<br /> <br /> There is no protection from being higher Rank, so a Rank 5-6 Garou is vulnerable to this power, and losing your Rank (say becoming a Ronin and dropping to Rank 0) won't depower this Gift. Supernaturals are effected identically to mortals, which isn't an issue at Rank 4, but still worth noting. And it's a simple contest of Willpower. If you have a high WP, this Gift can utterly destroy not only the balance of encounters, but the way the setting works.<br /> <br /> There is nothing fun or interesting about walking into a big confrontation with a crowd of BSDs and forcing them all to submit while the rest of your Pack dismantle the defenses and desecrated relics. If this power works the game loses all its stakes.<br /> <br /> It isn't that a save-or-suck effect is inherently bad, it's more that an AOE save-or-suck which applies to an indeterminate number of enemies in an indeterminate range, with no restrictions or caveats, is terrible. There is no cost, and it isn't like Willpower is something people tend to skimp on, it is one of the best things to invest in in the whole game.<br /> <br /> Redeeming factors I can think of would be if someone escaping the power can rally others to resist it, or if supernatural foes can defy you but suffer a significant debuff to all their rolls, or if the power only worked when you were enforcing the Litany or had a solid legal basis to assert authority. Hell, if this power only functioned inside of Caerns (including desecrated ones!) it'd be a step in the right direction, or if it relied on the Gauntlet being thin.<br /> <br /> I don't consider this Gift fit for use. Yes, you might get torn to shreds before you can finish executing the power, as anyone who has yet to fall prey to this ability is free to act normally (very all or nothing!) but the maximum possible outcome just doesn't work with the game, and a whole chronicle pivoting on the result of one Willpower roll fells silly. I also consider this power to be poor worldbuilding.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Scent of Beyond:</b></u></span> This is what I'm looking for. Birds-eye view of any place you're familiar with, flinging your senses across the physical or spirit world. Comparison to Scrying is probably sensible, and this power is superior in a few small ways. Your senses are sent, rather than just getting images. Scrying mentions that there are occult means of protecting against it, which will apply to many important places. This power doesn't require an item from the location. Both powers will almost always be difficulty 8, but Scent of Beyond is slightly better quality (all senses) and easier to use overall.<br /> <br /> Since Scrying is cheaper as a Rank 3 Gift, but it's a very Tribe-specific Gift (EB aren't known for sharing their Gifts freely) and so this Gift feels very relevant despite the similarities. One downside is that you're forced to look at the scene from above like a bird, which will make certain details much harder to spot. That said, Scent is free!<br /> <br /> I would argue that the ideal combination is to have Scent of Beyond AND Scrying, since you get two POVs, two opportunities, and there are situations where one will work and the other won't; Scrying might give better close-up detail (you're like a fly on the wall), while Scent gives sound and smells and so on. There are problems with this Gift and certain mysteries, but at Rank 4 it goes without saying that some mysteries will be expedited by supernatural powers. Garou have spirit spies and insane senses, so I don't see this as a problem, more a feature.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Take The True Form:</b></u></span> no cost, and with a relatively simple roll you can force a creature into its 'true' form. Manipulation + Primal, difficulty 7, the target is stuck in their true shape for one turn per success. This would be particularly handy against Homids and Lupus, Gangrel, Tzimisce, and even some Changelings and Mages. If the form you knock someone out of took a long time to create, or is expensive to enter, or takes a long time to slip into, this power is even better.<br /> <br /> It's also worth noting that this power has a 'healing' potential. If someone is turned into a pig or toad by a witch, or a spirit transforms a Garou into a rock, this Gift is carte blanche to revert them to their true form. A Sabbat Ghoul warped by Vicissitude reverting back to their true form could turn a sorry mess of limbs into a normal person, and while a typical Ghoul is no trouble, there are some horrors which would be significantly depowered by this ability.<br /> <br /> Use this on a Garou who has sprouted Pegasus wings with the relevant Gift to make them plummet to the earth as a human or wolf, or wingless Crinos. Use this on a Frenzying packmate to force them into their human or wolf form. Use this on a raging Khan to show him that you aren't interested in his ultrapredator roars and that you will be talking like adults. Use this on yourself when you're forcibly shapeshifted, transformed, or about to enter frenzy. The clever applications are endless and make me wish this was a Rank 2 or 3 Gift! But it fits well at Rank 4.<br /> <br /> Given that it is at Rank 4, I do wish there was an option to spend a point of Gnosis and make the effect last for a day per success, or similar, since that would help a punishment theme of the Auspice, but perhaps that would work better at Rank 5. This is a rare example of a Rank 4 Gift I'd want to buy on any character, even if it isn't in-list. I really find myself wishing this was a Metis Gift as well!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-11T06:38:57-08:00","id":1548400,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Galliard Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Bridge Walker*:</b></u></span> This Gift is very hard to rank because we aren't really given explicit mechanics for all of its uses. What's important is that we DO know that it can permit you to make a journey in 1% of the time it would normally take, that only you can make the journey, and that you can extend the duration of the bridge to a full month by spending extra resources.<br /> <br /> All in all, the ability to do what seems to be a teleport power, where you suddenly slip into a moon bridge and then reach your destination a moment later (or a few moments later if it's further away) is great, but the power needs further clarifications from the ST. For escapes and ambushes this power could be great. I have no idea how you're meant to rule the anime combo of appearing behind foes, since the book doesn't tell us, but I would argue that this combo is awful since spending Gnosis means you can't do Rage bonus attacks, which is part of what makes an ambush so valuable.<br /> <br /> This Gift gets a VERY tentative Green with a *.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Gift of Dreams:</u></b> This Gift lets you do Inception, sorta, that is you can breathe a really impactful dream about the beauty of mother nature into the mouth of a redeemable enemy and hope they wake up with an epiphany about not selling their business to that &quot;PENTEX&quot; group.<br /> <br /> Basically this is a plot dream. If you have a plan which involves using it, it becomes a bit of a plot device, with a variable outcome. This makes it so ST-dependent that I'm gonna leave it unrated. If you need this for the plot, absolutely take it. If you have it and you don't need it for the plot, it becomes a fun way to flex and do nice things for people.<br /> <br /> All of that said, a very nasty application of this power is that Garou tend to take imagery in their dreams and visions as gospel; assuming it came from the Wyrm without evidence costs you Renown! So a tricksy PC or NPC could use this power against other Garou to mislead them quite terribly. Just expect spirits to snitch on you if that's the case, if things get serious enough that the Garou is about to really mess up over the fake omens you gave them.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Shadows by the Firelight:</b></u></span> This feels like it is mostly a flavour or NPC power, and in fact, would be better as a Rite taught to Galliards. That said, the key to this Gift's balance is how it works with unwilling participants. You make a Manipulation + Performance roll against the target's Willpower, along with a Gnosis expenditure, and the 'spell' holds for a scene or until the target is attacked.<br /> <br /> There are a million interesting yet tenuous things you could do with this, if you had written a story which lined up perfectly with what you need to happen. This mind control could have someone open doors for you, solve problems for you, or generally do anything you need them to do. The problem with that is that you're narrating a story, and the power will be extremely obvious. More to the point, walking around a Pentex base using this ability to make an executive do all the things you need him to do to turn on the self destruct REALLY stretches this power's intended mechanics, even if I think it's okay with the RAW.<br /> <br /> Basically, this is a weird one. You could skip it and never need it. You could take it and use it solely to flex, though I think flexing with powerful Gifts is usually boring. If the Garou culture you're immersed in really values stories you might get good mileage out of this Renown wise, but at Rank 4 your problem isn't going to be increasing your Renown, it's going to be challenging someone to become an Elder, and generally overcoming the huge barriers between Rank 4 and Rank 5, and even moreso for Rank 6.<br /> <br /> I'm gonna call this an Amber, since 'fun but not necessarily useful' falls in there. If you find a great use for it, that's a bonus.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Rank 4 Ahroun Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Body Shift:</b></u></span> See Homid list. Sadly an Ahroun of Metis or Lupus Breed isn't any better at using this.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Clenched Jaw:</b></u></span> Not a fan of biting, though this caps off a 'bite build' quite nicely. While you're clinging on you can do a difficulty 3 bite each turn, and getting you off is much harder. I would wager that at Rank 4 you're probably dealing with foes who instead of trying to tear you off will instead be doing horrible things to you while you're relatively immobilized (though of course you can stop at any time).<br /> <br /> For me this doesn't do enough as a Rank 4 Gift, but it's good enough at what it does to be Amber rather than Red. If you are into bites this is a handy Gift, it provides some neat benefits, and there's nothing stopping it from stacking with various other Gifts. If you're more into Claws you could keep someone chomped with this Gift and use all your Rage Actions to attack with claws too.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Full Moon's Light:</b></u></span> spend a Gnosis, anyone within 1 mile who is actively working against you (whether as rivals or enemies, so this would highlight a prankster messing with your hunt as much as an assassin trying to kill you) gets a lovely glow which messes with invisibility, stealth and also makes them easier to differentiate from bystanders.<br /> <br /> This Gift is somewhat situational, but the number of situations you can MAKE it work in is exceptional, so long as veil breaches aren't an issue. For example at a Moot where you are acting as a guard, with the duty of preventing any breaches in the Litany or attacks on the Moot, an AOE crime detector is great. There are many situations where this Gift could pick out impostors, find assassins, thieves, the corrupt and so on.<br /> <br /> Since it's an Ahroun Gift, it's easy to think of combat situations, but this is one of those Gifts which is pretty universal in how you can apply it, again, so long as Veil breaches aren't an issue. In a murder mystery this Gift could highlight anyone who is trying to stop your pack from solving the mystery, which will at least give you a bunch of clues and help you to identify obstacles, even if you don't get an immediate reveal of who the killer is.<br /> <br /> The main issue with this Gift is that it's pretty blunt; you literally make people glow. Even if you're dealing solely with supernaturals, activating this Gift can cause a lot of different cogs to start turning outside or your line of sight. As a result I'd say that this could be Amber or Green depending on the intelligence with which it is used.<br /> <br /> Overall I give this a tentative Green since there are so many things you can solve with this Gift which otherwise you might really struggle with. I'm also a big fan of anything which lets a Garou contribute to tasks their Auspice wouldn't usually complement.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Stoking Fury's Furnace:</b></u></span> This is a great one for longevity. The most important thing this does is let you spend a single point of Rage each turn, so long as it is JUST one Rage, for free. So if you're spending 1 Rage to transform on your turn without a roll, instantly, and then you spend no other Rage that turn, great. If you are in a REALLY long battle where waves of enemies keep coming and coming, you can spend 1 Rage each turn to get a single bonus attack without eating into your Rage pool.<br /> <br /> Since you are an Ahroun if you have this Gift most likely, chances are you're gaining 1 Rage per turn with Silver Claws. That means that if you're regenerating 1 Rage each turn with Silver Claws, and you're spending no Rage each turn thanks to this Gift (doing one bonus attack, for example), you'll be recovering a point of Rage each turn, allowing you to save up for a big burst of attacks when a powerful enemy is vulnerable.<br /> <br /> This power is a huge QOL improvement, even before you get to the OTHER big benefit of the Gift. If you take damage in a given turn you'll regain 1 point of Rage. This is a buff to Primal Anger, and also lets you recover Rage consistently when you're under attack. Between this and Silver Claws and the 'free' Rage each round, this Gift makes a Rank 4 Ahroun MUCH scarier than prior Ranks, which is impressive. This is a good example of great Gift design; the floor for Ahrouns is already quite high, so it's tricky to design a Gift which makes them even better at their job without raising the ceiling too high. The consistency granted by this is spectacular.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-11T14:45:49-08:00","id":1548408,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1548400#post1548400\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This feels like it is mostly a flavour or NPC power...</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly, the Rank 4, 5, and 6 Gifts all should get hit with this on some level. It doesn't change the value of judging them on their merits, but the design of them was so oriented towards cool signature plot devices that their utility as player widgets is always suspect.<br /> <br /> -----------<br /> <br /> And this might surprise you a bit... but Clenched Jaw should be Red.<br /> <br /> I <i>like</i> biting builds, and I never take this.<br /> <br /> 1) One of the big benefits of biting is it's already got a lower diff than claws (5 vs 6). Rat's the biting Garou's best friend with an additional -1 diff to bites... so I can make a character that bites at diff 4 without any Gifts at all. Diff 3 bites isn't a big deal. One of the reasons biting is popular is because without Gifts, Hispo bites are better than Crinos claws (or bites), they're lower diff, you have more Dex to hit with (and act earlier in init), and Hispo gives a damage boost that offsets the reduced Strength. If you're playing to this advantage, the boost from the Gift is minor.<br /> <br /> 2) Grapple with Catfeet (Lupus get the best Bite Build Gifts, and the -2 diff to all Grapple stuff only helps here), then hit them with the Jaw Lock maneuver (something you can just start with); a Rank 4 Ahroun should have the Rage to spare to just do this as two actions instead of activating a Gift. Apply bonus damage from Gnaw, more if your ST lets you hold the Jaw Lock and shift to Hispo after doing a Grapple in Crinos/etc., and the soak penalty from Go For the Jugular (OK, that's a W20 supplement Gift, not core, but still...), and kill pretty much anything.<br /> <br /> 3) Spend the XP you just saved by not taking Clenched Jaw on something better (like saving up for any of the Gifts that make touching you deadly ala Gift of the Porcupine).<br /> <br /> The loss of bonus dice to hold the Clenched Jaw is completely offset by Jaw Lock's difficulty modifiers; especially since spirits don't have Brawl ratings and at this level they're a major consideration though your ST might not let you Jaw Lock spirits.<br /> <br /> Clenched Jaw exists to paper over a problem in the rules (WtA left biting in Grapples out where it's explicitly something you can do in other games, and despite how much wolves are built to bite down and not let go, lupus and hispo forms can't initiate Grapple) that should just be fixed. You shouldn't need a Rank 4 Gift to bite down and hold on to someone as a wolf. A Rank 4 Gift should make the idea of a wolf just killing you seem like a mercy compared to them deciding to grab you and shake you around while clamped in those teeth.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-13T02:17:55-08:00","id":1548462,"title":"","content":"Noted! And yeah I do appreciate that many of the higher rank Gifts will be more plot-device-y. If a Gift seems genuinely fun, it's not gonna wind up as Red, since there's a way to get value from it. It's very hard to think of a power you can have in a TTRPG which is fun to use and will NEVER be useful, even if you have to work at making it useful.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1548408#post1548408\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And this might surprise you a bit... but Clenched Jaw should be Red.<br /> <br /> I <i>like</i> biting builds, and I never take this.</div> </div> </div> </div>This surprises me! So to my taste, going from difficulty 5 to difficulty 3 is a huge buff. But say you have the Rat Totem, which is a bit niche since it dictates a bonus for the whole party, so the whole party would need to have been happy with that choice. But yeah, comparing difficulty 3 to difficulty 4, you're going to see an average of +1 successes on your attacks if you have ~10 dice, and almost +2 successes if you have ~15 dice.<br /> <br /> Furthermore, while this won't come up all the time, quite often there will be something increasing the difficulty of an attack, whether it's environmental conditions, an enemy's defensive Gift, a debuff you've suffered or whatever. With this Gift you have a base difficulty of 3 for bites, you might suffer +1 difficulty from some modifier, and then the Rat Totem bonus would apply a -1 difficulty putting it back to difficulty 3. So the bonus isn't redundant, but it's still a bit of an annoyance.<br /> <br /> Comparing difficulty 5 to difficulty 3, there's definitely a neat benefit, but I definitely see what you mean about the Gift having problems. Part of the problem with these Gifts is that they're built on top of a slightly wonky combat system which, in my experience, is very rarely run RAW since many WoD STs are more focused on the lore, story and the &quot;advantages&quot; part of the mechanics (the Disciplines, the Gifts, the Spheres etc.) rather than the mechanical core. So when you have a Gift which lets you keep someone grappled with a bite, things get a bit weird since many STs would just let you do that anyways so long as the outcome wasn't busted.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-13T02:38:41-08:00","id":1548463,"title":"","content":"<b><u>Rank 4 Black Fury Gifts</u></b><br /> <br /> <b><u>Beast Life:</u></b> Remains unrated, see Lupus entry. Black Furies are Wyld-themed so this Gift makes good sense, though it feels quite late in the day to become Snow White.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Body Wrack:</b></u></span> I feel like I've seen this Gift too many times, which is annoying. W20's Gifts needed a thorough edit, including organizing them and removing some copies and redundancy.<br /> <br /> Spend a Gnosis, roll Gnosis (not a great start!) against a difficulty of Stamina +3 (ahhhhh). The victim gets stunned for one round per success, and subtracts 1 die from all their rolls for the scene.<br /> <br /> What's annoying about this one, beyond the Gnosis spend severely hampering you, is that at Rank 4 you've likely already had a few chances to pick up Gifts which boringly stun opponents, and you're likely facing enemies who are in Crinos form, monstrous Fomori shapes, or Spirits. Anything with a Stamina of 7+ or a Spirit with a Willpower of 7+ is going to be an absolute nightmare to use this power on.<br /> <br /> While this power isn't fundamentally useless, and you COULD make it work if it was your only stunning / debuff option, considering how deep we are into the Gifts and that this costs 12xp, and that it won't work against the most dangerous enemies who you would want to use it against, I'm going to call this Red.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Wasp Talons:</b></u></span> I really like this one! A ranged claw attack is fun and fits the weird animal kingdom vibe of the Garou. That said, the cost of a Rage point, and the slow regeneration of the claws without clear language dictating exactly how the Gift works (do you fire a single claw with each attack?) make this one annoying. A standard range of 20 yards also isn't that impressive.<br /> <br /> Annoyingly, despite me really liking this one in concept, you'd be better off using Hare's Leap to close the gap in most cases, or using a different ranged Gift. This power still works in tandem with powers which transform your claws (presumably), which is handy, so it isn't terrible, just very unimpressive at Rank 4.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-13T02:50:11-08:00","id":1548464,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Bone Gnawer Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Attunement:</b></u></span> Finally! This is what I'm expecting from a Rank 4 Tribal Gift. Much as you'd expect the Bone Gnawer Elder to mystically pick up a sense that there is a den of leeches somewhere in the town, your PC can now, late in the game, discover that there is an unannounced den of Garou hiding somewhere downtown which might be worth investigating.<br /> <br /> The cost, the Perception + Streetwise roll, they're fine. Big fan of this one, it's simple but very cool, great for traveling games and for games where you stay in one place.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Blink:</b></u></span> This is a pretty solid stealth and travel power. You go somewhere shady, you pop out somewhere else shady. The range is good so long as you get decent successes, and this power could get you out of (and into) a lot of trouble. The power is a bit vague, however, it's unclear whether you need to have an idea of where you're going, or if you have a rough menu of &quot;choices&quot; like a fast travel tooltip or something. If you need to know an eligible location to hit with this power you might be in trouble.<br /> <br /> Also worth noting that even without this power, stepping sideways is an option, as is going Lupus and leaping away as fast as you can. Both those options have problems, absolutely, especially in cities, so this power is very valuable, but you could get by without it. Gonna call it Green because it genuinely adds something new to your repertoire and it isn't competing for the short list of things you want to be doing during a fight.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Infest:</b></u></span> You create a horrible infestation which at bare minimum needs an exterminator, and worst case scenario makes the building entirely unlivable. That's fine, sure. Gnosis cost, whatever. The problem is that this Gift feels like it belongs at Rank 2. It's also something you can achieve with a bit of time and effort bribing animals or spirits.<br /> <br /> &quot;A structure no bigger than a large building&quot; is a very, very funny piece of writing for what it's worth. Ah good, so it won't work on any structures which are bigger than a large building, good to know. So it won't work on a large complex composed of multiple buildings. Or, uh, a town. Or a giant space station. Good to know.<br /> <br /> Ultimately this Gift lets you do Garou-y stuff non-violently. A worksite is being developed near a Caern? No it isn't. A bridge is being built over a sacred river? No it isn't. A local casino is meeting with a PENTEX rep to discuss selling up? No they aren't. But the problem is that this Gift won't actually stop anything important from happening long term, it just delays things. Though, hilariously, that also applies to the standard (well, depending who you ask) Garou response of &quot;run in and kill the people who are physically there, right now&quot;. So for delaying tactics in complex long-running problems, this Gift could work quite well. It's also decently fun. I can't give it Red, basically, because despite it being Rank 4, it's still something you could reasonably buy and get mileage out of.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-13T03:37:43-08:00","id":1548466,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Children of Gaia Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <u><b>Beast Life:</b></u> Again?! Still unrated, see Lupus. For Children of Gaia this fits into a peaceful master of nature vibe, great, but the Gift needs work.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Serenity:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis, roll Charisma + Expression (difficulty of target's Willpower), and you block the target from spending Rage, frenzying, and make sure they fail all Rage rolls for the duration. What's annoying is that this Gift overlaps a lot with Calm The Savage Beast, which Children of Gaia already get access to. This costs Gnosis, while Calm costs Willpower. The difficulties vary as well. Overall while Calm can stop a frenzy, and doesn't block it from recurring, this Gift can prevent a frenzy, and stop it from happening for a while. It's a VERY small improvement on Calm, and it should have been written as an upgraded power which improves Calm the Savage Beast.<br /> <br /> My rewrite would be that Serenity has Calm the Savage Beast as a prerequisite, and simply improves upon the prior mechanics, allowing you to spend Gnosis on a success to block frenzy for the whole scene.<br /> <br /> One of the saving graces of this Gift is that it blocks a Remain Active test, which makes it Amber. An even more potent application of the Gift, however, is against Spirits. &quot;Fail All Rage Rolls&quot; is a BUSTED effect against any spirit. That's an automatic fail to all damage rolls. That's an automatic fail to any strength-like roll. A Spirit hit with Serenity becomes, RAW, as weak as a kitten. If this effect works at your table against spirits, after all the power doesn't specify, it would be Green, and potentially overpowered given that it could, strictly RAW, make a hugely powerful spirit become powerless. That said, a Spirit which goes beyond normal limits of WP/Rage would typically be very, VERY hard to use this Gift on, due to a Willpower of 10+.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Strike the Air:</b></u></span> Spending a Willpower and succeeding on a Wits + Athletics test (difficulty of the opponent's Willpower) makes you unhittable, so long as you don't attack, and no one attacks for you. This Gift makes you 100% unkillable against a single foe, and you can use it repeatedly to make yourself resistant to a whole pack of enemies.<br /> <br /> If the enemy is smart enough to figure out that you are not just dodging really well, but rather that you are impossible to hit, then they will likely adjust their plan accordingly. But there is basically nothing they can do to stop you from leaping around fulfilling your objective. The really nasty thing about this Gift is that a Child of Gaia could make themselves unhittable for a Garou, and then attack all of the Garou's packmates or Kinfolk, while they are unable to harm the Child due to this Gift. Nothing in the Gift blocks you from attacking things OTHER than the target of the Gift, mind. You could destroy objects, allies, steal things they need and so on.<br /> <br /> The sheer absoluteness of this one is very handy, and it's a pretty great Gift all in all. This hits the spot for where high level Gifts ought to be. Using this in a Challenge is also very likely to give you a distinct edge, or at the very least force a (dishonourable?) stalemate in a duel.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Uncaught Since the Primal Morn:</b></u></span> The flavour of this one is great, and the lore it hints at is fun. With this Gift you can, in theory, outrun any pursuer, making yourself impossible to catch. The problem is the roll; you roll Stamina + Athletics, against the highest Stamina + Athletics of any of your pursuers. If you drop this when your enemies are still in Homid this should be very doable. If your enemies are in Crinos this power becomes very hard to use. It also lasts for one scene per success, which is pretty generous.<br /> <br /> Needing to run is already a bit niche. Needing to run, alone, is more niche. This power is cool and all, but unless you're on your own it means abandoning your Pack, unless you have a homid or two clinging onto your Crinos for dear life.<br /> <br /> Theoretically you could use this Gift, along with a Mage or Changeling ally, in order to travel at a speed faster than light (you just need to be chased by something moving at the speed of light, which is achievable with some Sphere/Unleashing nonsense) and break through some unbreakable barrier or kill some powerful foe, but the problem with that is that you'll likely be reduced to slurry, or perhaps down to your composite atoms. Very unlikely to matter, but funny to picture.<br /> <br /> Given that this power is hard to use against something genuinely dangerous (a powerful spirit, a Crinos-form pursuer) and you run alone, I'd ultimately say that this one isn't good enough. This one is a niche of a niche, and I don't really put much value in being able to escape alive when your whole pack is torn apart; you should be investing XP to avoid these situations, not to escape them when you've lost. Even if an encounter is meant to be very hard and the chance of losing is very high, fleeing in Lupus is a great basekit. If you can't figure out a fun way to escape, that is likely a skill issue.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 02-22-2025, 06:41 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-13T06:21:19-08:00","id":1548469,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure it changes much, but something Serenity does that the lower level calming Gifts don't do (and is pretty relevant for higher level stuff): cuts off enemy BSDs from recovering via Rage rolls after getting knocked to incapacitated. While a lot of STs don't roll this for enemies because it tends to get tedious fast, being able to absolutely shut this down is still useful to have at your disposal. Even if the difficulty is meh, just the chance to say, &quot;Nope, I don't think so,&quot; when you take down a BBEG BSD and the music ramps up for round two instead of the victory music is a handy thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-22T03:39:06-08:00","id":1548694,"title":"","content":"You know what - that makes Serenity a solid Amber! Blocking attempts to return to life is pretty handy. It would also stop a lot of Homid/Lupus enemies from taking Crinos form, unless they pass the test to transform, which might take a few turns.<br /> <br /> I've also edited it to mention that it could be used against spirits, and the weirdness that comes with that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 02-22-2025, 06:42 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-22T04:21:34-08:00","id":1548696,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Fianna Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Balor's Gaze:</b></u></span> The most interesting thing about this Gift is its name. Balor was a Fomorian of immense power who once defeated the Tuatha De Danann in a great war, took a Tuathan bride, and whose grandson killed him. He had one eye, and anyone he gazed upon died. What ancient pacts with the Fae make this Gift possible? Is it simply named for Balor due to the human legend, or due to Garou links with the true Fae war? Does this Gift reek of the Fomorian Dream to Changelings?<br /> <br /> Anyways, I'm pretty bored of paralysis powers at this point, so a power which inflicts lasting agony is a good step in the right direction. This power costs 1 Rage and 1 Gnosis (you can spend both at once thanks to the rules of W20, though you can't spend any more Rage or Gnosis for the rest of the turn of course) and requires a difficulty 8 Perception + Occult test.<br /> <br /> FOR THE REST OF THE SCENE if you glare at someone, they suffer intense agony unless they beat your successes in a Difficulty 8 Willpower test. If they don't EXCEED your successes, they are struck with a -5 die penalty from agony for the scene, and if they're already Crippled they become Incapacitated. It's unclear whether the other detriments of being Crippled (movement impacts) apply or not.<br /> <br /> It's worth noting that this lasts for the scene and while healing and regeneration normally undo wound penalties by removing the wounds, this is wound-agnostic. Targets who ignore wound penalties (via the Rank 1 Gift) or who benefit from pain (BSDs, Fomori and some rare Vampires and other supernaturals etc. do this) may be bad targets for this...<br /> <br /> All in all, this is what I'm expecting at Rank 4. This Gift can be set up once, either right before a battle or right as it starts, and you can continue to debuff enemies for a whole scene with it simply by glaring at them. The cost is a bit intense at 1R 1G, and taking a turn to set it up isn't great, but across a fight it could inflict a lot of harm on foes. It's also non-lethal and a great way to torture foes reversibly.<br /> <br /> That said, unfortunately, the fact that any being in the WoD can, RAW, ignore wound penalties by spending a point of WP makes this... a little sad. Spirits can't, sure, as they can't spend WP. But everyone else will spend WP to ignore the penalty when it counts. In a longer fight this is a great way to burn a lot of enemy WP, and the power isn't bad, it's worth grabbing if you're a Fianna, but it's ultimately sitting where most Gifts should be; it's good, it helps, but it isn't a game-changer.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Phantasm:</b></u></span> This Gift lets you make an immobile and fully immersive illusion, including olfactory elements, sound, touch and so on. Since it impacts smell, presumably taste also applies. The cost is high, 1 Gnosis per 3m radius, but the fact that you can make a fully believable tableau is great. You could leave behind a phantasmal corpse of yourself in your Breed Form, you could create fake evidence against someone to make them slip up and admit their crimes, you could frame someone, you could hide a trap beneath false ground.<br /> <br /> All in all I'd call this Gift perfectly balanced; it's Green if you are a genius, and Amber otherwise. Generally speaking people won't be breaking through this illusion since they only roll their Perception + Alertness to see through it if they are given reason to do so. Being &quot;given reason&quot; might mean, for example, noticing that there are no flies around an old body, or that a body doesn't seem to be getting any older, or seeing tracks leading away from a corpse which match the paws/boots of the corpse. So if you take time to come up with something convincing, it's unlikely anyone will even roll to see through it.<br /> <br /> If immobile simply means the illusion is static (no roaring fire, no buzzing flies) then that's fine. If immobile means the illusion can't be moved around (the fake corpse is stuck on the floor) then any deeper examination will reveal it to be fake, even if the illusion isn't beaten. It would help if there were some suggestion that the Gift is psychoactive and convinces people under its spell that what they're seeing is real, but as it stands it seems to be a fully sensory illusion.<br /> <br /> =======================================<br /> <br /> Only two Gifts at Rank 4 - bit sad for the Fianna! Personally I'd give them another Gift at Rank 4 which relates to plants, or something from the Galliard list.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-22T04:36:33-08:00","id":1548697,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Get of Fenris Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Body Shift:</b></u></span> Still bad, see Homid list. If this were better it would really complement the heavy war focus of the Get.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Heart of the Mountain:</b></u></span> Spend a Rage, Spend a WP, for the rest of the scene you can't fail any task involving Stamina. You'll never be broken by torturers, you'll never die from drowning, you'll never tire of marching, you'll never fall asleep. This is a pretty great Gift! It also guarantees that you will always soak 1 point of damage. From what I can see this overrides a source of damage being unsoakable, so if you were taking 1 point of unsoakable damage every turn, this Gift would nullify the damage. If you were taking 3 points of unsoakable damage, you'd take 2.<br /> <br /> All in all this is a pretty great Gift. It's niche, and it comes down to ST judgement on a lot of things, but it makes you outright immune to a lot of non-damaging threats and it helps to somewhat reduce the damage you take, especially if the damage normally can't be soaked. All in all, given that you don't have to roll to activate it and it costs Rage rather than Gnosis, I'd call this a Green. Basically the amount of stuff this lets you fully or mostly sidestep (elite pentex knockout gas, having to run over puddles of molten silver etc.) is good enough that it's worth Green. This is a power which lets you overcome stuff designed to take out Garou, which is pretty handy.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Hero's Stand:</b></u></span> Roll WP. Each success grants +1 to all physical pools, which is pretty great. No cost. You're stuck in one place, unable to move from that spot. You cannot be surprised, all attacks count as frontal (so no benefits to trying to backstab etc.) and with that bonus to your dice pools you'll fight well.<br /> <br /> That said... an intelligent foe may well keep their distance and just pelt you with ranged attacks. Nothing stopping you from returning fire with Gifts or with ranged weapons, but still, the 'stuck in one place' restriction is big.<br /> <br /> This gets a solid Amber. It's worth grabbing, it's handy, it has some handy benefits, but you could definitely live without it. The ideal situation is rolling insanely well and getting +3 to all your physical dice pools - +3 to hit, to dodge, to soak, to damage, ain't bad at all. Though at Rank 4 that isn't a game-changer on its own.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Scream of Gaia*:</b></u></span> See Lupus list. Lots of repeats at this Rank!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-22T04:54:55-08:00","id":1548699,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Glass Walker Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Attunement:</b></u></span> See Bone Gnawer list. Suits the GWs perfectly.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Doppleganger:</b></u></span> A perfect disguise to match any human, wolf or Garou is pretty great! The cost and the roll are fine, and a duration of 1 day per success is perfect. Since traits aren't copied, the best way to watch out for a doppleganger is with codes, a secret language, testing Gifts/Knowledges/Skills etc.<br /> <br /> Using this to make yourself look like an Elder, a packmate, an enemy, a helpless ally such as a vulnerable kinfolk, letting yourself get kidnapped and then going Crinos once you're back at base... the possible uses are endless.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Signal Rider:</b></u></span> Costs 1 Gnosis and 1 WP, and only requires a roll if you use a computerized method of transfer such as a video call. The range is 8km per dot of Gnosis you have, so 40km with Gnosis 5, or 80km with a Gnosis of 10!<br /> <br /> You instantly arrive at your destination; so you can leap out of the pocket of a pentex rep if you have their mobile number. I don't think I need to get into why this is great. A GW pack can leap across a city near-effortlessly with a network of telephones, only restricted by running out of Gnosis.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Tech Speak:</b></u></span> Often useless, and clunky when it's useful. If you have access to a piece of technology, there is a good chance you have access to a phone or computer. But, you might be out of signal, sure. &quot;Often useless&quot; means that often, this Gift has absolutely no use at all. You just use your mobile phone. But you don't have it, or it doesn't have signal or battery.<br /> <br /> So you need this Gift. Now you have to spend Gnosis (which you could use to teleport with Signal Rider, immediately above this Gift!) and make a potentially very difficult Charisma + Science test. Difficulty 4-9 depending on distance of the receiver of the message. If there are communications devices near the person you want to contact, they print/show/shout the message. More successes means a longer message. This could be really handy, don't get me wrong, but again, at this rank you can teleport. You are also a rank 4 Garou. You can bash down a wall or go Lupus and run away.<br /> <br /> Yes, yes, subtlety are important. Being able to send a text without touching your phone is great! But this feels like it could even be a Rank 1-2 Gift, not Rank 4. Again, at this Rank we're teleporting using phones, so sending a text using a door alarm feels a bit... lacking. Amber purely because having this Gift will almost inevitably be useful at SOME point, but I don't have to like it. The fact that 1 success sends only a SINGLE word is also horrible and feels like a real 'fuck you' to someone who wasted 12xp and a point of Gnosis to get there!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-22T05:12:41-08:00","id":1548700,"title":"","content":"I would keep it Amber, but Hero's Stand's ideal situation is when you have time to create a tactical advantage via controlling the terrain. A Get that's protected by a blind corner that's also acting as a bottleneck forcing the enemy to have to go through that passage way to get where they want to go is going to be very limited in their options (esp. if throwing explosives around the corner risks collapsing the passage way and blocking them off in a different fashion). Especially since it would limit how many enemies could attack the Get effectively at one time.<br /> <br /> Obviously that's very difficult to guarantee, though it's easiest for a high Ranking Garou that's focused on defending a Sept and thus has time and incentive to create good choke-points to funnel invading Wyrm forces into.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-22T15:58:49-08:00","id":1548715,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1548699#post1548699\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Signal Rider:</b></u></span> Costs 1 Gnosis and 1 WP, and only requires a roll if you use a computerized method of transfer such as a video call. The range is 8km per dot of Gnosis you have, so 40km with Gnosis 5, or 80km with a Gnosis of 10!<br /> <br /> You instantly arrive at your destination; so you can leap out of the pocket of a pentex rep if you have their mobile number. I don't think I need to get into why this is great. A GW pack can leap across a city near-effortlessly with a network of telephones, only restricted by running out of Gnosis.</div> </div> </div> </div>Reach out, touch spirit","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-22T16:25:28-08:00","id":1548716,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Red Talon Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> First we have to deal with Gorge. I like this one enough that I'm annoyed the Red Talons of all Tribes get it - I wish it were a Lupus Gift! Since it's more like three different Gifts, best to rank all three separately.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#e74c3c\"><u><b>Gorge (Rage):</b></u></span> Being able to hold 13 points of temporary Rage instead of 10 feels like a side note. Holding 10 points of Rage is already a bad idea, holding 13 isn't WORSE per se, but it's still a bad idea. If the Gift clarified that the bonus capacity didn't impact Frenzy tests, it'd be handy... ish? For example if an Ahroun could carry 8 Rage at the start of a session instead of 5, without increasing their Rage pool for the purposes of Frenzy tests, that'd be nice? The problem is that Rage is cheap, Rage is easily regained, and there are many Gifts which grant Rage.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Gorge (Gnosis)*:</b></u></span> Having capacity for more Gnosis is pretty good, since there are times where it's easy to fill up (spiritual hunt, access to a Caern) and times where it's almost impossible. This Gift is as good as your best use for Gnosis is. It also counters some of the difficulty of carrying Silver; if your normal Gnosis is 5, and you're carrying a silver object, you'll have 7 points of Gnosis to spend instead of 4.<br /> <br /> This could be Amber or Green depending on your best uses for Gnosis. If you can heal Aggravated damage with every point of Gnosis you have, it's amazing. If your best use for Gnosis is a meh Gift, it's meh.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Gorge (Willpower):</b></u></span> Willpower is important, and being able to carry +3 points is great. For example say you have a WP of 7, being able to carry 10 temporary points instead of 7 is amazing. This has niche interactions with powers which target your temporary Willpower instead of your permanent Willpower (such as Dominate powers and some other supernatural abilities).<br /> <br /> This power lets you pay 12xp to gain +3 capacity without getting the benefits of +3 dots. For 12xp you could increase your actual Willpower by 1 dot, or more if it's very low. But the important thing is that increasing WP is hard to justify and most STs won't let you have a WP of 9-10, meanwhile this Gift is easily justified, at least if it's in-tribe for you. All in all I'd call this Green, so long as you aren't spending xp which would be better spent on actual Willpower!<br /> <br /> Keep in mind that spending WP is incredible and can get a lot of great outcomes. It also lets you ignore wound penalties and overcome your instincts and terrible impulses, as well as interacting with a lot of supernatural abilities (e.g. stunned if you don't spend WP).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Howl of Death:</b></u></span> This Gift has no cost, and can target anyone you can see and who can hear you. The damage is lethal, which is a bit weak for Werewolf, but doesn't really matter against Spirits, who treat all damage types the same. It also only matters against Garou if you care about regeneration, which won't always matter. This is a pretty neat ranged attacking action which could make a group of Red Talons absolutely terrifying (by WTA standards) as you get absolutely shredded from a ridge a mile away by a howling pack.<br /> <br /> Worth noting that this Gift does LEVELS of damage rather than dice. It's soakable, and it's Lethal, sure, but this is a neat power. Is it dodgeable? I'd assume so, but most targets won't try to dodge the first attack since it won't seem like an attack.<br /> <br /> All in all I'd call this Green since it has great advantages over some other ranged options. For a Red Talon Galliard this could become their go-to attacking power. Naturally this won't be a good attacking option unless you have a dice pool to back it up!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Quicksand:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis, make a check, turn an area to quicksand. You and your pack move through it normally. Anyone else is slowed, takes +1 difficulty to combat tests and can't make combat maneuvers which require overland movement (like a charge, presumably) at all. This power is great for tying down a group of enemies, reducing their damage output (less likely to hit) and increasing the damage they take (they either can't dodge at all or attempt it at +1 difficulty).<br /> <br /> This power is pretty handy; the Gnosis cost and it being Rank 4 means it's bound to be Amber, but it's worth grabbing. The possible clever uses of this power are also pretty broad, but would rely on the ST's take on it. For example, it seems to me that using this below a pillar of a building would make that pillar structurally unsafe, but how that plays out would be down to the ST.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-22T16:57:14-08:00","id":1548718,"title":"","content":"While, I get that Gorge (Gnosis) is variable based on what you can spend Gnosis on... there's a reason why Theurges and other Rite focused characters tend to want as much Gnosis as they can get their hands on. Gnosis 10 + Gorge (Gnosis) + the Rite of the Opened Sky = is an insanely potent combo since it makes using Opened Sky wherever you want instead of it only really being functional at a Caern (at a Caern the base cost of the Rite is 1 Gnosis... outside of one the base cost jumps up to 10 Gnosis), and you only need one success on the Rite for it to work, so it's not some insanely hard rite that requires big investments in Charisma + Rituals to have a chance of doing. High Gnosis pools, like high Willpower pools, are useful to almost every Garou even if Gnosis doesn't have the same always available expenditures like auto-successes. Unless a player has actively made a character that avoids any Gnosis spending what-so-ever, is there really a character that wouldn't want 3 extra points to have on hand?<br /> <br /> And Howl of Death is already Green... but it's worth remembering that for the Red Talons, Eye of the Eagle (Rank 2 Lupus) is fairly easy to get, so that &quot;visual range&quot; limitation can become fairly meaningless. When your visual range is measured in miles and you can endlessly spam Howl of Death, the damage being lethal is basically the only thing that even remotely avoids this being completely OP.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-24T07:03:44-08:00","id":1548753,"title":"","content":"Loving the thread. Glad to have you back <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11620_5.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11620\" data-vbnamecard=\"11620\">twig</a><br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1548718#post1548718\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And Howl of Death is already Green... but it's worth remembering that for the Red Talons, Eye of the Eagle (Rank 2 Lupus) is fairly easy to get, so that &quot;visual range&quot; limitation can become fairly meaningless. When your visual range is measured in miles and you can endlessly spam Howl of Death, the damage being lethal is basically the only thing that even remotely avoids this being completely OP.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mentioned this on a <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1526067#post1526067\">previous post</a>, also something similar for <i>Terror of the Dire Wolf</i> + <i>Shadows of the Impergium</i> wolf on <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1523372#post1523372\">another post</a>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4967,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","user":"lbeaumanior","date":". 02-24-2025, 10:06 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-25T11:47:32-08:00","id":1548767,"title":"","content":"In my defense, that was awhile ago!<br /> <br /> It does illustrate a problem with the WtA Gift system though: the synergies (or dissonances) in the lists are really opaque. Frequently it feels like even the developers struggle with this. The way powers are listed doesn't make it easy to look for Gifts that might compliment each other such as the combos you linked to, or when a Rank 2 Gift is probably better for out-of-splat characters because there's a Rank 4 Gift that makes the earlier one obsolete but for the price it's only worth getting one of them; so in-splat PCs will prefer the Rank 4 one.<br /> <br /> While this isn't the thread to fully discuss different ways to implement things, I'd love to see the WtA Gifts re-written (though this would probably end up with some house rules in the process) more like Exalted Charms with some basic mechanical tags at the start like what kind of action it takes to use them (or if they're always on), the costs that are used (so you can spot Rage/Gnosis overlaps without having the read the whole text), some keywords that could help with spotting overlaps/synergies, etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-28T07:15:47-08:00","id":1548816,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Shadow Lords Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Open Wounds:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis, Strength + Medicine difficulty 7. On your next attack, and note that it says attack, not melee attack, not unarmed attack, you cause the target to bleed continuously. They take 1 level of unsoakable Lethal damage each turn, for a number of turns equal to successes. A Crinos form Garou preparing to ambush a target could very easily activate this and roll a great many successes; even with a Strength of 1 and a Medicine of 1, this Gift could still be very handy if you took to Crinos before rolling.<br /> <br /> But let's say you get 10 successes. How good is 10 turns of an enemy taking 1 point of lethal damage each turn? Well, that really depends! If you're hitting them with a sniper round from 10 miles away and then leaving the scene, this might help to 100% guarantee that they will die. Let's say that you're fighting a powerful ancient vampire; 1 point of lethal every turn is actually quite bad for them! Let's say you're punching someone, and then watching smugly as they bleed out from the sudden gush of blood that follows their lost tooth.<br /> <br /> This power has a marginal benefit in combat (DOT against a foe, especially a foe who lacks regeneration, or whose regeneration is already 'busy' or disabled) which would make it Amber on its own. The more clever uses, like killing someone with an attack which really should never have hurt them at all (beyond 1 bashing), is great. All in all I'd call this a solid Green, I couldn't imagine hitting rank 4 on a Shadow Lord and not taking this!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Durance*:</b></u></span> When using a Talen, spend a Gnosis, and keep the Talen instead of its spirit escaping. This is a really great ability which would let you use a rare or irreplaceable Talen in perpetuity. This is very hard to rate since it's as good or as weak as your best Talen option. Unfortunately it doesn't undrink potions or unexplode grenades, but for Talens which aren't inherently wasted in their using this is great.<br /> <br /> The problem is that many, MANY Talens are consumables, or ammunition, or would reasonably be broken or lost in their usage. So this is good to get endless use out of a really good Talen which sticks around, like an Easy Money Credit Card for instance, which essentially would give you unlimited cash (though never spending more than your credit limit in a single scene).<br /> <br /> Easiest to simply say that this is Amber, with the * potential to be Green. If you have the right Talens which could be great, but that's so table-dependent that it feels a bit silly to get into a list here.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Strength of the Dominator:</b></u></span> Wits + Intimidation, difficulty of the target's Willpower. For one turn per success they will lose one Rage, and that Rage will go to the Shadow Lord. No cost, and it can only effect a single target once per scene. Noteworthy that you could use this Gift on a whole pack of enemies from hiding in the run-up to a fight, and then blitz them with an insane number of attacks each turn, however this would be dependent on your usual maximum Rage. An Ahroun with a Rage of 7 might well benefit greatly from this Rage income each turn, making 4 bonus attacks each round... but then again, at Rank 4 you have a lot of Gift options to generate Rage.<br /> <br /> The unique aspect of stealing Rage is great, but this sets up a slow siphon. It's handy, hell it could be absolutely incredible in some situations, but most of the time it's just a neat option to have. Worth noting that a pack of Shadow Lords could completely rob a Garou of all their Rage with this. Also important to keep in mind that the potential targets are limited. A tiny number of Fomori, all Garou, most Fera, some Kami, and that's it. If you're constantly fighting BSDs and Fera, great, if you aren't this is less handy.<br /> <br /> There is a side effect of being able to siphon Rage out of a rampaging cub who might not know how to burn it, and you could use this on a Frenzying Garou to rob them of their Rage and slow down their rampage (fewer bonus actions)... but at Rank 4 you probably have other, better options for this, including Direct the Storm, which lets you outright choose what a Frenzying Garou does.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-28T11:31:41-08:00","id":1548820,"title":"","content":"Blah, just inspiring me to go on tangents regarding the issues with the rest of the game these powers have to interact with. One thing I've noticed with powers like Durance (it's not actually the first time WtA has published something for this purpose) is that it just encourages players to make custom Talens that don't get consumed by being used. WtA's item crafting rules have always run mostly on &quot;feels right&quot; than any sort of mechanical rigor. I don't think it's bad that the rules for this are fairly loose, but they wouldn't sacrifice anything valuable if they had more functional guidelines. Something as simple as &quot;Talens with reusable physical vessels sacrifice a bit of potency for the ability to rebind spirits into them, while Talens that destroy the physical vessel get some extra oomph for it,&quot; would reduce the variability of the value by taable of a Gift like Durance significantly. The practical value to any given character would still be hit or miss (largely based on personal crafting ability or access to skilled Garou or Kin artisans), but it's at least a lot easier to see why characters would consider Durance if they aren't focused on making Talens on their own and thus want to avoid running out of useful ones.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-28T13:45:15-08:00","id":1548823,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Silent Strider Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Attunement:</b></u></span> See the Bone Gnawer list. Silent Striders may choose cities or wilderness when they learn the Gift, both of which are great. Ideally you'd learn both, though the RAW doesn't explicitly permit this. Presumably the 'wilderness' version is still taught by a rat-spirit, but I would be open to making it practically any Naturae or animal-spirit which is comfortable adapting to lots of different kinds of wilderness.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#e74c3c\"><u><b>Black Mark:</b></u></span> After hitting with claws, but before inflicting damage, you can spend a point of Gnosis. For every level of damage you wind up inflicting the target becomes haunted for a day. So if you hit someone for 6 Aggravated damage, they'll be haunted for 6 days.<br /> <br /> Here's the thing. If you're clawing at someone, you should be aiming to kill them. You achieve that by spending Rage to hit a bunch of extra attacks in this one turn. On the off-chance someone is just about to escape and you have time to land a single attack... what does this even do?<br /> <br /> The Pentex rep escapes with a nasty black wound which draws in spectres for 48 hours. What does that MEAN for him? No NPC is going to be murdered by ghosts offscreen, and it's very unlikely that them being harried by ghosts is going to play into the events of the chronicle. If your ST is very Wraith-savvy this might help you to track down the target, guided by a savvy detective Wraith who has heard tell of this Quick fella who has been drawing in lots of vengeful ghosts. But you're a Rank 4 Garou. It's likely that you can track down targets with a variety of Gifts.<br /> <br /> The existence of Mark of the Death-Wolf means that this Gift is somewhat redundant, all things considered. If you are fighting, fight to kill. If you aren't fighting, curse people with the Mark. This is a strange hybrid power which only helps in strange situations, and feels like it would be a trap most of the time, where you throw away a chance to kill a target (spending Rage for bonus actions) to instead mess with them.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Dam the Heartflood*:</b></u></span> Inherently niched, but that's somewhat okay; you wouldn't buy this unless you were in the right Chronicle, and a Rank 4 Silent Strider with a sensible ST will be running into Vampires and Ghouls. This power, when it works, totally blocks all magics related to blood. This presumably doesn't just block Blood Sorcery (Thaumaturgy, Dur-An-Ki, Setite Sorcery, Koldunism) and should include Disciplines, which from a WTA perspective feel like &quot;blood magic&quot;, but ask your ST. There is a bonus effect which blocks all blood expenditure. The duration is 1 turn per success on a Manipulation + Occult, difficulty of the target's Willpower, and it costs 1 Gnosis.<br /> <br /> Is it worth using this? Well if you're up against a powerful Vampire, Ghoul or Ananasi... maybe? RAW there is nothing protecting an Antedeluvian from this power, so there's that! When this power is relevant it could be useful, but it should be saved for hitting targets you can't simply kill in one round, and at Rank 4 you should be able to kill most Vampires in one round. Honestly this power should be able to hit a whole COTERIE of Vampires at Rank 4, at which point it would have a great use at the start of a pack-vs-coterie fight, but still. I'll call this Amber, assuming Vampires and blood-related magics are relevant to your story.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#e74c3c\"><u><b>Speed Beyond Thought:</b></u></span> Boring, irrelevant, niche and overly restricted.<br /> <br /> This Gift lets you run at 10x speed for 8 hours, concentrating on doing nothing but running. When you finish running you have to eat immediately or Frenzy. To top it all off it costs Gnosis and there is a roll required, which feels entirely pointless. You'll probably pass, but still, you could fail; it's difficulty 7. At Rank 4, as a Silent Strider, you probably have better transport options, including GROUP transport options. If this let you bring along your whole pack it might be okay. It would still be pretty meh.<br /> <br /> You'll only get use out of this because you bought it and the ST wrote in a situation where it could shine. Pass.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-28T14:04:38-08:00","id":1548824,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Silver Fang Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Mastery:</b></u></span> Suits the Silver Fangs to a tee, though it should probably include Garou Kinfolk. This Gift lets you force Garou to obey you, including BSDs and Ronin etc.<br /> <br /> Charisma + Leadership, difficulty of Wits +3, so 4-8 typically. On a success you get the target to fulfil a single non-suicidal command lasting for one turn per success. Your Pure Breed probably adds to your pool here.<br /> <br /> Did you notice? There's no cost. No Gnosis, no WP, you just roll.<br /> <br /> At Rank 4 you can completely change your role in combat and just start going &quot;BSD #1, help us attack your Alpha!&quot;, &quot;BSD #2, help us attack your Alpha!&quot;, &quot;BSD #3, help us attack your Alpha!&quot; Gradually you can completely shift the action economy to suit your party. Auto-take for any Rank 4 Silver Fang. It's also important to note that being Rank 5 or 6 grants no protection against this Gift, RAW.<br /> <br /> Oh, and for what it's worth, based on what we're told about the Silver Fangs and how they see the other Tribes, this Gift has probably done more harm to Gaia and the Garou Nation than most others.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Mindblock:</b></u></span> This, with Mastery, feels like a bit of a kid in the playground situation. &quot;I can control other Werewolves! Nuh-uh you can't control me back, I'm immune!&quot;<br /> <br /> Attempts to control your mind are difficulty 10. Lots of enemies in the WOD will struggle to possess, control and command you as a result. It's worth noting that this grants no immunity to powers which are based on emotion; you can be terrified into freezing, Presence powers will still work on you, and so on. There's no reason not to take this; it's passive and will likely come up often enough to be worth the investment. It also lets you confront Vampires and other Silver Fangs / Shadow Lords with more confidence.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sidestep Death:</b></u></span> Flavour-wise this might seem a bit off, but assassination attempts on rulers are very prominent throughout history. Personally I would also have this on the Bone Gnawer list, taught by Rat instead of Lion.<br /> <br /> This Gift lets you spend 3 WP, once a scene, to completely evade an attack which would put you below Crippled (before Soak). It could be a nuke, or a gunshot, or a claw strike. Some teleportation is baked in; chances are you'll shift a few inches away for 99% of attacks, but very rarely you could be thrown a huge distance away.<br /> <br /> Ideally you don't want to need this Gift, but I can't see any reason not to take it. You could use it for non-suicidal suicide-bombing, though Stepping Sideways already has that covered. Much like Mindblock this isn't exciting, and it feels a little bit... boring? Like a power baked up to remove all potential weaknesses from your character, a &quot;ha- did you think that would work on me? \u00ac\u203f\u00ac&quot; situation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-28T14:18:35-08:00","id":1548825,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Stargazer Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Preternatural Awareness:</b></u></span> This Gift makes you harder to hit for a whole scene, and handily subtracts dice instead of manipulating difficulty. This can be better or worse than manipulating difficulty.<br /> <br /> Spending Gnosis and rolling Perception + Athletics (difficulty 7) activates the Gift. For every success, all attackers subtract 1 die from all their attacks to hit the Stargazer. At the upper end you can expect 3-4 successes. Subtracting 3-4 dice from all incoming attacks can make it so that weaker enemies have no chance to hit you (remember that many spirits have a WP on the lower end!), and removing 3-4 dice from the attack rolls of powerful attackers can reduce their hit chance or reduce their overall damage output by reducing successes. Since it lasts for a Scene and applies to all incoming attacks, there's really no reason not to take this or not to use this!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b><u>Mindblock:</u></b></span> See the Silver Fang list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Strike the Air:</b></u></span> See the Children of Gaia list. Arguably you should either take Preternatural Awareness or Strike the Air, since they have overlaps in their utility, but arguably it's worth having both.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-28T14:21:26-08:00","id":1548826,"title":"","content":"Speed Beyond Thought always reminds me of how much the WtA books seem to forget how distances work. Like, I'm find with &quot;distance&quot; being a funky concept when it comes to spirit magic so some Gifts work variably because spirit magic doesn't care about precise human measurements. But these sorts of Gifts just modify your mundane speed. Speed Beyond Thought lets a Dex 5 (in homid) character travel about 2,200 miles. While is impressive in a vacuum, when is that actually practical? Like, you can run straight across the US from one coast to the other without having to fly... but you could also just fly on a Silver Fang's private jet. Or use a Moon Bridge and get there a few orders of magnitude faster (even if the Moon Bridge isn't going to land you exactly where you want to go, a few hundred miles is still easy enough to deal with for a Garou). The normal long running rules lets any Garou travel that kind of distance on foot in 2-3 days depending on their Stamina without any powers beyond basic shifting for extra Stamina.<br /> <br /> There's just zero use case for it because the distance is way too far to be useful for shorter trips, and ends up in a category of way better options (most of which don't cost XP to get), and it doesn't do anything if that massive distance runs into things like crossing large bodies of water/etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-28T15:26:05-08:00","id":1548829,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1548826#post1548826\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Speed Beyond Thought always reminds me of how much the WtA books seem to forget how distances work.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fully agreed. Silent Striders deserved gifts which help them AND THEIR PACK to travel through the natural world, and through the Umbra, with speed and silence and while limiting their risks of running into trouble. They should have Gifts which naturally lead them to paths which avoid ambushes, Gifts which guide them towards safe havens they normally wouldn't be able to find (like a hideout of the Underground Railroad in Wyld West), and Gifts which grant additional dice to them or even the whole pack for any challenges they encounter during travel.<br /> <br /> Instead they get weird &quot;uh you can run 10x faster for 8 hours&quot; and &quot;you always know which way Up is&quot; powers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-28T15:37:05-08:00","id":1548830,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Elder Brother Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Call Elemental:</b></u></span> It would be very easy, and a mistake, to simply view this as a &quot;combat summon power&quot;. It can be that, but it can also achieve so much more.<br /> <br /> This Gift costs one Gnosis, you have to roll Gnosis at a difficulty of the local Gauntlet, and then you have to roll a Manipulation + Occult (difficulty 7) to convince the Elemental to help you, which is a little annoying but flavourful. If you convince them to help you (convincing the water elemental to be your guide, convincing the air elemental to calm the storm threatening your city, convincing the fire elemental to incinerate your foes, convincing the earth elemental to guard and block a path while you escape) they will, RAW, remain under the control of the ST. They're an NPC, not a simple extension of your character sheet, and they'll behave in a manner that is helpful from their elemental and sometimes alien POV.<br /> <br /> That means the Earth Elemental won't likely be convinced to let your ally who you thought was dead but was just KO'd pass (you convinced them to block the way!), and the Fire Elemental you convince to help you incinerate your foes may well incinerate an enemy who you had thoughts of trying to redeem who you weren't expecting to show up, or indeed blow up someone who was being mind controlled. So while this power is amazing, it should be used with caution.<br /> <br /> The Elemental statblocks we are given in W20 are for <i>minor</i> elementals, but they are all we have. Simply reading the Gift you might think this one would be unrated (black) or perhaps Amber, but in truth it's a solid Green due to its versatility.<br /> <br /> All spirits have Airt Sense, Materialize, Realm Sense and Re-form, in addition to the Charms listed.<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b><u>Earth</u></b></span><br /> W9, R4, G5, E20<br /> Charms: Armour, Umbraquake<br /> <br /> Earth Elementals are hard to hit, soak well, hit well, and their damage is middling (though a Willpower of 9 will carry over for more damage via successes on the hit). Armour lets it spend 2 Essence to gain +5 soak for a scene. Umbraquake lets it knock everyone present to the ground, inflicting 2 dice of bashing damage on all present.<br /> <br /> Arguably the best option for a combat, depending on whether you value Dexterity/Stamina (Willpower) or Strength (Rage). This spirit could very easily act as the tank of your party, holding a choke point. With a soak of 9 or 14 and 20ish points of 'health' to work with, an Earth Elemental could be hugely influential in protecting your pack or guarding some vulnerable NPCs.<br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#e74c3c\"><u>Air</u></span></b><br /> W3, R8, G7, E18<br /> Charms: Create Wind, Updraft<br /> <br /> There isn't any reason you'd want to use this, since Fire Elementals get +2 Willpower and +2 Rage. If you want mental or spiritual assistance, you'd go for Water. This also has the least Essence. Create Wind lets you start or stop wind patterns, including starting or calming a tornado, which is great, but specialist. So if you want to inflict or avert powerful weather patterns, Wind Elemental is a great choice. Updraft allows the Elemental to throw a man-sized target up into the air... by rolling its Willpower. Which is 3.<br /> <br /> Red unless you need to start or stop a storm for some reason.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b><u>Fire</u></b></span><br /> W5, R10, G5, E20<br /> Charms: Blast (Flame), Create Fires<br /> <br /> Blast is, to be frank, broken. You don't roll to hit, there is no chance to dodge, and you just skip to rolling damage. By spending 2 Essence the Fire Elemental can automatically inflict a ranged attack which deals 10 dice of Aggravated damage. If you can summon a Fire Elemental and instruct it to destroy your enemy, it may well oneshot them. This is a great ranged support option, with the Pack working to stop the Fire Elemental from being surrounded and ripped apart.<br /> <br /> With a Dexterity/Stamina of 5 and a Strength of 10, this is a handy ally to have around. A Rage of 10 makes them highly aggressive and brutal, likely setting fire to things for the hell of it, and making the situation much more caustic and aggravated! Keep in mind that you have convinced an NPC to help you. The ST should keep control of that NPC and have them do things they see as helpful, unless you take direct control with a Rite or Gift.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b><u>Water</u></b></span><br /> W6, R4, G10, E20<br /> Charms: Cleanse the Blight, Flood, Healing<br /> <br /> Gnosis is Charisma, Manipulation, Appearance, Intelligence, Wits and Perception. Even before you get to powers, a Water Elemental is a great source of advice, wisdom, lore, social support with spirits, a graceful companion and so on. While this spirit is handy enough in a fight with a Willpower of 6 and a Rage of 4, you'd generally be better off using Earth or Fire for combat. That said, this spirit can be very helpful in combat if you keep it protected.<br /> <br /> By spending 2 Essence it can use the Healing Charm, rolling its Gnosis (10) against a difficulty of 6 or 8 (depending on damage type). On a success it heals 10 points of damage to the target! This will fully restore an unconscious Huge Size PC to full health in a single go. This Charm can only be used on a single target once per scene.<br /> <br /> Cleanse the Blight allows a Water Elemental to purify water (a stream, a pond, a lake, a river), removing Wyrmish corruption and pollution. Rolling Gnosis against a difficulty reflecting the intensity of the corruption, this is a great &quot;clean-up&quot; power to trigger after you've killed the most powerful Banes and put a stop to the source of the pollution.<br /> <br /> Flood is a tad niche but it's pretty impressive; by spending a single point of Essence the spirit can flood a small or large area depending on its power; at the lower end it can flood a city block! This alone could massively influence a session, though it's worth noting that the flood is natural; it must relate to a body of water which already exists, and it can't flood a skyscraper or a mansion at the top of a dry hill.<br /> <br /> Water Elementals are the best at Airt Sense, making them exceptional guides; with a Gnosis of 10 they will travel through the Umbra and find obscure locations with relative ease.</div> <br /> All in all the damage, healing and overall support potential of this power is incredible. While you could easily summon and command an Elemental using Rites, these will tend to be slower and require the right setup and equipment; this Gift allows you to pull off a lot of spiritual shenanigans in a very short period.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Durance*:</b></u></span> See Shadow Lords list. Arguably a stronger Gift for the Uktena who specialize in such matters.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#e74c3c\"><u><b>Hands of the Earth Lords:</b></u></span> This power grants some very strong-ish telepathy. Spending a Gnosis and making a difficulty 7 Dexterity + Occult check lets the Garou move an object weighing up to 900kg for one turn per success. This is equivalent to lifting with a Strength of 11, so a Garou with a base Strength of 5 (9 in Crinos) would need to roll Willpower or use a Gift to shift it. This lifting can be done with a gesture of a hand and requires no direct contact. The object moving at 20mph ain't bad!<br /> <br /> What you're looking at is, unfortunately, a slightly light and slightly slow wrecking ball. 8.9 meters per second just isn't fast enough, at this mass, to get the momentum you need for a serious collision. You can't pick up and throw a car (a random Volvo will be 2000ish kg), and I feel like if you can't throw cars around what's the point?<br /> <br /> If this power didn't cost a Gnosis, or came at a lower rank, I might like it. But all in all I just find this boring and weirdly conservative with its numbers. The effect is meh, it requires a roll, it costs Gnosis, and it's Rank 4. All of this just tips it over into Red, for me.<br /> <br /> ============================<br /> <br /> Call Elemental was a real revelation for me, and a rare example of a Rank 4 power I'd go out of my way to purchase out-of-Tribe. I think most Theurges would love to have such an ability!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-28T16:36:53-08:00","id":1548831,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Younger Brother Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <u><b>Call of the Cannibal Spirit:</b></u> Interesting call to have a summon power with no stats for the summon. Wendigo is in W20 Corebook, but purely as a Totem Spirit, we get no idea of the stat-line for his Avatar.<br /> <br /> Searching around online gets us this from an older edition:<br /> <br /> W8 R10 G6 E32<br /> <br /> Blast (Ice), Create Wind, Freeze, Tracking<br /> <br /> Assuming you're ready to use those stats, or find a version of Wendigo in one of the books, or the ST is happy to invent some stats for him, it could work well. You have to have a piece of the target, and you have to dance for three turns before spending 1 Gnosis and 1 Rage. The Gift does demand a Charisma + Occult test, difficulty 8, and on a botch you will be devoured by Wendigo (though presumably he has to beat you in combat, rather than just instantly swallowing you whole).<br /> <br /> Similarly, if the spirit fails to devour your target, it will come back to eat you.<br /> <br /> All in all my takeaway with this Gift is that it's silly. You are a Rank 4 Garou, likely in a pack of Rank 3+ Garou. You are a pack of murderous Garou killing machines. Why are you sending a spirit on a side quest to devour one of your foes? If you can get past that, and assuming the stats are decent, this Gift is pretty good. There is no max duration mentioned, though the Gift does say that it can be 'prevented' from getting its target which suggests a time limit of some kind, or Wendigo simply giving up eventually if it can't find the target or the target is too well protected. Given how few details we have, and how the basic formula of things being resolved on-screen is challenged by this Gift, this winds up feeling more like a poorly defined Rite or NPC Gift, and thus I'll leave it unrated.<br /> <br /> The either/or of an enemy being killed outright offscreen, or Wendigo crashing back into your Caern to devour you, is silly. Don't bother with this one.<br /> <br /> If you wanted to rework it to be more interesting, simply use the stats listed here, and have this as a power where you entreat Great Wendigo to help you with a hunt. You pay upfront with some of your Rage and Gnosis, along with a piece of the target, and no roll is required. You have entered into a bargain with Wendigo where the two of you will travel together and hunt tirelessly until the target you have fed to his Avatar is devoured by the spirit. Your pack can follow, sure, but it's a bond between you and the Avatar. If you either refuse to keep going or try to divert from the hunt, or otherwise offend the spirit, he will attack you mercilessly, and when one of you is dead the effect of the Gift is finished. If the hunt does succeed, Wendigo disappears when he devours the target's heart. Now it's a player-driven power where you can find yourself hunting down a single BSD for weeks on end to the exclusion of all other targets because you entered into this bargain and you aren't sure you'd win the fight if you backed out now. It's risky but in a frenetic, interesting way where your role in the hunt matters; after all you might be able to track the target in a way that the spirit can't, like by checking their social media or phone signals...<br /> <br /> For what it's worth, I would give my version Amber. Solid, not broken.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Chill of Early Frost:</b></u></span> This lets you drive a large area to a temperature below freezing. Intelligence + Occult at a difficulty depending on season (4/6/9), lasting for one day per success, and impacts a 8km radius. Creatures without a natural coat of fur lose 2 dice from all their actions, likely due to the mystical nature of the cold. If it was already freezing, this Gift can make it even colder. The narrative impacts are potentially impactful, including stopping construction as people are stuck indoors, blocking roads with ice, bursting pipes and so on.<br /> <br /> Against most non-Garou enemies this Gift's -2 to dice pools is very handy for fights and other challenges. If you're being chased by a Pentex team or by a gang of Mages or perhaps some Fomori, they'll be at -2 dice to all their attempts to follow your tracks. Human hunters going after your wolf-kin will struggle to find them, struggle to hit them, struggle to chase them, and be more easily found by Garou and Kinfolk. All in all this is a great conservation power and could do a lot to help the Garou tilt the odds in favour of wolves and the Garou Nation. Given that it could influence many battles and challenges and scenes playing out at once, I give this Gift a tentative Green. If you're dealing with complex events which span a whole area, rather than problems which boil down to &quot;go to place, solve problem which is located purely in place&quot;, Gifts like this can help a lot.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Hero's Stand:</b></u></span> See the Get of Fenris list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Scream of Gaia:</b></u></span> See the Lupus list.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-28T17:04:06-08:00","id":1548833,"title":"","content":"Speaking of pet peeves... I really hate that Hands of the Earth Lords doesn't just use the Feats of Strength chart. It doesn't change the issues with how inefficient the Gift is, but you could get so much out of just, &quot;You can perform a Feat of Strength (p. XX) as if you had Strength 11.&quot; It would just feel better.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-28T17:27:48-08:00","id":1548834,"title":"","content":"<i><b>A note on Rank 5 Gifts; I expect them to be character-defining. Expect a lot of Reds, and I'll be giving suggestions on how to fix Gifts that fall short.</b></i><br /> <br /> <u><b>Rank 5 Homid Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Assimilation:</b></u></span> Cool in concept, bad in execution. The Gift is meant to make you assimilate into a foreign culture, loving that. You can haggle in a Soukh or debate in a Mosque despite being an extraordinarily white New Yorker, love it. The problem is the execution. Assimilating into a culture which is 'foreign' to you is difficulty 9. That is, using the Gift in the only situation where it would meaningfully help you is almost impossible.<br /> <br /> More to the point, this Gift isn't mind control. It doesn't make you seem like you fit into the BSD Hive, it just makes you behave and talk and understand in a way which would blend in. So you still need a disguise, or some kind of cover story (&quot;my visions led me here, I am a Theurge of the Festering Pit, you might have heard of us&quot;).<br /> <br /> I would fix this Gift by making it 100% passive and requiring no roll. You embody the Cosmopolitan Human and the Cosmopolitan Garou. You are at home in every city, every temple, every Caern, every office, every caravan. While you don't magically blend in, you will always know the cultural expectations and lingo, the language, the customs. This would come with an innate -3 difficulty to all Etiquette rolls as well.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Beyond Human:</b></u></span> This feels like a very neat capstone to the Homid Garou, conceptually. That Curse which has kept you apart from the world of man from the day of your First Change? It becomes flipped on its head. Now you are a feral creature of intimidating magnetism. That on its own, turning the Curse from a weakness into a strength, would make a pretty neat Gift and probably be Amber. The ability to spend Rage or Gnosis to boost your social stats, including above 5, for a whole scene really pushes the Gift into something spectacular. The ability to fortify your Manipulation for Rites, your Charisma/Manipulation/Appearance for Gifts, and simply to ace simple Social rolls, is great. If you have a good way to generate Rage then this Gift becomes a very easily fueled means of mastering social situations.<br /> <br /> I have no suggested changes here; the Gift works perfectly.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#e74c3c\"><u><b>Part the Veil:</b></u></span> Boring and pointless. This Gift allows you to grant immunity to Delirium to a single human for the duration of a scene. If they later encounter some cause of Delirium they will forget much of what they learned.<br /> <br /> Not to be mean to regular mortals, since they absolutely are important in the WOD, immunity to Delirium is already pretty common. Kinfolk, Fomori, Kami, Mages, Vampires, and so on. If for some reason you need to prove you are a supernatural to a trustworthy regular human, such as a romantic partner, chances are you need to do that before you hit Rank 5. But say you do - you'd turn into a wolf. Or show off some obviously supernatural ability. There are real uses for this power, like stopping a friendly cowboy from running off in a panic when you go Crinos, but honestly there is already a form that solves this problem; Hispo.<br /> <br /> Parting the Veil should be a Rite, and it should grant immunity to Delirium lasting a whole lunar month. Meanwhile I would replace this Gift with an totally new one which allows you to turn a willing human into Kinfolk. A permanent cost or limit would need to be baked in, such as you only being able to bring a human into the fold with the approval of a revered Ancestor Spirit, or perhaps having to spend a permanent dot of Gnosis.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-01T01:37:12-08:00","id":1548838,"title":"","content":"Parting the Veil has a more basic problem: it's inherently a violation of the Litany to use it. Sure, there are plenty of grey areas in the Litany and sometimes revealing your supernatural nature might be forgivable; but that slims down the use cases by an even vaster degree than the number of supernatural critters that are inherently immune or resistant to Delirium. If an Elder needs to whip out high powered forms in front of humans, they can get a Monkey Puzzle instead (not a bad place to look for a replacement power inspiration really).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-01T14:44:15-08:00","id":1548851,"title":"","content":"Yeah the Gift breaching the Litany is a big reason I don't like it, and why I would prefer a Gift which turns regular humans into Kinfolk; though arguably that should be a Rank 6 Gift concept rather than Rank 5. The idea would be that instead of breaking the litany by revealing your secrets to an outsider, you make them an insider, which is a charming loophole.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-01T17:44:42-08:00","id":1548857,"title":"","content":"Oh, I do get the whole concept of making new Kinfolk... though I'm not sure it's really &quot;Gift&quot; territory for design compared to Rites or spirit quests. I could see a Rank 5 or 6 Gift that could turn a willing human into a Possessed (allowing for four different versions like Attunement, possibly the Gorgon/Drone/Fomori being Rank 5, and Kami Rank 6) that gets a free rating in the Power that grants them additional status as Kinfolk even if they don't normally qualify. It feels a bit more appropriate for a high level Gift.<br /> <br /> I like the general idea of a Rank 5 Gift for Homids that lets them operate at full power in front of a human as well though. But as I mentioned, there's a Fetish that does pretty much what you'd want for it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-01T17:58:41-08:00","id":1548858,"title":"","content":"There are a couple of Rank 5? Rites from the Furies and Coggies that essentially create Kinfolk if I recall.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/479-damian-may","name":"Damian May","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar479_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-01T18:35:58-08:00","id":1548859,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 Metis Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b><u>Madness*:</u></b></span> This is the capstone of Metis as an oppressed and hated group, festering with internalized resentment. It captures that theme quite well.<br /> <br /> Spending a Gnosis and passing a Manipulation + Intimidation roll (difficulty of the target's Willpower) allows you to inflict a Derangement for one day per success. You get to control the intensity of the insanity, making it worse or better at will. The exact Derangement appears to be the ST's call, since it varies by individual.<br /> <br /> How good is this? Fugue, Hysteria, Multiple Personalities, OCD, Paranoia, Schizophrenia... it depends on how debilitating they wind up being for the NPC in question, whether they matter immediately or are more of a slow burn, how the ST implements the idea of you &quot;intensifying&quot; the madness at will, and whether your Chronicle includes NPCs who you want to oppose but can't kill.<br /> <br /> This is a bit of a lesson-teaching power, if nothing else. It isn't incredible, it isn't bad, it's suitably broad and story-driving for a Rank 5 power. I wish it were more powerful, less restrictive, and had a few more guidelines. All in all this is okay enough to be Amber.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Protean Form*:</b></u></span> This is the capstone of Metis as born Garou, fluid by nature. Partial Transformation becomes enhanced to allow you to apply +2 dice to a type of attack (e.g. grappling, clawing, biting) or +2 dice to the damage inflicted by a certain kind of attack. It also allows you to increase your speed.<br /> <br /> Note that there is no limit on the number of partial transformations you can undertake! So, in essence, this Gift grants you the ability to add +2 dice to all your attacks and +2 to all your damage rolls, with enough preparation, and the Form Mastery Gift.<br /> <br /> Beyond the obvious and RAW applications of this Gift, it's quite obvious that there are many situations where suckers on your palms would help, or where a mouth in your hand might be useful, or where a false gullet which diverts things you don't want to drink into an internal reservoir could save your life. If your ST is chill with these adaptations helping you the ways you'd expect them to help you, great. Given that it's a Rank 5 Gift you expect that kind of thing.<br /> <br /> But I need to judge the Gift based on the bare bones, and I'm left to consider whether +2 to hit and +2 to damage is enough. There are a lot of Gifts which can do these sorts of bonuses, but with Form Mastery this comes without cost and is basically 'free' if you have even a little prep time. If you're attacking several times in one round this sort of bonus is... pretty great! It might not be revolutionary in a given turn, but across a whole battle it will certainly add up. That, on its own, would make the Gift a dry yet functional Rank 5. The flavour and storytelling opportunities baked into the variety of transformations you can do with this Gift does bump it up a tad.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Totem Gift*:</b></u></span> Divine Intervention from the Totem Spirit of your Tribe. What you can expect from this Gift will depend on your Tribe (I'd rather be able to invoke Unicorn than Griffon, but that's just me!), but ultimately you are only limited with this Gift by your Gnosis and your dice pool. Rolling Charisma + Rituals, difficulty 7, with 1 success giving minor results and 10 successes giving results which might effect a whole country or completely change a destiny, opens a route to things which you likely couldn't achieve otherwise.<br /> <br /> This Gift could, for example, cause a major scientific breakthrough for environmentalism. Or bring a Pack of dead Garou back to life. Or create a new thriving Caern from nothing, where a Caern of legend stood 1000 years ago. Or produce a Klaive befitting the nature of your Totem.<br /> <br /> Personally I would, if I were adjusting the Gifts, broaden it to apply to both the Totem of your Tribe and the Totem of your Pack, making the Gift cover more ground for a single Garou. For example a Child of Gaia who was in a Pack bound to Eagle would have many 'domains' to call upon, from life and defense to travel and pride.<br /> <br /> This gets a Green, but the results are so ST-dependent that it has to take an asterisk.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-01T22:54:51-08:00","id":1548865,"title":"","content":"If you use Gifts like Create Element or Inundate (a Rokea standby) to soak targets with water or completely submerge them, Chill of Early Frost should become absolutely deadly. While rules for hypothermia tend to get shuffled to the side in stuff like Vampire, that can basically party wipe their ghoul or mortal backup. First Teams? It doesn't matter if you took your survival gear, it's been compromised, and your gun's trigger will no longer work because it is frozen in place.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-02T02:56:26-08:00","id":1548873,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 Lupus Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Many Lupus Gifts suffer from being Gifts which would be useful to a wolf, rather than being useful to a Garou who was born a wolf. Hopefully we can shake that off for their final set!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Elemental Gift:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis, roll Gnosis (difficulty 8). With a Gnosis of 5, which is the Lupus base, this gives a ~64% chance of passing the test, with an average of 1.8 successes.<br /> <br /> If you are successful you summon an Elemental ally which has power roughly equivalent to that of a Nexus Crawler. I won't bother getting into how strong that is beyond the fact that you can expect an ally who is a wise perceptive socially capable genius (Gnosis 10) with immense Strength (Rage 10). Naturally them being an Elemental of immense power will interfere with their problem-solving abilities somewhat!<br /> <br /> For each success you roll, for the duration of the 'summon', you can control a larger and larger volume of the element in question. So you could help to shield your pack from a storm, direct a flood of water, shape a wildfire, or carve a path through a mountain.<br /> <br /> All in all the ability to call upon a spirit on the upper end of 'normal' power levels by spending a single point of Gnosis is great. The roll being difficulty 8 is a little tricky; your odds of passing are decent but far from guaranteed, and even if you have a Gnosis of 9 you will still fail 25% of the time. Simply trying the Gift again does work, and strictly speaking there's nothing stopping you from summoning more than one elemental with this Gift either!<br /> <br /> This takes a Green much for the same reason that Call Elemental takes a Green; it's an excellent ability which makes you stronger and is certainly worth 15xp, even if it has some flaws. If this Gift's difficulty for the roll were the local Gauntlet, or were reduced significantly by how powerful the element of the Elemental you are summoning is in the area (easy to call upon a Fire Elemental in a firestorm, easy to call upon an Earth Elemental in the tunnels below a mighty mountain etc.) it would also be much better.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Song of the Great Beast:</b></u></span> I have very rarely spoken about the learning process for Gifts, but it's noteworthy here that the spirits which are capable of teaching Garou this Gift are secret, and possibly near-extinct. That doesn't take points off, but it makes the Gift less relevant even than most other Rank 5 Gifts.<br /> <br /> This Gift lets you spend 2 Gnosis and roll Charisma + Primal-Urge, difficulty 8, to try and summon an extinct hyperbeast of some kind. Personally I'd go for one of those giant slots the size of an elephant. The beast will solve a problem for you in a way which suits its nature, which will likely be a very blunt and savage approach for most animals, though not all. This power is flexible, expensive, difficult to use, and hard to define. That said we are told that the results should always be 'impressive'. More successes simply make the beast more friendly towards you.<br /> <br /> Oh and you have to howl deep in the deepest wilderness to use the Gift at all.<br /> <br /> Really, really hard to judge this one. Whether you have means of reducing the difficulty of the test with a Merit or Fetish or Gift, whether you have a boost to your Charisma, whether you have plentiful Gnosis... this is without even getting into what your ST produces when you tell them you want a sabretooth! The results may well be impressive, but compare with Totem Gift, for example, which is similarly loose in its definition, costs 1 Gnosis instead of 2, and can potentially do ANYTHING rather than the narrow set of things which might be achieved with a Really Big Animal.<br /> <br /> Ultimately I'd call this one narrow enough that it's Amber. Perfectly worth grabbing, it could do something huge and impactful, but between the cost and the difficulty and how narrow the potential outcomes are (big animal shows up and helps you with a problem a big animal would be able to help solve), along with the GIANT veil breach and the really nasty restriction of needing to be 'deep in the wilderness' to use it... it's not quite what I'd hope for.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-02T03:29:24-08:00","id":1548874,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 &amp; 6 Ragabash Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#e74c3c\"><u><b>Thieving Talons of the Magpie:</b></u></span> I think it's easy to get a bit confused with this one.<br /> <br /> Think of a random enemy. A mighty BSD Alpha. One of the Urge Wyrms itself. A Duke or Duchess of Malfeas. A Nexus Crawler.<br /> <br /> Now imagine depriving them of a single supernatural ability, one carefully chosen for best effect. You could steal a Spirit's 're-form' Charm, which may be useless to you, but would stop them from escaping. You could rob a Mage or Vampire of whatever ritual they are performing. You could steal a Nexus Crawler's ability to reshape reality to its will, and turn that power to your own designs.<br /> <br /> But here's the problem; you have to get THREE SUCCESSES on a Wits + Larceny roll, difficulty of the target's Willpower, and for every turn you want to keep the power in your possession you have to spend a Gnosis!<br /> <br /> This is sadly Red, despite its immense potential. It won't reliably work on any power worth stealing, because it will belong to someone with decent Willpower.<br /> <br /> My fix would be to make the theft last for one scene by default, and be extended by a whole scene if you spend a point of Gnosis. The Gift should require you to pluck something from the target as if you were pickpocketing them, and thus should be quite easy if you can take them unawares. So the test would either be a Wits + Larceny, difficulty determined by the ST ('stealing' the power as if it were a wallet in their pocket).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Thousand Forms:</b></u></span> This Gift lets you take the form of any animal within a size range of the smallest birds up to bison. You spend a Gnosis and roll Intelligence + Animal Ken, difficulty 6. For the rest of the scene you can roll to transform without spending Gnosis. Turning into a supernatural animal such as a Griffon or Pegasus or Unicorn requires a difficulty 9 roll and costs 2 Gnosis rather than 1.<br /> <br /> This is... neat. The stronger version of the Gift is very hard to use, but turning into a Dragon with all of your Gifts is a pretty neat trick. The main appeal is the flexibility granted by being able to turn into any number of disguises and utility forms. Posing as a rat, lizard, raven or big cat could have any number of effects when it comes to interacting with Fera. This Gift allows a Garou to become a fluid problem-solver who can remain out of reach, slipping into tiny spaces, blending into the environment, producing venoms, charming wild beasts, showing respect to spirits, playing tricks, performing assassinations, and so on. The supernatural side of the power could be better, but it's usable; if you get lucky it could produce a pretty spectacular result!<br /> <br /> And, for Legendary Ragabash...<br /> <br /> <u><b>Firebringer*:</b></u> Ultimately useless to you, personally, but potentially very impactful to the Garou Nation.<br /> <br /> In theory.<br /> <br /> Firstly it needs to be a power which can be used ON you. There are many great and wonderful abilities in the WoD which this could work with, such as a Nexus Crawler's reality-bending or a powerful yet corrupt Gift of the BSDs, the memory-altering powers of Dominate, the purification of Pyretics, the total rebuilding of a person or thing which comes with Naming, or even simply being able to learn a True Name with a glance. But you, the Garou in question, can never learn this ability. Instead you spend a point of permanent Gnosis and become the first teacher of this 'new gift', which winds up having a level and spirit-teacher the ST thinks is appropriate. And again, it has to be an ability which can be targeted onto a person; many, many powers of the WoD will be out of reach as a result.<br /> <br /> So you perform a great sacrifice for the Garou Nation, and now new Gift exists. But is the Garou Nation in trouble because it doesn't have the right <i>gifts</i>? In my honest opinion the Garou Nation is in trouble because every time it checks whether its gun is loaded, it does so by shooting itself in the paw.<br /> <br /> This one is ultimately impossible to rate, which is fine. If you have a NPC use it, great, if you have a PC use it for the good of the Pack and the whole of the Garou Nation, great. There are some really impressive Fera Gifts which would be amazing to import using this power! It would be very good, however, to have an example. Pick a pretty neat Gift and mention that it was first created using this power, to give some context for its history in the setting. This goes unrated, for obvious reasons.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-02T04:05:01-08:00","id":1548875,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 &amp; 6 Theurge Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#e74c3c\"><u><b>Feral Lobotomy:</b></u></span> Just use Beast Mind. You are a Garou, you can kill your enemies, doing a really hard roll and spending loads of Gnosis to permanently kneecap someone's Intelligence doesn't matter.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Malleable Spirit:</b></u></span> Now this is what I want. A Garou and a Spirit enter into a resisted roll of Gnosis vs Gnosis, with no cost or restrictions on when it can be used.<br /> <br /> The Spirit's difficulty is the Garou's Gnosis. The Garou's difficulty is based on what they're trying to do. Shifting dots around is difficulty 6 (e.g. a spirit could be calmed by having its Rage funneled into Willpower and Gnosis), changing disposition is difficulty 7 (e.g. a spirit could be made to love the Theurge or fear them, or hate the pack they guide), and entirely changing the type of Spirit is difficulty 9 (turning a mighty Bane into a Gaian spirit).<br /> <br /> This is exceptional, but to be quite honest it's too good, especially at extremes of Gnosis. If you had a Gnosis of 10 you can expect to roll 10 dice against a difficulty of 6-9, resisted by 1-10 dice against a difficulty of 10. There's nothing stopping you from trying again, nothing stopping you from using the Gift repeatedly, and at this point Spirits become clay you can remold at will. This can partly be controlled by the fact that the Umbra is full of many billions of spirits and that messing with the ecosystem unwisely has consequences. A Theurge who used this on, say, one of the Wyrm's most favoured servants would become public enemy #1!<br /> <br /> This is very obviously Green, but it requires some kind of guideline or limitation. The simplest one is to say that the spirit must be in some way willing, even if under duress. Flat outright refusal means this power won't work, but a Spirit which has promised to submit to this ability if they are defeated, outwitted, or break their word etc. would be a valid target. That would result in a situation where a common use is to offer this as a 'way out' to a spirit when they are at the brink of total defeat (though not all spirits care about such things, as many lack individuality), or to bargain with a spirit so that they can be transformed (e.g. many Banes are shortsighted and driven by immediate, ravenous desires. There are likely Banes which would agree to be turned into Naturae in exchange for sufficient Chiminage, since all that matters to them is that they get to fulfil their desires, and it's unlikely that they will understand how their desires will be changed by being transformed.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Ultimate Argument of Logic:</b></u> This Gift convinces someone of a fact, true or false, about the nature of reality. Manipulation + Performance requiring 3 successes to work, and the difficulty is the target's Wits + Enigmas, so a difficulty of 7+ is pretty believable. For Spirits you target Gnosis (Wits), which could be 10. For important Fera and Garou you might face a difficulty as low as 4, or as high as 8, who knows.<br /> <br /> But you need 3 successes, and the result is... potentially pretty big, but hard to define. Making a Pentex exec (perhaps Wits 4, Enigmas 0) believe that Pentex is about to go bust and that he needs to dump his stocks immediately could form a pretty important part of a plan! So if you try to use this Gift on targets with a high difficulty, expect disappointment. But it's cost-free gaslighting which you can spam against the whole world of low-Gnosis spirits and no-Enigmas mortals and supernaturals.<br /> <br /> The wording of the Gift is a little odd; you convince people of an 'aspect of existence', true or false, and the examples given are pretty cosmic and big. Can the Gift work for things like &quot;I am your brother&quot;, or &quot;you need to sell your stocks now?&quot; I don't think it can unfortunately! It's more for big, giant, all-encompassing things like &quot;Capitalism is bad&quot;.<br /> <br /> When you have the Capitalism is Bad power in your back pocket, ready to convert most of the people you run into, you do then come across the problem of the power changing things about the setting too easily. NPC Rank 5 BSD Theurges can, RAW, walk around telling Garou that Gaia has betrayed them, that Gaia is dead, and that the only goal which can be accomplished is trying to survive the Apocalypse. That doesn't sound like a fun ability to be on the receiving end of for a player!<br /> <br /> The lack of a cost, or limitations, or duration, or anything, makes this one a bit of a mess. It's arguably incredibly strong, it won't work at all if the difficulty is high most of the time, but when it does work it could allow you to completely change the expectations, beliefs and allegiances of far too many spirits, supernaturals and mortals.<br /> <br /> <i>&quot;It's a Rank 5 power, you need to be wise and sensible to access it!&quot;</i> is an argument that can be made for any silly Gift at this Rank, and I get why people make it, but you do need to account for PCs being driven by players, and for characters going insane, winding up corrupted, or having their normal patience stripped away by an absolute emergency. That's before getting into the 'Wisdom' Renown of the Garou Nation having a tangential relationship with being wise in a conventional sense.<br /> <br /> I'll call this one a wash and leave Ultimate Argument of Logic unrated. It needs significant reworking.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>As In The Beginning:</b></u></span> Tearing a hole in the Gauntlet and allowing a horde of friendly spirits through is always gonna be good. It isn't well-defined but it's very strong and it works well. There really isn't much to say about this one beyond the mechanics making sense (a cost of 3 Gnosis works, the difficulty matching the local Gauntlet works), and it being a Wits + Occult roll adds up pretty well with the stats you'll likely have invested in to reach Rank 6 as a Crescent Moon.<br /> <br /> Reducing the Gauntlet by 1 in the area, permanently, is a fun bonus. That alone grants this power some neat utility which could make things better or worse. Given how hellish the Penumbra is in many places, making the Gauntlet weaker could be a very bad idea! The flavour opportunities for how this Gift might vary by Tribe are also nice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-02T05:36:40-08:00","id":1548877,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 &amp; 6 Philodox Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <u><b>Geas:</b></u> Enforcing a Geas and binding a target, or a group, to fulfil some task is a pretty nice high-level ability. We are told that the request cannot go against the target's nature, so presumably you cannot task a group of BSDs with destroying the Wyrm.<br /> <br /> We aren't really given any indication of how attempting to resist the compulsion works. Is it literally impossible? Sure, easy to go &quot;yes, it's literally impossible&quot; but how does that manifest? What happens if you're slow? What happens if you don't give the mission your all? This is one of these things where a lot more information is needed.<br /> <br /> The cost and the roll are fine, but this remains unrated as it doesn't actually give much of a guideline at all. In trying to implement this Gift, the ST essentially ends up writing their own homebrew version of the power, since the Gift is incomplete.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Wall of Granite:</b></u></span> While it's hard to picture this Gift playing out, since all I can see is horrible uncanny CGI, this Gift is pretty neat. You create a small barrier of stone which can completely protect you and a number of allies up to your Gnosis score, which also moves with you! With a Soak of 10 dice and 15 health levels it's very unlikely that it will come to harm, though personally I would have put a minimum threshold for damage.<br /> <br /> A single Gnosis point is spent, and there is no roll; the wall simply forms. I love this because even if it breaks you could erect another one! This Gift is amazing for protecting your Pack or defenseless allies, holding a position, protecting an important object, or buying time to escape via Stepping Sideways or some other method of transport. The uses are pretty flexible and even in an outright fight this grants you a great defensive option to hide behind while spirits or mind controlled enemies cause chaos outside.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Break the Bonds:</b></u></span> Immunity to supernatural coercion, Strength 15 when dealing with 'bonds' to herself and others, and for free you can banish the mental bonds others are held by. This requires a roll, Manipulation + Leadership at a difficulty of 11 - the target's WP, but you could free a lot of supernatural slaves in the WoD using this method. Spirits bound into slavery, supernaturals and mortals held by the blood bond, victims of Dominate and even those under a Geas. This Gift makes you the potential worst enemy of the Ventrue, Silver Fangs, Sidhe and so on, which could be great fun.<br /> <br /> This Gift has an absoluteness to it which I expect at levels 5 and 6.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-03T03:31:20-08:00","id":1548899,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 &amp; 6 Galliard Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Fabric of the Mind:</b></u></span> With no innate cost (we'll get to that!) you can imagine, storytell and dream a new being into existence. This being is limited only by your imagination and your successes, with an extended Intelligence + Performance slowly adding more and more dots of Attributes. You could, in theory, tell the tale of the mightiest Garou to ever live, granting this imagined being a Gnosis, Rage, Willpower of 10, 10 in every Attribute, and a plethora of Abilities, then send them into battle to win a glorious victory for Gaia.<br /> <br /> There's a problem with this, however.<br /> <br /> We're not told what happens when you Fail or Botch. We're not told how getting Gifts, Charms or other Traits onto this creature works; is a Level 3 Gift worth 3 successes? What about Charms? What about innate features of a being you might describe, like a Spirit being ephemeral or a Garou having regeneration?<br /> <br /> And then there's the difficulty. Difficulty 8. There may well be difficulty breaks you can use here, and a Performance spec in Storytelling would help for 10s granting 2 successes, but come on. Unless Failing or Botching does nothing at all (which would be very silly) that high base difficulty is going to cut your legend short.<br /> <br /> My version would be to sum up all of your Social and Mental Attributes, Willpower and Gnosis, along with your dots in Empathy, Performance, Subterfuge, Expression, Intimidation and Etiquette, to get a total number. Bit of faff to add them all up, but once you've done it you've done it, and the number will only change when you improve one of those Traits. That number is a value which you can distribute between Traits for anything you create, with a base of 1 in all Attributes already applied. You roll Intelligence + Performance once, with the difficulty determined by what you're describing. A mortal or Kinfolk is difficulty 5, a Garou is difficulty 6, another supernatural being is difficulty 7, and a legendary creature is difficulty 8. You allocate stats and the being you've evoked comes with anything you'd expect it to have by default, such as regeneration, stepping sideways, flight or what have you. It lasts for one scene per success.<br /> <br /> Anyways, back to the RAW Gift. The creature lasts for 1 scene if you spend 1 Gnosis, and so on, with you being able to spend Gnosis as it goes to keep it going. The horror of your creation potentially becoming aware of its impending death and begging you to keep it alive aside, this Gift could be great, but is held back by a lack of thought and testing. As written, it's Red, though if you roll nothing but 10s with the right specialty you can expect to create a being beyond compare. That simply isn't statistically reasonable however.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Head Games:</b></u></span> A Gift of complete emotional control, Galliard Elders can completely rewrite the emotions of a target with a Manipulation + Empathy check against a difficulty of the target's Willpower. You could make someone look upon their own child with utter disgust, or with love they've never felt. Given the problems Garou have regarding the other Tribes, their Kin, and their feelings towards the Fera, this Gift could be responsible for great good (moral qualms aside!) or great evil.<br /> <br /> The implanted emotions don't last beyond the scene itself, but they can stick around if reinforced. Basically this Gift could permit a breakthrough where one would otherwise be impossible. Keep in mind that emotions are not mind control, however. A BSD who feels warmly towards you might decide that you are destined for the spiral, or some other awful fate. Emotions and core values are different; a person who controls and mistreats the things and people he loves is a dangerous person to be admired by! This opens up a lot of interesting narrative space within the game's story; that Elder may well resent and dislike his Metis son, but even if you remove those negative emotions, all of that history and all of those messed up values still exist. &quot;I love you, but I can't be seen with you&quot; is arguably more painful to hear than &quot;I don't want to see you&quot;.<br /> <br /> A solid Green. Just be careful what you wish for.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Break the Bonds:</b></u></span> See the Philodox list. Very strange to see a Level 6 Gift repeated!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-03T03:44:19-08:00","id":1548900,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 &amp; 6 Ahroun Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Kiss of Helios:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis. Become immune to natural sources of fire and heat entirely. Artificial and supernatural flames will never deal more than a single point of Bashing damage. To top this all off, you can boost your Brawl damage by 2 dice of Aggravated.<br /> <br /> If you take account for the possibility of those you grapple being harmed by the flames, that many creatures of the Wyrm fear and shun fire, and that many enemies use fire as a weapon, and finally that +2 dice of Aggravated damage on an Ahroun can add up to a lot of additional damage across a whole round... this is pretty good!<br /> <br /> Abusing this power to the absolute maximum requires similar thought to Master of Fire from Homid's list. Unfortunately this Gift makes MoF entirely redundant, so I would suggest refunding 3xp to a Homid who wound up with both.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Strength of Will:</b></u></span> Spend a point of Willpower, roll Charisma + Leadership difficulty 8. All allies within 100 feet, and all packmates within 100 miles, regain 1 WP per success.<br /> <br /> Assuming you have no way of reducing the difficulty, this is risky, but definitely worth having. The benefit provided by reinforcing the Willpower points of many, many allies is great. Your immediate goal should be to stack your dice pool and reduce the difficulty as much as humanly possible, and ideally you should have a way of recovering Willpower yourself, whether through a Fetish, ally, Gift or Charm.<br /> <br /> All said, this is a great Gift. Given that you can feed WP to allies within 100 miles, this is a handy way to reinforce your friends when you can't be with them. You could be miles away and granting many, many points of WP to your packmate every single turn while they struggle in a duel against a mighty servant of the Wyrm. This also feeds into the idea of the Ahroun as a leader not only in war, but in all things. Providing a pack with endless WP is a huge boon to any project, whether it is spying, ritual, legal, performance or warfare.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Unstoppable Warrior:</b></u></span> Anything which builds upon the basekit of the Garou, and passively improves it, is great. Very few Gifts do this, annoyingly. This Gift lets you regenerate and heal from Aggravated damage as if it were Lethal damage, unless it comes from silver. This means that you can take 6 points of Aggravated from claws, and 6 turns later you'll be completely back to normal, with no special healing, no Fetish, no Charm, no Gift. If you have anything which speeds up your natural regeneration or lets your body heal Lethal very quickly, this will help make the best use of this Gift.<br /> <br /> It isn't exciting, but it's cool, and useful. Solid Green. I could wish for more, like it adjusting the Remain Active test you take when you would be 'killed', but it's fine as is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-03T03:57:00-08:00","id":1548901,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 Black Fury Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Gorgon's Gaze:</b></u></span> A truly terrifying ability which is hard to acquire due to the rarity and danger of the Basilisk in the Umbra, this Gift allows you to paralyze a foe or even turn them to stone.<br /> <br /> Rolling Appearance + Occult against a difficulty of the target's Willpower causes them to freeze for one turn per success, absolutely paralyzed, and with 5 successes you turn them entirely to stone. The cost is 1 Gnosis.<br /> <br /> This... sadly isn't that great. By now you've had opportunities to get freezing powers, paralyzing powers, banish-in-fear powers. If your Appearance is 2 and your Occult is 3, the odds of you getting 5 successes are very slim. Don't get me wrong, the sheer AURA of being able to glance at an idiot mortal and turn them into a statue is great, it's a terrifying thing to be able to do, but you can clown on people with a Willpower of 3 when you're a Cub, most likely. The requirement of eye contact only makes it more niche.<br /> <br /> Extremely cool, but probably one I wouldn't bother buying, unless there are a significant number of low Willpower high importance NPCs knocking about. It's perfectly reasonable for an Elder to have a surprisingly low Willpower, at which point this becomes less weak.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Thousand Forms:</b></u></span> See the Ragabash list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Wyld Warp:</b></u></span> A pretty neat summoning power with a difficulty based on Gauntlet, which is much better than having a difficulty of 8 as so many high level Gifts do. Spending a Gnosis and a Rage lets you roll Wits + Enigmas, and the more successes you get the more intense the impact of the arriving Wyldlings. Their behaviour is unpredictable but beneficial, and likely extremely powerful. Even if the Gift just causes all of the fodder to be torn apart, thrown out of windows, sent flying into the sky, leaving the Big Bad for you to fight alone, that could be an enormous effect. Plus you could get healed, or find your Gnosis/Rage restored, and so on. A random effect table would be neat, to be honest, to help guide the ST towards something genuinely random, but as it stands this is a cool effect.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-03T05:18:45-08:00","id":1548902,"title":"","content":"Giving Fabric of the Mind red instead of black feels out of keeping with other entries as the top tier Gifts get into strange powers that are very iffy in their mechanics.<br /> <br /> ---------<br /> <br /> While I'm not sure it changes many of the Gifts rankings, it is worth keeping in mind that unless you have to pay a WP to use the Gift, you can always spend a WP on the activation roll. Yeah, the diffs can be high, WtA also has a lot of diff reduction in its material as well, but Elders tend to have 8-10 dice in their core dice-pools, so passing diff 8 tests is fairly likely. Having any number of things that might knock the diff down 2 and spending a WP on the roll can make large number of successes come up way more.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-03T05:41:29-08:00","id":1548903,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1548902#post1548902\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Giving Fabric of the Mind red instead of black feels out of keeping with other entries as the top tier Gifts get into strange powers that are very iffy in their mechanics.<br /> <br /> ---------<br /> <br /> While I'm not sure it changes many of the Gifts rankings, it is worth keeping in mind that unless you have to pay a WP to use the Gift, you can always spend a WP on the activation roll. Yeah, the diffs can be high, WtA also has a lot of diff reduction in its material as well, but Elders tend to have 8-10 dice in their core dice-pools, so passing diff 8 tests is fairly likely. Having any number of things that might knock the diff down 2 and spending a WP on the roll can make large number of successes come up way more.</div> </div> </div> </div>The rules explicitly forbid spending a Willpower on a Gift activation roll. And I've left Fabric of the Mind as Red because even if you try your best to run it RAW, the most likely outcome is a useless 'summon' which has a few points of attributes. The Gift needs a lot of work to make it fully playable, but even if you ignore all of its problems and just try playing it as is, I don't think it's very good at all. Say you get 10 successes total - that doesn't even give you enough for Dex 5 + Brawl 5 + Strength 5, so what you have created is next to useless, and also has a Willpower, Perception, Intelligence, Gnosis etc. of 0.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-03-2025, 08:49 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-03T05:47:58-08:00","id":1548904,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Automatic Successes: Spending a Willpower point on an action gives the player one extra success on any roll. Only one point can be spent this way each turn, but the success is guaranteed. Spending Willpower in this way completely negates the effects of a botch. Some rolls do not allow a character to spend Willpower, including damage rolls or any roll to activate Gifts. </div> </div> </div>Here you go.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-03T12:39:26-08:00","id":1548917,"title":"","content":"Blah, that's a silly change I completely missed all this time apparently. Probably didn't help that at least one of the W20 devs said the exact opposite when people complained about how high the default diffs in WtA were over the years as something they really wanted W20 to address... oh well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-03T13:04:38-08:00","id":1548920,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1548917#post1548917\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Blah, that's a silly change I completely missed all this time apparently. Probably didn't help that at least one of the W20 devs said the exact opposite when people complained about how high the default diffs in WtA were over the years as something they really wanted W20 to address... oh well.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think Willpower not working on Gifts is fine, so long as the difficulties of Gifts are reasonable. The writers seem to think that an Elder Gift ought to be hard to use, but part of the POINT of Gifts is that they are breathed into you, become part of you, and that they are second nature to use once you have them.<br /> <br /> If I were rewriting the Gifts (probably the culmination of this project!) I'd change the emphasis towards passive effects and effects which grant you a new way of using a standard mechanic. You can write Level 5 Gifts where you spend Gnosis and a thing just HAPPENS, you shouldn't be rolling for everything.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-03T13:31:52-08:00","id":1548922,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 Bone Gnawer Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Riot:</b></u></span> Immediately I find it interesting that the spirits provoking a riot are 'malevolent'. It's a relatively regressive framing off the bat and makes this feel more like a BSD Gift. Regardless, the riot which forms goes after a target of your choice. However, it tends to spiral out of control and have unexpected results, which makes sense for a riot.<br /> <br /> Wits + Subterfuge, difficulty 8, with a riot radius of one mile per success. Cost of 1 Gnosis, naturally.<br /> <br /> You could say this about a lot of Gifts, but I would argue that this is a very good example of something which is better to do organically. Using Kinfolk, using community leaders, using a variety of Gifts to reach a larger goal, invoking spirits. This Gift isn't terrible, it can fulfil a lot of different needs (distraction, pinning down a business etc.) but I would argue that it very unwisely puts the &quot;protectorate&quot; of the Bone Gnawers at immense risk.<br /> <br /> When you start a riot with supernatural mind control, against a target of your choice, you put the lives of many marginalized people into the hands of fate, and more directly, the cops. Communities which are disproportionately likely to contain your Kinfolk, and your future Tribe members in rare cases. Like many Level 5 Gifts this feels like an ability which a sensible Elder will almost never use since it takes a nuke to the problem.<br /> <br /> All in all I'd call this a solid Amber, it isn't bad by any means, and it could have huge positive effects, but the inherent risks are also terrible.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Survivor:</b></u></span> I really wish this was passive.<br /> <br /> Survivor lets you become immune to certain requirements, like needing food, sleep, water. You also don't have to worry about extreme temperatures, natural disease, and natural poison. Special Wyrmish stuff has half the normal effect. Turning it on costs 1 Gnosis and a difficulty 6 Stamina + Survival, lasting for one day per success.<br /> <br /> By spending a second Gnosis point you can get a 10 round effect where you have +3 Stamina and ignore all wound penalties. The Gift then expires. When this Gift expires, either after (successes) days or after 10 rounds, you have to sleep for 8 hours. Pretty terrible in a battle!<br /> <br /> My immediate thought is that the Gift should make you inherently immune to natural threats (needing food, water, sleep) and that its active form should cost 1 Gnosis, last for a scene, and grant special benefits like immunity to Wyrmish toxins, bonus Stamina, and various other things. Still, the Gift isn't bad by any means.<br /> <br /> How good is +3 stamina for 10 rounds? Decent. Not amazing for this point in your career, but good. But falling asleep the moment those rounds end? Awful.<br /> <br /> So, ignoring the special 10 round version where you spend a second Gnosis, how does the base effect fare? I would argue that if you're a Rank 5 Bone Gnawer you should be able to handle surviving. A Gift which makes you not have to worry about stuff like this should apply to your whole pack, or a large group of mortals, since those are the people who are going to need help with such situations. An Elder Bone Gnawer should be helping a whole family of Kinfolk to ignore that they're going hungry for a few days while they run far into the wilds to hunt for food, not making it so they personally don't need a snack.<br /> <br /> Even if you're in a place like Malfeas where a good meal is hard to come by and impossible to keep clean of Wyrm taint, this Gift ONLY helps you. Your allies aren't going to benefit, which is just... far too little at this stage in the game.<br /> <br /> Sadly a Red, but I can see a much better Gift hidden in here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-03T14:01:46-08:00","id":1548924,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 Children of Gaia Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Halo of the Sun:</b></u></span> I love the subtle solar theme the Children of Gaia have going on. They're one of the Tribes which enjoy using the Greet the Sun Minor Rite, for example. It could be more present, but this Gift is a great way to bring it home.<br /> <br /> Spend a Gnosis, the Gift lasts for a whole Scene. Thank Gaia. I am tired of Level 5 Gifts where you randomly have to pass a difficulty 8 Appearance + Science roll or whatever the hell. The benefits are pretty great:<br /> <br /> - people facing towards you suffer +3 to all their difficulties<br /> - Vampires take Aggravated damage every turn from the sunlight<br /> - +2 dice of Aggravated damage to your attacks<br /> - Homid and Glabro deal Aggravated<br /> <br /> I would suggest including Lupus in the final point, but all in all this Gift is great. Becoming a beacon of raw sunlight which is difficulty 9 to hit is great on its own, and if you have anything else increasing the difficulty to hit you then the difficulty might hit 10. A problem with this Gift is that your allies may fall victim to it, but if they are stood to your side and facing away from you they shouldn't be effected. Enemies which don't rely on sight will likely be unbothered, but the vast majority of enemies do need to see you thankfully, even among Spirits.<br /> <br /> I'm such a Coggie fanboy that I would probably include some passive taint-removal into this Gift, as Helios' raw light burns away corruption all around, but the Gift is great as it is.<br /> <br /> <u><b>The Living Wood:</b></u> And we crash right down to earth. Spend a Gnosis, do a difficulty 8 roll, and for every success you bring a tree to life, wandering around like an ent. What are the stats? We don't know. We aren't even told if they're meant to be powerful allies or not. This is impossible to rate since we have no metric for what these trees should be like.<br /> <br /> Notably there isn't even a time limit. RAW this Gift may well animate a tree permanently, which is very funny to me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-03T14:19:18-08:00","id":1548925,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1548920#post1548920\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If I were rewriting the Gifts (probably the culmination of this project!) I'd change the emphasis towards passive effects and effects which grant you a new way of using a standard mechanic. You can write Level 5 Gifts where you spend Gnosis and a thing just HAPPENS, you shouldn't be rolling for everything.</div> </div> </div> </div>Having gotten a good deal into this a few times myself... I have some thoughts about how to approach this (tl;dr it depends a lot on some other design matters you'll invariably need to decide on first). Not sure if you want to chat about it at all, but if you do, I'm around.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1548924#post1548924\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><u><b>Rank 5 Children of Gaia Gifts</b></u>I would suggest including Lupus in the final point, but all in all this Gift is great.</div> </div> </div> </div>For every Breed but the lupus, this is already true for bites (I have no idea why you'd claw in lupus unless the situation really called for it or you're jacking up on Claw Gifts already and you'd get Agg from those as it is). <br /> <br /> Though boy do I hate how these small little differences seem to never get addressed in the books. Nothing in the books really addresses things like, &quot;lupus can't effectively teach homids how to fight as a wolf, because homids do agg and lupus can't soak agg if both are in their wolf-forms.&quot; Despite the fact that wolves roughhouse all the time, it's basically less dangerous to spar on all fours with everyone in Hispo instead... which is ridiculous.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-03T14:22:34-08:00","id":1548926,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1548925#post1548925\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Having gotten a good deal into this a few times myself... I have some thoughts about how to approach this (tl;dr it depends a lot on some other design matters you'll invariably need to decide on first). Not sure if you want to chat about it at all, but if you do, I'm around.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> For every Breed but the lupus, this is already true for bites (I have no idea why you'd claw in lupus unless the situation really called for it or you're jacking up on Claw Gifts already and you'd get Agg from those as it is).<br /> <br /> Though boy do I hate how these small little differences seem to never get addressed in the books. Nothing in the books really addresses things like, &quot;lupus can't effectively teach homids how to fight as a wolf, because homids do agg and lupus can't soak agg if both are in their wolf-forms.&quot; Despite the fact that wolves roughhouse all the time, it's basically less dangerous to spar on all fours with everyone in Hispo instead... which is ridiculous.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd be down for that! Once I've done all the mop-up with interesting Fera Gifts, lost Tribe Gifts etc. and maybe Rites, I'll definitely hit you up.<br /> <br /> And yeah, Lupus sucking in Lupus is just... so, so dumb.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-03T15:20:04-08:00","id":1548928,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 Fianna Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Call the Hunt*:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis, roll Charisma + Occult (difficulty 8). If you succeed the Huntsman shows up, along with a single hound. Booooo. But you can spend additional Gnosis and Rage to add more Hounds! So with a Gnosis and Rage of 5, you could potentially get 10 hounds.<br /> <br /> <u>Huntsman</u><br /> W10 R10 G5 E40<br /> Armour, Tracking<br /> <br /> <u>Hounds</u><br /> W6 R7 G2 E18<br /> Tracking<br /> <br /> Notice something? Low Gnosis. Gnosis is Perception and Wits. I would, if I were making this guy, swap the Rage and the Gnosis. He also lacks any form of ranged attack, weirdly. A Water Elemental is a better hunter than this dude, with its Gnosis of 10, which improves its Airt Sense, which is what you need to best follow the tracks of spirits and find the way into their realms. Yes, Tracking does let you unerringly hunt your target with a point of Essence, but many of the tricks and traps which smart prey will put down to mess with a hunter would be sensed with Perception or Wits. Poor Airt Sense may mean that while you can easily find your target, you enter the Realm they are in through a poor choice of entrance and find yourself having to fight a lot of enemies to reach them.<br /> <br /> Notably if you summon this guy and he doesn't think your reason for calling him is good enough (the target isn't evil enough, or strong enough) he will hunt you instead. Honestly if I were a Fianna Elder I would boast at a Moot that I could escape The Hunter himself, and then summon him, and hide from his awful Gnosis 5 (though of course this requires inventiveness due to the Tracking Charm). Great way for a Ragabash to farm some Renown, but whatever.<br /> <br /> As written this is unfortunately not as good as the designers probably intended. The Huntsman is a decent combat summon with his physical stats set to 10, but an ally that has 10 to hit and 10 to damage is equivalent to a Dexterity 5 Melee 5 Strength 5 ally holding a giant sword. Nothing to sniff at, but hardly a gamechanger at Rank 5. Yes his soak and his massive Essence pool are great, absolutely, and with Armour his soak will be even better, but anyone outside of his melee range has nothing to fear from him.<br /> <br /> How about his Hounds? They're fast and they're quite mean too. I would wager that many powerful 'boss' enemies could be overwhelmed if you brought a huge number of Hounds, simply because you would completely overwhelm the action economy... so this Gift does have potential to be good.<br /> <br /> All in all if you burn a huge amount of resources on this, and you pass the test, and you're facing problems where a huge mob of spirits can fix them... great! But I would argue that there are Level 4 Gifts which do the same thing but better. There is really nothing stopping you from spending the same Gnosis to summon 5 Fire Elementals! Though that would require five rolls.<br /> <br /> There's also a question of whether you'd be better off not learning this Gift at all, and simply using a Rite to summon the Huntsman, who automatically comes with 9, or a multiple of 9, hounds! I'm gonna call this a wash and say Amber with an asterisk.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Fog on the Moor:</b></u></span> This Gift lets you take on an intangible, ghostly form which can pass through anything but silver. Silver attacks can harm you, but nothing else can. You can't regenerate in this form, and you can pass through objects. Spend a Willpower, roll Gnosis at difficulty 7 (I would rewrite it as local Gauntlet, but sure), and instantly assume a ghostly form. You stay in this form for one one turn per success.<br /> <br /> It only costs a point of Willpower, and the roll to do it is doable. A break of a few turns in which you can flee, drop to a lower floor, run through walls, shout the evidence you want to give without being dragged away, is great. But the result being measured in turns is bad. Children of Gaia get a 'nobody can hit me' Gift at Rank 4, for example. The possibility that you will fail the roll is a little worrying at difficulty 7, not super likely, but a reasonable thing to worry about.<br /> <br /> This is a great option for infiltration, for assassinations, for thieving, for surviving insane explosive gambits, for escaping a huge attack... but like many Gifts it would be far more fitting to its Rank if you could apply it to your Pack for some extra cost. I think this one provides a strong enough effect that it deserves Green, after all it isn't that much more expensive than a Level 4 Gift to which it is equivalent, but it's still a little lean for its level.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Gift of the Spriggan:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis and roll Stamina + Primal-Urge, difficulty 8. On a success you shrink to the size of a small puppy or increase your size up to 3x. If you shrink your stats stay the same, including the hilarious potential for being a puppy-sized Crinos. If you grow you gain +3 Strength (or 'strength dice', which I think means the Attribute goes up, but they could just mean +3 dice to rolls based on Strength) for doubling in size, and +6 Strength for tripling in size.<br /> <br /> If your Crinos Strength is 9, increasing it to 15 is pretty great! You become a huge target, but presumably if you're going for this option you're happy with that. Anyways, with a Strength of 15 you can 'hurl a pickup truck', step through walls, destroy buildings with relative ease, and so on. This is the mother of all Veil breaches, but I like it. Lifting 6000lbs (nearly 3000kg) ain't bad either.<br /> <br /> This one is fun and useful enough to get Green, though you do run the risk of drawing the attention of the most powerful spirit in the scene and getting hit with a Blast which just outright kills you. The biggest Werewolf in the fight draws the most attention. Arguably the tiny option, for stealth and surprise suplexes, is far better.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T02:47:50-08:00","id":1548938,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 &amp; 6 Get of Fenris Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Endurance of Heimdall:</b></u></span> Spend 2 Gnosis, roll Willpower (difficulty 6), and if you're successful you double your Stamina for the scene. In Crinos with high base Stamina this could be huge, and there's no reason it wouldn't stack with other Stamina boosts. Part of the problem with Werewolf enemies is that you start running into foes who deal 15 dice of Aggravated damage, meaning that on average having 8 dice of soak against Aggravated still leaves you with an attack which could kill you in one, and so dodging becomes your main option. But if you have a Soak of 16 instead of 8, now you're looking at a much better average outcome, possibly no damage at all!<br /> <br /> Not super inventive or exciting, but very good. You could easily just delete the Willpower roll.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Horde of Valhalla*:</b></u></span> Fenris' Wolves are mechanically identical to the Hounds we see above. And those aren't THAT great. But you spend as much Gnosis and Rage as you like, say 10 points, and you make a difficulty 6 roll, and if you pass then you get a number of wolves equal to what you spent. So perhaps 10+ mighty wolves of Fenris manifesting. The fact that you risk wasting a HUGE amount of Gnosis and Rage annoys me, and the summons just... aren't that great! But there absolutely are battles where overwhelming your enemy with a crowd of summons will massively swing things in your favour, and there's nothing stopping AOE buffs from applying to these spirits.<br /> <br /> The fact that you could have 10 temporary Rage when you use this, and spend 1 Gnosis on it, to get 11 hounds, and then recover your Rage and go again... means I have to give this Green. But just remember that the summoned spirits aren't THAT great, their strength is their numbers, and their ability to track foes when you need to. WoD doesn't really have rules for being forced to engage certain enemies, so whether swarming your foe with weaker threats will make a difference is down to the ST. In theatre of the mind it's very easy to hear descriptions of a NPC just 'finding their way' to where they need to go, since without the strictures of a battle map there isn't anything blocking their concrete, defined list of possible paths. So, buyer beware.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Fenris' Bite:</b></u></span> Strength + Primal-Urge, difficulty of the target's Stamina +3, and on a success if your next bite attack hits it inflicts 3 automatic, unsoakable levels of Aggravated damage. This is roughly equivalent to adding +6 to your Strength or your damage dice pool. This also mangles one of their limbs! Great. If you get 5 successes on the initial test to activate the Gift it severs the limb instead.<br /> <br /> A limb mangled with this effect is useless until healed; if you hit a Garou with this twice they are completely out of claw and melee options and will be stuck trying to claw you with their hind legs or biting you. Hit anything running on four legs with this and you cripple their movement, and a humanoid creature will be very slow with one leg mangled.<br /> <br /> Great, multi-use, and it's boosting your damage output significantly if you get it to work. But a difficulty of Stamina +3 does mean that this is going to be most useful against enemies you could kill in one hit anyways. Think about it. A BSD in Crinos will have a Stamina of 4-8. That's a difficulty of 7+, immediately. If you ambush a target in Homid this Gift could be great, but a powerful Garou enemy who is in open battle with you will be nearly impossible to use this Gift on, as a base Stamina of 3, +3 from Crinos, +3 from this Gift, gives a difficulty of 9 already. Even with your Strength boosted by Crinos this Gift isn't likely to go off.<br /> <br /> So you become absolutely peerless at ripping limbs off of... weak enemies. Hmm. And by spending that Rage, you're doing that instead of a whole bonus attack. This Gift is far from useless, don't get me wrong, and if you face Spirits with a really low Willpower it's great, but it's... sadly not quite there. A fixed difficulty would be much better.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Call Great Fenris*:</b></u></span> This is excellent. You make a difficulty 6 roll (Stamina + Occult, easy), spend a single point of Gnosis, and Great Fenris shows up to fight for you. His stats are absolutely superb and he debuffs every spirit opponent he faces by 2 dice. His to-hit is 15, his damage is 22 base, and with a Rage of 20 he has strength far beyond any individual Garou. This thing could probably crack right through a thick steel wall.<br /> <br /> There is a problem however. If he isn't happy with what you've summoned him for, he eats you. Fine. But worse, even if he's happy, he takes payment in the form of one of your hands before leaving. This is where things get weird.<br /> <br /> Can you heal the battle scar? Mother's Touch allows that, but it feels disrespectful to the Totem. Can you replace your hand with a cybernetic techno-Fetish? Well that will depend on your allies. Missing one of your hands is huge, especially for a Lupus, since now you're hobbling around on 3 limbs instead of 4. On the assumption that you have some good way of handling the loss, whether it's healing or replacing the limb, I'll mark this down as a Green, but it definitely needs an asterisk.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-04-2025, 05:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T03:03:13-08:00","id":1548939,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 Glass Walker Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Chaos Mechanics:</b></u></span> This Gift lets you passively use Rage and Gnosis in the same turn with no penalties. So, so many Gifts become better with this Gift. So many Gifts are bad purely because spending Gnosis means you can't spend Rage in the same turn. Now you are no longer held back by that restriction! It's an amazing boost to your abilities. For just one example, with this Gift you could use Signal Rider to instantly arrive at someone's location using their mobile phone, and then take a bunch of Rage bonus actions at the end of the round to kill everyone in the room.<br /> <br /> The utility of Fetish weapons also increases massively with this ability. A Fetish which requires a Gnosis roll to activate its special effect is balanced mostly by the fact that you can't swing it a bunch of times a turn using Rage. Not any more! Arguably the best Gift in all of WTA, though it would be competing with things like Sense Wyrm and Mother's Touch.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Summon Net-Spider:</b></u></span> Once you've used this Gift, even once, your difficulties for checks relating to building, using and hacking computers is halved. So a difficulty 10 test would become difficulty 5.<br /> <br /> Anyways, for this Gift you spend a Gnosis and roll Charisma + Computer (difficulty 8), a Net-Spider appears, and it follows your instructions. Their powers are described as pretty immense, disrupting or destroying almost any system. An obvious Green, easy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T03:28:11-08:00","id":1548941,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 &amp; 6 Red Talon Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Curse of Lycaon:</b></u></span> This Gift can force a Garou into Lupus form, or turn any human or animal into a wolf permanently. You simply spend a single Gnosis and roll against a difficulty of the target's Willpower.<br /> <br /> Someday I'm going to play a VTM PC who got hit with this power and is masquerading as the Wolf Ghoul of another PC. Imagine realizing that this actual factual wolf is Kindred/Cainite and thinking that someone embraced a wolf somehow. But, do note that the Gift says &quot;normal&quot; wolf.<br /> <br /> The utility of this Gift is insane. It feels like a Level 6 Gift. Many many foes the Garou face simply aren't a threat if they become a wolf. Sure, they'll try to bite and claw at you, but that might be much less dangerous to Gaia than their stock portfolio and investment strategy was. Given that wolves are almost extinct in many places, threatened in many others, an ability which lets you repopulate wolves by glaring ominously at foxes is pretty great!<br /> <br /> There are a million stories you can tell with this Gift, and it feels like it should be mentioned more in the stories of the setting! If ANY Red Talon Elder can use this Gift, I just can't see how they're worried about wolves dying out. Kinfolk? Absolutely. But wolves? It also feels like this Gift could be very useful for any Tribe who wants to fortify the Lupus side of their bloodline. A human Kinfolk could be turned into a wolf, and presumably they'd remain Kinfolk, though the Gift does say &quot;normal&quot; of course. Ethics aside this could massively bolster the likelihood of the Tribe producing Pure Breed Lupus, or at least give them more wolves to work with.<br /> <br /> Hell, a Red Talon who fell in love with a human could petition an Elder to turn them into a wolf. The potential for drama is great!<br /> <br /> <u><b>Gaia's Vengeance:</b></u> They didn't bother writing this one. Unrated.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Scabwalker Curse:</b></u></span> Snarl at a target within a short range, spend a Gnosis, roll Manipulation + Survival (difficulty 7). For one day per success the target will take a level of Bashing damage for every turn they are in contact with a synthetic or artificial material. This Gift is absolutely a death sentence against humans and some supernaturals, but the damage can be soaked, so with a Stamina of 2 the target will be taking 1 level of Bashing every other turn on average, so it may take them 14ish turns to die. Given that the duration is measured in days, 14 turns is nothing, but it's a long time to take to kill a single human.<br /> <br /> This Gift could absolutely be a huge bother to a Vampire, Mage, Changeling and so on. They'll have more options to deal with it in terms of better soak, access to healing, and places to escape to.<br /> <br /> The thing is that at Rank 5 I'm not particularly excited about a new way to be really, REALLY mean to someone who isn't a threat to me already. Yeah you can use this on a real threat, like a powerful Fomori or Garou, but is this actually going to kill them? Really? Anything with healing or regen will be fine, anything capable of escaping into the Umbra will be fine, and so on. Against a real threat this power is just an annoyance, to the table as a whole. &quot;Oh yeah, remember he has to roll to soak- oh he soaked it again, fine.&quot;<br /> <br /> I would rebuild this Gift so that the allergic reaction to Weaver tools manifests as dice and/or difficulty penalties, which are going to be more relevant than this weird normal-person-killing ability. Yes, this power does let you kill someone in a way which can never be proven, but if you're a Rank 5 Garou you aren't desperately looking for ways to get away with murdering someone, any Rank 3 Ragabash worth their salt can kill someone and make it look like an accident.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Shield of Gaia*:</b></u> Choose something within the realm of the Weaver, and become fully immune to it. The examples given are bullets, photography, electricity, chemical toxins. This is a perfectly well written Gift, but very hard to rate. Chances are you'll pick something being used by a major antagonist, or by the faction you are hunting down and destroying. This Gift could be the source of a big legend about a Rank 6 Red Talon, so it's an exercise in creativity and shared Storytelling.<br /> <br /> This stays unrated with an asterisk, but that's simply because it is in fact 100s of different Gifts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-04-2025, 06:36 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T03:51:07-08:00","id":1548943,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 Shadow Lord Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <u><b>Obedience*:</b></u> Spend a Gnosis and roll Charisma + Leadership. Everyone present rolls Willpower. Both rolls are difficulty 8. If you win by 1 success, everyone in the vicinity has to follow orders which don't inconvenience them. If you win by 3 successes, everyone treats you loyally as if you were their Alpha. If you win by 5 success they'll do virtually anything including things which are practically suicidal. The effects last for a full day.<br /> <br /> Now, imagine you stack this Gift. Pure Breed. Merits which reduce the difficulty. A fitting Leadership specialty. You've buffed up your Charisma with Beyond Human. Ancestors setup. You might be rolling 15+ dice at a difficulty of 5-6 against a crowd of allies and enemies who are rolling 4-8 dice at a difficulty of 8.<br /> <br /> Basically, if you maximize the potential of this Gift, getting 5+ successes is very believable. The potential to get everyone in a huge battle to obey all your orders is hilarious but also does, sadly, break the story. At its best this Gift is so good that it's bad, annoyingly, and there is nothing stopping it from applying to outright lethal enemies. I would edit it to say that those who are full-out enemies of the user of the Gift can spend a single point of Willpower to ignore the effects, which makes the Gift become much more about inspiring obedience in your Sept, or among rivals.<br /> <br /> This is so swingy and dependent on your setup, Merits, Backgrounds, and a bit too underwritten, so I'm leaving it unrated with an asterisk. If it were ran RAW it would be Green, with the right setup, but potentially straying into &quot;too good, so it's bad&quot; territory.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Shadow Pack:</b></u></span> This Gift lets you summon a small army of shadows, which is a bit anime, though I'm not an expert. You roll Gnosis, difficulty 8, only needing one success. For every point of Gnosis you spend, you create a shadowy duplicate of yourself. They have your Attributes and Abilities but can't use Gifts, Fetishes, Gnosis, Willpower or Rage. They have one health level.<br /> <br /> Let's imagine you're a beast of an Ahroun. 5 in all physicals. You pour 5 Gnosis into this, summoning 5 duplicates. You do so in Crinos, so they have Strength 9, Dexterity 6, Stamina 8. They have Aggravated damage claws and fangs. They have a dodge pool of 6 + Athletics, and a Soak of 8, which will stop random weak attacks from banishing them.<br /> <br /> Best case scenario, they all stay alive, and you have an additional 5 attacks each round. That's pretty great! One of you could keep a foe grappled while the other 5 go crazy attacking as fast as they can. The difficulty of 8 on the Gnosis roll is very annoying, however, so I worry a little about wasting all that Gnosis! With a Gnosis of 5 your odds of succeeding are only 60%, so 40% of the time you'll waste all of the Gnosis you're trying to spend. Even with a Gnosis of 10 your odds are below 80%.<br /> <br /> Regardless, the duplicates last for a scene. I really struggle to rate this one because the difficulty of 8 means it simply isn't a safe place to pour so much Gnosis into. The difference between difficulty 6 and 8 is huge, the gap is far bigger than the devs ever seem to realize! And even if you summon 5+ shadows, a single point of damage hitting the whole room bursts them all, which is very achievable for high level threats.<br /> <br /> I must, with great sadness, give this an Amber.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T07:26:02-08:00","id":1548949,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 Silent Strider Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Gate of the Moon*:</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis per 100 miles you need to travel, roll Intelligence + Alertness, difficulty determined by ST, and on a single success you instantly arrive.<br /> <br /> While you can't bring your allies with you, I like this enough that it redeems the Silent Strider list. It also makes those really DUMB run for hours Gifts entirely pointless. This is just a huge upgrade on those. With this Gift you can appear inside your Caern, yell that your pack needs urgent help at a certain location, then teleport back to join your allies. You can move from Caern to Caern with messages. You can appear in front of a BSD pack to give them a warning before disappearing to rejoin your pack. It's great.<br /> <br /> The only thing that should give us pause is that the Gift's cost adds up, even if you're jumping within 100 miles every time. If your Chronicle has very poor access to Gnosis recovery, then the * may drop this down to Amber, since the travel becomes much less convenient to use.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Reach the Umbra:</b></u></span> If I were rating these on names, this would be a weak one. Along with all of the &quot;X of Gaia&quot; ones.<br /> <br /> You can instantly step sideways with no roll. If you need to roll for some reason, presumably as part of a Gift or Rite, or leading a pack across the Gauntlet, you get a -2 difficulty. You annoyingly can't spend Rage on the same turn you step sideways using this Gift, and you also can't use this ability to dodge. That said, being able to do this is amazing. You can simply blink between the two layers of reality at will, which is a huge QOL upgrade, and exactly what I want Gifts to do/be. Stepping Sideways is core to Garou, this improves on that, which is great.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-04-2025, 06:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T07:39:27-08:00","id":1548951,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 &amp; 6 Silver Fang Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Luna's Avenger:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis and concentrate for a full turn, your whole body turns into silver. This grants a few benefits:<br /> <br /> - +2 Stamina<br /> - +1 health level<br /> - all of your attacks are treated as being silver<br /> - you suffer none of the negative effects of silver<br /> <br /> Given that this costs a single point of Gnosis and there's no roll, and that it lasts for a whole scene... this is pretty great. It's especially good against Garou and hunters of Garou, and against silver-vulnerable Fera. But in all circumstances the +2 Stamina and additional health level are pretty great.<br /> <br /> Socially speaking, this Gift is a bit of a crowning ability for a Garou to have. For any Garou, to see a so-called King turn into a being of living silver would be terrifying and awe-inspiring; you can understand why many would believe that such a creature was a born ruler, especially with 4-5 points of Pure Breed backing it up.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Paws of the Newborn Cub:</b></u></span> Metis stay winning! This Gift turns you into your natural form with no special Gifts or supernatural tricks. The duration is one turn per success, on a Gnosis roll, difficulty of the target's Willpower. So say a Rank 5 Silver Fang were fighting my Rank 1 Metis Child of Gaia. They spend 2 Gnosis, a big expense, roll Gnosis at a difficulty of 8, and then say they get 1 success. He's stuck with no Gifts, no regeneration, for one turn, and that's... pretty much it. No stepping sideways, probably no Fetish uses, and his claws might deal Lethal instead of Aggravated, but they might stay Lethal. Same question with bites. When in reality, chances are it would be far better to just tear him apart with Rage actions.<br /> <br /> All things considered this Gift isn't... great. The cost is extreme, and it's Gnosis. Two points. You roll Gnosis, always a bad pool compared to other options, and the difficulty is WP. WP is high in enemies worth disabling. And a duration of one turn per success really sucks.<br /> <br /> If this cost a single point of Gnosis and on success the target lost all their supernatural abilities and tricks for three turns per success, now we'd be talking! But all things considered this is very meh. Yes, you could stop a 4th Generation Vampire from using their terrible powers, but if you're face to face with them you'd be better off just clawing them to death. That said, perhaps their Dominate or Presence stops that. I get it.<br /> <br /> Amber, simply because in extreme circumstances it would be hugely beneficial. But it isn't a GREAT Gift.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Renew the Cycle:</b></u></span> You know how Paws of the Newborn might be useful simply because you might find yourself fighting an ancient vampire you otherwise couldn't harm?<br /> <br /> Well, that. This Gift is bad. Say you're dealing with a 1000 year old Vampire, which is certainly powerful enough to be a threat to Elder Garou. Why waste 10 Gnosis and a difficulty 8 Gnosis vs Willpower on attempting to turn him to dust when you could just make him helpless with Paws.<br /> <br /> This is extremely niche for a Level 6 Gift, which is fine, but it should be EXCEPTIONAL within that niche. The time scaling is also off. The Vampires who will be the biggest threats to Garou will be too old for this Gift to touch. More to the point, why isn't this a Silent Strider Gift? If you ever happened to be playing a Rank 6 Silver Fang, you'd probably be better off learning a Level 6 Gift for your Auspice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T09:38:58-08:00","id":1548953,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1548922#post1548922\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><u><b>Rank 5 Bone Gnawer Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Riot:</b></u></span> Immediately I find it interesting that the spirits provoking a riot are 'malevolent'. It's a relatively regressive framing off the bat and makes this feel more like a BSD Gift. Regardless, the riot which forms goes after a target of your choice. However, it tends to spiral out of control and have unexpected results, which makes sense for a riot.<br /> <br /> Wits + Subterfuge, difficulty 8, with a riot radius of one mile per success. Cost of 1 Gnosis, naturally.<br /> <br /> You could say this about a lot of Gifts, but I would argue that this is a very good example of something which is better to do organically. Using Kinfolk, using community leaders, using a variety of Gifts to reach a larger goal, invoking spirits. This Gift isn't terrible, it can fulfil a lot of different needs (distraction, pinning down a business etc.) but I would argue that it very unwisely puts the &quot;protectorate&quot; of the Bone Gnawers at immense risk.<br /> <br /> When you start a riot with supernatural mind control, against a target of your choice, you put the lives of many marginalized people into the hands of fate, and more directly, the cops. Communities which are disproportionately likely to contain your Kinfolk, and your future Tribe members in rare cases. Like many Level 5 Gifts this feels like an ability which a sensible Elder will almost never use since it takes a nuke to the problem.<br /> <br /> All in all I'd call this a solid Amber, it isn't bad by any means, and it could have huge positive effects, but the inherent risks are also terrible.</div> </div> </div> </div> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=buvSIrFi0Hw\" data-code=\"buvSIrFi0Hw\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/buvSIrFi0Hw/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <u><b>The Living Wood:</b></u> And we crash right down to earth. Spend a Gnosis, do a difficulty 8 roll, and for every success you bring a tree to life, wandering around like an ent. What are the stats? We don't know. We aren't even told if they're meant to be powerful allies or not. This is impossible to rate since we have no metric for what these trees should be like.<br /> <br /> Notably there isn't even a time limit. RAW this Gift may well animate a tree permanently, which is very funny to me. </div> </div> </div>There are some concrete mechanics in WoD Combat of all places, but they're kind of underwhelming. Maybe there's something to be said about the ability of most of what gets animated to stake vampires readily, but this is mostly going to be a way to divide an enemy's focus in a fight, though that seems unlikely given that they're typically going to be frightened to death or possibly action by anyone in Crinos.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T10:11:43-08:00","id":1548954,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 Stargazer Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Circular Attack:</b></u></span> By spending a WP and rolling Wits + Athletics, difficulty of the highest Wits of your opponents +3 (e.g. if there is a Wits 5 enemy, difficulty 8), you can dodge or redirect a number of attacks equal to your successes this turn. On the turn you use this Gift you can't spend Rage for bonus actions.<br /> <br /> This is a pretty neat Gift for use against physical enemies; a difficulty of 8 is achievable, and redirecting a swing of a Grand Klaive to hit a different enemy is great. At this level of play you'll be facing enemies who could theoretically one-shot you, so getting them to hit their allies instead is great.<br /> <br /> That said, this Gift is terrible against any Spirits with decent Gnosis. Gnosis = Wits, so a Gnosis of 7 makes this Gift difficulty 10 to use. If I were rewriting it I would specify that against spiritual enemies you half their Gnosis, rounding up, then add +3 (e.g. Gnosis 10 becomes Difficulty 8). Basically if the pack attacking you has a single high-Gnosis spirit helping them, this Gift doesn't work as intended.<br /> <br /> All in all I'm happy to call it Amber; it's amazing in the situations where it works, but a passive ability where you can redirect attacks which you dodge would be much much better.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Harmonious Unity of the Emerald Mother:</b></u></span> Lupus get ignored once again. This Gift makes it so that you get all of the benefits of Crinos in Homid. Not in Glabro, not in Lupus, just Homid. This Gift lasts for one day and costs a point of Gnosis. How good is having regeneration in Homid? Well, Metis and Lupus get it for free as Rank 0 Cubs/Ronin. How good is having +4 Strength, +1 Dexterity and +3 Stamina in Homid? Vampires can do this at baseline by spending Vitae for Blood Buff, though it costs them a lot of course. It's neat, but is it THAT good? It's helpful when you're stuck in Homid, sure. And if you go Glabro the benefits vanish, you get weaker for some reason.<br /> <br /> Sadly Red, but easily rewritten to be Green. Make it permanent, passive, and apply in all forms. Hell, expand it to include the senses of Lupus, so that you have the best of all your forms in every form. Just go &quot;You have Strength +4, Dexterity +2 and Stamina +3 in all forms, Regeneration in all forms, you can soak Aggravated damage in all forms, and you subtract 2 from the difficulties of Perception checks in all forms.&quot; Very strong, but it speaks to the Stargazer wisdom of the divisions between the forms being illusory.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Wisdom of the Seer*:</b></u> I'll be honest, I hate this kind of ability. For starters, there are already Gifts, Rites and Backgrounds which pull this off. But more to the point, &quot;honest, yet vague and symbolic&quot; is going to vary wildly by your ST.<br /> <br /> Spend a Gnosis, roll Intelligence + Enigmas (difficulty 7), more successes means more clarity in the answer. I struggle to imagine how questions like &quot;are my pack alive?&quot; would go, since I don't know how to give an &quot;honest, yet vague and symbolic&quot; answer to that. The flower of the wolf yet blooms? To which the player goes &quot;... what?&quot; and I go &quot;yeah they're alive&quot;. It just comes off as pretentious!<br /> <br /> If it's information the ST wants you to have, it will come to you in dreams and messages from spirits. If it's information that will be useful but you don't need for the plot to work, this power could be helpful. The problem is that this ability is so vague, and arguably so redundant in a game full of spirit dreams and Rites and related Backgrounds and Merits and Gifts etc. Leaving it unrated, I imagine it would often be Red or Amber, but with an overly permissive ST it could be Green.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T10:24:01-08:00","id":1548955,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 Elder Brother Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Fabric of the Mind:</b></u></span> See the Galliard list. A mess of a Gift, but if you run it as it is written, it'll probably work out as Red.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Fetish Doll:</b></u></span> Spend a week building and enchanting a doll. This requires a Perception + Crafts (difficulty 8) roll. You also need something from your intended victim, or something belonging to them. You must then roll Intelligence + Medicine, difficulty equal to the target's Willpower. Every success inflicts a level of Aggravated damage, which is still soakable. After 10 successes the doll stops working.<br /> <br /> So, how good is this? Well, it's an absolutely guaranteed way to murder almost any mortal. Untraceable too. It will work on most supernaturals; even a Vampire of immense power might have no Fortitude, or a low value. Against Garou you run up against their soak, which will be impressive in Crinos, but there's every chance you could kill them in short order.<br /> <br /> What's neat is that even with an enemy with a Willpower of 10, you'll eventually inflict 10 levels of damage though if you deal them 1-2 points at a time it'll be much easier to soak,. Even if it takes a lot of time and effort and accidentally stabbing your thumb. Against my boy it would be a difficulty 8 roll, so 10 dice would average 2-3 successes each go. In Homid form he'd soak 2-3 on average with a Stamina of 5, and during the attack he'd likely shift into Crinos to better protect himself against this invisible attacker, at which point his Stamina would shoot up to 8, soaking 3-4 points on average.<br /> <br /> Basically this Gift is very neat, but you won't kill even a Rank 1 Garou with it, unless they have very bad Stamina, or they're already injured, or their soak is somehow compromised, or they're incapable of shifting into Crinos to protect themselves, and so on. So the best time to use this against a Garou is to play dirty. Injure a rival mid battle. Injure a rival mid challenge. Injure a rival at the moment one of their packmates punches them. Injure a rival while he's drinking something so he thinks someone has tried to poison him, or if he dies the blame will go to someone involved with the drink.<br /> <br /> I'm going to go ahead and give this a Green. It's a faff to set up, but the results are incredible, and they reward patience as well as forward planning. This ability is a great seed for plots, for example an Elder Brother promising a Kinfolk of the Fianna that she will win the heart of the man she loves, a young hero of her tribe, and that she could make her a good luck charm to help with it, if she had a piece of his hair, fur, perhaps a keepsake of his. With this Gift you can make people unwitting accomplices in murder, and I imagine a few Garou who helped to free Banes or steal Caerns in the Pure Lands probably wound up victims to this Gift in the end.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T10:36:16-08:00","id":1548956,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 Younger Brother Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Invoke the Spirits of the Storm:</b></u></span> This power seems to allow you to summon any weather effect relating to water and air, so fog and tornadoes, blizzards, sure, but you can't bring a heat wave for example. Spend a Gnosis, roll Willpower, difficulty is based by how severe the change is, so a sunny day turning to a terrible blizzard might be difficulty 9 or 10, depending on your ST. For every success the weather effect covers 10 miles. If you call for a thunderstorm you can deal damage by spending Gnosis, Wits + Occult to hit, dealing 10 dice of Aggravated damage.<br /> <br /> So, how good is a ranged attack of Wits + Occult which hits for 10 dice of Aggravated? It isn't bad at all, but using the Gift at all costs 1 Gnosis, and then the attack is 1 Gnosis a go. And you can change the weather with the help of an air elemental, which any Theurge or Elder Brother would be able to call upon.<br /> <br /> As for the general weather changes, you already have a pretty great weather-changing Gift, Chill of Early Frost, which hits all furless creatures with a nasty two dice debuff. All in all this is... well it's pretty mediocre. Perhaps you stand a lot to gain by killing huge numbers of people indiscriminately, as you would do with a surprise blizzard or tornado, but I can't see why unless you were serving the Wyrm, or extremely misled in your ways. I'll call this Red and suggest you just invoke the help of an Air Elemental if you really need school called off or the roads closed. At Rank 5 you will have Theurges subordinate to you, or a friendly relationship with at least one Elder Brother.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Heart of Ice:</b></u></span> So there are a lot of Aggravated damage mystical ranged attack Gifts in W20, but this one is sorta special since the damage is unsoakable. Now, the Blast Charm is better, sure, but not everyone is going to be summoning spiritual allies into battle, so considering this as a Gift it isn't bad at all.<br /> <br /> You spend a Gnosis, whisper the target's name into the wind (does there have to be wind?) and roll Wits + Occult against a target of the target's Willpower. For every success you inflict a level of unsoakable Aggravated damage, but the damage comes one turn at a time. So with 7 successes you'd inflict 7 Aggravated, over 7 turns, 1 per turn.<br /> <br /> What this means is that the power can be a death sentence, especially against someone who is already injured, but if they have a means of regenerating Aggravated damage (very rare) or healing it (less rare, but still rare) they might be fine.<br /> <br /> The power is perfectly fine. Enemies who are worth slowly freezing from the inside out will typically have pretty good Willpower however. Call it Amber; I wouldn't take it unless I was in a game with a bunch of failson Elders who have been elevated through circumstance and privilege and whose actual WP is shockingly low, at which point this Gift would be pretty great.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T10:52:30-08:00","id":1548957,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Skin Dancer Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Mostly irrelevant to PCs, but worth covering for completion.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>See Past The Skin (1):</b></u></span> Roll Gnosis, no cost, on a success you can recognize other Skin Dancers by looking at them. Extremely situational but handy for finding others of your kind.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Mask Taint (5):</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis, roll Perception + Subterfuge (difficulty 8), suppress all of your detectable Wyrm taint for one scene per success. Comes far too late. And what Skin Dancer hits Elder Rank anyways? The high difficulty also makes this pretty mediocre.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Boli Zousizhe Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Technically any Glass Walker could learn these Gifts, so they may be more relevant. The BZ are the Asian branch of the Glass Walkers, and apparently they are more traditional. I'm not enough of an expert to comment on execution of Asian content in WoD, so I'd welcome input from someone who knows what they're talking about.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Sheng-Nong's Eyes (1):</b></u></span> This Gift is a little confusing; it's intended to help you to work with two tools at once, including your own hands, granting you the focus for this task. Mechanically you spend a Gnosis to get a bonus action, either for the turn, or for the scene. Presumably for the turn. In addition, for the rest of the scene you can ignore visual and off-hand penalties, so you could strike a near-invisible enemy without penalties and manage twin blades.<br /> <br /> This one is... confusing. The wording needs much more clarity. Assuming it does the following:<br /> <br /> - On the turn you activate it you get an additional action<br /> - For the rest of the scene you ignore visual and offhand penalties relating to attacking<br /> <br /> Then it is... Amber? Let's say Amber.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Fu Xi's Honour (2):</b></u></span> If the ST agrees that a member of your character's family or pack is helpless to defend themselves, you may use this Gift. In this situation you may spend a single point of Rage, and add your Honour rating to your Physical Attributes for the duration of the Scene.<br /> <br /> This is busted beyond belief. +1 to Strength, Dexterity and Stamina for a Scene is great. +10, which is perfectly achievable in a long campaign even if your Rank is slow to rise to match your Honour, is one of the most powerful Gifts in the game. Green, but so great it's busted. My &quot;much honour upon you, in this honourable family, of honour&quot; senses are also tingling, but again, I'm not an expert on Asian folklore and perhaps this Gift is fine. It just feels like a bad parody, to me a random English dude, that the Gift where you get a buff from your Honour stat is the Asian Werewolf Gift.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Yao's Commands (3):</b></u> See Elemental Favour. The Gift isn't playable in its written form.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Yu's Endurance (4):</b></u></span> See Heart of the Mountain in the Get of Fenris list. Simply adding this to the Glass Walker list is... interesting. Even if you have to go on a trip to Asia to justify it for some reason.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Huang Di's Sacrifice (5)*:</b></u></span> Spend 2 Gnosis and roll Stamina + Leadership (difficulty 8). For every success a packmate can heal (successes) damage, including Aggravated. So for example if you lucked out and get 5 successes, your 5 packmates could each heal 5 damage, but with 2 successes you could have 2 packmates heal for 2 damage. If you stack this with difficulty reductions, Crinos Stamina boost, or hell if for some reason you have +10 to your Stamina from being <i>very honourable </i>then this Gift is much better.<br /> <br /> On the assumption you stack the roll I'll call this Green, with an asterisk.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T12:23:18-08:00","id":1548963,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Kucha Ekundu Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Similar disclaimer; my knowledge of African folklore is limited. These Gifts are learnt by the Kucha Ekundu, who are an offshoot of the Red Talons, and whose animal Kinfolk are the African Hunting Dogs. RAW playing a KE is mechanically directly superior to playing a RT, and realistically you're going to have fewer people going &quot;oh a Red Talon, I know what THOSE are like&quot;, so I consider it a win-win. In character you'll have people going &quot;... what? You're dogs?&quot;. African Hunting Dogs are misnamed, they're not very closely related to either dogs or wolves, so the interbreeding is Spirit Magic of course.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Speed of Thought (1):</b></u></span> See Silent Strider list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Feed the Pack (2):</b></u></span> This is interesting. This Gift is better, in my opinion, than the Bone Gnawer, Red Talon and Lupus Gifts with similar deals. Instead of a Gift which helps you find food, this is a Gift which allows you to eat on behalf of others while they sit at home in safety. It's still very niche and IMHO more suited to a Level 1 Gift, but it's a step up from &quot;you can find lots of food&quot;. Before you eat something you can roll your Gnosis, difficulty 6 for packmates, difficulty 7 for kinfolk, and on a success you can grant the nourishment of what you're consuming to one other person.<br /> <br /> Now while this Gift is niche, I do think that the ability to keep your packmate who got stuck in The Abyss or Malfeas or who is running non-stop to deliver a message is... handy. It's good enough that I can convince myself that it's Amber, partly for inventiveness. If you have a Ragabash packmate they can always go on hunger strike for a whole month to impress people with their fortitude, or set a formal Challenge of not having any food or drink pass their lips, which this Gift doesn't break. Ultimately there are enough uses you can find for this Gift that I'll give it a pass.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Predator's Many Eyes (3)*:</b></u></span> This Gift is like Territory, but instead of urinating to mark a territory, you mark an animal (Fera, and presumably Garou, are valid targets, with their consent) simply by looking at them. Charisma + Animal Ken, difficulty 7 is used, and the range is sight, which is pretty great. Thereafter you can see through the target's eyes.<br /> <br /> IMO this Gift is best used in two ways:<br /> <br /> - you can currently see an animal, right now, and you want to get its POV, for example you see a bird flying around and you want its bird's eye view; you might return to using the POV later, but your main intent with using the Gift is to check things out from their angle now<br /> - applying it to several shapechangers, with their consent, so that you can flip through their POVs (i.e. you can see through the eyes of the other PCs, or through members of a few packs)<br /> <br /> Territory letting you simply cast your senses to a location is sometimes superior since with this Gift you might be stuck looking at something boring, or the animal could be asleep! The limitation to predators only is also possibly a bit restrictive. So this Gift is possibly worse at building a network of places you can check like supernatural CCTV. With that in mind there's a decent argument for getting both Predator's Many Eyes and Territory, since Kucha Ekundu technically get both in their Tribal List.<br /> <br /> * Do keep in mind that while this Gift is, in my opinion, pretty clear if you read it carefully, there are also contradictions between its text and the text of Territory. I would argue that these contradictions aren't a problem since the Gift says &quot;in all other respects, as Territory&quot;, so if it contradicts Territory, the contradiction is an explicit exception to the way that Gift typically works. That said the Gift could be written much more clearly.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Clenched Jaw (4):</b></u></span> See the Ahroun list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Crocodile Pact (5)*:</b></u></span> This is a &quot;call for powerful help&quot; Gift, but a particularly cool and flavourful one with good lore. You spend 2 Gnosis, and you roll Charisma + Rituals (difficulty 7). With even one success, you get help within the hour. This could be Mokole from a local Clutch, or powerful Mokole Ancestor Spirits, or some other boon from the Umbra. For me this Gift has the edge because you only need one success, and the result you get is somewhat tailored to the problem. Rampaging Werecrocodiles for battles, ancestral wisdom for problems. There's also no side effect of &quot;if the problem isn't big enough you get devoured by a giant crocodile&quot;, which is nice.<br /> <br /> The * reflects that this Gift's utility will be somewhat hampered if there are no Mokole within an hour's journey of you, since the possible results of this Gift become much more limited.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-05-2025, 04:53 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T12:49:31-08:00","id":1548964,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Hakken Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> If you're a Japanese Shadow Lord you add these to your list. Again, there is no cost to doing this, beyond the buy-in of your character being from Japan and people perhaps mistrusting them as a foreigner to the Garou Nation.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Dream of a Thousand Cranes (1):</b></u></span> You have to fold an origami crane to activate this Gift of good fortune, which takes a turn. When you do so, spend a Gnosis and roll Charisma + Occult, difficulty 6. Every success you get translates to a single die which you can add to other checks in the same scene.<br /> <br /> Immediately this makes you a far better ritualist, crafter, and in fact anything where you roll for a project. In a PBP game, or a game with Downtime, this Gift would be a nightmare to balance. Yeah there's that one Philodox from the Hakken whose katana Fetishes are incredible, yeah his planning and crafting and Ritual rolls to make the sword and turn it into a Fetish get loads of bonus dice from folding cranes-<br /> <br /> This Gift is great, but I wouldn't suggest using it in your games.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Fair Path (1)*:</b></u></span> This Gift lets you wash yourself from battle by touching clean water, and on a successful activation with no cost you add +1 to your Appearance. You can think of this as a free (3xp) way to boost your Appearance by 1, with an activation. If your Appearance is 4, this is much cheaper than buying Appearance 5. Is it worth it? Well does Appearance matter in your games?<br /> <br /> If you have a Gift or Ritual where you roll Appearance, or you're dealing with Garou/Supernaturals/Spirits where beauty and fashion are important... who knows. Let's call this Amber with a *, assuming that Appearance is at least somewhat useful (which is the RAW!) and you care enough about Appearance to pay 3xp to boost it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Storm Winds Slash (2)*:</b></u></span> Feels extremely cliche, but I like this, much like I enjoy the Gift which lets you stab a target's shadow. This Gift lets you use a melee weapon at great range by spending a Gnosis and rolling Dexterity + Melee difficulty 7. Notably some weapons are difficulty 7 anyways, like a massive greatsword, so this Gift is best with such heavy weapons. The Gift specifies that targets 'take lethal damage as normal for a melee strike' but doesn't make it clear whether you can inflict Aggravated damage if the weapon is a Fetish or similar.<br /> <br /> Targets can dodge as normal.<br /> <br /> Assuming you always deal an ordinary Lethal weapon strike, with no special 'rider' effects, this is a perfectly fine Amber. If we assume the Gift lets you strike at a distance with a Fetish weapon like a Klaive or Grand Klaive, it's Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Dark of Night (3):</b></u></span> By spilling a vial of ink you can blind an enemy. Spend a Gnosis, roll Manipulation + Subterfuge (difficulty 6), resisted by the target's Gnosis if they have it (difficulty 6). Every success blinds the target for a whole hour.<br /> <br /> Multiple hours of blindness. This Gift is incredible, even with a Gnosis cost and at level 3. You can gradually blind a whole pack of enemies. The effect is very safe, it isn't a claw strike to the eyes, it isn't acid, it's like mystical ink inside the eyes based on the flavour of the power, so blinding a spirit or Garou or mortal with this power won't cause permanent damage or any long term offense (unless they hold a grudge).<br /> <br /> If you were planning to use this Gift, I'd suggest that the target should be blinded for two turns per success.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Living Treasure (4):</b></u></span> This Gift can convince any object's spirit, whether it's a Fetish or something weirder or an ordinary object, to answer your questions.<br /> <br /> Touch an object, roll Manipulation + Etiquette (difficulty 7), each success gets you a single answer. You can only use this Gift once per object, ever! No cost, however. This Gift is incredible for discovering lost lore of Fetishes and things like Faerie relics. If you were stood at the standing stones of some Garou sacred site you could ask them who put them there, for example, and they might answer 'the fae folk' or 'wolf-men with hair of red'. Pretty amazing for detective work.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Divine Wind (5):</b></u></span> This Gift lets you summon a storm. There's too many of these.<br /> <br /> It costs Rage only, which is nice, and you can spend a load of additional Rage to help boost the storm. Stamina + Enigmas (difficulty 7), every success and every point of Rage gives a radius of 1.6km. The storm lasts no more than a scene and is strong enough to uproot trees and throw cars around.<br /> <br /> This, to be honest, sounds like it will damage wildlife and probably do more harm than good. But it might help you in an emergency, especially in a city, though wild destruction in a city is bad for nature too due to the environmental damage (spills, resources used up for rebuilding etc.).<br /> <br /> I'm gonna call this Amber simply because it doesn't have any 'rider' effect which would help make it useful.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T13:31:13-08:00","id":1548965,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Siberakh Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> This list has no new Gifts, and is constructed interestingly. The Siberakh have every Silver Fang Gift except for those taught by Falcon and his servants, and every Younger Brother Gift except for those taught by Great Wendigo and his servants.<br /> <br /> Here's a list with that in mind:<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Inspiration</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Lambent Flame</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Sense Wyrm</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Hand Blade</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Luna's Armour</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Sense Silver</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Unity of the Pack</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Burning Blade</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Silver Claws</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Wrath of Gaia</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Sidestep Death</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Luna's Avenger</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Renew the Cycle</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Call the Breeze</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Camouflage</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Ice Echo</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Resist Pain</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Claws of Frozen Death</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Salmon Swim</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Speak with Wind Spirits</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>True Fear</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Blood of the North</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Wisdom of the Ancient Ways</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Hero's Stand</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Scream of Gaia</b></span><br /> <br /> See the entries for details. Can you build a good list from this? Absolutely! Lambent Flame, Sense Wyrm, Ice Echo, Resist Pain, Luna's Armour, True Fear, Silver Claws... with this list you get two very, very good Ahroun Gifts (Silver Claws and True Fear).<br /> <br /> What you lose isn't worth that much. Arguably Siberakh could be the best list in W20, at least for a Garou, though it comes with the cost of being part Silver Fang (the worst European tribe) and part Younger Brother (the worst American tribe, though in a competition of two).<br /> <br /> Losing Empathy, Mastery and Mindblock kinda hurts, but most of the Talents you lose aren't that incredible. Similar deal with the Younger Brother Gifts.<br /> <br /> Edit: After reminding me of the Siberakh lore, I really like them. They're basically stuck in Siberia, so if you're playing one you're probably a weirdo or outcast, but they are Silver Fangs who rejected the inbreeding of their parent Tribe and inter-bred with the Younger Brother Tribe. They seem pretty cool but sadly mostly irrelevant. I do like that the main Tribe hates them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-04-2025, 05:19 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T14:23:38-08:00","id":1548966,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1548964#post1548964\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><u><b>Hakken Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> If you're a Japanese Shadow Lord you add these to your list. Again, there is no cost to doing this, beyond the buy-in of your character being from Japan and people perhaps mistrusting them as a foreigner to the Garou Nation.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Dream of a Thousand Cranes (1):</b></u></span> You have to fold an origami crane to activate this Gift of good fortune, which takes a turn. When you do so, spend a Gnosis and roll Charisma + Occult, difficulty 6. Every success you get translates to a single die which you can add to other checks in the same scene.<br /> <br /> Immediately this makes you a far better ritualist, crafter, and in fact anything where you roll for a project. In a PBP game, or a game with Downtime, this Gift would be a nightmare to balance. Yeah there's that one Philodox from the Hakken whose katana Fetishes are incredible, yeah his planning and crafting and Ritual rolls to make the sword and turn it into a Fetish get loads of bonus dice from folding cranes-<br /> <br /> This Gift is great, but I wouldn't suggest using it in your games.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Hakken explicitly never bind spirits into katana (or wakizashi, but those don't have quite the same... reputation of use in gaming circles). It's considered a violation of the principle of benevolence, as the ordinary mortal steel is used to dispatch mortal beings and mockeries as a kind of kindness and checking against excessive force, since Garou claws are substantially more likely to wreck someone than a katana.<br /> <br /> Of course, this social principle got really weird when it practically became the Beast Court's stance on all fetish weapons in Hammer &amp; Klaive. Which is rather awkward, because there were a number of fetish weapons in Hengeyokai.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Fair Path (1)*:</b></u></span> This Gift lets you wash yourself from battle by touching clean water, and on a successful activation with no cost you add +1 to your Appearance. You can think of this as a free (3xp) way to boost your Appearance by 1, with an activation. If your Appearance is 4, this is much cheaper than buying Appearance 5. Is it worth it? Well does Appearance matter in your games?<br /> <br /> If you have a Gift or Ritual where you roll Appearance, or you're dealing with Garou/Supernaturals/Spirits where beauty and fashion are important... who knows. Let's call this Amber with a *, assuming that Appearance is at least somewhat useful (which is the RAW!) and you care enough about Appearance to pay 3xp to boost it. </div> </div> </div>I think this may be less about the Appearance boost and more about not appearing in disarray around spirits that wouldn't like that. It also potentially gives you an alibi if you aren't spattered in evidence.<br /> <br /> RE: Fu Xi's Honor, Rage of the Nest Mother for Mokole actually uses the same system. So, hurray for the Warding in taking the importance of Honor international, I suppose?<br /> <br /> Honor as a Garou and Fera Renown category also has a few connotations that humans would find rather seedy. For example, while you get Honor from protecting the helpless, you also get it from making sure that normal humans don't learn too much, which means misdirecting investigations, destroying evidence, disappearing witnesses, and other general work in making sure that the Veil Shall Not Be Lifted. How ruthless any given changer gets at this is up to them, but I doubt that they have witness to otherworldly horror and gore protection programs, and not everyone has memory-tampering Gifts; a fair number of disappearances are probably going to involve quietly killing otherwise innocent folks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T14:31:09-08:00","id":1548967,"title":"","content":"Yeah the Changing Breeds concept of &quot;Wisdom&quot; or &quot;Honour&quot; is very alien to us. Learning a bunch of Rites which you violently stole from a dying tribe doesn't make you wise, forcing a bunch of people to marry you doesn't make you honourable, but there will be Garou who were congratulated and awarded Wisdom/Honour for these things.<br /> <br /> And thanks for the details about the Fetish weapon situation! The key point stands but it's good to know. It's still absolutely something a PC would want to do, and use the bonus from the Gift for, it's a really great &quot;downtime&quot; Gift.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T15:23:16-08:00","id":1548970,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Bunyip Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> The Bunyip Gifts are primarily here, I assume, for games set during their genocide, and also for Garou who are interested in Lost Tribes to try and recover their Gifts and Rites, and also for a possible Bunyip Recovery situation. I like that they put effort into them! Ultimately, the also show what was lost, even if you ignore the ethical atrocities. On a purely tactical level the Garou Nation really shot itself in the foot destroying a Tribe with Mother's Touch and a bunch of unique Gifts.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Bunyip's Spell (1):</b></u></span> A cost-free paralysis power where you roll Willpower, resisted by Willpower. On a success the target is frozen in place for one turn per success. If the target is an ordinary mortal you even get to prolong the duration for a whole scene by spending Willpower.<br /> <br /> A big downside is that this Gift doesn't work on undead or on shapeshifters, but it does work on Fomori, so long as they are 'mostly mortal'. Presumably it would work on Mages, Sorcerers and so on, but possibly not on Changelings who are technically 'shapeshifters' by default given their Fae Mien and the ability to go Wyrd.<br /> <br /> The best thing about this Gift is that a mortal who is stunned by this power will forget what happened to them. They'll remember being paralyzed but not anything else, so this is a great way to deal with Litany Breaches and mortals wandering where they shouldn't be. Amazing Gift with many uses.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Mother's Touch (1):</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Theurge entry. Wiping out a Tribe with a Gift like this is a huge own goal.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Resist Toxin (1):</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Bone Gnawer entry.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Sense Prey (1):</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Lupus entry.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Coils of the Serpent (2):</b></u></span> See the Rank 2 Elder Brother entry.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Crocodile's Cunning (2):</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis, breathe underwater for an hour, and gain +2 to stealth tests when underwater or submerged in mud.<br /> <br /> This... isn't great. This Gift could be 100% passive and still work great. I'd call it Amber.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Lonesome Voice of the Bunyip (3)*:</b></u></span> Spend one Gnosis. Roll a Charisma + Performance (difficulty 7). Everyone within earshot who isn't a Bunyip loses 1 temporary Willpower point per success and can't regain Willpower until they leave the area where the Gift was used.<br /> <br /> This Gift is a weapon of a Tribe at the brink of extinction, and it lets a single survivor scream at people coming to their home to leave them alone. This Gift is very powerful, but it has problems for a non-Bunyip to use. You, the user of the Gift, are not explicitly immune to the power. Bunyip are immune. If you aren't a Bunyip, you'll suffer the effects, RAW. Even if that isn't the case, all of your allies will get hit.<br /> <br /> This is a Green with a big asterisk. It's amazing in the intended use case (a Bunyip using it) but can be pretty bad otherwise.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Pulse of the Invisible (3):</b></u></span> See the Rank 3 Theurge entry.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Dance of the Lightning Snakes (4)*:</b></u></span> A Gift which can only be used in the Umbra is a great idea. A bunch of outlandish &quot;video gamey&quot; Gifts should be Umbra only. It would really help the setting feel more cohesive, like when you have a mobile shield of earth following you around, which is much easier to visualize in our world.<br /> <br /> Spend a Gnosis, roll Willpower, difficulty 5-9 (5 if a storm is already brewing in the Penumbral skies, 9 if there is no weather at all or the skies are clear). The difficulty is 1 lower in the native lands of the Bunyip, which is something I'd love to see more often in Gifts, not super often, but at Level 4+ I'd love to see more &quot;this Elder Brother Gift is at -1 difficulty in North America&quot; type Gifts.<br /> <br /> The storm gathers in 3 turns and covers an equivalent to 5 miles per success. The difficulty or Essence cost of fire, travel or perception Charms increases by 1. A sympathetic storm can also appear in the mortal world.<br /> <br /> While the storm exists the Garou can attack using the lightning snakes, Charisma + Occult, difficulty 7, 10 dice of Aggravated damage. A ranged attack spanning miles is pretty amazing!<br /> <br /> All in all... I'd call this Gift pretty cool. The Umbra-only restriction is annoying but it's pretty amazing for huge-scale Umbra battles. Assuming that you can use it at great range and that you are able to fight enemies in the Umbra, I'll call this Amber as you can strike from stealth (the attack comes from the sky directly above, I would imagine).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Quicksand (4):</b></u></span> See the Rank 4 Red Talon entry.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Billabong Bridge (5):</b></u></span> This Gift lets you enter any body of fresh water and exit any other body of fresh water, so long as you are familiar with your destination. With enough travel in your history you could teleport anywhere on earth with this Gift, though if the place you arrive is high Gauntlet you may need to step sideways. The cost is 2 Gnosis and there is usually no roll.<br /> <br /> This is pretty great; by the time you're an Elder you will know a lot of valid targets for this Gift. That said, as an Elder you probably have access to moon bridges. This is very neat but it's expensive in terms of Gnosis and very late in the game too. Definitely worth buying, could help you escape a lot of terrible situations, but the cost is bad.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Invoke the Spirits of the Storm (5):</b></u></span> See the Rank 5 Younger Brother entry.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-04-2025, 06:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T15:30:32-08:00","id":1548971,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1548949#post1548949\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Gate of the Moon:</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis per 100 miles you need to travel, roll Intelligence + Alertness, difficulty determined by ST, and on a single success you instantly arrive.<br /> <br /> While you can't bring your allies with you, I like this enough that it redeems the Silent Strider list. It also makes those really DUMB run for hours Gifts entirely pointless. This is just a huge upgrade on those. With this Gift you can appear inside your Caern, yell that your pack needs urgent help at a certain location, then teleport back to join your allies. You can move from Caern to Caern with messages. You can appear in front of a BSD pack to give them a warning before disappearing to rejoin your pack. It's great.</div> </div> </div> </div>This feels like it's massively downplaying the Gnosis costs of using this. Instant teleport is always going to be in Green territory if not too burdened, but 1 Gnosis per 100 miles (again, distances in this game...) per trip is pretty punitive. It's 2 Gnosis do to the little Nightcrawler style appear, warn, disappear trick.<br /> <br /> Outside of the most traditionalist Caerns, or traditionalist Striders that are opposed to it on their end of things.., we have nearly instantaneous global communication, and Moon Bridges already allow the Garou to low cost travel around the world in minutes. The steep Gnosis cost and roll with some nasty potential consequences if you need to make one that's higher diff, feels like it should * Green or near Amber for a Rank 5 Gift even if teleporting is always good to have.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1548963#post1548963\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Predator's Many Eyes (3):</b></u></span> This Gift is like Territory, but instead of urinating to mark a territory, you mark an animal (Fera, and presumably Garou, are valid targets, with their consent) simply by looking at them. Charisma + Animal Ken, difficulty 7, is used, and the range is sight, which is pretty great. Thereafter you can see through the target's eyes.<br /> <br /> For me, this is significantly better than Territory. You can see a bird in the distance flying and immediately gain the ability to see through its eyes. There's also nothing stopping you from learning the Territory Gift if you need it for some reason also.</div> </div> </div> </div>This one needs a big old asterisk on it because there's a contradiction in how it's written and is very ST dependent. The description section says you can see through the target's eyes, while the mechanics section says it works like Territory (which means you have your normal sense from the position of the animal). Which is supposed to be the case really impacts how it compares to Territory. It also only works on predators, which can limit you a lot dependent on how your ST considers things like insectivores (aka a lot of birds). This Gift also offers no control over the animal in question, which puts a lot of ST control over what you can get out of it. &quot;You see your eagle-target's nest full of chicks because it's feeding them right now,&quot; would suck to have to put up with unless you get some of the animal-control Gifts.<br /> <br /> IMO the White Howlers got the best version of this because they can mark stones by carving a symbol into them. The stones are reusable (though you have to carve them each time so if you use this a lot on one stone it'll get obvious), but you can also use a stone you put somewhere. So you can get to far more places than where you can urinate (toss a small stone into an enemy encampment and freely spy on them from a distance), and stones don't have lives of their own to go attend to like the animals you mark with Predator's Many Eyes.<br /> <br /> Though at least we also get another example of how Gifts can be varied slightly by Tribe out of it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T15:53:58-08:00","id":1548972,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> keep in mind that Predator's Many Eyes lets you see through the eyes of your entire pack, based on the RAW, and through all of the eyes of your Fera allies, with the right setup. We're told that it works like Territory, so presumably you can have a maximum number of allies you see through the eyes of equal to your max Gnosis, but even with just that you can see through the eyes of your packmates most likely.<br /> <br /> &quot;You can see through the eyes of all of your packmates&quot; is a pretty great Gift for starters - but I'll edit the entry to mention the Predator restriction and the issues with the mechanics.<br /> <br /> And yeah I think I forgot that the cost from Gate of the Moon adds up - worth a * since in a game with very poor Gnosis recovery you'll struggle to get the best results from it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-04T18:47:25-08:00","id":1548974,"title":"","content":"That's kinda skipping that the text is internally contradictory though. <br /> <br /> &quot;As the Red Talon Gift: Territory. The Red Talon needs not urinate on the animal it wishes to mark; the player must merely roll Charisma + Animal Ken (difficulty 7) while the predator is in sight. Using this Gift on other Fera is possible but requires the Fera\u2019s consent. In all other respects, this Gift functions as Territory.&quot;<br /> <br /> The mechanics don't back up the statement that you see through the target's eyes, because that's not how Territory functions. If the last line of the description text wasn't there, you'd assume this power lets you see as if you were next to the target of it, not through their eyes, because Territory doesn't see things from the target's perspective, but from next to it's perspective. And Predator's Many Eyes only changes the need to urinate, the ability to target animals and Fera, and what the roll is. Then ends with, &quot;In all other respects, this Gift functions as Territory.&quot; Seeing through their eyes isn't obeying that phrasing.<br /> <br /> Yes, the &quot;through the eyes of&quot; vs. &quot;as if you were standing next to,&quot; isn't going to make a huge difference when it comes to using it on willing allies (though you can give people stone pendants for the same thing with the White Howler Territory variant). But it's still a Gift that is poorly written in a fashion that directly impacts how it stacks up to Territory based on ST interpretation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-05T01:13:31-08:00","id":1548978,"title":"","content":"That's true! But my understanding is that the RAW is that the text of Predator's Many Eyes overrides the text of Territory; the Gift says as much. Basically &quot;here's how this works, for all the other details, see Territory&quot;, or &quot;in all other respects, as Territory&quot;. Now that's a messy way to write a Gift! But I don't think there's that much ambiguity.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-05T01:41:04-08:00","id":1548979,"title":"","content":"The issue is the &quot;see through the eyes&quot; isn't in the part that's followed by, &quot;in all other respects.&quot; That's in the previous section. I'm not saying you're reading it &quot;wrong&quot; or something. Just that it's a messy wording and reading it the other way isn't wrong either (it's far from the first time the general descriptor of what the Gift doesn't isn't really accurate to how the mechanics work)... which is normally grounds for an * or something to denote that ST interpretation (and other issues like using on animals when you don't also have any animal control powers is a crap shoot depending on your ST) can drastically impact how the Gift functions.<br /> <br /> If a partial goal in all this is a reference where you can go over Gifts to see which ones need work... are you really arguing this one doesn't need some tweaking to be clearer and less dependent on the ST's judgement so it even works like how you feel would be good (or even just make it all more balanced so the three different versions of Territory are more on par with each others instead of the default Red Talon one being objectively worse)?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-05T01:46:28-08:00","id":1548980,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Middle Brother Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Middle Brother are meant to fall between the other Tribes of North America. So while Elder Brother might be scheming of some plan many moons away with spirits and rituals, and Younger Brother might be demanding violent action tonight, Middle Brother mediates. Stoicism, endurance and balance are meant to be key to their Gifts.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Master of Fire (1):</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Homid list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Mindspeak (1):</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Galliard list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Turtle Body (1):</b></u></span> This Gift lets a Garou enter a torpor-like trance for hours at a time, ignoring poisons, bleeding wounds, needing to breathe, extremes of heat and cold, and so on. Spending a Gnosis and rolling Gnosis (difficulty 6) lets you enter this trance for one hour per success. It's perfectly... fine? It's a handy way to play dead, hardly a bad Gift at all, but the Tribe would be much better off with Resist Toxin or similar. The Gift also really doesn't need a Gnosis cost.<br /> <br /> My ultimate problem with this, beyond the cost, the fact you roll Gnosis (a relatively weak pool), is that you have to go inactive. Not needing to breathe for 3 hours is great, but if you just lay there immobile for 3 hours, then return to the land of the living to suffocate as normal, that's pretty terrible. You absolutely can make this work, but there are much better Gifts for the same job, like Cocoon. At any other level than 1 this would be <span style=\"color:#c0392b\">Red</span>, but it scrapes an <span style=\"color:#f39c12\">Amber</span> since it only costs 3xp. That said, it's taking up a slot which a superior survival Gift could be used for!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Wyld Resurgence (1):</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Black Fury list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Strength of Purpose (2):</b></u></span> See the Rank 2 Philodox list. Infinite Willpower! Annoyingly, for a Tribe about balance, this does mean you have a weird incentive to play a different Auspice. An Ahroun or Theurge with Strength of Purpose would be pretty incredible for instance.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Turtle Shell (2):</b></u></span> With this Gift you can create a defensive shell with variable size and power depending on your successes. Annoyingly we don't get any clues as to whether the shell is meant to move, but I'm happy to say that the intent is almost certainly for it to be immobile, based on my reading.<br /> <br /> 2 soak per success, 2 health levels per success, covers 2 yards per success, and it costs 1 Gnosis. The roll is Stamina + Survival, which is pretty easy to boost by taking Crinos. If you can get 12 dice, this will consistently get ~5 successes, granting an average of 10 soak, 10 health levels, and being big enough to shelter 10 Crinos Garou. Having a protective barrier you and your whole pack, plus allies, can hide behind is a pretty great Gift! Many foes simply won't be able to harm you, and of those that can, wasting some of their time is handy.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Call Earth Spirit (3):</b></u></span> Costing a Gnosis for a damaging attack is a bad start, and this Gift is difficulty 8 to invoke. Furthermore if you botch it hurts you instead. Basically this Gift lets you send an earth spirit 30 yards in a direction to hit everyone in its path for 10 dice of lethal. But Manipulation + Occult difficulty 8? Not great!<br /> <br /> If you can manage the difficulty somehow, great, but I don't know of a way to make this Gift easier to use. Wasting a Gnosis on a difficulty 8 test isn't fun, and the result is a straight line AOE attack which inflicts lethal. Even if you luck out and inflict 10 lethal on every enemy in this straight line, chances are that enemies worth fighting at Rank 3 will soak a good amount of it. If this were difficulty 7 it would be far better, the probability curve is much more steep than the writers seemed to realize.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Shell (3):</b></u></span> See the Rank 3 Metis list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Hands of the Earth Lords (4):</b></u></span> See the Rank 4 Elder Brother list. It would be interesting, given that the EB Tribe are said to have 'recently uncovered' a bunch of their Gifts, if this Gift was originally a MB Gift which has been recovered by the EB.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Stronger on Stone (4):</b></u></span> Spending a Gnosis and a Rage, a Garou using this Gift can gain a bonus to Strength and/or Stamina based on where they're standing. This is 1-4 points to distribute between the two Attributes, so for example while standing on naked stone they might get +4 Strength, or +4 Stamina, or anywhere in between.<br /> <br /> +4 Stamina is neat, don't get me wrong, but the situational nature of the Gift is a little annoying. It would be easy to fix the Gift by making it give a bonus to Strength and Stamina, rather than a bonus you split between them, but no matter. The cost of G+R is also a bit much.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Katanka-Sonnak's Spear (5):</b></u></span> This Gift is shockingly amazing. Spend a Gnosis, target anyone you can see (including miles away), and roll Dexterity + Occult (difficulty 6). If the strike hits, a sunbeam from the sky, the target takes 10 dice of Aggravated damage every single turn. The fire burns for 10 turns, can't be doused by normal water, and will very likely kill most targets. That said, the effect is canceled if the Gift is &quot;dispelled&quot;, if a water spirit puts out the fire, or if the fire does no damage on a specific turn.<br /> <br /> For example, 10 dice of Aggravated, you roll 5 successes. Rank 1 Garou gets hit, they only soak 3, so they take 2 Aggravated. Next turn they are in Crinos form, you roll 4 damage, they soak all 4, and the fire dies down.<br /> <br /> Still, the Gift only costs 1 Gnosis, the range is incredible, and with any Gift which helps you to see much further it'll be even better. Solid Green and a fitting Rank 5.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Survivor (5):</b></u></span> See the Rank 5 Bone Gnawer list.<br /> <br /> The list winds up pretty middling, sadly!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-05T01:51:49-08:00","id":1548981,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> yeah sure I can throw in a *, since it needs careful reading.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-05T15:38:08-08:00","id":1548995,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11620_5.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11620\" data-vbnamecard=\"11620\">twig</a> this is my favourite thread for the World of Darkness.<br /> <br /> Are you going to do the Changing Ways gifts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude18","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-06T03:33:54-08:00","id":1549004,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>lbeaumanior</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1548995#post1548995\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11620_5.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11620\" data-vbnamecard=\"11620\">twig</a> this is my favourite thread for the World of Darkness.<br /> <br /> Are you going to do the Changing Ways gifts?</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks! I think for the time-being I'm only going to do Garou stuff, however I might make a Fera thread at some point and try to do a deep dive into who gets the best lists etc.<br /> <br /> Right now the main things left to do are White Howlers, and the other random W20 Gifts scattered throughout the different books, so long as they can be learnt by a Werewolf I'll be doing them. I know that technically you can learn the unique Gifts of Fera, at +1 level I believe (so a level 1 Ratkin Gift is level 2 to Garou) but that's a whole can of worms to get into.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-06T05:04:21-08:00","id":1549009,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549004#post1549004\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Thanks! I think for the time-being I'm only going to do Garou stuff, however I might make a Fera thread at some point and try to do a deep dive into who gets the best lists etc.<br /> <br /> Right now the main things left to do are White Howlers, and the other random W20 Gifts scattered throughout the different books, so long as they can be learnt by a Werewolf I'll be doing them. I know that technically you can learn the unique Gifts of Fera, at +1 level I believe (so a level 1 Ratkin Gift is level 2 to Garou) but that's a whole can of worms to get into.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for taking the time to reply.<br /> <br /> Changing Ways is the one that has W20 gifts (new auspice and breed gifts for W20), Changing <i>Breeds </i>it is the one for Fera on W20.<br /> <br /> There are some others in the W20 line that I remember... White Howlers book, the Wyld West, Book of the Wyrm.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-06T12:26:02-08:00","id":1549021,"title":"","content":"There's also a couple in Rage Across the World (I think half are for the Singing Dogs alone though). KABA also has Gifts in it, and it's probably valuable to look at how their Gifts stack up to normal Garou Rank 1 ones. Icons of Rage has Jonas's signature Rank 6 Gift updated for W20 in it IIRC.. which I think is actually all of the rest of the books with Gifts in them.<br /> <br /> W20 was, generally, better about not slapping lots of new Gifts into every source book and most of the ones in supplements are justifiably present for being relevant to the subject of the book. Fetishes and Rites are a different matter...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-06T13:59:22-08:00","id":1549023,"title":"","content":"<u><b>White Howler Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> The White Howlers were Pictish, and their Gifts are intended to have heavy themes of morbidity, darkness and passion. It's said that the Howlers were extremely passionate for hunting and battles, and that their rites and mystical Gifts have eluded Theurges attempting to recreate them. While the best White Howler stuff is in their Rites, let's see what their Gift list is like. Note that I am including all of the Gifts in their Tribebook, which is currently the only Tribebook I own for W20, so this list will be VERY beefy.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Blood Scent (1):</b></u></span> Passive buff to tracking individuals whose blood you have tasted. This buff of +2 dice applies to mundane tracking and also to supernatural means such as those given by Gifts, and presumably by Fetishes. With this Gift it's a good idea to have tasted the blood of your packmates, with their permission, to help track them down if they escape in a Fox Frenzy or become subject to mind control. An interesting thing to consider with this Gift is whether it helps you to recognize people whose blood you have tasted. You have +2 dice to track them, but them as in the person whose blood you have tasted. Would this Gift give you a +2 bonus to notice that the person you are looking at isn't your packmate? Though the senses provided by Lupus mostly cover this issue, since an impostor will often smell wrong.<br /> <br /> Handy buff, well worth 3xp, but not good enough to be a Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Death Sight (1):</b></u></span> This is equivalent to Visions of Duat, see the Rank 1 Silent Strider list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Desperate Strength (1):</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Bone Gnawer list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Haunting Howl (1):</b></u></span> This looks horrible and pretty worthless, on a quick examination. By rolling Charisma + Intimidation, difficulty 7, you can make all enemies within earshot suffer +1 to Willpower tests for 3 turns. 3 turns per success, so with a few successes it lasts longer. No impact on spending Willpower, just the difficulty of the rolls.<br /> <br /> Anyone figured out why it's good yet? No, it isn't because it messes with Tremere. It definitely isn't because it interferes with Feats of Strength.<br /> <br /> It's because it works on Spirits.<br /> <br /> Willpower is Dexterity and Stamina. Spirits roll Willpower to hit, and they roll Willpower for soak, plus they roll Willpower for some Charms. They roll Willpower to sneak around, they roll Willpower to dodge, they roll Willpower to parry. Now, given that this Gift costs 3xp to purchase and it has no cost at all to use, just a difficulty 7 roll, and given that it has a generous duration (most likely 3, 6 or 9 turns), and given that it adds +1 difficulty to the attacks, dodges, and soak attempts made by all enemy spirits, without harming allied spirits... yeah this is actually pretty great! It's niche, but it's hard to overstate how much impact +1 difficulty can have across a whole battle. If you're not convinced, ask yourself how you react to a Flaw which gives your character +1 difficulty to all of their attacks.<br /> <br /> Against enemies other than Spirits? Well it'll mess with some Gifts. It'll interfere with blood magic, and with all the things that might call for Willpower tests depending on the ST, like whether enemies flee or hold the line.<br /> <br /> The key question is how important is rolling Willpower in Werewolf, for Garou, Fera, Fomori, and various other enemies. Cause Insanity, a Fomori power, is a WP roll. Warp Reality is a WP roll (difficulty 7), and that is arguably the most fearsome ability of the Nexus Crawler. Garou roll Willpower to avoid Fox Frenzy.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Primal Anger (1):</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Metis list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sense Wyrm (1):</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Metis list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Shining Sigils (1)*:</b></u></span> This Gift lets you present your Renown ratings as shining symbols appearing all over your body, like woad spirals. There is no cost, and it grants you a pool of bonus dice equal to your Rank which you can benefit from in a given scene.<br /> <br /> So, as a Rank 2 White Howler you might have 2 dice to call upon, and you could split those between two rolls or use them all in one go. We are told they work on &quot;any Social roll (including Intimidation)&quot;, so presumably they would work with a number of social Gifts. While this Gift isn't as good as Persuasion, for example, it's still pretty great. In many scenes there will only be a single social roll, and it may be a totally pivotal one. Being able to add 1-5 dice to that test, combined with any difficulty reductions you've gained from Merits, Gifts or similar, can have a very big impact. All in all this is a pretty solid 3xp investment, and it may well work out as Green if you have the right setup and you choose the social rolls you use it on carefully.<br /> <br /> In an absolutely ideal setup, where you're maximizing every possible input (Pure Breed, Ancestors, all the right Merits, the right Fetish, maxed out social stats, this Gift, Persuasion, and a bunch of other stuff including the right Totem) everything compounds on itself. When you're rolling 20 dice at a difficulty of 3, you naturally get results like 14 successes. This Gift is well worth taking for flavour alone, and for the non-mechanical impact of being able to literally prove your Rank and Renown to strangers through a Gift. I just wish there was a Level 2 White Howler Gift which allows you to see the invisible markings of Rank &amp; Renown on others, for example immediately recognizing that a stranger is Rank 4 with a certain combo of Honour, Glory and Wisdom, due to the invisible spirit-markings trailing across their form.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Howl of the Banshee (2):</b></u></span> See the Rank 2 Fianna list. This was added in the WH Book, and its absence was pretty conspicuous. This is a Gift which combos well with Haunting Howl. This Gift remains far too powerful for a level 2, due to the ability to trigger a panic across a huge part of a city.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Howls in the Night (2):</b></u></span> See the Rank 2 Galliard list. Funny to imagine a BSD using this to piss off or punish all of their allies.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Lion's Bite (2)*:</b></u></span> This is an interesting one. Instead of doing a Dexterity + Brawl attack to bite, you do a Strength + Brawl. You inflict your 'normal' Bite damage and the target becomes 'grappled' in your maw as you try to crush their throat or equivalent. Every round your victim can try a resisted test of Strength to escape (this is where a Gift like Pain-Strength would theoretically be handy, but that doesn't save it from being terrible), which is their action for the turn, or they can try and do something else at a +2 difficulty.<br /> <br /> How good is this in practice? Well, I'm not a Bite Scientist, but gaining access to a means of attacking which is Strength-based is favourable for Garou, with their +4 to Strength in Crinos. An automatic grapple into the bargain ain't bad either. After a number of rounds equal to 2x the target's stamina they fall unconscious, at which point they start taking Lethal damage every round, which can't be regenerated. It isn't explicitly stated, but presumably this damage ignores Soak. This does grant a means of harming a target who otherwise might be impossible to harm.<br /> <br /> For me, personally, as a person who isn't a huge fan of bites, this is decent enough. A Strength-based combat action with damage and a grapple all baked in together is neat. Normally to grapple someone you need to devote an action to a non-damaging grapple, and start damaging the next turn. With this you can get started a turn early. I'd call this an objective upgrade well worth 6xp, but mileage will vary depending on playstyle and what attacks you favour. Combining with Clenched Jaw (Ahroun, Rank 4) is probably tempting as it will grant you a bonus to the Strength test to keep the target grappled, and reduce the difficulty of bite attacks while grappling the foe down to 3. All pretty good. Calling this Amber, but it could easily be Green if you prefer biting and made the right 'build'. Much like with Clenched Jaw this feels like a Gift covering something which should be in the combat rules chapter; ordinary wolves do this!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Pain-Strength (2):</b></u></span> When wounded, you can spend a Rage point to activate Pain-Strength. While the Gift is active, wound penalties give a boost to your Strength rating, and to your damage pools, essentially reversing the penalty.<br /> <br /> So, for example, if you would normally suffer a -5 to all dice pools, you suffer a -5 to your to-hit but you get a +5 to your damage pool. Notably you still suffer the penalty to Strength + Ability pools, which means if you are grappling someone you'll have neither a bonus nor a debuff, since you get +5 strength and -5 wound penalty.<br /> <br /> Is this good? Well, is it good to be wounded? No. Is it good to suffer dice penalties to your attack rolls, your dodges, and so on? No. In fact, a lot of effort goes into avoiding wound penalties. Spending WP, getting Resist Pain, and so on. And this Gift is explicitly not compatible with any Gift which lets you ignore your wound modifiers. Furthermore, being at -5 means you can only crawl; you're horribly wounded.<br /> <br /> The Gift is flavourful and pretty cool, but it's just horrible to use. Resist Pain is better, any day. Spending WP to ignore wound penalties is better, and getting healed is better. If this Gift were 100% passive you could make an argument for &quot;well you want to avoid wound penalties, of course, but if you ARE wounded this kicks in and gives you an upside, even if you want to get healed ASAP&quot;. Unfortunately the Rage cost messes with that! All in all, not a fan of this one, especially since it doesn't fully apply to Strength + Brawl and Strength + Athletics tests, which would give this Gift a great niche for Challenges!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Spectral Authority (2)*:</b></u></span> This is equivalent to Call to Duty (Philodox Rank 2), but it works on Ghosts. You must know the full name of the Ghost in question, and the Gift may only summon a single Wraith at a time, there is no urgent call for all allied ghosts as you find with Call to Duty. This works well with the ghostly theme of the White Howlers, but it's worth remembering that Wraiths are inherently less relevant to Werewolf as a game. The lack of a Gnosis cost is handy, and a network of Wraith spies with this Gift is totally doable. It's Amber, but it could easily be Green with the right ST, story, and setup time. A Werewolf ST who is intimately familiar with Wraith would be a godsend with this Gift.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Blooding Fury (3)*:</b></u></span> Once per scene you may take a level of Aggravated damage and recover ALL of your Rage. This Gift is somewhat redundant in terms of game design when sat next to Primal Anger. There isn't much point writing both. That doesn't mean the Gift is mechanically redundant, however. Primal Anger works once per scene and grants you 3 temporary Rage regardless of your normal limits. If you are at 0/5 you go to 3/5, if you are at 1/1 you go to 4/1. Blooding Fury is very different, it can take you from 0/1 to 1/1 or from 0/10 to 10/10, or from 4/6 to 6/6, and so on.<br /> <br /> With that in mind, a combination of Blooding Fury and Primal Anger lets you go from 0/5 to 8/5 by taking two points of Aggravated damage. If you have a means of healing that damage easily (such as a Water Spirit, which you'll be able to summon from next rank) then this combination could be very good; it's basically a 100% recharge of your Rage between fights. Yes, some Werewolf players like to say &quot;you get rage for stubbing your toe&quot;, but that isn't the RAW. The intended mechanics include Rage being a resource which is sometimes a struggle to recover when you really need it.<br /> <br /> Given that this is Rank 3, and there is a bit of overlap with another Gift which is only Rank 1, and this Gift comes with the inherent downside of needing to hurt yourself... Amber is just fine. If healing the damage is utterly trivial to you then the Gift is even better, but I'd say that Green is a push unless your Rage rating is very high, for example 7. 0/7 to 10/7 in a quick combo with your Water Spirit or Theurge healing the damage? Sure. Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Sense of the Deep (3)*:</b></u></span> A bunch of really neat passive benefits. While underground you always know which way is North, you can always retrace your steps, and you can always see normally, ignoring penalties from darkness. Used aggressively, this Gift could be exploited in any fight below ground level. Sabotage the lighting, or even have a Challenge in a subway or some other underground chamber. The flavour of this Gift is also pretty great, as it highlights the role of the WHs and ties into how they were lost.<br /> <br /> This is Amber, mostly because it's a bit niche. It could very easily be Green if you are constantly fighting underground, invading Wyrmish pits, chasing spirits into their tunnels and so on.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Ley Lines (3):</b></u></span> See the Rank 3 Fianna list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Territory (3):</b></u></span> Mechanically identical to the Red Talon Gift, but Homid and Metis White Howlers may mark stones instead. Already a great Gift, arguably improved here.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Waking the Dead (3):</b></u></span> This Gift is very Dungeons and Dragons, though in the case of this Gift I mean that as a compliment. A corpse, any corpse, can be made to answer one question per point of Gnosis spent on activating the Gift. The body of a Kinfolk ally, for example, can be made to answer 5 questions by spending 5 Gnosis. There is no roll, but the Gift can only be used on a body once. After this it is impossible to use the Gift again.<br /> <br /> It's interesting to note that this is a phantasm conjured from the body, it isn't a true Wraith, or any kind of spirit, and the fact that you can use it on a Vampire is very funny to me. The answers to a problem being in the mind of an ancient Kinfolk who was embraced by a Leech in the 1100s, for example, is a possible plot thread you could pull using this Gift.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Call Elemental (4):</b></u></span> See the Rank 4 Elder Brother list. Losing the only European Tribe with this Gift probably wasn't ideal.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Collapse the Barrow (4)*:</b></u></span> This Gift lets you collapse an underground structure, ideally after the Wyrmish inhabitants have been killed and the area cleansed. Strength + Occult, difficulty can be as low as 4 or as high as 9 depending on the integrity of the structure, but 6 is the default. There is no cost. For every success a 5-foot area collapses, which is... not huge, all things considered.<br /> <br /> Using this Gift to block off means of escape, with yourself on the inside with the enemy, or with the enemy stuck inside while you lay siege outside, is interesting. For example you could block every tunnel leading into a Wyrmish Pit, forcing all of your enemies to come in and out through a single tunnel.<br /> <br /> Having the cost-free option of using Strength + Occult to demolish stone, earthen and underground structures is neat. But &quot;other Fomori will reuse this underground cave system&quot; feels like a niche problem to have, especially given that you can introduce animals (even wolves!) into the space after it is purified. At Rank 4 I struggle to imagine &quot;A Cave Exists&quot; is a huge problem for you, given that there will be many Garou and Kinfolk under your command. Red, but could be Amber in the right setting.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Hero's Stand (4):</b></u></span> See the Rank 4 Get of Fenris list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Maddening Howl (4)*:</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis and a Rage, roll Charisma + Performance (difficulty 7), and all non-White Howlers who can hear the howl have to roll Willpower difficulty 7. Worth noting that the Willpower roll will almost certainly be difficulty 8 thanks to Haunting Howl! If any victim gets fewer successes than you they fall into a state of madness, picking one of five random results from the Delirium table. The ST chooses, but the idea is that the result is more random than characteristic to the target. So a Get of Fenris Elder may well fall into a state of complete panic while a peaceful Child of Gaia could go into a berserk killing spree.<br /> <br /> This has the potential to be very, very good. You can hit everyone who hears it, everyone... including you and your pack, if you aren't White Howlers. Even if we assume the user of this Gift is immune (and we can't really!), your pack all being hit is pretty bad. You could have them protect their hearing before you use the Gift, and remove that protection after. But still. Heavy asterisk on this one; it's absolutely incredible for a White Howler who is either solo or who has a pack of White Howlers. The resulting chaos from this Gift is probably much what you'd expect, which could be highly beneficial to the PCs. A Caern or Pit full of powerful enemies is impossible to escape, meanwhile a Caern or Pit which is full of people fleeing, screaming, fighting eachother, going into complete denial about their surroundings is much more manageable, even if it's still a horror show.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Visions of Slaughter (4):</b></u></span> This Gift allows you to haunt someone, mortal, animal, supernatural, with visions of any animals or people they have caused to die through action or inaction. After smearing the target with your bodily fluids (saliva, please) you make an Intelligence + Occult, difficulty of the target's WP. The visions last for one day per success.<br /> <br /> While 'haunted' by the visions of those they have killed the victim must roll Willpower once a day (Haunting Howl synergy!) against a difficulty determined by how many victims they have had. 4 is the minimum (having killed the odd mouse or hit something with their car) while 9 is the maximum (centuries of slaughter). Even if you don't care about all the mortals you've murdered over the years, seeing them all following you around is still pretty distracting! If you're effected by the Gift you can't recover Willpower by any means, and once your Willpower falls below half your maximum you suffer a +1 difficulty to all rolls (besides soak and damage).<br /> <br /> So, how good is this? Well, I could easily see this as a Punishment Rite rather than a Gift. But since it is a Gift... the requirement of getting some of your saliva, blood or some other bodily fluid onto the target is hard to do without starting an altercation. The roll against Willpower means that this Gift is harder to pull off against an enemy you actually care to curse. But only one success is really needed in a proper battle, since you want them dead! With that one success they will hopefully become effected, since even with a Willpower of , a difficulty 7-9 test (what one of your enemies will typically need to pass) could be challenging. The lack of a Gnosis cost on this Gift is also a bit odd.<br /> <br /> This might do a lot, or it might do nothing. It's a bit of a slow burn, but it's a unique and interesting concept. Just gonna call it Amber.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Fog on the Moor (5):</b></u></span> See the Rank 5 Fianna list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Howl of Healing (5)*:</b></u></span> Intelligence + Expression, difficulty 7, all White Howlers within earshot recover 1 point of damage (any kind) per success. This is pretty great! It costs 1 Gnosis, and it's extremely powerful as a form of healing. Compare with Mother's Touch, which is single-target, typically a lower difficulty, but can effect anyone. Howl of Healing could effect 100s of White Howlers... if 100s exist. If you are the Elder of a WH Sept and there is a thriving WH Tribe, this is Green. Otherwise it's likely Red. If a Child of Gaia were able to salvage this Gift and have it reformed by Unicorn it could be a great boon; if I were the ST in such a game I'd be open to this Gift being picked up by a dedicated Garou of a different tribe, with them being able to work to convert it to apply to their tribe instead.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Mad Strength (5):</b></u></span> This is a passive boon, granting you +2 Strength and +1 Stamina while in a Berserk Frenzy or Thrall of the Wyrm. Great flavour for a pre-fall Tribe, suits the lore of the Howlers, and matches their Totem too. How good is it? Well, it's niche, and the bonus is decent. Having 11 Strength instead of 9, and 9 Stamina instead of 8, would be pretty good. But I'd much prefer a Gift like this if it were at a lower level, like 3 or even 2.<br /> <br /> Making the most of this Gift is like trying to make the most of Pain-Strength. A lot of Elder powers I find myself thinking &quot;well, let it apply to the whole pack&quot;, but &quot;everyone in my pack has +2 Strength while frenzying&quot; is a very mixed blessing. The potential for this Gift to backfire and help you just barely do enough damage to kill an ally shouldn't be ignored. If this Gift were stronger, and followed on from an earlier Gift where you voluntarily enter a Frenzy with some specific benefits and limitations (e.g. the Frenzy ends when a specific foe is dead) then the rating would be very different.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Gaia's Vengeance (5):</b></u> See the Rank 5 Red Talons list. The Gift is basically unwritten, but I can see how it would be helpful in underground battles, if it had mechanics.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>White Fire (5):</b></u></span> The bone-fire of the White Howlers, later corrupted to the balefire of the BSDs, has purifying as well as damaging properties. This Gift allows you to fling it at people! There is a cool benefit that those killed with this power won't become ghosts or undead, and most likely a Fomori destroyed with the Gift won't have its Bane return to Malfeas, instead being destroyed.<br /> <br /> The attack costs one Gnosis, which is expensive for a single-shot attack, but the attack is a Dexterity + Occult (difficulty 6) which deals 6 dice of Aggravated, with bonus successes carrying over. Ranged Aggravated is always great, and a damage of 6 + Successes is also pretty great, though a typical claw attack will do more. What's important, however, is that if you get 3+ successes on the attack you will also blind your target for the whole scene. If you have a difficulty break on the Dexterity + Occult attack, for example from an ambush or the enemy being stunned, it's very likely you'll get 3+ successes.<br /> <br /> How good is a solid attack which comes with a blinding effect, plus a lot of handy narrative benefits relating to purifying the targets you kill? Well, the fact that you can create bone fire is pretty neat. The blinding is neat. The attack is neat. That said, this could be a lower level Gift, given that every attack has a Gnosis cost. At Rank 5 this Gift could easily have a Rage cost. We'll call it Amber.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 05-23-2025, 07:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-06T15:04:04-08:00","id":1549027,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Homid Gifts (Changing Ways)</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Weaver's Eyes (2):</b></u></span> Spend a Gnosis. For one scene you get to hear everyone declare their actions before you choose yours, regardless of your position in initiative. This is inferior to actually having better initiative of course (Spirit of the Fray is far, far better than this), but if you're playing the game exactly as intended the strategic benefits you get from this Gift could be very handy.<br /> <br /> For example, the guy who is going to go first may well be planning to cover the floor of the whole lab with a caustic slime. Knowing this, you might bake crossing the room in an unusual way into your plan for your turn rather than having to change plans when moving. For a highly strategic game where the rules are followed carefully this could be pretty great! The problem is the Gnosis cost, combined with the fact that it needs to be activated at all. This could be entirely passive and still work.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Rally the Troops (3):</b></u></span> This Gift lets you rally bystanders (but not supernatural bystanders like Garou or supernaturally powerful Kinfolk) to help you and also grants bonus dice to a task the group could help you with. There is no activation cost and the Charisma + Leadership is only difficulty 6. If the group is mostly Kinfolk you also find the task easier, at -1 difficulty. Important NPCs do get to opt out of your rallying call if they have good reason to.<br /> <br /> This is pretty neat; it would arguably be fine at Rank 2, but it somewhat overcomes the problem of The Curse when it comes to interacting with large groups of humans, so long as your goal is short term intense group action. It's important to note that what you rally the crowd to do doesn't have to be violent, the examples given include protest, rescue and heckling. You could use this Gift to inspire the bull-pen to trade like they've never traded before, which would suit a banking Ragabash Glass Walker perfectly. Assuming an urban setting this is handy enough to be Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Web of Knowledge (4):</b></u></span> This one is a little hard to parse. It basically lets you pick up info from the ether. I won't do a deep dive into the mechanics, the main thing is that it lets you do a bunch of types of rolls as a simple check without having to take time to research, examine and so on. It's a cool concept, the Gnosis cost makes sense since it feels pretty magical, and it lets you do that BBC Sherlock &quot;I figured it out using magic, I mean, genius&quot; trick where you make giant leaps in logic and then the plot agrees with you. Which is bad TV, but great in a game. The max difficulty of any check you can make with this Gift is 7, since anything of higher difficulty is too obscure.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Metis Gifts (Changing Ways)</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Stoat's Guise (2):</b></u></span> As always I love Metis Gifts which pick up on the weirder capabilities of animals who would be so easily overlooked by Garou. Metis being beloved by the 'oddballs' of nature is a lovely theme to me. Here we have a Gift based on species who change fur types depending on the season. With a Manipulation + Subterfuge (difficulty 6) you can alter features of your body to suit your needs. Plus or minus 20% on size, skin tone, hair type, eye colour, fur length, hiding scars, adjusting markings, you name it. Assuming a decent dice pool you can pull off a dazzling array of disguises with this Gift, though of course your scent is going to stay the same. Incredible for any Garou, but especially for a Metis Ragabash who will find themselves changing their looks and suppressing their scent.<br /> <br /> Notably this Gift can never totally hide a Metis deformity, which actually makes albinism have a sleeper downside! My Metis is an absolute giant, a unit, and as well as being very obviously blind his hair is a very stark white. Even if he made himself 20% smaller, changed his skull shape, shortened his hair and gave himself a few scars, at absolute most he'd wind up looking like himself slouched over with a haircut. If you have a very distinctive Metis deformity chances are the disguise potential of this Gift will remain limited, but it's still a solid Green.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Deny the Birthright (3)*:</b></u> This Gift lets a Metis ignore their Metis deformity for a whole scene, though at great risk to their Honour if they are part of the Garou Nation. The Gift requires a point of Willpower and Gnosis each, and lasts for one scene, though it can be extended at a rate of one Scene per point of Gnosis spent. While using this Gift the Metis can't regain Gnosis.<br /> <br /> Being found to possess this Gift costs you a permanent dot of Honour, and being caught using it costs you a temporary point of both Honour and Wisdom. The Garou Nation and Litany see a Metis' deformities as being a punishment from Gaia for the sin of their birth after all. If you aren't a member of the Garou Nation, or you are part of an ultra-progressive Sept of Children of Gaia or Glass Walkers, it's possible that these Renown penalties won't apply to you, much as in many Septs an Elder won't lose Renown for refusing to end their life if they develop a disability which requires care. The value of this Gift is inherently variable, and is determined by the deformity (blindness vs albinism) and by politics. It's balanced and well written, but impossible to rate.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Rage of the Underdog (3):</b></u></span> This is a Gift which depends on you suffering your wound penalties instead of ignoring them, which we know I dislike. The ideal scenario is that you're at -2 from wounds, your target has no wounds at all, you spend a point of Rage when you hit, you deal +2 dice of damage, and if you put them to -2 on wound penalties or beyond you get back that point of Rage. A situational bonus to your damage pool if you are damaged and your opponent isn't is... okay. A handy passive. The Rage cost is annoying however.<br /> <br /> Ultimately you are better off ignoring your wounds entirely. Get Resist Pain, or high Willpower, or a method of Willpower restoration. Or invest in dodging. Or healing.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Tenacious Fury (4):</b></u></span> This Gift lets you spend Rage on your Remain Active roll to guarantee successes, but it almost certainly guarantees that you will Frenzy, since you can't spend Willpower to resist Frenzy if you use this Gift, and frenzying requires 3 successes rather than 4 if you utilize it.<br /> <br /> A potential use of this Gift is to spend a number of Rage points to ensure that you will fully heal yourself back to normal when you do the test, say for example you are an Ahroun and you spend 5 Rage to get 5 successes, plus whatever you happen to roll on the test. The problem with this is that Thrall of the Wyrm is almost certain! But, life or death situations... all in all it might be best to spend a single point of Rage on this to help ensure you get a good bit of healing, or perhaps 2 or 3 points. This Gift could do without its downsides, but the ability to help save your life is pretty neat.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Lupus Gifts (Changing Ways)</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Go for the Jugular (2):</b></u></span> I've seen this Gift cited as the reason Changing Ways is BUSTED and BROKEN and should NEVER BE ALLOWED. My hot take is that this Gift isn't so bad. It should be +2 difficulty, not +3, absolutely, but Rage is best spent on bonus actions, or to avoid being stunned if necessary. Spending it to make a soak roll be difficulty 9 instead of difficulty 6 doesn't increase the maximum amount of damage you do, it just reduces the average amount the target soaks. Let's be as generous as possible and say our target has a soak pool of 8 (being a BSD or perhaps a Spirit), which sees their average soak fall from 3.5 to 1.8. That's not a giant damage spike, it just reduces the amount soaked by ~2. +2 damage is great, don't get me wrong, but there are more busted things in W20 than +2 damage. There are Fetish weapons which double your damage output, for instance.<br /> <br /> If you think this Gift is too busted, experiment with +2 difficulty to soak instead of +3. I personally like that this Gift allows a Lupus to successfully chomp a Stamina 8 Fortitude 8 Ventrue Methuselah. No, the Gift won't cause that Vampire to instantly implode, but it will reduce their soak by a good bit, from an average of 6.5 to 2.7, which might make the difference between some damage and no damage. Do keep in mind that you still need to successfully hit, and spend the Rage on every hit you want to use the Gift with, and roll good damage for the Gift to have been worth it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Shattering Howl (3):</b></u></span> Wow. Gaia's true enemy. Shoddily made structures. This Gift lets you destroy stuff, stuff made by man's hands, how iconic, groundbreaking in fact. We've seen this Gift before a dozen times, this one costs Gnosis and the list of things it destroys is weirdly limited, don't take it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Hibernation (4):</b></u></span> A pretty complex and wordy Gift which lets you slip into hibernation for prolonged periods. Successes can result in a week, month, year or decade of hibernation, depending on expenditure. This Gift might be plot-relevant, but if it is, the party can't benefit from it unless they're all Rank 4 Lupus Garou. Which is silly. This falls into the classic &quot;should have been a Rite&quot; situation. Don't take this. If the ST needs you and the pack to be hibernating for 100 years, that will happen.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 04-02-2025, 11:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-07T12:48:28-08:00","id":1549050,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Ragabash Gifts (Changing Ways)</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Disguise Fetish (1):</b></u></span> A cost-free ability to make a Fetish look like a mundane object. Roll Manipulation + Subterfuge, difficulty 6, which makes a Talen or Fetish look ordinary. Any special ability to find Fetishes resists the successes you get. The result lasts for one scene.<br /> <br /> This is a handy way for a Ragabash to get away with having a Klaive. If I were STing and there were a Rank 1 Ragabash with this Gift who started with a 4-5 point Fetish, I'd be much more likely to go &quot;yeah, this works!&quot; especially if the story of the acquisition was interesting and well thought out. Many Gifts in this vein cost Gnosis, so this is refreshingly cheap to use.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Petal Float (2)*:</b></u></span> Roll a Dexterity + Athletics while falling, difficulty 6, every success reduces your fall distance by 10 feet. Pretty neat, though honestly a Gift which just makes you immune to fall damage by spending a Rage/Gnosis or equivalent could work fine.<br /> <br /> Say you were falling 100 feet, terminal velocity in W20's rules, you take 10 dice of lethal damage. So, every success with this Gift reduces your number of dice of damage by 1. For a Lupus Ragabash this Gift could combine very well with Hare's Leap. If you want to use this Gift aggressively, tackle a target off of a cliff. That said, at Rank 3 you get Catfeet which makes you fully immune to falls; for most Garou it would be better to get Catfeet. Personally I would refund the cost of this Gift, but the overlap does make this potentially <span style=\"color:#c0392b\">Red</span>.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Play Dead (2):</b></u></span> Stamina + Subterfuge, difficulty 5, no cost. In the course of one action you take a form resembling a corpse. If used outside of battle this makes you seem like you've been dead for a few days, if used in a battle this makes you seem like a fresh corpse. The effect lasts for one turn per success.<br /> <br /> Using the Gift in response to a fake 'killing blow' costs a point of Gnosis, which is the fresh corpse option. Either way you are stiff, you can return to breed form in the same way a Garou would upon dying, you lack a pulse and your body matches what you'd expect for a corpse in every way. Interestingly you could probably use this to pretend to be a Vampire, though timing would be important, and it's unclear whether the Gift ends if you move or 'break' the illusion by acting alive.<br /> <br /> Handy gift, neat effect, definitely worth taking if you like the concept and you have 6xp spare. Unfortunately many enemies of the Garou love to infest, desecrate, burn, devour etc. the corpses of their foes, so this could be a bit niche, but well worth using against many foes. Better to 'die' to Pentex so you can later rescue your kidnapped packmates rather than be captured with them, or killed at the scene of the battle.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Terrain Shift (3):</b></u></span> This Gift lets you create a means to travel through the wilderness (a small bridge or tunnel, thick thorns opening to let you through) or close a path behind him. It costs a point of Gnosis and requires no roll, which should be the case for more Gifts. If you want to open a path for yourself and then close it behind you, that costs 2 Gnosis.<br /> <br /> This is solid enough, and there's nothing stopping you from using this Gift for ambushes, guerilla tactics, escapes from wilderness crime scenes, accessing a glade or Caern or similar through a path that should be impossible, and so on.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Misdirection (4):</b></u></span> This Gift permits a Ragabash to essentially distract a target in a way which lets them get away with daylight robbery or murder. The cost is a single Willpower point, and the Gift utilizes the Fast-Talk mechanics from W20 Corebook. More Gifts should key off of core mechanics like this; Hare's Leap is a good example of a Gift which works with a basic mechanic instead of introducing a new one.<br /> <br /> The problem is that victims of fast-talking can simply spend a point of Willpower to overpower your nonsense. The core mechanic this Gift keys off is a bit... unsteady, and this Gift doesn't fix that. Since the basic mechanic is a little wonky, it's hard to rate this. Assuming that this Gift works as the flavour intends, and you can basically dazzle a single target while doing insanely provocative stuff like searching through their wallet or replacing their pills with poison... it's neat. But for a Level 4 Gift? Not good enough. Assuming you follow the RAW, where an enemy can spend a WP to go &quot;okay, no, ignoring this&quot; as per W20 Core, this Gift is sadly Red.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-07-2025, 05:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-07T14:00:28-08:00","id":1549051,"title":"","content":"Thank you for taking the time to go through these!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/448972/Hearthbound-Tribe-Supplement?affiliate_id=4046720\" target=\"_blank\">Hearthbound</a> and Project Director for <a href=\"https://www.tumblr.com/songoftrillium/729204622290550784/likes-dont-increase-visibility-to-others-please\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf: the Essentials</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27167-mundusartis","name":"mundusartis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27167_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"89"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-07T14:21:09-08:00","id":1549052,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mundusartis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549051#post1549051\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thank you for taking the time to go through these!</div> </div> </div> </div>Listen man I'm just farming Honour and Wisdom Renown with my Glass Walker Sept.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude02","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-07T14:22:50-08:00","id":1549053,"title":"","content":"Petal Float is basically Red for Lupus Ragabash because they get Catfeet next Rank which a permanent immunity to fall damage from falls of 100 ft or less (on top of bonuses to other actions). 6 XP for other Breeds taking Petal Float vs. 15 XP for Catfeet is at least a bit better of a case for Petal Float to exist; but I can't imagine any case where, if you have the choice, you wouldn't bank the 6 XP to go towards Catfeet instead because Catfeet is one of the more insanely good Gifts like that. Considering that 10L isn't that deadly to Garou, and at Rank 2 there's already some broad soak boosters you could use to up your soak instead of reduce the damage dice anyway, Petal Float could have really used something more active beyond reducing fall damage.<br /> <br /> I'd much rather have seen Patagia from the old Shadow Lord Camp list come back (since you can learn it from flying squirrel spirits too, you can avoid any socially risky entanglements from dealing with Bat's brood) where you can just grow gliding membranes between your arms and legs at will. The Children of the Bat also deserved a lot better at Rank 3 than just gliding considering how many spirits they deal with are either wind/storm based, or animals that can fly instead of just glide.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-08T02:27:22-08:00","id":1549070,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Theurge Gifts (Changing Ways)</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Hear the Silence (1):</b></u></span> This Gift allows you to hear when someone has a secret weighing on them, and the intensity of its importance from their POV. An example of the Gift in action would be using it when a Kinfolk has died. You might notice that another Kinfolk is carrying a very large secret - it would be easy to assume that the secret is that they killed their own kin, but it may be that there was some unknown tie between them, or they have stolen some inheritance, or they suspect who the killer is (rightly or wrongly) and isn't telling anyone.<br /> <br /> The Gift requires activation and costs a point of Gnosis, but has no roll requirement. This makes it useful and interesting; you can drop this Gift during a classic &quot;gather everyone in the drawing room&quot; scene in a murder mystery. Since the Gift gives you no clues as to what the secret is, but context will likely lead you in the right direction, and simply interrogating the target is a valid (though potentially difficult) option, this is pretty good. If it didn't give you a sense of how important or intense the secret is to the person this Gift wouldn't be that great.<br /> <br /> Simply because this costs 3xp, has no roll, and you can trigger this in a moment where you discover there is a doppleganger hiding in your pack and likely get led to the target, I'll call it a solid Green. Having this option can solve problems you have no other reasonable way of solving.<br /> <br /> <i><span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Eyes of the Lynx (2):</b></u></span></i> ... it's very weird to give Theurges cost-free xray vision in a supplementary book. Rolling Perception + Alertness difficulty 6 lets you see through solid matter for the whole scene, but only through a number of yards equal to your successes. So with 2 successes you'd be able to see a body buried under 6 feet (2 yards) of earth.<br /> <br /> This is exceptionally good but it absolutely needs a Gnosis cost. Or even Willpower. The Gift is simply too good as a power you can spam; you can see through walls and ceilings and floors, down into graves, through tombs, you can see enemies hiding behind cover, you can see enemies waiting to ambush you up in the trees etc. all without cost. Very strong, but in the 'so good it might be bad' category.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Blood Life (2):</b></u></span> This Gift lets you spend a point of Gnosis upon tasting blood (mostly from bite attacks) to get a vision of mystical importance relating to the target. It must be the target's own blood, so according to this Gift if you taste the blood of a vampire you'll get a faint memory from one of their many victims. The vision must be of importance and give you insight, and it could grant 1-5 bonus dice to the next check you make relating to the target (if the ST thinks it makes sense).<br /> <br /> So, for example, you might taste the blood of your mysterious Ronin ally and see a mighty wolf wearing a silvered crown with many cubs suckling from her. She growls at the runt every time he tries to feed or play, and the pitiful creature turns away from its family. If the Theurge then took a small risk and said &quot;... you know there aren't any Silver Fangs in our Sept, as it happens&quot; while trying to convince the Ronin to come and meet some allies it might add a bonus 1-5 dice to the relevant check. The tricky thing is that you need to ask to taste someone's blood for this whole scenario to play out, or bite them. So this will primarily be used against enemies... though the Gnosis cost means that you can't take Rage actions in the same turn.<br /> <br /> The Gnosis cost in combination with the other restrictions makes this Gift too niche unfortunately. But the value of a powerful vision relating to a plot-critical enemy could be hugely influential, which saves this from being Red.<br /> <br /> <i><span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Watchful Eyes (3):</b></u></span></i> Spending a point of Gnosis reduces the difficulty of all checks to impress, intimidate or persuade by (7 - Gauntlet). So, for example, in a typical village you might reduce your difficulties by 1 due to a Gauntlet of 6. The Gift works by attracting local spirits to judge proceedings, and they will typically act as a backup squad to the Theurge, accentuating what they say and do.<br /> <br /> If used in the Umbra this reduces the difficulty of such tests to 3 instead. Which... RAW, would turn a difficulty 10 test down to difficulty 3, regardless of the context. This Gift is just a bad idea and poorly written.<br /> <br /> For starters, the flavour is weird. The judging attention of the world of spirits influencing social tests is a great idea! The interaction with the Gauntlet is a great idea. But the execution is simply bad. My version would be that the Gift is a passive effect where if your Gnosis exceeds the local Gauntlet you gain a certain benefit, and if you want to gain that benefit when the Gauntlet exceeds your Gnosis you spend a point of Gnosis.<br /> <br /> I'm marking this Green because, strictly RAW, it's a very good Gift. Difficulty 3 social tests are great, this Gift would make you a social powerhouse in a Moot. But Theurges aren't meant to be social powerhouses. Perhaps if the Gift granted you a social benefit if you have a spirit backing you up? So if you're bringing the message of an Incarna you might get +5 to the relevant checks, and a weaker bonus with weaker spirits. Anyways. I wouldn't permit this Gift, not without significant reworking.<br /> <br /> <i><span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Spirit Blossom (4):</b></u></span></i> Spend 2 Gnosis, plant some part of your body (which can be fingernails, which is a little lame), and let it grow a spirit plant. Within a week the plant appears, and it's one of your choice. Once per day you can try to take Gnosis from the plant, rolling Gnosis, and getting points of Gnosis equal to your successes. If the plant is destroyed or ripped from its roots you suffer unsoakable Lethal equal to your permanent Gnosis.<br /> <br /> This... I don't like this. Conceptually, for starters. Gaia is dying. Getting Gnosis hard. Yes, a Gift which is only for Rank 4+ Theurges is a good start for such a Gift, and the investment concept works, the risk works, but ultimately this Gift is just functionally unlimited Gnosis for one PC so long as no one destroys this plant. It's one of those &quot;it's so great, or it's useless&quot; things which is too big a swing from one extreme to the other.<br /> <br /> The simplest way to deal with this Gift is just to not allow it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-08T05:03:05-08:00","id":1549075,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Philodox Gifts (Changing Ways)</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sense Trauma (1):</b></u></span> I would add a note that one success simply gives a sense of whether the target has a Derangement or not, and more successes gives more information. But the RAW Gift works perfectly well; you make a Perception + Empathy (difficulty 6) and success lets you smell things like trauma, split personality, magical insanity and so on. It also lets you identify people who are suffering from the effects of Delirium, precisely what type, which could be useful if you encounter humans who were recently exposed to Garou or Fera and have forgotten, but are still suffering from the after-effects of Delirium.<br /> <br /> Amazing Roleplay potential with allies and enemies. Determining that the &quot;Ronin&quot; you are chasing is in a Fugue state might lead to you uncovering that they match the description of a missing Garou from elsewhere in the region, one who disappeared when their pack was killed. Now you have a picture of someone who denied his memories and went into a fugue state because he watched his whole pack die, which might help you to help him. Great for the Auspice's job. I would also add this to the Children of Gaia list at my own table. Though I am biased.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Firm Stance (2):</b></u></span> This Gift is socially defensive, which is rare, and much-needed. When your Garou has a social test made against them, they can spend Willpower (this seems to be an OOC-driven decision based on the flavour of the Gift, not an IC one, so the ST shouldn't be saying &quot;but you don't know anything is up, why are you activating the Gift&quot;) to roll their Honour (difficulty 6), and every success reduces the successes on the social test against you. The Gift fails against enemies with higher Honour, including BSDs and Fera notably!<br /> <br /> Pretty handy, especially when it comes to overcoming lies and bullying. There will be Garou of higher Rank than you whose Honour is lower than yours due to different Auspice requirements.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Building a Legend (2):</b></u></span> When you gain a permanent dot of Renown, regain your Willpower. Pointless, especially in an Auspice which gets infinite Willpower regeneration. Reworking this Gift so that when you gain a dot of Renown you temporarily get set to 10 points of temporary Willpower would work.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Flow Like Water (3):</b></u></span> A very valid move when using Rage actions is to devote the main action on your turn to dodging and all of your Rage actions to attacking. This Gift makes that combination far, far better, and also keeps you alive by incentivizing it. Spending a Willpower when dividing your attention between attacking and defending (either with Rage or split dice pools) reduces the difficulty of both by 1 for the round.<br /> <br /> So, for example, if you were to dodge one attack and then make two attacks, all of them would be at -1 difficulty. -1 difficulty really adds up over time, it means you're inflicting more damage and taking less damage, and I'm a big fan. Solid green, and it's nice to see more combat-refining Gifts on lists other than Ahroun.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Rebirth (4):</b></u></span> Pointless and bad. You waste a bunch of health bringing an animal back to life... for one scene. NPC power, or perhaps better as a Rite. Perhaps this Gift could allow an animal Kinfolk to mate one last time and keep their species / lineage going, or you could restore a recently dead shark/elephant/tiger to fight for you for the scene, but you're taking a lot of damage to pull this off. The corpse must also be free of Wyrm taint and it can't be too old (Gnosis months).<br /> <br /> The whole thing needs reworking, either as a better Philodox Gift or as a Rite favoured by the Red Talons. That said, I'm not a fan of the 'necromancy' flavour of this Gift, even if the text implies this is all natural and cool, it will look like necromancy to the Garou Nation and would likely be rejected as such. In my book the Garou Nation will only put up with restoring things to life if those things are Garou, or Kinfolk at a push. See the whole Gurahl thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-08T12:13:35-08:00","id":1549088,"title":"","content":"Spirit Blossom is in a weird place for me because, from a perspective of just W20, you're completely right. From the perspective of WtA's long history of trying to do powers along these lines... it's the most nerfed version yet (seriously, if you think Spirit Blossom is bad for the themes of the game, look up The Rite of Spirit Brew from 2e).<br /> <br /> The balance of &quot;Gnosis should be kinda hard to recover for themes,&quot; and, &quot;Gnosis needs to be accessible so you can enjoy all the stuff that uses it,&quot; is a tough one. And one that really deserves to have more dials for different play styles. M20, for example, made the Quintessence economy of Nodes a dial based on the style of game the ST wants to run. The fact that WtA has consistently opted to make Gnosis the most &quot;management&quot; heavy advantage by making it relatively easy to top up in downtime, but hard to regain in the middle of important events, only makes the desire to get something better with the Gnosis economy that much stronger. <br /> <br /> Though there's a very simple fix for this that isn't just dumping the whole Gift: Have the presence of your Spirit Blossom make the meditation-for-Gnosis roll better. More frequent rolls (default is 1/week), reduced difficulty (esp. if you make it more personal like Auspice moon based increases to that), more Gnosis per success, etc. As you've said, link stuff back to existing systems if you can.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-08T15:44:36-08:00","id":1549094,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Galliard Gifts (Changing Ways)</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Canine Call (1):</b></u></span> With this Gift, a howl will be heard only by Garou and canines (dogs and wolves primarily). This works with the various Garou howls, and with Gifts, but doesn't work with Rites. The Gift costs one Gnosis to activate.<br /> <br /> Great quality of life upgrade and a very important lore detail to make the Garou Nation feel like it's feasible. City Galliards will definitely benefit from this Gift.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Battle Cry (2):</b></u></span> Roll Charisma + Expression (difficulty 6), and allied Garou who hear the call gain a bonus to initiative rolls (remember that initiative is rerolled every turn) equal to the successes.<br /> <br /> Adding a bonus to the initiative of every Garou in your pack, even a small bonus, could completely change the outcome of a fight. Adding a bonus to the initiative of many Garou could change the outcome of a battle or war. And if you're a competent Galliard chances are you'll be granting a decent bonus. With Pure Breed, a good Charisma and Expression, the right specialty and so on chances are you'll be providing a very good bonus consistently.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Ritual Tuning (2):</b></u></span> Do a performance to set the stage for a Rite. This Gift costs one Gnosis and requires no roll; you automatically add your dots in Performance (usually 1-5) to the dice pool for the Rite. This could massively improve the chances of success.<br /> <br /> If you're having difficulties with this Gift, cap the bonus dice so that they can't exceed the Rituals rating of the Garou performing the Rite. This would stop a dice pool of 2 (inept Theurge Cliath) spiking up to 7 thanks to a rock concert from a Performance 5 Galliard. There is a mention of Performance specialties which is garbled nonsense and hard to understand; it's unclear what benefit the specialty applies to the result of the Gift. An additional +1 die? 10s counting as two successes on the Rite? Very unclear. The Gift is strong even if you can't decipher this weird line.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Social Butterfly (3):</b></u></span> A pretty simple yet very wordy Gift where you can set yourself up to later have Contacts. Charisma + Etiquette/Streetwise, difficulty is variable, your successes give dots in Contacts which are temporary and more general 'generic' contacts (I can ask around) as opposed to having a solid high quality contact (I'll call this judge I know). I like that the whole pack benefits also; far, FAR more Gifts should benefit the whole party.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Words Like Wind (4):</b></u></span> This Gift lets you force something (a statement, short performance etc.) to go viral, being spread from person to person by word of mouth and also by technology, whatever means it has of spreading. If you make a social roll as part of what you're making go viral, that applies to everyone. Your lie with 10 successes is hitting every ear with 10 successes, typically, even if the people spreading it normally wouldn't sound convincing.<br /> <br /> People with Willpower that beats your successes on the test ignore your social roll, but they'll spread the message anyways.<br /> <br /> Also, you get Reputation (+3 dice on social rolls) in a 10 mile radius while the Gift is active. All this for a point of Gnosis! It's actually pretty good. With this Gift you can really mess around with information and identity and reputation in ways which wouldn't be possible without magic. You could massively convince people to go green, you could turn a whole town against the company bringing in the new mining venture, you could spread certainty that someone is a killer through a whole jury. This Gift lets you solve the kinds of problems a Rank 4 Galliard may actually be struggling with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-09T03:57:03-07:00","id":1549101,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Ahroun Gifts (Changing Ways)</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Rhythm of War (1)*:</b></u></span> Immunity to being knocked over, and immunity to being stunned by damage. These are both activated by spending a point of Rage, and maintained by attacking every turn. If you don't attack on a given turn you lose the benefit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If - after the soak roll - a character suffers health-level damage that equals or exceeds her Stamina rating, she\u2019s stunned until the end of the following turn. The stunned character can\u2019t do anything except perhaps stumble around in a daze, and attack rolls against her during that time reduce their difficulty by two. </div> </div> </div>You can overcome this kind of stunning by spending a point of Rage, so if you got stunned by damage once in the whole fight, this Gift could break even. It would need to happen multiple times to save you Rage. And the problem is that if you know you're in a fight, chances are you're in a Form which grants bonus Stamina. Not necessarily, but probably. And with that form, you'd need to be taking a lot of post-soak damage all at once to get stunned. With a Stamina of 3, and being in Crinos, you'd need to take 6 damage to get stunned. And if that happened you'd spend a point of Rage to ignore the stunning. Even with regeneration and healing, that's unlikely.<br /> <br /> Even with this only costing 3xp, it's still... just alright. With the cost and the attack-every-turn aspect, it needs to do more than occasionally stop you from being stunned. It also stops knockdown, which is inflicted by many Gifts and Charms, as well as many battle mechanics. Getting up from being knocked down costs an action, so you'd need to split your dice pool to do it. So this is handy... but the setup and possibility the Gift will stop make it not as strong as you'd hope. If you are constantly running into enemies who knock you prone, this could be Amber or even Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Pack Shield (2):</b></u></span> This Gift allows you to tank more effectively by putting out a 'taunt' mechanic which forces enemies to attack you in some cases, or suffer penalties if they attack others. Note that this doesn't just protect your pack, it protects ANYONE else that they might attack.<br /> <br /> Roll Charisma + Intimidation, difficulty equal to the <u><i>lowest</i></u> Willpower of all your enemies. Enemies who try to attack anyone but you suffer a dice penalty equal to your successes, so with 5 successes they'd suffer a -5 die penalty to attacking bystanders, allies and so on. They can attack you as normal, but you may well be very hard to hit. Enemies whose Willpower is lower than your successes (e.g. Willpower 4 vs 5 successes) can only attack you and no one else.<br /> <br /> Now, is this Gift very easy to abuse, RAW? Absolutely. But WTA's Storytelling section very swiftly points out that:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <i>There\u2019s only one true rule in Werewolf: There are no rules - just guidelines to make the game more fun for everyone.</i> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> So, if you use this Gift then run away, that's clearly not in the spirit of the Gift's. This Gift draws fire by you being a top priority threat. It supernaturally enforces that idea, and you don't have to be killing fifty enemies with a single swing of your claws, but if you behave like a total non-threat then the Gift isn't gonna work, is it. Could the Gift be better written? Possibly, but it might also be a waste of ink.<br /> <br /> Now, what are some fun uses? Well, make yourself very hard to hit (with Gifts, Dexterity or Wits, Athletics, the right specs) and make yourself very hard to damage (good Stamina, enter Crinos, hell you could wear Fetish Armour or use a Gift for bonus Stamina or soak). Being forced to attack the Child of Gaia with Luna's Armour, whose soak pool is 50% bigger than your damage pool, might make you less keen to fight.<br /> <br /> Story-wise, this Gift enforces a &quot;pick on someone your own size&quot; situation. Some bigots are messing with a Metis cub, or tormenting Kinfolk, or causing trouble for your ward? Well, now their only valid option is to attack you. Two people who hate eachother, but like you, are about to fight? Make it clear that if they start fighting, you will be intervening, and they'll be forced to get through you to get to eachother. A frenzying packmate is clawing at someone you need alive? Grab them and activate this Gift; now they can either attack you, or attack the person they're furious at with a huge penalty. Great Gift, fun mechanics, just make sure you don't bite off more than you can chew. If the enemy are spreading their damage, that might be for the best. Only bother drawing fire onto yourself if it actually helps!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Loyal Defender (2):</b></u></span> +1 difficulty to any attacks you make against your packmates, permanently. If mind control or Frenzy causes you to damage one of your packmates, you may roll Willpower with a difficulty equal to the successes on whatever made you attack your packmate, for example 5 successes on a Dominate effect would be difficulty 5. If you succeed the effect stops and you gain a point of Rage.<br /> <br /> The primary effect is... alright. +1 difficulty to attacks against your pack. But this includes willing ones, such as attacks (tackles, punches) against possessed packmates. The main impact is that you get this Willpower roll when you successfully damage an ally. This Gift freeing you from Frenzy, Thrall or supernatural mind control can be arranged by a packmate deliberately getting in your way. All in all, a pretty solid Gift!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Home Turf (3):</b></u></span> With this Gift you can establish an area of 10 x Rage yards which is your pack's 'turf'. This requires a Charisma (not Strength?) + Intimidation, difficulty of (8 - number of packmates present). If you succeed, attempts to intimidate you and your pack lose dice equal to your Rage (-5 minimum), and your pack's own intimidation attempts gain bonus dice equal to your Rage as well.<br /> <br /> The usefulness of this Gift will vary, but the mechanics are pretty neat. If your ST runs the kind of game where scaring challengers off solves problems consistently, this might even be <span style=\"color:#27ae60\">Green</span>.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Black Mamba Strike (4)*:</b></u></span> RAW, moving more than a foot or two takes up an action. So, if you run W20 strictly by the RAW, which the Gifts assume you will of course, this Gift grants you quite a big effect across the whole battle. With this Gift you can move up to your running speed for 'free' each turn, without using one of your actions. It costs a point of Rage, but across a whole fight it'll save you a decent amount of Rage.<br /> <br /> If at your table characters get something of a 'free move', this Gift becomes defunct. Unfortunately most of the obvious ways to repurpose this Gift, if you do that, don't really work. Double your free movement? The main thing is moving at all, doubling it won't often be useful. Say your run distance is 35 feet, or perhaps 37 because you're on all fours in Crinos, doubling that to 70 or 74 feet isn't nearly as important as going from 1-2 feet to 35. It's a difference of kind vs difference of degree sorta thing.<br /> <br /> Assuming you play the game RAW and moving requires an action or splitting dice pool, this is great. One Rage frees you from the mechanics for the whole scene. While this is great, and would be worth getting at any Rank, this Gift could easily be Level 1.<br /> <br /> You might be wondering about Tackles, Leaping Rake and so on. Yes, there are combat actions which move you. But they're quite specific; with this Gift you can do your full run and then attack however you like (shoot from best position, use your Klaive, use a specific attacking Gift, and so on). This Gift is also very useful in non-combat action scenes. Escapes, chases, deadly puzzles, challenges and so on.<br /> <br /> Green, assuming the game is run RAW. If it isn't, this Gift needs reworking entirely.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-09T07:23:31-07:00","id":1549102,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:#1abc9c\"><u><b><span style=\"font-size:20px\">WYRMISH GIFTS</span></b></u></span><br /> <br /> These Gifts, from Book of the Wyrm, need important context. If a Gift is inherently unsuitable for a non-corrupted Garou, as many of these Gifts are, it will be rated with a $ sign.<br /> <br /> Some of the Gifts are feasible for ordinary Garou however, as neither the Gift nor the teacher is inherently corrupt. Ratings will assume that you can learn and use the Gift. So, a powerful attack which is a must-have will be a Green, regardless of whether the attack involves a puppy in the Umbra dying.<br /> <br /> I will also make note of when BSDs lose access to a certain Gift. They could have learnt the Gift before falling, of course, but these differences are worth noting all the same. Furthermore, my reading of this chapter is that while a Glass Walker will learn Power Surge in a Gaia-approved way, another Homid can only learn Power Surge at the in-list cost (3xp, rather than 5xp) if they learn it in the corrupt fashion that a Wyrmish Garou would. This is because the Breed and Auspice Gifts are suggested to be part a constellation of corrupted blessings which are learnt in polluted and mercenary ways. Ultimately ask your ST. If you and your table think that this list adds Power Surge to the in-Breed list for all Homids, and not just Homids messing around with Wyrmish stuff, go wild!<br /> <br /> <u><b>Homid Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Wyrmish Homids do not get access to Calm the Savage Beast via their Breed list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Aura of Poison (1):</b></u></span> Focus for a turn and spend a point of Gnosis. No one will attack you unless you attack first. Creatures with Rage can ignore this, so for example a Garou will attack you normally, but an Ananasi won't! Naturally all conventional spirits have Rage, so this is of little use in the Umbra, but homid BSDs simply being able to sidestep combat with Vampires and many Fomori, for instance, is very impressive.<br /> <br /> Arguably this falls into &quot;so good it's bad&quot; territory as it is ripe for abuse. Imagine if Bone Gnawers got a Gift with this effect, and with a less Wyrmish flavour! But I would point out that being unable to do violence is different from being powerless. A Vampire under the effect of this Gift can still Dominate or Presence you, a Mage can send you away or trap you or try to purify you, Kinfolk can shout for Garou allies and try to block you inside a certain location. If you attack in response to any of these then you're giving up the benefits of the Gift. Assuming that the ST does not passively let abuse of this Gift happen (abuse as in abuse of the story!) it should be fine.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Power Surge (2):</b></u></span> See the Glass Walker Rank 2 list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Call the Rust (3):</b></u></span> See the Bone Gnawer Rank 3 list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Feast of Man-Flesh (4):</b></u></span> This is a passive buff to cannibalism. So long as you eat at least a pound of a human's flesh you can gain facts the person knew, or abilities they possessed. You can get a maximum number of benefits equal to your Gnosis. So a Gnosis 3 Garou could learn 3 things a dead human knew, or get 3 dots of abilities from the person, but not in excess of what they had. So a Garou with Etiquette 0 could eat the flesh of a socialite and get Etiquette 3, if that socialite had Etiquette of 3 or higher.<br /> <br /> I really like the flavour of this one as an 'evil power' and I would gladly give it to a Bone Gnawer or Younger Brother character if they were going down a 'cannibal Werewolf' route. Naturally since this breaks the Litany it's $. We also get a handy list of who is 'human' per this power and who isn't.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Metis Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Wyrmish Metis do not get access to Totem Gift via their Breed list. Given that Metis are key to the Dancers I was hoping for more Gifts, to be honest, and some very cool ones! Perhaps a Gift where you weaponize your deformity or spread it like a plague.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>$ Bleed (1):</b></u></span> Produce big quantities of fake blood. You can use this to play dead or frame someone, or pretend you have terrible injuries. The blood is fake, a spiritual ichor. This Gift's teacher is the only thing that makes it $. The Gnosis cost makes this bad, so don't bother trying to find a Gaian equivalent for this. Yeah, you're lying in a giant pool of blood, but this won't fool Garou senses.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Nobody's Bastard (3):</b></u></span> Become unrecognizable. With this Gift you're just 'a stranger' to people, and that's how they remember you, not as 'you'. This costs Gnosis and requires a Manipulation + Stealth (difficulty 7) but there is no way to resist and no way to overcome it, save for things like Auspex or special Gifts, or anything granting immunity to illusions. Very neat, great concept, great execution. The teacher is what makes this corrupt; given that this Gift is pretty good it might be worth trying to find a purified alternative. A spirit of abandonment teaches it currently, but presumably a spirit of anonymity could achieve the same.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Visceral Agony (3):</b></u></span> See the Rank 3 Black Fury list. A great addition to any Metis PC if your ST will let you.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Lupus Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Wyrmish Lupus get all the typical Lupus Gifts, plus a few more.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Ways of the Urban Wolf (1):</b></u></span> Taught by Wyrmish Elementals, this Gift grants a -2 reduction to the difficulties of Perception, Stealth and Survival rolls relating to hunting intelligent prey (humans, Vampires, Garou) in urban environments. If a regular Lupus could learn this from an ordinary elemental, or a city father, or perhaps from Lupus Ancestors, that would be a great tool for Gaia. Solid Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Thousand Teeth (3):</b></u></span> We have a Gift Gaian Garou can explicitly learn! Taught either by Scrags or Shark-spirits, this Gift makes you develop loads of bonus teeth. Spend a Rage, roll Stamina + Primal-Urge (difficulty 5), get +3 damage to bite attacks, lasting for one bite per success. Great preparation power for bite-lovers, which suits the Lupus well.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Instincts Unbound (5):</b></u></span> Spend Rage and Gnosis, roll Wits + Primal-Urge against a difficulty of the target's Willpower. For one day per success they give into every instinct they have. If they want to eat, they eat, if they want to kill, they kill, they become raw unfiltered id, with no respect for law or consequences or the litany. It's interesting to consider whether a Metis born as a result of this Gift (two packmates follow their instincts with no care for the consequences) would lead to punishment of the parents. Still, this is a NPC power if ever there was one, though it's not fundamentally unsuited to a PC either.<br /> <br /> If a target wants to resist they can reduce the duration by 12 hours for every point of Willpower they spend. Which makes this an alright way to shed a target's Willpower before you try to brainwash or indoctrinate them anyways. An impactful Gift, but perfectly average for Rank 5.<br /> <br /> ================================================== ============================<br /> <br /> The important takeaways for a standard game are that Lupus get a bonus biting Gift and that Metis might get Visceral Agony.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-09T07:56:27-07:00","id":1549103,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Ragabash Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Wyrmish Ragabash do not get access to Luna's Blessing from their Auspice list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Bestowing the Predator's Shadow (1):</b></u></span> Get at least a tiny bit of yourself onto a target (spit, blood, fur etc.) and then spend Gnosis and roll Manipulation + Subterfuge (difficulty 6). For one hour per success the target looks and sounds like you. Their smell is unchanged, however. Amazing Gift with lots of incredible uses. You can let someone else take your punishment while you escape, let someone get shot up by their own security guards, swap appearances with another Ragabash in your Pack for scheming purposes, you name it.<br /> <br /> A very fun use for this Gift would be to use it on a Gaian Garou and then let their own pack murder them. Scent may well save them... with a PC pack. NPCs may well take a 'slaughter first, ask questions later' approach given the history of the Garou. Plus, if a villain has this Gift, they can play mind games. If a BSD who the party know has this Gift is all &quot;oh thank Gaia you're here - there was this intruder, and then he was gone, but my voice sounds all- did he do something to me?!&quot; then the players might think they're talking to a Kinfolk or Garou who has had the Gift used on them, rather than the BSD themselves.<br /> <br /> Anyways this Gift makes me a little smug about my Metis being blind, since he relies on people's scents, though if you combine this Gift with any kind of scent manipulation you could cover for that weakness. If Ragabash of the Garou Nation could master this Gift it would be a huge improvement for the whole Auspice given that it is Level 1. It is taught by Phantasmi, the remnants of dead Fomori, so perhaps an equivalent Gift could be taught by the spiritual remnants of dead Kami?<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>$ Touch of the Eel (3):</b></u></span> Terrible. Spend Rage, 3 dice of Lethal at range (only for a conductor, mind!), 4 dice of Lethal close up. Difficulty 7 to soak, sure, but it'll get soaked. And if it doesn't, it's 4 damage maximum. Would still be terrible even as a Level 1 Gift.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Cassandra's Blessing (4):</b></u></span> Touch the target, spend Gnosis, roll Wits + Subterfuge against a difficulty of the target's Willpower. For one hour per success no one believes anything they say, no matter how good the evidence. Great flavour, extremely fun Gift, helps solve the kinds of problems that Rank 4 Ragabash need to solve. Nocturnae are not simply spirits of darkness or night, but corrupted spirits which turn dreams to nightmares. They are possibly fragments of the Wyrm's nightmares, and feel almost like evil Chimerlings. Ultimately if a Garou were to try and find a pure version of this Gift they might have to resort to a being like Coyote.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Silver Reprisal (4)*:</b></u></span> Spend Gnosis, no roll required. For the rest of the scene, anyone hurting you using silver (silver bullets, a Klaive etc.) suffers silver-like injuries in return. Individuals suffering this Gift take one level of automatic Aggravated damage, which is treated as silver for those vulnerable to it. This is for each attack, so long as the attack causes damage. Anyone who is injured by this Gift must roll Stamina, difficulty 8, or drop what they're holding. Even if they keep ahold of their silver hammer, however, they'll have to suffer a +2 difficulty spike from the injuries to their hands.<br /> <br /> Great Gift, no real notes. It's niche, but it requires no roll and lasts for a scene. My delight at this Gift doesn't make it Green, since so few enemies wield silver, but leaving an asterisk to note that it could be superior in a game full of Klaive-wielding enemies. If you needed a Gaian version of this Gift, perhaps a fire spirit, or even a servant of Helios, could help. The teacher for the Gift is a Furmling, an Elemental of Balefire.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Patience of the Wyrm (5):</b></u></span> Taught by a Nexus Crawler of all things. This lets you delay a non-damaging Gift by spending a point of Willpower and setting a timer (e.g. 8 hours) or condition (e.g. &quot;when she next transforms into Homid&quot;). Infinite combinations exist and make this an incredible Gift. If a Gaian alternative could be taught by a Spirit of Patience, or perhaps an Avatar of Gaia, it would be a great weapon for the Garou Nation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-09-2025, 03:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-09T08:22:57-07:00","id":1549104,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Theurge Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Wyrmish Theurges do not learn As In The Beginning, as they have their own version. Mother's Touch remains on their list but it is very rare for Wyrm-corrupted Garou to learn it.<br /> <br /> <u><b>$ A Thousand Voices (2):</b></u> A hidden Garou may use this Gift to evoke images of allies approaching from the shadows. For each point of Willpower spent an illusory ally is created. All bystanders roll their Perception + Alertness, difficulty of 4-9, lower if it's a crowded area (dense forest) and higher if it's a sparse area (an open plain). More successes from the bystanders, the targets of the illusion, weirdly makes the illusion worse. 1 success makes Garou, 3 successes makes Fera allies, and 5 successes adds Lovecraft Monsters.<br /> <br /> This Gift feels more like a NPC power than anything else. Not worth rating annoyingly, and not worth taking without a rework.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Poisoned Gauntlet (2):</b></u></span> Spend time concentrating, spend Gnosis, and roll Intelligence + Occult against a difficulty of the local Gauntlet. Anyone crossing the Gauntlet within 100 x Gnosis meters takes one die of Aggravated damage for every success on the roll. The poison lasts until the next sunrise or sunset, and it is resisted by the Gift Resist Toxin.<br /> <br /> This is a handy offensive Gift for Theurges to use against Garou and Fera primarily. Unfortunately the damage is going to be more impactful against Mages than it is against Garou. If you can repeatedly force someone through the Gauntlet with another Gift, that works. Alternatively you could use this near a place important to your enemies to interfere with their work. If the damage were unsoakable, or got worse if you repeatedly cross the Gauntlet in that location (dose of poison building up) then this would be even better. Solid Amber. A Gaian equivalent which turns the webs of the Gauntlet razor sharp would be a great project for a Glass Walker Theurge, but would probably be easier to use in a place with a strong Gauntlet, rather than a weak one.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Feast of Essence (3):</b></u></span> Spend a Willpower when dealing the finishing blow to a spirit's Essence. Regain 1-3 Gnosis depending on the spirit. This is taught by a Thinbones, but with the right work a clever Theurge might be able to work out an equivalent Gift taught by Englings. A Gaian equivalent might need to be Level 4 rather than 3, since the necessary purification of Essence from slain Banes would make the Gift more complex and 'powerful'.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Prelude to the Apocalypse (6):</b></u></span> Equivalent to As In The Beginning; basically just a rename with Wyrmish spirits. See the Rank 5 &amp; 6 Theurge post.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-09-2025, 03:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-09T08:53:28-07:00","id":1549105,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Philodox Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Wyrmish Philodox do not get access to Fangs of Judgement as part of their in-Auspice list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Acid Talons (1):</b></u></span> An amazing claw buff, and I love claws. This Gift, which costs a point of Rage, gives +1 damage to claw attacks and increases the difficulty of Soak against the Garou's claws by 1. If I could get this on my boy, I would. As an Ahroun it would be in-list for him, however the Gift is taught by an Acid Elemental and this is more the domain of the Wyrm. A purified version of this Gift which uses poison, however, would be quite interesting.<br /> <br /> <u><b>$ Smell Fear (1):</b></u> This Gift costs 1 Gnosis for every hour it's maintained, and alerts you if anyone uses Sense Wyrm or another sensory Gift. There's also some nonsense about making a roll against the 'target' which comes before the rest is explained which makes the whole Gift just not scan. Regardless, the fact that you pick up on ANY sensory Gift, and Smell Fear doesn't tell you whether it lets you tell the difference between Sense Wyrm and other senses, means this is impossible to rate. Poorly written and in need of a rewrite.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Omen Claws (5):</b></u></span> Spend Gnosis, roll Rage. For one turn per success your claws are effected by the Gift. Anyone taking damage from your claws while the Gift is active must make a Willpower roll, difficulty of your Gnosis + the damage of the attack, maximum 9. If they fail they become incapacitated and haunted by terrible visions, and I like the flavour described for those. A victim can roll to escape the visions once each turn, but with a Rank 5 BSD on top of you chances are you're either dead or wishing you were by the time you wake up.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Galliard Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Wyrmish Galliards do not get access to Howls in the Night as part of their in-Auspice list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Allies Below (2):</b></u></span> Summons a tremor with Gnosis. 1 Gnosis knocks people prone if they can't get 3+ successes on a Dexterity + Athletics check, 3 collapses tunnels, uproots trees and opens sinkholes, 5 collapses walls and damages buildings. Collapsing stuff inflicts 5 dice of damage only. Booooooooo.<br /> <br /> Weirdly, the absolute best use of this Gift is spending 1 Gnosis on it. Pricey, but knocking every enemy within 10 meters to the floor is great. Even with a good Dexterity + Athletics, or Willpower for spirits, you won't necessarily get 3+ successes. Amber simply for the ability to waste actions for a huge crowd of Garou who otherwise would be tearing you to bits. Just hope you have a way to get away while they're on the ground!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Howl of the Hunter (2):</b></u></span> A replacement for Howls in the Night. Costs Gnosis, and your intended target can't sleep if they're in range. There is a check, and mechanics for difficulty spikes from the terror, but there isn't anything worth doing a deep dive into. If keeping your target awake for 9 hours helps, that's great, but often you'd prefer your target be asleep so you can ambush them! It's a fitting power for the BSDs but not worth taking unless your ST is highly simulationist or very eager to show the impacts of the Gift playing out.<br /> <br /> Is this better or worse than Howls in the Night? Depends on the situation, certainly. One targets a specific person, the other targets all Wyrm creatures. So it'll depend on the problem and the story of the Chronicle.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Shadows of the Impergium (2):</b></u></span> See the Rank 2 Red Talon list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Howl of Death (4):</b></u></span> See the Rank 4 Red Talon list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Madness (5)*:</b></u></span> See the Rank 5 Metis list.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-09-2025, 03:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-09T09:02:01-07:00","id":1549106,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Ahroun List</b></u><br /> <br /> Wyrmish Ahroun do not get access to Full Moon's Light as part of their in-Auspice list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Acid Talons (1):</b></u></span> See the Wyrmish Philodox list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Horns of the Impaler (2):</b></u></span> Grants horns of varying appearance. These horns inflict Strength + 2 Aggravated, seemingly in any form, and can be used twice each turn without splitting pools. So what this basically means is that if you use them for your main attack action, you get to attack twice in that action. That's pretty great! Yeah, it's a shame that loads of claw and fang Gifts don't apply to these horns, but a free bonus attack is a free bonus attack.<br /> <br /> Downsides of this Gift are pretty obvious. Nasty for the Veil, mess with headgear, and Garou consider horns to be a feature of prey. Still, the free attack is definitely worth it, especially at Rank 1. If later in the game you come to rely more on claws or bites, being able to grow horns like these when you're muzzled and handcuffed will definitely be useful. Solid Green.<br /> <br /> Though this Gift would likely be mocked by many Garou, it would make a fitting Metis Gift, if Ram, Bison or similar were to make the right arrangements with a clever Theurge or similar. Such a Gift, turning a 'mutation' for 'prey' into a powerful weapon, would suit the Metis of the Garou Nation perfectly.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Tar Shadow (2):</b></u></span> Your shadow becomes a tar pit! That's absolute genius and makes this Gift a great weapon. Spend Gnosis, and for the rest of the scene anyone attacking you up close has to pass a Difficulty 8 Dexterity + Athletics or get trapped in your shadow. While trapped a target can't move and their dodges all fail. This doesn't make a Garou harmless, but certainly much less dangerous. Creatures trapped in the tar of a shadow can roll to escape using their Strength + Athletics (difficulty 6) using an action.<br /> <br /> Making a pure version of this to get onto the Shadow Lord and Ahroun list for Gaian Garou would be huge.<br /> <br /> If you find this Gift problematic at your table, consider making it so that once a target has broken free of the tar-shadow they can't be bound again by it for the rest of the scene. This means that if someone gets trapped and escaped they can then press the attack without just getting trapped again. There's also the question of someone anticipation the Gift and deliberately avoiding the Ahroun's shadow... which is hard to judge since it'll be very ST-dependent. The mechanics imply that you have a tarry shadow trapping anyone who attacks you up close, that's simple enough.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Visceral Agony (3):</b></u></span> See the Rank 3 Black Fury list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Strength Without Limit (5):</b></u></span> Spend 2 Rage, suffer a level of Aggravated, and gain +1 to all your physical Attributes as well as gaining +2 (+3 with the +1 strength) to the damage of your Brawl attacks. Very solid, but pretty boring for a Level 5 despite the cool flavour of roiding out with Wyrm energy bursting from your muscles. Across a giant battle +1 to STR/DEX/STA will add up, as will the bonus damage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-09-2025, 03:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-09T12:15:45-07:00","id":1549109,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 1 Black Spiral Dancer Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Bale Armour:</b></u></span> Activated for 1 Willpower, lasts for a scene, and applies a 1 die penalty to all incoming attacks. Successful Brawl attacks lead to the attacker taking a level of Bashing damage. Relatively cheap resource, lights a big area, draws a lot of attention, slight protection, harms attackers (though not ones with soak and regen). This is decent enough to be Green, since it's cheap, and the bonus stacks well with other defensive measures.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>$ Bale Aura:</b></u></span> Costs 1 Willpower, lasts for a scene, lights a good area and those in the light will Frenzy with one fewer success on a Rage roll. This is a great way to push people towards Thrall of the Wyrm. Handy for corrupting others but not that useful generally speaking.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Bane Protector:</b></u></span> Essential for BSDs and apparently taught during their initiation. This Gift allows a Dancer to petition Banes for aid. Spend 1 Gnosis, Manipulation + Leadership (difficulty 6), more successes means a longer duration for the 'agreement' with the Bane. Given the number of Banes in cities and their sheer strength, this is pretty great.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Repress Taint:</b></u></span> No Gnosis cost! Roll Perception + Subterfuge, difficulty 7. Successes increase the difficulty to detect taint, one per success, to a maximum of 9. This compares very favourably with the Level 5 Skin Dancer Gift Mask Taint, which BSDs also get for some reason.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Resist Pain:</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Philodox list. BSDs with this Gift may feel the pain but not suffer any downsides from it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Resist Toxin:</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Bone Gnawer list. Absolutely necessary for survival in many Wyrmish locales.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sense Wyrm:</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Metis list. Find friends!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Shroud:</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Elder Brother list. European Garou only encountering this Gift from BSDs, and then meeting the Elder Brothers for the first time and seeing them using an identical Gift, probably didn't help.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Spiral-Shadow Dance*:</b></u></span> Passive +3 initiative when in poor lighting. Solid Amber so long as you don't have any favourite Gifts which generate light.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-09T13:17:22-07:00","id":1549111,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 2 Black Spiral Dancer Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Ears of the Bat:</b></u></span> Your ears become strangely shaped in all your Forms. When you choose to activate this Gift, your ears unfold to be like a bat's, and you gain echolocation. You can sense the size and shape of everything within the range of your hearing, and you can beat supernatural forms of hiding (e.g. Obfuscate) with a Perception + Occult. Great incentive to use Lupus.<br /> <br /> <u><b>$ Grave Claws:</b></u> Your fangs and claws become anchors to the Low Umbra, guaranteeing that individuals you slay in that scene will become Wraiths. The only way to free such a Wraith is to kill the killer, or to journey into the Shadowlands. This costs 1 Gnosis and the transformation of your natural weapons lasts for one scene. The Wraith is mystically bound to you and can't harm you, but it's haunted by its darkest emotions (i.e. its Shadow would be very powerful and it'll suffer quite badly in terms of the Wraith mechanics). Feels like a good plot point for a Silent Strider PC!<br /> <br /> Going to leave this unrated since it's more of a NPC power, and if a PC uses it the result will be 100% dependent on how the ghosts wind up being run. Mostly this feels like a trophy of how mean you are. It could stop someone from rejoining Gaia as an Ancestor Spirit, for example, which could hurt the Garou Nation, but that's a very tenuous benefit. Arguably this could get you loads of Renown in the Garou Nation, as you can show off your wailing entourage of Garou Ghosts. So yeah, if you want to make your collection of anguished dead Garou your 'thing' this could be the inspiration of your weird Deed Name, sure, but it's more aesthetic than mechanically useful.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Hidden Killer:</b></u></span> A slight upgrade on Hidden Killer. See the Rank 1 Red Talons list. If you have a piece of a Garou you may alter your victim so that investigation reveals that other Garou, and not yourself, as the killer. For example the exact shape and pattern of the claw marks may change to one more indicative of that other Garou's height, weight and strength, while any fur left on the victim would match your framing target.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Submit:</b></u></span> A slight upgrade on Kneel. See the Rank 2 Black Furies list. Targets resisting the power suffer a -2 penalty to dice pools which can only be avoided with Resist Pain. This fixes the main problem with Kneel; with that Gift it either works (stuck kneeling) or does nothing (they spend a point of blood, or Gnosis, or equivalent and just avoid it). Here if it 'does nothing' the pain of resisting the impulse to kneel still impacts the scene with the -2 penalty.<br /> <br /> How good is a 'free' -2 penalty? Well that really depends. There is still an activation roll, and this Gift takes up time, but if you can get the -2 penalty to go off it could pay huge dividends across a fight. A universal -2 debuff which you can apply to almost any enemy, and not just in fights, is pretty good. Just remember that spirits don't take wound penalties, and Resist Pain (and similar effects) will make this ineffective. Kneel remains poorly written and messy, but Submit is definitely an upgrade, just not enough to be Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Wyrm Hide:</b></u></span> Spend one Gnosis, roll Manipulation + Leadership (difficulty 6), and get a bonus point of Soak per success, up to a maximum of 3.<br /> <br /> Weirdly, this Gift also lets you treat Lethal as Bashing so long as you don't botch the soak roll. Which... mostly does nothing! It might help if you need to heal normally without Regeneration, or if healing which only works on Bashing is applied, or if you half Bashing for some reason, or regenerate it more quickly for some reason... all in all a bit odd, but not entirely useless.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-09T13:50:01-07:00","id":1549112,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549102#post1549102\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Ways of the Urban Wolf (1):</b></u></span></div> </div> </div> </div>I gotta admit this one baffles me as a Wyrm-Gift... nobody would bat an eye to see this as a default Lupus Gift with a more neutral description that matches its actual effect, or something in a few Tribe's lists (BGs and GWs obviously, but it fits for the RTs very well too). It's nearly OP for a Rank 1 Gift too. I took this on a Homid GW Ragabash for 5 XP and took a big Renown hit for convincing a Theurge to use their Gifts and Rites to force a Wyrm Elemental into teaching it... and it was totally worth it. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Thousand Teeth (3):</b></u></span> </div> </div> </div>OK... maybe my love of bite builds makes me more picky about these Gifts... I actually get a bit annoyed at that limited uses for a Rank 3 Gift; esp. one that has such an obvious supernatural effect anyone can see worked into it. If it did something more than just extra damage dice (which you can get from plenty of other sources, Gnaw might be a funky Gift but you get the utility side of it and +2 damage for a scene), or it was just a scene length effect, I would like it a lot more. Even with the activation roll being diff 5, you can easily use up all your boosted bites in a 2-3 rounds of attacks if you're a front-liner Ahroun.<br /> <br /> Stack this up against Razor Claws, a Rank 1 Gift... +3 damage limited number of attacks, or +2 damage/-1 attack diff for the whole scene. Thousand Teeth his harder to hide being active, and doesn't take a full turn to activate, but it's a Rank 3 vs. a Rank 1... that's very underwhelming.<br /> <br /> Now, yeah, for a bite-build you still want it, because 1 Rage is cheap and all that bonus damage is great, but it's far from a top priority.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's interesting to consider whether a Metis born as a result of this Gift (two packmates follow their instincts with no care for the consequences) would lead to punishment of the parents. </div> </div> </div>Given that the setting has been described where at least the more traditional Garou will still punish female Garou that get raped for having a Crinos-born child, I can't imagine much leniency because of supernatural mind powers leading to the situation. Obviously the more progressive Tribes aren't going to throw punishment Rites down for this, but only the Children of Gaia and Black Furies are described as having support networks for Garou in this sort of situation to help them instead of just not being harsh with them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-09T13:54:48-07:00","id":1549113,"title":"","content":"Also: I think the Wyrm Hide lethal/bashing divide is an old edition holdover. In some editions healing in combat is automatic for Bashing and rolled for Lethal, in W20 it's rolled for both. If you didn't roll for Bashing, you could see the automatic appeal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-09T13:57:13-07:00","id":1549114,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549103#post1549103\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><u><b>Ragabash Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Wyrmish Ragabash do not get access to Luna's Blessing from their Auspice list.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's new, and helps with setting cohesiveness, I think.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>$ Touch of the Eel (3):</b></u></span> Terrible. Spend Rage, 3 dice of Lethal at range (only for a conductor, mind!), 4 dice of Lethal close up. Difficulty 7 to soak, sure, but it'll get soaked. And if it doesn't, it's 4 damage maximum. Would still be terrible even as a Level 1 Gift. </div> </div> </div>The intended use of this Gift might be the way that the electrified guy in Ninja Scroll uses his powers - with a lengthy wire garrote. In any case, the Glass Walkers unquestionably get a better deal using electricity, since I think that they can do that and make it agg, so, still red over here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-09T15:15:44-07:00","id":1549115,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> one of the themes of Book of the Wyrm is this idea that Glass Walkers and Bone Gnawers are good in cities, but the 'scabs' are the natural home of the Spiral Dancers. So while I would love to explore purifying that Gift to give Gaian Lupus the same deal, I also think it does make sense that the 'hunt people through the city like it's a concrete forest' Gift is BSD, at least in its origins. I actually like the idea that not all of their Gifts are explicitly evil, &quot;evil&quot; isn't the summation of their whole deal, it's just part of it. On top of being super evil, and at home in cities, they also have themes of rebellion, insanity, mutation and discarded things.<br /> <br /> RE 1000 Teeth, it's definitely a problem in terms of drawing attention, and the bite marks will be weird, but I'd also point out that people being ripped open by fangs draws attention and looks weird. 9xp for +3 damage is solid enough to be Green in my book, simply because it doesn't conflict with anything else. If it cost Gnosis or took a full turn to set up or whatever I'd be much less favourable towards it. That said I wouldn't prioritize it over, say, boosting Brawl.<br /> <br /> And yeah, Garou 'justice' often focuses on what happened rather than why it happened. People seem to be punished for being victims as a matter of routine. One thing I like about Changing Ways is that it highlights this very well. Cliaths apparently get savage beatings for using the phrase 'hulk out' to describe transforming. 'Disrespect' to many Garou isn't a case of what is profound (behaving in a way which suggests you have no respect for your powers), but what is superficially obvious (using modern slang to describe transformation). For instance I don't like it when every single Tribebook in an edition has a sidebar where the POV character goes &quot;yeah unlike the other Tribes we're actually pretty chill about X, don't believe what people say&quot;. Which leaves a &quot;majority&quot; of 1-2 Tribes doing the evil thing we're supposed to believe is the status quo. But I digress!<br /> <br /> RE Bashing vs Lethal, that's good to know! In W20 there's a few important differences between them but they won't often come up under normal play conditions for a Werewolf PC. Still, somewhat important distinction all in all.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar23_3.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"23\" data-vbnamecard=\"23\">Saur Ops Specialist</a> yeah the Eel Gift is just awful, I get the impression it was either copy + pasted or just never thought through, which often happens with the Gifts for dead or antagonistic groups. <br /> <br /> Oh and just as a general PSA the Wyrmish Ahroun post is up, but it got hit with a &quot;spam&quot; strike when I edited it. Gonna just wait for it to be unlocked by the mods.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-09T15:41:29-07:00","id":1549116,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 3 Black Spiral Dancer Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Beautiful Lie:</b></u></span> This is a mass memory alteration Gift which is specific to atrocities and terrible crimes. Costs one Gnosis, Wits + Subterfuge (difficulty 6 typically), on success people develop vague deluded memories which cover up what really occurred. Arson becomes a tragic accident, a slaughter by a pack of Garou becomes an attack by feral dogs, and so on. With 3+ successes there's even evidence!<br /> <br /> Pretty strong, and helps BSDs with the kinds of things they consistently need to do. Given that BSDs are meant to be a secretive Tribe which is difficult to learn about, Gifts like this explain how they manage to stay under the radar.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Claws of Corrosion:</b></u></span> This is 'one of the greatest Black Spiral Gifts' according to Book of the Wyrm, and I can easily believe it! With this Gift a Garou can turn a spirit into a Bane with claw attacks. On a successful attack you must spend a point of Willpower, Gnosis and Rage and make a Intelligence + Occult (difficulty 6!) roll. The spirit gains Corruption points equal to your successes, slowly loses them over time, and if its Corruption ever exceeds its current Essence then the target begins transforming into a Bane. This means the Gift is great against spirits which are already badly injured, or already quite corrupt from exposure to Wyrm taint or recent upheaval in its 'domain'.<br /> <br /> Having a Gift which fundamentally changes the nature of a spirit at Rank 3 is something Gaian Theurges would be very envious of! The Gift is expensive and the impacts aren't immediate, but the Renown possibilities are fantastic. You could enter BSD legend as the hero who turned a mighty servant of Gaia to the Wyrm's service.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Foaming Fury:</b></u></span> Anyone you bite, as a passive benefit, must roll Stamina (difficulty 8) or go into Frenzy. If you spend a Gnosis right after attacking you get to pick whether the target flees or goes berserk. Great ability, extremely handy, but do note that you can't turn it off, and it does influence your behaviour. Assuming you like the idea of your BSD acting like a rabid dog or twitchy fox in his mannerisms, this Gift is great.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>$ Gift of the Tainted Totem:</b></u></span> This has all the problems with Banish Totem, but comes with the complication of replacing the Totem with your own. That makes it better (you taint your enemies and cause them to panic as a Wyrmish spirit takes root in their Pack's soul) and also worse (the benefits of having a Totem remain available, including whatever unique abilities your Totem comes with).<br /> <br /> A clever Theurge hit with this Gift will figure out the Ban of the Totem and break it as fast as possible in the hope of getting the 'tainted totem' to leave as fast as possible. Regardless, this is more of a NPC power, and while it'd be very interesting to experience this as a player, it isn't much use to a PC, even in a BSD game, unless it is a highly psychological one with an emphasis on slowly breaking down a cast of recurring 'heroes' to villainy.<br /> <br /> Purifying the underlying spirit magic of this Gift so that instead of forcibly pushing your Totem onto an enemy you could temporarily lend your Totem to an ally would be pretty interesting! That sort of thing is normally the work of Rites, but picture a situation where two Garou packs work together and absolutely need to share some power or connection. If I were playing an Elder Brother Theurge who encountered this Gift I'd probably be drawn towards understanding it and breaking it down to something useful, for good or ill.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Ichor Blade:</b></u></span> Hand Blade, but dripping with green Wyrmish poisons. Costs one Rage and transforms an arm into a poison blade. Dexterity + Melee to hit, difficulty 6, and on a hit your target will suffer a -2 penalty for one turn per success. This can be negated by Resist Toxin or Resist Pain, but most foes won't have those. Handy for a melee BSD, and the painful toxin is a nice angle, but the Gift suffers a little given the plethora of incredible melee weapons in the game. That said if you want to leave your Brawl at 0 for some reason, this could be handy.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Patagia:</b></u></span> Reflexively unfurl flaps which allow you to glide on the air. The details of the Gift include high speed and options to speed up or gain altitude with Rage. Inferior to true flight, but the fact that it happens with no cost when you're falling or require it for some reason is pretty great! I really can't see why a non-&quot;deforming&quot; version of this Gift can't be learnt by Metis or Lupus from a Flying Squirrel spirit, so if you're desperate for this Gift perhaps you could look into it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-10T08:22:15-07:00","id":1549127,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Black Spiral Dancer Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Crawling Poison:</b></u></span> After a successful claw or bite attack you may roll Gnosis vs the target's Stamina (both difficulty 8). If you beat your target they lose Regeneration for an hour per success.<br /> <br /> The problem here is the Gnosis roll. It'll block you from spending Rage in the same turn, so now you're giving up a lot to block regeneration. Situationally useful against a foe who is extremely hard to damage and who is going to take a while to kill due to high Soak or unusually powerful regeneration. Typically you're better off just rushing them down.<br /> <br /> If this Gift works against the Remain Active version of Regeneration it's better, and useful against Garou enemies, but the difficulty 8 Gnosis still an issue; that's a tough dice pool especially at that difficulty!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Howl of the Bane*:</b></u></span> See Call of the Cannibal Spirit. This Gift, however, allows a BSD to summon any Bane with an Essence of 30 or lower. This makes it somewhat more defined than Call, which summons a spirit which lacks any stats in W20 to the best of my knowledge.<br /> <br /> The ability to call upon ANY Bane with an Essence of 30 or lower is pretty neat, all things considered. This would let you summon a 30 Essence Furmling (see below), a 30 Essence Scryer (excellent spy), a Scrag (send it to possess a mortal), and so on. You could even get a weak (only 30 Essence) Nexus Crawler! Or you could evoke a Bitter Rage and send it after a Pack to send them into wild frenzies. The flexibility here is great.<br /> <br /> The problem is that the roll is still difficulty 8, the summoning takes 3 turns, it costs Gnosis and Rage, and if you botch the summoned spirit tries to kill you. Still very limited, unfortunately, but more worth the risk than the original. If for some reason you can reduce the difficulty of the roll this could be Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Hungry Rust:</b></u></span> Spend 2 Gnosis and put a Bane inside a machine. Intelligence + Crafts, difficulty variable depending on complexity. If someone uses the tool (shoots the gun, starts the car etc.) it fuses with them horrifically. This can be resisted with a Wits + Occult roll on the part of the wielder, requiring more successes than the BSD. Humans are stuck fused, Garou can rip free.<br /> <br /> The ability to fuse a Garou enemy into the driver's seat of his car so he's a horrible part-werewolf-part-car monster is hilarious, but the intense setup for this Gift makes it tricky to use. Requires setup, and it's expensive, and the roll might be very hard, and the results are... well the results are HORRIBLE for the character who you target, but that doesn't necessarily make the power that great. The Gift sounds quite fun to use, if you're playing someone evil, but it isn't that great in terms of utility.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Open Wounds:</b></u></span> See the Rank 4 Shadow Lord list. The flavour of the Gift changes slightly but the mechanics are identical.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Stolen Hide*:</b></u></span> This turns ghosts enslaved with Grave Claws into armour. Each ghost worn as armour grants two additional levels of Bruised health which go 'first' when you are attacked. Any ghost whose health levels (worn by you) are removed is permanently destroyed.<br /> <br /> How good is this? Well assuming that you can use Grave Claws to set up before a big battle this could be immensely powerful. The problem is that Grave Claws costs Gnosis and Stolen Hide costs Gnosis. For 2 points of Gnosis you can, with rolls and setup, gain 2 health levels. Now you could, in theory, stack up 1000 bonus health levels using 500 enslaved ghosts. That would cost you 1000 Gnosis, but you could do it.<br /> <br /> So, in essence, this Gift is entirely at the mercy of the Gnosis economy like no other. And while you can regenerate damage done to the 'stolen hide' of ghosts, it's very limited. If you take one point of damage, you could regenerate that. If you take two, that ghost is used up and gone, and those health levels are now missing. It's powerful but hard to utilize well. It also does mean losing the ghosts you've enslaved, though chances are this is the best use for them, and a good threat to use against the ones you do keep!<br /> <br /> Call it Amber, potential to be Green if you get access to insane quantities of Gnosis, and call it a day.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Summon Wyrm Elemental:</b></u></span> See Call Elemental, which is covered in the Rank 4 Elder Brother list. This Gift is first given to BSDs in the Corebook under the name Call Elemental, with a simple note that BSDs will summon Furmlings, H'rugglings, Wakshaani or Hoglings instead of the typical results. Summon Wyrm Elemental refines this slightly, changing the name of the Gift, keeping the same mechanics, and noting that many BSDs don't bother to placate the spirit they summon, instead stopping with the summoning roll and then doing what they wish while the elemental causes havoc. This is a handy use for the Gift, but of course the best results require placating the summoned spirit.<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Hogling (Smog)</b></u></span><br /> W3, R8, G7, E18-40<br /> Charms: Create Wind, Updraft<br /> <br /> Not useful unless you need to stop or start a storm. Just a nasty air elemental.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Furmling (Balefire)</b></u></span><br /> W5, R10, G5, E20-40<br /> Charms: Blast (Fire)<br /> <br /> Identical to the Fire Elemental, but missing &quot;Create Fires&quot; as a Charm, which is likely an oversight. Its Rage and its Blast make it horribly dangerous. As a Bane with a Rage of 10, and embodying the concept of balefire and toxic fires out of control, simply summoning one of these and running away laughing is probably a great move for a BSD.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>H'ruggling (Sludge)</b></u></span><br /> W9, R3, G5, E17-40<br /> Charms: Umbraquake<br /> <br /> Basically just a worse Earth Elemental. Earth gets Armour, this doesn't, and for some reason its Rage is worse.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Wakshaani (Toxin)</b></u></span><br /> W3, R7, G7, E17-40<br /> Charms: Blighted Touch, Flood<br /> <br /> Blighted Touch has a chance of temporarily or permanently making someone damaged by the Bane become dominated by their worst personality traits. All in all this is a Water Elemental with the healing, Willpower and Gnosis reduced. A small buff to Rage doesn't really make up for it!</div> <br /> <br /> Notably none of these Spirits have a Gift which explicitly allows them to spread corruption, but that's fine. The important thing is that a flood, storm, earthquake or fire started by these guys should be corrupt and do the Wyrm's work, in the most obvious sense.<br /> <br /> Despite this Gift still being good, the options are significantly worse. A Sludge or Balefire Elemental may well be useful in a fight, but some of the best things about Call Elemental are lost here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-10T10:47:50-07:00","id":1549131,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 Black Spiral Dancer Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Balefire:</b></u></span> A neat supernatural ranged attack with no cost. Roll Dexterity + Athletics, base difficulty 6, treated a bit like using a gun. Each success on this test inflicts a point of Aggravated damage which can only be soaked at difficulty 8!<br /> <br /> How good is this? Well a typical claw attack is Dexterity + Brawl, inflicting Strength + 2 + Attack Successes dice of damage, roll damage at difficulty 6, soak that damage at difficulty 6. How Balefire compares to a conventional attack will ultimately depend on your Strength, and the Soak of the target, and whether you have an applicable spec, and so on. All I can say is that Garou can roll HUGE damage dice pools thanks to a Strength of up to 9, +2 and beyond from their claws, and loads of successes on the attack. Swapping that out for an attack where your successes just automatically inflict levels of damage is a handy option, but keep in mind that you lose a few things in exchange.<br /> <br /> The damage you inflict is hard to heal and comes with horrific mutations. Not only do you have to heal the Aggravated damage as one normally would, you have to physically remove the damage with fire, acid, surgery and so on, which will likely come as a surprise to a Theurge or Child of Gaia trying to use Mother's Touch on the injuries as usual.<br /> <br /> Assuming you use this ability correctly it's a solid Green due to its utility and lack of cost. It's best leveraged against enemies whose soak makes them relatively resistant to normal attacks, since the +2 difficulty to soak has a much larger potential impact against a large dice pool.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>$ Cloak of Anthelios:</b></u></span> The idea of a stolen piece of sun magic is very fun to me. This is basically a corruption of sunlight, and the name of a brand of suncream. Spend 1 Gnosis, effects last for a scene, and unlike the original Gift this doesn't hurt Vampires, as they are servants of the Wyrm.<br /> <br /> While using this Gift you gain +2 dice of Aggravated damage on your Brawl attacks, anyone facing you has +2 difficulty to attack tests, and if you deal significant damage you can put a Wyrmish mark onto your victims which makes them reek of Wyrm taint. Pretty great all in all!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>$ Mask Taint:</b></u></span> See the Skin Dancer list. I rated Mask Taint as Amber for Skin Dancers. For BSDs... well it's somewhat redundant given Repress Taint, which is Level 1. But note that while Repress Taint makes it harder to detect your taint (up to a maximum difficulty of 9), this Gift fully represses it. The roll is still hard, and the Gift is pretty mediocre... but it being Amber seems about right.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>The White Howl*:</b></u></span> A Gift where the spiritual teacher is dead and the Gift is now only passed down Garou to Garou sounds pretty cool! This Gift would be far better for Gaian Garou, for what it's worth. This is a massive AOE which inflicts unsoakable Lethal damage equal to successes on a difficulty 6 Charisma + Primal-Urge. The only creatures harmed are Wyrm-tained creatures, including the BSD using this Gift. The negative effect on Gaians is that they all lose a point of WP per success, and if they reach WP 0 they go into Harano.<br /> <br /> Potential uses for this Gift are fights with other servants of the Wyrm, or with anything you've tainted. You could also use this to destroy a huge swarm of Banes which is opposing you. The potential of throwing a whole Moot into Harano is also neat. The trouble is that if you get more successes than you expect you can just immediately kill yourself, or your pack, or your allies or servants.<br /> <br /> This Gift mostly exists to show how awful the loss of the White Howlers is, in a way, since the first thought you have when reading it is &quot;this Gift would be great in the hands of Gaian Garou&quot;. So, that's the ideal use case of the Gift. If you restore the White Howlers chances are you'll get a lot of Renown as a result (sounds like a fast track to Elder or Legend if ever there was one!) and this Gift would be the most important thing to get once you reach Elder status.<br /> <br /> How balanced is this Gift if you're playing a White Howler in modern times? Well the cost is 2G 2R, and the damage is &quot;only&quot; Lethal. But it's unsoakable, and a Charisma + Primal-Urge is relatively easy to boost up. There are a number of Gifts, specializations, Merits, Backgrounds and Rites which could influence your dice pool or difficulty. Say you optimize your dice pool to 10+ and you get the difficulty down from 6 to 4... you're looking at ~6 unsoakable lethal to every Wyrm-tainted creature within range of your howl. Barring harming innocents who have been tainted temporarily, this is amazing. What about the Willpower cost for Gaian Garou within earshot? Well if you've restored the White Howler Tribe then that shouldn't be an issue! If you can use this for Gaia, I'd call it a solid Green. The cost balances it, somewhat, but it's absolutely a horrific nuke befitting a Level 5 (or 6!) ability.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-10T12:15:53-07:00","id":1549133,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Homid Gifts (Wyld West)</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Jam Gun (1):</b></u></span> Only works on guns, and only on a single gun at a time. Costs Gnosis. Requires a roll, Manipulation + Crafts, though the roll is only difficulty 5. The target gun is jammed for one turn per success. Naturally this is inferior to Jam Weapon, which effects all weapons present and has no cost, but this Gift may be slightly superior if you only want to take out a single enemy weapon. This Gift could absolutely save lives, and it could swing a fight, so it's not terrible. The specificity is handy. And comparisons to Jam Weapon, arguably one of the best Gifts in the game, are a tad unfair. Call it Amber, nice and easy.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Spirit Brand (2)*:</b></u></span> Lets you explain the whole &quot;getting around with horses&quot; situation, but given that, it would make way more sense to make this a Level 1 Gift. Still, this comes with a wide variety of bonuses. Usable on any animal (other than wolf kinfolk). Lasts for multiple months. Causes the animal to serve you loyally and follow orders. Tracking the animal is at +3 difficulty and it gets +2 Stamina. The details of the Gift's execution are all pretty reasonable (Intelligence + Animal Ken difficulty 6) and it only costs one Gnosis.<br /> <br /> Assuming that having a horse or equivalent is very important to you, or that an animal companion like a dog, wolf, or similar is critical to your character, I'd call this a solid Green. But in most situations it's distinctly Amber.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Weave of Steel (4):</b></u></span> This one is interesting! It can strengthen any manmade object or structure, so for example a coat could become armour or a rusting disused bridge can take the weight of a heavy carriage.<br /> <br /> The roll is Strength + Crafts (difficulty 7), the cost is a point of Gnosis. Most objects gain +1 health level per success, while worn items grant +1 soak per success. There is no duration given, so it seems this is permanent! Do note that &quot;armour&quot; created with this Gift grants no protection against Bashing, full protection against Lethal, and half protection against Aggravated, generally speaking. I'd wager that this Gift would be best used on real armour to 'fortify' it, but if I were STing I would limit the bonus soak you could grant to +1, or perhaps +2 in extreme cases, especially since you can apply the benefit permanently! This Gift would be best used on Fetishes and sacred relics of the Garou to ensure that they are never destroyed, assuming they are valid targets (manmade, using crafted materials).<br /> <br /> <u><b>Metis Gifts (Wyld West)</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Rattler's Bite (3):</b></u></span> Spend a Rage before biting. If the attack hits and deals damage, the damage after soak is doubled! There is some risk here, but the ability to inflict a very heavy blow with a single bite is pretty great. Stackable with other biting Gifts and superior to options like adding some damage. The ability to possibly one-shot a dangerous enemy, especially by ambush or with setup from your team (blinded, immobilized etc.) is great.<br /> <br /> Note that this is Rank 4 in W20. Not sure if this is an error or an intentional buff to the Metis list.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Lupus Gifts (Wyld West)</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Healing Sleep (3):</b></u></span> This Gift lets you sleep off any damage, including Aggravated, which is pretty neat. Spend a Gnosis, roll Stamina + Primal-Urge, after a full 24 hour sleep you're fully healed. I'd be kinder to Gifts like these if they could apply to the whole Pack!<br /> <br /> If this were free I'd be quite happy with this, after all, using this Gift could save your pack a point of Gnosis since less healing is needed. But this Gift requires Gnosis anyways!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-12-2025, 06:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-10T12:30:25-07:00","id":1549134,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Ragabash Gifts (Wyld West)</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Spider's Song (1):</b></u></span> This Gift is Level 1 in the Wyld West rather than Level 2. See the Rank 2 Ragabash List. Telegraph lines are treated like landlines, and you don't need to understand Morse code to use the Gift. Stronger because it only costs 3xp and you can get it at char-gen, weaker because messages are much less common and important now.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Man With No Name (2):</b></u></span> Leave behind fading or even no memories of yourself when you move on from a town. The Gift's mechanics are a little involved, but the net result is that more successes means less of a memory of you is left behind. The problem is that Garou move in a pack; unless the Ragabash rides a horse into town and all of the other Garou remain in the wilderness? This Gift would make a Lone Wolf Ragabash concept stronger, but nothing in the game supports that! The Gift is free to use, so adding an optional Gnosis cost to apply it to your whole Pack would make good sense.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Ghost Town (5)*:</b></u></span> Hide a town or settlement of some kind, making it and its occupants invisible. People will remember the place as abandoned. Locals can't see outsiders, outsiders can't see locals. The duration of 'one Story' is very rare and pretty useful! The ability to hide a settlement of Garou Kinfolk, or a tribe of natives, from those who would harm them is most key. The cost of 2 Willpower and duration of a Story means you could use this ability in perpetuity to keep a family or town hidden forever. Handy, if you're playing a stationary campaign, or a story where protecting innocents and settlements, or indigenous peoples, is very important.<br /> <br /> Could be anywhere from Red to Green depending on the Chronicle.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Ahroun Gifts (Wyld West)</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Trick Shot (1):</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Glass Walker list. This Gift is slightly better for Ahroun given their higher starting Glory and their role's emphasis on gaining Glory as a champion of the Garou.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-10T12:58:14-07:00","id":1549135,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Black Fury Gifts (Wyld West)</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Song of the Seasons (1):</b></u></span> Ignore any temperature extremes which aren't attacks. Lasts for a day and costs 1 Gnosis. Any attack roll based on temperature is made at +1 difficulty against you. Pretty unusual for a Rank 1 Gift, but not gamechanging.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Dispel the Golden Waste (4):</b></u></span> This Gift lets you banish gold to protect an area from prospectors and gold rushes. Roll Gnosis, difficulty 8, on a success all of the gold in an area (no size given) turns to dust and escapes to some unknown location. Assuming that you are aiming to preserve the sanctity of nature in the Wyld West, this Gift is handy, but it falls firmly in the &quot;should have been a Rite&quot; department.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Fianna Gifts (Wyld West)</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Prospector's Luck (3)*:</b></u></span> Costs 1 Willpower, roll Perception + Alertness (difficulty 7), scan an area of a half mile radius per dot of Gnosis.<br /> <br /> More successes give more info, and you can only use this Gift in a given area once. Which, oddly, means that you're actually better off using this with a lower Gnosis score. With 1 success and 1 Gnosis, you know for a fact that there are precious metals somewhere in a half mile radius. With 1 success and 5 Gnosis, you know there are precious metals somewhere in a 2.5 mile radius. That's 25x more area to cover. What this means is that the Gift wasn't very well considered unfortunately!<br /> <br /> Red by default, given that it has a cost, can only be used once in an area, and the poor interaction with Gnosis increasing. Could be Amber or even Green if you find yourself in an unusual &quot;get as rich as possible!&quot; campaign where you try to become a Rockefeller.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-10T19:55:17-07:00","id":1549147,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549134#post1549134\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Ghost Town (5)*:</b></u></span> Hide a town or settlement of some kind, making it and its occupants invisible. People will remember the place as abandoned. Locals can't see outsiders, outsiders can't see locals. The duration of 'one Story' is very rare and pretty useful! The ability to hide a settlement of Garou Kinfolk, or a tribe of natives, from those who would harm them is most key. The cost of 2 Willpower and duration of a Story means you could use this ability in perpetuity to keep a family or town hidden forever. Handy, if you're playing a stationary campaign, or a story where protecting innocents and settlements, or indigenous peoples, is very important.<br /> <br /> Could be anywhere from Red to Green depending on the Chronicle.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Rock Ridge, Rock Ridge... splendid!&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-11T05:52:02-07:00","id":1549153,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Iron Rider Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> While this effects every Tribe, and not just the 'Iron Riders' of the Wyld West, it's worth noting here that certain Gifts are rare or not yet invented in this time period.<br /> <br /> <u>Rare Gifts:</u> City Running, Control Complex Machine, Diagnostics, Signal Rider (telegraph lines only)<br /> <u>Unavailable Gifts:</u> Cybersenses, Jam Technology, Plug and Play, Tech Speak<br /> <br /> The losses effect Homids and Iron Riders mostly, however there are alternate and variant Gifts to take their place.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Well-Oiled Running (1)*:</b></u></span> Costs Gnosis, protects objects from entirely natural wear and tear for one day per success. It's a difficulty 7 Stamina + Crafts, and you can't effect anything bigger than a covered wagon.<br /> <br /> How good is this? Well, it only works on natural stuff. Say your pack are sheltering inside a covered wagon during a horrific storm which could easily kill an ordinary human, and for them, would at least leave them injured, scattered, and acting without rest. The Iron Rider could spend a Gnosis, do the difficulty 7 roll, and if they succeed the wagon will be 100% immune to the storm. That makes this Gift very ST-dependent. Not everyone gets how dangerous weather is, or how much sand and dirt and tiny insects mess with your stuff. If the ST remembers that the Wyld West is very hostile to Weaver-work... this Gift could actually be very good! Though the 'Gnosis tax' which comes with keeping your stuff in good shape could become a little annoying.<br /> <br /> I'm going to charitably call this Amber, but it could very easily be Red if the hazards you encounter are only supernatural or manmade.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Iron Coat (2):</b></u></span> This is a full-on upgrade of Steel Fur (see the Rank 2 Glass Walkers post). While I don't think this Gift is as good as the other 'armour' Gifts, I do think it is better written and should be used instead of Steel Fur.<br /> <br /> The important changes are that Iron Coat gives you +1 soak, since you get +2 soak for 1 success, rather than +1 soak for 1 success, and from there it increases by 1 soak per success. That bonus point might be handy. The Gift also functions for Homids and Glabro, just not fully. A Homid subtracts 2 from the bonus soak, a Glabro subtracts 1. So, for example, if you got 5 successes on the activation for this Gift, you'd get +6 soak in furry forms, +5 soak in Glabro, and +4 soak in Homid. The ability for a man or woman to turn the tiny invisible hairs covering most of their skin to steel is very fun to me, and I love the image of an Iron Rider's beard turning to metal wire! The sneaky use case of using this Gift before shaving to get big quantities of metal is also quite funny to me, though presumably the metal will turn back to hair later.<br /> <br /> All this said, the Gift still suffers from the +1 difficulty to all your Dexterity rolls. That's pretty terrible given that Dexterity is dodging, hitting, and by successes carrying over, damage. Big improvement, but still weak. Like I said with the original, increasing the difficulty of Dexterity rolls is a cardinal sin and you'd need a huge benefit to overcome it. If for some reason you're stuck crouching at an engine, doing some stationary work, and you need the bonus soak while you're unable to dodge at all... well this Gift could have benefits. Tied up and completely helpless? Might as well get +4 soak! Fighting in a place with no room to dodge? Might as well get +4 soak. Can't see the enemy at all, so you can't attack them or dodge? Might as well get the soak. So with all that said, this Gift stays Red because on top of the Dexterity debuff, you also have to roll at Difficulty 7 to activate it. So you might get very little bonus Soak in exchange for the loss in mobility.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Pulse of the Railroad (2):</b></u></span> Allows you to listen to anything happening along a railroad by pressing your ear against part of it. Perception + Science, base difficulty 4 (within 20 miles). The difficulty increases for every 10 miles. So if you wanted to hear something happening 80 miles away you'd need to make a difficulty 10 roll. Another problem is that 1 success isn't immediately a perfect result, you need more successes to hear everything clearly over the 'chatter' of iron spirits. Like many other Gifts this one is afraid of handing the player a win - if you invest in this Gift it should do its job reliably, and being able to overhear the prospector arguing with his brother about the 'devil voice' in the cave should just be a given if you pass the roll with 1 success.<br /> <br /> Another problem is that you have to select a specific area for eavesdropping. The Gift should let you listen along the track sequentially, working your way down the line until you reach something of interest, perhaps at a greater difficulty, but still. Assuming you play this Gift exactly as written, it's unfortunately quite poor. The limited locations it can spy on, the fact you're limited to sound, the fact that you have to pick a location to spy on and can't work your way along the track, the fact the difficulty spikes horribly if you're 60+ miles away (not a big distance for a wolf!), and the fact that you need more successes to get a clear listen over the spirits... it is sadly Red. But I can see a very strong Gift in this with a few little tweaks.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Repel Metal (3):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis, roll Strength + Science difficulty 7, and more successes means heavier ferrous objects get knocked back. Since this only works on magnetic metals it won't repel a silver object. Despite this being spirit magic. Personally I would make repelling iron and other ferrous metals free, and repelling any other metal costs Gnosis since you need to 'charge' it. The distance you repel objects is fixed, and the Gift only effects a relatively small area. Sadly this is pretty bad! Yeah you could theoretically knock the guns from the hands of a huge gang of enemies... but they do get a resisted Strength to hold onto them, and you're paying a Gnosis. Compares very poorly with, say, Jam Weapons.<br /> <br /> Ultimately this Gift falls victim to having a Gnosis cost when one really isn't necessary. It would also be much improved if you had the option to push or pull depending on your needs! Having a cowboy's gun fly from their hand straight into yours is a great image.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Gift of the Iron Horse (4):</b></u></span> While touching railroad tracks your run speed is doubled and you gain a +1 to your Physical Attributes. This means that a Lupus Garou with a base Dexterity of 5 can run very, very fast with this Gift, thanks to the 2x speed and the +1 Dexterity, as well as bonus Stamina to keep them going for longer.<br /> <br /> This is very niche but also pretty fun! If you can draw a fight onto railroad tracks then the +1 to your Physicals is pretty great. I would say that you could easily make this a Level 1 Gift though. Plus, as an Iron Rider, chances are that you'd be taking the train, or a wagon. If you're going to make a Gift like this, I want it to give your Hispo form, while on rails, the dragging power and speed of a steam train! The Gift has very little ambition in the way it's written, so sadly it gets a Red.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-11T06:05:55-07:00","id":1549154,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Red Talons Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Dust Squall (3):</b></u></span> Amazing focused debuff, definitely worth grabbing in a modern game. Spend 1 Gnosis, roll Charisma + Primal-Urge (difficulty 6). A dust devil goes after a single target for one turn per success. Anyone hit by the dust devil is at +2 difficulty to all rolls, and any rolls relating to sight or communication get set at difficulty 10. Hitting the most dangerous foe with this Gift prior to an ambush or assault is a great idea.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Stargazer Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Threads of the Weaver (1):</b></u></span> Lets you peek into the Penumbra, but only to see the work of Weaver-spirits. Why not just Peek, or Step Sideways? Well this is easier... kinda? Peeking is a Gnosis roll against a difficulty of the local Umbra. This Gift is a Perception + Enigmas, same difficulty. So the dice pool is likely better. But the result is niche; all you get to see are the webs and wires and railings left in the wake of Weaver-spirits. You also get to make a Wits + Enigmas (difficulty 8) to try and figure out what those trails might represent or signify, like &quot;a powerful Weaver-spirit is marching its forces Westward&quot;.<br /> <br /> All in all, you're better off peeking. If this Gift's difficulty were fixed at 5, sure, I could see the point. Because the big problem with this Gift, and with trying to spy on Weaver-spirits by Peeking, is that the local Gauntlet will be pretty strong in a place where the Weaver is at work. Not certain, but likely. So unless you're on the wild hilltop looking down on the fort or train station, chances are your difficulty will be quite high anyways. If you're going to write a Gift specifically to spy on the Weaver, ignoring the high difficulty you'd normally face from a thick Gauntlet is probably quite important!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Counting Coup (4):</b></u></span> A great pacifying attack. Originally created by the 'Pure Tribes', this Gift has been learnt by the Stargazers from native Ancestor Spirits.<br /> <br /> Dexterity + Brawl/Melee, and spend 1 Gnosis if you hit. Instead of dealing damage you completely 'break' your opponent's will to fight, assuming you get more successes on the attack roll than the target currently has in temporary Willpower. So, for example, with 7 successes on a Dexterity + Brawl you could spend 1 Gnosis to pacify an enemy with a temporary Willpower of 6. This even ends Frenzy! Enemies with no minds are unaffected, but this works on humans, animals, Garou, Fera and so on.<br /> <br /> Presumably this Gift doesn't work on spirits at all, as they don't have a temporary Willpower stat. If it does, use their permanent Willpower. Great Gift, well worth buying, but should probably also be on the Children of Gaia list for Wyld West!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-12T10:36:58-07:00","id":1549192,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Elder Brother Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Counting Coup (4):</b></u></span> As above.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Eyes of the Thunderbird (5):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Gnosis, roll Manipulation + Occult difficulty 7. For every success you create a bolt of lightning inside your eyes. You can either release all of these bolts at once, or release them as you go, but no matter how many you release at once it counts as an action. The bolt hits automatically and deals 5 dice of Aggravated, so an average of 2-3 Aggravated before soak.<br /> <br /> If for some reason you have lightning inside your eyes when the scene ends, you take 5 dice of Aggravated for every unused bolt. Also, while your eyes are full of lightning, you can only see your intended target and no one else.<br /> <br /> It is not 100% clear whether getting hit by 2 bolts would mean soaking 5 dice of Aggravated twice, or 10 dice of Aggravated once. If it were the latter then it would be called out specifically, however, so we are left to read that getting hit by 5 bolts would mean 5 x 5 dice of Aggravated damage, so say 2, 4, 4, 4, and 0 damage. If the target can't soak Aggravated at all that would be 14 Aggravated, utterly obliterating them. If they can, and that seems quite likely, it could be anywhere from 0-14 Aggravated.<br /> <br /> An Aggravated damage ranged attack is always nice, and being able to shoot a bunch of bolts all at once with no roll to hit is great, but the cost being Gnosis, the roll being difficulty 7, and each bolt dealing 5 <i>dice</i> of Aggravated... this feels like a great Gift to smite weak enemies, and one which will be much, much weaker against powerful foes. And at Rank 5, powerful foes are likely the main problem for a PC in a WTA game.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Younger Brother Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Dust Storm (2):</b></u></span> This Gift summons a pretty large dust storm, which is actually pretty neat, unlike many weather Gifts which are higher level and worse. If you're in the wrong place for a sandy dust storm then the Gift will substitute leaves, bark, snow, detritus and so on, though this makes it harder to use.<br /> <br /> Roll Gnosis, difficulty based on where you are, minimum 5 in the desert and maximum 10 in a city. On a success you cover an area of up to a mile! Anyone inside the effect halves their Perception dice pools, so for example a Perception + Alertness of 10 would be reduced to 5.<br /> <br /> The storm messes with machinery, puts out small fires, and will sometimes make larger fires grow unexpectedly due to the sudden winds. This is very much an era-specific Gift in some ways, given that desert and plains are more of a battleground in the Wyld West than they are in modern games. The lack of a cost is nice, needing to roll Gnosis can be very nasty for Homids.<br /> <br /> Assuming a good Gnosis pool, such as 3+, and assuming the right setting... this is pretty neat. Could be as bad as Red depending on the location, but Amber feels fair for a rough average. The trouble is that this Gift lasts for 2 turns per success. On a Gnosis roll which may be very, very difficult. I don't care about changing the weather if it lasts for less than 30 seconds. In fact that's more of a Veil risk, since freak weather starting and then immediately stopping is weirder than it carrying on for a few minutes or hours. If this lasted longer it might be Amber, but as it stands it has to be a Red.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Burning Tumbleweeds (3):</b></u> This Gift requires tumbleweeds, which actually don't arrive until the 1870s based on my VERY brief research. These species are invasive, by my understanding, but I welcome being corrected if there were any present in the American South prior to introduction of foreign plants.<br /> <br /> Anyways, let's assume that tumbleweeds exist in the world and in the penumbra, because otherwise this Gift won't have been invented yet. The cost is Rage, the roll is pretty easy, but the problem is that we're just told that the weed becomes a rolling fireball and heads in the direction of your choosing due to a stiff breeze. We don't get told what kind of damage it'll do on contact or how far it travels.<br /> <br /> All in all easier to leave this unrated. Having a mining station suddenly be set on fire, with subsequent explosions, is very neat. But we have no mechanics to roll that scenario out. And &quot;I use the Gift, and now the whole camp is on fire&quot; isn't a very satisfying interaction! It'd be more interesting to have the party gather up tumbleweeds, set them on fire, and use a spirit to blow them through town.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Counting Coup (4):</b></u></span> See above. We're told that the Tribe have all but stopped using this Gift, marking their increasingly violent and isolationist stance in the face of the Wyrmbringer invasion.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Tornado Rider (5):</b></u></span> Summon a tornado and ride it! The Gift doesn't let you ride natural twisters unfortunately, which would be a great passive benefit, and very useful in some parts of the Umbra.<br /> <br /> Spend 2 Gnosis, roll Willpower, difficulty 6. Nice dice pool, nice difficulty. The twister then forms on the following turn and makes landfall two turns later... which is a little slow, but still, the results are worth it. You pick the rough direction the twister goes in, and you get to ride it if you wish. It will destroy any object &quot;within reason&quot; which it touches, and inflicts 5 automatic levels of damage to any living thing in its path, who might take even more damage if they're thrown into or off something.<br /> <br /> Finally, the gift ends after a minimum of 4 turns since forming, 3 base +1 per success, so with a Willpower of 8 you might expect roughly 4 successes, resulting in 7 turns of twister mayhem.<br /> <br /> Simply being able to cut a path of destruction through a huge gathering of humans or spirits, destroying carts and wagons and buildings as you go, is very powerful indeed. The image of a Werewolf riding that twister is also extremely intimidating! I'd rate this as a poor method of travel. The tornado will move at 10-20 mph based on very brief research, but possibly as fast as 60 if the wind is blowing the way you want to travel. If the tornado is very fast, and you get a good duration, you might travel 0.7 miles before it fades, or about 1.1km. That makes this a very handy mobility option within a small area, and it's a good way to deal with enemies and obstacles in your path, leaving a trail of destruction for a pack or other allies to follow!<br /> <br /> I'm only rating this Amber because it's expensive to buy at Level 5 and expensive to use at a cost of 2 Gnosis. It's definitely better than the average Level 5 Gift.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-12T14:20:43-07:00","id":1549208,"title":"","content":"Approaching the end! I appreciate the messages asking about this thread. If anyone knows of any W20 Gifts I've missed, or any Gifts from prior editions which seem like they'd complement W20 particularly well, please let me know.<br /> <br /> To the best of my knowledge the only Gifts left for me to review are in Wyld West - there are a few Storm Eater based ones which are unlikely to be super relevant but I'll do for completion. If there are any Gifts tucked away in weird corners of books that I missed please let me know, like I say, especially if they're W20. I've decided I won't go out of my way to do the Singing Dogs ones because... it's a &quot;Tribe&quot; of like 5 people, you know? I felt insane enough doing the Gifts of the offshoots of the major Tribes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-12T15:35:12-07:00","id":1549212,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:14px\">So, just for fun - who has the best list. Rough and ready approach is to list Green Gifts by Rank, splitting into the various lists. This is based on my own assessment of a huge number of Gifts, and I'm not accounting for overlap between Lists. This is also very character optimization brained. Don't take this to mean that a Homid + Ragabash + Bone Gnawer is invalid or won't be fun or powerful, it's more that such a character will have fewer high-quality Gifts in-list, and will either focus away from Gifts, or get some out-of-list Gifts.<br /> <br /> Homids<br /> 2 0 2 3 1 0 (Includes Weave of Steel)<br /> Metis<br /> 2 2 3 3 2 0 (Rattler's Bite may be Level 3 instead of Level 4, in which case 2 2 4 2 2 0)<br /> Lupus<br /> 1 3 3 2 1 0 (Includes Thousand Teeth)<br /> <br /> <b>Metis get 12 Green Gifts in total, Lupus get 10, Homids get 8. Metis and Lupus are the most front-loaded.</b><br /> <br /> Ragabash<br /> 4 2 2 1 1 0<br /> Theurge<br /> 4 3 4 4 1 1<br /> Philodox<br /> 5 2 3 2 1 1<br /> Galliard<br /> 1 5 2 2 1 1<br /> Ahroun<br /> 2 3 2 3 2 1<br /> <br /> <b>Theurges get 17, Philodox get 14, Ahroun get 13, Galliards get 12, Ragabash get 10. Theurge and Philodox are very frontloaded.</b><br /> <br /> Black Furies<br /> 1 2 4 0 1 1<br /> Bone Gnawers<br /> 1 2 0 2 0 0<br /> Children of Gaia<br /> 3 2 2 1 1 0<br /> Fianna<br /> 4 3 4 0 2 0<br /> Get of Fenris<br /> 2 1 0 2 2 1<br /> Glass Walkers<br /> 2 1 2 3 2 0<br /> Red Talons<br /> 0 2 2 2 1 0 (Includes Dust Squall)<br /> Shadow Lords<br /> 1 2 2 1 0 0<br /> Silent Striders<br /> 1 0 3 1 2 0<br /> Silver Fangs<br /> 2 1 2 3 1 0<br /> Stargazers<br /> 4 1 0 4 0 0 (Includes Counting Coup)<br /> Elder Brother<br /> 4 2 2 2 1 0 (Includes Counting Coup)<br /> Younger Brother<br /> 3 2 1 3 0 0 (Includes Counting Coup)<br /> <br /> <b>Fianna get 13, Elder Brother get 11, Glass Walkers get 10, Black Furies / Children of Gaia / Silver Fangs / Stargazers / Younger Brother get 9, Get of Fenris get 8, Red Talons &amp; Silent Striders get 7, Shadow Lords get 6, Bone Gnawers get 5.<br /> <br /> Fianna are also frontloaded, as are Stargazers, Elder and Younger Brother. Children of Gaia are front-loaded and include Mother's Touch, along with the unique Grandmother's Touch.</b><br /> <br /> Boli Zousizhe<br /> 2 2 2 4 3 0 (Glass Walker+)<br /> Kucha Ekundu<br /> 0 2 2 2 2 0 (Red Talon+)<br /> Hakken<br /> 2 2 3 2 0 0 (Shadow Lord+)<br /> Siberakh<br /> 4 2 2 3 1 0 (Hybrid of Silver Fangs and Younger Brother) (Includes Counting Coup)<br /> <br /> <b>Boli Zousizhe get 13, Siberakh get 11, Hakken get 9, Kucha Ekundu get 8.</b><br /> <br /> Bunyip<br /> 3 1 2 1 0 0<br /> Middle Brother<br /> 0 2 1 0 1 0<br /> White Howlers<br /> 3 1 2 2 2 0<br /> <br /> <b>White Howlers get 10, Bunyip get 7, Middle Brother get 4.</b><br /> <br /> If you were looking to play a character who has access to a variety of other excellent Gifts, combinations include:<br /> <br /> Metis / Ahroun / Children of Gaia - access to Sense Wyrm, combat Gifts and healing Gifts along with many utilities.<br /> Lupus / Theurge / Fianna - extremely long list of Gifts including Spirit Speech, Sense Wyrm and all of the excellent stuff Fianna get including Persuasion.<br /> Homid / Galliard / Elder Brother - covers basically every base with Persuasion, Apecraft's, all of the excellent options from Galliard and Sense Magic, along with Sense Wyrm of course.<br /> <br /> The BZ, KE, Hakken and arguably the Siberakh are straightforward upgrades of their parent Tribes, but I wouldn't endorse playing one just to broaden your Gift options. I'd much prefer that a player stuck with Glass Walkers, Red Talons, Shadow Lords or Silver Fangs / Younger Brother, and instead explored the relationship with the sub-clan to access their Gifts if it improved the experience or added to the story.<br /> <br /> Remember that WTA is a team-based game, so really don't feel like you need to glean anything from this summary. You could cover a niche which other PCs don't, you don't need to be a Munchkin. That said, I like having lots of options, even if I end up leaving most of them on the table in the long run.</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-12-2025, 06:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-12T18:58:01-07:00","id":1549224,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549212#post1549212\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">If you were looking to play a character who has access to a variety of other excellent Gifts, combinations include:<br /> <br /> Metis / Ahroun / Children of Gaia - access to Sense Wyrm, combat Gifts and healing Gifts along with many utilities.<br /> Lupus / Theurge / Fianna - extremely long list of Gifts including Spirit Speech, Sense Wyrm and all of the excellent stuff Fianna get including Persuasion.<br /> Homid / Galliard / Elder Brother - covers basically every base with Persuasion, Apecraft's, all of the excellent options from Galliard and Sense Magic, along with Sense Wyrm of course.<br /> <br /> The BZ, KE, Hakken and arguably the Siberakh are straightforward upgrades of their parent Tribes, but I wouldn't endorse playing one just to broaden your Gift options. I'd much prefer that a player stuck with Glass Walkers, Red Talons, Shadow Lords or Silver Fangs / Younger Brother, and instead explored the relationship with the sub-clan to access their Gifts if it improved the experience or added to the story.<br /> <br /> Remember that WTA is a team-based game, so really don't feel like you need to glean anything from this summary. You could cover a niche which other PCs don't, you don't need to be a Munchkin. That said, I like having lots of options, even if I end up leaving most of them on the table in the long run.</span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Nice summary, of course a powergaming review would also be biased towards repeated gifts in the lists, complementary powers and starting willpower values for each tribe.<br /> <br /> I did some base charopt a while ago, and got a small list of the best-of for starting characters:<ul><li>Metis Philodox Uktena has the sensory powers: <i>Scent of the True Form + Sense Magic + Sense Wyrm</i> (this is clearly a Perception build).</li> <li>Homid Ragabash Fianna has the social package: <i>Liar\u2019s Face + Persuasion + Two Tongues</i> (a Charisma + Subterfuge build).</li> <li>Homid Anything Glass Walker has the technical nous: <i>Apecraft\u2019s Blessings + Plug and Play + Anything</i> (I prefer <i>Spirit Speech</i> so Theurge).</li> <li>Lupus Ahroun Stargazer is the all rounder, with high Gnosis, Rage and Willpower: <i>Chanelling + Falling Touch + Sense Wyrm</i> (if using the rules for the beast courts you can replace a breed or auspice gift with one from the general Hengeyokai list).</li> </ul><br /> <br /> <br /> Of course this would have not been possible without you insightful analysis.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4967,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","user":"lbeaumanior","date":". 03-13-2025, 09:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T05:43:40-07:00","id":1549230,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:14px\"><u><b>Storm-Eater Gifts</b></u></span><br /> <br /> All of these Gifts exist only so long as the Storm-Eater does, meaning that they do fade from history, though they could still be passed down from Garou to Garou, or potentially learnt in the Umbra.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Galliards</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Song of the Storm (5):</b></u></span> This Gift lets you essentially unleash the raw power of creation and change with some sense of direction. I'm reminded of Unleashing in CTD. For example you could point to a dying packmate and intend for them to receive 'help', but whether that takes the form of teleporting them to a safe place, summoning help to defend them, a wave of healing energy, them transforming into a more robust form, or literally any other helpful outcome, is in the hands of the Wyld (and the Storyteller).<br /> <br /> Identify a target and a goal, spend 2 Gnosis and roll Wits + Expression, and a random event takes place driven by the Wyld nature of the world which drives things towards your goal. The example given is that if you want to stop a train, 1 success might turn the coal to lumps of ice, while 5 successes might turn the track into a giant spider which throws the train off the track and kills the guards.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> While the effects of this Gift are entirely at the Storyteller\u2019s discretion, remember that it is a Level Five Gift, and should work in the Gift user\u2019s favor. The Galliard\u2019s player should rightly anticipate just how random the Gift\u2019s outcomes may be, but shouldn\u2019t feel discouraged from using such a hard-won prize. </div> </div> </div>This Gift is a great 'help me' button, akin to Totem Gift, and it's an excellent tool for the Galliards. Any Gibbous Moon learning this Gift in the time of the Wyld West would do well to ensure it's passed down to other Elders who are worthy custodians of the power. The side-note on how the power should help the character and leave the player feeling satisfied, along with the roll being pretty achievable at a difficulty of (Gauntlet), makes this pretty great.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Black Furies</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Wyldstorm (5):</b></u></span> With this Gift a Black Fury can create a powerful Umbral Storm and push it through the Gauntlet. The resulting storm is very clearly unnatural.<br /> <br /> Spend 1 Rage and 1 Willpower, and then roll Manipulation + Enigmas against the local Gauntlet. 1 success produces a minute-long storm which might tear apart a camp, while 6+ successes creates the equivalent of a major hurricane which lasts for hours.<br /> <br /> How to rate this? Using this is a giant Veil Breach, of course, but it's so incredibly destructive that the Veil becomes somewhat moot. If a Rank 5 Black Fury were to, say, destroy the city of New York using this Gift, I think the Veil would be in the top 5 list of concerns, but it might not be in the top 3. This Gift, very unusually, has the potential to kill millions of people.<br /> <br /> Yes, you won't find a hurricane on record which has killed more people than the low &quot;hundreds of thousands&quot; range, but that's because hurricanes appear as tropical cyclones, progress naturally, make landfall, and people are hunkered down by the time they get there, including evacuations and emergency efforts being prepared. A full-scale giant hurricane with spirit lightning appearing in, say, the most densely populated part of Manila, or right in the middle of London or Tokyo, would create an unprecedented disaster which would most likely have enormous repercussions for the entire world, and the entire Umbra.<br /> <br /> Given the sheer destructive potential of this Gift I do have to give it a Green, but having a nuke in your pocket is a little tricky since there won't be any situation in which you benefit from using it. This Gift's existence being known could play a role in diplomacy, but not necessarily for good, especially since it's difficult to envision a use of this Gift's upper limit of power which would help the Garou Nation. That said, to be fair, it's easy to imagine using this Gift to destroy a great weapon of war, an aircraft carrier, an oil rig in construction, an area overwhelmed by Fomori, and so on. Just be careful you don't get more successes than you bargained for - consider splitting your dice pool!<br /> <br /> <u><b>Iron Riders</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Quell the Storm (5):</b></u></span> This Gift uses the power of the Weaver to quell the Wyld storms of the Umbra. Spend 2 Gnosis, roll Gnosis (difficulty 8). One success would slow a strong wind or stop a breeze, five successes could completely remove an Umbral storm.<br /> <br /> Alternatively you can target a Wyld spirit. The Gift is the same but instead of rolling Gnosis difficulty 8, the difficulty is equal to the spirit's Gnosis, so anywhere from 3 to 10, most likely somewhere in the middle. The targeted spirit loses 5 Essence per success, making this much worse than an attack, but it's worth noting that a gesture and a word of power completely calming a Wyld spirit until it disappears into stillness is, narratively, very different from ripping it apart with your claws. There may well be Wyld spirits which thrive off the chaos of battle, story-wise, and who are best dismissed with a power like this. Very niche, but possible.<br /> <br /> Whatever you use the Gift for, it can't target the same storm or spirit more than once in a given scene.<br /> <br /> Here the difficulty being so high, and the pool being Gnosis, are real issues. Even a difficulty of 7 would be a huge improvement. And while calming a giant Umbral storm is a very big deal, it's also not a common problem to face (well, outside of the reign of the Storm-Eater of course!) and it is very hard to achieve with 5 successes. Even with Gnosis 10, the odds of you getting 5 successes with a difficulty this high aren't great. In fact they're quite bad. Between the cost, the excessively high difficulty, and this being a Level 5 Gift, it sadly gets Red.<br /> <br /> My suggestion to improve this Gift would be that spirits and shapeshifters who are subservient to a Weaver-associated Totem (Cockroach, Queen Ananasa etc.) make the Gift easier to use. So, for example, if there is a small army of telegraph wire and railroad spirits ready to help calm the storm the difficulty will drop significantly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T07:12:32-07:00","id":1549237,"title":"","content":"I admit that my eyes tend to glaze over a bit when it comes to crunch, as opposed to fluff - here and now, one must confess that I\u2019ve followed more than one setting with enthusiasm without actually rolling a single dice (Physical or digital) - but this thread nonetheless offers some fascinating insights into the Garou and their tribes.<br /> <br /> It was especially intriguing to realise how very Tricksy the Fianna can be - more than one of their tribal gifts say \u2018Ragabash\u2019 quite as much as \u2018Galliard\u2019 - and one can only wonder if the Tribe\u2019s reputation for heavy drinking was cultivated before or after the invention of Resist Toxin.<br /> <br /> My impression is that more than one contributor to the thread assumes that the Fianna acquired the Gift because they were a bit too fond of the booze, but I think there\u2019s room to suggest that the ancestors who originally cultivated the gift<br /> might have done so and only <i>then</i> cultivated a reputation for being the life and soul of the party, because they could guarantee that whomsoever made a fool of themselves with drink, it wouldn\u2019t be the Fianna (While this may suggest something along the lines of a practical joke, keeping your wits whilst others are washing them away with booze could equally be seen as a practical necessity for professional entertainers or a gambit by shrew politicians looking to cover their calculations with purest bonhomie).<br /> <br /> If nothing else, the list of their tribal gifts make it easy to see why the Bone Gnawers and the Children of Stag get on so well, given half the chance - both Tribes have a strong element of the Trickster and the Huckster, the Fierce Ones just make a better job of their PR.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T07:20:02-07:00","id":1549238,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>lbeaumanior</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549224#post1549224\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Nice summary, of course a powergaming review would also be biased towards repeated gifts in the lists, complementary powers and starting willpower values for each tribe.<br /> <br /> I did some base charopt a while ago, and got a small list of the best-of for starting characters:<ul><li>Metis Philodox Uktena has the sensory powers: <i>Scent of the True Form + Sense Magic + Sense Wyrm</i> (this is clearly a Perception build).</li> <li>Homid Ragabash Fianna has the social package: <i>Liar\u2019s Face + Persuasion + Two Tongues</i> (a Charisma + Subterfuge build).</li> <li>Homid Anything Glass Walker has the technical nous: <i>Apecraft\u2019s Blessings + Plug and Play + Anything</i> (I prefer <i>Spirit Speech</i> so Theurge).</li> <li>Lupus Ahroun Stargazer is the all rounder, with high Gnosis, Rage and Willpower: <i>Chanelling + Falling Touch + Sense Wyrm</i> (if using the rules for the beast courts you can replace a breed or auspice gift with one from the general Hengeyokai list).</li> </ul><br /> <br /> <br /> Of course this would have not been possible without you insightful analysis.</div> </div> </div> </div> Between this post and <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11620_5.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11620\" data-vbnamecard=\"11620\">twig</a> above it you have the beginnings of a veritable Silver Pack! Out of curiosity, if you were putting together a flexible team of Big D**** Heroes built with a degree of built-in redundancy but without actually playing Munchkin games, might I please ask what mix of tribes, breeds and gifts you would assign to each auspice?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T10:41:01-07:00","id":1549242,"title":"","content":"Wait. Ahadi and Hengeyokai.<br /> <br /> One last push!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><u><b>Gifts of the Ahadi</b></u></span><br /> <br /> These Gifts are available to the 'Ahadi', a young organization of shapechangers working together in Africa. Founded to fight against a Simba dictatorship, the Ahadi promote teamwork between the various Fera.<br /> <br /> If you are a member of the Ahadi you can learn their Gifts at <u>5x level</u> cost. So these Gifts are essentially always &quot;out-of-list&quot;. They can indeed be learnt by various Fera, but for the purposes of this list the most important thing is that they can be learnt by Garou. If the Ahadi are still a very young organization then it's possible that only the lower level Gifts will exist, so ask your ST. And of course these Gifts can make for great inspiration, or you can simply copy them, if you wind up 'making your own' Gift in the course of a game.<br /> <br /> Finally, a unique thing about these Gifts (at least according to the Mokole, who you would expect to be accurate) is that the new ones all have two animal teachers who work in concert.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Rank 1 Ahadi Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Blood Webs:</b></u></span> Costs 1 Willpower and lasts for a scene. Anyone inflicting Lethal or Aggravated damage against you with a &quot;hand-to-hand&quot; attack needs to make a Dexterity + Brawl roll, difficulty 9 (!), or get covered in sticky red webbing. Every time this happens a character gets a -1 penalty to Dexterity rolls, which accumulates over time.<br /> <br /> Removing a single point of the penalty takes a full action and a Strength + Athletics (difficulty 7).<br /> <br /> What this means, essentially, is that if you're being struck repeatedly (say by an enemy with Rage actions) then your foe is going to get less and less agile, hitting less often, dealing less damage, dodging fewer attacks, and getting slower as well. If you have plenty of soak or regen this could be a very good defensive mechanism. Ideally you'd suffer a single level of Lethal damage to trigger this, but unfortunately it's more likely that you'll be taking Aggravated damage when this goes off. I wouldn't suggest deliberately letting yourself get hit! This Gift is a handy debuff against foes which will make them suffer for hurting you, but don't mistake it for something you should directly weaponize, unless you have a means of healing or regenerating Aggravated damage.<br /> <br /> Solid stacking debuff which will stack well with any difficulty boosts, and wound penalties.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Dragon Musk:</b></u></span> Produce a powerful musk which helps your pack to find you and upsets enemies.<br /> <br /> Spend 1 Willpower. For the rest of the scene characters, apart from your 'pack', within 5-25 yards (depending on Rank) add +2 difficulty to all Perception and Stamina rolls, including Soak. Your 'pack' apply a -2 difficulty modifier to finding you.<br /> <br /> Increasing the soak difficulties of all enemies within 10 yards of you, at Rank 2 for example, by 2 is a really important effect. When you're fighting Spirits with a Willpower of 10, Wyrmish Shapeshifters with a Stamina of 8, turning the difficulty of Soak from 6 to 8 can be a very big difference! Solid green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Ogun's Blessing:</b></u></span> Spirits of metal help you with warfare. Spend 1 Willpower and for the rest of the scene you add +1 die and reduce difficulties by 1 when you do anything using a metal object for warfare. Examples given include shooting enemies with a gun, chasing foes with a vehicle, or using a lockpick to break into an ammo dump.<br /> <br /> Somewhat situational, and certainly more powerful if you're in a country which is at war, but Garou are always at war. A big advantage of this Gift is that it would reduce the difficulty to swing a Klaive by 1, and grant a bonus die to swing it, which for a Dexterity 4 Melee 4 Garou would take their swing of a Grand Klaive from 8 dice difficulty 7 (2.9 successes average) to 9 dice difficulty 6 (3.9 successes average). Your chances to hit also go from 83% to 94%, and the odds that someone will successfully parry or dodge drop significantly.<br /> <br /> Assuming you make some use of Melee, Firearms, Drive, Athletics (thrown weapons) or similar, chances are this Gift will do quite a lot for only 5xp.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sense Wyrm:</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Metis list. Only noteworthy if you're from a Fera species which doesn't learn Sense Wyrm at all, in which case becoming Ahadi may grant you access.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Spirit Speech:</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Theurge list. Ditto; if your Fera species doesn't learn Spirit Speech for some reason then this will help.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Three Thousand Tongues*:</b></u></span> Understand and speak every African language which is spoken today. Very handy, but doesn't cover reading and writing. There is an activation roll (Intelligence + Academics) but there is no cost to activate, so you can likely get this to work whenever you like. Given how many different languages are spoken in Africa, in an Ahadi game this could be even more important to have than Spirit Speech. Green, with a * naturally.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T10:52:58-07:00","id":1549243,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 2 Ahadi Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Anansi's Guile*:</b></u></span> Simply a rename of Beast Speech, and a Rank higher than it normally would be. See the Rank 1 Galliard post. Since this will cost 10xp here, only useful if this Gift would normally be inaccessible. If you can simply learn Beast Speech as an out-of-list Gift for 5xp (since it's a Level 1 Gift) then don't bother with Anansi's Guile, which costs 10xp.<br /> <br /> Red based on cost; if it's your only choice for talking to animals then it may be Amber.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Fury Jaws:</b></u></span> Spend a point of Rage prior to biting. If the bite is successful then the target takes a -3 dice penalty to anything they try to do other than attack you, due to the intensity of the itch from the bite. This lasts for a number of rounds equal to your Rank +1, so 3-6.<br /> <br /> Assuming 3 rounds... it's alright? Forcing someone to focus fire on you, especially an enemy which isn't going to want to punch or claw or chase after you, is pretty neat. Use this on a tricksy enemy and 'taunt' them into only using attacking moves, nice. It's a handy option. But a -3 dice penalty isn't impossible to overcome, and the duration is pretty short. Decent concept, stronger and stronger at higher Ranks, worth getting if you're keen on bites.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Impenetrable Veil*:</b></u></span> This Gift strengthens the Delirium response. Roll Appearance + Primal-Urge (difficulty 7), and for every success reduce the Willpower of onlookers for the purposes of Delirium by 1. So, for example, with 5 successes a Willpower 8 human (Curiosity) will be changed to the Willpower 3 response (Disbelief).<br /> <br /> Handy ability to help you to overcome ordinary humans. No cost, and the roll is manageable. Solid Amber, could be Green in a game where human enemies and threats are absolutely key.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>River's Bosom:</b></u></span> Costs 1 Gnosis, requires a roll, and grants you situational invisibility. So long as you remain perfectly still and submerged in water you're invisible. Neat, but a Gift this niche shouldn't cost Gnosis. Attacking ends the Gift, naturally.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Spirit of the Fish:</b></u></span> See the Rank 2 Elder Brother list. Useful if you normally would not be able to access this Gift at all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T11:10:52-07:00","id":1549245,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 3 Ahadi Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Brother's Skin:</b></u></span> This extremely cool Gift lets you turn into the animal forms of your kganmadi, or mixed-fera-pack. So, in a very diverse pack, you might gain 4 or 5 animal forms you can turn into. This Gift does cost 15xp, but the ability for a Garou to turn into a raven, or a cheetah, or a swarm of spiders (at Rank 4) would be too fun to pass up.<br /> <br /> Costs 1 Willpower, and the shapeshifting roll is difficulty 8. If you have something to help with transformations like high Primal-Urge or a Gift then that may help. Worth noting that the main barrier between different Fera races interbreeding is removed by this Gift. If some Ahadi wanted to unify bloodlines they could likely achieve it with this Gift, sharing animal kinfolk with their fellows, however given how precarious a position those Kinfolk populations are in the odds of that ever happening are very slim. Still, interesting to think that it might be possible for a Cheetah to be born of a union which technically makes it Garou Kinfolk! There's a super complicated backstory for one of you.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Exorcism*:</b></u></span> See the Rank 3 Theurge post. Useful if you would normally not be able to access the Gift.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Ghost-Eating Technique:</b></u></span> Strike ghosts possessing corporeal forms, and devour defeated spirits/ghosts to permanently destroy them. Ethical issues aside, a very potent ability.<br /> <br /> When striking a person you may spend a point of Rage to only damage the Ghost inside, rather than the body of the host. Also, when defeating a Spirit or a Ghost, you may spend a point of Willpower to regain 1-3 Gnosis depending on the power of the spirit. No mention is made of the taint which comes with consuming a dark spirit or shadow, so presumably the Gift has no problems with this. It's noted that this Gift could cost you Renown depending on your species and how you use it.<br /> <br /> Being able to trade Willpower for 1-3 Gnosis when you fight enemies is pretty great, as is having a Wraith-killing punch. Great Gift all in all. Though if I were rating them on names I would call this one a bit anime.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Shadows Conceal Fangs:</b></u></span> Speaking of anime moves! This Gift lets you go from concealment in one shadow to concealment in another to make a sneak attack.<br /> <br /> Spend a Rage while hidden inside a shadow (or 'concealed', so possibly people can know you're there). Roll Strength + Stealth difficulty 7. Emerge from a shadow within 5x successes yards and make a surprise attack; the difficulty to soak the damage from this attack is boosted by 2, so combined with Dragon Musk the difficulty would be 10, making soak mostly impossible. Pretty great transport Gift with a handy use. Only costs Rage, or Blood for Ananasi, or Willpower for Nuwisha, so whatever you're playing this isn't super expensive. Relocating to do a very powerful attack (much easier to hit, enemy won't dodge, more damage etc.) is a great combo.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Venom Blood:</b></u></span> See the Rank 3 Get of Fenris post.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-14-2025, 10:49 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T12:07:04-07:00","id":1549250,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 4 Ahadi Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Armoury of the Sun:</b></u></span> This Gift allows you to send a Fetish or Talen to Helios' armoury in the Umbra. There's no cost, you just throw the object up 'into the sky' (presumably you must be under the open sky) and with a Wits + Occult (difficulty 6) it vanishes. The same roll lets you 'catch' the same object as it falls from the sky. Basically you can banish your Talens into a pocket dimension and get them back with no cost. This is Fetish Fetch but much, much better.<br /> <br /> It's worth noting that if you die after sending one of your Fetishes to Helios' armoury, it's gonna be stuck there. If this Gift has been in use for a while there will be a pile of Talens and Fetishes somewhere in Helios' realm which belonged to various different Ahadi now dead, or, if you're a thief, to Ahadi still living. Imagine breaking into Helios' personal realm in the Umbra to reclaim a Fetish belonging to your ancestors which was stolen years prior by an Ahadi who was slain by your kin? Not only a great Gift, a great plot hook as well.<br /> <br /> Personally I'd love it if this Gift were just a long-standing Elder Brother Gift. For Rank 4 it suits them perfectly. Perhaps make it Armoury of the Moon, given the stronger tie to Luna. And if the Ahadi were to ever form an accord with the Garou Nation, adopting this Gift into the Ragabash list would definitely be a boon to the Garou.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Boundless Heart:</b></u> This is renamed from Gorge. Essentially three different Gifts, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Gorge (Rage)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Gorge (Willpower)</b></span> and <b><span style=\"color:#f39c12\">Gorge (Gnosis)</span></b>. See the Rank 4 Red Talons post.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Call Elemental:</b></u></span> See the Rank 4 Elder Brother post. 20xp is a lot for a Gift, but if you're from a Changing Breed which doesn't normally get this Gift, go for it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Dam the Heartflood*:</b></u></span> See the Rank 4 Silent Striders post.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T12:16:41-07:00","id":1549251,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Rank 5 Ahadi Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Marriage of the Sun and Moon:</b></u></span> Go from being weak to silver to being weak to gold, or visa versa. Since we are ranking these Gifts for Garou, what does it mean to go from being weak to silver to being weak to gold?<br /> <br /> Klaives won't have any special benefits against you. That said, they deal Aggravated damage anyways. Damage you can now soak, to be fair, so if you have a good soak pool that could make Klaives significantly less deadly. Carryin silver will now have no effect on your Gnosis, so you can weigh yourself down with insane quantities of silver bling, silver Fetishes, silver ammo and as many Klaives as you can get your hands on.<br /> <br /> Hunters are going to be less of a threat, as are angry Kinfolk, certain Garou enemies, various supernaturals who appreciate the importance of silver (Mages, Vampires, Changelings etc.).<br /> <br /> The problem is that this Gift costs 2 Gnosis and lasts for a single scene. Not until the next sunrise, not a day, not 12 or 24 hours, a scene. Sadly that makes this pretty bad. Yeah, you can trigger it when someone with a Grand Klaive runs at you, but it's still going to slow you down by wasting a turn of Rage actions. Red, but with a tiny tweak on duration this might even be Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Thousand Forms:</b></u></span> Unfortunately this makes Brother's Skin somewhat redundant. That said, Brother's Skin allows the user to become Crawlerling Form (if Rank 4+ with a Werespider in the 'pack'), and it would allow a Garou to enter a form outside of Thousand Forms' size limit, if applicable. So if one of your 'pack' has a natural animal form which is bigger than a bison then Brother's Skin might see another use there. This Gift is wonderful all the same, and really speaks to the spirit of the Ahadi; an Ahadi Elder who can take the forms of almost every beast on Gaia's back is a representation of the unity the Changing Breeds need.<br /> <br /> Anyways, for more on this Gift, see the Rank 5 Ragabash post.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Unity of All Beasts:</b></u></span> Choose one of your Gifts. That Gift becomes a 'bonus' Gift offered by your Totem, but only one member of the group (besides yourself) may use it at a time.<br /> <br /> So, for example, you could put a Gift like Mother's Touch into the pool. Now in any given turn any member of your 'pack' may use Mother's Touch. If you decide to replace Mother's Touch with a combat Gift, say, Spirit of the Fray, then with each passing round one of your allies would benefit from the +10 to Initiative bonus of the Gift.<br /> <br /> The ability to nudge one of your rare or unusual Gifts into the pool so another ally can wield it, possibly better than intended by combining it with their strange Forms and Gifts, is amazing. It's also easy to imagine that this Gift-sharing would make it easier for your allies to later learn the Gifts, if the ST allowed them to be taught with the 'hard way' method.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T13:07:39-07:00","id":1549256,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549238#post1549238\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Between this post and <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11620_5.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11620\" data-vbnamecard=\"11620\">twig</a> above it you have the beginnings of a veritable Silver Pack! Out of curiosity, if you were putting together a flexible team of Big D**** Heroes built with a degree of built-in redundancy but without actually playing Munchkin games, might I please ask what mix of tribes, breeds and gifts you would assign to each auspice?</div> </div> </div> </div>I would go for Hengeyokai, having multiple changing breeds and court auspice gifts will offer incredible flexibility for a pack. This is the <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1436799-w20-enhancing-the-pack-optimal-initial-char-gen-pack\">best starting pack</a> I can build for Garou, but the best social Garou pales to an <a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/WhiteWolfRPG/comments/ac55wv/comment/l0zepzi/\" target=\"_blank\">optimized Kitsune</a>, and you could do similar stuff with other roles and other breeds.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4967,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","user":"lbeaumanior","date":". 03-13-2025, 04:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T17:13:12-07:00","id":1549277,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:14px\"><u><b>Hengeyokai</b></u></span><br /> <br /> Garou in the Beast Courts gain a few changes worth noting.<br /> <br /> - Hengeyokai Garou receive access to the General Hengeyokai Gift List and the an additional Auspice List, both cost 4x Current Level<br /> - They can exchange one of their three starting Gifts for a General Hengeyokai Gift<br /> - They can exchange their starting Auspice Gift for a Hengeyokai Auspice Gift<br /> <br /> Assuming there is at least one General and one Auspice Gift you want, the exchange is sensible. What you're losing costs 3xp, what you're gaining costs 4xp. Exceedingly character-optimization brained, but I like noting these things.<br /> <br /> Before we get into what these lists are, I do want to address a side-bar I find funny:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <i><b>WAIT A MINUTE, MY HAKKEN GETS FIVE GIFT LISTS?</b><br /> <br /> Effectively, yes. Two of those lists (general Hengeyokai and Beast Court Auspice) are more expensive than native Gifts, but they\u2019re still less expensive than, say, picking up a Silver Fang Gift.<br /> <br /> So what gives? Why are hengeyokai so much stronger than Western shapeshifters?<br /> <br /> The answer has nothing to do with Asian mysticism or superiority, and everything to do with history and hard work. The Changing Breeds elsewhere in the world have spent all of history since the War of Rage in isolated, balkanized enclaves when they weren\u2019t either going to ground to avoid discovery altogether - or tearing one another\u2019s throats out. The Fera retreated from the world, mistrustful of the Rage of the werewolves and of each other.<br /> <br /> The hengeyokai, while not without their shortcomings and problems, managed to hold it together to a far greater degree. They came together and built something. They took their strength, and made it the strength of their brothers. Beast Court characters have more advantages than other shapeshifters because they\u2019re the beneficiaries of thousands of years of hard-won unity and back-breaking spiritual labor undertaken by their ancestors. Because of this, Beast Court characters have some perks to emphasize this point - unity and understanding bring concrete benefits, while the wage of infighting is death. Perhaps, if there is any time left before the Apocalypse, the Sunset People could learn from the Hengeyokai.</i> </div> </div> </div>I find this quite charming. &quot;It isn't that they have 5 Gift lists because they're better, they have 5 Gift lists because they're better!&quot; <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I'm being facetious, and I appreciate that the history is very different. Ultimately it's totally harmless that Asian Garou have a leg up; there are fewer Tribe choices (only two that I'm aware of - Hakken and Stargazer, and maybe Boli Zouhisze at a push, though they aren't members of the Beast Courts) and the bonus Gifts are only slightly cheaper than they would be otherwise. The main benefit, in fact, isn't to do with experience, it's to do with accessing Gifts you'd otherwise struggle to justify accessing. Getting ahold of the most important Gift associated with a Breed, Auspice or Tribe requires clout, and it risks looking like you're trying to act outside the role you're expected to act within. So your Metis Hakken Ahroun being able to get Persuasion is a pretty big deal. It costing 4xp instead of 5xp barely matters, but the fact that they didn't have to plead their case like a loser is a big advantage.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Hengeyokai General Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Remember that these cost 4x Level xp, so the main improvement is that you can access them easily. I will note that I'm not a fan of a 'General' list existing for the Beast Courts, since it makes some Gifts so ubiquitous that it lowers the stakes and makes the setting feel less cohesive (really hard to imagine certain threats flourishing in a place where every shapeshifter can get Sense Wyrm for 3-4xp!), but that's personal taste.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Balance (1):</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Stargazer post.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Create Element (1):</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Metis post.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Mindspeak (1):</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Galliard post.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sense Wyrm (1):</b></u></span> Ah great, now everyone has it. A sign of a society with its priorities in order, to be fair. See the Metis Rank 1 post!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Blaze Talons (2):</b></u></span> Costs 1 Gnosis, roll Strength + Primal-Urge. For every success your forelimbs are set afire for two rounds. Damaging Brawl attacks using your forelimbs gain one automatic level of Aggravated damage.<br /> <br /> How good is a bonus level of Aggravated? It's roughly akin to +2 dice, but more consistent. Fire also scares Vampires and various other threats.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Courtly Speech (2):</b></u></span> Renamed from Speech of the World. See the Rank 2 Homid list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Resist Temptation (2):</b></u></span> See the Rank 2 Stargazer list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Sense Balance (2):</b></u></span> See the Rank 3 Philodox list. Accessing this early is a big improvement, and here you're paying 8xp (2 x 4) instead of 9 or 15xp (3 x 3 or 3 x 5).<br /> <br /> I rated Sense Balance as Amber with a * marking it as a potential Red, depending on the table. Getting it at Rank 2, and for 8xp instead of 9xp, makes it a little bit better. But honestly if you're Peeking through the Umbra and you can't tell an area is heavily Weaver or Wyld aligned, or you can't tell a Drone/Gorgon from a regular person, you may need to invest into Perception and Abilities before you think about sensing Triat energies with Gifts. I maintain that most of the time you're better off relying on common sense and inferring from what you can see in the two worlds.<br /> <br /> Still, solid Amber here, especially given the variety of relationships with the Triat held within the Beast Courts.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Clarity (3):</b></u></span> See the Rank 3 Stargazer list.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Part the Wall (3):</b></u> Irrelevant to Garou; this Gift allows those who normally wouldn't be able to step sideways to do so. Unless you're a Metis whose 'deformity' is that you can't step sideways on your own, this Gift won't do anything for you.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Waking Dream of Unity (3):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Willpower, up to 30 willing individuals present at activation enter into silent telepathic communion. Excellent power for managing large battles and perhaps a social event or similar. Great for espionage.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Exorcism (4)*:</b></u></span> See the Rank 3 Theurge list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Mindblock (4):</b></u></span> See the Rank 4 Silver Fang list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Walking the Bone Road (4):</b></u></span> Modifies the Stepping Sideways mechanic so that you can step sideways into the Low Umbra instead of the Middle Umbra. The main utility of this, for my money, is being able to use stepping sideways to escape from enemies who can step sideways themselves. If you're fighting a Garou, as a Garou, and they vanish into thin air, well you're going to step sideways to chase them... only to find they're nowhere to be seen.<br /> <br /> Incredibly strong ability, well worth getting on anyone who is allowed to purchase it. The bonus utility of being able to easily access a whole different layer of reality is also neat. The best use of accessing the Low Umbra in a typical Werewolf game is avoiding Banes whilst also avoiding the real world, in my book, but that's still an excellent result.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Elemental Gift (5):</b></u></span> See the Rank 5 Lupus list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><u><b>Way of Ten Thousand Cities (5):</b></u></span> Renamed from Assimilation. See the Rank 5 Homid list.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-14-2025, 05:16 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T17:31:08-07:00","id":1549279,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549277#post1549277\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><u><b>Hengeyokai</b></u></span><br /> <br /> Garou in the Beast Courts gain a few changes worth noting.<br /> <br /> - Hengeyokai Garou receive access to the General Hengeyokai Gift List and the an additional Auspice List, both cost 4x Current Level<br /> - They can exchange one of their three starting Gifts for a General Hengeyokai Gift<br /> <b>- They can exchange one of their three starting Gifts for a Hengeyokai Auspice Gift</b><br /> <br /> Assuming there is at least one General and one Auspice Gift you want, the exchange is sensible. What you're losing costs 3xp, what you're gaining costs 4xp. Exceedingly character-optimization brained, but I like noting these things.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is not exactly like you write, they can exchange the <i>Auspice </i>gift for a Hengeyokai Auspice gift.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Beast Court characters may exchange one of their<br /> starting Gifts (an Auspice or Aspect Gift, if the char-<br /> acter has such things) for a Gift from his Beast Court<br /> Auspice list. </div> </div> </div>The detail is important, for Garou is mainly Galliards who start with a weak level 1 list who should be doing the shift, but it also works for other changing breeds that might have weak first level options.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4967,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","user":"lbeaumanior","date":". 03-13-2025, 08:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-13T20:26:16-07:00","id":1549281,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549277#post1549277\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><u><b>Hengeyokai</b></u></span><br /> <br /> Garou in the Beast Courts gain a few changes worth noting.<br /> <br /> - Hengeyokai Garou receive access to the General Hengeyokai Gift List and the an additional Auspice List, both cost 4x Current Level<br /> - They can exchange one of their three starting Gifts for a General Hengeyokai Gift<br /> - They can exchange one of their three starting Gifts for a Hengeyokai Auspice Gift<br /> <br /> Assuming there is at least one General and one Auspice Gift you want, the exchange is sensible. What you're losing costs 3xp, what you're gaining costs 4xp. Exceedingly character-optimization brained, but I like noting these things.<br /> <br /> Before we get into what these lists are, I do want to address a side-bar I find funny:<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I find this quite charming. &quot;It isn't that they have 5 Gift lists because they're better, they have 5 Gift lists because they're better!&quot; <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I'm being facetious, and I appreciate that the history is very different. Ultimately it's totally harmless that Asian Garou have a leg up; there are fewer Tribe choices (only two that I'm aware of - Hakken and Stargazer, and maybe Boli Zouhisze at a push, though they aren't members of the Beast Courts)</div> </div> </div> </div>According to prior material, Bone Gnawers, Children of Gaia, and Red Talons as well, and more varied Glass Walkers. (Hengeyokai p. 58, Caerns: Places of Power in the Glass Walkers' section). The collapse of Garou in the Beast Courts to just the Stargazers and Hakken was really, really dumb; almost as dumb as trying to tie the Hakken to Chiyou, a figure from Chinese folklore tradition, in Blood and Silk*.<br /> <br /> (Nothing is as dumb as Blood and Silk's language sidebar, full stop.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> and the bonus Gifts are only slightly cheaper than they would be otherwise. The main benefit, in fact, isn't to do with experience, it's to do with accessing Gifts you'd otherwise struggle to justify accessing. Getting ahold of the most important Gift associated with a Breed, Auspice or Tribe requires clout, and it risks looking like you're trying to act outside the role you're expected to act within. So your Metis Hakken Ahroun being able to get Persuasion is a pretty big deal. It costing 4xp instead of 5xp barely matters, but the fact that they didn't have to plead their case like a loser is a big advantage. </div> </div> </div>Depending on your breed and court auspice, along with other splat lists, the lists that you do get in the end might also be very redundant. An Ahroun who is a Fist, for example, gets access to three Gifts that they would not otherwise (and there's no telling if the Beast Courts auspice grants astrological Gift access like it does with Garou, where an Ahroun also technically gets access with in-splat costs to the Nerigal, Rorg, and Katanka-Sonnak lists).<br /> <br /> <u><b>Hengeyokai General Gifts</b></u><br /> <br /> Remember that these cost 4x Level xp, so the main improvement is that you can access them easily. I will note that I'm not a fan of a 'General' list existing for the Beast Courts, since it makes some Gifts so ubiquitous that it lowers the stakes and makes the setting feel less cohesive (really hard to imagine certain threats flourishing in a place where every shapeshifter can get Sense Wyrm for 3-4xp!), but that's personal taste.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Balance (1):</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Stargazer post.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Create Element (1):</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Metis post.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Mindspeak (1):</b></u></span> See the Rank 1 Galliard post.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Sense Wyrm (1):</b></u></span> Ah great, now everyone has it. A sign of a society with its priorities in order, to be fair. See the Metis Rank 1 post!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Blaze Talons (2):</b></u></span> Costs 1 Gnosis, roll Strength + Primal-Urge. For every success your forelimbs are set afire for two rounds. Damaging Brawl attacks using your forelimbs gain one automatic level of Aggravated damage.<br /> <br /> How good is a bonus level of Aggravated? It's roughly akin to +2 dice, but more consistent. Fire also scares Vampires and various other threats.<br /> [/quote]<br /> <br /> Middle Dragons, Sharks, and Serpents learn this one rank later, incidentally.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Courtly Speech (2):</b></u></span> Renamed from Speech of the World. See the Rank 2 Homid list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Resist Temptation (2):</b></u></span> See the Rank 2 Stargazer list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Sense Balance (2):</b></u></span> See the Rank 3 Philodox list. Accessing this early is a big improvement, and here you're paying 8xp (2 x 4) instead of 9 or 15xp (3 x 3 or 3 x 5).<br /> <br /> I rated Sense Balance as Amber with a * marking it as a potential Red, depending on the table. Getting it at Rank 2, and for 8xp instead of 9xp, makes it a little bit better. But honestly if you're Peeking through the Umbra and you can't tell an area is heavily Weaver or Wyld aligned, or you can't tell a Drone/Gorgon from a regular person, you may need to invest into Perception and Abilities before you think about sensing Triat energies with Gifts. I maintain that most of the time you're better off relying on common sense and inferring from what you can see in the two worlds. </div> </div> </div>High Autonomy in the possessed might make picking up on something in their day to day life more difficult. Detecting something off early can also allow you to head off the threat with a potentially much lower cost in time, effort, and casualties.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Still, solid Amber here, especially given the variety of relationships with the Triat held within the Beast Courts.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Clarity (3):</b></u></span> See the Rank 3 Stargazer list.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Part the Wall (3):</b></u> Irrelevant to Garou; this Gift allows those who normally wouldn't be able to step sideways to do so. Unless you're a Metis whose 'deformity' is that you can't step sideways on your own, this Gift won't do anything for you.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Waking Dream of Unity (3):</b></u></span> Spend 1 Willpower, up to 30 willing individuals present at activation enter into silent telepathic communion. Excellent power for managing large battles and perhaps a social event or similar. Great for espionage.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Exorcism (4)*:</b></u></span> See the Rank 3 Theurge list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Mindblock (4):</b></u></span> See the Rank 4 Silver Fang list.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Walking the Bone Road (4):</b></u></span> Modifies the Stepping Sideways mechanic so that you can step sideways into the Low Umbra instead of the Middle Umbra. The main utility of this, for my money, is being able to use stepping sideways to escape from enemies who can step sideways themselves. If you're fighting a Garou, as a Garou, and they vanish into thin air, well you're going to step sideways to chase them... only to find they're nowhere to be seen.<br /> <br /> Incredibly strong ability, well worth getting on anyone who is allowed to purchase it. The bonus utility of being able to easily access a whole different layer of reality is also neat. The best use of accessing the Low Umbra in a typical Werewolf game is avoiding Banes whilst also avoiding the real world, in my book, but that's still an excellent result. </div> </div> </div>Some banes might surprise you, though.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:10px\">*A &quot;barbarian&quot; enemy of the Yellow Emperor, who fought with said legendary Emperor's forces but lost due to an inspired invention of the same. The description of the Wan Xian vs. Xiong Ren fight in B&amp;S (how appropriate an abbreviation) was meant to parallel this fight, culminating in Garou Chiyou becoming the only changer to reside in the Thousand Hells, because heaven thought it an injustice that the corrupted Wan Xian were being socked in the eye or something. Quite honestly, a hot mess of an attempt to Goth up the legend.</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-14T02:18:27-07:00","id":1549290,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>lbeaumanior</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549279#post1549279\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It is not exactly like you write, they can exchange the <i>Auspice </i>gift for a Hengeyokai Auspice gift.<br /> <br /> The detail is important, for Garou is mainly Galliards who start with a weak level 1 list who should be doing the shift, but it also works for other changing breeds that might have weak first level options.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah nice, fixed! If someone were paying me to build a perfectly optimized Beast Courts Garou I'd definitely look at their secondary Auspice list and look for any Greens. If there are any there which aren't in-list for the Garou normally (say their Beast Courts Auspice has Persuasion, and normally they wouldn't get that) then I'd suggest starting with that instead of their normal starting Auspice Gift. It only saves 1xp, but marginal gains add up. For my part I would want my players to be aware of the cost difference, but I wouldn't encourage them in either direction.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-14T03:08:49-07:00","id":1549291,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549281#post1549281\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">According to prior material, Bone Gnawers, Children of Gaia, and Red Talons as well, and more varied Glass Walkers. (Hengeyokai p. 58, Caerns: Places of Power in the Glass Walkers' section).<br /> <br /> Depending on your breed and court auspice, along with other splat lists, the lists that you do get in the end might also be very redundant. An Ahroun who is a Fist, for example, gets access to three Gifts that they would not otherwise (and there's no telling if the Beast Courts auspice grants astrological Gift access like it does with Garou, where an Ahroun also technically gets access with in-splat costs to the Nerigal, Rorg, and Katanka-Sonnak lists).</div> </div> </div> </div>Good to know! In that case the options broaden up a lot, and that makes good sense. It would be insane if the Garou Nation, which is the most &quot;pressure people into running away&quot; organization in the WoD, didn't have anyone flee eastward to the Beast Courts. Any Garou-Fera couple, any Garou with Asian heritage who visited home, it makes sense that various Tribes would have footholds in the Beast Courts, even if they're very small.<br /> <br /> And regarding the secondary Auspice my assumption is that a player will tend to, and I don't blame them for this, pick a Court Auspice which complements their Garou Auspice (only focusing on Garou here by the way - I'll do a different thread for Fera someday).<br /> <br /> So, for example, I'm not sure if a typical Ahroun would pick Fist as their Court Auspice. Perhaps they should, and most do, but to me the idea of an Ahroun who is a Courtier or Lantern is more interesting. Then, to be frank, you have all the additional Gifts you can access at 4x instead of 5x, which will be very tempting to players. Basically if my Child of Gaia Metis Ahroun were suddenly in the Beast Courts, I might well end up going Fist, but I'd be very tempted by the other lists because then there would actually be a mechanical advantage to him being at the Beast Courts, which is just the kind of thing that feels fun. Ultimately playing an Ahroun-Lanter, or Ahroun-Mirror, or Ahroun-Courtier feels more superficially compelling, even if there is something to be said for the simplicity of &quot;Warrior. Warrior. No new Gifts, but that doesn't matter.&quot;, a move I would respect in any player.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-14T03:25:47-07:00","id":1549292,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Lantern</b></u><br /> <br /> These Gifts are learnt at 4x Level. Lanterns are akin to Philodox, so a Philodox Garou in the Beast Courts is most likely to be a lantern, however that is not guaranteed.<br /> <br /> Gifts a Philodox Garou would not normally get access to are <u>underlined</u>.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Fangs of Judgement</b></span>, <b><span style=\"color:#27ae60\">Persuasion</span></b>, <b><span style=\"color:#27ae60\">Scent of the True Form</span></b>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Truth of Gaia</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Command the Gathering</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>King of Beasts</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Strength of Purpose</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Mental Speech</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Wisdom of Ancient Ways</b></span>,<br /> <b>Roll Over</b>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Take the True Form</b></span><br /> <b>Geas</b>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Strength of Will</b></u></span><br /> <br /> Now, let's analyze this list, and you'll see why I would advocate letting your poor Philodox pick anything other than Lantern.<br /> <br /> What do we see here? A list of Philodox Gifts. If you're playing a Philodox in the Beast Courts, your Court Auspice isn't a way to grant you new abilities, it's a way to grant your abilities to all of the Fera who get their own special stuff you'd want access to but can't get.<br /> <br /> If this list were written with a bunch of Fera Gifts folded in, basically if the list were &quot;what are the Fera Gifts which would really suit the Philodox?&quot; whilst also being a list of the necessary judge, investigator and lawman Gifts, like the ones listed above, that would be better. That way a random Fera can have the Philodox basekit, but the Philodox who chooses Lantern doesn't suffer any losses.<br /> <br /> As it stands, if you're making a Lantern Philodox in the Beast Courts the only thing you get from this list is Strength of Will, at Rank 5, for 20xp instead of 25xp. That's it! This is an excellent list for practically any Garou other than a Philodox. My Metis Ahroun would love Persuasion and Scent of the True Form, he'd love Truth of Gaia, and Strength of Purpose would redefine his access to Willpower. Basically I can't envision a world where I wouldn't dip into that new pool, especially if I'm surrounded by Werespiders and Wererats and Werefoxes who are getting the best things about being a Garou (an abbreviated version of my Garou Auspice list) whilst getting to keep all of their Fera Gifts which I still can't access.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-14-2025, 09:35 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-14T06:32:22-07:00","id":1549297,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Fist</b></u><br /> <br /> These Gifts are learnt at 4x Level. Fists are akin to Ahroun, so a Garou in the Beast Courts is most likely to be a Fist, but the lists have a lot of overlap.<br /> <br /> Gifts an Ahroun normally would not get access to are <u>underlined</u>.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Falling Touch</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Inspiration</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Pack Tactics</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Razor Claws</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b><u>Resist Pain</u></b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Shield of Rage</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Snarl of the Predator</b></u></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Spirit of the Fray</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>True Fear</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Combat Healing</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Thunder of Susano-o (Might of Thor)</b></u></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Wind's Returning Favour</b></u></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Wrath of Gaia</b></u></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Full Moon's Light</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Stoking Fury's Furnace</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Kiss of Helios</b></span><br /> <br /> Adding Resist Pain, Snarl of the Predator and Wrath of Gaia is pretty good going. The lists still have a load of overlap, but an Ahroun Fist isn't doing too terribly either. This list is at its best, however, when stuck onto a Garou whose regular Auspice doesn't include any of these amazing combat Gifts. Spirit of the Fray is arguably one of the most important Gifts in WTA given how many fights are decided in the first round.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-14T06:45:17-07:00","id":1549298,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Mirror</b></u><br /> <br /> These Gifts are learnt at 4x Level. Mirrors are akin to Theurges.<br /> <br /> Gifts a Theurge normally would not get access to are <u>underlined</u>.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Liar's Face</b></u></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Persuasion</b></u></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Spirit Snare</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Spirit Speech</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Umbral Tether</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Battle Mandala</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Call to Duty</b></u></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Command Spirit</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Name the Spirit</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Liar's Craft</b></u></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Pulse of the Invisible</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Umbral Camouflage</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Web Walker</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Blurring the Mirror</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Grasp the Beyond</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Scent of the Beyond</b></u></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Spirit Drain</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Spirit Ward</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Malleable Spirit</b></span>, <b>Ultimate Argument of Logic</b><br /> <br /> The social role of the Theurge is expanded and they get access to a few Philodox Gifts which help with spiritual matters. A Ragabash influence is also seen. Actually a worthwhile combination with Theurge. Annoying it's an even better combination with, say, any other Auspice. Gaining a bunch of great Ragabash and Theurge Gifts in one single package is pretty amazing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-14T07:02:34-07:00","id":1549299,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Leaf</b></u><br /> <br /> These Gifts are learnt at 4x Level. Leaves are akin to Ragabash.<br /> <br /> Gifts a Ragabash normally would not get access to are <u>underlined</u>.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Blur of the Milky Eye</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Open Seal</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Scent of Running Water</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Whisper Catching</b></u></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Blissful Ignorance</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Pulse of the Prey</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Spider's Song</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Taking the Forgotten</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Monkey Tail</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Pathfinder</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Umbral Camouflage</b></u></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Umbral Dodge</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Whelp Body</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Paws of the Newborn Cub</b></u></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Thousand Forms</b></span><br /> <br /> Given that there is a pretty decent chance you're Hakken, Whisper Catching likely isn't a new Gift option for you. So the net benefit for a Hakken Ragabash Leaf is getting Umbral Camouflage for 12xp instead of 15xp, and maybe someday learning Paws of the Newborn Cub, which isn't that great.<br /> <br /> Who is this list good for then? I would say non-Ragabash who are taking on sneaky roles in court. An Ahroun with Open Seal and Blur of the Milky Eye is a much more capable assassin than they would be otherwise.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-14T07:34:35-07:00","id":1549302,"title":"","content":"Checking through regional Tribes ( and variants) east of The Fertile Crescent -<br /> <br /> Hakken. ( Shadow Lords)<br /> Wang Tong. ( Bone Gnawers)<br /> Stargazers.<br /> Boli Zousizhe ( Glass Walkers- Saur Ops can weigh in on that phrase)<br /> Scarlet Talons ( Red Talons )<br /> Steppe Striders ( Silent Striders)<br /> Firehair Fianna<br /> Ainu Garou ( honestly considering Older Brothers ' outreach program' there should be a bunch of Older Brother present throughout the region.<br /> Siberahk ( at the southern edge of their range)<br /> House Blood Red Crest of the Silver Fangs.<br /> Singing Dogs ( Mostly Melanesia but.....technically SE Asia due to Indonesias annexing of Papua)<br /> <br /> I don't really see any reason that the Furies, Coggies and Fenrir wouldn't be present in some numbers but they aren't mentioned anywhere I can find.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/479-damian-may","name":"Damian May","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar479_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-14T07:39:59-07:00","id":1549304,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Pillar</b></u><br /> <br /> These Gifts are learnt at 4x Level. Pillars are akin to Galliards.<br /> <br /> Gifts a Galliard would normally not get access to are <u>underlined</u>.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Beast Speech</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Call of the Wyld</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Heightened Senses</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Infectious Laughter</b></u></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Mother's Touch</b></u></span>, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Perfect Recall</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Call of the Wyrm</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Command the Gathering</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Distractions</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Dreamspeak</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>Sight from Beyond</b></u></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Eye of the Cobra</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Song of Heroes</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Song of Rage</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Song of the Siren</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Bridge Walker</b></span>, <b>Gift of Dreams</b><br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Fabric of the Mind</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Wall of Granite</b></u></span><br /> <br /> For a Galliard the main addition is Mother's Touch. A Gift that can redefine your role in a group or a whole society. Beyond that there aren't many meaningful changes. For a non-Galliard the list is pretty great; Mother's Touch, Call of the Wyrm, Distractions, Song of Heroes, all pretty amazing. A very good 'bonus' list for anyone who wants to add to the social aspect of their character, especially if they already get access to Persuasion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-14T08:35:51-07:00","id":1549307,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Courtier</b></u><br /> <br /> These Gifts are learnt at 4x Level. This Auspice has no parallel among Luna's Auspices, and this means I actually find it the most intriguing. Courtiers are dedicated to guarding Caerns (Dragon Nests) among other things. It's arguably the easiest to justify taking on your character, since it doesn't match your Auspice, but it doesn't match <i>any</i> Auspice.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Breath of the Wyld</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Lightning Reflexes</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Master of Fire</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Perfect Recall</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Persuasion</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Flame Dance</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Sight From Beyond</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Staredown</b></span>, <b><span style=\"color:#f39c12\">Wuxing</span></b><br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Exorcism</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Open Moon Bridge</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Scent of the Oathbreaker</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Song of the Siren</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Attunement</b></span>, <b>Beast Life</b>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Bridge Walker</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Head Games</b></span>, <b>Geas</b><br /> <br /> Note that while the General Hengeyokai Gift List does give Exorcism, the Courtier gets it at Level 3 instead of Level 4.<br /> <br /> Persuasion, Flame Dance and Scent of the Oathbreaker are the major takeaways here. This Auspice gives you abilities which help you with managing a base, but they also feel like a generalist who is meant to deal with anything from an attack on the Caern to accepting visitors for diplomatic discussions. Management of Moon Bridges is a worthwhile addition given the importance of the bridges for Caerns, but... honestly just learning the relevant Rites is probably fine. Still, good to have them. This would be a good choice for Court Auspice if you weren't sure which one to take; you're almost guaranteed to get something you didn't have before.<br /> <br /> Also, I have gone back and edited the Lightning Reflexes entry, changing it from Red to Amber. See The Get of Fenris Rank 1 post for details. That said, on this list specifically, Flame Dance makes Lightning Reflexes mostly redundant. With the Flame Dance Gift you can just always declare an attack, and if you're attacked you can automatically roll for a dodge with a Rage spend, no worries. For a Courtier, the only reason to get Lightning Reflexes would be if you don't plan to get Flame Dance, say for example if you are Rage 1 (Ragabash, for instance) and don't plan to improve your Rage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-14T10:35:17-07:00","id":1549311,"title":"","content":"With that, to the best of my knowledge, every single Gift available to Garou through normal means is covered.<br /> <br /> If I've missed any please let me know! Someday I'll do a similar thread for Fera and a vaguely similar thread for Rites.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-14T10:52:37-07:00","id":1549313,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549311#post1549311\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With that, to the best of my knowledge, every single Gift available to Garou through normal means is covered.<br /> <br /> If I've missed any please let me know! Someday I'll do a similar thread for Fera and a vaguely similar thread for Rites.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you for this effort, amazing thread.<br /> <br /> <img width=\"760\" height=\"323\" data-align=\"none\" data-size=\"full\" loading=\"lazy\" border=\"0\" src=\"https://usagif.com/wp-content/uploads/applause-28.gif\" alt=\"\" data-fullsize-url=\"https://usagif.com/wp-content/uploads/applause-28.gif\" data-thumb-url=\"https://usagif.com/wp-content/uploads/applause-28.gif\" data-title=\"Click on the image to see the original version\" data-caption=\"\" class=\"bbcode-attachment thumbnail js-lightbox bbcode-attachment--lightbox\" itemprop=\"image\"/><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-14T12:14:17-07:00","id":1549316,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Damian May</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549302#post1549302\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Checking through regional Tribes ( and variants) east of The Fertile Crescent -<br /> <br /> Hakken. ( Shadow Lords)<br /> Wang Tong. ( Bone Gnawers)<br /> Stargazers.<br /> Boli Zousizhe ( Glass Walkers- Saur Ops can weigh in on that phrase)</div> </div> </div> </div>It was mangled to Zouhisze in Revised. Even as Zousizhe, it might not be what the writers actually wanted, since Zousizhe means smuggler, and not just someone who goes secretly amidst glass (the conveyed meaning of their claw glyph).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Scarlet Talons ( Red Talons )<br /> Steppe Striders ( Silent Striders)<br /> Firehair Fianna<br /> Ainu Garou ( honestly considering Older Brothers ' outreach program' there should be a bunch of Older Brother present throughout the region. </div> </div> </div>You might think that, but the Stargazers supposedly cornered the space among them for changers, according to both the 2nd edition and revised tribebooks. Elder Brother doesn't have any specific breath to spend on them in their own Revised tribebook, even though Elder Brother as among the Ainu has been hyped up since the 1st edition core.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Siberahk ( at the southern edge of their range)<br /> House Blood Red Crest of the Silver Fangs.<br /> Singing Dogs ( Mostly Melanesia but.....technically SE Asia due to Indonesias annexing of Papua)<br /> <br /> I don't really see any reason that the Furies, Coggies and Fenrir wouldn't be present in some numbers but they aren't mentioned anywhere I can find. </div> </div> </div>It seems like there would be room for the Get as a regional variation, given the story of the Tiangou to work with, but, well, we all know how ParaWolf would have felt about that proposal...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-17T09:02:23-07:00","id":1549387,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Damian May</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549302#post1549302\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Checking through regional Tribes ( and variants) east of The Fertile Crescent -<br /> <br /> Hakken. ( Shadow Lords)<br /> Wang Tong. ( Bone Gnawers)<br /> Stargazers.<br /> Boli Zousizhe ( Glass Walkers- Saur Ops can weigh in on that phrase)<br /> Scarlet Talons ( Red Talons )<br /> Steppe Striders ( Silent Striders)<br /> Firehair Fianna<br /> Ainu Garou ( honestly considering Older Brothers ' outreach program' there should be a bunch of Older Brother present throughout the region.<br /> Siberahk ( at the southern edge of their range)<br /> House Blood Red Crest of the Silver Fangs.<br /> Singing Dogs ( Mostly Melanesia but.....technically SE Asia due to Indonesias annexing of Papua)<br /> <br /> I don't really see any reason that the Furies, Coggies and Fenrir wouldn't be present in some numbers but they aren't mentioned anywhere I can find.</div> </div> </div> </div> While there may not be any purely plot- or character-based reason, I\u2019d argue that the World of Darkness running on dice rolls is an excellent justification for a faction not showing up where they have some justification for doing so - somebody may well have tried a Big Move in the past, but the dice clearly did not fall in their favour.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-17T09:05:13-07:00","id":1549388,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549311#post1549311\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With that, to the best of my knowledge, every single Gift available to Garou through normal means is covered.<br /> <br /> If I've missed any please let me know! Someday I'll do a similar thread for Fera and a vaguely similar thread for Rites.</div> </div> </div> </div> Clearly this is Labour and an Achievement not only worthy of Glory and Wisdom, but of a Deed Name - I propose that we hereafter refer to this most admirable Theurge as \u2018Keeper of Gifts\u2019.<br /> <br /> What says the Moot?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-17T09:24:31-07:00","id":1549394,"title":"","content":"Fellow Garou, having stumbled onto a Deed Name for <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11620_5.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11620\" data-vbnamecard=\"11620\">twig</a> that is also a pun, I would like to revise my suggestion to \u2018Rapper of Gifts\u2019 in honour of the \u2018twigs rather astonishing ability to put out articles the way a talented rapper spits verses.<br /> <br /> Also, did I mention the Play on Words?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-30T11:08:12-07:00","id":1549643,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549281#post1549281\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> According to prior material, Bone Gnawers, Children of Gaia, and Red Talons as well, and more varied Glass Walkers. (Hengeyokai p. 58, Caerns: Places of Power in the Glass Walkers' section). The collapse of Garou in the Beast Courts to just the Stargazers and Hakken was really, really dumb; almost as dumb as trying to tie the Hakken to Chiyou, a figure from Chinese folklore tradition, in Blood and Silk*.<br /> <br /> (Nothing is as dumb as Blood and Silk's language sidebar, full stop.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Can you provide the pages for both references in Blood and Silk? Now I <i>must </i>see them, specially the sidebar.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-30T12:23:38-07:00","id":1549646,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>lbeaumanior</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549643#post1549643\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Can you provide the pages for both references in Blood and Silk? Now I <i>must </i>see them, specially the sidebar.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Naming and Transliteration sidebar is on page 8. It is... aggressively ignorant in its declarations, the most egregious of which is that Yale is a dominant style of Romanization in the modern day, and that it is somehow responsible for names used in centuries past in spite of having existed since the leadup to the US sending support to China around WW2.<br /> <br /> Chiyou being a Hakken is on page 136, as is a lot of bizarre crowbarring of the Hakken into moving from the mainland to Japan. And also being responsible for a very post-Sengoku view of Bushido before Bushido - or the Buddhism that influenced it - existed. I don't think that there was a very concrete timeline for B&amp;S (heh) and that led to a lot of confusion about what was supposed to be going on on the Beast Courts side of things in the latter 4th Age.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-30T12:51:13-07:00","id":1549648,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549311#post1549311\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With that, to the best of my knowledge, every single Gift available to Garou through normal means is covered.<br /> <br /> If I've missed any please let me know! Someday I'll do a similar thread for Fera and a vaguely similar thread for Rites.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I found some missing:<br /> <br /> Techgnosis<br /> Lupus, Level 2.<br /> W20 Howls of the Apocalypse p. 8<br /> <br /> One on One<br /> Unique, Level 6<br /> W20 Icons of Rage p. 9<br /> <br /> Trapper\u2019s Bane<br /> Lupus, Level 2<br /> W20 Rage Across the World p. 81<br /> <br /> Free Flowing<br /> Singing Dog, Level 1<br /> W20 Rage Across the World p. 119<br /> <br /> Global Citizen<br /> Homid, Level 2<br /> W20 Rage Across the World p. 119<br /> <br /> Singing Dog\u2019s Cry<br /> Singing Dog, Level 3<br /> W20 Rage Across the World p. 119<br /> <br /> Speech of All Things<br /> Children of Gaia, Level 3<br /> W20 Rage Across the World p. 119","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4967,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","user":"lbeaumanior","date":". 02-15-2026, 08:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-31T03:29:17-07:00","id":1549662,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:16px\"><u><b>W20 Icons of Rage</b></u></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><u><b>One on One</b></u></span> (Level 6 Unique Gift)<br /> <br /> This is a very fitting Level 6 ability. Luna permits you the power to force yourself and a foe into a duel in which no one can interfere and nothing unsporting may take place. No escape, no illusions, no summoning help, nothing that would desecrate honourable combat. The Gift also teleports you to the target's location; so a Rank 6 Ahroun could say &quot;Adrian Newberry&quot;, teleport into the CEO's penthouse, and then slaughter him while no spirits could intervene and no escape would be possible.<br /> <br /> The downsides are that you can lose the fight, especially if you pick a target worthy of a Rank 6 Garou, that you burn a permanent point of Gnosis to activate the Gift, and that you are left standing where the enemy you teleported to was. So, with this Gift, you can travel to the heart of Malfeas and destroy one of the mighty Archdukes, but you're quite likely to be slaughtered in the moments that follow.<br /> <br /> This is very solid, all in all, and it has an Absoluteness which is great for Gifts, and especially high level Gifts. Burning a Gnosis point is awful, but it highlights how serious a threat you need to be going after for this Gift to make sense. This is a Gift which could define the ending of a Chronicle quite easily... though personally I think it would work better if you put this ability into a unique Talen containing an aspect of Luna herself, created specifically to kill some threat to the Garou in ancient times, and then lost. Found again for the first time in centuries, is it what the players need to take down Villain McVillainface?<br /> <br /> My other suggestion, if using this in a game, is to broaden it up a bit. The Talen or Totemic intervention or whatever brings the whole pack to face a suitable group. Both groups are aware that they will only be released when there's only one group standing, pack vs pack, pack vs swarm, you name it. That lets you include the whole table in an epic duel for the fate of a realm or nation, rather than putting one PC in a 1v1 with a single NPC.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><u><b>W20 Howls of the Apocalypse</b></u></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Techgnosis*</b></u></span> (Level 2 Lupus Gift)<br /> <br /> Spend 1 Gnosis, roll Wits + Primal-Urge (difficulty 7), and for the rest of the scene you can use your Primal-Urge instead of your Crafts, Drive, Etiquette, Firearms or Larceny. Spending 1 Willpower then broadens this list to include Academics, Computer, Law, Science and Technology.<br /> <br /> The flavour of this one is very neat; with the aid of Gnosis you enhance your instincts and call upon whatever you're working with (a book, a gun, a tank) to work with your instincts, reaching out to 'help you out' as you try to intuit your way through things. Given that you have 5+ points of Gnosis and this Gift lasts for a whole scene, allowing you to go from 0 dice to perhaps 5 dice in a useful Skill/Knowledge... I like this a lot. The ability for a Lupus Garou to experiment and intuit their way through life by calling upon the power of their instincts, and not rote learning, feels really beautiful.<br /> <br /> Note that this combines well with Heightened Senses, which gives you +1 to Primal-Urge for the scene. Pangaea also becomes a perfect place to craft stuff or do your homework, as you get +1 automatic success to all Primal-Urge rolls. If you can find a Totem or Fetish or Gift or Merit which helps with your Primal-Urge, granting more dice or successes or reducing the difficulty, absolutely go for it.<br /> <br /> On the assumption that you invest in the right setup for this Gift to be useful, that you're Lupus or perhaps even Metis, that you have plenty of Gnosis, that your Primal-Urge is 4+, that you do what you can to boost it, and that you use this Gift cleverly... the potential to save you 100s of xp is amazing.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><u><b>W20 Rage Across the World</b></u></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Free Flowing</b></u></span> (Level 1 Singing Dogs Gift)<br /> <br /> This Gift is entirely passive and lets you move through the air at your top speed, so long as you have something to grab, climb, or navigate the space with. Examples given are vines, ledges, branches, windows, laundry lines, rooftops and so on.<br /> <br /> So long as you aren't in a featureless void, this Gift is basically &quot;move for free on your turn, instead of spending an action or Splitting to do so, and also you can move rapidly through the air, up and down surfaces and so on, without any rolls&quot;. This, as a utility option, falls just short of flight. It's passive, no roll, no cost, and you're spending 5xp to get it. This, in my opinion, would be a must-have on any Garou if you happen to pass through New Guinea or encounter the Singing Dogs (or their Totem) in the Umbra.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Singing Dog's Cry*</b></u></span> (Level 3 Singing Dogs Gift)<br /> <br /> This Gift allows you to touch someone from a great distance for the purposes of supernatural abilities. So, for example, you could use the song of this Gift to touch a foe and slam them with Falling Touch from miles away, or heal them with Mother's Touch.<br /> <br /> The cost is 1 Gnosis, and a difficulty 7 Charisma + Expression is required. Every success lets you do a single 'touch' without having to activate the Gift again, so 1 Gnosis could become 5 touches if you roll 5 successes. You can also touch a bunch of targets all at once, in a single turn, by burning multiple successes at once, which might help with a Gift which impacts everyone you touch.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Mother's Touch</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Falling Touch</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Breath of the Wyld</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Scent of Sweet Honey</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Lover's Touch</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Electroshock</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Hidden Killer</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Render Down</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Living Treasure</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Under the Gun</b></span>, and various other Gifts will work with Singing Dog's Cry. Look at your Gifts, and the Gifts available to you, and be sure to ask your ST whether any of them can work via touch! Many Gifts don't specify their range or how you activate them, weirdly.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Global Citizen</b></u></span> (Level 2 Homid Gift)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Garou blends into different cultures, and inherently understands minor cultural traditions, faux pas, and quirks. To the untrained observer, she appears part of the group. Her skin and facial structure doesn\u2019t change, but nobody seems to notice any difference in heritage. </div> </div> </div>+3 difficulty to any roll relating to realising you aren't part of the culture, or to convince others you're an outsider. If you encounter unfamiliar behaviour you can magically puzzle it out with an Intelligence + Etiquette, variable difficulty. Now while you could presumably figure out alien customs with Etiquette even without this Gift, the quality of the information you recover will be better; &quot;this is a dominance hierarchy ritual, you MUST push back by showing how big and strong you are, perhaps in Crinos&quot; is more useful than &quot;well, 2 successes... he might be trying to see if he can successfully bully you? In some cultures you're meant to submit, in others you're meant to push back&quot;.<br /> <br /> The final benefit of the Gift is arguably the best, however. You can spend 1 point of Gnosis to get (Wisdom) points of Backgrounds, Allies and Contacts, which are temporary but can be used normally. That's just great. You are now a Garou who can spend a point of Gnosis and come back to your pack going &quot;alright, I spoke to the local doctors, vets, paramedics and nurses, and here's what's up with those dead homeless people-&quot;.<br /> <br /> This outdoes Assimilation, a Level 5 Gift. Green.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b><u>Trapper's Bane</u></b></span> (Level 2 Lupus Gift)<br /> <br /> <i>The Storyteller rolls Perception + Primal Urge when the werewolf comes within (Gnosis x 5) yards of a trap\u2019s trigger. The difficulty is 5 for the werewolf to detect simple snares, pits, and poisoned bait in the area. Identifying more technologically complex traps such as electrified objects and motion-activated traps is difficulty 6. Supernatural traps are difficulty 7. Additionally, whenever the werewolf approaches an ambush, the Storyteller rolls Perception + Primal Urge against difficulty 6 for the werewolf to recognize the ambush before it springs. The werewolf does not have to activate this Gift, it provides a \u201csixth sense\u201d at all times.</i><br /> <br /> This is a truly phenomenal Gift, and superior in many situations to a similar Ratkin / Gurahl Gift. As a Lupus you'll be detecting in a 30 yard radius by default, and the difficulty of this check should probably be reduced if you're in your Lupus form. Even if your ST doesn't adjust the difficulty, this is still the best 'danger sense' Gift the Garou get.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><u><b>Speech of All Things</b></u></span> (Level 3 Children of Gaia Gift)<br /> <br /> Passively understand any and all languages, and so long as you are physically capable of replying, you may do so. For example you could understand the whispers of Nagah, and you could try your best to reply with your own anatomy, probably producing something legible but making yourself sound a bit sssssilly.<br /> <br /> Writing and reading requires a roll, difficulty 7 or 9, and lasts for a scene. No cost, and for the most part the Gift is entirely passive. Great Gift, suits the Children of Gaia very well, and makes you a truly universal translator. You can understand a dead language remembered only to Mnesis, but not understood by the Mokole themselves, you can pick up the Wyrm's schemes in the ramblings of Banes whose tongues are too forbidden to be picked up with Spirit Speech, you can understand the strange faerie tongues of the Arcadian Sidhe, and so on.<br /> <br /> To me this is a must-have since it does its job so well. 9xp is a lot, arguably, but as a Child of Gaia you're going to want to be able to make peace between groups who don't understand one another. <hr /><br /> That's it! Thank you to <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar4967_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"4967\" data-vbnamecard=\"4967\">lbeaumanior</a> for helping me find a few Gifts I'd missed!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 02-16-2026, 05:02 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-02T08:06:59-07:00","id":1549733,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1549027#post1549027\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">. It's balanced and well written, (...)</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you very much. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Freelance Writer and Storyteller's Vault contributor. Find my work here: <a href=\"http://www.storytellersvault.com/index.php?affiliate_id=17903\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.storytellersvault.com/ind...liate_id=17903</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1535-jackob","name":"Jackob","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1535_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-02T04:04:33-07:00","id":1550575,"title":"","content":"Below I'm going to write up some summaries of how the Gift Lists of the various Breeds, Auspices and Tribes work out. This is intended as a sort of master-reference.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Green</b></span> Gifts are must-haves, being very powerful or essential, and generally I'd suggest anyone who can access them get them. <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Amber </b></span>Gifts are solid, they tend to be perfectly good, worth grabbing, but you could live without them. <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Red </b></span>Gifts are ones you'd tend to want to avoid. There are exceptions of course, but the ratings should give a good outline of which Gifts are excellent to have on your list and which ones aren't.<br /> <br /> Gifts in (Brackets) are added to the relevant list in Book of the Wyrm. Whether a non-Wyrmish Garou can learn these Gifts will depend on the Storyteller and the Gift in question. For example Power Surge, Visceral Agony and 1000 Teeth are quite reasonably just things a Homid, Metis and Lupus can respectively access now, but certain Gifts presented in that book are very clearly tied to the Wyrm by default.<br /> <br /> Gifts in [Brackets] are added to the relevant list in the Wyld West expansion pack. Whether you can learn these in other time periods will depend on your Storyteller. Some will have faded from use, while some will still be available but harder to find.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Homid</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Apecraft's Blessings</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>(Aura of Poison)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>City Running</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>[Jam Gun]</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Master of Fire</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Persuasion</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Smell of Man</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Global Citizen</b></span>,<span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b> Jam Technology</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Mark of the Wolf</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>(Power Surge)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Speech of the World</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>[Spirit Brand]</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Staredown</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Weaver's Eyes</b></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#c0392b\">(Call the Rust)</span></b>,<span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Calm the Savage Beast</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Cowing the Bullet</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Disquiet</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Rally the Troops</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Reshape Object</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Body Shift</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Bury the Wolf</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Cocoon</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>(Feast of Man-Flesh)</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Spirit Ward</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>[Weave of Steel]</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#f39c12\"><b>Web of Knowledge</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Assimilation</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#27ae60\"><b>Beyond Human</b></span>, <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>Part the Veil</b></span><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking#post1511902\">Rank 1</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1512873#post1512873\">Rank 2</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1513945#post1513945\">Rank 3</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1511902-garou-gifts-ranking-thinking-overthinking?p=1521448#post1521448\">Rank 4</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-